(209,3,NULL,'learn',10,'Teacher-guided lesson checklist','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','LESSON PLAN 3: ORAL LANGUAGE – GIVING AND FOLLOWING 1-TO-2- STEP ORAL DIRECTIONS IN DAILY SITUATIONS I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Give clear 1-2 step oral directions using polite language (e.g., \"can you,\" \"please\"); 2. Follow 1-2 step directions given by a peer; 3. Identify 2 key elements of effective directions (clarity, order) and polite communication. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Giving and Following 1-2-Step Directions (e.g., Asking a Sibling to Tidy Up Toys) References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and social skills development resources 45 Materials: Visual aid (chart) with: Polite phrase starters: \"Can you…?\", \"Please…\", \"Thank you!\" Action words with pictures: keep away, put in, pick up, close Toy props (dolls, cars, blocks, a toy box) Scenario cards (e.g., \"Your younger sister left toys on the floor\", \"Your brother left books on the couch\") Informal evaluation checklist III. PROCEDURE 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up (5 minutes): Lead a \"Follow My Lead\" game: Give 1-2 step directions (e.g., \"Pick up one block and put it on your desk\") and have the class do them together. This review following directions before moving to giving them. Motivation (5 minutes): Show a photo of a messy playroom with toys scattered. Ask: \"Have you ever seen a room like this? If your younger sister made this mess, what would you say to ask her to clean it up?\" Let 2-3 students share quick ideas, then say: \"Today, we’ll learn to ask nicely with clear steps so she knows exactly what to do!\" 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES (15 minutes) Introduce Polite Directions: Display the visual aid with polite phrases. Explain that when asking someone to do something, we use kind words like \"can you\" and \"please\" to be respectful. 46 Model Giving 2-Step Directions: Use the toy props to act out the scenario: \"Let’s pretend this doll is my younger sister, and these toys are on the floor. I’ll say: “‘Can you please keep your toys away and put them in the toy box?’\" Point to the action pictures (\"keep away,\" \"put in\") as you speak to show order. Practice Making Directions: Have the class repeat the model direction together. Then, guide them to make their own 2-step direction for the same scenario (e.g., \"Can you pick up your cars and put them on the shelf?\"). 3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION Ask: \"What polite words did I use when asking my sister?\" (Let students point to the visual aid.) Follow up: \"What was the first step I asked her to do? The second step? Why is order important?\" (e.g., \"So, she doesn’t get confused,\" \"So, she does everything needed\"). Ask: \"How do you think your sister would feel if you asked nicely vs. yelling?\" (Emphasize that polite language helps others want to help.) 4. GUIDED PRACTICE Role-Play Activity: Divide students into pairs. Give each pair a scenario card and toy props. 1. One student plays the \"older sibling\" and gives a clear 2-step direction using polite language. 2. The other student plays the \"younger sibling,\" listens, and follows the directions. 3. Pairs switch roles after 5 minutes. 47 Circulate around pairs to support: Remind them to use polite phrases, keep directions clear, and follow steps in order. Example scenario prompt for pairs: \"Your younger sister left her dolls on the bed. What 2 things will you ask her to do?\" IV. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student during the role-play: Uses polite Gives clear Listens language (can 2-step directions carefully and Criteria you, please) in the correct follows peer’s when giving order directions directions accurately Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by observing each pair once. Give positive feedback like, \"Great job asking nicely!\" or \"Perfect order of steps!\" V. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: Find a time to give one 2-step direction to a family member (sibling, parent, or grandparent) using polite language (e.g., \"Can you please pass the spoon and put it on my plate?\"). Ask the family member if they understood the directions. Share what direction they gave and how it went with the class in our next lesson.','assets/images/level1/lesson03/page-052.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson03/page-052.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson03/page-053.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson03/page-054.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson03/page-055.jpg\"]','LESSON PLAN 3: ORAL LANGUAGE – GIVING AND FOLLOWING 1-TO-2- STEP ORAL DIRECTIONS IN DAILY SITUATIONS I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Give clear 1-2 step oral directions using polite language (e.g., \"can you,\" \"please\"); 2. Follow 1-2 step directions given by a peer; 3. Identify 2 key elements of effective directions (clarity, order) and polite communication. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Giving and Following 1-2-Step Directions (e.g., Asking a Sibling to Tidy Up Toys) References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and social skills development resources 45 Materials: Visual aid (chart) with: Polite phrase starters: \"Can you…?\", \"Please…\", \"Thank you!\" Action words with pictures: keep away, put in, pick up, close Toy props (dolls, cars, blocks, a toy box) Scenario cards (e.g., \"Your younger sister left toys on the floor\", \"Your brother left books on the couch\") Informal evaluation checklist III. PROCEDURE 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up (5 minutes): Lead a \"Follow My Lead\" game: Give 1-2 step directions (e.g., \"Pick up one block and put it on your desk\") and have the class do them together. This review following directions before moving to giving them. Motivation (5 minutes): Show a photo of a messy playroom with toys scattered. Ask: \"Have you ever seen a room like this? If your younger sister made this mess, what would you say to ask her to clean it up?\" Let 2-3 students share quick ideas, then say: \"Today, we’ll learn to ask nicely with clear steps so she knows exactly what to do!\" 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES (15 minutes) Introduce Polite Directions: Display the visual aid with polite phrases. Explain that when asking someone to do something, we use kind words like \"can you\" and \"please\" to be respectful. 46 Model Giving 2-Step Directions: Use the toy props to act out the scenario: \"Let’s pretend this doll is my younger sister, and these toys are on the floor. I’ll say: “‘Can you please keep your toys away and put them in the toy box?’\" Point to the action pictures (\"keep away,\" \"put in\") as you speak to show order. Practice Making Directions: Have the class repeat the model direction together. Then, guide them to make their own 2-step direction for the same scenario (e.g., \"Can you pick up your cars and put them on the shelf?\"). 3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION Ask: \"What polite words did I use when asking my sister?\" (Let students point to the visual aid.) Follow up: \"What was the first step I asked her to do? The second step? Why is order important?\" (e.g., \"So, she doesn’t get confused,\" \"So, she does everything needed\"). Ask: \"How do you think your sister would feel if you asked nicely vs. yelling?\" (Emphasize that polite language helps others want to help.) 4. GUIDED PRACTICE Role-Play Activity: Divide students into pairs. Give each pair a scenario card and toy props. 1. One student plays the \"older sibling\" and gives a clear 2-step direction using polite language. 2. The other student plays the \"younger sibling,\" listens, and follows the directions. 3. Pairs switch roles after 5 minutes. 47 Circulate around pairs to support: Remind them to use polite phrases, keep directions clear, and follow steps in order. Example scenario prompt for pairs: \"Your younger sister left her dolls on the bed. What 2 things will you ask her to do?\" IV. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student during the role-play: Uses polite Gives clear Listens language (can 2-step directions carefully and Criteria you, please) in the correct follows peer’s when giving order directions directions accurately Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by observing each pair once. Give positive feedback like, \"Great job asking nicely!\" or \"Perfect order of steps!\" V. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: Find a time to give one 2-step direction to a family member (sibling, parent, or grandparent) using polite language (e.g., \"Can you please pass the spoon and put it on my plate?\"). Ask the family member if they understood the directions. Share what direction they gave and how it went with the class in our next lesson.',NULL,0,52,'LESSON PLAN 3: ORAL LANGUAGE – GIVING AND FOLLOWING 1-TO-2- STEP ORAL DIRECTIONS IN DAILY SITUATIONS I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Give clear 1-2 step oral directions using polite language (e.g., \"can you,\" \"please\"); 2. Follow 1-2 step directions given by a peer; 3. Identify 2 key elements of effective directions (clarity, order) and polite communication. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Giving and Following 1-2-Step Directions (e.g., Asking a Sibling to Tidy Up Toys) References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and social skills development resources 45 Materials: Visual aid (chart) with: Polite phrase starters: \"Can you…?\", \"Please…\", \"Thank you!\" Action words with pictures: keep away, put in, pick up, close Toy props (dolls, cars, blocks, a toy box) Scenario cards (e.g., \"Your younger sister left toys on the floor\", \"Your brother left books on the couch\") Informal evaluation checklist III. PROCEDURE 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up (5 minutes): Lead a \"Follow My Lead\" game: Give 1-2 step directions (e.g., \"Pick up one block and put it on your desk\") and have the class do them together. This review following directions before mo