n our next lesson.',NULL,0,56,'48 LESSON PLAN 4: ORAL LANGUAGE – USING DESCRIBING WORDS FOR PEOPLE, OBJECTS, THINGS, AND PLACES I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Use describing words to talk about people, objects, things, and places. (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.) 2. Match describing words to the attributes of what they are talking about. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Recognizing and Using Describing Words (Attributes: Color, Shape, Size, Height, Weight, Length, Distance) References: Adapted from elementary oral language curricula and vocabulary development resources Materials: Visual aid (chart) titled \"My Describing Word Toolkit\" – with words and pictures: red, round, big, tall, heavy, long, far, cozy Props with different attributes: colored blocks, toys (ball, box, car, doll), books (thick, thin), plants (tall, short), photos of places (sunny park, quiet room) \"Describe It!\" cards (with pictures of people, objects, or places) Informal evaluation checklist III. PROCEDURE 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up (5 minutes): Lead a \"Word Clue\" game: Say, \"I’m thinking of something in this room that’s red. Can you guess what it is?\" Have students call out guesses, then reveal the object. Repeat with 3-4 more clues (e.g., \"It’s round,\" \"It’s big\"). 49 Motivation (5 minutes): Show a plain drawing of a cat and a colorful, detailed drawing of the same cat. Ask: \"Which drawing tells you more about the cat? Why?\" Explain: \"Today, we’ll learn about ‘describing words’ – these are like magic clues that help us tell others exactly what something looks like, feels like, or where it is!\" 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES Introduce Describing Words: Display the \"Describing Word Toolkit\" chart. Point to each word and its picture, and say: \"These words describe attributes – what something is like. ‘Red’ is a color, ‘round’ is a shape, ‘big’ is a size, and ‘far’ is a distance.\" Have students repeat eac