te to support: Remind students to use words from the toolkit and match them to the picture’s attributes. IV. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student during the pair activity: Recognizes Uses describing Matches and names at words to talk about describing Criteria least 4 people/objects/places words to the describing correct words attributes Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by listening to each pair’s descriptions. Give positive feedback like, \"Great use of ‘cozy’ for the bedroom!\" or \"Perfect – ‘tall’ matches the boy!\" V. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: 1. Pick one thing at home (a toy, a pet, a room) and think of 3 describing words for it. 2. Tell a family member about it using those words (e.g., \"My dog is brown, fluffy, and small\"). Be ready to share their description with the class in our next lesson.',0,1,'none'),
(212,4,NULL,'learn',14,'Additional intervention materials','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','29 Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Use describing words for people, objects, things and places (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.) Instructions: Show a red ball. Teacher says: Tell me something about the object I am holding. Rubrics 1 2 3 4 Pupil says “ball” Pupil says “a Pupil says “A Pupil says “It is ball” red ball” a red ball.” (or tell more something about it.) Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Talk about topics of interest Instruction: Tell me what you think about this picture. Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Asking Questions Instructions: Things you should know: • Number of Players: 2-10 • Setup: Choose 1 player to be “it,” and have them think of a secret person, place or thing. • Object of the game: Other players attempt to guess the secret person, place, or thing, but they only get 20 questions to do so.','assets/images/level1/lesson04/page-036.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson04/page-036.jpg\"]','29 Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Use describing words for people, objects, things and places (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.) Instructions: Show a red ball. Teacher says: Tell me something about the object I am holding. Rubrics 1 2 3 4 Pupil says “ball” Pupil says “a Pupil says “A Pupil says “It is ball” red ball” a red ball.” (or tell more something about it.) Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Talk about topics of interest Instruction: Tell me what you think about this picture. Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Asking Questions Instructions: Things you should know: • Number of Players: 2-10 • Setup: Choose 1 player to be “it,” and have them think of a secret person, place or thing. • Object of the game: Other players attempt to guess the secret person, place, or thing, but they only get 20 questions to do so.',NULL,0,36,'29 Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Use describing words for people, objects, things and places (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.) Instructions: Show a red ball. Teacher says: Tell me something about the object I am holding. Rubrics 1 2 3 4 Pupil says “ball” Pupil says “a Pupil says “A Pupil says “It is ball” red ball” a red ball.” (or tell more something about it.) Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Talk about topics of interest Instruction: Tell me what you think about this picture. Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Asking Questions Instructions: Things you should know: • Number of Players: 2-10 • Setup: Choose 1 player to be “it,” and have them think of a secret person, place or thing. • Object of the game: Other players attempt to guess the secret person, place, or thing, but they only get 20 questions to do so.',0,1,'none'),
(213,5,NULL,'learn',10,'Teacher-guided lesson checklist','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','52 LESSON PLAN 5: ORAL LANGUAGE – TALKING ABOUT TOPICS OF INTEREST THROUGH PICTURE DISCUSSION I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Share their thoughts, feelings, and observations about a picture on a topic of interest; 2. Use simple sentence starters to organize their ideas clearly; 3. Listen respectfully to peers’ thoughts and ask one follow-up question. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Talking About Topics of Interest (e.g., Toys, Pets, Parks, Festivals) \"Tell Me What You Think About This Picture\" References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and expressive language development resources Materials: Visual aid (chart) with sentence starters: \"I see…\", \"I think…\", \"I like…\", \"It makes me feel…\" Colorful pictures of kid-friendly topics (e.g., a dog playing in a park, a birthday party with toys, a colorful festival, a cozy pet corner) \"Picture Card Packs\" (one pack per small group, with 3-4 pictures on different interests) informal evaluation checklist. 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up Lead a \"Quick Share\" circle: Ask students to say one sentence about something they love (e.g., \"I love my cat\" or \"I love playing with blocks\"). Encourage them to speak clearly and look at the class. valuation checklist 53 Motivation Hold up a bright picture of a dog playing in a sunny park. Ask: \"Who likes dogs? What do you see in this picture? What do you think the dog is feeling?\" Let 2-3 students call out quick ideas, then say: \"Today, we’ll learn to share our thoughts about pictures we like – and hear what our friends think too!\" 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES Introduce Picture Discussion: Display the visual aid with sentence starters. Explain: \"When we talk about a picture, these sentences help us share what’s in our minds clearly. ‘I see’ tells what’s in the picture, ‘I think’ tells our ideas, and ‘I like’ tells what we enjoy about it.\" Model Sharing Thoughts: Focus on the dog park picture and share your own ideas: \"I see a brown dog running with a red ball. I think the dog is happy because it’s playing in the sun. I like this picture because it makes me want to go to the park too!\" Point to each sentence starter on the chart as you speak. Practice Sentence Starters: Have the class repeat the sentence starters together. Then, guide them to share one thought about the dog picture using one of the starters (e.g., \"I see green grass\" or \"It makes me feel happy\"). 3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION Ask: \"What did I do to help you understand my thoughts about the picture?\" (Let students point to the sentence starters.) Follow up: \"Why is it fun to talk about pictures we like?\" (e.g., \"Because we can share what we love,\" \"We can learn what our friends like too\"). Remind them: \"When others are talking, we listen carefully – that’s how we show we care about their ideas. We can even ask a small question, like ‘Why do you like that?’ to learn more!\" 54 4. GUIDED PRACTICE \"Picture Talk\" Groups: Divide students into small groups (4-5 students each) and give each group a \"Picture Card Pack\" with topics they’ll enjoy. 1. One student picks a picture from the pack and shares their thoughts using at least 2 sentence starters. 2. The other students listen, then one peer asks a follow-up question (e.g., \"What do you think the cat in the picture is eating?\"). 3. Everyone in the group takes a turn picking a picture and sharing. Circulate around groups to support: Remind them to use the sentence starters, speak clearly, and listen respectfully. III. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student during group discussion: Shares Uses at least 2 Listens thoughts/observations sentence respectfully to Criteria about the picture starters to peers and asks organize ideas a follow-up question Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by observing each student’s participation. Give positive feedback like, \"Great use of ‘I think’!\" or \"Nice question – that helped us learn more!\" IV. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: 1. - Find a picture at home (in a book, on a calendar, or on a device with family help) of something they love. 2. - Talk to a family member about it using the sentence starters: \"I see…\", \"I think…\", \"I like…\". Be ready to share their picture and thoughts with the class in our next lesson.','assets/images/level1/lesson05/page-060.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson05/page-060.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson05/page-061.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson05/page-062.jpg\"]','52 LESSON PLAN 5: ORAL LANGUAGE – TALKING ABOUT TOPICS OF INTEREST THROUGH PICTURE DISCUSSION I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Share their thoughts, feelings, and observations about a picture on a topic of interest; 2. Use simple sentence starters to organize their ideas clearly; 3. Listen respectfully to peers’ thoughts and ask one follow-up question. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Talking About Topics of Interest (e.g., Toys, Pets, Parks, Festivals) \"Tell Me What You Think About This Picture\" References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and expressive language development resources Materials: Visual aid (chart) with sentence starters: \"I see…\", \"I think…\", \"I like…\", \"It makes me feel…\" Colorful pictures of kid-friendly topics (e.g., a dog playing in a park, a birthday party with toys, a colorful festival, a cozy pet corner) \"Picture Card Packs\" (one pack per small group, with 3-4 pictures on different interests) informal evaluation checklist. 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up Lead a \"Quick Share\" circle: Ask students to say one sentence about something they love (e.g., \"I love my cat\" or \"I love playing with blocks\"). Encourage them to speak clearly and look at the class. valuation checklist 53 Motivation Hold up a bright picture of a dog playing in a sunny park. Ask: \"Who likes dogs? What do you see in this picture? What do you think the dog is feeling?\" Let 2-3 students call out quick ideas, then say: \"Today, we’ll learn to share our thoughts about pictures we like – and hear what our friends think too!\" 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES Introduce Picture Discussion: Display the visual aid with sentence starters. Explain: \"When we talk about a picture, these sentences help us share what’s in our minds clearly. ‘I see’ tells what’s in the picture, ‘I think’ tells our ideas, and ‘I like’ tells what we enjoy about it.\" Model Sharing Thoughts: Focus on the dog park picture and share your own ideas: \"I see a brown dog running with a red ball. I think the dog is happy because it’s playing in the sun. I like this picture because it makes me want to go to the park too!\" Point to each sentence starter on the chart as you speak. Practice Sentence Starters: Have the class repeat the sentence starters together. Then, guide them to share one thought about the dog picture using one of the starters (e.g., \"I see green grass\" or \"It makes me feel happy\"). 3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION Ask: \"What did I do to help you understand my thoughts about the picture?\" (Let students point to the sentence starters.) Follow up: \"Why is it fun to talk about pictures we like?\" (e.g., \"Because we can share what we love,\" \"We can learn what our friends like too\"). Remind them: \"When others are talking, we listen carefully – that’s how we show we care about their ideas. We can even ask a small question, like ‘Why do you like that?’ to learn more!\" 54 4. GUIDED PRACTICE \"Picture Talk\" Groups: Divide students into small groups (4-5 students each) and give each group a \"Picture Card Pack\" with topics they’ll enjoy. 1. One student picks a picture from the pack and shares their thoughts using at least 2 sentence starters. 2. The other students listen, then one peer asks a follow-up question (e.g., \"What do you think the cat in the picture is eating?\"). 3. Everyone in the group takes a turn picking a picture and sharing. Circulate around groups to support: Remind them to use the sentence starters, speak clearly, and listen respectfully. III. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student during group discussion: Shares Uses at least 2 Listens thoughts/observations sentence respectfully to Criteria about the picture starters to peers and asks organize ideas a follow-up question Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by observing each student’s participation. Give positive feedback like, \"Great use of ‘I think’!\" or \"Nice question – that helped us learn more!\" IV. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: 1. - Find a picture at home (in a book, on a calendar, or on a device with family help) of something they love. 2. - Talk to a family member about it using the sentence starters: \"I see…\", \"I think…\", \"I like…\". Be ready to share their picture and thoughts with the class in our next lesson.',NULL,0,60,'52 LESSON PLAN 5: ORAL LANGUAGE – TALKING ABOUT TOPICS OF INTEREST THROUGH PICTURE DISCUSSION I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Share their thoughts, feelings, and observations about a picture on a topic of interest; 2. Use simple sentence starters to organize their ideas clearly; 3. Listen respectfully to peers’ thoughts and ask one follow-up question. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Talking About Topics of Interest (e.g., Toys, Pets, Parks, Festivals) \"Tell Me What You Think About This Picture\" References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and expressive language development resources Materials: Visual aid (chart) with sentence starters: \"I see…\", \"I think…\", \"I like…\", \"It makes me feel…\" Colorful pictures of kid-friendly topics (e.g., a dog playing in a park, a birthday party with toys, a colorful festival, a cozy pet corner) \"Picture Card Packs\" (one pack per small group, with 3-4 pictures on different interests) informal evaluation checklist. 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up Lead a \"Quick Share\" circle: Ask students to say one sentence about something they love (e.g., \"I love my cat\" or \"I love playing with blocks\"). Encourage them to speak clearly and look at the class. valuation checklist 53 Motivation Hold up a bright picture of a dog playing in a sunny park. Ask: \"Who likes dogs? What do you see in this picture? What do you think the dog is feeling?\" Let 2-3 students call out quick ideas, then say: \"Today, we’ll learn to share our thoughts about pictures we like – and hear what our friends think too!\" 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES Introduce Picture Discussion: Display the visual aid with sentence starters. Explain: \"When we talk about a picture, these sentences help us share what’s in our minds clearly. ‘I see’ tells what’s in the picture, ‘I think’ tells our ideas, and ‘I like’ tells what we enjoy about it.\" Model Sharing Thoughts: Focus on the dog park picture and share your own ideas: \"I see a brown dog running with a red ball. I think the dog is happy because it’s playing in the sun. I like this picture because it makes me want to go to the park too!\" Point to each sentence starter on the chart as you speak. Practice Sentence Starters: Have the class repeat the sentence starters together. Then, guide them to share one thought about the dog picture using one of the starters (e.g., \"I see green grass\" or \"It makes me feel happy\"). 3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION Ask: \"What did I do to help you understand my thoughts about the picture?\" (Let students point to the sentence starters.) Follow up: \"Why is it fun to talk about pictures we like?\" (e.g., \"Because we can share what we love,\" \"We can learn what our friends like too\"). Remind them: \"When others are talking, we listen carefully – that’s how we show we care about their ideas. We can even ask a small question, like ‘Why do you like that?’ to learn more!\" 54 4. GUIDED PRACTICE \"Picture Talk\" Groups: Divide students into small groups (4-5 students each) and give each group a \"Picture Card Pack\" with topics they’ll enjoy. 1. One student picks a picture from the pack and shares their thoughts using at least 2 sentence starters. 2. The other students listen, then one peer asks a follow-up question (e.g., \"What do you think the cat in the picture is eating?\"). 3. Everyone in the group takes a turn picking a picture and sharing. Circulate around groups to support: Remind them to use the sentence starters, speak clearly, and listen respectfully. III. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student during group discussion: Shares Uses at least 2 Listens thoughts/observations sentence respectfully to Criteria about the picture starters to peers and asks organize ideas a follow-up question Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by observing each student’s participation. Give positive feedback like, \"Great use of ‘I think’!\" or \"Nice question – that helped us learn more!\" IV. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: 1. - Find a picture at home (in a book, on a calendar, or on a device with family help) of something they love. 2. - Talk to a family member about it using the sentence starters: \"I see…\", \"I think…\", \"I like…\". Be ready to share their picture and thoughts with the class in our next lesson.',0,1,'none'),
(214,5,NULL,'learn',11,'Additional intervention materials','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','29 Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Use describing words for people, objects, things and places (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.) Instructions: Show a red ball. Teacher says: Tell me something about the object I am holding. Rubrics 1 2 3 4 Pupil says “ball” Pupil says “a Pupil says “A Pupil says “It is ball” red ball” a red ball.” (or tell more something about it.) Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Talk about topics of interest Instruction: Tell me what you think about this picture. Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Asking Questions Instructions: Things you should know: • Number of Players: 2-10 • Setup: Choose 1 player to be “it,” and have them think of a secret person, place or thing. • Object of the game: Other players attempt to guess the secret person, place, or thing, but they only get 20 questions to do so.\n\n30 \"This game works best with a small to medium-sized group of people, so that everyone gets a chance to ask a question. Choose a random player to be “it.” This could be the youngest player in the group, the player who’s had the most recent birthday, or something silly, like who can eat a piece of pizza the fastest. Question 1 Think of a person, place, or thing for other players to guess if you’re “it. ”Think of someone or something that you know enough about to answer some basic questions about, and keep it a secret! If you choose a person, they can be living, deceased, or even fictional. Make sure you choose a person, place, or thing that most people in your group know about. • For example, your item could be “Manny Pacquiao,” since he is famous enough that most people will be able to make guesses about him. You could also choose something like your town, Philippines, or even clouds or the sun. • Avoid items like “my mom” or “my dog” unless you are with your siblings or best friends, since the players might not know enough about them to guess. Question 2 Ask yes-or-no questions if you\'re not \"it. \"If you’re not “it,” your goal is to figure out what the “it” person is thinking of by asking questions to narrow down the possibilities. Try using a fairly general opening question that can be answered with “yes” or “no” to narrow down your options.\n\n31 For example: • “Is it alive?” • “Is it an object?” • “Is it fictional?” • “Is it bigger than a toaster? Question 3 Take turns asking more specific yes-or-no questions to narrow it down. You can ask questions in any order that you’d like, but make sure each player gets to ask at least 1 question. If a player asks a question that can’t be answered with “yes” or “no,” ask them to rephrase it so that it can be, since the person who’s “it” can only say “yes” or “no.” • For example, a player couldn’t ask, “How old are they?” or “What do they look like?” They could ask, “Are they older than 50?” or, “Do they have blonde hair?” Think about the questions that have already been asked before you ask new questions. For example, you could go from \"Is it a person?\" to \"Is it a man?\" to \"Are they alive?\" to \"Do they live in the Philippines?\" Play until you reach 10 questions or someone guesses the right answer. Become the next “it” person if you guess the correct answer. The person who guesses the right answer is next to be “it,” and they think up their own secret person, place, or thing. If no one guessed the person, place, or thing at all, whoever wants to go next can have a turn. Keep the game going until everyone has had a chance to be “it.” • If someone guesses correctly but they’ve already been “it,” let someone else have a turn instead. Giving everyone a turn makes the game more inclusive and lets everybody have some fun!','assets/images/level1/lesson05/page-036.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson05/page-036.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson05/page-037.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson05/page-038.jpg\"]','29 Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Use describing words for people, objects, things and places (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.) Instructions: Show a red ball. Teacher says: Tell me something about the object I am holding. Rubrics 1 2 3 4 Pupil says “ball” Pupil says “a Pupil says “A Pupil says “It is ball” red ball” a red ball.” (or tell more something about it.) Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Talk about topics of interest Instruction: Tell me what you think about this picture. Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Asking Questions Instructions: Things you should know: • Number of Players: 2-10 • Setup: Choose 1 player to be “it,” and have them think of a secret person, place or thing. • Object of the game: Other players attempt to guess the secret person, place, or thing, but they only get 20 questions to do so.\n\n30 \"This game works best with a small to medium-sized group of people, so that everyone gets a chance to ask a question. Choose a random player to be “it.” This could be the youngest player in the group, the player who’s had the most recent birthday, or something silly, like who can eat a piece of pizza the fastest. Question 1 Think of a person, place, or thing for other players to guess if you’re “it. ”Think of someone or something that you know enough about to answer some basic questions about, and keep it a secret! If you choose a person, they can be living, deceased, or even fictional. Make sure you choose a person, place, or thing that most people in your group know about. • For example, your item could be “Manny Pacquiao,” since he is famous enough that most people will be able to make guesses about him. You could also choose something like your town, Philippines, or even clouds or the sun. • Avoid items like “my mom” or “my dog” unless you are with your siblings or best friends, since the players might not know enough about them to guess. Question 2 Ask yes-or-no questions if you\'re not \"it. \"If you’re not “it,” your goal is to figure out what the “it” person is thinking of by asking questions to narrow down the possibilities. Try using a fairly general opening question that can be answered with “yes” or “no” to narrow down your options.\n\n31 For example: • “Is it alive?” • “Is it an object?” • “Is it fictional?” • “Is it bigger than a toaster? Question 3 Take turns asking more specific yes-or-no questions to narrow it down. You can ask questions in any order that you’d like, but make sure each player gets to ask at least 1 question. If a player asks a question that can’t be answered with “yes” or “no,” ask them to rephrase it so that it can be, since the person who’s “it” can only say “yes” or “no.” • For example, a player couldn’t ask, “How old are they?” or “What do they look like?” They could ask, “Are they older than 50?” or, “Do they have blonde hair?” Think about the questions that have already been asked before you ask new questions. For example, you could go from \"Is it a person?\" to \"Is it a man?\" to \"Are they alive?\" to \"Do they live in the Philippines?\" Play until you reach 10 questions or someone guesses the right answer. Become the next “it” person if you guess the correct answer. The person who guesses the right answer is next to be “it,” and they think up their own secret person, place, or thing. If no one guessed the person, place, or thing at all, whoever wants to go next can have a turn. Keep the game going until everyone has had a chance to be “it.” • If someone guesses correctly but they’ve already been “it,” let someone else have a turn instead. Giving everyone a turn makes the game more inclusive and lets everybody have some fun!',NULL,0,36,'29 Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Use describing words for people, objects, things and places (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.) Instructions: Show a red ball. Teacher says: Tell me something about the object I am holding. Rubrics 1 2 3 4 Pupil says “ball” Pupil says “a Pupil says “A Pupil says “It is ball” red ball” a red ball.” (or tell more something about it.) Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Talk about topics of interest Instruction: Tell me what you think about this picture. Additional Intervention Material Learning Competency: Asking Questions Instructions: Things you should know: • Number of Players: 2-10 • Setup: Choose 1 player to be “it,” and have them think of a secret person, place or thing. • Object of the game: Other players attempt to guess the secret person, place, or thing, but they only get 20 questions to do so.\n\n30 \"This game works best with a small to medium-sized group of people, so that everyone gets a chance to ask a question. Choose a random player to be “it.” This could be the youngest player in the group, the player who’s had the most recent birthday, or something silly, like who can eat a piece of pizza the fastest. Question 1 Think of a person, place, or thing for other players to guess if you’re “it. ”Think of someone or something that you know enough about to answer some basic questions about, and keep it a secret! If you choose a person, they can be living, deceased, or even fictional. Make sure you choose a person, place, or thing that most people in your group know about. • For example, your item could be “Manny Pacquiao,” since he is famous enough that most people will be able to make guesses about him. You could also choose something like your town, Philippines, or even clouds or the sun. • Avoid items like “my mom” or “my dog” unless you are with your siblings or best friends, since the players might not know enough about them to guess. Question 2 Ask yes-or-no questions if you\'re not \"it. \"If you’re not “it,” your goal is to figure out what the “it” person is thinking of by asking questions to narrow down the possibilities. Try using a fairly general opening question that can be answered with “yes” or “no” to narrow down your options.\n\n31 For example: • “Is it alive?” • “Is it an object?” • “Is it fictional?” • “Is it bigger than a toaster? Question 3 Take turns asking more specific yes-or-no questions to narrow it down. You can ask questions in any order that you’d like, but make sure each player gets to ask at least 1 question. If a player asks a question that can’t be answered with “yes” or “no,” ask them to rephrase it so that it can be, since the person who’s “it” can only say “yes” or “no.” • For example, a player couldn’t ask, “How old are they?” or “What do they look like?” They could ask, “Are they older than 50?” or, “Do they have blonde hair?” Think about the questions that have already been asked before you ask new questions. For example, you could go from \"Is it a person?\" to \"Is it a man?\" to \"Are they alive?\" to \"Do they live in the Philippines?\" Play until you reach 10 questions or someone guesses the right answer. Become the next “it” person if you guess the correct answer. The person who guesses the right answer is next to be “it,” and they think up their own secret person, place, or thing. If no one guessed the person, place, or thing at all, whoever wants to go next can have a turn. Keep the game going until everyone has had a chance to be “it.” • If someone guesses correctly but they’ve already been “it,” let someone else have a turn instead. Giving everyone a turn makes the game more inclusive and lets everybody have some fun!',0,1,'none'),
(215,6,NULL,'learn',7,'Teacher-guided lesson checklist','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','55 LESSON PLAN 6: ORAL LANGUAGE – DESCRIBE AND DRAW I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Use specific describing words (color, shape, size, position) to clearly describe a picture; 2. Listen carefully to a peer’s description and draw what they hear; 3. Identify 2-3 key details that make a description easy to understand. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: \"Describe and Draw\" – Using Oral Language to Share Visual Details References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and expressive/receptive language development resources Materials: Teacher’s model picture (colorful, with clear details: e.g., a red bird on a green tree branch next to a yellow flower) Set of \"secret pictures\" (one per pair, different from each other, with simple, distinct elements) Paper and crayons for each student Visual aid (chart) with key describing words: color words, round, square, big, small, on top, next to, under Informal evaluation checklist III. PROCEDURE 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up Lead a \"Quick Draw\" game: Say, \"I’m going to describe something—draw what you hear!\" Describe a simple object (e.g., \"a small, blue square\") and have students draw it. Show their drawings and talk about how clear words helped them. Motivation Hold up your model picture (but don’t show it to the class yet). Say: \"I have a secret picture! If I describe it well enough, you’ll be able to draw it without even seeing it. Want to try? Today, we’ll play a ‘Describe and Draw’ game where we’re like ‘picture messengers’—using words to send images to our friends!\" 56 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES Introduce Key Description Details: Display the visual aid. Explain: \"When we describe a picture, we need to share details like what color things are, what shape they are, how big they are, and where they are. These details help the person drawing know exactly what to put on their paper.\" Teacher Modeling: Show the model picture to the class for 10 seconds, then hide it. Describe it slowly and clearly: \"First, there’s a big, green tree branch in the middle. On top of the branch, there’s a small, red bird with a yellow beak. Next to the bird, there’s a bright yellow flower with five petals. The sky behind is light blue.\" As you describe, point to the words on the visual aid. Have students draw along as you speak. Check and Discuss: Show the model picture again and let students compare their drawings. Ask: \"What details helped you draw it right?\" (e.g., \"‘on top’ told me where the bird was,\" \"‘red’ told me its color\"). 3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION Ask: \"What would happen if I forgot to say ‘small’ about the bird? Or ‘next to the bird’ about the flower?\" (Let students share: \"I might draw a big bird,\" \"I might put the flower somewhere else\"). Follow up: \"Why is it important to describe things in order (e.g., ‘first the branch, then the bird’)?\" (e.g., \"So the drawer knows what to draw first,\" \"It’s easier to follow\"). Remind students: \"As a describer, your job is to be clear. As a drawer, your job is to listen closely—no peeking at the picture!\" 4. GUIDED PRACTICE Pair \"Describe and Draw\" Activity: Divide students into pairs. Give one student in each pair a \"secret picture\" (they must not show it 57 to their partner). Assign roles: Describer (has the picture) and Drawer (listens and draws). 1. The Describer looks at their picture and describes it using key details and the visual aid words. 2. The Drawer listens carefully and draws what they hear—they can ask clarifying questions (e.g., \"Is the cat big or small?\"). 3. After 5 minutes, have pairs switch roles with a new secret picture. Circulate around pairs to support: Remind Describers to use specific details and Drawers to listen actively. IV. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student in both roles Uses specific Captures key Listens Criteria describing words details from the respectfully and (color, shape, description asks clarifying position) As Drawer questions (when As Describer drawing) Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by observing pairs and looking at their drawings. Give positive feedback like, \"Great job saying ‘under the tree’—that helped your partner draw it right!\" V. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: 1. Pick a favorite toy at home (e.g., a doll, car, teddy bear). 2. Describe the toy in detail to a family member, who will draw it based on their description. 3. Bring both the toy and the family’s drawing to share with the class in our next lesson.','assets/images/level1/lesson06/page-063.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson06/page-063.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson06/page-064.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson06/page-065.jpg\"]','55 LESSON PLAN 6: ORAL LANGUAGE – DESCRIBE AND DRAW I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Use specific describing words (color, shape, size, position) to clearly describe a picture; 2. Listen carefully to a peer’s description and draw what they hear; 3. Identify 2-3 key details that make a description easy to understand. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: \"Describe and Draw\" – Using Oral Language to Share Visual Details References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and expressive/receptive language development resources Materials: Teacher’s model picture (colorful, with clear details: e.g., a red bird on a green tree branch next to a yellow flower) Set of \"secret pictures\" (one per pair, different from each other, with simple, distinct elements) Paper and crayons for each student Visual aid (chart) with key describing words: color words, round, square, big, small, on top, next to, under Informal evaluation checklist III. PROCEDURE 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up Lead a \"Quick Draw\" game: Say, \"I’m going to describe something—draw what you hear!\" Describe a simple object (e.g., \"a small, blue square\") and have students draw it. Show their drawings and talk about how clear words helped them. Motivation Hold up your model picture (but don’t show it to the class yet). Say: \"I have a secret picture! If I describe it well enough, you’ll be able to draw it without even seeing it. Want to try? Today, we’ll play a ‘Describe and Draw’ game where we’re like ‘picture messengers’—using words to send images to our friends!\" 56 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES Introduce Key Description Details: Display the visual aid. Explain: \"When we describe a picture, we need to share details like what color things are, what shape they are, how big they are, and where they are. These details help the person drawing know exactly what to put on their paper.\" Teacher Modeling: Show the model picture to the class for 10 seconds, then hide it. Describe it slowly and clearly: \"First, there’s a big, green tree branch in the middle. On top of the branch, there’s a small, red bird with a yellow beak. Next to the bird, there’s a bright yellow flower with five petals. The sky behind is light blue.\" As you describe, point to the words on the visual aid. Have students draw along as you speak. Check and Discuss: Show the model picture again and let students compare their drawings. Ask: \"What details helped you draw it right?\" (e.g., \"‘on top’ told me where the bird was,\" \"‘red’ told me its color\"). 3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION Ask: \"What would happen if I forgot to say ‘small’ about the bird? Or ‘next to the bird’ about the flower?\" (Let students share: \"I might draw a big bird,\" \"I might put the flower somewhere else\"). Follow up: \"Why is it important to describe things in order (e.g., ‘first the branch, then the bird’)?\" (e.g., \"So the drawer knows what to draw first,\" \"It’s easier to follow\"). Remind students: \"As a describer, your job is to be clear. As a drawer, your job is to listen closely—no peeking at the picture!\" 4. GUIDED PRACTICE Pair \"Describe and Draw\" Activity: Divide students into pairs. Give one student in each pair a \"secret picture\" (they must not show it 57 to their partner). Assign roles: Describer (has the picture) and Drawer (listens and draws). 1. The Describer looks at their picture and describes it using key details and the visual aid words. 2. The Drawer listens carefully and draws what they hear—they can ask clarifying questions (e.g., \"Is the cat big or small?\"). 3. After 5 minutes, have pairs switch roles with a new secret picture. Circulate around pairs to support: Remind Describers to use specific details and Drawers to listen actively. IV. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student in both roles Uses specific Captures key Listens Criteria describing words details from the respectfully and (color, shape, description asks clarifying position) As Drawer questions (when As Describer drawing) Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by observing pairs and looking at their drawings. Give positive feedback like, \"Great job saying ‘under the tree’—that helped your partner draw it right!\" V. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: 1. Pick a favorite toy at home (e.g., a doll, car, teddy bear). 2. Describe the toy in detail to a family member, who will draw it based on their description. 3. Bring both the toy and the family’s drawing to share with the class in our next lesson.',NULL,0,63,'55 LESSON PLAN 6: ORAL LANGUAGE – DESCRIBE AND DRAW I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to: 1. Use specific describing words (color, shape, size, position) to clearly describe a picture; 2. Listen carefully to a peer’s description and draw what they hear; 3. Identify 2-3 key details that make a description easy to understand. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: \"Describe and Draw\" – Using Oral Language to Share Visual Details References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula and expressive/receptive language development resources Materials: Teacher’s model picture (colorful, with clear details: e.g., a red bird on a green tree branch next to a yellow flower) Set of \"secret pictures\" (one per pair, different from each other, with simple, distinct elements) Paper and crayons for each student Visual aid (chart) with key describing words: color words, round, square, big, small, on top, next to, under Informal evaluation checklist III. PROCEDURE 1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES Warm-Up Lead a \"Quick Draw\" game: Say, \"I’m going to describe something—draw what you hear!\" Describe a simple object (e.g., \"a small, blue square\") and have students draw it. Show their drawings and talk about how clear words helped them. Motivation Hold up your model picture (but don’t show it to the class yet). Say: \"I have a secret picture! If I describe it well enough, you’ll be able to draw it without even seeing it. Want to try? Today, we’ll play a ‘Describe and Draw’ game where we’re like ‘picture messengers’—using words to send images to our friends!\" 56 2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES Introduce Key Description Details: Display the visual aid. Explain: \"When we describe a picture, we need to share details like what color things are, what shape they are, how big they are, and where they are. These details help the person drawing know exactly what to put on their paper.\" Teacher Modeling: Show the model picture to the class for 10 seconds, then hide it. Describe it slowly and clearly: \"First, there’s a big, green tree branch in the middle. On top of the branch, there’s a small, red bird with a yellow beak. Next to the bird, there’s a bright yellow flower with five petals. The sky behind is light blue.\" As you describe, point to the words on the visual aid. Have students draw along as you speak. Check and Discuss: Show the model picture again and let students compare their drawings. Ask: \"What details helped you draw it right?\" (e.g., \"‘on top’ told me where the bird was,\" \"‘red’ told me its color\"). 3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION Ask: \"What would happen if I forgot to say ‘small’ about the bird? Or ‘next to the bird’ about the flower?\" (Let students share: \"I might draw a big bird,\" \"I might put the flower somewhere else\"). Follow up: \"Why is it important to describe things in order (e.g., ‘first the branch, then the bird’)?\" (e.g., \"So the drawer knows what to draw first,\" \"It’s easier to follow\"). Remind students: \"As a describer, your job is to be clear. As a drawer, your job is to listen closely—no peeking at the picture!\" 4. GUIDED PRACTICE Pair \"Describe and Draw\" Activity: Divide students into pairs. Give one student in each pair a \"secret picture\" (they must not show it 57 to their partner). Assign roles: Describer (has the picture) and Drawer (listens and draws). 1. The Describer looks at their picture and describes it using key details and the visual aid words. 2. The Drawer listens carefully and draws what they hear—they can ask clarifying questions (e.g., \"Is the cat big or small?\"). 3. After 5 minutes, have pairs switch roles with a new secret picture. Circulate around pairs to support: Remind Describers to use specific details and Drawers to listen actively. IV. EVALUATION Use an informal checklist to assess each student in both roles Uses specific Captures key Listens Criteria describing words details from the respectfully and (color, shape, description asks clarifying position) As Drawer questions (when As Describer drawing) Excellent Good Satisfactory Note: Assess by observing pairs and looking at their drawings. Give positive feedback like, \"Great job saying ‘under the tree’—that helped your partner draw it right!\" V. AGREEMENT For homework, students will: 1. Pick a favorite toy at home (e.g., a doll, car, teddy bear). 2. Describe the toy in detail to a family member, who will draw it based on their description. 3. Bring both the toy and the family’s drawing to share with the class in our next lesson.',0,1,'none'),
(216,6,NULL,'learn',8,'Additional intervention materials','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','32 Additional Intervention Material DESCRIBE and DRAW Instructions: 1. Show a picture. The picture might be a page from a storybook that the children are familiar with, or a picture like one of these. 2. Ask children what they see. “What do you see in the pictures?” or “What do you notice?” Learners’ answers may vary. Accept their responses. We want to get children to begin to describe what they see in their own words. • “I see a house.” • “I see a door.” • “I see a girl flying.” • “I see a weird tree.” • “I see squares.” 3. Take the children\'s lead and respond to their ideas by asking follow-up questions. • \"Where do you see the squares? Where do you see the tree?\" • \"Can you tell me more about the ?\" • \"What do you notice about what’s in the back corner?\" • \"How do you know that this is a triangle? Are those both triangles?\" 4. Ask students to draw what they see. • Keep the picture available; this is not a memory task. • This is not a tracing task either. We are asking children to draw what they see. (Expect a wide variety!) • Children often begin by drawing portions of the picture (just the tree, just the oval for the body of the tree, etc.)\n\n33 5. Move around and check in with them as they are drawing. You can ask questions based on what they drew to get them to add detail to their drawing. It is important to remember that the children should not think that the drawing has to look exactly like the picture. “You draw what YOU see.” (You might want to remind children that what they see might be different from what someone else sees.) 6. As children finish, ask them to tell you about what they drew. Older children could describe their own drawing to a partner, and vice versa. After discussion, children should go back and add detail to their own drawing. 7. Consider sharing a couple of examples of children\'s drawings and ask the child to describe to the class what they drew. Let other children add what they see. Use this as an opportunity to highlight one idea you want to engage the group in (perhaps noticing a particular shape, or using specific language such as above or below, or describing where an object is in relation to other objects).\n\n34','assets/images/level1/lesson06/page-039.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson06/page-039.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson06/page-040.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson06/page-041.jpg\"]','32 Additional Intervention Material DESCRIBE and DRAW Instructions: 1. Show a picture. The picture might be a page from a storybook that the children are familiar with, or a picture like one of these. 2. Ask children what they see. “What do you see in the pictures?” or “What do you notice?” Learners’ answers may vary. Accept their responses. We want to get children to begin to describe what they see in their own words. • “I see a house.” • “I see a door.” • “I see a girl flying.” • “I see a weird tree.” • “I see squares.” 3. Take the children\'s lead and respond to their ideas by asking follow-up questions. • \"Where do you see the squares? Where do you see the tree?\" • \"Can you tell me more about the ?\" • \"What do you notice about what’s in the back corner?\" • \"How do you know that this is a triangle? Are those both triangles?\" 4. Ask students to draw what they see. • Keep the picture available; this is not a memory task. • This is not a tracing task either. We are asking children to draw what they see. (Expect a wide variety!) • Children often begin by drawing portions of the picture (just the tree, just the oval for the body of the tree, etc.)\n\n33 5. Move around and check in with them as they are drawing. You can ask questions based on what they drew to get them to add detail to their drawing. It is important to remember that the children should not think that the drawing has to look exactly like the picture. “You draw what YOU see.” (You might want to remind children that what they see might be different from what someone else sees.) 6. As children finish, ask them to tell you about what they drew. Older children could describe their own drawing to a partner, and vice versa. After discussion, children should go back and add detail to their own drawing. 7. Consider sharing a couple of examples of children\'s drawings and ask the child to describe to the class what they drew. Let other children add what they see. Use this as an opportunity to highlight one idea you want to engage the group in (perhaps noticing a particular shape, or using specific language such as above or below, or describing where an object is in relation to other objects).\n\n34',NULL,0,39,'32 Additional Intervention Material DESCRIBE and DRAW Instructions: 1. Show a picture. The picture might be a page from a storybook that the children are familiar with, or a picture like one of these. 2. Ask children what they see. “What do you see in the pictures?” or “What do you notice?” Learners’ answers may vary. Accept their responses. We want to get children to begin to describe what they see in their own words. • “I see a house.” • “I see a door.” • “I see a girl flying.” • “I see a weird tree.” • “I see squares.” 3. Take the children\'s lead and respond to their ideas by asking follow-up questions. • \"Where do you see the squares? Where do you see the tree?\" • \"Can you tell me more about the ?\" • \"What do you notice about what’s in the back corner?\" • \"How do you know that this is a triangle? Are those both triangles?\" 4. Ask students to draw what they see. • Keep the picture available; this is not a memory task. • This is not a tracing task either. We are asking children to draw what they see. (Expect a wide variety!) • Children often begin by drawing portions of the picture (just the tree, just the oval for the body of the tree, etc.)\n\n33 5. Move around and check in with them as they are drawing. You can ask questions based on what