(219,8,NULL,'learn',10,'Teacher-guided lesson checklist','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','64 LESSON PLAN 8: ORAL LANGUAGE USING PICTURE TALK ACTIVITIES I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Describe visible details in a picture using simple words and phrases. 2. Ask and answer basic questions about the picture (who, what, where, when, why). 3. Share a short personal connection to the picture in 1-2 clear sentences. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Oral Language Development through Picture Talk Activities: \"See & Say,\" \"Picture Question Circle,\" \"My Connection Share\" Materials: 1 large, colorful, detailed picture (e.g., a group of children playing at a park with slides, swings, trees, a dog, and ice cream vendor) Smaller copies of the same picture for each group (optional) Whiteboard or chart paper Marker \"Question Cards\" (with who/what/where/when/why written on them) Skills: Speaking, listening, observing, questioning Values Integration: Sharing, active listening, appreciating others’ experiences III. . LEARNING PROCEDURE A. MOTIVATION \"Mystery Picture Peek:\" Hold up the large picture with most of it covered, showing only a small part (e.g., the dog’s tail). Ask: \"What do you think this is? What do you think is in the rest of the picture?\" Let 3-4 students guess, then reveal the full picture with a big \"ta-da!\" to build excitement. 65 B. LESSON PROPER 1. ACTIVITY 1: SEE & SAY Display the large picture prominently. Explain: \"Let’s play \'See & Say\'! Look closely at the picture, and when you see something, stand up and say what it is. For example: \'I see a slide!\'\" Let students take turns sharing what they notice. As they speak, write key words on the board (e.g., children, park, slide, dog, ice cream). Encourage more details: \"Great! What color is the slide?\" or \"How many children do you see?\" Check-in: Point to words on the board and ask students to point to the corresponding item in the picture. 2. ACTIVITY 2: PICTURE QUESTION CIRCLE Have students sit in a large circle. Give out the 5 \"Question Cards\" to 5 different students. Instructions: 1. The student with the \"who\" card asks: \"Who is in the picture?\" Let 2-3 students answer. 2. Pass the card to the next person, then the \"what\" card holder asks: \"What are the children doing?\" 3. Continue with \"where\" (\"Where are they?\"), \"when\" (\"When do you think this is happening?\"), and \"why\" (\"Why do you think they’re happy?\"). Model how to ask questions clearly and how to listen before answering. For shy students, offer sentence starters: \"I think they’re at...\" or \"Maybe it’s because...\" 3. ACTIVITY 3: \"MY CONNECTION SHARE\" Explain: \"Now, think about a time you did something like what’s in the picture. Can you share 1 or 2 sentences about it?\" 66 Model with your own connection: \"I remember going to the park with my friend. We played on the swings and ate ice cream!\" Let students share in pairs first (to build confidence), then invite 4-5 pairs to share their connections with the class. Praise all contributions: \"That’s a wonderful story! I’m glad you had fun at the park too.\" C. GENERALIZATION Gather students back together and ask: \"What did we learn today about pictures?\" (Expected answers: \"Pictures have many things to see,\" \"We can ask questions about them,\" \"We can tell stories about our own lives from them.\") Conclude: \"Pictures are like secret doors to talking and sharing stories. You can use any picture to practice speaking!\" IV. EVALUATION Formative Assessment: Ask each student to say one thing they saw in the picture or one connection they made. Use a simple smiley face checklist to record if they spoke clearly and participated V. ASSIGNMENT At home, find a picture in a book or on a calendar. Tell your family 3 things you see in it and one time you did something similar. Be ready to share with the class tomorrow! VI. REFLECTION (For teacher’s use only: Note which students struggled with questioning/sharing connections, which parts of the picture sparked the most discussion, and adjustments for future lessons.) Picture Description (for the main visual): A bright, sunny park with green grass and tall trees. Three children (2 girls, 1 boy) are playing – one on the slide, one on the swings, and one petting a brown dog. An ice cream vendor with a colorful cart stands nearby, handing an ice cream cone to a girl. There are blue skies with white clouds, and a bench where a woman is sitting and reading a book.','assets/images/level1/lesson08/page-072.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson08/page-072.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson08/page-073.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson08/page-074.jpg\"]','64 LESSON PLAN 8: ORAL LANGUAGE USING PICTURE TALK ACTIVITIES I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Describe visible details in a picture using simple words and phrases. 2. Ask and answer basic questions about the picture (who, what, where, when, why). 3. Share a short personal connection to the picture in 1-2 clear sentences. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Oral Language Development through Picture Talk Activities: \"See & Say,\" \"Picture Question Circle,\" \"My Connection Share\" Materials: 1 large, colorful, detailed picture (e.g., a group of children playing at a park with slides, swings, trees, a dog, and ice cream vendor) Smaller copies of the same picture for each group (optional) Whiteboard or chart paper Marker \"Question Cards\" (with who/what/where/when/why written on them) Skills: Speaking, listening, observing, questioning Values Integration: Sharing, active listening, appreciating others’ experiences III. . LEARNING PROCEDURE A. MOTIVATION \"Mystery Picture Peek:\" Hold up the large picture with most of it covered, showing only a small part (e.g., the dog’s tail). Ask: \"What do you think this is? What do you think is in the rest of the picture?\" Let 3-4 students guess, then reveal the full picture with a big \"ta-da!\" to build excitement. 65 B. LESSON PROPER 1. ACTIVITY 1: SEE & SAY Display the large picture prominently. Explain: \"Let’s play \'See & Say\'! Look closely at the picture, and when you see something, stand up and say what it is. For example: \'I see a slide!\'\" Let students take turns sharing what they notice. As they speak, write key words on the board (e.g., children, park, slide, dog, ice cream). Encourage more details: \"Great! What color is the slide?\" or \"How many children do you see?\" Check-in: Point to words on the board and ask students to point to the corresponding item in the picture. 2. ACTIVITY 2: PICTURE QUESTION CIRCLE Have students sit in a large circle. Give out the 5 \"Question Cards\" to 5 different students. Instructions: 1. The student with the \"who\" card asks: \"Who is in the picture?\" Let 2-3 students answer. 2. Pass the card to the next person, then the \"what\" card holder asks: \"What are the children doing?\" 3. Continue with \"where\" (\"Where are they?\"), \"when\" (\"When do you think this is happening?\"), and \"why\" (\"Why do you think they’re happy?\"). Model how to ask questions clearly and how to listen before answering. For shy students, offer sentence starters: \"I think they’re at...\" or \"Maybe it’s because...\" 3. ACTIVITY 3: \"MY CONNECTION SHARE\" Explain: \"Now, think about a time you did something like what’s in the picture. Can you share 1 or 2 sentences about it?\" 66 Model with your own connection: \"I remember going to the park with my friend. We played on the swings and ate ice cream!\" Let students share in pairs first (to build confidence), then invite 4-5 pairs to share their connections with the class. Praise all contributions: \"That’s a wonderful story! I’m glad you had fun at the park too.\" C. GENERALIZATION Gather students back together and ask: \"What did we learn today about pictures?\" (Expected answers: \"Pictures have many things to see,\" \"We can ask questions about them,\" \"We can tell stories about our own lives from them.\") Conclude: \"Pictures are like secret doors to talking and sharing stories. You can use any picture to practice speaking!\" IV. EVALUATION Formative Assessment: Ask each student to say one thing they saw in the picture or one connection they made. Use a simple smiley face checklist to record if they spoke clearly and participated V. ASSIGNMENT At home, find a picture in a book or on a calendar. Tell your family 3 things you see in it and one time you did something similar. Be ready to share with the class tomorrow! VI. REFLECTION (For teacher’s use only: Note which students struggled with questioning/sharing connections, which parts of the picture sparked the most discussion, and adjustments for future lessons.) Picture Description (for the main visual): A bright, sunny park with green grass and tall trees. Three children (2 girls, 1 boy) are playing – one on the slide, one on the swings, and one petting a brown dog. An ice cream vendor with a colorful cart stands nearby, handing an ice cream cone to a girl. There are blue skies with white clouds, and a bench where a woman is sitting and reading a book.',NULL,0,72,'64 LESSON PLAN 8: ORAL LANGUAGE USING PICTURE TALK ACTIVITIES I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Describe visible details in a picture using simple words and phrases. 2. Ask and answer basic questions about the picture (who, what, where, when, why). 3. Share a short personal connection to the picture in 1-2 clear sentences. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Oral Language Development through Picture Talk Activities: \"See & Say,\" \"Picture Question Circle,\" \"My Connection Share\" Materials: 1 large, colorful, detailed picture (e.g., a group of children playing at a park with slides, swings, trees, a dog, and ice cream vendor) Smaller copies of the same picture for each group (optional) Whiteboard or chart paper Marker \"Question Cards\" (with who/what/where