ay the picture on a screen or print it out for the children to see. This allows everyone to focus on the same visual stimulus.',NULL,0,45,'38 Story 11 \"The Leaf on the Bench\" \"I sat on the park bench and picked up a leaf from the ground. It was crimson—a bright, deep red—like a little fire! I pressed it between two pages of my notebook to keep it safe. It’s my favorite leaf ever.\" Unknown word: crimson (a dark red color) Story 12 \"The Cookie Cutter in the Kitchen\" \"I was helping mom bake cookies when I found a tiny cookie cutter in the drawer. It had an intricate shape—like a little star with lots of points! We used it to make star cookies, and they tasted so good.\" Unknown word: intricate (having many small, detailed parts) Story 13 \"The Button at the Bus Stop\" \"While waiting for the bus, I found a button on the ground. It was silver and it glinted in the sunlight. It is like a tiny moon! I showed it to the bus driver. The driver helped me put it in the ‘lost and found’ box.\" Unknown word: glinted (shone with a quick, bright flash) Additional Intervention Material PICTURE TALK Picture talk activities are engaging ways to enhance children\'s communication skills, imagination, and comprehension through discussions bas ed on images. What is a Picture Talk Activity? A picture talk activity involves using images to spark discussions among children. This method encourages kids to observe, analyze, and express their thoughts about the pictures, fostering their communication and literacy skills. It can be adapted to various topics, making it a versatile tool for learning. Steps to Create Picture Talk Activities 1. Choose a Topic: Select a theme that interests the children, such as nature, family, or sports. This helps to engage them more effectively. 2. Select a Picture: Find a picture that aligns with the chosen topic. It should be visually stimulating and rich in details to encourage discussion. 3. Project the Image: Display the picture on a screen or print it out for the children to see. This allows everyone to focus on the same visual stimulus.',0,1,'none'),
(221,9,NULL,'learn',8,'Teacher-guided lesson checklist','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','67 LESSON PLAN 9: RECITE, CLAP & SMILE – “Little Voices Shining Through Poems\" I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Recite a short, familiar poem or nursery rhyme with a clear, loud voice. 2. Match their tone (happy, silly, soft) to the poem’s feeling. 3. Use simple gestures or actions to go with key words in the poem. 4. Listen respectfully to peers’ recitations and give a positive \"thumbs up.\" II. MATERIALS Picture Poem Pack: Short, colorful poems/nursery rhymes with big images (e.g., \"Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star,\" \"Baa, Baa, Black Sheep,\" \"Five Little Ducks,\" \"The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\"). Audio clips of fun, upbeat recitations (with sound effects, e.g., a \"quack\" for ducks, \"twinkle\" chimes). Visual \"Smile & Shine Recitation” Checklist\" (with emojis: = clear voice, = happy tone, = gestures). Simple props (stuffed animals, toy spiders, paper stars, tiny hats) for performances. A \"Shining Star\" sticker for each student. III. PROCEDURE A. Warm-Up: \"Rhyme & Clap Dance\" Gather students in a circle. The teacher says a rhyming word pair (e.g., \"cat – hat\") and claps twice. Students repeat the words and clap back. Add simple dances: \"dog – frog\" → jump like a frog; \"bird – word\" → flap wings. Goal: Get them moving, practicing rhymes, and using their voices confidently. 68 B. Motivation The Itsy-Bitsy Spider by Anonymous Itsy bitsy spider climbed up the waterspout. down came the rain and washed the spider out; out came the sun and dried up all the rain; and the itsy-bitsy spider climbed up the spout again. Hold up a picture poem book, \"The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\" and point to the images. Ask: \"Who knows this poem? What does the spider do?\" Play a 1-minute audio clip of the poem with a silly, wiggly voice and \"spider crawl\" sound effects. C. LESSON PROPER \"How did the voice sound? Was it happy? Silly?\" Explain: \"Today, we’ll pick a poem, practice saying it with our best voice, and add dances – just like the audio!\" Main Activity: \"My Turn to Recite!\" (15 minutes) Break into 3 simple, hands-on steps: Step 1: Pick & Explore (5 minutes) Lay out the Picture Poem Pack on tables. Students choose a poem they know or like (based on the pictures). The teacher helps each student point to the words as they say the poem aloud once (to practice reading along with their voice). Example: A student picks \"Five Little Ducks\" and points to each duck as they count down. Step 2: Practice with a Buddy & Props (7 minutes) Pair students up (buddy up a confident reciter with a quieter one). 69 Give each pair a prop (e.g., stuffed duck for \"Five Little Ducks,\" toy spider for \"Itsy Bitsy\"). Buddies take turns reciting: Use the Smile & Shine Checklist to remind each other: \"Speak loud!\" \"Smile!\" \"Add a dance!\" Example: For \"Twinkle, Twinkle,\" students hold up paper stars and twirl them in the air when they say \"twinkle.\" Step 3: Polish with the Teacher (3 minutes) The teacher circulates to listen to 1–2 lines from each student, giving quick feedback: \"Great job wiggling like a spider!\" or \"Try speaking a little louder so I can hear you better!\" Closure: \"Little Voices Showcase\" (5 minutes) Invite 4–5 volunteers to recite their poem for the class (or have all pairs share in small \"mini-circles\" if time is tight). After each recitation, the class gives a positive response: \"Thumbs up for [student’s name]!\" or a quick clap. Ask 1–2 students: \"What was fun about reciting your poem?\" (e.g., \"Jumping like a duck!\") End with a class cheer: \"Recite, clap, smile – our little voices shine!\" Give every student a \"Shining Star\" sticker for participating. IV. EVALUATION (Formative) Use the visual Smile & Shine Checklist to observe: Clear, audible voice Tone matches the poem’s feeling (happy, silly, etc.) Simple gestures/actions to support key words Observe participation in the warm-up, buddy practice, and showcase. Check that students listen respectfully to peers.','assets/images/level1/lesson09/page-075.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson09/page-075.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson09/page-076.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson09/page-077.jpg\"]','67 LESSON PLAN 9: RECITE, CLAP & SMILE – “Little Voices Shining Through Poems\" I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Recite a short, familiar poem or nursery rhyme with a clear, loud voice. 2. Match their tone (happy, silly, soft) to the poem’s feeling. 3. Use simple gestures or actions to go with key words in the poem. 4. Listen respectfully to peers’ recitations and give a positive \"thumbs up.\" II. MATERIALS Picture Poem Pack: Short, colorful poems/nursery rhymes with big images (e.g., \"Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star,\" \"Baa, Baa, Black Sheep,\" \"Five Little Ducks,\" \"The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\"). Audio clips of fun, upbeat recitations (with sound effects, e.g., a \"quack\" for ducks, \"twinkle\" chimes). Visual \"Smile & Shine Recitation” Checklist\" (with emojis: = clear voice, = happy tone, = gestures). Simple props (stuffed animals, toy spiders, paper stars, tiny hats) for performances. A \"Shining Star\" sticker for each student. III. PROCEDURE A. Warm-Up: \"Rhyme & Clap Dance\" Gather students in a circle. The teacher says a rhyming word pair (e.g., \"cat – hat\") and claps twice. Students repeat the words and clap back. Add simple dances: \"dog – frog\" → jump like a frog; \"bird – word\" → flap wings. Goal: Get them moving, practicing rhymes, and using their voices confidently. 68 B. Motivation The Itsy-Bitsy Spider by Anonymous Itsy bitsy spider climbed up the waterspout. down came the rain and washed the spider out; out came the sun and dried up all the rain; and the itsy-bitsy spider climbed up the spout again. Hold up a picture poem book, \"The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\" and point to the images. Ask: \"Who knows this poem? Wha