tive) Use the visual Smile & Shine Checklist to observe: Clear, audible voice Tone matches the poem’s feeling (happy, silly, etc.) Simple gestures/actions to support key words Observe participation in the warm-up, buddy practice, and showcase. Check that students listen respectfully to peers.',NULL,0,75,'67 LESSON PLAN 9: RECITE, CLAP & SMILE – “Little Voices Shining Through Poems\" I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Recite a short, familiar poem or nursery rhyme with a clear, loud voice. 2. Match their tone (happy, silly, soft) to the poem’s feeling. 3. Use simple gestures or actions to go with key words in the poem. 4. Listen respectfully to peers’ recitations and give a positive \"thumbs up.\" II. MATERIALS Picture Poem Pack: Short, colorful poems/nursery rhymes with big images (e.g., \"Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star,\" \"Baa, Baa, Black Sheep,\" \"Five Little Ducks,\" \"The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\"). Audio clips of fun, upbeat recitations (with sound effects, e.g., a \"quack\" for ducks, \"twinkle\" chimes). Visual \"Smile & Shine Recitation” Checklist\" (with emojis: = clear voice, = happy tone, = gestures). Simple props (stuffed animals, toy spiders, paper stars, tiny hats) for performances. A \"Shining Star\" sticker for each student. III. PROCEDURE A. Warm-Up: \"Rhyme & Clap Dance\" Gather students in a circle. The teacher says a rhyming word pair (e.g., \"cat – hat\") and claps twice. Students repeat the words and clap back. Add simple dances: \"dog – frog\" → jump like a frog; \"bird – word\" → flap wings. Goal: Get them moving, practicing rhymes, and using their voices confidently. 68 B. Motivation The Itsy-Bitsy Spider by Anonymous Itsy bitsy spider climbed up the waterspout. down came the rain and washed the spider out; out came the sun and dried up all the rain; and the itsy-bitsy spider climbed up the spout again. Hold up a picture poem book, \"The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\" and point to the images. Ask: \"Who knows this poem? Wha