(222,10,NULL,'learn',7,'Teacher-guided lesson checklist','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','70 LESSON PLAN 10: TALK, PLAY & SHARE – LITTLE VOICES CONNECTING Communicative Language Teaching (CLT) Strategy I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Greet peers and ask simple questions (e.g., \"What’s your name?\" \"What do you have?\"). 2. Share personal information about a favorite toy (e.g., \"This is my teddy. He’s soft.\"). 4. Collaborate with a friend to create a short, simple \"toy story\" using spoken words. 5. Listen respectfully to peers and respond with a simple comment (e.g., \"Cool!\" \"I like it too!\"). II. MATERIAL Stuffed toys or toy pictures (one per student; or let students bring their own favorite toy). \"Talking Flashcards\" with simple questions/responses (e.g., \"What’s this?\" → \"It’s a...\"). A \"Chat Box\" with small items (toy car, doll, ball) for picking prompts. Visual \"Talk & Connect Checklist\" (emojis: = greet, = ask, = share, = listen). \"Friendship Sticker\" for each student. III. PROCEDURE 1. Preparatory Activities A. Warm-Up/Motivation: \"Hello, Friend! Circle\" (5 minutes) Gather students in a circle. The teacher models: \"Hi! My name is [Teacher’s name]. How are you, [Student’s name]?\" 71 Students take turns greeting the friend next to them, using the same pattern. Add a fun twist: they can wave, high-five, or do a silly dance while greeting. Goal: Practice real, face-to-face interaction and basic greetings. 2. Developmental Activities Introduction: \"Our Toys, Our Stories\" (5 minutes) Hold up a stuffed toy and say: \"This is my bunny. Her name is Floppy. She likes to jump!\" Ask: \"Who has a toy at home? What’s your toy like?\" Let 2–3 students share quickly. 3. Analysis/Discussion Explain: \"Today, we’ll talk to our friends about our toys—ask them questions, share stories, and play together with words!\" Show the Talk & Connect Checklist and point to each emoji: \"These are our super skills for talking today!\" 4. Guided Practice Main Activity: \"Chat & Play Stations\" Divide students into 3 small groups. Rotate stations every 5 minutes (keep it fast-paced for little attention spans!). Station 1: \"Toy Show & Tell Chat\" Task: Students take turns showing their toy (or picture) and sharing 1–2 things about it (e.g., \"This is my car. It goes ‘vroom!’\"). Peer Interaction: After each share, friends ask a simple question (using flashcards as a guide): \"What color is it?\" or \"Do you like it?\" Why it’s CLT: Real sharing and question-asking about personal things. Station 2: \"Find a Toy Match\" Task: Students walk around the station with their toy/picture. They ask each other: \"Do you have a [car/doll/teddy]?\" Goal: Find a friend with the same type of toy. Once they match, they high-five and say: \"We both have a [toy]!\" Why it’s CLT: Real-world \"searching and matching\" conversation. 72 Station 3: \"Make a Toy Story Together\" Task: Pairs pick a toy from the Chat Box. They create a 2-sentence story together (e.g., \"This is a ball. The ball bounces to the dog!\"). Bonus: Add simple actions (bounce like a ball, bark like a dog) while saying the story. Why it’s CLT: Collaborative communication to create something meaningful. Closure: \"Share Our Toy Stories\" (5 minutes) Invite 3–4 pairs to share their short toy story with the class. After each story, the class responds with a positive comment: \"That’s funny!\" or \"I love your ball! “Ask 1–2 students: \"What was fun about talking to your friend today?\" (e.g., \"I found a friend with a teddy too!\"). End with a class cheer: \"Talk, play, share—we’re great at caring and sharing!\" Give every student a \"Friendship Sticker\" for using their talking skills. IV. EVALUATION Use the visual Talk & Connect Checklist to observe: Greets peers appropriately. Asks simple questions. Shares ideas clearly (even if words are simple). Listens when others talk. Note how students interact—do they take turns? Do they respond to their friends’ words? No formal testing—focus on participation and effort to communicate.','assets/images/level1/lesson10/page-078.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson10/page-078.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson10/page-079.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson10/page-080.jpg\"]','70 LESSON PLAN 10: TALK, PLAY & SHARE – LITTLE VOICES CONNECTING Communicative Language Teaching (CLT) Strategy I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Greet peers and ask simple questions (e.g., \"What’s your name?\" \"What do you have?\"). 2. Share personal information about a favorite toy (e.g., \"This is my teddy. He’s soft.\"). 4. Collaborate with a friend to create a short, simple \"toy story\" using spoken words. 5. Listen respectfully to peers and respond with a simple comment (e.g., \"Cool!\" \"I like it too!\"). II. MATERIAL Stuffed toys or toy pictures (one per student; or let students bring their own favorite toy). \"Talking Flashcards\" with simple questions/responses (e.g., \"What’s this?\" → \"It’s a...\"). A \"Chat Box\" with small items (toy car, doll, ball) for picking prompts. Visual \"Talk & Connect Checklist\" (emojis: = greet, = ask, = share, = listen). \"Friendship Sticker\" for each student. III. PROCEDURE 1. Preparatory Activities A. Warm-Up/Motivation: \"Hello, Friend! Circle\" (5 minutes) Gather students in a circle. The teacher models: \"Hi! My name is [Teacher’s name]. How are you, [Student’s name]?\" 71 Students take turns greeting the friend next to them, using the same pattern. Add a fun twist: they can wave, high-five, or do a silly dance while greeting. Goal: Practice real, face-to-face interaction and basic greetings. 2. Developmental Activities Introduction: \"Our Toys, Our Stories\" (5 minutes) Hold up a stuffed toy and say: \"This is my bunny. Her name is Floppy. She likes to jump!\" Ask: \"Who has a toy at home? What’s your toy like?\" Let 2–3 students share quickly. 3. Analysis/Discussion Explain: \"Today, we’ll talk to our friends about our toys—ask them questions, share stories, and play together with words!\" Show the Talk & Connect Checklist and point to each emoji: \"These are our super skills for talking today!\" 4. Guided Practice Main Activity: \"Chat & Play Stations\" Divide students into 3 small groups. Rotate stations every 5 minutes (keep it fast-paced for little attention spans!). Station 1: \"Toy Show & Tell Chat\" Task: Students take turns showing their toy (or picture) and sharing 1–2 things about it (e.g., \"This is my car. It goes ‘vroom!’\"). Peer Interaction: After each share, friends ask a simple question (using flashcards as a guide): \"What color is it?\" or \"Do you like it?\" Why it’s CLT: Real sharing and question-asking about personal things. Station 2: \"Find a Toy Match\" Task: Students walk around the station with their toy/picture. They ask each other: \"Do you have a [car/doll/teddy]?\" Goal: Find a friend with the same type of toy. Once they match, they high-five and say: \"We both have a [toy]!\" Why it’s CLT: Real-world \"searching and matching\" conversation. 72 Station 3: \"Make a Toy Story Together\" Task: Pairs pick a toy from the Chat Box. They create a 2-sentence story together (e.g., \"This is a ball. The ball bounces to the dog!\"). Bonus: Add simple actions (bounce like a ball, bark like a dog) while saying the story. Why it’s CLT: Collaborative communication to create something meaningful. Closure: \"Share Our Toy Stories\" (5 minutes) Invite 3–4 pairs to share their short toy story with the class. After each story, the class responds with a positive comment: \"That’s funny!\" or \"I love your ball! “Ask 1–2 students: \"What was fun about talking to your friend today?\" (e.g., \"I found a friend with a teddy too!\"). End with a class cheer: \"Talk, play, share—we’re great at caring and sharing!\" Give every student a \"Friendship Sticker\" for using their talking skills. IV. EVALUATION Use the visual Talk & Connect Checklist to observe: Greets peers appropriately. Asks simple questions. Shares ideas clearly (even if words are simple). Listens when others talk. Note how students interact—do they take turns? Do they respond to their friends’ words? No formal testing—focus on participation and effort to communicate.',NULL,0,78,'70 LESSON PLAN 10: TALK, PLAY & SHARE – LITTLE VOICES CONNECTING Communicative Language Teaching (CLT) Strategy I. OBJECTIVES By the end of the lesson, students will be able to: 1. Greet peers and ask simple questions (e.g., \"What’s your name?\" \"What do you have?\"). 2. Share personal information about a favorite toy (e.g., \"This is my teddy. He’s soft.\"). 4. Collaborate with a friend to create a short, simple \"toy story\" using spoken words. 5. Listen respectfully to peers and respond with a simple comment (e.g., \"Cool!\" \"I like it too!\"). II. MATERIAL Stuffed toys or toy pictures (one per student; or let students bring their own favorite toy). \"Talking Flashcards\" with simple questions/responses (e.g., \"What’s this?\" → \"It’s a...\"). A \"Chat Box\" with small items (toy car, doll, ball) for picking prompts. Visual \"Talk & Connect Checklist\" (emojis: = greet, = ask, = share, = listen). \"Friendship Sticker\" for each student. III. PROCEDURE 1. Preparatory Activities A. Warm-Up/Motivation: \"Hello, Friend! Circle\" (5 minutes) Gather students in a circle. The teacher models: \"Hi! My name is [Teacher’s name]. How are you, [Student’s name]?\" 71 Students take turns greeting the friend next to them, using the same pattern. Add a fun twist: they can wave, high-five, or do a silly dance while greeting. Goal: Practice real, face-to-face interaction and basic greetings. 2. Developmental Activities Introduction: \"Our Toys, Our Stories\" (5 minutes) Hold up a stuffed toy and say: \"This is my bunny. Her name is Floppy. She likes to jump!\" Ask: \"Who has a toy at home? What’s your toy like?\" Let 2–3 students share quickly. 3. Analys