e visual Talk & Connect Checklist to observe: Greets peers appropriately. Asks simple questions. Shares ideas clearly (even if words are simple). Listens when others talk. Note how students interact—do they take turns? Do they respond to their friends’ words? No formal testing—focus on participation and effort to communicate.',0,1,'none'),
(223,11,NULL,'learn',8,'Teacher-guided lesson checklist','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','73 LESSON PLAN 11: ORAL LANGUAGE – WORD FRIENDS (WORD ASSOCIATION) I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the lesson, the pupils are expected to: 1. Think of words that go together (e.g., “cat” → “milk,” “paws,” “meow”). 2. Find 3 ways words connect: same meaning, opposites, or things that go together. 3. Share their word friends and explain why they connect. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Word Friends (How Words Connect) Materials: Big pictures (cat, cake, park, sun). Toy props (toy cat, ball, small umbrella). Smiley face stickers. Simple chart with examples: Same meaning: “happy” → “glad.” Opposites: “big” → “small.” Things that go together: “bread” → “butter.” III. PROCEDURE 1. Warm-Up: “Word Catch!” (5 minutes) Game: Toss a soft ball. Teacher says a word (e.g., “red”). Student catches the ball and says a word friend (e.g., “apple”). Class claps if it makes sense! 2. Motivation: “Guess My Word Friend!” (5 minutes) Hold up a toy cat. Ask: “What words do you think of? Meow? Milk? Paws?” Say: “Today, we’ll play with word friends—words that love to go together!” 74 3. Activity 1: “Word Friends on the Board” (10 minutes) Step 1: Stick a picture of a cat on the board. Ask: “What words go with cat?” Students shout out (e.g., “milk,” “tail”). Add their words with sticky notes. Step 2: Show how words connect: “Milk” → goes with cat. “Small” → opposite of big. “Happy” → same as glad. 4. Activity 2: “Word Chain Game” (10 minutes) Groups of 4: Sit in circles. Teacher gives a starting word (e.g., “sun”). Each student adds a word friend (e.g., “sun” → “hot” → “ice cream” → “sprinkles”). Use props (e.g., toy umbrella for “rain”) to help them think! 5. Wrap-Up: “Why Word Friends?” (5 minutes) Ask: “Why is ‘cat’ friends with ‘milk’?” (They go together!) “How do word friends help us talk?” (They make stories fun!) Sticker Time: Give everyone a smiley sticker for participating! IV. EVALUATION Observe: Did the student share a word friend? Did they try to explain the connection (even with gestures)? Symbols: = Shared 3+ words and explained. = Shared 1-2 words. = Tried their best! V. HOMEWORK 1. Draw & Tell: Draw a cat or sun and tell your family 2-word friends. 2. Bring a Prop: Bring a small toy or picture of their favorite word friend for show-and-tell next class. 75 TEACHER TIPS Use actions (e.g., meowing for “cat”) to help explain connections. Keep it silly! If a student says “cat → spaceship,” ask, “Why?” and laugh together. Sing a goodbye song: “Bye-bye, word friends, see you soon!”','assets/images/level1/lesson11/page-081.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson11/page-081.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson11/page-082.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson11/page-083.jpg\"]','73 LESSON PLAN 11: ORAL LANGUAGE – WORD FRIENDS (WORD ASSOCIATION) I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the lesson, the pupils are expected to: 1. Think of words that go together (e.g., “cat” → “milk,” “paws,” “meow”). 2. Find 3 ways words connect: same meaning, opposites, or things that go together. 3. Share their word friends and explain why they connect. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Word Friends (How Words Connect) Materials: Big pictures (cat, cake, park, sun). Toy props (toy cat, ball, small umbrella). Smiley face stickers. Simple chart with examples: Same meaning: “happy” → “glad.” Opposites: “big” → “small.” Things that go together: “bread” → “butter.” III. PROCEDURE 1. Warm-Up: “Word Catch!” (5 minutes) Game: Toss a soft ball. Teacher says a word (e.g., “red”). Student catches the ball and says a word friend (e.g., “apple”). Class claps if it makes sense! 2. Motivation: “Guess My Word Friend!” (5 minutes) Hold up a toy cat. Ask: “What words do you think of? Meow? Milk? Paws?” Say: “Today, we’ll play with word friends—words that love to go together!” 74 3. Activity 1: “Word Friends on the Board” (10 minutes) Step 1: Stick a picture of a cat on the board. Ask: “What words