(224,12,NULL,'learn',8,'Teacher-guided lesson checklist','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','LESSON PLAN 12: ORAL LANGUAGE – FUN WITH TONGUE TWISTERS I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the lesson, the pupils are expected to: 1. Say simple tongue twisters clearly, focusing on sounds like /p/, /b/, and /s/. 2. Practice saying tongue twisters slowly, then faster. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Tongue Twisters for Clear Speaking References: Grade 1 Phonics Activities Materials: Big posters with 3 tongue twisters (with pictures): 1. “Peter Piper picked pickled peppers.” (Picture: Peter and peppers) 2. “Betty Botter bought butter.” (Picture: Betty and butter) 3. “Six snails slide slowly.” (Picture: snails sliding) Sound cards with pictures (e.g., popcorn for /p/, ball for /b/, snake for /s/). “Tongue Twister Stars/Badge” stickers. Thumbs-up chart for group activities. 76 III. PROCEDURE 1. Preparatory Activities A. Warm-Up (5 minutes): Play “Copy the Sound!”: Teacher says: “/p/ like popping popcorn!” (Students mimic popping and say /p/). Repeat with /b/ (bounce like a ball) and /s/ (slither like a snake). B. Motivation (3 minutes): Say: “Let’s play with silly sentences that twist our tongues! Who can say ‘Peter Piper’ fast without mixing up words?” 2. Developmental Activities Introduce Tongue Twisters: Show the poster and read “Peter Piper” slowly. Clap for each word. Point to the /p/ sound card every time you say “p.” Say it faster like a robot! Group Practice: Repeat with “Betty Botter” (/b/ sound) and “Six snails” (/s/ sound). Students take turns saying one line in pairs. 3. Analysis/Discussion Ask: “Why is ‘Peter Piper’ tricky to say?” (They’ll say: “Too many /p/ sounds!”). “How do tongue twisters help us?” (Answer: “They make our mouths strong for clear talking!”). 4. Guided Practice Tongue Twister Game: In small groups, students pick a tongue twister. 77 Say it slowly, then fast. Friends give a thumbs up if they hear the sounds clearly! Add fun: Hop or clap while saying it! IV. EVALUATION Use a simple checklist: Give “Tongue Twister Star” stickers to everyone who tries! Criteria (Great!) (Good!) (Keep Trying!) Says sounds clearly Tries slow and fast Participates with a smile V. AGREEMENT For homework: 1. Practice one tongue twister with your family. 2. Draw a picture of your favorite tongue twister (e.g., Peter Piper or snails). NOTE FOR TEACHER: Keep it playful! Praise effort over perfection. Use lots of movements (clapping, hopping) to make sounds memorable','assets/images/level1/lesson12/page-083.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson12/page-083.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson12/page-084.jpg\", \"assets/images/level1/lesson12/page-085.jpg\"]','LESSON PLAN 12: ORAL LANGUAGE – FUN WITH TONGUE TWISTERS I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the lesson, the pupils are expected to: 1. Say simple tongue twisters clearly, focusing on sounds like /p/, /b/, and /s/. 2. Practice saying tongue twisters slowly, then faster. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Tongue Twisters for Clear Speaking References: Grade 1 Phonics Activities Materials: Big posters with 3 tongue twisters (with pictures): 1. “Peter Piper picked pickled peppers.” (Picture: Peter and peppers) 2. “Betty Botter bought butter.” (Picture: Betty and butter) 3. “Six snails slide slowly.” (Picture: snails sliding) Sound cards with pictures (e.g., popcorn for /p/, ball for /b/, snake for /s/). “Tongue Twister Stars/Badge” stickers. Thumbs-up chart for group activities. 76 III. PROCEDURE 1. Preparatory Activities A. Warm-Up (5 minutes): Play “Copy the Sound!”: Teacher says: “/p/ like popping popcorn!” (Students mimic popping and say /p/). Repeat with /b/ (bounce like a ball) and /s/ (slither like a snake). B. Motivation (3 minutes): Say: “Let’s play with silly sentences that twist our tongues! Who can say ‘Peter Piper’ fast without mixing up words?” 2. Developmental Activities Introduce Tongue Twisters: Show the poster and read “Peter Piper” slowly. Clap for each word. Point to the /p/ sound card every time you say “p.” Say it faster like a robot! Group Practice: Repeat with “Betty Botter” (/b/ sound) and “Six snails” (/s/ sound). Students take turns saying one line in pairs. 3. Analysis/Discussion Ask: “Why is ‘Peter Piper’ tricky to say?” (They’ll say: “Too many /p/ sounds!”). “How do tongue twisters help us?” (Answer: “They make our mouths strong for clear talking!”). 4. Guided Practice Tongue Twister Game: In small groups, students pick a tongue twister. 77 Say it slowly, then fast. Friends give a thumbs up if they hear the sounds clearly! Add fun: Hop or clap while saying it! IV. EVALUATION Use a simple checklist: Give “Tongue Twister Star” stickers to everyone who tries! Criteria (Great!) (Good!) (Keep Trying!) Says sounds clearly Tries slow and fast Participates with a smile V. AGREEMENT For homework: 1. Practice one tongue twister with your family. 2. Draw a picture of your favorite tongue twister (e.g., Peter Piper or snails). NOTE FOR TEACHER: Keep it playful! Praise effort over perfection. Use lots of movements (clapping, hopping) to make sounds memorable',NULL,0,83,'LESSON PLAN 12: ORAL LANGUAGE – FUN WITH TONGUE TWISTERS I. OBJECTIVE At the end of the lesson, the pupils are expected to: 1. Say simple tongue twisters clearly, focusing on sounds like /p/, /b/, and /s/. 2. Practice saying tongue twisters slowly, then faster. II. SUBJECT MATTER Topic: Tongue Twisters for Clear Speaking References: Grade 1 Phonics Activities Materials: Big posters with 3 tongue twisters (with pictures): 1. “Peter Piper picked pickled peppers.” (Picture: Peter and peppers) 2. “Betty Botter bought butter.” (Picture: Betty and butter) 3. “Six snails slide slowly.” (Picture: snails sliding) Sound cards with pictures (e.g., popcorn for /p/, ball for /b/, snake for /s/). “Tongue Twister Stars/Badge” stickers. Thumbs-up chart for group activities. 76 III. PROCEDURE 1. Preparatory Activities A. Warm-Up (5 minutes): Play “Copy the Sound!”: Teacher says: “/p/ like popping popcorn!” (Students mimic popping and say /p/). Repeat with /b/ (bounce like a ball) and /s/ (slither like a snake). B. Motivation (3 minutes): Say: “Let’s play with silly sentences that twist our tongues! Who can say ‘Peter Piper’ fast without mixing up words?” 2. Developmental Activities Introduce Tongue Twisters: Show the poster and read “Peter Piper” slowly. Clap for each word. Point to the /p/ sound card every time you say “p.” Say it faster like a robot! Group Practice: Repeat with “Betty Botter” (/b/ sound) and “Six snails” (/s/ sound). Students take turns saying one line in pairs. 3. Analysis/Discussion Ask: “Why is ‘Peter Piper’ tricky to say?” (They’ll say: “Too many /p/ sounds!”). “How do tongue twisters help us?” (Answer: “They make our mouths strong for clear talking!”). 4. Guided Practice Tongue Twister Game: In small groups, students pick a tongue twister. 77 Say it slowly, then fast. Friends give a thumbs up if they hear the sounds clearly! Add fun: Hop or clap while saying it! IV. EVALUATION Use a simple checklist: Give “Tongue Twister Star” stickers to everyone who tries! Criteria (Great!) (Good!) (Keep Trying!) Says sounds clearly Tries slow and fast Participates with a smile V. AGREEMENT For homework: 1. Practice one tongue twister with your family. 2. Draw a picture of your favorite tongue twister (e.g., Peter Piper or snails). NOTE FOR TEACHER: Keep it playful! Praise effort over perfection. Use lots of movements (clapping, hopping) to make sounds memorable',0,1,'none'),
(225,12,NULL,'learn',9,'Additional intervention materials','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next when you are ready.','35 Additional Intervention Material TONGUE TWISTER 1. Sheena leads, Sheila needs. 2. How can a clam cram in a clean cream can? 3. Top chopstick shops stock top chopsticks. 4. Selfish shellfish. 5. I saw a kitten eating chicken in the kitchen. 6. Near an ear, a nearer ear, a nearly eerie ear. 7. Roberta ran rings around the Roman ruins. 8. He threw three free throws. 9. A happy hippo hopped and hiccupped. 10. Toy boat. Toy boat. Toy boat. 11. Smelly shoes and socks shock sisters. 12. Four fine fresh fish for you. 13. Red lorry, yellow lorry (x3) 14. Freshly-fried flying fish. (x3)','assets/images/level1/lesson12/page-042.jpg','[\"assets/images/level1/lesson12/page-042.jpg\"]','35 Additional Intervention Material TONGUE TWISTER 1. Sheena leads, Sheila needs. 2. How can a clam cram in a clean cream can? 3. Top chopstick shops stock top chopsticks. 4. Selfish shellfish. 5. I saw a kitten eating chicken in the kitchen. 6. Near an ear, a nearer ear, a nearly eerie ear. 7. Roberta ran rings around the Roman ruins. 8. He threw three free throws. 9. A happy hippo hopped and hiccupped. 10. Toy boat. Toy boat. Toy boat. 11. Smelly shoes and socks shock sisters. 12. Four fine fresh fish for you. 13. Red lorry, yellow lorry (x3) 14. Freshly-fried flying fish. (x3)',NULL,0,42,'35 Additional Intervention Material TONGUE TWISTER 1. Sheena leads, Sheila needs. 2. How can a clam cram in a clean cream can? 3. Top chopstick shops stock top chopsticks. 4. Selfish shellfish. 5. I saw a kitten eating chicken in the kitchen. 6. Near an ear, a nearer ear, a nearly eerie ear. 7. Roberta ran rings around the Roman ruins. 8. He threw three free throws. 9. A happy hippo hopped and hiccupped. 10. Toy boat. Toy boat. Toy boat. 11. Smelly shoes and socks shock sisters. 12. Four fine fresh fish for you. 13. Red lorry, yellow lorry (x3) 14. Freshly-fried flying fish. (x3)',0,1,'none'),
(226,13,25,'pre',1,'Let’s get ready','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next.','Try each question in order. It is okay to be unsure. Your answers will be saved for your teacher. This online activity is untimed; the original teacher-led oral assessment is separate.',NULL,'[]','Try each question in order. It is okay to be unsure. Your answers will be saved for your teacher. This online activity is untimed; the original teacher-led oral assessment is separate.',NULL,0,6,'___\n      Phoneme              Pretest\n        Isolation\n  A. Directions: Look at the picture. Listen to your teacher\n     as she reads the word. Write the letter for the missing\n     beginning sound.\n\n\n                         1. ____ o g\n\n                         2. ____ n t\n\n\n                         3. ____ a n\n\n\n                         4. ____ e d\n\n\n\n                         5. ____ a n',0,1,'none'),
