e future That we\'re building Here today You\'ll show the Whole world They will be amazed I am You are He is third person She is It is We are awesome You are They are subject pronouns With the verb to be',20,18,'14 \n \n \n \n \n \nhttps://www.mindomo.com/image/e8042fb6029f4797bce2cc426727c24d/mind-map-Verb%20to%20be%20-%20Mind%20Map.png \n \nIt\'s easy as \nOne two three \nJust practice \nEveryday \nThis is the future \nThat we\'re building \nHere today \nYou\'ll show the \nWhole world \nThey will be amazed \nYeah \nI am \nYou are \nHe is third person \nShe is \nIt is \nWe are awesome \nYou are \nThey are \nsubject pronouns \nWith the verb to be \nWhen I say subject \nYou say pronoun \nSubject (pronoun) \nSubject (pronoun) \nWhen I say verb to \nYou say be \nVerb to (Be) \nVerb to (Be) \nThis is the future \nThat we\'re building \nHere today \nYou\'ll show the \nWhole world \nThey will be amazed \nI am \nYou are \nHe is third person \nShe is \nIt is \nWe are awesome \nYou are \nThey are \nsubject pronouns \nWith the verb to be \nTypes of Mis-\ncues \nMispronun-\nciation \nOmis-\nsion \nSubsti-\ntution \nInser-\ntion \nRepe-\ntition \nTranspo-\nsition \nRever-\nsal \nTotal \nNumber of \nMiscues \nNumber of Words in the Passage \n123 \nOral Reading Score = 123 ________ / 123 X 100 \nReading Level (Ind-98-100, Ins-90-96, Frus– 89 below) \nReading Speed = Words Read/Reading time in seconds X 60 \nwords per Minute',1,1,'answer'),
(605,119,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: Daisies','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: Daisies\nAuthor: Frank Dempster Sherman\nWords: 80\n\nAt evening when I go to bed\nI see the stars shine overhead;\nThey are the little daisies white\nThat dot the meadow of the Night.\nAnd often while I\'m dreaming so,\nAcross the sky the Moon will go;\nIt is a lady, sweet and fair,\nWho comes to gather daisies there.\nFor, when at morning I arise,\nThere\'s not a star left in the skies;\nShe picked them all and dropped them down\nInto the meadows of the town.','assets/images/level4/g3-p19-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g3-p19-1.webp\"]','Daisies. At evening when I go to bed I see the stars shine overhead; They are the little daisies white That dot the meadow of the Night. And often while I\'m dreaming so, Across the sky the Moon will go; It is a lady, sweet and fair, Who comes to gather daisies there. For, when at morning I arise, There\'s not a star left in the skies; She picked them all and dropped them down Into the meadows of the town.','At evening when I go to bed I see the stars shine overhead; They are the little daisies white That dot the meadow of the Night. And often while I\'m dreaming so, Across the sky the Moon will go; It is a lady, sweet and fair, Who comes to gather daisies there. For, when at morning I arise, There\'s not a star left in the skies; She picked them all and dropped them down Into the meadows of the town.',20,19,'15 \n \n \nhttps://www.nurse.com/blog/wp-content/ \nuploads/2019/02/1200x628-Cover-.jpg \n \nDaisies \nby Frank Dempster Sherman \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nAt evening when I go to bed \nI see the stars shine overhead; \nThey are the little daisies white \nThat dot the meadow of the Night. \n \nAnd often while I\'m dreaming so, \nAcross the sky the Moon will go; \nIt is a lady, sweet and fair, \nWho comes to gather daisies there. \nFor, when at morning I arise, \nThere\'s not a star left in the skies; \nShe picked them all and dropped them down  \nInto the meadows of the town. \nhttps://discoverpoetry.com/poems/frank-dempster-sherman/daisies/ \n \n \n \n \nTypes of Mis-\ncues \nMispronun-\nciation \nOmis-\nsion \nSubsti-\ntution \nInser-\ntion \nRepe-\ntition \nTranspo-\nsition \nRever-\nsal \nTotal \nNumber of \nMiscues \nNumber of Words in the Passage \n80 \nOral Reading Score = 80 ________ / 80 X 100 \nReading Level (Ind-98-100, Ins-90-96, Frus– 89 below) \nReading Speed = Words Read/Reading time in seconds X 60 \nwords per Minute',1,1,'answer'),
(606,120,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: A Robot Dog','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: A Robot Dog\nWords: 403\n\nwalked and walked. After a little bit, they walked past a big tree. Poof! Rocky was a robot dog. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog. “What is going on?” asked Andrew. “Who turned my dog into a robot dog?” Andrew looked all around him. He looked up the street. He looked down the street. He looked behind the tree. He looked up into the tree. No one was there. He stopped and stood still. “Hee, hee, hee,” he heard. It came from the tree. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog at the tree. Andrew looked at the tree. He could not see anything different about this tree. He could not think of what to do. “Tree, are you a real tree?” asked Andrew. The tree did not say anything. Rocky the robot dog came over and smelled the tree. “Woof, woof,” he barked at it again. Andrew was mad. He did not like it that Rocky was a robot dog. Andrew walked over to the tree and tickled it. “Stop that,” said the tree. “Why did you tickle me?” “I think you turned my dog into a robot dog,” said Andrew. “Maybe I did, and maybe I didn’t,” said the tree who was holding his branches in front of himself so Andrew could not tickle him again. “It is kind of funny to see a dog become a robot.” Andrew got real mad this time and went up to the tree. He pushed away the tree’s branches and tickled him some more. “Oh, stop, stop,” said the tree. “I don’t want to be tickled anymore.” “I still think you turned Rocky into a robot dog,” said Andrew who was about to tickle the tree again. “Okay, okay,” said the tree. “I turned your dog into a robot dog. Just don’t tickle me anymore.” “Well, I don’t think it is funny,” said Andrew. “Now turn Rocky the robot dog back into Rocky the real dog.” “Okay,” said the tree. Poof! Rocky was Rocky the real dog again. “Now don’t do that anymore,” said Andrew. “Someone might get mad and tickle you again. You would not like that.” “Okay, I won’t turn dogs into robot dogs anymore,” said the tree. Andrew and Rocky walked home. The tree never turned any more dogs into robots, but every now and then, Andrew would see a robot butterfly go flying past his home.','assets/images/level4/g3-p20-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g3-p20-1.webp\", \"assets/images/level4/g3-p21-1.webp\"]','A Robot Dog. walked and walked. After a little bit, they walked past a big tree. Poof! Rocky was a robot dog. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog. “What is going on?” asked Andrew. “Who turned my dog into a robot dog?” Andrew looked all around him. He looked up the street. He looked down the street. He looked behind the tree. He looked up into the tree. No one was there. He stopped and stood still. “Hee, hee, hee,” he heard. It came from the tree. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog at the tree. Andrew looked at the tree. He could not see anything different about this tree. He could not think of what to do. “Tree, are you a real tree?” asked Andrew. The tree did not say anything. Rocky the robot dog came over and smelled the tree. “Woof, woof,” he barked at it again. Andrew was mad. He did not like it that Rocky was a robot dog. Andrew walked over to the tree and tickled it. “Stop that,” said the tree. “Why did you tickle me?” “I think you turned my dog into a robot dog,” said Andrew. “Maybe I did, and maybe I didn’t,” said the tree who was holding his branches in front of himself so Andrew could not tickle him again. “It is kind of funny to see a dog become a robot.” Andrew got real mad this time and went up to the tree. He pushed away the tree’s branches and tickled him some more. “Oh, stop, stop,” said the tree. “I don’t want to be tickled anymore.” “I still think you turned Rocky into a robot dog,” said Andrew who was about to tickle the tree again. “Okay, okay,” said the tree. “I turned your dog into a robot dog. Just don’t tickle me anymore.” “Well, I don’t think it is funny,” said Andrew. “Now turn Rocky the robot dog back into Rocky the real dog.” “Okay,” said the tree. Poof! Rocky was Rocky the real dog again. “Now don’t do that anymore,” said Andrew. “Someone might get mad and tickle you again. You would not like that.” “Okay, I won’t turn dogs into robot dogs anymore,” said the tree. Andrew and Rocky walked home. The tree never turned any more dogs into robots, but every now and then, Andrew would see a robot butterfly go flying past his home.','walked and walked. After a little bit, they walked past a big tree. Poof! Rocky was a robot dog. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog. “What is going on?” asked Andrew. “Who turned my dog into a robot dog?” Andrew looked all around him. He looked up the street. He looked down the street. He looked behind the tree. He looked up into the tree. No one was there. He stopped and stood still. “Hee, hee, hee,” he heard. It came from the tree. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog at the tree. Andrew looked at the tree. He could not see anything different about this tree. He could not think of what to do. “Tree, are you a real tree?” asked Andrew. The tree did not say anything. Rocky the robot dog came over and smelled the tree. “Woof, woof,” he barked at it again. Andrew was mad. He did not like it that Rocky was a robot dog. Andrew walked over to the tree and tickled it. “Stop that,” said the tree. “Why did you tickle me?” “I think you turned my dog into a robot dog,” said Andrew. “Maybe I did, and maybe I didn’t,” said the tree who was holding his branches in front of himself so Andrew could not tickle him again. “It is kind of funny to see a dog become a robot.” Andrew got real mad this time and went up to the tree. He pushed away the tree’s branches and tickled him some more. “Oh, stop, stop,” said the tree. “I don’t want to be tickled anymore.” “I still think you turned Rocky into a robot dog,” said Andrew who was about to tickle the tree again. “Okay, okay,” said the tree. “I turned your dog into a robot dog. Just don’t tickle me anymore.” “Well, I don’t think it is funny,” said Andrew. “Now turn Rocky the robot dog back into Rocky the real dog.” “Okay,” said the tree. Poof! Rocky was Rocky the real dog again. “Now don’t do that anymore,” said Andrew. “Someone might get mad and tickle you again. You would not like that.” “Okay, I won’t turn dogs into robot dogs anymore,” said the tree. Andrew and Rocky walked home. The tree never turned any more dogs into robots, but every now and then, Andrew would see a robot butterfly go flying past his home.',20,20,'16 \n \n \n \nA Robot Dog \n \nwalked and walked. After a little bit, they walked past a big tree. \nPoof! Rocky was a robot dog. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the ro-\nbot dog. “What is going on?” asked Andrew. “Who turned my dog \ninto a robot dog?” Andrew looked all around him. He looked up \nthe street. He looked down the street. He looked behind the tree. \nHe looked up into the tree. No one was there. He stopped and \nstood still. “Hee, hee, hee,” he heard. It came from the tree. \n“Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog at the tree. Andrew \nlooked at the tree. He could not see anything different about this \ntree. He could not think of what to do. \n“Tree, are you a real tree?” asked Andrew. The tree did not \nsay anything. Rocky the robot dog came over and smelled the tree. \n“Woof, woof,” he barked at it again. Andrew was mad. He did not \nlike it that Rocky was a robot dog. \n \n \nhttps://m.media-amazon.com/images/ \nI/51o6-E2DADS._AC_SY580_.jpg',1,1,'answer'),
(607,121,61,'post',1,'Let’s get ready','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next.','Your teacher will listen as you read a story aloud. Read at your own pace, the way you talk. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','assets/images/level4/g3-p23-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g3-p23-1.webp\"]','Your teacher will listen as you read a story aloud. Read at your own pace, the way you talk. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.',NULL,5,23,'20 \n \nhttps://i.ytimg.com/vi/mTz7rMkc4-c/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEmCOADEOgC8quKqQMa8AEB-\nAHSBoAC4AOKAgwIABABGH8gHCglMA8=&rs=AOn4CLAQLi0VjlZ27DcsC2ous9aCKrRaoQ \nPost test Passage \nThe Ant and the Fly \nAesop Fable \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nOnce, an Ant and a Fly were fighting over who was \nmore important. The Fly said, “Ant! What do you think \nyou are? How can you even think about comparing \nyourself to me? Look at me! You have to work hard but \nI pass my time with the rich and the learned. I fly into the \ntemples and taste the offerings. I can sit on the King’s \ncrown and kiss the Queen’s forehead. I do not work, yet \nhave the best life!” The Ant replied, “Don’t be so proud! \nYou are always hated when you enter the temples. You \nare driven away as soon as you sit on the King’s crown \nor the Queen’s forehead. You do not have anything left \nfor the difficult times as you are lazy. In winters, you \nfeed yourself on a pile of cow dung. Look at me! I work \nhard and gather a store of grain for the winter. Later on, \nwhen you shiver in the cold, I am safe and at peace in \nmy cozy home. I am prepared to live in any season of \nthe year.” \nLook at your own faults, before finding faults \nin others. \nhttps://shortstoriesshort.com/story/the-ant-and-the-fly/ \n \nTypes of Mis-\ncues \nMispronun-\nciation \nOmis-\nsion \nSubsti-\ntution \nInser-\ntion \nRepe-\ntition \nTranspo-\nsition \nRever-\nsal \nTotal \nNumber of \nMiscues \nNumber of Words in the Passage \n199 \nOral Reading Score = 199 ________ / 199 ',0,1,'none'),
(608,121,61,'post',2,'Read aloud: The Ant and The Fly','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: The Ant and The Fly\nAuthor: Aesop Fable\nWords: 199\n\nOnce, an Ant and a Fly were fighting over who was more important. The Fly said, “Ant! What do you think you are? How can you even think about comparing yourself to me? Look at me! You have to work hard but I pass my time with the rich and the learned. I fly into the temples and taste the offerings. I can sit on the King’s crown and kiss the Queen’s forehead. I do not work, yet have the best life!” The Ant replied, “Don’t be so proud! You are always hated when you enter the temples. You are driven away as soon as you sit on the King’s crown or the Queen’s forehead. You do not have anything left for the difficult times as you are lazy. In winters, you feed yourself on a pile of cow dung. Look at me! I work hard and gather a store of grain for the winter. Later on, when you shiver in the cold, I am safe and at peace in my cozy home. I am prepared to live in any season of the year.”\n\nLook at your own faults, before finding faults in others.','assets/images/level4/g3-p23-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g3-p23-1.webp\"]','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Once, an Ant and a Fly were fighting over who was more important. The Fly said, “Ant! What do you think you are? How can you even think about comparing yourself to me? Look at me! You have to work hard but I pass my time with the rich and the learned. I fly into the temples and taste the offerings. I can sit on the King’s crown and kiss the Queen’s forehead. I do not work, yet have the best life!” The Ant replied, “Don’t be so proud! You are always hated when you enter the temples. You are driven away as soon as you sit on the King’s crown or the Queen’s forehead. You do 