(639,147,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The House on the Hill','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: The House on the Hill\nAuthor: Edwin Arlington Robinson\nWords: 107\n\nThey are all gone away,\nThe House is shut and still,\nThere is nothing more to say.\nThrough broken walls and gray\nThe winds blow bleak and shrill:\nThey are all gone away.\nNor is there one to-day, To speak them good or ill:\nThere is nothing more to say.\nWhy is it then we stray, Around the sunken sill?\nThey are all gone away,\nAnd our poor fancy-play, For them is wasted skill:\nThere is nothing more to say.\nThere is ruin and decay, In the House on the Hill:\nThey are all gone away, There is nothing more to say.','assets/images/level4/g6-p13-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p13-1.webp\"]','The House on the Hill. They are all gone away, The House is shut and still, There is nothing more to say. Through broken walls and gray The winds blow bleak and shrill: They are all gone away. Nor is there one to-day, To speak them good or ill: There is nothing more to say. Why is it then we stray, Around the sunken sill? They are all gone away, And our poor fancy-play, For them is wasted skill: There is nothing more to say. There is ruin and decay, In the House on the Hill: They are all gone away, There is nothing more to say.','They are all gone away, The House is shut and still, There is nothing more to say. Through broken walls and gray The winds blow bleak and shrill: They are all gone away. Nor is there one to-day, To speak them good or ill: There is nothing more to say. Why is it then we stray, Around the sunken sill? They are all gone away, And our poor fancy-play, For them is wasted skill: There is nothing more to say. There is ruin and decay, In the House on the Hill: They are all gone away, There is nothing more to say.',20,13,'9 \n \n \nThe House on the Hill \n \nThey are all gone away, \nThe House is shut and still, \nThere is nothing more to say. \n \nThrough broken walls and gray \nThe winds blow bleak and shrill: \nThey are all gone away. \n \nNor is there one to-day, To speak them good or ill: \nThere is nothing more to say. \n \nWhy is it then we stray, Around the sunken sill? \nThey are all gone away, \n \nAnd our poor fancy-play, For them is wasted skill: \nThere is nothing more to say. \n \nThere is ruin and decay, In the House on the Hill: \nThey are all gone away, There is nothing more to say. \n \n \nTypes of Mis- \ncues \nMispronun- \nciation \nOmis- \nsion \nSubsti- \ntution \nInser- \ntion \nRepe- \ntition \nTranspo- \nsition \nRever- \nsal \nTotal \nNumber of \nMiscues \nNumber of Words in the Passage \n107 \nOral Reading Score = 107 _______ / 107 X 100 \nReading Level (Ind-98-100, Ins-90-96, Frus– 89 below) \nReading Speed = Words Read/Reading time in seconds X 60 \nwords per Minute',1,1,'answer'),
(640,148,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Golden Egg','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: The Golden Egg\nWords: 218\n\nOnce upon a time, there lived a cloth merchant in a village with his wife and two children. They were indeed quite well-off. They had a beautiful hen which laid an egg every day. It was not an ordinary egg, rather, a golden egg. But the man was not satisfied with what he used to get daily. He was a get rich-trice kind of a person.\n\nThe man wanted to get all the golden eggs from his hen at one single go. So, one day he thought hard and at last clicked upon a plan. He decided to kill the hen and get all the eggs together.\n\nSo, the next day when the hen laid a golden egg, the man caught hold of it, took a sharp knife, chopped off its neck and cut its body open. There was nothing but blood all around & no trace of any egg at all. He was highly grieved because now he would not get even one single egg.\n\nHis life was going on smoothly with one egg a day but now, he himself made his life miserable. The outcome of his greed was that he started becoming poorer & poorer day by day and ultimately became a pauper. How jinxed and how much foolish he was.','assets/images/level4/g6-p14-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p14-1.webp\"]','The Golden Egg. Once upon a time, there lived a cloth merchant in a village with his wife and two children. They were indeed quite well-off. They had a beautiful hen which laid an egg every day. It was not an ordinary egg, rather, a golden egg. But the man was not satisfied with what he used to get daily. He was a get rich-trice kind of a person. The man wanted to get all the golden eggs from his hen at one single go. So, one day he thought hard and at last clicked upon a plan. He decided to kill the hen and get all the eggs together. So, the next day when the hen laid a golden egg, the man caught hold of it, took a sharp knife, chopped off its neck and cut its body open. There was nothing but blood all around & no trace of any egg at all. He was highly grieved because now he would not get even one single egg. His life was going on smoothly with one egg a day but now, he himself made his life miserable. The outcome of his greed was that he started becoming poorer & poorer day by day and ultimately became a pauper. How jinxed and how much foolish he was.','Once upon a time, there lived a cloth merchant in a village with his wife and two children. They were indeed quite well-off. They had a beautiful hen which laid an egg every day. It was not an ordinary egg, rather, a golden egg. But the man was not satisfied with what he used to get daily. He was a get rich-trice kind of a person. The man wanted to get all the golden eggs from his hen at one single go. So, one day he thought hard and at last clicked upon a plan. He decided to kill the hen and get all the eggs together. So, the next day when the hen laid a golden egg, the man caught hold of it, took a sharp knife, chopped off its neck and cut its body open. There was nothing but blood all around & no trace of any egg at all. He was highly grieved because now he would not get even one single egg. His life was going on smoothly with one egg a day but now, he himself made his life miserable. The outcome of his greed was that he started becoming poorer & poorer day by day and ultimately became a pauper. How jinxed and how much foolish he was.',20,14,'10 \n \n \nThe Golden Egg \n \n \nOnce upon a time, there lived a cloth merchant in a village with \nhis wife and two children. They were indeed quite well-off. They had \na beautiful hen which laid an egg every day. It was not an ordinary \negg, rather, a golden egg. But the man was not satisfied with what \nhe used to get daily. He was a get rich-trice kind of a person. \nThe man wanted to get all the golden eggs from his hen at one \nsingle go. So, one day he thought hard and at last clicked upon a \nplan. He decided to kill the hen and get all the eggs together. \nSo, the next day when the hen laid a golden egg, the man \ncaught hold of it, took a sharp knife, chopped off its neck and cut its \nbody open. There was nothing but blood all around & no trace of \nany egg at all. He was highly grieved because now he would not get \neven one single egg. \nHis life was going on smoothly with one egg a day but now, he \nhimself made his life miserable. The outcome of his greed was that \nhe started becoming poorer & poorer day by day and ultimately be- \ncame a pauper. How jinxed and how much foolish he was. \n \nTypes of Mis- \ncues \nMispronun- \nciation \nOmis- \nsion \nSubsti- \ntution \nInser- \ntion \nRepe- \ntition \nTranspo- \nsition \nRever- \nsal \nTotal \nNumber of \nMiscues \nNumber of Words in the Passage \n218 \nOral Reading Score = 218 _______ / 218 X 100 \nReading Level (Ind-98-100, Ins-90-96, Frus– 89 below) \nReading Speed = Words Read/Reading time in seconds X 60 \nwords per Minute',1,1,'answer'),
(641,149,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: To the River','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: To the River\nAuthor: Edgar Allan Poe - 1809-1849\nWords: 78\n\nFair river! in thy bright, clear flow\nOf crystal, wandering water,\nThou art an emblem of the glow\nOf beauty—the unhidden heart—\nThe playful maziness of art\nIn old Alberto’s daughter;\nBut when within thy wave she looks—\nWhich glistens then, and trembles—\nWhy, then, the prettiest of brooks\nHer worshipper resembles;\nFor in my heart, as in thy stream,\nHer image deeply lies—\nHis heart which trembles at the beam\nOf her soul-searching eyes.','assets/images/level4/g6-p15-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p15-1.webp\"]','To the River. Fair river! in thy bright, clear flow Of crystal, wandering water, Thou art an emblem of the glow Of beauty—the unhidden heart— The playful maziness of art In old Alberto’s daughter; But when within thy wave she looks— Which glistens then, and trembles— Why, then, the prettiest of brooks Her worshipper resembles; For in my heart, as in thy stream, Her image deeply lies— His heart which trembles at the beam Of her soul-searching eyes.','Fair river! in thy bright, clear flow Of crystal, wandering water, Thou art an emblem of the glow Of beauty—the unhidden heart— The playful maziness of art In old Alberto’s daughter; But when within thy wave she looks— Which glistens then, and trembles— Why, then, the prettiest of brooks Her worshipper resembles; For in my heart, as in thy stream, Her image deeply lies— His heart which trembles at the beam Of her soul-searching eyes.',20,15,'11 \n \n \nTo the River \nEdgar Allan Poe - 1809-1849 \nFair river! in thy bright, clear flow \nOf crystal, wandering water, \nThou art an emblem of the glow \nOf beauty—the unhidden heart— \nThe playful maziness of art \nIn old Alberto’s daughter; \n \nBut when within thy wave she looks— \nWhich glistens then, and trembles— \nWhy, then, the prettiest of brooks \nHer worshipper resembles; \nFor in my heart, as in thy stream, \nHer image deeply lies— \nHis heart which trembles at the beam \nOf her soul-searching eyes. \n \nTypes of Mis- \ncues \nMispronun- \nciation \nOmis- \nsion \nSubsti- \ntution \nInser- \ntion \nRepe- \ntition \nTranspo- \nsition \nRever- \nsal \nTotal \nNumber of \nMiscues \nNumber of Words in the Passage \n78 \nOral Reading Score = 78 ________ / 78 X 100 \nReading Level (Ind-98-100, Ins-90-96, Frus– 89 below) \nReading Speed = Words Read/Reading time in seconds X 60 \nwords per Minute',1,1,'answer'),
(642,150,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: Be Glad Your Nose is on Your Face','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: Be Glad Your Nose is on Your Face\nAuthor: Jack Prelutsky\nWords: 140\n\nBe glad your nose is on your face, not pasted on some other place,\nfor if it were where it is not, you might dislike your nose a lot.\nImagine if your precious nose, were sandwiched in between your toes,\nthat clearly would not be a treat, for you\'d be forced to smell your feet.\nYour nose would be a source of dread were it attached atop your head,\nit soon would drive you to despair, forever tickled by your hair.\nWithin your ear, your nose would be\nan absolute catastrophe,\nfor when you were obliged to sneeze,\nyour brain would rattle from the breeze.\nYour nose, instead, through thick and thin,\nremains between your eyes and chin,\nnot pasted on some other place--\nbe glad your nose is on your face.','assets/images/level4/g6-p16-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p16-1.webp\"]','Be Glad Your Nose is on Your Face. Be glad your nose is on your face, not pasted on some other place, for if it were where it is not, you might dislike your nose a lot. Imagine if your precious nose, were sandwiched in between your toes, that clearly would not be a treat, for you\'d be forced to smell your feet. Your nose would be a source of dread were it attached atop your head, it soon would drive you to despair, forever tickled by your hair. Within your ear, your nose would be an absolute catastrophe, for when you were obliged to sneeze, your brain would rattle from the breeze. Your nose, instead, through thick and thin, remains between your eyes and chin, not pasted on some other place-- be glad your nose is on your face.','Be glad your nose is on your face, not pasted on some other place, for if it were where it is not, you might dislike your nose a lot. Imagine if your precious nose, were sandwiched in between your toes, that clearly would not be a treat, for you\'d be forced to smell your feet. Your nose would be a source of dread were it attached atop your head, it soon would drive you to despair, forever tickled by your hair. Within your ear, your nose would be an absolute catastrophe, for when you were obliged to sneeze, your brain would rattle from the breeze. Your nose, instead, through thick and thin, remains between your eyes and chin, not pasted on some other place-- be glad your nose is on your face.',20,16,'12 \n \n \nBe Glad Your Nose is on Your Face \nBy Jack Prelutsky \n \n \nBe glad your nose is on your face, not pasted on some other place, \nfor if it were where it is not, you might dislike your nose a lot. \n \nImagine if your precious nose, were sandwiched in between your toes, \nthat clearly would not be a treat, for you\'d be forced to smell your feet. \n \nYour nose would be a source of dread were it attached atop your head, \nit soon would drive you to despair, forever tickled by your hair. \n \nWithin your ear, your nose would be \nan absolute catastrophe, \nfor when you were obliged to sneeze, \nyour brain would rattle from the breeze. \nYour nose, instead, through thick and thin, \nremains between your eyes and chin, \nnot pasted on some other place-- \nbe glad your nose is on your face. \n \nTypes of Mis- \ncues \nMispronun- \nciation \nOmis- \nsion \nSubsti- \ntution \nInser- \ntion \nRepe- \ntition \nTranspo- \nsition \nRever- \nsal \nTotal \nNumber of \nMiscues \nNumber of Words in the Passage \n140 \nOral Reading Score = 140 _______ / 140 X 100 \nReading Level (Ind-98-100, Ins-90-96, Frus– 89 below) \nReading Speed = Words Read/Reading time in seconds X 60 \nwords per Minute',1,1,'answer'),
(643,151,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Crocodile and the Monkey','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: The Crocodile and the Monkey\nWords: 435\n\nOnce upon a time, there lived a monkey on a mango tree on the banks of a river. The mangoes of that tree were the sweetest of all. The tree bore fruits year-round. The monkey used to live very happily on the tree eating the tasty fruits and playing on the tree. On a particular day, a crocodile came near that mango tree. He seemed very exhausted. The monkey offered him some delicious mangoes from the tree. The crocodile loved those mangoes and thanked the monkey for the same. From then on, the crocodile used to visit the monkey every day and they became good friends. One day the crocodile thought to take some mangoes for his wife. When his wife ate the mangoes, she demanded something very absurd from the crocodile. She said that these fruits are so tasty, then how delicious will be the heart of that monkey who regularly eats these mangoes! She ordered the crocodile to bring the monkey’s heart for her. The crocodile was shocked at her demand. The monkey was his friend. How could he betray him? He declined to kill the monkey and bring his heart to her. The crocodile’s wife was adamant about getting the monkey’s heart. She told the crocodile that she is not keeping well and the doctor has recommended her to eat a monkey’s heart to recover. She even threatened him that if the crocodile didn’t get the monkey’s heart for her, she would surely die. The crocodile had to give in to her order. With a heavy heart, the crocodile left to get the monkey. He went to the monkey and said, “Friend, my wife loved the mangoes sent by you. She has invited you to our house to thank you.” The monkey agreed and sat on the crocodile’s back so that the crocodile could take him to his house. In the middle of the river, the crocodile began sinking in. The monkey got frightened and he asked the crocodile as to why he was sinking. The crocodile, knowing that now there was no escape for the monkey, replied, “Forgive me, my friend. My wife needs to eat your heart to save her life. That’s why I am taking you with me.” The monkey was outraged. The monkey was smart. He kept calm and told the crocodile that he would be more than happy to save his wife’s life but he left his heart at the mango tree. The monkey told him that they can quickly go and get his heart from the tree. The crocodile accepted it at once and turned back. As soon as they reached the tree, the monkey jumped onto the tree and said, “O foolish crocodile, how can someone take out his heart and keep it somewhere else? You have deceived me as a friend. Now go and never come back. The ashamed crocodile went back to his home.','assets/images/level4/g6-p17-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p17-1.webp\"]','The Crocodile and the Monkey. Once upon a time, there lived a monkey on a mango tree on the banks of a river. The mangoes of that tree were the sweetest of all. The tree bore fruits year-round. The monkey used to live very happily on the tree eating the tasty fruits and playing on the tree. On a particular day, a crocodile came near that mango tree. He seemed very exhausted. The monkey offered him some delicious mangoes from the tree. The crocodile loved those mangoes and thanked the monkey for the same. From then on, the crocodile used to visit the monkey every day and they became good friends. One day the crocodile thought to take some mangoes for his wife. When his wife ate the mangoes, she demanded something very absurd from the crocodile. She said that these fruits are so tasty, then how delicious will be the heart of that monkey who regularly eats these mangoes! She ordered the crocodile to bring the monkey’s heart for her. The crocodile was shocked at her demand. The monkey was his friend. How could he betray him? He declined to kill the monkey and bring his heart to her. The crocodile’s wife was adamant about getting the monkey’s heart. She told the crocodile that she is not keeping well and the doctor has recommended her to eat a monkey’s heart to recover. She even threatened him that if the crocodile didn’t get the monkey’s heart for her, she would surely die. The crocodile had to give in to her order. With a heavy heart, the crocodile left to get the monkey. He went to the monkey and said, “Friend, my wife loved the mangoes sent by you. She has invited you to our house to thank you.” The monkey agreed and sat on the crocodile’s back so that the crocodile could take him to his house. In the middle of the river, the crocodile began sinking in. The monkey got frightened and he asked the crocodile as to why he was sinking. The crocodile, knowing that now there was no escape for the monkey, replied, “Forgive me, my friend. My wife needs to eat your heart to save her life. That’s why I am taking you with me.” The monkey was outraged. The monkey was smart. He kept calm and told the crocodile that he would be more than happy to save his wife’s life but he left his heart at the mango tree. The monkey told him that they can quickly go and get his heart from the tree. The crocodile accepted it at once and turned back. As soon as they reached the tree, the monkey jumped onto the tree and said, “O foolish crocodile, how can someone take out his heart and keep it somewhere else? You have deceived me as a friend. Now go and never come back. The ashamed crocodile went back to his home.','Once upon a time, there lived a monkey on a mango tree on the banks of a river. The mangoes of that tree were the sweetest of all. The tree bore fruits year-round. The monkey used to live very happily on the tree eating the tasty fruits and playing on the tree. On a particular day, a crocodile came near that mango tree. He seemed very exhausted. The monkey offered him some delicious mangoes from the tree. The crocodile loved those mangoes and thanked the monkey for the same. From then on, the crocodile used to visit the monkey every day and they became good friends. One day the crocodile thought to take some mangoes for his wife. When his wife ate the mangoes, she demanded something very absurd from the crocodile. She said that these fruits are so tasty, then how delicious will be the heart of that monkey who regularly eats these mangoes! She ordered the crocodile to bring the monkey’s heart for her. The crocodile was shocked at her demand. The monkey was his friend. How could he betray him? He declined to kill the monkey and bring his heart to her. The crocodile’s wife was adamant about getting the monkey’s heart. She told the crocodile that she is not keeping well and the doctor has recommended her to eat a monkey’s heart to recover. She even threatened him that if the crocodile didn’t get the monkey’s heart for her, she would surely die. The crocodile had to give in to her order. With a heavy heart, the crocodile left to get the monkey. He went to the monkey and said, “Friend, my wife loved the mangoes sent by you. She has invited you to our house to thank you.” The monkey agreed and sat on the crocodile’s back so that the crocodile could take him to his house. In the middle of the river, the crocodile began sinking in. The monkey got frightened and he asked the crocodile as to why he was sinking. The crocodile, knowing that now there was no escape for the monkey, replied, “Forgive me, my friend. My wife needs to eat your heart to save her life. That’s why I am taking you with me.” The monkey was outraged. The monkey was smart. He kept calm and told the crocodile that he would be more than happy to save his wife’s life but he left his heart at the mango tree. The monkey told him that they can quickly go and get his heart from the tree. The crocodile accepted it at once and turned back. As soon as they reached the tree, the monkey jumped onto the tree and said, “O foolish crocodile, how can someone take out his heart and keep it somewhere else? You have deceived me as a friend. Now go and never come back. The ashamed crocodile went back to his home.',20,17,'13 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nOnce upon a time, there lived a monkey on a mango tree on the banks of a river. \nThe mangoes of that tree were the sweetest of all. The tree bore fruits year-round. The \nmonkey used to live very happily on the tree eating the tasty fruits and playing on the \ntree. \nOn a particular day, a crocodile came near that mango tree. He seemed very ex- \nhausted. The monkey offered him some delicious mangoes from the tree. The crocodile \nloved those mangoes and thanked the monkey for the same. From then on, the croco- \ndile used to visit the monkey every day and they became good friends. \nOne day the crocodile thought to take some mangoes for his wife. When his wife \nate the mangoes, she demanded something very absurd from the crocodile. She said \nthat these fruits are so tasty, then how delicious will be the heart of that monkey who \nregularly eats these mangoes! \nShe ordered the crocodile to bring the monkey’s heart for her. The crocodile was \nshocked at her demand. The monkey was his friend. How could he betray him? He de- \nclined to kill the monkey and bring his heart to her. \nThe crocodile’s wife was adamant about getting the monkey’s heart. She told the \ncrocodile that she is not keeping well and the doctor has recommended her to eat a \nmonkey’s heart to recover. She even threatened him that if the crocodile didn’t get the \nmonkey’s heart for her, she would surely die. The crocodile had to give in to her order. \nWith a heavy heart, the crocodile left to g',1,1,'answer'),
(644,152,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: Pygmalion: Henry and Eliza','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: Pygmalion: Henry and Eliza\nWords: 522\n\nGeorge Bernard Shaw’s play Pygmalion was the basis for the famous musical My Fair Lady. First performed in 1913, Pygmalion is the story of Eliza Doolittle, a poor and unsophisticated flower seller on the streets of London, who wants to learn to speak “like a lady.” In this passage from Act I, she has met by chance Professor Henry Higgins, a grumpy professor of phonetics, near a church during a rainstorm. They are amongst a group of people who have gathered under the church’s portico to stay dry. Henry has been listening to the other people as part of his research. Eliza was afraid for a time that he was a policeman trying to arrest her and is just beginning to recover from her fear. Henry is “The Note Taker” below. Eliza is “The Flower Girl,” and “The Gentleman” is Colonel Pickering, who was part of the group. The rain has stopped.\n\nThe Flower Girl: Poor girl! Hard enough for her to live without being worried and chivied.\n\nThe Gentleman: [returning to his former place on the note taker’s left] How do you do it, if I may ask? The Note Taker: Simply phonetics. The science of speech. That’s my profession; also my hobby. Happy is the man who can make a living by his hobby! You can spot an Irishman or a Yorkshireman by his brogue. I can place any man within six miles. I can place him within two miles in London. Sometimes within two streets.\n\nThe Flower Girl: Ought to be ashamed of himself, unmanly coward!\n\nThe Gentleman: But is there a living in that?\n\nThe Note Taker: Oh yes. Quite a fat one. This is an age of upstarts. Men begin in Kentish Town with 80 pounds a year, and end in Park Lane with a hundred thousand. They want to drop Kentish Town; but they give themselves away every time they open their mouths. Now! can teach them— The Flower Girl: Let him mind his own business and leave a poor girl— The Note Taker: [explosively] Woman: cease this detestable boohooing instantly; or else seek the shelter of some other place of worship.\n\nThe Flower Girl: [with feeble defiance] I’ve a right to be here if I like, same as you.\n\nThe Note Taker: A woman who utters such depressing and disgusting sounds has no right to be anywhere—no right to live. Remember that you are a human being with a soul and the divine gift of articulate speech: that your native language is the language of Shakespeare and Milton and The Bible; and don’t sit there crooning like a bilious pigeon.\n\nThe Flower Girl: [quite overwhelmed, and looking up at him in mingled wonder and deprecation without daring to raise her head] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow—oo!\n\nThe Note Taker: [whipping out his book] Heavens! what a sound! [He writes; then holds out the book and reads, reproducing her vowels exactly] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow— ow— oo!\n\nThe Flower Girl: [tickled by the performance, and laughing in spite of herself] Garn!','assets/images/level4/g6-p18-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p18-1.webp\"]','Pygmalion: Henry and Eliza. George Bernard Shaw’s play Pygmalion was the basis for the famous musical My Fair Lady. First performed in 1913, Pygmalion is the story of Eliza Doolittle, a poor and unsophisticated flower seller on the streets of London, who wants to learn to speak “like a lady.” In this passage from Act I, she has met by chance Professor Henry Higgins, a grumpy professor of phonetics, near a church during a rainstorm. They are amongst a group of people who have gathered under the church’s portico to stay dry. Henry has been listening to the other people as part of his research. Eliza was afraid for a time that he was a policeman trying to arrest her and is just beginning to recover from her fear. Henry is “The Note Taker” below. Eliza is “The Flower Girl,” and “The Gentleman” is Colonel Pickering, who was part of the group. The rain has stopped. The Flower Girl: Poor girl! Hard enough for her to live without being worried and chivied. The Gentleman: [returning to his former place on the note taker’s left] How do you do it, if I may ask? The Note Taker: Simply phonetics. The science of speech. That’s my profession; also my hobby. Happy is the man who can make a living by his hobby! You can spot an Irishman or a Yorkshireman by his brogue. I can place any man within six miles. I can place him within two miles in London. Sometimes within two streets. The Flower Girl: Ought to be ashamed of himself, unmanly coward! The Gentleman: But is there a living in that? The Note Taker: Oh yes. Quite a fat one. This is an age of upstarts. Men begin in Kentish Town with 80 pounds a year, and end in Park Lane with a hundred thousand. They want to drop Kentish Town; but they give themselves away every time they open their mouths. Now! can teach them— The Flower Girl: Let him mind his own business and leave a poor girl— The Note Taker: [explosively] Woman: cease this detestable boohooing instantly; or else seek the shelter of some other place of worship. The Flower Girl: [with feeble defiance] I’ve a right to be here if I like, same as you. The Note Taker: A woman who utters such depressing and disgusting sounds has no right to be anywhere—no right to live. Remember that you are a human being with a soul and the divine gift of articulate speech: that your native language is the language of Shakespeare and Milton and The Bible; and don’t sit there crooning like a bilious pigeon. The Flower Girl: [quite overwhelmed, and looking up at him in mingled wonder and deprecation without daring to raise her head] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow—oo! The Note Taker: [whipping out his book] Heavens! what a sound! [He writes; then holds out the book and reads, reproducing her vowels exactly] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow— ow— oo! The Flower Girl: [tickled by the performance, and laughing in spite of herself] Garn!','George Bernard Shaw’s play Pygmalion was the basis for the famous musical My Fair Lady. First performed in 1913, Pygmalion is the story of Eliza Doolittle, a poor and unsophisticated flower seller on the streets of London, who wants to learn to speak “like a lady.” In this passage from Act I, she has met by chance Professor Henry Higgins, a grumpy professor of phonetics, near a church during a rainstorm. They are amongst a group of people who have gathered under the church’s portico to stay dry. Henry has been listening to the other people as part of his research. Eliza was afraid for a time that he was a policeman trying to arrest her and is just beginning to recover from her fear. Henry is “The Note Taker” below. Eliza is “The Flower Girl,” and “The Gentleman” is Colonel Pickering, who was part of the group. The rain has stopped. The Flower Girl: Poor girl! Hard enough for her to live without being worried and chivied. The Gentleman: [returning to his former place on the note taker’s left] How do you do it, if I may ask? The Note Taker: Simply phonetics. The science of speech. That’s my profession; also my hobby. Happy is the man who can make a living by his hobby! You can spot an Irishman or a Yorkshireman by his brogue. I can place any man within six miles. I can place him within two miles in London. Sometimes within two streets. The Flower Girl: Ought to be ashamed of himself, unmanly coward! The Gentleman: But is there a living in that? The Note Taker: Oh yes. Quite a fat one. This is an age of upstarts. Men begin in Kentish Town with 80 pounds a year, and end in Park Lane with a hundred thousand. They want to drop Kentish Town; but they give themselves away every time they open their mouths. Now! can teach them— The Flower Girl: Let him mind his own business and leave a poor girl— The Note Taker: [explosively] Woman: cease this detestable boohooing instantly; or else seek the shelter of some other place of worship. The Flower Girl: [with feeble defiance] I’ve a right to be here if I like, same as you. The Note Taker: A woman who utters such depressing and disgusting sounds has no right to be anywhere—no right to live. Remember that you are a human being with a soul and the divine gift of articulate speech: that your native language is the language of Shakespeare and Milton and The Bible; and don’t sit there crooning like a bilious pigeon. The Flower Girl: [quite overwhelmed, and looking up at him in mingled wonder and deprecation without daring to raise her head] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow—oo! The Note Taker: [whipping out his book] Heavens! what a sound! [He writes; then holds out the book and reads, reproducing her vowels exactly] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow— ow— oo! The Flower Girl: [tickled by the performance, and laughing in spite of herself] Garn!',20,18,'14 \n \n \nPygmalion: Henry and Eliza \nGeorge Bernard Shaw’s play Pygmalion was the basis for the \nfamous musical My Fair Lady. First performed in 1913, Pygmalion is \nthe story of Eliza Doolittle, a poor and unsophisticated flower sell- er \non the streets of London, who wants to learn to speak “like a la- dy.” \nIn this passage from Act I, she has met by chance Professor Henry \nHiggins, a grumpy professor of phonetics, near a church dur- ing a \nrainstorm. They are amongst a group of people who have gath- ered \nunder the church’s portico to stay dry. Henry has been listen- ing to \nthe other people as part of his research. Eliza was afraid for a time \nthat he was a policeman trying to arrest her and is just begin- ning to \nrecover from her fear. Henry is “The Note Taker” below. Eliza is “The \nFlower Girl,” and “The Gentleman” is Colonel Pickering, who was \npart of the group. The rain has stopped. \nThe Flower Girl: Poor girl! Hard enough for her to live without being worried and \nchivied. \nThe Gentleman: [returning to his former place on the note taker’s left] How do you \ndo it, if I may ask? The Note Taker: Simply phonetics. The science of speech. That’s my \nprofession; also my hobby. Happy is the man who can make a living by his hobby! You \ncan spot an Irishman or a Yorkshireman by his brogue. I can place any man within six \nmiles. I can place him within two miles in London. Sometimes within two streets. \nThe Flower Girl: Ought to be ashamed of himself, unmanly coward! \nThe Gentle',1,1,'answer'),
(645,153,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: My First Baseball Game','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: My First Baseball Game\nAuthor: Randy Ryan\nWords: 257\n\nI remember my first Cubs game. My uncle came to take my sister and me to it. It was a sunny Saturday. He drove all the way from Indiana to get us. Then we took the train to the ballpark. He said it would be hard to find a parking space there, so he didn’t drive there. He was right. When we got there it was so crowded. We had tickets that were for seats far back. It seemed like I climbed about 300 steps to get to them. But when we got there and I turned around I could see everything. I saw the whole field. It was much better than watching it on TV. Then the game started. Everyone cheered. The man next to me said, “This is going to be the year. They are playing really well.” My uncle said to him, “Let’s hope this is the one.” But then he whispered to us, “Jerome and Felicia, we’ll have to wait and see. Remember last year? They started out well and then they lost at the end.” “Hey, Hey, Hey!” The Cub batter hit a ball far to the wall.\n\nJust then I heard “Hot dogs!” “Peanuts!” I looked and saw a man with a big tray hanging from his shoulders. It was full of hotdogs in plastic wrap and bags of peanuts. “Three hot dogs,” my uncle shouted. Then he gave the man next to us $6. He passed it to the man with the hot dogs.','assets/images/level4/g6-p19-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p19-1.webp\"]','My First Baseball Game. I remember my first Cubs game. My uncle came to take my sister and me to it. It was a sunny Saturday. He drove all the way from Indiana to get us. Then we took the train to the ballpark. He said it would be hard to find a parking space there, so he didn’t drive there. He was right. When we got there it was so crowded. We had tickets that were for seats far back. It seemed like I climbed about 300 steps to get to them. But when we got there and I turned around I could see everything. I saw the whole field. It was much better than watching it on TV. Then the game started. Everyone cheered. The man next to me said, “This is going to be the year. They are playing really well.” My uncle said to him, “Let’s hope this is the one.” But then he whispered to us, “Jerome and Felicia, we’ll have to wait and see. Remember last year? They started out well and then they lost at the end.” “Hey, Hey, Hey!” The Cub batter hit a ball far to the wall. Just then I heard “Hot dogs!” “Peanuts!” I looked and saw a man with a big tray hanging from his shoulders. It was full of hotdogs in plastic wrap and bags of peanuts. “Three hot dogs,” my uncle shouted. Then he gave the man next to us $6. He passed it to the man with the hot dogs.','I remember my first Cubs game. My uncle came to take my sister and me to it. It was a sunny Saturday. He drove all the way from Indiana to get us. Then we took the train to the ballpark. He said it would be hard to find a parking space there, so he didn’t drive there. He was right. When we got there it was so crowded. We had tickets that were for seats far back. It seemed like I climbed about 300 steps to get to them. But when we got there and I turned around I could see everything. I saw the whole field. It was much better than watching it on TV. Then the game started. Everyone cheered. The man next to me said, “This is going to be the year. They are playing really well.” My uncle said to him, “Let’s hope this is the one.” But then he whispered to us, “Jerome and Felicia, we’ll have to wait and see. Remember last year? They started out well and then they lost at the end.” “Hey, Hey, Hey!” The Cub batter hit a ball far to the wall. Just then I heard “Hot dogs!” “Peanuts!” I looked and saw a man with a big tray hanging from his shoulders. It was full of hotdogs in plastic wrap and bags of peanuts. “Three hot dogs,” my uncle shouted. Then he gave the man next to us $6. He passed it to the man with the hot dogs.',20,19,'15 \n \n \n \nMy First Baseball Game \nRandy Ryan \nI remember my first Cubs game. My uncle came to take my \nsister and me to it. It was a sunny Saturday. He drove all the way \nfrom Indiana to get us. Then we took the train to the ballpark. He \nsaid it would be hard to find a parking space there, so he didn’t \ndrive there.   He was right.  When we got there it was so crowded. \nWe had tickets that were for seats \nfar back. It seemed like I climbed \nabout 300 steps to get to them. \nBut when we got there and I \nturned around I could see every- \nthing. I saw the whole field. It \nwas much better than watching it \non TV. Then the game started. \nEveryone cheered. The man next \nto me said, “This is going to be the \nyear. \nThey \nare \nplaying \nreally \nwell.”  My uncle said to him, \n“Let’s hope this is the one.” But \nthen he whispered to us, “Jerome \nand Felicia, we’ll have to wait and \nsee. Remember last year? They \nstarted out well and then they lost \nat  the  end.”    “Hey,  Hey,  Hey!” \nThe Cub batter hit a ball far to the https://media.istockphoto.com/id/140233789/photo/young-slugger.jpg? \nwall. \ns=612x612&w=0&k=20&c=KuTiL8vpQSQpk7oHKHSDabjH4xWrDwimKSuu \nNBggSfQ= \nJust then I heard “Hot dogs!” “Peanuts!” I looked and saw a \nman with a big tray hanging from his shoulders. It was full of \nhotdogs in plastic wrap and bags of peanuts. “Three hot dogs,” my \nuncle shouted. Then he gave the man next to us $6. He passed it \nto the man with the hot dogs.  (257 Words) \nTypes of Mis- \ncues \nMispron',1,1,'answer'),
(646,154,67,'post',1,'Let’s get ready','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next.','Your teacher will listen as you read a story aloud. Read at your own pace, the way you talk. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','assets/images/level4/g6-p21-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p21-1.webp\"]','Your teacher will listen as you read a story aloud. Read at your own pace, the way you talk. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.',NULL,5,21,'Post- test Passage \n \n17 \n\"Bad Girl\" \n \nHey! Everybody seems to be staring at me.. \nYou! You! All of you! \nHow dare you to stare at me? \nWhy? Is it because I\'m a bad girl? \nA bad girl I am, A good for nothing teen ager, a \nproblem child? \nThat\'s what you call me! \nI smoke. I drink. I gamble at my young tender \nage. \nI lie. I cheat, and I could even kill, If I have too. \nYes, I\'m a bad girl, but where are my parents? \nYou! You! You are my good parents? \nMy good elder brother and sister in this society \nwhere I live? \nLook…look at me…What have you done to me? \nYou have pampered and spoiled me,neglected me when I needed you \nmost! \nEntrusted me to a yaya, whose intelligence was much lower than mine! \nWhile you go about your parties, your meetings and gambling session… \nThus… I drifted away from you! \nLonging for a father\'s love, yearning for a mother\'s care! \nAs I grew up, everything changed! \nYou too have changed! \nYou spent more time in your poker, mahjong tables, bars and night \nclubs. You even landed on the headlines of the newspaper as crooks, \npeddlers and you racketeers. \nNow, you call me names, accuse me of everything I do to myself? \nTell me! How good are you? \nIf you really wish to ensure my future… \nThen hurry….hurry back home! Where I await you, because I need you… \nProtect me from all evil influences that will threaten at my very own \nunderstanding… \nBut if I am bad, really bad…then, you\'ve got to help me! \nHelp me! Oh please…Help me \n \nTypes of Mis- \ncues \nMispronu',0,1,'none'),
(647,154,67,'post',2,'Read aloud: \"Bad Girl\"','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Title: \"Bad Girl\"\nWords: 269\n\nHey! Everybody seems to be staring at me..\nYou! You! All of you!\nHow dare you to stare at me?\nWhy? Is it because I\'m a bad girl?\nA bad girl I am, A good for nothing teenager, a\nproblem child?\nThat\'s what you call me!\nI smoke. I drink. I gamble at my young tender\nage.\nI lie. I cheat, and I could even kill, If I have too.\nYes, I\'m a bad girl, but where are my parents?\nYou! You! You are my good parents?\nMy good elder brother and sister in this society\nwhere I live?\nLook…look at me…What have you done to me?\nYou have pampered and spoiled me,neglected me when I needed you\nmost!\nEntrusted me to a yaya, whose intelligence was much lower than mine!\nWhile you go about your parties, your meetings and gambling session…\nThus… I drifted away from you!\nLonging for a father\'s love, yearning for a mother\'s care!\nAs I grew up, everything changed!\nYou too have changed!\nYou spent more time in your poker, mahjong tables, bars and night\nclubs. You even landed on the headlines of the newspaper as crooks,\npeddlers and you racketeers.\nNow, you call me names, accuse me of everything I do to myself?\nTell me! How good are you?\nIf you really wish to ensure my future…\nThen hurry….hurry back home! Where I await you, because I need you…\nProtect me from all evil influences that will threaten at my very own\nunderstanding…\nBut if I am bad, really bad…then, you\'ve got to help me!\nHelp me! Oh please…Help me','assets/images/level4/g6-p21-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level4/g6-p21-1.webp\"]','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy and speed.','Hey! Everybody seems to be staring at me.. You! You! All of you! How dare you to stare at me? Why? Is it because I\'m a bad girl? A bad girl I am, A good for nothing teenager, a problem child? That\'s what you call me! I smoke. I drink. I gamble at my young tender age. I lie. I cheat, and I could even kill, If I have too. Yes, I\'m a bad girl, but where are my parents? You! You! You are my good parents? My good elder brother and sister in this society where I live? Look…look at me…What have you done to me? You have pampered and spoiled me,neglected me when I needed you most! Entrusted me to a yaya, whose intelligence was much lower than mine! While you go about your parties, your meetings and gambling session… Thus… I drifted away from you! Longing for a father\'s love, yearning for a mother\'s care! As I grew up, everything changed! You too have changed! You spent more time in your poker, mahjong tables, bars and night clubs. You even landed on the headlines of the newspaper as crooks, peddlers and you racketeers. Now, you call me names, accuse me of everything I do to myself? Tell me! How good are you? If you really wish to ensure my future… Then hurry….hurry back home! Where I await you, because I need you… Protect me from all evil influences that will threaten at my very own understanding… But if I am bad, really bad…then, you\'ve got to help me! Help me! Oh please…Help me',20,21,'Post- test Passage \n \n17 \n\"Bad Girl\" \n \nHey! Everybody seems to be staring at me.. \nYou! You! All of you! \nHow dare you to stare at me? \nWhy? Is it because I\'m a bad girl? \nA bad girl I am, A good for nothing teen ager, a \nproblem child? \nThat\'s what you call me! \nI smoke. I drink. I gamble at my young tender \nage. \nI lie. I cheat, and I could even kill, If I have too. \nYes, I\'m a bad girl, but where are my parents? \nYou! You! You are my good parents? \nMy good elder brother and sister in this society \nwhere I live? \nLook…look at me…What have you done to me? \nYou have pampered and spoiled me,neglected me when I needed you \nmost! \nEntrusted me to a yaya, whose intelligence was much lower than mine! \nWhile you go about your parties, your meetings and gambling session… \nThus… I drifted away from you! \nLonging for a father\'s love, yearning for a mother\'s care! \nAs I grew up, everything changed! \nYou too have changed! \nYou spent more time in your poker, mahjong tables, bars and night \nclubs. You even landed on the headlines of the newspaper as crooks, \npeddlers and you racketeers. \nNow, you call me names, accuse me of everything I do to myself? \nTell me! How good are you? \nIf you really wish to ensure my future… \nThen hurry….hurry back home! Where I await you, because I need you… \nProtect me from all evil influences that will threaten at my very own \nunderstanding… \nBut if I am bad, really bad…then, you\'ve got to help me! \nHelp me! Oh please…Help me \n \nTypes of Mis- \ncues \nMispronu',1,1,'answer'),
(648,155,68,'pre',1,'Pre-assessment · page 1','open','Listen and look at the page. Type or say your answers with their numbers, for example 1-b, 2-a.','PRETEST (Activity 1-10)\nVocabulary Development (1-10)\nA. Instructions: Draw a line to match the word to the\ncorrect picture.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. Nose\na.\n2. Teddy bear\nb.\n3. Mother\nc.\n4. Shovel\nd.\n5. Chair\ne.','assets/images/level5/g1/p007-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level5/g1/p007-1.webp\", \"assets/images/level5/g1/p007-2.webp\", \"assets/images/level5/g1/p007-4.webp\", \"assets/images/level5/g1/p007-3.webp\"]','PRETEST (Activity 1-10) Vocabulary Development (1-10) A. Instructions: Draw a line to match the word to the correct picture. Column A Column B 1. Nose a. 2. Teddy bear b. 3. Mother c. 4. Shovel d. 5. Chair e.',NULL,10,7,'PRETEST (Activity 1-10)\nVocabulary Development (1-10)\nA. Instructions: Draw a line to match the word to the\ncorrect picture.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. Nose\na.\n2. Teddy bear\nb.\n3. Mother\nc.\n4. Shovel\nd.\n5. Chair\ne.',1,1,'answer'),
(649,155,68,'pre',2,'Pre-assessment · page 2','open','Listen and look at the page. Type or say your answers with their numbers, for example 1-b, 2-a.','B. Instructions: Draw a line to match the meaning to\nthe correct picture.\n1. An animal that barks\na.\n2. Used for writing\nb.\n3. Comes from chicken\nc.\n4. The father of your mother\nd.\nor father\n5. A dish used for holding\ne.\nliquids often with handle','assets/images/level5/g1/p008-3.webp','[\"assets/images/level5/g1/p008-3.webp\", \"assets/images/level5/g1/p008-4.webp\", \"assets/images/level5/g1/p008-1.webp\", \"assets/images/level5/g1/p008-2.webp\", \"assets/images/level5/g1/p008-5.webp\"]','B. Instructions: Draw a line to match the meaning to the correct picture. 1. An animal that barks a. 2. Used for writing b. 3. Comes from chicken c. 4. The father of your mother d. or father 5. A dish used for holding e. liquids often with handle',NULL,10,8,'B. Instructions: Draw a line to match the meaning to\nthe correct picture.\n1. An animal that barks\na.\n2. Used for writing\nb.\n3. Comes from chicken\nc.\n4. The father of your mother\nd.\nor father\n5. A dish used for holding\ne.\nliquids often with handle',1,1,'answer'),
(650,155,68,'pre',3,'Pre-assessment · page 3','open','Listen and look at the page. Type or say your answers with their numbers, for example 1-b, 2-a.','A. Listening Comprehension:\nInstructions: Listen carefully as your teacher reads the\nsentences/passage. Then, answer the questions below.\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n(Read Aloud) “My Toys” These are my toys. My mother and\nfather gives it to me! I can name my favorite toys.\n1. “My parents gave me something I can throw and catch.\nIt’s round and bouncy. What is it?”\na. Kite\nb. Ball\nc. Doll\nd. Teddy Bear\n2. “I love to play with my toy on the water. It has a sail and\nhelps me pretend I’m a sailor. What am I playing with?\na. Car\nb. Train\nc. Boat\nd. Yoyo\n3. (Read Aloud) “My Pets” These are my pets. I will take\ncare of my pets. I will give them food. I love my pets.\nWrite T if it is true and F if it is false.\n______ A dog can bark.\n______ A fish can fly in the sky\n4. (Read Aloud) “My Things” These are my things. I will take\ncare of my things. I will keep it clean.',NULL,'[]','A. Listening Comprehension: Instructions: Listen carefully as your teacher reads the sentences/passage. Then, answer the questions below. Choose the letter of the correct answer. (Read Aloud) “My Toys” These are my toys. My mother and father gives it to me! I can name my favorite toys. 1. “My parents gave me something I can throw and catch. It’s round and bouncy. What is it?” a. Kite b. Ball c. Doll d. Teddy Bear 2. “I love to play with my toy on the water. It has a sail and helps me pretend I’m a sailor. What am I playing with? a. Car b. Train c. Boat d. Yoyo 3. (Read Aloud) “My Pets” These are my pets. I will take care of my pets. I will give them food. I love my pets. Write T if it is true and F if it is false. blank A dog can bark. blank A fish can fly in the sky 4. (Read Aloud) “My Things” These are my things. I will take care of my things. I will keep it clean.',NULL,10,9,'A. Listening Comprehension:\nInstructions: Listen carefully as your teacher reads the\nsentences/passage. Then, answer the questions below.\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n(Read Aloud) “My Toys” These are my toys. My mother and\nfather gives it to me! I can name my favorite toys.\n1. “My parents gave me something I can throw and catch.\nIt’s round and bouncy. What is it?”\na. Kite\nb. Ball\nc. Doll\nd. Teddy Bear\n2. “I love to play with my toy on the water. It has a sail and\nhelps me pretend I’m a sailor. What am I playing with?\na. Car\nb. Train\nc. Boat\nd. Yoyo\n3. (Read Aloud) “My Pets” These are my pets. I will take\ncare of my pets. I will give them food. I love my pets.\nWrite T if it is true and F if it is false.\n______ A dog can bark.\n______ A fish can fly in the sky\n4. (Read Aloud) “My Things” These are my things. I will take\ncare of my things. I will keep it clean.',1,1,'answer'),
(651,155,68,'pre',4,'Pre-assessment · page 4','open','Listen and look at the page. Type or say your answers with their numbers, for example 1-b, 2-a.','What should you do with your things?\na. Break them\nb. Take care of them\nc. Make it tidy\nWhat does “keep it clean” mean?\na. Make it dirty\nb. Make it new\nc. Make it tidy\n5. (Read Aloud) “My Food” These are the foods I eat. My\nfather buys them for me. My mother cook these for me.\nWho cooks the food in the story?\na. Father\nb. Mother\nc. Friend\n6. (Read Aloud) “My Family” This is my family. My mother\nand my father. My brother and my sister. And our baby\nboy. Who is in the family?\na. Friends\nb. Relatives\nc. Mother, Father, Brother, Sister, and Baby\n7. (Read Aloud) “My Home” This is my home. We have\ntables and chairs in our home. We have rooms and',NULL,'[]','What should you do with your things? a. Break them b. Take care of them c. Make it tidy What does “keep it clean” mean? a. Make it dirty b. Make it new c. Make it tidy 5. (Read Aloud) “My Food” These are the foods I eat. My father buys them for me. My mother cook these for me. Who cooks the food in the story? a. Father b. Mother c. Friend 6. (Read Aloud) “My Family” This is my family. My mother and my father. My brother and my sister. And our baby boy. Who is in the family? a. Friends b. Relatives c. Mother, Father, Brother, Sister, and Baby 7. (Read Aloud) “My Home” This is my home. We have tables and chairs in our home. We have rooms and',NULL,10,10,'What should you do with your things?\na. Break them\nb. Take care of them\nc. Make it tidy\nWhat does “keep it clean” mean?\na. Make it dirty\nb. Make it new\nc. Make it tidy\n5. (Read Aloud) “My Food” These are the foods I eat. My\nfather buys them for me. My mother cook these for me.\nWho cooks the food in the story?\na. Father\nb. Mother\nc. Friend\n6. (Read Aloud) “My Family” This is my family. My mother\nand my father. My brother and my sister. And our baby\nboy. Who is in the family?\na. Friends\nb. Relatives\nc. Mother, Father, Brother, Sister, and Baby\n7. (Read Aloud) “My Home” This is my home. We have\ntables and chairs in our home. We have rooms and',1,1,'answer'),
(652,155,68,'pre',5,'Pre-assessment · page 5','open','Listen and look at the page. Type or say your answers with their numbers, for example 1-b, 2-a.','beds in our home. We have the kitchen and the\ncomfort rooms too. What is the story about?\na. My Garden\nb. My Home\nc. My Kitchen\n8. (Read Aloud) “Our Tools” This is our tools. We have\nshovel and rake. We have to take care of our tools. Tools\nare very important for us. What is the main idea of the\nstory?\na. Tools are fun\nb. Tools are useful and we should take care of\nthem\nc. Tools are expensive',NULL,'[]','beds in our home. We have the kitchen and the comfort rooms too. What is the story about? a. My Garden b. My Home c. My Kitchen 8. (Read Aloud) “Our Tools” This is our tools. We have shovel and rake. We have to take care of our tools. Tools are very important for us. What is the main idea of the story? a. Tools are fun b. Tools are useful and we should take care of them c. Tools are expensive',NULL,10,11,'beds in our home. We have the kitchen and the\ncomfort rooms too. What is the story about?\na. My Garden\nb. My Home\nc. My Kitchen\n8. (Read Aloud) “Our Tools” This is our tools. We have\nshovel and rake. We have to take care of our tools. Tools\nare very important for us. What is the main idea of the\nstory?\na. Tools are fun\nb. Tools are useful and we should take care of\nthem\nc. Tools are expensive',1,1,'answer'),
