(1029,356,88,'pre',4,'The Bees','open','Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice','Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the tree with the hive, what could be the result for the bees?\nA. They would continue making honey in the same place.\nB. They might have to find a new place to live.\nC. The queen bee would sting the young bees.\nD. The workers would become lazy.\n\nIf the bees did not make honey from nectar, what might happen?\nA. The young bees might go hungry.\nB. The drones would start collecting nectar.\nC. The hive would produce more nectar.\nD. The workers would leave the swarm.','assets/images/level6/g3/p012-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g3/p012-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the tree with the hive, what could be the result for the bees?\nA. They would continue making honey in the same place.\nB. They might have to find a new place to live.\nC. The queen bee would sting the young bees.\nD. The workers would become lazy.\n\nIf the bees did not make honey from nectar, what might happen?\nA. The young bees might go hungry.\nB. The drones would start collecting nectar.\nC. The hive would produce more nectar.\nD. The workers would leave the swarm.',NULL,10,12,'Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the t',1,1,'answer'),
(1030,357,NULL,'learn',1,'A Rainy Day','open','Direction: Read the story below and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','Direction: Read the story below and encircle the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Rainy Day\nIt was a rainy day. The children could not go out to play. So Lucy, Zeny and Perla stayed in the house.\n\n“Let’s cut out paper fruits for our store,” said Lucy.\n\nSo, the girls got their papers, scissors and crayons. Lucy cut out 5 apples. She colored them red. Zeny cut out 6 mangoes. She colored them yellow. Perla cut out 4 guavas. She colored them green. Then they counted their paper fruits.\n\n“Now we have many pretty paper fruits for our store,” said Perla.\n\nWhat kind of day was it?\nA. Sunny\nB. rainy\nC. bright\n\nHow many girls are in the story?\nA. two\nB. three\nC. four\n\nWhere did the girls stay?\nA. In school\nB. In the yard\nC. In the house\n\nWhat did Lucy cut out?\nA. 5 apples\nB. 6 mangoes\nC. 4 guavas\n\nWhat color were the apples?\nA. Green\nB. yellow\nC. red\n\nWhy did the girls couldn’t go out?\nA. Because it’s raining.\nB. Because it’s so hot .\nC. Because it’s stormy.\n\nHow did the girls make use of their time on a rainy day?\nA. cut out paper fruits\nB. played hide-and-seek\nC. cooked soup\n\nIf you were the girls, how would you spend a rainy day?\nA. play under the rain\nB. sleep\nC. read stories','assets/images/level6/g3/p015-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g3/p015-1.webp\"]','Direction: Read the story below and encircle the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Rainy Day\nIt was a rainy day. The children could not go out to play. So Lucy, Zeny and Perla stayed in the house.\n\n“Let’s cut out paper fruits for our store,” said Lucy.\n\nSo, the girls got their papers, scissors and crayons. Lucy cut out 5 apples. She colored them red. Zeny cut out 6 mangoes. She colored them yellow. Perla cut out 4 guavas. She colored them green. Then they counted their paper fruits.\n\n“Now we have many pretty paper fruits for our store,”