Tomorrow\n\nWhat did the farmer have?\nA. A mango tree\nB. A corn field\nC. A garden\n\nWhat words describe the farmer’s corn?\nA. Small and sour\nB. Big and sweet\nC. Rotten and old\n\nWhat happened to the farmer’s corn?\nA. It was eaten by animals\nB. Some of it was gone and plants had no fruits\nC. It was harvested for sale\n\nWhere did the farmer hide to catch the thief?\nA. Behind a mango tree\nB. In a corn plot\nC. On the roof',1,1,'answer'),
(1068,377,89,'post',4,'The Bees','open','Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice','Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the tree with the hive, what could be the result for the bees?\nA. They would continue making honey in the same place.\nB. They might have to find a new place to live.\nC. The queen bee would sting the young bees.\nD. The workers would become lazy.\n\nIf the bees did not make honey from nectar, what might happen?\nA. The young bees might go hungry.\nB. The drones would start collecting nectar.\nC. The hive would produce more nectar.\nD. The workers would leave the swarm.','assets/images/level6/g3/p062-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g3/p062-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the tree with the hive, what could be the result for the bees?\nA. They would continue making honey in the same place.\nB. They might have to find a new place to live.\nC. The queen bee would sting the young bees.\nD. The workers would become lazy.\n\nIf the bees did not make honey from nectar, what might happen?\nA. The young bees might go hungry.\nB. The drones would start collecting nectar.\nC. The hive would produce more nectar.\nD. The workers would leave the swarm.',NULL,10,62,'Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the t',1,1,'answer'),
(1069,378,90,'pre',1,'Ana’s Pet','open','Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.','Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nAna’s Pet\nMother bought two piglets. She had a pigpen ready for them. “These pigs should be given good care,” she told Ana. “Can you help me take care of them?” “Of course, Mother,” answered Ana. “l’ll help you take care of them.” So, Ana became the caretaker of the piglets. She cleaned the pen early in the morning and gave them baths. She fed them swill which she got from their neighbors. Soon the piglets grew big and fat. When they were ready for sale, Ana felt sad. She knew Mother would get a good price for them but she would lose her pet.\n\nWhat did Ana’s Mother buy?\nA. Two piglets\nB. Three piglets\nC. Four piglets\n\nWhere did she keep them?\nA. In their yard\nB. In a pigpen\nC. Under the house\n\nWhom did she ask to help her take care of them?\nA. Ana\nB. her maid\nC. her neighbor\n\nDid Ana like to take care of the pigs?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Perhaps\n\nWhat new job did Ana have?\nA. nurse of the pigs\nB. friend of the pigs\nC. caretaker of the pigs\n\nAt what time of the day did she clean the pigpen?\nA. early in the morning\nB. late in the afternoon\nC. at noon\n\nWho gave baths to the pigs?\nA. Ana herself\nB. Ana’s sister\nC. Ana’s mother\n\nWhere did Ana get the swill for the pigs?\nA. from the market\nB. from a nearby store\nC. from their neighbors\n\nWhat happened with the pigs?\nA. Became sick\nB. Grew big and fat\nC. Did not grow at all\n\nHow did Ana feel when the pigs were ready for sale?\nA. very happy\nB. angry\nC. sad','assets/images/level6/g4/p007-1.w