w a little red feather on her blanket. She thought her bird was beside her. Then she found other feathers scattered on the floor. Slowly she got up and looked at her bird in the cage. The cage was empty.\n\nDolor cried bitterly. She cried and cried and would not eat. Her mother was worried. Jun was very sorry.\n\nThen Jun got his savings and went out. “I must do something for Dolor,” he said.\n\nWhat happen with the bird?\nA. It flew away.\nB. It got sick and die.\nC. It was eaten by the cat.\n\nWhat might happen to dolor due to the disappearance of her bird?\nA. She might get well very soon.\nB. She might get worse.\nC. She might become kinder.\n\nWhat will June do with his savings?\nA. give it to Dolor.\nB. buy doll for Dolor.\nC. buy another bird for Dolor.',1,1,'answer'),
(1085,390,NULL,'learn',1,'Banana Peelings','open','Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.','Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nBanana Peelings\nBen and Lito always pray and eat lunch in school together. One day after eating several ripe bananas which Lito brought, the two boys played war. They threw banana peeling at each other. After playing they forgot about banana peelings which they threw on the cemented porch. Then, the school bell rang. The boys and girls formed their lines. As they passed by the porch to their room, something happened. Three boys helped Lisa to the school clinic. Lisa was then brought to a hospital because of a broken knee. Her knee was then placed in plaster cast. How sorry Ben and Lito were. How they wished they did not play war after eating those bananas! How they wish they throw those bananas peelings in th garbage can at once.\n\nWhat happened when the boys and girls passed by the porch?\nA. some girls and boys ate bananas, too.\nB. some boys played war, too.\nC. a girl slipped when she stepped on the banana peelings.\n\nWhy boys and girls formed their lines?\nA. because they’ll buy food to canteen.\nB. because it’s flag ceremony time.\nC. because it is time to get inside their room.\n\nHow long do you think would it take before Lisa could move about again?\nA. one day\nB. one-month\nC. one week','assets/images/level6/g4/p033-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p033-1.webp\"]','Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nBanana Peelings\nBen and Lito always pray and eat lunch in school together. One day after eating several ripe bananas which Lito brought, the two boys played war. They threw banana peeling at each other. After playing they forgot about banana peelings which they threw on the cemented porch. Then, the school bell rang. The boys and girls formed their lines. As they passed by the porch to their room, something happened. Three boys helped Lisa to the school clinic. Lisa was then brought to a hospital because of a broken knee. Her knee was then placed in plaster cast. How sorry Ben and Lito were. How they wished they did not play war after eating those bananas! How they wish they throw those bananas peelings in th garbage can at once.\n\nWhat happened when the boys and girls passed by the porch?\nA. some girls and boys ate bananas, too.\nB. some boys played war, too.\nC. a girl slipped when she stepped on the banana peelings.\n\nWhy boys and girls formed their lines?\nA. because they’ll buy food to canteen.\nB. because it’s flag ceremony time.\nC. because it is time to get inside their room.\n\nHow long do you think would it take before Lisa could move about again?\nA. one day\nB. one-month\nC. one week',NULL,10,33,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nBanana Peelings\nBen and Lito always pray and eat lunch in school together. One day after eating several ripe bananas which Lito brought, the two boys played war. They threw banana peeling at each other. After playing they forgot about banana peelings which they threw on the cemented porch. Then, the school bell rang. The boys and girls formed their lines. As they passed by the porch to their room, something happened. Three boys helped Lisa to the school clinic. Lisa was then brought to a hospital because of a broken knee. Her knee was then placed in plaster cast. How sorry Ben and Lito were. How they wished they did not play war after eating those bananas! How they wish they throw those bananas peelings in th garbage can at once.\n\nWhat happened when the boys and girls passed by the porch?\nA. some girls and boys ate bananas, too.\nB. some boys played war, too.\nC. a girl slipped when she stepped on the banana peelings.\n\nWhy boys and girls formed their lines?\nA. because they’ll buy food to canteen.\nB. because it’s flag ceremony time.\nC. because it is time to get inside their room.\n\nHow long do you think would it take before Lisa could move about again?\nA. one day\nB. one-month\nC. one week',1,1,'answer'),
(1086,391,NULL,'learn',1,'Months of the Year','open','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nMonths of the Year\nDo you know the months of the year in their proper order? You may use a scratch paper if you like, in following the directions below.\n\nWrite the first month of the year.\n\nWhat month has the shortest name? Write it down.\n\nWrite the month which comes after July.\n\nWrite the month which comes before September?\n\nWrite the name of the month when Christmas is celebrated.','assets/images/level6/g4/p034-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p034-1.webp\"]','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nMonths of the Year\nDo you know the months of the year in their proper order? You may use a scratch paper if you like, in following the directions below.\n\nWrite the first month of the year.\n\nWhat month has the shortest name? Write it down.\n\nWrite the month which comes after July.\n\nWrite the month which comes before September?\n\nWrite the name of the month when Christmas is celebrated.',NULL,10,34,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nMonths of the Year\nDo you know the months of the year in their proper order? You may use a scratch paper if you like, in following the directions below.\n\nWrite the first month of the year.\n\nWhat month has the shortest name? Write it down.\n\nWrite the month which comes after July.\n\nWrite the month which comes before September?\n\nWrite the name of the month when Christmas is celebrated.',1,1,'answer'),
(1087,392,NULL,'learn',1,'The English Alphabet','open','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nThe English Alphabet\nHow well do you know the English alphabet? Use a scratch paper if you think you need one in following these directions.\n\nWrite down the first letter in the English alphabet.\n\nWrite down the last letter in the English alphabet and encircle it.\n\nDraw a square. Inside it, write the third letter of the alphabet.\n\nWrite the word “boil”. Cross out its, first letter. In its place, write the third letter of the alphabet.\n\nWrite the letter in the alphabet that comes after “m”.','assets/images/level6/g4/p035-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p035-1.webp\"]','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nThe English Alphabet\nHow well do you know the English alphabet? Use a scratch paper if you think you need one in following these directions.\n\nWrite down the first letter in the English alphabet.\n\nWrite down the last letter in the English alphabet and encircle it.\n\nDraw a square. Inside it, write the third letter of the alphabet.\n\nWrite the word “boil”. Cross out its, first letter. In its place, write the third letter of the alphabet.\n\nWrite the letter in the alphabet that comes after “m”.',NULL,10,35,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nThe English Alphabet\nHow well do you know the English alphabet? Use a scratch paper if you think you need one in following these directions.\n\nWrite down the first letter in the English alphabet.\n\nWrite down the last letter in the English alphabet and encircle it.\n\nDraw a square. Inside it, write the third letter of the alphabet.\n\nWrite the word “boil”. Cross out its, first letter. In its place, write the third letter of the alphabet.\n\nWrite the letter in the alphabet that comes after “m”.',1,1,'answer'),
(1088,393,NULL,'learn',1,'Our Nipa Hut','open','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nOur Nipa Hut\nSee our nipa hut, It’s home to all of us; It’s here that we live, Father, Mother and I. Inside our nipa hut, There’s not much to see; A chair, a table, a bed, Which to visitors we give. Behind our nipa hut, Are fruit trees big and small; Duhat, santol, balimbing, Guava, chico, and atis too.\n\nDraw a nipa hut.\n\nCopy the title of the poem in printed form.\n\nCopy the line that tells who lives in the nipa hut.\n\nDraw one of the things that can be seen inside the nipa hut.\n\nWrite at least 3 fruit trees that can be found behind the nipa hut.','assets/images/level6/g4/p036-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p036-1.webp\"]','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nOur Nipa Hut\nSee our nipa hut, It’s home to all of us; It’s here that we live, Father, Mother and I. Inside our nipa hut, There’s not much to see; A chair, a table, a bed, Which to visitors we give. Behind our nipa hut, Are fruit trees big and small; Duhat, santol, balimbing, Guava, chico, and atis too.\n\nDraw a nipa hut.\n\nCopy the title of the poem in printed form.\n\nCopy the line that tells who lives in the nipa hut.\n\nDraw one of the things that can be seen inside the nipa hut.\n\nWrite at least 3 fruit trees that can be found behind the nipa hut.',NULL,10,36,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nOur Nipa Hut\nSee our nipa hut, It’s home to all of us; It’s here that we live, Father, Mother and I. Inside our nipa hut, There’s not much to see; A chair, a table, a bed, Which to visitors we give. Behind our nipa hut, Are fruit trees big and small; Duhat, santol, balimbing, Guava, chico, and atis too.\n\nDraw a nipa hut.\n\nCopy the title of the poem in printed form.\n\nCopy the line that tells who lives in the nipa hut.\n\nDraw one of the things that can be seen inside the nipa hut.\n\nWrite at least 3 fruit trees that can be found behind the nipa hut.',1,1,'answer'),
(1089,394,NULL,'learn',1,'Understanding Directions','open','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nUnderstanding Directions\n\nWrite the name of the place in your school where children borrow and read books, newspapers and magazines.\n\nCopy the three words in the list below that refer to animals. Words in the list: vegetable, kitchen, cat, bark, fruit, kettle, tiger, elephant, bag, castle\n\nCopy the word below and write its opposite. Clean-\n\nWrite the word “boil”. Cross out its first letter.\n\nWrite down the name of our country. Underline its first letter.','assets/images/level6/g4/p037-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p037-1.webp\"]','Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nUnderstanding Directions\n\nWrite the name of the place in your school where children borrow and read books, newspapers and magazines.\n\nCopy the three words in the list below that refer to animals. Words in the list: vegetable, kitchen, cat, bark, fruit, kettle, tiger, elephant, bag, castle\n\nCopy the word below and write its opposite. Clean-\n\nWrite the word “boil”. Cross out its first letter.\n\nWrite down the name of our country. Underline its first letter.',NULL,10,37,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nUnderstanding Directions\n\nWrite the name of the place in your school where children borrow and read books, newspapers and magazines.\n\nCopy the three words in the list below that refer to animals. Words in the list: vegetable, kitchen, cat, bark, fruit, kettle, tiger, elephant, bag, castle\n\nCopy the word below and write its opposite. Clean-\n\nWrite the word “boil”. Cross out its first letter.\n\nWrite down the name of our country. Underline its first letter.',1,1,'answer'),
(1090,395,NULL,'learn',1,'The Kingfisher and the Cat','open','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Kingfisher and the Cat\nOne fine morning, a kingfisher sat on a branch of a tree. She was busy eating a fresh fish which she caught in the river. A hungry cat ran under the tree and asked a piece of it. The kingfisher did not want to share the fish.\n\nThe cat was very hungry so she thought of a way to get the fish from the kingfisher. She asked the kingfisher to sing.\n\nThe silly kingfisher could not sing at all. But she was so flattered that she opened her mouth and tried. Of course, she dropped the fish.\n\nThe cat gulped it down hurriedly. Then she ran away laughing.','assets/images/level6/g4/p038-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p038-1.webp\", \"assets/images/level6/g4/p038-2.webp\"]','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Kingfisher and the Cat\nOne fine morning, a kingfisher sat on a branch of a tree. She was busy eating a fresh fish which she caught in the river. A hungry cat ran under the tree and asked a piece of it. The kingfisher did not want to share the fish.\n\nThe cat was very hungry so she thought of a way to get the fish from the kingfisher. She asked the kingfisher to sing.\n\nThe silly kingfisher could not sing at all. But she was so flattered that she opened her mouth and tried. Of course, she dropped the fish.\n\nThe cat gulped it down hurriedly. Then she ran away laughing.',NULL,10,38,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Kingfisher and the Cat\nOne fine morning, a kingfisher sat on a branch of a tree. She was busy eating a fresh fish which she caught in the river. A hungry cat ran under the tree and asked a piece of it. The kingfisher did not want to share the fish.\n\nThe cat was very hungry so she thought of a way to get the fish from the kingfisher. She asked the kingfisher to sing.\n\nThe silly kingfisher could not sing at all. But she was so flattered that she opened her mouth and tried. Of course, she dropped the fish.\n\nThe cat gulped it down hurriedly. Then she ran away laughing.',1,1,'answer'),
(1091,396,NULL,'learn',1,'A Dream That Became a Reality','open','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Dream That Became a Reality\nFor many, many years, people had always dreamed of going to the moon. This seemed impossible because the moon is very far from Earth.\n\nNot even the biggest, fastest and most powerful airplane could ever reach the moon. Then, very powerful rocket ships were made. These rocket ships could travel fast enough to escape the earth’s gravity, withstand the sun’s heat and land on the moon. On July 20, 1969, an almost impossible dream came true. Neil Armstrong, together with two other American astronauts, made a trip to the moon aboard Apollo 11, a spacecraft. Neil Armstrong became the very first man to land on the moon.','assets/images/level6/g4/p039-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p039-1.webp\"]','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Dream That Became a Reality\nFor many, many years, people had always dreamed of going to the moon. This seemed impossible because the moon is very far from Earth.\n\nNot even the biggest, fastest and most powerful airplane could ever reach the moon. Then, very powerful rocket ships were made. These rocket ships could travel fast enough to escape the earth’s gravity, withstand the sun’s heat and land on the moon. On July 20, 1969, an almost impossible dream came true. Neil Armstrong, together with two other American astronauts, made a trip to the moon aboard Apollo 11, a spacecraft. Neil Armstrong became the very first man to land on the moon.',NULL,10,39,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Dream That Became a Reality\nFor many, many years, people had always dreamed of going to the moon. This seemed impossible because the moon is very far from Earth.\n\nNot even the biggest, fastest and most powerful airplane could ever reach the moon. Then, very powerful rocket ships were made. These rocket ships could travel fast enough to escape the earth’s gravity, withstand the sun’s heat and land on the moon. On July 20, 1969, an almost impossible dream came true. Neil Armstrong, together with two other American astronauts, made a trip to the moon aboard Apollo 11, a spacecraft. Neil Armstrong became the very first man to land on the moon.',1,1,'answer'),
(1092,397,NULL,'learn',1,'The Ant and the Grasshopper','open','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Ant and the Grasshopper\nThe ant and the grasshopper were good friends. The ant was industrious but the grasshopper was lazy. While the ant was busy storing food for the rainy days, the grasshopper did nothing all day but sing and play. When the rain came, the lazy grasshopper shivered with the cold and hunger. He went to the ant’s house and begged for some food. The ant gave him food but he kept on coming back for more.\n\nThe ant lost her patience and said, “You did nothing but sing and play during the sunny days. Now you can sing and play in the rain. I have nothing more to give you”','assets/images/level6/g4/p040-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p040-1.webp\"]','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Ant and the Grasshopper\nThe ant and the grasshopper were good friends. The ant was industrious but the grasshopper was lazy. While the ant was busy storing food for the rainy days, the grasshopper did nothing all day but sing and play. When the rain came, the lazy grasshopper shivered with the cold and hunger. He went to the ant’s house and begged for some food. The ant gave him food but he kept on coming back for more.\n\nThe ant lost her patience and said, “You did nothing but sing and play during the sunny days. Now you can sing and play in the rain. I have nothing more to give you”',NULL,10,40,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Ant and the Grasshopper\nThe ant and the grasshopper were good friends. The ant was industrious but the grasshopper was lazy. While the ant was busy storing food for the rainy days, the grasshopper did nothing all day but sing and play. When the rain came, the lazy grasshopper shivered with the cold and hunger. He went to the ant’s house and begged for some food. The ant gave him food but he kept on coming back for more.\n\nThe ant lost her patience and said, “You did nothing but sing and play during the sunny days. Now you can sing and play in the rain. I have nothing more to give you”',1,1,'answer'),
(1093,398,NULL,'learn',1,'A Medal on the Barong Filipino','open','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Medal on the Barong Filipino\nManuel is a young artist. He is good in drawing almost anything. Some of his works have been shown at school exhibit. One day, he joined an art contest. His painting won the first prize. He was invited to attend a program where he would be given the prize. One of his friends said, “In this program you will be given a medal. It will be nice to wear coat and tie.” “What? Wear coat and tie for that medal? I’m going to wear a Barong Filipino. The Barong Filipino is just as good as any other clothes for a medal of honor.” The medal was pinned on Manuel’s Barong Filipino.','assets/images/level6/g4/p041-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g4/p041-1.webp\"]','Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Medal on the Barong Filipino\nManuel is a young artist. He is good in drawing almost anything. Some of his works have been shown at school exhibit. One day, he joined an art contest. His painting won the first prize. He was invited to attend a program where he would be given the prize. One of his friends said, “In this program you will be given a medal. It will be nice to wear coat and tie.” “What? Wear coat and tie for that medal? I’m going to wear a Barong Filipino. The Barong Filipino is just as good as any other clothes for a medal of honor.” The medal was pinned on Manuel’s Barong Filipino.',NULL,10,41,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Medal on the Barong Filipino\nManuel is a young artist. He is good in drawing almost anything. Some of his works have been shown at school exhibit. One day, he joined an art contest. His painting won the first prize. He was invited to attend a program where he would be given the prize. One of his friends said, “In this program you will be given a medal. It will be nice to wear coat and tie.” “What? Wear coat and tie for that medal? I’m going to wear a Barong Filipino. The Barong Filipino is just as good as any other clothes for a medal of honor.” The medal was pinned on Manuel’s Barong Filipino.',1,1,'answer'),
(1094,399,91,'post',1,'Ana’s Pet','open','Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.','Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nAna’s Pet\nMother bought two piglets. She had a pigpen ready for them. “These pigs should be given good care,” she told Ana. “Can you help me take care of them?” “Of course, Mother,” answered Ana. “l’ll help you take care of them.” So Ana became the caretaker of the piglets. She cleaned the pen early in the morning and gave them baths. She fed them swill which she got from their neighbors. Soon the piglets grew big and fat. When they were ready for sale, Ana felt sad. She knew Mother would get a good price for them but she would lose her pet.\n\nWhat did Ana’s Mother buy?\nA. Two piglets\nB. Three piglets\nC. Four piglets\n\nWhere did she keep them?\nA. In their yard\nB. In a pigpen\nC. Under the house\n\nWhom did she ask to help her take care of them?\nA. Ana\nB. her maid\nC. her neighbor\n\nDid Ana like to take care of the pigs?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Perhaps\n\nWhat new job did Ana have?\nA. nurse of the pigs\nB. friend of the pigs\nC. caretaker of the pigs\n\nAt what time of the day did she clean the pigpen?\nA. early in the morning\nB. late in the afternoon\nC. at noon\n\nWho gave baths to the pigs?\nA. Ana herself\nB. Ana’s sister\nC. Ana’s mother\n\nWhere did Ana get the swill for the pigs?\nA. from the market\nB. from a nearby store\nC. from their neighbors\n\nWhat happened with the pigs?\nA. Became sick\nB. Grew big and fat\nC. Did not grow at all\n\nHow did Ana feel when the pigs were ready for sale?\nA. very happy\nB. angry\nC. sad','assets/images/level6/g4/p043-1.w