ut is an interesting legend.\n\nIt happened during the time of the Holy Wars or Crusades when the Christian knights were fighting the Mohammedans to regain the Holy Land.\n\nIn one of the holy wars, a brave knight climbed the walls surrounding the Holy City to open the gates so that the other Christian soldiers could enter. A Moorish soldier saw him and shot him with an arrow. As he fell over the wall, his blood dropped on a shrub which later bore red flowers.\n\nPoinsettias bloom during the __________.\nA. Christmas season\nB. dry season\nC. summer months\n\nPoinsettias have become a _________.\nA. Christmas symbol\nB. bridal shower\nC. national flower\n\nThe poinsettia is _________.\nA. odorous\nB. odorless\nC. fragrant\n\nIt begins to bloom in __________.\nA. December\nB. March\nC. November\n\nThe brilliant red petals are actually __________.\nA. leaves\nB. seeds\nC. stems\n\nThe poinsettia came to have red color during ___________.\nA. World War I\nB. the Holy War\nC. the Japanese-Filipino War\n\nAnother name for those wars was the ___________.\nA. Civil War\nB. World War\nC. Crusades\n\nThe Christians were fighting against the ___________.\n',1,1,'answer'),
(1131,422,94,'post',2,'Exercise 8','open','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe food of fishes is as varied as the food of land animals. Many fishes eat other fish. Some eat only crustaceans such as crabs, shrimps, and sea mantis.\n\nMany other fishes have great strong teeth for crushing the shells of oysters, clams, and barnacles which they feed on. Some kinds of fish eat water plants called “lumot” and “digman”.\n\nYoung fishes and some species of large fishes feed on plankton. Plankton is the name given to microscopic plants and animals that live in rivers, lakes, and seas. Plankton is also called “pasture of the sea”.\n\nWhat is the selection about?\nA. kinds of fish\nB. food of fishes\nC. food value of fish\n\nWhich sentence is true?\nA. Fishes have different kinds of food.\nB. Fishes do not eat other fishes.\nC. Fishes eat only small plants and animals.\n\nWhy is plankton called “pasture of the sea”?\nA. They look like grazing meadows on the land.\nB. They serve as food for people.\nC. They serve as food for many kinds of fish',NULL,'[]','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe food of fishes is as varied as the food of land animals. Many fishes eat other fish. Some eat only crustaceans such as crabs, shrimps, and sea mantis.\n\nMany other fishes have great strong teeth for crushing the shells of oysters, clams, and barnacles which they feed on. Some kinds of fish eat water plants called “lumot” and “digman”.\n\nYoung fishes and some species of large fishes feed on plankton. Plankton is the name given to microscopic plants and animals that live in rivers, lakes, and seas. Plankton is also called “pasture of the sea”.\n\nWhat is the selection about?\nA. kinds of fish\nB. food of fishes\nC. food value of fish\n\nWhich sentence is true?\nA. Fishes have different kinds of food.\nB. Fishes do not eat other fishes.\nC. Fishes eat only small plants and animals.\n\nWhy is plankton called “pasture of the sea”?\nA. They look like grazing meadows on the land.\nB. They serve as food for people.\nC. They serve as food for many kinds of fish',NULL,10,54,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe food of fishes is as varied as the food of land animals. Many fishes eat other fish. Some eat only crustaceans such as crabs, shrimps, and sea mantis.\n\nMany other fishes have great strong teeth for crushing the shells of oysters, clams, and barnacles which they feed on. Some kinds of fish eat water plants called “lumot” and “digman”.\n\nYoung fishes and some species of large fishes feed on plankton. Plankton is the name given to microscopic plants and animals that live in rivers, lakes, and seas. Plankton is also called “pasture of the sea”.\n\nWhat is the selection about?\nA. kinds of fish\nB. food of fishes\nC. food value of fish\n\nWhich sentence is true?\nA. Fishes have different kinds of food.\nB. Fishes do not eat other fishes.\nC. Fishes eat only small plants and animals.\n\nWhy is plankton called “pasture of the sea”?\nA. They look like grazing meadows on the land.\nB. They serve as food for people.\nC. They serve as food for many kinds of fish',1,1,'answer'),
(1132,422,94,'post',3,'A Mother’s Expectation','open','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Mother’s Expectation\nMrs. Cruz was very proud of her son, Juanito. Since Juanito was in Grade One, he had always topped his class. He had been a gold medalist for four years. Now, Mother was expecting another gold medal for Juanito.\n\nA few weeks before the closing of school, Juanito got sick. He missed most of his lessons. Luckily though, he passed all his tests.\n\nOn Awards Day, Mother was waiting to be invited. But she was not.\n\nHow did Mother feel?\nA. embarrassed\nB. surprised\nC. disappointed\n\nWhy was Mother not invited?\nA. Juanito would not receive any medal.\nB. Father would pin the medal on Juanito\nC. Juanito would pin the medal on himself.\n\nDid Juanito pass the tests?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe',NULL,'[]','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Mother’s Expectation\nMrs. Cruz was very proud of her son, Juanito. Since Juanito was in Grade One, he had always topped his class. He had been a gold medalist for four years. Now, Mother was expecting another gold medal for Juanito.\n\nA few weeks before the closing of school, Juanito got sick. He missed most of his lessons. Luckily though, he passed all his tests.\n\nOn Awards Day, Mother was waiting to be invited. But she was not.\n\nHow did Mother feel?\nA. embarrassed\nB. surprised\nC. disappointed\n\nWhy was Mother not invited?\nA. Juanito would not receive any medal.\nB. Father would pin the medal on Juanito\nC. Juanito would pin the medal on himself.\n\nDid Juanito pass the tests?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe',NULL,10,55,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Mother’s Expectation\nMrs. Cruz was very proud of her son, Juanito. Since Juanito was in Grade One, he had always topped his class. He had been a gold medalist for four years. Now, Mother was expecting another gold medal for Juanito.\n\nA few weeks before the closing of school, Juanito got sick. He missed most of his lessons. Luckily though, he passed all his tests.\n\nOn Awards Day, Mother was waiting to be invited. But she was not.\n\nHow did Mother feel?\nA. embarrassed\nB. surprised\nC. disappointed\n\nWhy was Mother not invited?\nA. Juanito would not receive any medal.\nB. Father would pin the medal on Juanito\nC. Juanito would pin the medal on himself.\n\nDid Juanito pass the tests?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe',1,1,'answer'),
(1133,422,94,'post',4,'A Surprise for Rolando','open','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Surprise for Rolando\nIt was Wednesday morning, Mother prepared some nice dishes while Ariel cleaned the house. Armin arranged the furniture and Cecille arranged the curtains. They helped each other cheerfully. They wanted to surprise their youngest brother, Rolando, who’s celebrating his eleventh birthday.\n\nIn the afternoon, they all waited at the porch. Soon Rolando’s friends started to arrive.\n\nWrite the name and age of the boy who was celebrating his birthday.\n\nBox the name of the person who prepared the dishes.\n\nWrite the number of children in the family and their names.\n\nIs Rolando the eldest child? If not, write the opposite word of eldest.\n\nWrite the part of the house where they waited.',NULL,'[]','Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Surprise for Rolando\nIt was Wednesday morning, Mother prepared some nice dishes while Ariel cleaned the house. Armin arranged the furniture and Cecille arranged the curtains. They helped each other cheerfully. They wanted to surprise their youngest brother, Rolando, who’s celebrating his eleventh birthday.\n\nIn the afternoon, they all waited at the porch. Soon Rolando’s friends started to arrive.\n\nWrite the name and age of the boy who was celebrating his birthday.\n\nBox the name of the person who prepared the dishes.\n\nWrite the number of children in the family and their names.\n\nIs Rolando the eldest child? If not, write the opposite word of eldest.\n\nWrite the part of the house where they waited.',NULL,10,56,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Surprise for Rolando\nIt was Wednesday morning, Mother prepared some nice dishes while Ariel cleaned the house. Armin arranged the furniture and Cecille arranged the curtains. They helped each other cheerfully. They wanted to surprise their youngest brother, Rolando, who’s celebrating his eleventh birthday.\n\nIn the afternoon, they all waited at the porch. Soon Rolando’s friends started to arrive.\n\nWrite the name and age of the boy who was celebrating his birthday.\n\nBox the name of the person who prepared the dishes.\n\nWrite the number of children in the family and their names.\n\nIs Rolando the eldest child? If not, write the opposite word of eldest.\n\nWrite the part of the house where they waited.',1,1,'answer'),
(1134,422,94,'post',5,'The Octopus','open','Copy the letter of the correct answer.','Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nThe Octopus\nThe octopus is one of the strangest-looking animals that ever lived. It has two large eyes and snake-like arms that made it frightful to look at. Its name, octopus, means “eight” arms. These eight powerful arms, called tentacles are joined around its mouth.\n\nThis sea animal is also called “devil fish”. However, it is not a fish. The sea animal belongs to a class called mollusks. It feeds on other sea animals like crabs, oysters and shrimps. On the under sides of its tentacles are suckers. Using these suckers, the octopus grasps other sea animals and takes them to the mouth.\n\nOctopuses are different from each other in size. Some are just as small as a one-peso coin. Others are as large as a beach umbrella.\n\nBeneath the head of the octopus is a funnel. This funnel is very useful to the octopus. When attacked by other animals, this funnel gives off a black liquid which hides the octopus.\n\nThe octopus is an animal that lives _________________\nA. in the air\nB. on land\nC. in the water\n\nThe octopus is ______________________________.\nA. funny\nB. nice-looking\nC. strange-looking\n\nThe arms of the octopus are like _________________________.\nA. snakes\nB. stairs\nC. stems\n\nThe word octopus means ________________________.\nA. five arms\nB. eight arms\nC. ten arms\n\nAnother name for it is ___________________________________.\nA. angel fish\nB. devilfish\nC. snake fish',NULL,'[]','Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nThe Octopus\nThe octopus is one of the strangest-looking animals that ever lived. It has two large eyes and snake-like arms that made it frightful to look at. Its name, octopus, means “eight” arms. These eight powerful arms, called tentacles are joined around its mouth.\n\nThis sea animal is also called “devil fish”. However, it is not a fish. The sea animal belongs to a class called mollusks. It feeds on other sea animals like crabs, oysters and shrimps. On the under sides of its tentacles are suckers. Using these suckers, the octopus grasps other sea animals and takes them to the mouth.\n\nOctopuses are different from each other in size. Some are just as small as a one-peso coin. Others are as large as a beach umbrella.\n\nBeneath the head of the octopus is a funnel. This funnel is very useful to the octopus. When attacked by other animals, this funnel gives off a black liquid which hides the octopus.\n\nThe octopus is an animal that lives _________________\nA. in the air\nB. on land\nC. in the water\n\nThe octopus is ______________________________.\nA. funny\nB. nice-looking\nC. strange-looking\n\nThe arms of the octopus are like _________________________.\nA. snakes\nB. stairs\nC. stems\n\nThe word octopus means ________________________.\nA. five arms\nB. eight arms\nC. ten arms\n\nAnother name for it is ___________________________________.\nA. angel fish\nB. devilfish\nC. snake fish',NULL,10,57,'Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nThe Octopus\nThe octopus is one of the strangest-looking animals that ever lived. It has two large eyes and snake-like arms that made it frightful to look at. Its name, octopus, means “eight” arms. These eight powerful arms, called tentacles are joined around its mouth.\n\nThis sea animal is also called “devil fish”. However, it is not a fish. The sea animal belongs to a class called mollusks. It feeds on other sea animals like crabs, oysters and shrimps. On the under sides of its tentacles are suckers. Using these suckers, the octopus grasps other sea animals and takes them to the mouth.\n\nOctopuses are different from each other in size. Some are just as small as a one-peso coin. Others are as large as a beach umbrella.\n\nBeneath the head of the octopus is a funnel. This funnel is very useful to the octopus. When attacked by other animals, this funnel gives off a black liquid which hides the octopus.\n\nThe octopus is an animal that lives _________________\nA. in the air\nB. on land\nC. in the water\n\nThe octopus is ______________________________.\nA. funny\nB. nice-looking\nC. strange-looking\n\nThe arms of the octopus are like _________________________.\nA. snakes\nB. stairs\nC. stems\n\nThe word octopus means ________________________.\nA. five arms\nB. eight arms\nC. ten arms\n\nAnother name for it is ___________________________________.\nA. angel fish\nB. devilfish\nC. snake fish',1,1,'answer'),
(1135,423,95,'pre',1,'The Face in the Pool','open','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Face in the Pool\nNarcissus was out of breath from running when he came to a clear pool. He stopped to drink. Just as he was about to sip the cool water, he saw a face in the pool. It was handsome and he was attracted to it.\n\n“Who are you?” he asked.\n\n“Who are you?” echoed someone.\n\n“Why are you mocking me?” asked Narcissus.\n\n“Mocking me,” said the voice.\n\nNarcissus was puzzled. He wanted to talk with the face in the pool but it would not answer. He tried to catch it but to no avail. At last, he lost hope and plunged into the pool hoping to see the owner of the face.\n\nWhy was Narcissus out of breath?\nA. He was running.\nB. He was Jumping.\nC. He was skipping.\n\nWhich of these sentences is true?\nA. Narcissus came to a clear lake.\nB. Narcissus came to a clear pool.\nC. Narcissus came to a clear path.\n\nHe stopped to ____.\nA. drink\nB. swim\nC. bathe\n\nHe saw something which ____.\nA. attracted him\nB. frightened him\nC. drove him away\n\nWhat did he see in the pool?\nA. a boat\nB. a face\nC. a flower','assets/images/level6/g6/p009-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g6/p009-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Face in the Pool\nNarcissus was out of breath from running when he came to a clear pool. He stopped to drink. Just as he was about to sip the cool water, he saw a face in the pool. It was handsome and he was attracted to it.\n\n“Who are you?” he asked.\n\n“Who are you?” echoed someone.\n\n“Why are you mocking me?” asked Narcissus.\n\n“Mocking me,” said the voice.\n\nNarcissus was puzzled. He wanted to talk with the face in the pool but it would not answer. He tried to catch it but to no avail. At last, he lost hope and plunged into the pool hoping to see the owner of the face.\n\nWhy was Narcissus out of breath?\nA. He was running.\nB. He was Jumping.\nC. He was skipping.\n\nWhich of these sentences is true?\nA. Narcissus came to a clear lake.\nB. Narcissus came to a clear pool.\nC. Narcissus came to a clear path.\n\nHe stopped to ____.\nA. drink\nB. swim\nC. bathe\n\nHe saw something which ____.\nA. attracted him\nB. frightened him\nC. drove him away\n\nWhat did he see in the pool?\nA. a boat\nB. a face\nC. a flower',NULL,10,9,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Face in the Pool\nNarcissus was out of breath from running when he came to a clear pool. He stopped to drink. Just as he was about to sip the cool water, he saw a face in the pool. It was handsome and he was attracted to it.\n\n“Who are you?” he asked.\n\n“Who are you?” echoed someone.\n\n“Why are you mocking me?” asked Narcissus.\n\n“Mocking me,” said the voice.\n\nNarcissus was puzzled. He wanted to talk with the face in the pool but it would not answer. He tried to catch it but to no avail. At last, he lost hope and plunged into the pool hoping to see the owner of the face.\n\nWhy was Narcissus out of breath?\nA. He was running.\nB. He was Jumping.\nC. He was skipping.\n\nWhich of these sentences is true?\nA. Narcissus came to a clear lake.\nB. Narcissus came to a clear pool.\nC. Narcissus came to a clear path.\n\nHe stopped to ____.\nA. drink\nB. swim\nC. bathe\n\nHe saw something which ____.\nA. attracted him\nB. frightened him\nC. drove him away\n\nWhat did he see in the pool?\nA. a boat\nB. a face\nC. a flower',1,1,'answer'),
(1136,423,95,'pre',2,'Jay’s Curiosity','open','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nJay’s Curiosity\n“Is Father going hunting?” Jay asked his mother when he saw his father get his gun and go out of the house.\n\n“Didn’t you listen to the radio, Jay?” asked her mother. “Every citizen who owns a gun without license is asked to surrender it to the government.”\n\n“Will Father be punished for owning a gun?” asked Jay.\n\n“No, Jay,” answered Mother. “No questions will be asked to citizens who surrender their guns. They will also be issued receipts.”\n\n“That is a very good move to prevent crimes,” said Jay. “Ordinary citizens like us will have nothing to be afraid of anymore.”\n\n“You’re right, Jay,” agreed Mother. “There is peace around us now.”\n\nWhich word best describe what Mother thinks about our present society?\nA. fearful\nB. peaceful\nC. same as before\n\nWhich of these statements is true?\nA. Citizen fear for their lives.\nB. Citizen cannot rest easily.\nC. Citizen are not afraid anymore.\n\nWhat is expected of every citizen who possesses firearms without license?\nA. to keep it\nB. to fight with it\nC. to surrender it','assets/images/level6/g6/p011-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g6/p011-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nJay’s Curiosity\n“Is Father going hunting?” Jay asked his mother when he saw his father get his gun and go out of the house.\n\n“Didn’t you listen to the radio, Jay?” asked her mother. “Every citizen who owns a gun without license is asked to surrender it to the government.”\n\n“Will Father be punished for owning a gun?” asked Jay.\n\n“No, Jay,” answered Mother. “No questions will be asked to citizens who surrender their guns. They will also be issued receipts.”\n\n“That is a very good move to prevent crimes,” said Jay. “Ordinary citizens like us will have nothing to be afraid of anymore.”\n\n“You’re right, Jay,” agreed Mother. “There is peace around us now.”\n\nWhich word best describe what Mother thinks about our present society?\nA. fearful\nB. peaceful\nC. same as before\n\nWhich of these statements is true?\nA. Citizen fear for their lives.\nB. Citizen cannot rest easily.\nC. Citizen are not afraid anymore.\n\nWhat is expected of every citizen who possesses firearms without license?\nA. to keep it\nB. to fight with it\nC. to surrender it',NULL,10,11,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nJay’s Curiosity\n“Is Father going hunting?” Jay asked his mother when he saw his father get his gun and go out of the house.\n\n“Didn’t you listen to the radio, Jay?” asked her mother. “Every citizen who owns a gun without license is asked to surrender it to the government.”\n\n“Will Father be punished for owning a gun?” asked Jay.\n\n“No, Jay,” answered Mother. “No questions will be asked to citizens who surrender their guns. They will also be issued receipts.”\n\n“That is a very good move to prevent crimes,” said Jay. “Ordinary citizens like us will have nothing to be afraid of anymore.”\n\n“You’re right, Jay,” agreed Mother. “There is peace around us now.”\n\nWhich word best describe what Mother thinks about our present society?\nA. fearful\nB. peaceful\nC. same as before\n\nWhich of these statements is true?\nA. Citizen fear for their lives.\nB. Citizen cannot rest easily.\nC. Citizen are not afraid anymore.\n\nWhat is expected of every citizen who possesses firearms without license?\nA. to keep it\nB. to fight with it\nC. to surrender it',1,1,'answer'),
(1137,423,95,'pre',3,'Rice','open','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRice\nRice is a cereal native in Asia and Africa. It is the staple food of more than half of the people in the world.\n\nRice is a plant that grows the whole year round. It grows from two to four feet tall. It has round jointed stems, long-pointed leaves and seeds borne in dense heads on separate stalks.\n\nBrown rice with bran layer is rice in vitamin B, a cure for beriberi. Polished white rice is rich in starch but low in protein, fat, and vitamin B. In the Orient, the fine soft straw of the rice plant is used for weaving hats and slippers. Powdered grain is used for making laundry starch and distilled liquor.\n\nWhat would happen if rice would suddenly disappear?\nA. People would rejoice.\nB. People would not mind.\nC. Many people would starve.\n\nWhat will happen to a person with beriberi if he eats brown rice with the bran layer?\nA. He will get weaker.\nB. He will die of beriberi.\nC. He will be cured of beriberi.\n\nWhich do you think is more nutritious, brown rice, or white polished rice?\nA. brown rice\nB. neither of the two\nC. white polished ric','assets/images/level6/g6/p012-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g6/p012-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRice\nRice is a cereal native in Asia and Africa. It is the staple food of more than half of the people in the world.\n\nRice is a plant that grows the whole year round. It grows from two to four feet tall. It has round jointed stems, long-pointed leaves and seeds borne in dense heads on separate stalks.\n\nBrown rice with bran layer is rice in vitamin B, a cure for beriberi. Polished white rice is rich in starch but low in protein, fat, and vitamin B. In the Orient, the fine soft straw of the rice plant is used for weaving hats and slippers. Powdered grain is used for making laundry starch and distilled liquor.\n\nWhat would happen if rice would suddenly disappear?\nA. People would rejoice.\nB. People would not mind.\nC. Many people would starve.\n\nWhat will happen to a person with beriberi if he eats brown rice with the bran layer?\nA. He will get weaker.\nB. He will die of beriberi.\nC. He will be cured of beriberi.\n\nWhich do you think is more nutritious, brown rice, or white polished rice?\nA. brown rice\nB. neither of the two\nC. white polished ric',NULL,10,12,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRice\nRice is a cereal native in Asia and Africa. It is the staple food of more than half of the people in the world.\n\nRice is a plant that grows the whole year round. It grows from two to four feet tall. It has round jointed stems, long-pointed leaves and seeds borne in dense heads on separate stalks.\n\nBrown rice with bran layer is rice in vitamin B, a cure for beriberi. Polished white rice is rich in starch but low in protein, fat, and vitamin B. In the Orient, the fine soft straw of the rice plant is used for weaving hats and slippers. Powdered grain is used for making laundry starch and distilled liquor.\n\nWhat would happen if rice would suddenly disappear?\nA. People would rejoice.\nB. People would not mind.\nC. Many people would starve.\n\nWhat will happen to a person with beriberi if he eats brown rice with the bran layer?\nA. He will get weaker.\nB. He will die of beriberi.\nC. He will be cured of beriberi.\n\nWhich do you think is more nutritious, brown rice, or white polished rice?\nA. brown rice\nB. neither of the two\nC. white polished ric',1,1,'answer'),
(1138,423,95,'pre',4,'Wally’s Kite','open','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nWally’s Kite\nIt was Saturday morning and Wally was getting ready to fly his kite. Soon his friends Dennis and Berto came along. Together they went to the field. They tossed their kites up to the air. The kites flew up into the sky until they look like birds. The boys had a fine time. Suddenly the kite flew away up to the sky. He ran fast to follow his kite. Dennis ran to follow Wally. At last Wally’s kite landed on a treetop. Wally and Dennis reached the tree where the kite landed. Wally climbed the tree and brought his kite down, but it could not fly anymore. Its wings and tail were broken.\n\nWhat day was it?\n\nWrite the words that tell who came along while Wally was flying his kite.\n\nWhich of these phrases tell what the boys had. Copy the phrase.\nA. had a fine time\nB. had a bad time\nC. had an accident\n\nDraw the things that looked like birds.\n\nWhy Wally’s kite could not fly?\n\nIf you have a kite what design do you like to have and why?\n\nWhat object you can compare with a kite? Explain why?','assets/images/level6/g6/p013-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g6/p013-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nWally’s Kite\nIt was Saturday morning and Wally was getting ready to fly his kite. Soon his friends Dennis and Berto came along. Together they went to the field. They tossed their kites up to the air. The kites flew up into the sky until they look like birds. The boys had a fine time. Suddenly the kite flew away up to the sky. He ran fast to follow his kite. Dennis ran to follow Wally. At last Wally’s kite landed on a treetop. Wally and Dennis reached the tree where the kite landed. Wally climbed the tree and brought his kite down, but it could not fly anymore. Its wings and tail were broken.\n\nWhat day was it?\n\nWrite the words that tell who came along while Wally was flying his kite.\n\nWhich of these phrases tell what the boys had. Copy the phrase.\nA. had a fine time\nB. had a bad time\nC. had an accident\n\nDraw the things that looked like birds.\n\nWhy Wally’s kite could not fly?\n\nIf you have a kite what design do you like to have and why?\n\nWhat object you can compare with a kite? Explain why?',NULL,10,13,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nWally’s Kite\nIt was Saturday morning and Wally was getting ready to fly his kite. Soon his friends Dennis and Berto came along. Together they went to the field. They tossed their kites up to the air. The kites flew up into the sky until they look like birds. The boys had a fine time. Suddenly the kite flew away up to the sky. He ran fast to follow his kite. Dennis ran to follow Wally. At last Wally’s kite landed on a treetop. Wally and Dennis reached the tree where the kite landed. Wally climbed the tree and brought his kite down, but it could not fly anymore. Its wings and tail were broken.\n\nWhat day was it?\n\nWrite the words that tell who came along while Wally was flying his kite.\n\nWhich of these phrases tell what the boys had. Copy the phrase.\nA. had a fine time\nB. had a bad time\nC. had an accident\n\nDraw the things that looked like birds.\n\nWhy Wally’s kite could not fly?\n\nIf you have a kite what design do you like to have and why?\n\nWhat object you can compare with a kite? Explain why?',1,1,'answer'),
(1139,423,95,'pre',5,'A Fairy Tale from Japan','open','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nA Fairy Tale from Japan\nOnce upon a time there lived an old couple in one small village in Japan. They had no children so they went from temple to temple to offer prayers to their gods so that they might be blessed with a child. But their prayers were in vain.\n\nOne morning, after working in the garden, the couple sat down under a peach tree to rest. The husband was telling his wife how much he wanted to have a son to help him in his garden when a very big peach fell on his wife’s lap. She got the fruit and put it in her pocket.\n\nAfter a while, they went home for breakfast. As the husband was about to cut the peach, he heard a cry. “Let me out!” said the voice. When the wife looked into the fruit, she saw a child as big as her little finger. The woman lifted the child and kissed him.\n\n“I heard your prayers up there,” the child said, “So I came to be your son.”\n\n“We will love you very much,” the couple said.\n\nThe couple called the tiny child Peaching. They devoted their time in making him happy. They treated him like a real son. But Peaching did not grow big like other children. Yet he could help his father plow the field by sitting on the right ear of the plow horse and directing him where to go. He could also go marketing for his mother by carrying the coins in a small bag. He would sit in the market basket carried by a dog.\n\nHe always did his marketing early to avoid curios people who might notice him. The couple found a good helper in their tiny son. They loved him more each day.\n\nOne night as Peaching’s parents were sleeping, they both have similar dreams. They dreamed that if they would dip their tiny son into the brook that was in their yard, he would grow up like other children.\n\nThe next morning the happy parents told each other about their dreams They tiptoed to their tiny son’s room. The husband carried the bed where their tiny son was sleeping soundly. He immersed the bed with the tiny boy into the brook.\n\nThey were both surprised when the bed seemed to have disappeared from their hands. They were so frightened that they began looking for it among the waterlilies near the bank of the brook.\n\nThey heard a merry laughter behind them. When they turned, the couple saw a young man holding the tiny bed in his hands.\n\n“Father, Mother, I am Peaching,” he said as he rushed and embraced the couple.\n\n“A wicked fairy bewitched me and placed me inside a peach,” Peaching explained. “Because you loved me and baptized me in this brook, the enchantment was broken. Now I will really be a son to you.”\n\nThe more he became a great help to the couple. He loved his foster parents dearly. Not long after, Peaching met a lass from another barrio. He fell in love with her and they were married.\n\nThe old couple lived happily with their dear son and his family ever after.\n\nThe Japanese couple prayed for a ____.\nA. daughter\nB. son\nC. child\n\nThey sat down to rest under a ____.\nA. peach tree\nB. orange tree\nC. mango tree\n\nA big peach fell on the woman’s ____.\nA. shoulder\nB. head\nC. lap\n\nThe child they saw was no bigger than the couple’s little ____.\nA. head\nB. toes\nC. fingers\n\nThey called him ____.\nA. Peaching\nB. Orangeling\nC. Ting-a-ling\n\nThe tiny boy went marketing early to avoid ____.\nA. cars\nB. curious people\nC. his parents\n\nOne night, the couple dreamed that they should dip their tiny son into a ______.\nA. river\nB. well\nC. brook\n\nThe tiny boy was bewitched by a ____.\nA. fairy\nB. giant\nC. kapre\n\nThe enchantment was broken because the couple the boy ____.\nA. hated\nB. loved\nC. killed\n\nThe old couple lived with their dear son ____.\nA. happily\nB. sadly\nC. poorly.','assets/images/level6/g6/p014-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g6/p014-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nA Fairy Tale from Japan\nOnce upon a time there lived an old couple in one small village in Japan. They had no children so they went from temple to temple to offer prayers to their gods so that they might be blessed with a child. But their prayers were in vain.\n\nOne morning, after working in the garden, the couple sat down under a peach tree to rest. The husband was telling his wife how much he wanted to have a son to help him in his garden when a very big peach fell on his wife’s lap. She got the fruit and put it in her pocket.\n\nAfter a while, they went home for breakfast. As the husband was about to cut the peach, he heard a cry. “Let me out!” said the voice. When the wife looked into the fruit, she saw a child as big as her little finger. The woman lifted the child and kissed him.\n\n“I heard your prayers up there,” the child said, “So I came to be your son.”\n\n“We will love you very much,” the couple said.\n\nThe couple called the tiny child Peaching. They devoted their time in making him happy. They treated him like a real son. But Peaching did not grow big like other children. Yet he could help his father plow the field by sitting on the right ear of the plow horse and directing him where to go. He could also go marketing for his mother by carrying the coins in a small bag. He would sit in the market basket carried by a dog.\n\nHe always did his marketing early to avoid curios people who might notice him. The couple found a good helper in their tiny son. They loved him more each day.\n\nOne night as Peaching’s parents were sleeping, they both have similar dreams. They dreamed that if they would dip their tiny son into the brook that was in their yard, he would grow up like other children.\n\nThe next morning the happy parents told each other about their dreams They tiptoed to their tiny son’s room. The husband carried the bed where their tiny son was sleeping soundly. He immersed the bed with the tiny boy into the brook.\n\nThey were both surprised when the bed seemed to have disappeared from their hands. They were so frightened that they began looking for it among the waterlilies near the bank of the brook.\n\nThey heard a merry laughter behind them. When they turned, the couple saw a young man holding the tiny bed in his hands.\n\n“Father, Mother, I am Peaching,” he said as he rushed and embraced the couple.\n\n“A wicked fairy bewitched me and placed me inside a peach,” Peaching explained. “Because you loved me and baptized me in this brook, the enchantment was broken. Now I will really be a son to you.”\n\nThe more he became a great help to the couple. He loved his foster parents dearly. Not long after, Peaching met a lass from another barrio. He fell in love with her and they were married.\n\nThe old couple lived happily with their dear son and his family ever after.\n\nThe Japanese couple prayed for a ____.\nA. daughter\nB. son\nC. child\n\nThey sat down to rest under a ____.\nA. peach tree\nB. orange tree\nC. ma',NULL,10,14,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nA Fairy Tale from Japan\nOnce upon a time there lived an old couple in one small village in Japan. They had no children so they went from temple to temple to offer prayers to their gods so that they might be blessed with a child. But their prayers were in vain.\n\nOne morning, after working in the garden, the couple sat down under a peach tree to rest. The husband was telling his wife how much he wanted to have a son to help him in his garden when a very big peach fell on his wife’s lap. She got the fruit and put it in her pocket.\n\nAfter a while, they went home for breakfast. As the husband was about to cut the peach, he heard a cry. “Let me out!” said the voice. When the wife looked into the fruit, she saw a child as big as her little finger. The woman lifted the child and kissed him.\n\n“I heard your prayers up there,” the child said, “So I came to be your son.”\n\n“We will love you very much,” the couple said.\n\nThe couple called the tiny child Peaching. They devoted their time in making him happy. They treated him like a real son. But Peaching did not grow big like other children. Yet he could help his father plow the field by sitting on the right ear of the plow horse and directing him where to go. He could also go marketing for his mother by carrying the coins in a small bag. He would sit in the market basket carried by a dog.\n\nHe always did his marketing early to avoid curios people who might notice him. The couple found',1,1,'answer'),
(1140,424,NULL,'learn',1,'Rules to Follow','open','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRules to Follow\nThe Grade Six pupils decided to make their room clean, orderly and beautiful. Here are some of the rules everyone needs to follow: Wipe your shoes on the doormat before entering the room.\n\nPut the wastepaper, fruit peelings and candy wrappers in the wastebasket.\n\nKeep the walls, desks and tables free from pencil and chalk marks and scratches.\n\nPut the cleaning materials in a box.\n\nArrange the furniture properly.\n\nPut away your pencils, books, notebooks after using them.\n\nHang umbrellas and raincoats in one corner of the room.\n\nKeep books clean and well- covered.\n\nWhat did the pupils decide to do?\nA. make their room clean, orderly and beautiful\nB. make their homes clean, orderly and beautiful\nC. make their school surroundings clean, orderly and beautiful\n\nWhat did they agreed upon?\nA. to form a society\nB. to follow the rules\nC. to work on the school ground\n\nWhat will they do with their shoes?\nA. wipe on the doormat\nB. leave on the doormat\nC. put under the doormat\n\nFrom what will they keep the furniture free?\nA. books\nB. cover\nC. scratches\n\nWhere should the cleaning materials be kept?\nA. box\nB. drawer\nC. bookcase\n\nHow should the furniture be arranged?\nA. properly\nB. carelessly\nC. artistically\n\nWhere should one keep his pencils, books and notebooks after using them?\nA. in this bag\nB. on the desk\nC. on the teacher’s table\n\nWhere should wastepaper be thrown?\nA. in the desk\nB. in the drawer\nC. in the wastebasket\n\nWhere should one hang raincoats and umbrellas?\nA. on the desk\nB. in the cabinet\nC. on one corner of the room\n\nWhat should be kept clean and well-covered?\nA. desks\nB. books\nC. tables','assets/images/level6/g6/p018-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g6/p018-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRules to Follow\nThe Grade Six pupils decided to make their room clean, orderly and beautiful. Here are some of the rules everyone needs to follow: Wipe your shoes on the doormat before entering the room.\n\nPut the wastepaper, fruit peelings and candy wrappers in the wastebasket.\n\nKeep the walls, desks and tables free from pencil and chalk marks and scratches.\n\nPut the cleaning materials in a box.\n\nArrange the furniture properly.\n\nPut away your pencils, books, notebooks after using them.\n\nHang umbrellas and raincoats in one corner of the room.\n\nKeep books clean and well- covered.\n\nWhat did the pupils decide to do?\nA. make their room clean, orderly and beautiful\nB. make their homes clean, orderly and beautiful\nC. make their school surroundings clean, orderly and beautiful\n\nWhat did they agreed upon?\nA. to form a society\nB. to follow the rules\nC. to work on the school ground\n\nWhat will they do with their shoes?\nA. wipe on the doormat\nB. leave on the doormat\nC. put under the doormat\n\nFrom what will they keep the furniture free?\nA. books\nB. cover\nC. scratches\n\nWhere should the cleaning materials be kept?\nA. box\nB. drawer\nC. bookcase\n\nHow should the furniture be arranged?\nA. properly\nB. carelessly\nC. artistically\n\nWhere should one keep his pencils, books and notebooks after using them?\nA. in this bag\nB. on the desk\nC. on the teacher’s table\n\nWhere should wastepaper be thrown?\nA. in the desk\nB. in the drawer\nC. in the wastebasket\n\nWhere should one hang raincoats and umbrellas?\nA. on the desk\nB. in the cabinet\nC. on one corner of the room\n\nWhat should be kept clean and well-covered?\nA. desks\nB. books\nC. tables',NULL,10,18,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRules to Follow\nThe Grade Six pupils decided to make their room clean, orderly and beautiful. Here are some of the rules everyone needs to follow: Wipe your shoes on the doormat before entering the room.\n\nPut the wastepaper, fruit peelings and candy wrappers in the wastebasket.\n\nKeep the walls, desks and tables free from pencil and chalk marks and scratches.\n\nPut the cleaning materials in a box.\n\nArrange the furniture properly.\n\nPut away your pencils, books, notebooks after using them.\n\nHang umbrellas and raincoats in one corner of the room.\n\nKeep books clean and well- covered.\n\nWhat did the pupils decide to do?\nA. make their room clean, orderly and beautiful\nB. make their homes clean, orderly and beautiful\nC. make their school surroundings clean, orderly and beautiful\n\nWhat did they agreed upon?\nA. to form a society\nB. to follow the rules\nC. to work on the school ground\n\nWhat will they do with their shoes?\nA. wipe on the doormat\nB. leave on the doormat\nC. put under the doormat\n\nFrom what will they keep the furniture free?\nA. books\nB. cover\nC. scratches\n\nWhere should the cleaning materials be kept?\nA. box\nB. drawer\nC. bookcase\n\nHow should the furniture be arranged?\nA. properly\nB. carelessly\nC. artistically\n\nWhere should one keep his pencils, books and notebooks after using them?\nA. in this bag\nB. on the desk\nC. on the teacher’s table\n\nWhere should wastepaper be thrown?\nA. in the desk\nB. in the drawer\nC. in the wastebask',1,1,'answer'),
(1141,425,NULL,'learn',1,'Daphne','open','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nDaphne\nDaphne was a beautiful nymph. The sun god, Apollo, fell in love with her but she rejected him. Once, Apollo saw here and pursued her. Daphne sought shelter behind a big tree. She was out of breath from running. Now, as she leaned on the tree, she looked back with threw herself upon her knees and prayed to be saved from her pursuer. She rose with outstretched arms. Then her fingers fluttered gently, as if blown by a gentle breeze. Little by little she was changed from a beautiful nymph to a laurel tree. Slowly she broke into a smile. Now, she was safe. Apollo could not carry her away anymore.\n\nDaphne sought shelter behind ____.\nA. a big tree\nB. a laurel tree\nC. a large tree\n\nAs she leaned on the tree, she looked back with ____.\nA. round eyes\nB. smiling eyes\nC. frightened eyes\n\nShe was out of breath from ____.\nA. talking\nB. running\nC. laughing\n\nShe prayed to be saved from her ____.\nA. father\nB. pursuer\nC. husband\n\nSlowly she stretched out her ____.\nA. feet\nB. arms\nC. hands\n\nShe threw herself upon her ____.\nA. bed\nB. knees\nC. pillow\n\nShe was changed from a beautiful nymph into ____.\nA. an oak tree\nB. a narra tree\nC. a laurel tree\n\nHer fingers fluttered ____.\nA. slowly\nB. gently\nC. rapidly\n\nShe felt safe, after she became a ____.\nA. tree\nB. nymph\nC. goddess\n\nShe could not be carried away anymore by ____.\nA. Theus\nB. Midas\nC. Apollo','assets/images/level6/g6/p020-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level6/g6/p020-1.webp\"]','Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nDaphne\nDaphne was a beautiful nymph. The sun god, Apollo, fell in love with her but she rejected him. Once, Apollo saw here and pursued her. Daphne sought shelter behind a big tree. She was out of breath from running. Now, as she leaned on the tree, she looked back with threw herself upon