(1289,569,NULL,'learn',1,'Writing a News Story','open','Instruction/s: Write a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below.','Instruction/s: Write a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below.\n\nThe Structure\nTHE LEAD. Who did what; or What happened? Perhaps When? and Where? might be in this paragraph.\n2ND PARAGRAPH In many cases this second paragraph will answer the questions Why? or How?\n3RD PARAGRAPH This is where a quote would often appear. This is viewed by most reporters as essential to keep the story lively.\n4TH PARAGRAPH Why are you telling me this/what are the implications of this? (significance)\n5TH PARAGRAPH What will other people think about this? (Moving the story on, giving it a human context)\n6TH PARAGRAPH How did the event continue? (Important background)\n\nThe Lead\n\n2nd Paragraph\n\n3rd Paragraph\n\n4th Paragraph\n\n5th Paragraph\n\n6th Paragraph',NULL,'[]','Instruction/s: Write a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below.\n\nThe Structure\nTHE LEAD. Who did what; or What happened? Perhaps When? and Where? might be in this paragraph.\n2ND PARAGRAPH In many cases this second paragraph will answer the questions Why? or How?\n3RD PARAGRAPH This is where a quote would often appear. This is viewed by most reporters as essential to keep the story lively.\n4TH PARAGRAPH Why are you telling me this/what are the implications of this? (significance)\n5TH PARAGRAPH What will other people think about this? (Moving the story on, giving it a human context)\n6TH PARAGRAPH How did the event continue? (Important background)\n\nThe Lead\n\n2nd Paragraph\n\n3rd Paragraph\n\n4th Paragraph\n\n5th Paragraph\n\n6th Paragraph',NULL,10,13,'Instruction/s: Write a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below.\n\nThe Structure\nTHE LEAD. Who did what; or What happened? Perhaps When? and Where? might be in this paragraph.\n2ND PARAGRAPH In many cases this second paragraph will answer the questions Why? or How?\n3RD PARAGRAPH This is where a quote would often appear. This is viewed by most reporters as essential to keep the story lively.\n4TH PARAGRAPH Why are you telling me this/what are the implications of this? (significance)\n5TH PARAGRAPH What will other people think about this? (Moving the story on, giving it a human context)\n6TH PARAGRAPH How did the event continue? (Important background)\n\nThe Lead\n\n2nd Paragraph\n\n3rd Paragraph\n\n4th Paragraph\n\n5th Paragraph\n\n6th Paragraph',1,1,'answer'),
(1290,570,NULL,'learn',1,'Do What Is Asked','open','Direction: Do the following tasks:','Direction: Do the following tasks:\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Feature Writing\nFeature writing is an art that combines factual reporting with creative storytelling to produce an engaging and informative piece. By following these guidelines, writers can craft stories that resonate with their audience, providing depth and insight into a wide range of topics.\n\nKey Components\n1. Headline: Captivate readers and summarizes the essence of the story.\n2. Lead: An opening paragraph that hooks the reader, often with an anecdote, striking fact, or question.\n3. Nut Graf: A paragraph that outlines the central theme or purpose of the story, providing a roadmap for what follows.\n4. Body:\n› Background Information: Provides Essential details that set the scene or give context.\n› Development: Present the story’s main content, including arguments, descriptions, and narratives.\n› Supporting Elements: Quotes, statistics, anecdotes, and examples that enrich the narrative.\n5. Conclusion: Wraps up the story, often tying back to the lead or looking forward to the future.\n6. Call to Action: (Optional) Directs readers toward further engagement or exploration of the topic.\n\nEnumerate the elements of a feature article. Describe each element based on your understanding.\n\nAfter enumerating the elements, write a feature article on your English notebook. Choose one that interests you the most from the given topics below.\na. A day at a beach\nb. A Mother’s Day Celebration\nc. A Surprise for Father\nd. Life While staying at Home e. Rizal Our National Hero\n\nRecall an unforgettable experience travelling out of town or abroad. Where was it, and why did you consider the experience as memorable? Write a feature article that observes the five elements f feature writing. The final output should be written on one whole intermediate paper.',NULL,'[]','Direction: Do the following tasks:\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Feature Writing\nFeature writing is an art that combines factual reporting with creative storytelling to produce an engaging and informative piece. By following these guidelines, writers can craft stories that resonate with their audience, providing depth and insight into a wide range of topics.\n\nKey Components\n1. Headline: Captivate readers and summarizes the essence of the story.\n2. Lead: An opening paragraph that hooks the reader, often with an anecdote, striking fact, or question.\n3. Nut Graf: A paragraph that outlines the central theme or purpose of the story, providing a roadmap for what follows.\n4. Body:\n› Background Information: Provides Essential details that set the scene or give context.\n› Development: Present the story’s main content, including arguments, descriptions, and narratives.\n› Supporting Elements: Quotes, statistics, anecdotes, and examples that enrich the narrative.\n5. Conclusion: Wraps up the story, often tying back to the lead or looking forward to the future.\n6. Call to Action: (Optional) Directs readers toward further engagement or exploration of the topic.\n\nEnumerate the elements of a feature article. Describe each element based on your understanding.\n\nAfter enumerating the elements, write a feature article on your English notebook. Choose one that interests you the most from the given topics below.\na. A day at a beach\nb. A Mother’s Day Celebration\nc. A Surprise for Father\nd. Life While staying at Home e. Rizal Our National Hero\n\nRecall an unforgettable experience travelling out of town or abroad. Where was it, and why did you consider the experience as memorable? Write a feature article that observes the five elements f feature writing. The final output should be written on one whole intermediate paper.',NULL,10,14,'Direction: Do the following tasks:\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Feature Writing\nFeature writing is an art that combines factual reporting with creative storytelling to produce an engaging and informative piece. By following these guidelines, writers can craft stories that resonate with their audience, providing depth and insight into a wide range of topics.\n\nKey Components\n1. Headline: Captivate readers and summarizes the essence of the story.\n2. Lead: An opening paragraph that hooks the reader, often with an anecdote, striking fact, or question.\n3. Nut Graf: A paragraph that outlines the central theme or purpose of the story, providing a roadmap for what follows.\n4. Body:\n› Background Information: Provides Essential details that set the scene or give context.\n› Development: Present the story’s main content, including arguments, descriptions, and narratives.\n› Supporting Elements: Quotes, statistics, anecdotes, and examples that enrich the narrative.\n5. Conclusion: Wraps up the story, often tying back to the lead or looking forward to the future.\n6. Call to Action: (Optional) Directs readers toward further engagement or exploration of the topic.\n\nEnumerate the elements of a feature article. Describe each element based on your understanding.\n\nAfter enumerating the elements, write a feature article on your English notebook. Choose one that interests you the most from the given topics below.\na. A day at a beach\nb. A Mother’s Day Celebration\nc. A Surprise for Father\nd. L',1,1,'answer'),
(1291,571,NULL,'learn',1,'Editorial Log','open','This activity will serve your opportunity to expressing your unique perspective on the editorial. Read the editorial article carefully to understand the author\'s viewpoint, then provide the expected details as instructed. Grab your pens and let your thoughts flow!','This activity will serve your opportunity to expressing your unique perspective on the editorial. Read the editorial article carefully to understand the author\'s viewpoint, then provide the expected details as instructed. Grab your pens and let your thoughts flow!\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Editorial Cartooning\nCartooning is an illustration that depicts humorous events, whether serious or not. The word cartooning comes from two words related terms: Caricature and Lampoon.\n\n› Caricature is an exaggerated illustration of someone or something, often enlarging certain body parts to create humorous effect.\n› Lampoon is a form of writing that publicly criticizes someone or something.\n\nEditorial Cartoon\nAn editorial cartoon (also called Political Cartoon) is an opinionated drawing about political or social issues. It may be created to attack, criticize, entertain or praise. Editorial cartoons are usually paired with editorials but may also appear in features, literary sections, or other parts of a publication wgen no appropriate photograph is available.\n\nDOs\n• Do deal with a single topic and be original.\n• Do have humor and be entertaining if possible.\n• Do use symbols which are generally known by people in order to avoid confusion.\n• Drawings that represents something.\nEx. A crocodile represents a greedy person or corrupt politician.\n• Do have less texts like captions, labels, and dialogs.\n• Do have a balance between the artwork created and the opinion of the cartoonist.\n\nDONTs\n• Don’t over-exaggerate so as not to make it look corny.\n• Don’t or avoid hurting someone.\n• Don’t attack the person himself instead of his actions.\n• Don’t use several illustrations for it may confuse the reader. Around 3 – 5 illustrations will do.\n\nSteps in Doing Editorial Cartooning\n1. Issue – Know the issue you are concerned with and know the facts related to it.\n2. Stand – Questions like “Which side do you agree or disagree with?” and “What elements affect the issue?”\n3. Sketching – The creation of the guide or draft of your cartoon.\n4. Outlining/Sketching – The detailed part of the cartoon is created by using a pen to trace the shape of the sketch.\n5. Shading – An optional step where in you give depth to your drawing making it more realistic.\n\nEditorial: Stop Bullying, Schools Must Be a Safe Place\nSchools are places where we learn and build meaningful relationships with fellow students we meet along the way. At their core, they must be safe spaces place for learners.\n\nHowever, there lurks a disease in schools that needs to be cured -- bullying.\n\nThroughout the years, we have heard countless stories of students being bullied. Some were severely bullied and it led them to do unimaginable things for someone their age. Some committed suicide while others harmed fellow students.\n\nDavao City Councilor Enzo Villafuerte said schools have failed to address bullying cases within their premises.\n\n\"Nag-interview ko og mga parents kung naa ba silay experiences of bullying diri sa schools nato sa Davao, and daghan kog na-estoryahan nga naa gyud cases of bullying and wala\'y ginabuhat ang mga teachers nato and principals nato para i-address ang bullying (I have talked to some parents and asked them if they have experiences of bullying here in the schools in the city, and I found out that there really existing cases but neither teachers nor principals have acted on it),\" Villafuerte said during his interview on Davao City Disaster Radio on January 5, 2022.\n\nBullying takes a huge toll on the mental health of young people. Victims tend to show signs of depression, anxiety, stress, and detachment. They would harm themselves and be in a very dark place.\n\nWhat is worse is the mental and emotional stress on these young people is being seen as just a phase. Parents would seldom seek professional health for their children who are victims of bullying. Others would just ask the victim to \"pray the depression away.\"\n\nFor some, what some of these kids post on social media is just seen as trivial posts. We have to start taking their posts on depression, anxiety, and sadness more seriously. These posts should be seen as red flags and interventions must be made.\n\nThe responsibility of protecting students from being bullied or preventing the birth of bullies also lies with the school officials and the parents themselves. At home, parents must teach their kids the right values like being respectful of others. Schools themselves must have safeguards in place to protect students from bullies and reprimand the bullies.\n\nMaybe it is time that we intensify topics on good manners and right conduct in schools.\n\nLikewise, schools must truly and intentionally capacitate all teaching and non-teaching personnel with the knowledge on identifying students who are being bullied and those who are having mental or emotional issues.\n\nSchools must be safe places. That\'s why it falls upon the responsibility of the schools and the parents that where their children go is also psychologically, mentally, and emotionally safe for other kids.\n\nGetting Started: Dive into the article and check out how the author introduces the topic. What caught your attention in the beginning?\n\nWhat\'s Inside: Take a stroll through the main points in the article. What are the key things the author is talking about? Identify any details that stood out to you?\n\nThe Wrap-up: Look at how the article wraps things up. How did the author close the conversation? Is it a strong finish or something that leaves you thinking?\n\nAuthor\'s Take: What\'s the author\'s stand on the topic? Do they seem like they\'re sharing their personal take, giving us the facts, or maybe a bit of both?\n\nImpact on you: Let\'s get real for a moment. How did the article hit you? Do you think it might change how you see things or act in the future?\n\nHEART-TO-HEART: Now, spill the beans. What\'s your take on all of this? Do you agree with the author, or do you see things differently now? How did the article play with your thoughts?','assets/images/level7/g5/p018-1.webp','[\"assets/images/level7/g5/p018-1.webp\"]','This activity will serve your opportunity to expressing your unique perspective on the editorial. Read the editorial article carefully to understand the author\'s viewpoint, then provide the expected details as instructed. Grab your pens and let your thoughts flow!\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Editorial Cartooning\nCartooning is an illustration that depicts humorous events, whether serious or not. The word cartooning comes from two words related terms: Caricature and Lampoon.\n\n› Caricature is an exaggerated illustration of someone or something, often enlarging certain body parts to create humorous effect.\n› Lampoon is a form of writing that publicly criticizes someone or something.\n\nEditorial Cartoon\nAn editorial cartoon (also called Political Cartoon) is an opinionated drawing about political or social issues. It may be created to attack, criticize, entertain or praise. Editorial cartoons are usually paired with editorials but may also appear in features, literary sections, or other parts of a publication wgen no appropriate photograph is available.\n\nDOs\n• Do deal with a single topic and be original.\n• Do have humor and be entertaining if possible.\n• Do use symbols which are generally known by people in order to avoid confusion.\n• Drawings that represents something.\nEx. A crocodile represents a greedy person or corrupt politician.\n• Do have less texts like captions, labels, and dialogs.\n• Do have a balance between the artwork created and the opinion of the cartoonist.\n\nDONTs\n• Don’t over-exaggerate so as not to make it look corny.\n• Don’t or avoid hurting someone.\n• Don’t attack the person himself instead of his actions.\n• Don’t use several illustrations for it may confuse the reader. Around 3 – 5 illustrations will do.\n\nSteps in Doing Editorial Cartooning\n1. Issue – Know the issue you are concerned with and know the facts related to it.\n2. Stand – Questions like “Which side do you agree or disagree with?” and “What elements affect the issue?”\n3. Sketching – The creation of the guide or draft of your cartoon.\n4. Outlining/Sketching – The detailed part of the cartoon is created by using a pen to trace the shape of the sketch.\n5. Shading – An optional step where in you give depth to your drawing making it more realistic.\n\nEditorial: Stop Bullying, Schools Must Be a Safe Place\nSchools are places where we learn and build meaningful relationships with fellow students we meet along the way. At their core, they must be safe spaces place for learners.\n\nHowever, there lurks a disease in schools that needs to be cured -- bullying.\n\nThroughout the years, we have heard countless stories of students being bullied. Some were severely bullied and it led them to do unimaginable things for someone their age. Some committed suicide while others harmed fellow students.\n\nDavao City Councilor Enzo Villafuerte said schools have failed to address bullying cases within their premises.\n\n\"Nag-interview ko og mga parents kung naa ba silay experiences of bullying di',NULL,10,16,'This activity will serve your opportunity to expressing your unique perspective on the editorial. Read the editorial article carefully to understand the author\'s viewpoint, then provide the expected details as instructed. Grab your pens and let your thoughts flow!\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Editorial Cartooning\nCartooning is an illustration that depicts humorous events, whether serious or not. The word cartooning comes from two words related terms: Caricature and Lampoon.\n\n› Caricature is an exaggerated illustration of someone or something, often enlarging certain body parts to create humorous effect.\n› Lampoon is a form of writing that publicly criticizes someone or something.\n\nEditorial Cartoon\nAn editorial cartoon (also called Political Cartoon) is an opinionated drawing about political or social issues. It may be created to attack, criticize, entertain or praise. Editorial cartoons are usually paired with editorials but may also appear in features, literary sections, or other parts of a publication wgen no appropriate photograph is available.\n\nDOs\n• Do deal with a single topic and be original.\n• Do have humor and be entertaining if possible.\n• Do use symbols which are generally known by people in order to avoid confusion.\n• Drawings that represents something.\nEx. A crocodile represents a greedy person or corrupt politician.\n• Do have less texts like captions, labels, and dialogs.\n• Do have a balance between the artwork created and the opinion of the cartoonist.',1,1,'answer'),
(1292,572,NULL,'learn',1,'Writing Articles','open','Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:','Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:\n\nWaste Segregation\n\nThe Rise of Generative AI and Its Impact on Everyday Work\n\nThe Science Behind Earthquakes and Disaster Preparedness\n\nGamification in Learning and Workplace Productivity\n\nIndigenous Knowledge and Modern Scientific Collaboration',NULL,'[]','Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:\n\nWaste Segregation\n\nThe Rise of Generative AI and Its Impact on Everyday Work\n\nThe Science Behind Earthquakes and Disaster Preparedness\n\nGamification in Learning and Workplace Productivity\n\nIndigenous Knowledge and Modern Scientific Collaboration',NULL,10,20,'Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:\n\nWaste Segregation\n\nThe Rise of Generative AI and Its Impact on Everyday Work\n\nThe Science Behind Earthquakes and Disaster Preparedness\n\nGamification in Learning and Workplace Productivity\n\nIndigenous Knowledge and Modern Scientific Collaboration',1,1,'answer'),
(1293,573,NULL,'learn',1,'Writing Articles','open','Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:','Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:\n\nWhy Society is Becoming Increasingly Dependent on Technology?\n\nClassroom Technologies That Actually Make Learning Harder\n\nHow Online Misinformation Divides Communities?\n\nWhat I Learned After Moving Away From Home\n\nThe Fear and Beauty of Starting Over\n\nHow a Simple Routine Changed My Life?\n\nLessons From a Year Of Uncertainty\n\nRediscovering Childhood Passions as an Adult\n\nMy Journey Toward Better Mental Health\n\nThe Joy of Ordinary Days',NULL,'[]','Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:\n\nWhy Society is Becoming Increasingly Dependent on Technology?\n\nClassroom Technologies That Actually Make Learning Harder\n\nHow Online Misinformation Divides Communities?\n\nWhat I Learned After Moving Away From Home\n\nThe Fear and Beauty of Starting Over\n\nHow a Simple Routine Changed My Life?\n\nLessons From a Year Of Uncertainty\n\nRediscovering Childhood Passions as an Adult\n\nMy Journey Toward Better Mental Health\n\nThe Joy of Ordinary Days',NULL,10,20,'Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:\n\nWhy Society is Becoming Increasingly Dependent on Technology?\n\nClassroom Technologies That Actually Make Learning Harder\n\nHow Online Misinformation Divides Communities?\n\nWhat I Learned After Moving Away From Home\n\nThe Fear and Beauty of Starting Over\n\nHow a Simple Routine Changed My Life?\n\nLessons From a Year Of Uncertainty\n\nRediscovering Childhood Passions as an Adult\n\nMy Journey Toward Better Mental Health\n\nThe Joy of Ordinary Days',1,1,'answer'),
(1294,574,NULL,'learn',1,'Writing a Paragraph','open','Topic: Is social media helping or harming students today? · Instructions: Write 3–4 sentences giving your opinion about whether the school cafeteria should offer more healthy snacks.','Topic: Is social media helping or harming students today? · Instructions: Write 3–4 sentences giving your opinion about whether the school cafeteria should offer more healthy snacks.\n\nRemember\n• State your opinion clearly.\n• Give one reason to support your opinion.\n• End with a short, convincing sentence.\n\nExample:\nI think social media is harming students today. Many students spend too much time on apps and games, which can make them tired and distracted from their studies. For example, some students scroll through social media instead of doing homework. Students should use social media less so it does not affect their learning.\n\nWrite 3–4 sentences giving your opinion.',NULL,'[]','Topic: Is social media helping or harming students today? · Instructions: Write 3–4 sentences giving your opinion about whether the school cafeteria should offer more healthy snacks.\n\nRemember\n• State your opinion clearly.\n• Give one reason to support your opinion.\n• End with a short, convincing sentence.\n\nExample:\nI think social media is harming students today. Many students spend too much time on apps and games, which can make them tired and distracted from their studies. For example, some students scroll through social media instead of doing homework. Students should use social media less so it does not affect their learning.\n\nWrite 3–4 sentences giving your opinion.',NULL,10,21,'Topic: Is social media helping or harming students today? · Instructions: Write 3–4 sentences giving your opinion about whether the school cafeteria should offer more healthy snacks.\n\nRemember\n• State your opinion clearly.\n• Give one reason to support your opinion.\n• End with a short, convincing sentence.\n\nExample:\nI think social media is harming students today. Many students spend too much time on apps and games, which can make them tired and distracted from their studies. For example, some students scroll through social media instead of doing homework. Students should use social media less so it does not affect their learning.\n\nWrite 3–4 sentences giving your opinion.',1,1,'answer'),
(1295,575,NULL,'learn',1,'Editorial Writing','open','Direction: Write a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).','Direction: Write a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Editorial Writing\n› Editorials usually appear on the editorial or opinion page to differentiate them from news stories.\n› They comment on issues reported in the news articles. By weighing and presenting arguments, an editorial reaches a conclusion and makes a recommendation regarding an issue.\n› The editorial represents the official stand of a newspaper. Because of this, it is written in a way that conveys the opinion of the entire publication\n› “I” and “my” are not used because they indicate personal opinion of a single writer.\n› The editorial always includes a title.\n\nParts of Editorial Writing · 1. Introduction\nIt is the opening paragraph. It may contain the news upon which the editorial is based, in such in case, the introduction briefly states the background and topic.\n\nLast week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food were piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.\n\n2. Body\nThis section contains the facts about the news or issues including the writer’s opinion or viewpoints. It builds up the case through a logical arrangement of ideas supported by facts • Reaction – sets the reader up to receive the opinion The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.\n\n• Details – support your reaction and lead to a conclusion The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.\n\nAfter the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.\n\nThe canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.\n\n3. Conclusion\nThis is the final of the editorial. It either summarizes or drives home the point. It ties up the ideas and arguments presented in the body. It is sometimes referred to as the clincher.\n\nWe support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.\n\nParagraph 1:\n• Present your opinion clearly.\n• Explain one positive and one negative effect of social media.\n\nParagraph 2:\n• State which effect you think is stronger and why.\n• Provide evidence or examples (realistic, simple ones).\n• End with a strong call to action (e.g., encouraging balanced or responsible use)\n\nExample;\nSocial media is a powerful tool that can both help and harm students today. On the positive side, it allows students to connect with classmates, share ideas, and access educational content online. For example, students can join study groups or watch tutorials on difficult topics. However, social media can also distract students, cause stress, and reduce the time they spend on homework or outdoor activities.\n\nI believe the negative effects are stronger than the positive ones if social media is not used carefully. Students should be taught to use social media responsibly and set daily time limits. Parents and teachers can also encourage activities like reading, sports, or hobbies to balance screen time. By using social media wisely, students can enjoy its benefits without letting it harm their health or learning.\n\nParagraph 1\n\nParagraph 2',NULL,'[]','Direction: Write a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Editorial Writing\n› Editorials usually appear on the editorial or opinion page to differentiate them from news stories.\n› They comment on issues reported in the news articles. By weighing and presenting arguments, an editorial reaches a conclusion and makes a recommendation regarding an issue.\n› The editorial represents the official stand of a newspaper. Because of this, it is written in a way that conveys the opinion of the entire publication\n› “I” and “my” are not used because they indicate personal opinion of a single writer.\n› The editorial always includes a title.\n\nParts of Editorial Writing · 1. Introduction\nIt is the opening paragraph. It may contain the news upon which the editorial is based, in such in case, the introduction briefly states the background and topic.\n\nLast week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food were piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.\n\n2. Body\nThis section contains the facts about the news or issues including the writer’s opinion or viewpoints. It builds up the case through a logical arrangement of ideas supported by facts • Reaction – sets the reader up to receive the opinion The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.\n\n• Details – support your reaction and lead to a conclusion The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.\n\nAfter the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.\n\nThe canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.\n\n3. Conclusion\nThis is the final of the editorial. It either summarizes or drives home the point. It ties up the ideas and arguments presented in the body. It is sometimes referred to as the clincher.\n\nWe support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.\n\nParagraph 1:\n• Present your opinion clearly.\n• Explain one positive and one negative effect of social media.\n\nParagraph 2:\n• State which effect you think is stronger and why.\n• Provide evidence or examples (realistic, simple ones).\n• End with a strong call to action (e.g., encouraging balanced or responsible u',NULL,10,22,'Direction: Write a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Editorial Writing\n› Editorials usually appear on the editorial or opinion page to differentiate them from news stories.\n› They comment on issues reported in the news articles. By weighing and presenting arguments, an editorial reaches a conclusion and makes a recommendation regarding an issue.\n› The editorial represents the official stand of a newspaper. Because of this, it is written in a way that conveys the opinion of the entire publication\n› “I” and “my” are not used because they indicate personal opinion of a single writer.\n› The editorial always includes a title.\n\nParts of Editorial Writing · 1. Introduction\nIt is the opening paragraph. It may contain the news upon which the editorial is based, in such in case, the introduction briefly states the background and topic.\n\nLast week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food were piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.\n\n2. Body\nThis section contains the facts about the news or issues including the writer’s opinion or viewpoints. It builds up the case through a logical arrangement of ideas supported by facts • Reaction – sets the reader up to receive the opinion ',1,1,'answer'),
(1296,576,NULL,'learn',1,'Copyreading and Headline Writing','open','Direction: Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.','Direction: Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Copyreading\nCopyreading is editing or correcting errors made by the reporter and proofreading the errors made by the typesetter or printer.\n\n• It is the art of arranging, correcting, and selecting the quality and type of news\n• It is also called copyediting.\n• One who edits copies is called a copyreader or copyeditor\n\nWhat to copyread?\n1. Errors in fact\n2. Errors in grammar\n3. Errors in structure\n4. Errors in style\n5. Libelous and derogatory statements\n6. Seditious and rebellious matter\n7. Opinion, editorializing statements in news\n8. Verbal deadwoods, redundancy\n9. Errors in unity, coherence and emphasis\n\nProcedure in Copyreading\n1. Read the whole article first to determine what kind of story it is.\n2. Having determined the type of story, read the article again. This time determine what the lead paragraph is.\n3. Take note of errors in fact. Check on the accuracy of the statements and information. Verify when in doubt.\n4. Read the story again and mark all paragraph beginnings. Then, correct errors in grammar, spelling, punctuations, and style using copyreading symbols.\n5. Improve the organization of the story. Make sure the lead features the lead fact.\n6. Go over the whole story and again. Remove all unnecessary and irrelevant materials.\n7. Check the length of the story and then prepare the clean copy.\n8. Write a very catchy and fitting headline for the story.\n9. Mark the copy for the printer (size, type, column, width, etc.)\n10. Encircle the slug line (short title, name of reporter),\n11. End pagewith a paragraph. Do no tcut paragraph and continue on the next page.\n12. Write “more” at the bottom of the page of unfinished story and continue the next sheet. Do not use the back page of the paper.\n13. On the second page, instead of numbering as page 2, label it “first add” or “add one” followed by the slug. For example, “first add nabbed” or “add one nabbed”.\n14. End the story with number 30 or sharp (#). Encircle it.\n15. If the copy could hardly be read because of so many corrections, retype it if time permits to facilitate editing and typesetting.\n\nHeadline Writing\nHeadline is the title of the news story. It is the display window of the newspaper. It also serves as a source of information for busy newspaper readers.\n\nSteps in Headline Writing\n1. Read the whole story to understand its message.\n2. Underline the key words from the lead.\n3. Using these key words, write a short telegraphic sentence summary of the news (omit articles, unnecessary auxiliary verbs, prepositions, details).\n4. Divide according to units of thought into the number of lines required.\n5. Substitute simple, effective synonyms to reduce width within the limits of space allotted.\nExample: Charter polls / must go on, say experts\n\nRules in Writing Headline\n1. The headline must have a verb and it must be in the present tense. Wrong: Ang Limbukad copped 5 trophies in DSPC. Correct: Ang Limbukad cops 5 trophies in DSPC\n2. The first letter of the first word and proper nouns are generally capitalized. Example: Iran offers more oil in RP\n3. Do not use period.\n4. Minimize punctuation marks. Wrong: Rolling stores to sell rice and groceries. Correct: Rolling stores to sell rice, groceries\n5. Don’t use the articles a, an, the. Wrong: A fire hits the squatters’ area in Tondo. Correct: Fire hits squatters’ area in Tondo\n6. Don’t use names unless the person is well-known. Wrong: Santos electrocuted. Correct: Carpenter electrocuted\n7. Use the infinitives for future events. Wrong: City Hall will push anti-squatting drive. Correct: City Hall to push anti-squatting drive\n8. Use specific terms instead of generalities. Wrong: School official gets scholarship. Correct: Principal gets US scholarship\n9. Assertions in a headline should have a source or attribution. Poor: Principal orders suspension of classes. Strong: Classes suspended today, says principal. Good: Even SC can err-Cuevas. Good: Cuevas: Even SC can err\n10. Use the shorter word whenever possible, e.g., use “cut” for decrease, or “hike” for increase. Example: School hikes tuition fee / Studes urge class boycott\n\nThe Article to Copyread\ndoctor. Ma. Girlie Pinongan, provincial health officer, said there were 2,211 influenza-like cases reported from Jan. 1 to Dec. 2 this year in the 31 towns and cities of negros occidental compared to 616 cases in 2022.\nhealth officials urged the public to take precautions during the holiday as cases as cases of influenza-like illness in Negros Occidental increased by 258.93 percent this year.\nhe highest number of cases were recorded in Isabela town with 1,680 followed by San Carlos City with 129; Murcia, 80; Bago City, 57; Talisay City, 50; Hinigaran, 20; Cadiz City, 19; and Don Salvador Benedicto, Silay City and Victorias City with 16 each.\nStill, she said, the public should avoid close contact with persons manifesting influenza-like symptoms\nPinongan said the cases are not severe and are usually triggered by changes in conditions weather/\nShe also urged people to wear mask in closed and crowded spaces as a precaution.\n\nWrite the copyread article, the slug and the printers’ direction.\n\nHeadline 1 (barline)\n\nHeadline 2 (droplines)',NULL,'[]','Direction: Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Copyreading\nCopyreading is editing or correcting errors made by the reporter and proofreading the errors made by the typesetter or printer.\n\n• It is the art of arranging, correcting, and selecting the quality and type of news\n• It is also called copyediting.\n• One who edits copies is called a copyreader or copyeditor\n\nWhat to copyread?\n1. Errors in fact\n2. Errors in grammar\n3. Errors in structure\n4. Errors in style\n5. Libelous and derogatory statements\n6. Seditious and rebellious matter\n7. Opinion, editorializing statements in news\n8. Verbal deadwoods, redundancy\n9. Errors in unity, coherence and emphasis\n\nProcedure in Copyreading\n1. Read the whole article first to determine what kind of story it is.\n2. Having determined the type of story, read the article again. This time determine what the lead paragraph is.\n3. Take note of errors in fact. Check on the accuracy of the statements and information. Verify when in doubt.\n4. Read the story again and mark all paragraph beginnings. Then, correct errors in grammar, spelling, punctuations, and style using copyreading symbols.\n5. Improve the organization of the story. Make sure the lead features the lead fact.\n6. Go over the whole story and again. Remove all unnecessary and irrelevant materials.\n7. Check the length of the story and then prepare the clean copy.\n8. Write a very catchy and fitting headline for the story.\n9. Mark the copy for the printer (size, type, column, width, etc.)\n10. Encircle the slug line (short title, name of reporter),\n11. End pagewith a paragraph. Do no tcut paragraph and continue on the next page.\n12. Write “more” at the bottom of the page of unfinished story and continue the next sheet. Do not use the back page of the paper.\n13. On the second page, instead of numbering as page 2, label it “first add” or “add one” followed by the slug. For example, “first add nabbed” or “add one nabbed”.\n14. End the story with number 30 or sharp (#). Encircle it.\n15. If the copy could hardly be read because of so many corrections, retype it if time permits to facilitate editing and typesetting.\n\nHeadline Writing\nHeadline is the title of the news story. It is the display window of the newspaper. It also serves as a source of information for busy newspaper readers.\n\nSteps in Headline Writing\n1. Read the whole story to understand its message.\n2. Underline the key words from the lead.\n3. Using these key words, write a short telegraphic sentence summary of the news (omit articles, unnecessary auxiliary verbs, prepositions, details).\n4. Divide according to units of thought into the number of lines required.\n5. Substitute simple, effective synonyms to reduce width within the limits of space allotted.\nExample: Charter polls / must go on, say experts\n\nRules in Writing Headline\n1. The headline must have a verb',NULL,10,26,'Direction: Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Copyreading\nCopyreading is editing or correcting errors made by the reporter and proofreading the errors made by the typesetter or printer.\n\n• It is the art of arranging, correcting, and selecting the quality and type of news\n• It is also called copyediting.\n• One who edits copies is called a copyreader or copyeditor\n\nWhat to copyread?\n1. Errors in fact\n2. Errors in grammar\n3. Errors in structure\n4. Errors in style\n5. Libelous and derogatory statements\n6. Seditious and rebellious matter\n7. Opinion, editorializing statements in news\n8. Verbal deadwoods, redundancy\n9. Errors in unity, coherence and emphasis\n\nProcedure in Copyreading\n1. Read the whole article first to determine what kind of story it is.\n2. Having determined the type of story, read the article again. This time determine what the lead paragraph is.\n3. Take note of errors in fact. Check on the accuracy of the statements and information. Verify when in doubt.\n4. Read the story again and mark all paragraph beginnings. Then, correct errors in grammar, spelling, punctuations, and style using copyreading symbols.\n5. Improve the organization of the story. Make sure the lead features the lead fact.\n6. Go over the whole story and again. Remove all unnecessary and irrelevant materials.\n7. Check the length of the story and then p',1,1,'answer'),
(1297,577,NULL,'learn',1,'News Writing','open','Developing Context and Main Body Content · Theme: Technology','Developing Context and Main Body Content · Theme: Technology\n\nI. Background of the Task\nYou are a student journalist assigned to write a technology news article. Your task is to develop the context and main body content of the story based on the information below:\n\nNews Scenario:\nThree senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\n\nII. Review: What Is Context in News Writing?\nContext provides background information that helps the reader understand:\n• Why the news matters\n• What problem it addresses\n• The current situation related to the issue\n\nIII. Review: What Is the Main Body of a News Article?\nThe main body expands the story by adding:\n• Important details\n• Quotes (real or simulated)\n• Effects or implications\n• Supporting data or explanations\n\nA. Write the Context Paragraph · Explain the background of the dengue issue and why a detection app is important. (3–4 sentences)\n\nB. Write the Main Body Paragraph · Add more details about the app, how it works, who created it, and its possible impact. You may include invented quotes to enrich the story. (4–6 sentences)\n\nV. Reflection Question · How can technology created by students contribute to solving real community problems?',NULL,'[]','Developing Context and Main Body Content · Theme: Technology\n\nI. Background of the Task\nYou are a student journalist assigned to write a technology news article. Your task is to develop the context and main body content of the story based on the information below:\n\nNews Scenario:\nThree senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\n\nII. Review: What Is Context in News Writing?\nContext provides background information that helps the reader understand:\n• Why the news matters\n• What problem it addresses\n• The current situation related to the issue\n\nIII. Review: What Is the Main Body of a News Article?\nThe main body expands the story by adding:\n• Important details\n• Quotes (real or simulated)\n• Effects or implications\n• Supporting data or explanations\n\nA. Write the Context Paragraph · Explain the background of the dengue issue and why a detection app is important. (3–4 sentences)\n\nB. Write the Main Body Paragraph · Add more details about the app, how it works, who created it, and its possible impact. You may include invented quotes to enrich the story. (4–6 sentences)\n\nV. Reflection Question · How can technology created by students contribute to solving real community problems?',NULL,10,31,'Developing Context and Main Body Content · Theme: Technology\n\nI. Background of the Task\nYou are a student journalist assigned to write a technology news article. Your task is to develop the context and main body content of the story based on the information below:\n\nNews Scenario:\nThree senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\n\nII. Review: What Is Context in News Writing?\nContext provides background information that helps the reader understand:\n• Why the news matters\n• What problem it addresses\n• The current situation related to the issue\n\nIII. Review: What Is the Main Body of a News Article?\nThe main body expands the story by adding:\n• Important details\n• Quotes (real or simulated)\n• Effects or implications\n• Supporting data or explanations\n\nA. Write the Context Paragraph · Explain the background of the dengue issue and why a detection app is important. (3–4 sentences)\n\nB. Write the Main Body Paragraph · Add more details about the app, how it works, who created it, and its possible impact. You may include invented quotes to enrich the story. (4–6 sentences)\n\nV. Reflection Question · How can technology created by students contribute to solving real community problems?',1,1,'answer'),
(1298,578,NULL,'learn',1,'Feature Writing','open','A Day in the Life of ….','A Day in the Life of ….\n\nInstructions:\n1. Choose someone to feature: a classmate, teacher, family member, neighbor, or even a school staff member (e.g., librarian, guard, janitor).\n2. Interview the person using at least 5 questions (e.g., daily routine, hobbies, challenges, fun facts).\n3. Write a 3-4 paragraph feature article titled “A Day in the Life of ______.”\n4. Focus on capturing interesting details, personality, and real experiences.\n5. Add one photo or hand-drawn illustration (optional).\n\nExample: Title: A Day in the Life of Mr. Santos, the School Guard\nMr. Santos steps through the school gate before the sun has fully risen, his uniform crisp and his flashlight in hand. He smiles warmly as he greets the early students trickling in, locking the gate behind him. “I like being the first one here,” he says. “It gives me time to think and prepare for the day.”\n\nThroughout the morning, he patrols the corridors, making sure classrooms are secure and reminding students to put their things away. When someone forgets a book or backpack, he helps them carry it to their classroom. At recess, he watches students play, sometimes pausing to chat he asks about their day, what subjects they like, and even what games they play at home.\n\nIn the afternoon, Mr. Santos helps guide students out of the school grounds safely. He stands by the gate, waving goodbye, and makes sure no one lags behind. After the last student leaves, he walks around to check every door and window. He says, “My job isn’t just to guard the school — I guard their memories too. I want them to leave knowing they\'re safe.” As he closes up, his face shows a quiet pride: even in the small moments, he knows he’s made a difference.\n\nA Day in the Life of ______ (3-4 paragraphs)\n\nInstructions:\n1. Explore the school campus and identify a “hidden gem”—a place or feature students may overlook (e.g., a quiet reading corner, an art display, a garden spot).\n2. Describe what makes this place special or unique.\n3. Interview at least 1 person who knows something about the location (e.g., librarian, teacher, classmate).\n4. Write a short feature article (2–3 paragraphs) that includes descriptive details, sensory imagery, and interesting facts.\n5. Give the article a catchy title (e.g., “The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab”).\n\nExample: Title: The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab\nParagraph 1 – Description: Tucked behind the science lab, away from the bustling hallways, lies a small garden that most students pass by without noticing. Sunlight filters through the branches of a tall jacaranda tree, casting dappled shadows on the neatly arranged flowerbeds. The air is fragrant with the scent of blooming marigolds and jasmine, and the gentle sound of rustling leaves creates a peaceful atmosphere. A worn wooden bench sits under the tree, inviting anyone to pause and enjoy the quiet.\n\nParagraph 2 – Stories and Facts: Mrs. Alvarez, the school’s biology teacher, explains that the garden was planted by students five years ago as part of a sustainability project. “It started as a simple idea to grow flowers and herbs for the school, but it became a place where students could relax and observe nature,” she says. Over the years, students have added bird feeders, a small pond, and colorful stepping stones. Many report that spending time here helps them focus and feel calm between classes.\n\nParagraph 3 – Reflection / Significance: Though it may seem small and unassuming, this garden is a hidden treasure in the school. It reminds everyone that even in a busy campus, there are spaces to pause, reflect, and connect with nature. For those who take the time to explore it, the garden offers more than beauty—it offers a moment of calm and inspiration in the middle of a hectic school day.\n\nWrite your feature article with a catchy title (2–3 paragraphs).',NULL,'[]','A Day in the Life of ….\n\nInstructions:\n1. Choose someone to feature: a classmate, teacher, family member, neighbor, or even a school staff member (e.g., librarian, guard, janitor).\n2. Interview the person using at least 5 questions (e.g., daily routine, hobbies, challenges, fun facts).\n3. Write a 3-4 paragraph feature article titled “A Day in the Life of ______.”\n4. Focus on capturing interesting details, personality, and real experiences.\n5. Add one photo or hand-drawn illustration (optional).\n\nExample: Title: A Day in the Life of Mr. Santos, the School Guard\nMr. Santos steps through the school gate before the sun has fully risen, his uniform crisp and his flashlight in hand. He smiles warmly as he greets the early students trickling in, locking the gate behind him. “I like being the first one here,” he says. “It gives me time to think and prepare for the day.”\n\nThroughout the morning, he patrols the corridors, making sure classrooms are secure and reminding students to put their things away. When someone forgets a book or backpack, he helps them carry it to their classroom. At recess, he watches students play, sometimes pausing to chat he asks about their day, what subjects they like, and even what games they play at home.\n\nIn the afternoon, Mr. Santos helps guide students out of the school grounds safely. He stands by the gate, waving goodbye, and makes sure no one lags behind. After the last student leaves, he walks around to check every door and window. He says, “My job isn’t just to guard the school — I guard their memories too. I want them to leave knowing they\'re safe.” As he closes up, his face shows a quiet pride: even in the small moments, he knows he’s made a difference.\n\nA Day in the Life of ______ (3-4 paragraphs)\n\nInstructions:\n1. Explore the school campus and identify a “hidden gem”—a place or feature students may overlook (e.g., a quiet reading corner, an art display, a garden spot).\n2. Describe what makes this place special or unique.\n3. Interview at least 1 person who knows something about the location (e.g., librarian, teacher, classmate).\n4. Write a short feature article (2–3 paragraphs) that includes descriptive details, sensory imagery, and interesting facts.\n5. Give the article a catchy title (e.g., “The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab”).\n\nExample: Title: The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab\nParagraph 1 – Description: Tucked behind the science lab, away from the bustling hallways, lies a small garden that most students pass by without noticing. Sunlight filters through the branches of a tall jacaranda tree, casting dappled shadows on the neatly arranged flowerbeds. The air is fragrant with the scent of blooming marigolds and jasmine, and the gentle sound of rustling leaves creates a peaceful atmosphere. A worn wooden bench sits under the tree, inviting anyone to pause and enjoy the quiet.\n\nParagraph 2 – Stories and Facts: Mrs. Alvarez, the school’s biology teacher, explains that the garden was planted by students ',NULL,10,33,'A Day in the Life of ….\n\nInstructions:\n1. Choose someone to feature: a classmate, teacher, family member, neighbor, or even a school staff member (e.g., librarian, guard, janitor).\n2. Interview the person using at least 5 questions (e.g., daily routine, hobbies, challenges, fun facts).\n3. Write a 3-4 paragraph feature article titled “A Day in the Life of ______.”\n4. Focus on capturing interesting details, personality, and real experiences.\n5. Add one photo or hand-drawn illustration (optional).\n\nExample: Title: A Day in the Life of Mr. Santos, the School Guard\nMr. Santos steps through the school gate before the sun has fully risen, his uniform crisp and his flashlight in hand. He smiles warmly as he greets the early students trickling in, locking the gate behind him. “I like being the first one here,” he says. “It gives me time to think and prepare for the day.”\n\nThroughout the morning, he patrols the corridors, making sure classrooms are secure and reminding students to put their things away. When someone forgets a book or backpack, he helps them carry it to their classroom. At recess, he watches students play, sometimes pausing to chat he asks about their day, what subjects they like, and even what games they play at home.\n\nIn the afternoon, Mr. Santos helps guide students out of the school grounds safely. He stands by the gate, waving goodbye, and makes sure no one lags behind. After the last student leaves, he walks around to check every door and window. He says, “My job ',1,1,'answer'),
(1299,579,NULL,'learn',1,'Story Sequencing','open','Instructions: Arrange the story in a correct sequence. Label each part: Headline, Byline, Lead, Body, Conclusion.','Instructions: Arrange the story in a correct sequence. Label each part: Headline, Byline, Lead, Body, Conclusion.\n\nResidents of Barangay Maligaya gathered early Sunday morning for a clean-up drive after heavy rains caused flooding and scattered debris across several streets.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBy Jericho Dela Cruz, Campus Journalist\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nThe barangay plans to hold monthly clean-ups and educational talks on proper waste disposal in local schools.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nVolunteers, including students and parents, helped clear garbage from sidewalks and drains. Barangay officials said this is part of a larger campaign to prevent future floods. “We need to keep our surroundings clean to protect our health and homes,” said Barangay Captain Liza Ramos.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBarangay Holds Clean-Up Drive After Flood\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion',NULL,'[]','Instructions: Arrange the story in a correct sequence. Label each part: Headline, Byline, Lead, Body, Conclusion.\n\nResidents of Barangay Maligaya gathered early Sunday morning for a clean-up drive after heavy rains caused flooding and scattered debris across several streets.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBy Jericho Dela Cruz, Campus Journalist\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nThe barangay plans to hold monthly clean-ups and educational talks on proper waste disposal in local schools.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nVolunteers, including students and parents, helped clear garbage from sidewalks and drains. Barangay officials said this is part of a larger campaign to prevent future floods. “We need to keep our surroundings clean to protect our health and homes,” said Barangay Captain Liza Ramos.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBarangay Holds Clean-Up Drive After Flood\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion',NULL,10,6,'Instructions: Arrange the story in a correct sequence. Label each part: Headline, Byline, Lead, Body, Conclusion.\n\nResidents of Barangay Maligaya gathered early Sunday morning for a clean-up drive after heavy rains caused flooding and scattered debris across several streets.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBy Jericho Dela Cruz, Campus Journalist\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nThe barangay plans to hold monthly clean-ups and educational talks on proper waste disposal in local schools.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nVolunteers, including students and parents, helped clear garbage from sidewalks and drains. Barangay officials said this is part of a larger campaign to prevent future floods. “We need to keep our surroundings clean to protect our health and homes,” said Barangay Captain Liza Ramos.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBarangay Holds Clean-Up Drive After Flood\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion',1,1,'answer'),
(1300,580,NULL,'learn',1,'Scrambled Information','open','Instructions: Read the scrambled information, identify the WHO, WHAT, WHY, WHEN, WHERE. Write your answers on the space provided.','Instructions: Read the scrambled information, identify the WHO, WHAT, WHY, WHEN, WHERE. Write your answers on the space provided.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year. will resume in the voter’s registration\nat the City Hall Commission of Election on Monday\n\nWho\n\nWhat\n\nWhy\n\nWhen\n\nWhere',NULL,'[]','Instructions: Read the scrambled information, identify the WHO, WHAT, WHY, WHEN, WHERE. Write your answers on the space provided.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year. will resume in the voter’s registration\nat the City Hall Commission of Election on Monday\n\nWho\n\nWhat\n\nWhy\n\nWhen\n\nWhere',NULL,10,7,'Instructions: Read the scrambled information, identify the WHO, WHAT, WHY, WHEN, WHERE. Write your answers on the space provided.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year. will resume in the voter’s registration\nat the City Hall Commission of Election on Monday\n\nWho\n\nWhat\n\nWhy\n\nWhen\n\nWhere',1,1,'answer'),
(1301,581,NULL,'learn',1,'Scrambled Information','open','Instructions: Read the scrambled information. Identify first the Ws. Next, WRITE the WHO, WHAT, WHEN, and WHERE in a basic News Lead. The WHY will be written in the next paragraph. Write your answers on the space provided.','Instructions: Read the scrambled information. Identify first the Ws. Next, WRITE the WHO, WHAT, WHEN, and WHERE in a basic News Lead. The WHY will be written in the next paragraph. Write your answers on the space provided.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year.\nwill resume in the voter’s registration\nat the City Hall on Monday\nCommission of Election\n\nNews Lead: ______ (WHO) + ______ (WHAT) + ______ (WHEN) + ______ (WHERE).\n\nSecond Paragraph (the WHY)',NULL,'[]','Instructions: Read the scrambled information. Identify first the Ws. Next, WRITE the WHO, WHAT, WHEN, and WHERE in a basic News Lead. The WHY will be written in the next paragraph. Write your answers on the space provided.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year.\nwill resume in the voter’s registration\nat the City Hall on Monday\nCommission of Election\n\nNews Lead: ______ (WHO) + ______ (WHAT) + ______ (WHEN) + ______ (WHERE).\n\nSecond Paragraph (the WHY)',NULL,10,8,'Instructions: Read the scrambled information. Identify first the Ws. Next, WRITE the WHO, WHAT, WHEN, and WHERE in a basic News Lead. The WHY will be written in the next paragraph. Write your answers on the space provided.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year.\nwill resume in the voter’s registration\nat the City Hall on Monday\nCommission of Election\n\nNews Lead: ______ (WHO) + ______ (WHAT) + ______ (WHEN) + ______ (WHERE).\n\nSecond Paragraph (the WHY)',1,1,'answer'),
(1302,582,NULL,'learn',1,'A Dirty Cafeteria','open','Write an editorial using the following data:','Write an editorial using the following data:\n\nA Dirty Cafeteria\nLast week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Trash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food were piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.\n\nThe PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria causes health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.\n\nThe problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.\n\nAfter the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.\n\nThe canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.\n\nWe support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.\n\nWrite an editorial using the following data:\n• New policies of the administration\n• New grading system\n• K-12 Curriculum\n\nNote\n-describes the event, action, or proposal, then explains the causes and reasons involved. This is followed by an assessment of importance, a look at possible effects, then a statement of support or a call for further study.\n\nWrite your editorial.',NULL,'[]','Write an editorial using the following data:\n\nA Dirty Cafeteria\nLast week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Trash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food were piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.\n\nThe PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria causes health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.\n\nThe problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.\n\nAfter the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.\n\nThe canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.\n\nWe support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.\n\nWrite an editorial using the following data:\n• New policies of the administration\n• New grading system\n• K-12 Curriculum\n\nNote\n-describes the event, action, or proposal, then explains the causes and reasons involved. This is followed by an assessment of importance, a look at possible effects, then a statement of support or a call for further study.\n\nWrite your editorial.',NULL,10,9,'Write an editorial using the following data:\n\nA Dirty Cafeteria\nLast week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Trash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food were piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.\n\nThe PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria causes health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.\n\nThe problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.\n\nAfter the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.\n\nThe canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.\n\nWe support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.\n\nWrite an editorial using the following data:\n• New policies of the administration\n• New grading system\n• K-12 Curriculum\n\nNote\n-describes',1,1,'answer'),
(1303,583,NULL,'learn',1,'Solution: Return to the Basics','open','Write an editorial using the following data:','Write an editorial using the following data:\n\nSolution: Return to the Basics\nLengthening the elementary school up to Grade VII is not the solution to the poor quality of instruction in the public school system.\n\nCongress has deplored the proliferation of half-baked products of the present school system. Sen. Edgardo Angara has proposed to add one curriculum year each to both elementary and high schools.\n\nWe believe that this is not the solution to the deteriorating educational system. The best we should do is return to the basics-the mastery of the three Rs- Reading, Writing and Arithmetic.\n\nOnly competent and committed teachers should be employed. They should be given higher pay and more incentives and benefits.\n\nNo amount of frequent seminars, workshops and conferences will help. Let us do away with unnecessary innovations learned by educators abroad which are not applicable to Philippine setting. There are so many “approaches” but as one President of the Philippine Normal University said, “We never reach.”\n\nAlso, the added years to the elementary and high school would mean more expenses on the part of the parents\n\nWrite an editorial using the following data:\n• Policies which the staff considers unfair to the student\n• Management of a school facility that needs to be improved such as library, canteen\n\nNote\n-begins with a brief explanation of a problem and states the need for change. A discussion of weaknesses, errors, or causes follows. Then a solution, suggestion, or alternative is offered, along with supportive reasons for the proposed action.\n\nWrite your editorial.',NULL,'[]','Write an editorial using the following data:\n\nSolution: Return to the Basics\nLengthening the elementary school up to Grade VII is not the solution to the poor quality of instruction in the public school system.\n\nCongress has deplored the proliferation of half-baked products of the present school system. Sen. Edgardo Angara has proposed to add one curriculum year each to both elementary and high schools.\n\nWe believe that this is not the solution to the deteriorating educational system. The best we should do is return to the basics-the mastery of the three Rs- Reading, Writing and Arithmetic.\n\nOnly competent and committed teachers should be employed. They should be given higher pay and more incentives and benefits.\n\nNo amount of frequent seminars, workshops and conferences will help. Let us do away with unnecessary innovations learned by educators abroad which are not applicable to Philippine setting. There are so many “approaches” but as one President of the Philippine Normal University said, “We never reach.”\n\nAlso, the added years to the elementary and high school would mean more expenses on the part of the parents\n\nWrite an editorial using the following data:\n• Policies which the staff considers unfair to the student\n• Management of a school facility that needs to be improved such as library, canteen\n\nNote\n-begins with a brief explanation of a problem and states the need for change. A discussion of weaknesses, errors, or causes follows. Then a solution, suggestion, or alternative is offered, along with supportive reasons for the proposed action.\n\nWrite your editorial.',NULL,10,10,'Write an editorial using the following data:\n\nSolution: Return to the Basics\nLengthening the elementary school up to Grade VII is not the solution to the poor quality of instruction in the public school system.\n\nCongress has deplored the proliferation of half-baked products of the present school system. Sen. Edgardo Angara has proposed to add one curriculum year each to both elementary and high schools.\n\nWe believe that this is not the solution to the deteriorating educational system. The best we should do is return to the basics-the mastery of the three Rs- Reading, Writing and Arithmetic.\n\nOnly competent and committed teachers should be employed. They should be given higher pay and more incentives and benefits.\n\nNo amount of frequent seminars, workshops and conferences will help. Let us do away with unnecessary innovations learned by educators abroad which are not applicable to Philippine setting. There are so many “approaches” but as one President of the Philippine Normal University said, “We never reach.”\n\nAlso, the added years to the elementary and high school would mean more expenses on the part of the parents\n\nWrite an editorial using the following data:\n• Policies which the staff considers unfair to the student\n• Management of a school facility that needs to be improved such as library, canteen\n\nNote\n-begins with a brief explanation of a problem and states the need for change. A discussion of weaknesses, errors, or causes follows. Then a solution, suggestion, or alter',1,1,'answer'),
(1304,584,NULL,'learn',1,'The Way of Most Desks','open','Topic: RH Bill · Stand: Favor','Topic: RH Bill · Stand: Favor\n\nThe Way of Most Desks\nStudent-judges who inspected every room in connection with “Operation Cleanliness” found out that most of the desks had scratches and/or ink spots. One can hardly write on them without a thick sheet of paper for a pad.\n\nOutside the classrooms, some desks are placed for the students to sit on. Students really take advantage of them. They sit on top of the desks and place their muddy shoes on the seats instead.\n\nWhen the 220 new desks for Pasig Line will be made available for the students’ use, will they end up the way most desks go?\n\nTopic: RH Bill Stand: Favor\n1. Cite first some major arguments against the issue. Cite sources/authorities/church leaders\n2. Then, build your case (points) from strong to strongest\n3. Title,\n4. Intro (thesis)/ main point /stand (1-2 par)\n5. Body (3-5 par.)\n6. Conclusion (1 par)\n\nTitle\n\nIntro (thesis)/ main point /stand (1-2 par)\n\nBody (3-5 par.)\n\nConclusion (1 par)',NULL,'[]','Topic: RH Bill · Stand: Favor\n\nThe Way of Most Desks\nStudent-judges who inspected every room in connection with “Operation Cleanliness” found out that most of the desks had scratches and/or ink spots. One can hardly write on them without a thick sheet of paper for a pad.\n\nOutside the classrooms, some desks are placed for the students to sit on. Students really take advantage of them. They sit on top of the desks and place their muddy shoes on the seats instead.\n\nWhen the 220 new desks for Pasig Line will be made available for the students’ use, will they end up the way most desks go?\n\nTopic: RH Bill Stand: Favor\n1. Cite first some major arguments against the issue. Cite sources/authorities/church leaders\n2. Then, build your case (points) from strong to strongest\n3. Title,\n4. Intro (thesis)/ main point /stand (1-2 par)\n5. Body (3-5 par.)\n6. Conclusion (1 par)\n\nTitle\n\nIntro (thesis)/ main point /stand (1-2 par)\n\nBody (3-5 par.)\n\nConclusion (1 par)',NULL,10,11,'Topic: RH Bill · Stand: Favor\n\nThe Way of Most Desks\nStudent-judges who inspected every room in connection with “Operation Cleanliness” found out that most of the desks had scratches and/or ink spots. One can hardly write on them without a thick sheet of paper for a pad.\n\nOutside the classrooms, some desks are placed for the students to sit on. Students really take advantage of them. They sit on top of the desks and place their muddy shoes on the seats instead.\n\nWhen the 220 new desks for Pasig Line will be made available for the students’ use, will they end up the way most desks go?\n\nTopic: RH Bill Stand: Favor\n1. Cite first some major arguments against the issue. Cite sources/authorities/church leaders\n2. Then, build your case (points) from strong to strongest\n3. Title,\n4. Intro (thesis)/ main point /stand (1-2 par)\n5. Body (3-5 par.)\n6. Conclusion (1 par)\n\nTitle\n\nIntro (thesis)/ main point /stand (1-2 par)\n\nBody (3-5 par.)\n\nConclusion (1 par)',1,1,'answer'),
(1305,585,NULL,'learn',1,'Writing a Sports Coverage','open','Instructions : Write a sports coverage using the following facts.','Instructions : Write a sports coverage using the following facts.\n\nThe Facts\nWhat: Municipal Meet volleyball eliminations\nWhere: Arayata Sports Complex, Tanza, Cavite\nWhen : September 9 at 2 p.m.\nBetween : Saint John School (SJS) and Saint Mark Academy (SMA)\nScores: 25-19, 23-25, 25-20, 25-20\n\nSignificance of the game:\nSJS on 2-0 lead; routed Saint Joseph Institute (SJI) in previous outing\n\nHeroes of the game:\nJerome Arbis and Noli Cruz combined for 17 points in last set\n\nQuotes from SJS coach Rody Santos:\n“My boys are pumped up from their victory over the Saint Joseph Institute Tossers.”\n“This is a good start as we are gunning for the championship like our basketball varsity posting a 6-0 winning streak.”\n\nWeather condition:\nHeavy rains and thunderstorms brought by Typhoon Maring but the Arayata gym was filled to the rafter.\n\nSpectators’ attitude:\nSJS crowd favorite, predominately SJS crowd chanting cheers; SJS red and white clad mascot dancing in front of SJS contingent\n\nReported injury:\nSMA’s Jin Rivas sprained ankle after a bad fall in the second set; carried out of playing court and brought to Family General Hospital\n\nHighlights of the game:\nSMA came on strong in second set with Joseph Rom’s mounting strong wallops\nRom’s heroics aided by Dan Sibal’s stopping ball on a service by SJS Richard Montes, also in second set.\n\nWrite your sports coverage using the facts.',NULL,'[]','Instructions : Write a sports coverage using the following facts.\n\nThe Facts\nWhat: Municipal Meet volleyball eliminations\nWhere: Arayata Sports Complex, Tanza, Cavite\nWhen : September 9 at 2 p.m.\nBetween : Saint John School (SJS) and Saint Mark Academy (SMA)\nScores: 25-19, 23-25, 25-20, 25-20\n\nSignificance of the game:\nSJS on 2-0 lead; routed Saint Joseph Institute (SJI) in previous outing\n\nHeroes of the game:\nJerome Arbis and Noli Cruz combined for 17 points in last set\n\nQuotes from SJS coach Rody Santos:\n“My boys are pumped up from their victory over the Saint Joseph Institute Tossers.”\n“This is a good start as we are gunning for the championship like our basketball varsity posting a 6-0 winning streak.”\n\nWeather condition:\nHeavy rains and thunderstorms brought by Typhoon Maring but the Arayata gym was filled to the rafter.\n\nSpectators’ attitude:\nSJS crowd favorite, predominately SJS crowd chanting cheers; SJS red and white clad mascot dancing in front of SJS contingent\n\nReported injury:\nSMA’s Jin Rivas sprained ankle after a bad fall in the second set; carried out of playing court and brought to Family General Hospital\n\nHighlights of the game:\nSMA came on strong in second set with Joseph Rom’s mounting strong wallops\nRom’s heroics aided by Dan Sibal’s stopping ball on a service by SJS Richard Montes, also in second set.\n\nWrite your sports coverage using the facts.',NULL,10,12,'Instructions : Write a sports coverage using the following facts.\n\nThe Facts\nWhat: Municipal Meet volleyball eliminations\nWhere: Arayata Sports Complex, Tanza, Cavite\nWhen : September 9 at 2 p.m.\nBetween : Saint John School (SJS) and Saint Mark Academy (SMA)\nScores: 25-19, 23-25, 25-20, 25-20\n\nSignificance of the game:\nSJS on 2-0 lead; routed Saint Joseph Institute (SJI) in previous outing\n\nHeroes of the game:\nJerome Arbis and Noli Cruz combined for 17 points in last set\n\nQuotes from SJS coach Rody Santos:\n“My boys are pumped up from their victory over the Saint Joseph Institute Tossers.”\n“This is a good start as we are gunning for the championship like our basketball varsity posting a 6-0 winning streak.”\n\nWeather condition:\nHeavy rains and thunderstorms brought by Typhoon Maring but the Arayata gym was filled to the rafter.\n\nSpectators’ attitude:\nSJS crowd favorite, predominately SJS crowd chanting cheers; SJS red and white clad mascot dancing in front of SJS contingent\n\nReported injury:\nSMA’s Jin Rivas sprained ankle after a bad fall in the second set; carried out of playing court and brought to Family General Hospital\n\nHighlights of the game:\nSMA came on strong in second set with Joseph Rom’s mounting strong wallops\nRom’s heroics aided by Dan Sibal’s stopping ball on a service by SJS Richard Montes, also in second set.\n\nWrite your sports coverage using the facts.',1,1,'answer'),
(1306,586,NULL,'learn',1,'Writing a Sports Game Story','open','Instructions: Based on the information presented below, write a sports game story of at least 350 words. Statements as presented on this fact sheet may be treated as direct quotations or paraphrases. You are writing for the Sept. 17 issue of the Hays High Guidon.','Instructions: Based on the information presented below, write a sports game story of at least 350 words. Statements as presented on this fact sheet may be treated as direct quotations or paraphrases. You are writing for the Sept. 17 issue of the Hays High Guidon.\n\nBackground\nThe Varsity Girls’ Volleyball team began season-Sept. 15- v. Salina South. Hays lost 15-7, 13- 15, 11-15.\nThe next game: Sept. 19, at Junction City.\n\nThe Team\nThe team includes six returners,\nThree returners made all-district last year\nTwo returners advanced to the all-region roster, Amie Roberts and Leah Claycamp, both juniors.\nRhonda Ames, a senior, is predicted to be one of the district’s star players.\n\nSignificant Plays\nFirst game\nAmie Roberts scored the first point of the game, an off-speed spike, after only 10 seconds of play. She scored five points in the first game.\nKenda Von Feldt scored six points. Leah Claycamp scored four points\nThe Salina South team scored seven points.\nSecond game\nRoberts scored six points and blocked 10 spikes.\nClaycamp scored five points. Her most significant play was a dive that set up a point. She also made two fouls.\nVon Feldt scored two points-one after she made a dig to another player who returned it to her for the score.\nHays hit the ball out of bounds four times. The officials called three thrown ball penalties on Hays.\nSalina South won this game.\nThird game\nRhonda Ames scored the first five points of this game. She was injured while making a double block and did not play the rest of the game.\nClaycamp scored four points, one after a spin serve that Salina could not even return.\nVon Feldt scored two points.\nThe score was tied almost the entire way through the game. The winning four points all came within one minute of play when the Hays team looked exhausted.\n\nQuotations\nLarry Schmidt, varsity coach - “The first game of the season didn’t go as well as I hoped. We didn’t accomplish the team goals that we set. We didn’t serve and pass as well as I hoped to.We need to work on our movement and getting into the right position when it comes to playing defense.”\nJessica Bird, sophomore - “I think we were playing very well. We need to work on our fundamentals. Overall, I think that we are playing great and the season should be successful.”\nRhonda Ames, senior - “I think if we work hard and use our potential, we’ll do all right in state. Salina just beat us by playing good clean volleyball.”\nLori Billinger, senior - “I need to work on my passing, but otherwise, everything is going great for me so far this year. I think that out team should talk more to each other when we are on the courts.”\n\nWrite your sports game story (at least 350 words).',NULL,'[]','Instructions: Based on the information presented below, write a sports game story of at least 350 words. Statements as presented on this fact sheet may be treated as direct quotations or paraphrases. You are writing for the Sept. 17 issue of the Hays High Guidon.\n\nBackground\nThe Varsity Girls’ Volleyball team began season-Sept. 15- v. Salina South. Hays lost 15-7, 13- 15, 11-15.\nThe next game: Sept. 19, at Junction City.\n\nThe Team\nThe team includes six returners,\nThree returners made all-district last year\nTwo returners advanced to the all-region roster, Amie Roberts and Leah Claycamp, both juniors.\nRhonda Ames, a senior, is predicted to be one of the district’s star players.\n\nSignificant Plays\nFirst game\nAmie Roberts scored the first point of the game, an off-speed spike, after only 10 seconds of play. She scored five points in the first game.\nKenda Von Feldt scored six points. Leah Claycamp scored four points\nThe Salina South team scored seven points.\nSecond game\nRoberts scored six points and blocked 10 spikes.\nClaycamp scored five points. Her most significant play was a dive that set up a point. She also made two fouls.\nVon Feldt scored two points-one after she made a dig to another player who returned it to her for the score.\nHays hit the ball out of bounds four times. The officials called three thrown ball penalties on Hays.\nSalina South won this game.\nThird game\nRhonda Ames scored the first five points of this game. She was injured while making a double block and did not play the rest of the game.\nClaycamp scored four points, one after a spin serve that Salina could not even return.\nVon Feldt scored two points.\nThe score was tied almost the entire way through the game. The winning four points all came within one minute of play when the Hays team looked exhausted.\n\nQuotations\nLarry Schmidt, varsity coach - “The first game of the season didn’t go as well as I hoped. We didn’t accomplish the team goals that we set. We didn’t serve and pass as well as I hoped to.We need to work on our movement and getting into the right position when it comes to playing defense.”\nJessica Bird, sophomore - “I think we were playing very well. We need to work on our fundamentals. Overall, I think that we are playing great and the season should be successful.”\nRhonda Ames, senior - “I think if we work hard and use our potential, we’ll do all right in state. Salina just beat us by playing good clean volleyball.”\nLori Billinger, senior - “I need to work on my passing, but otherwise, everything is going great for me so far this year. I think that out team should talk more to each other when we are on the courts.”\n\nWrite your sports game story (at least 350 words).',NULL,10,13,'Instructions: Based on the information presented below, write a sports game story of at least 350 words. Statements as presented on this fact sheet may be treated as direct quotations or paraphrases. You are writing for the Sept. 17 issue of the Hays High Guidon.\n\nBackground\nThe Varsity Girls’ Volleyball team began season-Sept. 15- v. Salina South. Hays lost 15-7, 13- 15, 11-15.\nThe next game: Sept. 19, at Junction City.\n\nThe Team\nThe team includes six returners,\nThree returners made all-district last year\nTwo returners advanced to the all-region roster, Amie Roberts and Leah Claycamp, both juniors.\nRhonda Ames, a senior, is predicted to be one of the district’s star players.\n\nSignificant Plays\nFirst game\nAmie Roberts scored the first point of the game, an off-speed spike, after only 10 seconds of play. She scored five points in the first game.\nKenda Von Feldt scored six points. Leah Claycamp scored four points\nThe Salina South team scored seven points.\nSecond game\nRoberts scored six points and blocked 10 spikes.\nClaycamp scored five points. Her most significant play was a dive that set up a point. She also made two fouls.\nVon Feldt scored two points-one after she made a dig to another player who returned it to her for the score.\nHays hit the ball out of bounds four times. The officials called three thrown ball penalties on Hays.\nSalina South won this game.\nThird game\nRhonda Ames scored the first five points of this game. She was injured while making a double block and did no',1,1,'answer'),
(1307,587,NULL,'learn',1,'Summarizing a Story','open','Instructions: From the following reporter’s notes, prepare a summary story of at least 350 words for the February 18 issue of the Central High School News.','Instructions: From the following reporter’s notes, prepare a summary story of at least 350 words for the February 18 issue of the Central High School News.\n\nReporter’s Notes\nFebruary 5: Basketball game between Central High Bears and East High Wildcats in East High gymnasium, won by Central 44-36. John Gunnison of Central was high point man with 15 points. East was always within 5 points of Central until the last few minutes of the game. Game was marred by many fouls on both sides.\n\nGunnison: “They played us tough and stayed with us throughout, but we were able to get by them. They are a scrappy team. . .I don’t remember having this much trouble with them before. Still, we were able to pull it out.”\n\nCoach Turner: “Gunnison did the job tonight. When he is on his game, he can do some really good things. Tonight was his night.”\n\nFebruary 12: Central High v. Midvale Astronauts, at Central. Won by Midvale 54-52. High scoring game, with Central ahead at half time, 32-22, and keeping lead until last 10 minutes. Joe Small was hero of game for Midvale, making many scores in last part of game and encouraging his teammates. Guninson and Jimmy Smith of Central were outstanding, but team just wasn’t good enough, according to Coach Ed Turner.\n\nTurner: “A game like this one breaks your heart. We seemed to be on as far as our scoring was concerned. We scored more points than in any other game this year. Still, we have to play defense. Without defense, we are sitting ducks.”\n\nFebruary 19: Central High v. West High Gophers at West. West hasn’t won a game this year but has put up a good fight every time and almost defeated Midvale. Gunnison twisted his leg in practice early this week but should be able to play.\n\nFebruary 26: Central High v. River City Scorpions at Central. River City hasn’t been defeated yet but meets Midvale Feb. 19 in a game that may decide league championship. river City has excellent defensive team and a high-scoring center, Jack Sorrenson.\n\nTurner: “These two games are both going to be a challenge. People think that West is going to be an easy win, but they play tough despite their record. River City has been great all year. They just seem to be in a groove.\n\nMaybe we can trip them up, but we’ll see. A lot of it depends on how strong Gunnison is this week. He is taking it one day at a time, so hopefully things will pan out.”\n\nWrite your summary story (at least 350 words).',NULL,'[]','Instructions: From the following reporter’s notes, prepare a summary story of at least 350 words for the February 18 issue of the Central High School News.\n\nReporter’s Notes\nFebruary 5: Basketball game between Central High Bears and East High Wildcats in East High gymnasium, won by Central 44-36. John Gunnison of Central was high point man with 15 points. East was always within 5 points of Central until the last few minutes of the game. Game was marred by many fouls on both sides.\n\nGunnison: “They played us tough and stayed with us throughout, but we were able to get by them. They are a scrappy team. . .I don’t remember having this much trouble with them before. Still, we were able to pull it out.”\n\nCoach Turner: “Gunnison did the job tonight. When he is on his game, he can do some really good things. Tonight was his night.”\n\nFebruary 12: Central High v. Midvale Astronauts, at Central. Won by Midvale 54-52. High scoring game, with Central ahead at half time, 32-22, and keeping lead until last 10 minutes. Joe Small was hero of game for Midvale, making many scores in last part of game and encouraging his teammates. Guninson and Jimmy Smith of Central were outstanding, but team just wasn’t good enough, according to Coach Ed Turner.\n\nTurner: “A game like this one breaks your heart. We seemed to be on as far as our scoring was concerned. We scored more points than in any other game this year. Still, we have to play defense. Without defense, we are sitting ducks.”\n\nFebruary 19: Central High v. West High Gophers at West. West hasn’t won a game this year but has put up a good fight every time and almost defeated Midvale. Gunnison twisted his leg in practice early this week but should be able to play.\n\nFebruary 26: Central High v. River City Scorpions at Central. River City hasn’t been defeated yet but meets Midvale Feb. 19 in a game that may decide league championship. river City has excellent defensive team and a high-scoring center, Jack Sorrenson.\n\nTurner: “These two games are both going to be a challenge. People think that West is going to be an easy win, but they play tough despite their record. River City has been great all year. They just seem to be in a groove.\n\nMaybe we can trip them up, but we’ll see. A lot of it depends on how strong Gunnison is this week. He is taking it one day at a time, so hopefully things will pan out.”\n\nWrite your summary story (at least 350 words).',NULL,10,15,'Instructions: From the following reporter’s notes, prepare a summary story of at least 350 words for the February 18 issue of the Central High School News.\n\nReporter’s Notes\nFebruary 5: Basketball game between Central High Bears and East High Wildcats in East High gymnasium, won by Central 44-36. John Gunnison of Central was high point man with 15 points. East was always within 5 points of Central until the last few minutes of the game. Game was marred by many fouls on both sides.\n\nGunnison: “They played us tough and stayed with us throughout, but we were able to get by them. They are a scrappy team. . .I don’t remember having this much trouble with them before. Still, we were able to pull it out.”\n\nCoach Turner: “Gunnison did the job tonight. When he is on his game, he can do some really good things. Tonight was his night.”\n\nFebruary 12: Central High v. Midvale Astronauts, at Central. Won by Midvale 54-52. High scoring game, with Central ahead at half time, 32-22, and keeping lead until last 10 minutes. Joe Small was hero of game for Midvale, making many scores in last part of game and encouraging his teammates. Guninson and Jimmy Smith of Central were outstanding, but team just wasn’t good enough, according to Coach Ed Turner.\n\nTurner: “A game like this one breaks your heart. We seemed to be on as far as our scoring was concerned. We scored more points than in any other game this year. Still, we have to play defense. Without defense, we are sitting ducks.”\n\nFebruary 19: Cen',1,1,'answer'),
