. Some classmates assumed he was shy, while others simply forgot he was there. One afternoon, the class was assigned a group science project. Adrian\'s group struggled to organize their ideas. They argued over the experiment and could not agree on who would present. After listening quietly, Adrian finally raised his hand. \"May I suggest something?\" he asked. His classmates nodded. He explained how they could divide the work fairly. He also showed them a simple experiment he had learned in his previous school. Everyone agreed to follow his plan. Presentation day arrived. Their experiment worked perfectly, and the class applauded their teamwork. Their teacher praised not only the project but also the way everyone cooperated. During recess, Adrian no longer sat alone. His classmates invited him to eat with them, asking questions about his old school and sharing stories about their own experiences. Adrian realized that sometimes people are not noticed because they remain silent. Yet kindness, preparation, and willingness to help often speak louder than words. From that day forward, the last seat in the classroom became known as the place where a quiet leader once sat.',20,21,'21 \n \nPART II \nORIGINAL FLUENCY STORIES \n \nActivity 11 \nThe Last Seat in the Classroom \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nThe last seat in Room Seven was always empty until the second week of classes. \nOn Monday morning, a quiet boy named Adrian entered the room carrying an \nold backpack and a neatly folded notebook. He smiled politely as the teacher \nintroduced him to the class. \nSince all the front seats were already occupied, Adrian sat at the back. During \nlessons, he listened carefully and wrote detailed notes, but he rarely spoke. Some \nclassmates assumed he was shy, while others simply forgot he was there. \nOne afternoon, the class was assigned a group science project. Adrian\'s group \nstruggled to organize their ideas. They argued over the experiment and could not