(1333,613,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The New Student','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The New Student\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 227\n\nLena transferred to a new school halfway through the school year. Everything felt unfamiliar—from the classrooms to the faces she passed along the hallways. Although her teachers were welcoming, she found it difficult to begin conversations with her classmates.\n\nDuring lunch breaks, Lena usually sat alone beneath a large acacia tree near the school garden. She spent her time reading novels while other students laughed and played nearby.\n\nOne rainy afternoon, the school announced a clean-up drive after classes. Every section was assigned an area to organize. Lena quietly joined her classmates in clearing fallen leaves and arranging flower pots.\n\nAs they worked together, one of her classmates noticed that Lena knew the names of many different plants. Curious, they asked how she learned so much.\n\n\"My grandmother loves gardening,\" Lena explained. \"She taught me how to care for flowers and vegetables.\"\n\nSoon everyone was asking questions. Lena demonstrated how to prepare seedlings and explained why certain plants needed more sunlight than others.\n\nThe following week, their adviser appointed Lena as one of the leaders of the class gardening project. Her classmates eagerly worked beside her, planting vegetables and flowering shrubs that later brightened the school grounds.\n\nLena realized that friendships often begin with shared work rather than shared words. By simply offering her knowledge and helping others, she found a place where she truly belonged.',NULL,'[]','The New Student. Lena transferred to a new school halfway through the school year. Everything felt unfamiliar—from the classrooms to the faces she passed along the hallways. Although her teachers were welcoming, she found it difficult to begin conversations with her classmates. During lunch breaks, Lena usually sat alone beneath a large acacia tree near the school garden. She spent her time reading novels while other students laughed and played nearby. One rainy afternoon, the school announced a clean-up drive after classes. Every section was assigned an area to organize. Lena quietly joined her classmates in clearing fallen leaves and arranging flower pots. As they worked together, one of her classmates noticed that Lena knew the names of many different plants. Curious, they asked how she learned so much. \"My grandmother loves gardening,\" Lena explained. \"She taught me how to care for flowers and vegetables.\" Soon everyone was asking questions. Lena demonstrated how to prepare seedlings and explained why certain plants needed more sunlight than others. The following week, their adviser appointed Lena as one of the leaders of the class gardening project. Her classmates eagerly worked beside her, planting vegetables and flowering shrubs that later brightened the school grounds. Lena realized that friendships often begin with shared work rather than shared words. By simply offering her knowledge and helping others, she found a place where she truly belonged.','Lena transferred to a new school halfway through the school year. Everything felt unfamiliar—from the classrooms to the faces she passed along the hallways. Although her teachers were welcoming, she found it difficult to begin conversations with her classmates. During lunch breaks, Lena usually sat alone beneath a large acacia tree near the school garden. She spent her time reading novels while other students laughed and played nearby. One rainy afternoon, the school announced a clean-up drive after classes. Every section was assigned an area to organize. Lena quietly joined her classmates in clearing fallen leaves and arranging flower pots. As they worked together, one of her classmates noticed that Lena knew the 