o the Guidance Office. The guidance counselor carefully \nexamined the identification card and discovered that the wallet belonged to the \nfather of a Grade 8 learner. \nWithin thirty minutes, the grateful parent arrived. \n\"I thought I had lost this forever,\" he said with relief. \"This contains my salary \nfor the week.\" \nHe offered Claire a reward, but she politely refused. \n\"My reward is knowing everything was returned safely,\" she replied. \nDuring the next flag ceremony, the school recognized Claire for her honesty. \nHer cla',1,1,'answer'),
(1340,620,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: Race Against Time','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: Race Against Time\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nOnly two days remained before the Regional Robotics Competition, and Team Innovate was still struggling with their robot\'s navigation program.\n\nEvery afternoon after classes, the four members met inside the computer laboratory. While other students were already heading home, they continued testing sensors, correcting computer code, and redesigning small mechanical parts.\n\nSometimes the robot stopped without warning. At other times, it turned in the wrong direction or failed to recognize obstacles.\n\n\"We\'re running out of time,\" Ethan sighed.\n\nTheir adviser smiled calmly.\n\n\"Success rarely belongs to those who never fail,\" she said. \"It belongs to those who learn quickly from every failure.\"\n\nThe group divided the remaining tasks. Ethan corrected the programming, Mia recalibrated the sensors, Joshua improved the wheels, while Hannah documented every modification.\n\nLate Friday evening, they performed one final test.\n\nThe robot moved smoothly through every checkpoint without making a single mistake.\n\nAlthough the competition was challenging, Team Innovate proudly earned second place.\n\nAs they packed their equipment, Ethan realized that winning a medal was rewarding, but learning perseverance, teamwork, and problem-solving was an even greater achievement.',NULL,'[]','Race Against Time. Only two days remained before the Regional Robotics Competition, and Team Innovate was still struggling with their robot\'s navigation program. Every afternoon after classes, the four members met inside the computer laboratory. While other students were already heading home, they continued testing sensors, correcting computer code, and redesigning small mechanical parts. Sometimes the robot stopped without warning. At other times, it turned in the wrong direction or failed to recognize obstacles. \"We\'re running out of time,\" Ethan sighed. Their adviser smiled calmly. \"Success rarely belongs to those who never fail,\" she said. \"It belongs to those who learn quickly from every failure.\" The group divided the remaining tasks. Ethan corrected the programming, Mia recalibrated the sensors, Joshua improved the wheels, while Hannah documented every modification. Late Friday evening, they performed one final test. The robot moved smoothly through every checkpoint without making a single mistake. Although the competition was challenging, Team Innovate proudly earned second place. As they packed their equipment, Ethan realized that winning a medal was rewarding, but learning perseverance, teamwork, and problem-solving was an even greater achievement.','Only two days remained before the Regional Robotics Competition, and Team Innovate was still struggling with their robot\'s navigation program. Every afternoon after classes, the four members met inside the computer laboratory. While other students were already heading home, they continued testing sensors, correcting computer code, and redesigning small mechanical parts. Sometimes the robot stopped without warning. At other times, it turned in the wrong direction or failed to recognize obstacles. \"We\'re running out of time,\" Ethan sighed. Their adviser smiled calmly. \"Success rarely belongs to those who never fail,\" she said. \"It belongs to those who learn quickly from every failure.\" The group divided the remaining tasks. Ethan corrected the programming, Mia recalibrated the sensors, Joshua improved the wheels, while Hannah documented every modification. Late Friday evening, they performed one final test. The robot moved smoothly through every checkpoint without making a single mistake. Although the competition was challenging, Team Innovate proudly earned second place. As they packed their equipment, Ethan realized that winning a medal was rewarding, but learning perseverance, teamwork, and problem-solving was an even greater achievement.',20,37,'37 \n \n \n \nActivity 19 \nRace Against Time \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nOnly two days remained before the Regional Robotics Competition, and Team \nInnovate was still struggling with their robot\'s navigation program. \nEvery afternoon after classes, the four members met inside the computer \nlaboratory. While other students were already heading home, they continued \ntesting sensors, correcting computer code, and redesigning small mechanical \nparts. \nSometimes the robot stopped without warning. At other times, it turned in the \nwrong direction or failed to recognize obstacles. \n\"We\'re running out of time,\" Ethan sighed. \nTheir adviser smiled calmly. \n\"Success rarely belongs to those who never fail,\" she said. \"It belongs to those \nwho learn quickly from every failure.\" \nThe group divided the remaining tasks. Ethan corrected the programming, Mia \nrecalibrated the sensors, Joshua improved the wheels, while Hannah \ndocumented every modification. \nLate Friday evening, they performed one final test. \nThe robot moved smoothly through every checkpoint without making a single \nmistake. \nAlthough the competition was challenging, Team Innovate proudly earned \nsecond place. \nAs they packed their equipment, Ethan realized that winning a medal was \nrewarding, but learning perseverance, teamwork, and problem-solving was an \neven greater achievement.',1,1,'answer'),
(1341,621,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The School Garden','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The School Garden\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nAt the beginning of the school year, the vacant lot beside the library looked dry and neglected. Weeds covered the ground, and very few students paid attention to it.\n\nTheir Science teacher proposed a challenge.\n\n\"What if we transformed this empty space into a vegetable garden?\"\n\nAt first, many students doubted the idea.\n\nThe soil looked rocky, and the weather had been unusually hot.\n\nNevertheless, the class accepted the challenge.\n\nEvery Wednesday morning, they watered seedlings, removed weeds, added compost, and measured plant growth.\n\nSome vegetables grew quickly, while others struggled.\n\nThe students carefully observed how sunlight, rainfall, and soil nutrients affected each plant.\n\nAfter three months, the once-empty lot had become a colorful garden filled with tomatoes, eggplants, pechay, okra, and herbs.\n\nThe harvested vegetables were donated to the school\'s feeding program.\n\nDuring the recognition ceremony, the principal congratulated the class.\n\n\"You did not simply grow vegetables,\" she said.\n\n\"You cultivated responsibility, patience, and teamwork.\"\n\nThe students proudly realized that meaningful change often begins with small acts repeated consistently.',NULL,'[]','The School Garden. At the beginning of the school year, the vacant lot beside the library looked dry and neglected. Weeds covered the ground, and very few students paid attention to it. Their Science teacher proposed a challenge. \"What if we transformed this empty space into a vegetable garden?\" At first, many students doubted the idea. The soil looked rocky, and the weather had been unusually hot. Nevertheless, the class accepted the challenge. Every Wednesday morning, they watered seedlings, removed weeds, added compost, and measured plant growth. Some vegetables grew quickly, while others struggled. The students carefully observed how sunlight, rainfall, and soil nutrients affected each plant. After three months, the once-empty lot had become a colorful garden filled with tomatoes, eggplants, pechay, okra, and herbs. The harvested vegetables were donated to the school\'s feeding program. During the recognition ceremony, the principal congratulated the class. \"You did not simply grow vegetables,\" she said. \"You cultivated responsibility, patience, and teamwork.\" The students proudly realized that meaningful change often begins with small acts repeated consistently.','At the beginning of the school year, the vacant lot beside the library looked dry and neglected. Weeds covered the ground, and very few students paid attention to it. Their Science teacher proposed a challenge. \"What if we transformed this empty space into a vegetable garden?\" At first, many students doubted the idea. The soil looked rocky, and the weather had been unusually hot. Nevertheless, the class accepted the challenge. Every Wednesday morning, they watered seedlings, removed weeds, added compost, and measured plant growth. Some vegetables grew quickly, while others struggled. The students carefully observed how sunlight, rainfall, and soil nutrients affected each plant. After three months, the once-empty lot had become a colorful garden filled with tomatoes, eggplants, pechay, okra, and herbs. The harvested vegetables were donated to the school\'s feeding program. During the recognition ceremony, the principal congratulated the class. \"You did not simply grow vegetables,\" she said. \"You cultivated responsibility, patience, and teamwork.\" The students proudly realized that meaningful change often begins with small acts repeated consistently.',20,39,'39 \n \nActivity 20 \nThe School Garden \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nAt the beginning of the school year, the vacant lot beside the library looked dry \nand neglected. Weeds covered the ground, and very few students paid attention \nto it. \nTheir Science teacher proposed a challenge. \n\"What if we transformed this empty space into a vegetable garden?\" \nAt first, many students doubted the idea. \nThe soil looked rocky, and the weather had been unusually hot. \nNevertheless, the class accepted the challenge. \nEvery Wednesday morning, they watered seedlings, removed weeds, added \ncompost, and measured plant growth. \nSome vegetables grew quickly, while others struggled. \nThe students carefully observed how sunlight, rainfall, and soil nutrients \naffected each plant. \nAfter three months, the once-empty lot had become a colorful garden filled with \ntomatoes, eggplants, pechay, okra, and herbs. \nThe harvested vegetables were donated to the school\'s feeding program. \nDuring the recognition ceremony, the principal congratulated the class. \n\"You did not simply grow vegetables,\" she said. \n\"You cultivated responsibility, patience, and teamwork.\" \nThe students proudly realized that meaningful change often begins with small \nacts repeated consistently.',1,1,'answer'),
(1342,622,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Community Hero','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The Community Hero\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nEvery morning before sunrise, Mang Ernesto could already be seen sweeping the streets surrounding the public market.\n\nMany people knew him simply as the town\'s sanitation worker. Few realized that he had been serving the community for more than twenty-five years.\n\nDespite the hot weather and occasional heavy rain, he reported for work every day with a smile.\n\nOne Saturday, Grade 8 learners interviewed him for their Araling Panlipunan project about community helpers.\n\n\"What motivates you to continue this work?\" one student asked.\n\nMang Ernesto smiled before answering.\n\n\"When people wake up and find clean streets, they can begin their day safely and comfortably. Knowing that my work helps others is enough reason to continue.\"\n\nThe students listened attentively.\n\nThey realized that heroism is not always found in newspapers or television.\n\nSometimes heroes quietly perform ordinary tasks with extraordinary dedication.\n\nInspired by the interview, the class organized a monthly clean-up campaign within their barangay.\n\nMang Ernesto happily joined them.\n\nBy working together, the learners discovered that every citizen has the power to improve the community through simple acts of service.',NULL,'[]','The Community Hero. Every morning before sunrise, Mang Ernesto could already be seen sweeping the streets surrounding the public market. Many people knew him simply as the town\'s sanitation worker. Few realized that he had been serving the community for more than twenty-five years. Despite the hot weather and occasional heavy rain, he reported for work every day with a smile. One Saturday, Grade 8 learners interviewed him for their Araling Panlipunan project about community helpers. \"What motivates you to continue this work?\" one student asked. Mang Ernesto smiled before answering. \"When people wake up and find clean streets, they can begin their day safely and comfortably. Knowing that my work helps others is enough reason to continue.\" The students listened attentively. They realized that heroism is not always found in newspapers or television. Sometimes heroes quietly perform ordinary tasks with extraordinary dedication. Inspired by the interview, the class organized a monthly clean-up campaign within their barangay. Mang Ernesto happily joined them. By working together, the learners discovered that every citizen has the power to improve the community through simple acts of service.','Every morning before sunrise, Mang Ernesto could already be seen sweeping the streets surrounding the public market. Many people knew him simply as the town\'s sanitation worker. Few realized that he had been serving the community for more than twenty-five years. Despite the hot weather and occasional heavy rain, he reported for work every day with a smile. One Saturday, Grade 8 learners interviewed him for their Araling Panlipunan project about community helpers. \"What motivates you to continue this work?\" one student asked. Mang Ernesto smiled before answering. \"When people wake up and find clean streets, they can begin their day safely and comfortably. Knowing that my work helps others is enough reason to continue.\" The students listened attentively. They realized that heroism is not always found in newspapers or television. Sometimes heroes quietly perform ordinary tasks with extraordinary dedication. Inspired by the interview, the class organized a monthly clean-up campaign within their barangay. Mang Ernesto happily joined them. By working together, the learners discovered that every citizen has the power to improve the community through simple acts of service.',20,41,'41 \n \nActivity 21 \nThe Community Hero \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nEvery morning before sunrise, Mang Ernesto could already be seen sweeping \nthe streets surrounding the public market. \nMany people knew him simply as the town\'s sanitation worker. Few realized \nthat he had been serving the community for more than twenty-five years. \nDespite the hot weather and occasional heavy rain, he reported for work every \nday with a smile. \nOne Saturday, Grade 8 learners interviewed him for their Araling Panlipunan \nproject about community helpers. \n\"What motivates you to continue this work?\" one student asked. \nMang Ernesto smiled before answering. \n\"When people wake up and find clean streets, they can begin their day safely \nand comfortably. Knowing that my work helps others is enough reason to \ncontinue.\" \nThe students listened attentively. \nThey realized that heroism is not always found in newspapers or television. \nSometimes heroes quietly perform ordinary tasks with extraordinary \ndedication. \nInspired by the interview, the class organized a monthly clean-up campaign \nwithin their barangay. \nMang Ernesto happily joined them. \nBy working together, the learners discovered that every citizen has the power to \nimprove the community through simple acts of service.',1,1,'answer'),
(1343,623,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: Beyond the Screen','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: Beyond the Screen\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 168\n\nLike many teenagers, Daniel enjoyed spending hours playing online games and watching videos after school.\n\nAt first, he believed there was nothing wrong with it.\n\nHowever, he gradually noticed several changes.\n\nHe stayed up late every night.\n\nHe arrived at school feeling sleepy.\n\nHis assignments became incomplete, and his quiz scores slowly declined.\n\nOne afternoon, his older sister invited him to join a weekend hiking activity organized by their youth group.\n\nDaniel hesitated.\n\nHe almost refused.\n\nStill, he decided to give it a try.\n\nThe hike led them through forest trails, rivers, and scenic viewpoints.\n\nWithout realizing it, Daniel spent an entire day without checking his phone.\n\nInstead, he laughed with new friends, learned about native trees, and appreciated the beauty of nature.\n\nThat evening he reflected on his experience.\n\nTechnology is useful, he realized, but balance is essential.\n\nSince then, Daniel scheduled daily screen time while making room for reading, exercise, family conversations, and outdoor activities.\n\nLife beyond the screen offered experiences no device could fully replace.',NULL,'[]','Beyond the Screen. Like many teenagers, Daniel enjoyed spending hours playing online games and watching videos after school. At first, he believed there was nothing wrong with it. However, he gradually noticed several changes. He stayed up late every night. He arrived at school feeling sleepy. His assignments became incomplete, and his quiz scores slowly declined. One afternoon, his older sister invited him to join a weekend hiking activity organized by their youth group. Daniel hesitated. He almost refused. Still, he decided to give it a try. The hike led them through forest trails, rivers, and scenic viewpoints. Without realizing it, Daniel spent an entire day without checking his phone. Instead, he laughed with new friends, learned about native trees, and appreciated the beauty of nature. That evening he reflected on his experience. Technology is useful, he realized, but balance is essential. Since then, Daniel scheduled daily screen time while making room for reading, exercise, family conversations, and outdoor activities. Life beyond the screen offered experiences no device could fully replace.','Like many teenagers, Daniel enjoyed spending hours playing online games and watching videos after school. At first, he believed there was nothing wrong with it. However, he gradually noticed several changes. He stayed up late every night. He arrived at school feeling sleepy. His assignments became incomplete, and his quiz scores slowly declined. One afternoon, his older sister invited him to join a weekend hiking activity organized by their youth group. Daniel hesitated. He almost refused. Still, he decided to give it a try. The hike led them through forest trails, rivers, and scenic viewpoints. Without realizing it, Daniel spent an entire day without checking his phone. Instead, he laughed with new friends, learned about native trees, and appreciated the beauty of nature. That evening he reflected on his experience. Technology is useful, he realized, but balance is essential. Since then, Daniel scheduled daily screen time while making room for reading, exercise, family conversations, and outdoor activities. Life beyond the screen offered experiences no device could fully replace.',20,43,'43 \n \n \nActivity 22 \nBeyond the Screen \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nLike many teenagers, Daniel enjoyed spending hours playing online games and \nwatching videos after school. \nAt first, he believed there was nothing wrong with it. \nHowever, he gradually noticed several changes. \nHe stayed up late every night. \nHe arrived at school feeling sleepy. \nHis assignments became incomplete, and his quiz scores slowly declined. \nOne afternoon, his older sister invited him to join a weekend hiking activity \norganized by their youth group. \nDaniel hesitated. \nHe almost refused. \nStill, he decided to give it a try. \nThe hike led them through forest trails, rivers, and scenic viewpoints. \nWithout realizing it, Daniel spent an entire day without checking his phone. \nInstead, he laughed with new friends, learned about native trees, and \nappreciated the beauty of nature. \nThat evening he reflected on his experience. \nTechnology is useful, he realized, but balance is essential. \nSince then, Daniel scheduled daily screen time while making room for reading, \nexercise, family conversations, and outdoor activities. \nLife beyond the screen offered experiences no device could fully replace.',1,1,'answer'),
(1344,624,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Basketball Captain','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The Basketball Captain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nThe championship game was only minutes away.\n\nEveryone expected Team Falcons to win because they had remained undefeated throughout the season.\n\nAs captain, Marcus felt tremendous pressure.\n\nWhen the first quarter ended, however, his team trailed by twelve points.\n\nSome players began blaming one another for missed shots and defensive mistakes.\n\nDuring the timeout, Marcus gathered everyone into a circle.\n\n\"We cannot change the first quarter,\" he said calmly.\n\n\"But we can decide how we play the next three.\"\n\nHis teammates nodded.\n\nInstead of focusing on individual scoring, they emphasized teamwork.\n\nThey passed the ball more carefully, communicated during defense, and encouraged one another after every play.\n\nLittle by little, the gap narrowed.\n\nWith only fifteen seconds remaining, the score was tied.\n\nMarcus passed the ball to the newest member of the team, who made the winning basket.\n\nThe crowd erupted with applause.\n\nAfter the game, reporters asked Marcus why he did not take the final shot himself.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"A captain\'s greatest victory is helping the entire team succeed.\"',NULL,'[]','The Basketball Captain. The championship game was only minutes away. Everyone expected Team Falcons to win because they had remained undefeated throughout the season. As captain, Marcus felt tremendous pressure. When the first quarter ended, however, his team trailed by twelve points. Some players began blaming one another for missed shots and defensive mistakes. During the timeout, Marcus gathered everyone into a circle. \"We cannot change the first quarter,\" he said calmly. \"But we can decide how we play the next three.\" His teammates nodded. Instead of focusing on individual scoring, they emphasized teamwork. They passed the ball more carefully, communicated during defense, and encouraged one another after every play. Little by little, the gap narrowed. With only fifteen seconds remaining, the score was tied. Marcus passed the ball to the newest member of the team, who made the winning basket. The crowd erupted with applause. After the game, reporters asked Marcus why he did not take the final shot himself. He smiled. \"A captain\'s greatest victory is helping the entire team succeed.\"','The championship game was only minutes away. Everyone expected Team Falcons to win because they had remained undefeated throughout the season. As captain, Marcus felt tremendous pressure. When the first quarter ended, however, his team trailed by twelve points. Some players began blaming one another for missed shots and defensive mistakes. During the timeout, Marcus gathered everyone into a circle. \"We cannot change the first quarter,\" he said calmly. \"But we can decide how we play the next three.\" His teammates nodded. Instead of focusing on individual scoring, they emphasized teamwork. They passed the ball more carefully, communicated during defense, and encouraged one another after every play. Little by little, the gap narrowed. With only fifteen seconds remaining, the score was tied. Marcus passed the ball to the newest member of the team, who made the winning basket. The crowd erupted with applause. After the game, reporters asked Marcus why he did not take the final shot himself. He smiled. \"A captain\'s greatest victory is helping the entire team succeed.\"',20,45,'45 \n \nActivity 23 \nThe Basketball Captain \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nThe championship game was only minutes away. \nEveryone expected Team Falcons to win because they had remained undefeated \nthroughout the season. \nAs captain, Marcus felt tremendous pressure. \nWhen the first quarter ended, however, his team trailed by twelve points. \nSome players began blaming one another for missed shots and defensive \nmistakes. \nDuring the timeout, Marcus gathered everyone into a circle. \n\"We cannot change the first quarter,\" he said calmly. \n\"But we can decide how we play the next three.\" \nHis teammates nodded. \nInstead of focusing on individual scoring, they emphasized teamwork. \nThey passed the ball more carefully, communicated during defense, and \nencouraged one another after every play. \nLittle by little, the gap narrowed. \nWith only fifteen seconds remaining, the score was tied. \nMarcus passed the ball to the newest member of the team, who made the \nwinning basket. \nThe crowd erupted with applause. \nAfter the game, reporters asked Marcus why he did not take the final shot \nhimself. \nHe smiled. \n\"A captain\'s greatest victory is helping the entire team succeed.\"',1,1,'answer'),
(1345,625,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Secret of the Old Mango Tree','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The Secret of the Old Mango Tree\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 269\n\nThe oldest tree inside San Isidro National High School stood quietly behind the library. Its thick trunk and wide branches had provided shade for generations of learners. Teachers often joked that the tree had witnessed more examinations, graduations, and celebrations than anyone else in the school.\n\nOne afternoon, while researching for a local history project, three Grade 8 learners noticed a small metal box partially hidden beneath one of the tree\'s large roots. Curious but careful, they informed their adviser before touching it.\n\nTogether, they carefully removed the box from the soil. Inside they found several yellowed photographs, handwritten letters, and an old class program dated more than forty years earlier.\n\nThe school principal contacted retired teachers and alumni to help identify the people shown in the photographs. During the following week, former students visited the school and shared stories about their experiences as learners many years ago.\n\nThey spoke about studying without computers, participating in school programs under the same mango tree, and helping build classrooms through community volunteer work.\n\nThe learners listened with great interest.\n\nThey realized that although school buildings, technology, and teaching methods continue to change, the values of respect, perseverance, and service remain constant through every generation.\n\nInspired by what they had learned, the Student Government organized a Heritage Corner inside the library. The photographs, letters, and stories were carefully preserved so future learners could appreciate the rich history of their school.\n\nWhenever students gathered beneath the old mango tree, they no longer saw only its strong branches and broad leaves.\n\nThey saw living proof that every generation leaves behind lessons for the next.',NULL,'[]','The Secret of the Old Mango Tree. The oldest tree inside San Isidro National High School stood quietly behind the library. Its thick trunk and wide branches had provided shade for generations of learners. Teachers often joked that the tree had witnessed more examinations, graduations, and celebrations than anyone else in the school. One afternoon, while researching for a local history project, three Grade 8 learners noticed a small metal box partially hidden beneath one of the tree\'s large roots. Curious but careful, they informed their adviser before touching it. Together, they carefully removed the box from the soil. Inside they found several yellowed photographs, handwritten letters, and an old class program dated more than forty years earlier. The school principal contacted retired teachers and alumni to help identify the people shown in the photographs. During the following week, former students visited the school and shared stories about their experiences as learners many years ago. They spoke about studying without computers, participating in school programs under the same mango tree, and helping build classrooms through community volunteer work. The learners listened with great interest. They realized that although school buildings, technology, and teaching methods continue to change, the values of respect, perseverance, and service remain constant through every generation. Inspired by what they had learned, the Student Government organized a Heritage Corner inside the library. The photographs, letters, and stories were carefully preserved so future learners could appreciate the rich history of their school. Whenever students gathered beneath the old mango tree, they no longer saw only its strong branches and broad leaves. They saw living proof that every generation leaves behind lessons for the next.','The oldest tree inside San Isidro National High School stood quietly behind the library. Its thick trunk and wide branches had provided shade for generations of learners. Teachers often joked that the tree had witnessed more examinations, graduations, and celebrations than anyone else in the school. One afternoon, while researching for a local history project, three Grade 8 learners noticed a small metal box partially hidden beneath one of the tree\'s large roots. Curious but careful, they informed their adviser before touching it. Together, they carefully removed the box from the soil. Inside they found several yellowed photographs, handwritten letters, and an old class program dated more than forty years earlier. The school principal contacted retired teachers and alumni to help identify the people shown in the photographs. During the following week, former students visited the school and shared stories about their experiences as learners many years ago. They spoke about studying without computers, participating in school programs under the same mango tree, and helping build classrooms through community volunteer work. The learners listened with great interest. They realized that although school buildings, technology, and teaching methods continue to change, the values of respect, perseverance, and service remain constant through every generation. Inspired by what they had learned, the Student Government organized a Heritage Corner inside the library. The photographs, letters, and stories were carefully preserved so future learners could appreciate the rich history of their school. Whenever students gathered beneath the old mango tree, they no longer saw only its strong branches and broad leaves. They saw living proof that every generation leaves behind lessons for the next.',20,47,'47 \n \nActivity 24 \nThe Secret of the Old Mango Tree \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nThe oldest tree inside San Isidro National High School stood quietly behind the \nlibrary. Its thick trunk and wide branches had provided shade for generations \nof learners. Teachers often joked that the tree had witnessed more \nexaminations, graduations, and celebrations than anyone else in the school. \nOne afternoon, while researching for a local history project, three Grade 8 \nlearners noticed a small metal box partially hidden beneath one of the tree\'s \nlarge roots. Curious but careful, they informed their adviser before touching it. \nTogether, they carefully removed the box from the soil. Inside they found \nseveral yellowed photographs, handwritten letters, and an old class program \ndated more than forty years earlier. \nThe school principal contacted retired teachers and alumni to help identify the \npeople shown in the photographs. During the following week, former students \nvisited the school and shared stories about their experiences as learners many \nyears ago. \nThey spoke about studying without computers, participating in school \nprograms under the same mango tree, and helping build classrooms through \ncommunity volunteer work. \nThe learners listened with great interest. \nThey realized that although school buildings, technology, and teaching \nmethods continue to change, the values of respect, perseverance, and service \nremain constant through every generation. \nInspired by ',1,1,'answer'),
(1346,626,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: A New Beginning','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: A New Beginning\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 233\n\nGraduation day had finally arrived.\n\nRows of proud parents filled the covered court while graduates adjusted their togas and smiled nervously at one another.\n\nAmong them sat Nicole, quietly remembering her first day in junior high school.\n\nShe had once been afraid to answer questions in class.\n\nReading aloud made her anxious, and group presentations seemed impossible.\n\nLittle by little, everything changed.\n\nHer teachers patiently guided her.\n\nHer classmates encouraged her to participate.\n\nShe joined reading activities, accepted leadership roles, and volunteered during school programs.\n\nEach experience strengthened her confidence.\n\nWhen Nicole\'s name was called to receive the Leadership Award, the audience applauded warmly.\n\nAs she walked across the stage, she remembered every challenge she had overcome—not because life had become easier, but because she had become stronger.\n\nAfter the ceremony, a Grade 7 learner approached her.\n\n\"Ate Nicole,\" he asked, \"what advice can you give someone who is afraid of making mistakes?\"\n\nNicole smiled.\n\n\"Never let fear stop you from trying,\" she replied. \"Every mistake teaches something valuable. Every challenge prepares you for a greater opportunity.\"\n\nYears later, Nicole hoped to become a teacher. She wanted to help young learners discover what her own teachers had helped her realize:\n\nEducation is not simply about earning high grades.\n\nIt is about becoming a person who can improve the lives of others.\n\nEvery ending, she believed, is simply the beginning of another journey.',NULL,'[]','A New Beginning. Graduation day had finally arrived. Rows of proud parents filled the covered court while graduates adjusted their togas and smiled nervously at one another. Among them sat Nicole, quietly remembering her first day in junior high school. She had once been afraid to answer questions in class. Reading aloud made her anxious, and group presentations seemed impossible. Little by little, everything changed. Her teachers patiently guided her. Her classmates encouraged her to participate. She joined reading activities, accepted leadership roles, and volunteered during school programs. Each experience strengthened her confidence. When Nicole\'s name was called to receive the Leadership Award, the audience applauded warmly. As she walked across the stage, she remembered every challenge she had overcome—not because life had become easier, but because she had become stronger. After the ceremony, a Grade 7 learner approached her. \"Ate Nicole,\" he asked, \"what advice can you give someone who is afraid of making mistakes?\" Nicole smiled. \"Never let fear stop you from trying,\" she replied. \"Every mistake teaches something valuable. Every challenge prepares you for a greater opportunity.\" Years later, Nicole hoped to become a teacher. She wanted to help young learners discover what her own teachers had helped her realize: Education is not simply about earning high grades. It is about becoming a person who can improve the lives of others. Every ending, she believed, is simply the beginning of another journey.','Graduation day had finally arrived. Rows of proud parents filled the covered court while graduates adjusted their togas and smiled nervously at one another. Among them sat Nicole, quietly remembering her first day in junior high school. She had once been afraid to answer questions in class. Reading aloud made her anxious, and group presentations seemed impossible. Little by little, everything changed. Her teachers patiently guided her. Her classmates encouraged her to participate. She joined reading activities, accepted leadership roles, and volunteered during school programs. Each experience strengthened her confidence. When Nicole\'s name was called to receive the Leadership Award, the audience applauded warmly. As she walked across the stage, she remembered every challenge she had overcome—not because life had become easier, but because she had become stronger. After the ceremony, a Grade 7 learner approached her. \"Ate Nicole,\" he asked, \"what advice can you give someone who is afraid of making mistakes?\" Nicole smiled. \"Never let fear stop you from trying,\" she replied. \"Every mistake teaches something valuable. Every challenge prepares you for a greater opportunity.\" Years later, Nicole hoped to become a teacher. She wanted to help young learners discover what her own teachers had helped her realize: Education is not simply about earning high grades. It is about becoming a person who can improve the lives of others. Every ending, she believed, is simply the beginning of another journey.',20,49,'49 \n \n \nActivity 25 \nA New Beginning \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nGraduation day had finally arrived. \nRows of proud parents filled the covered court while graduates adjusted their \ntogas and smiled nervously at one another. \nAmong them sat Nicole, quietly remembering her first day in junior high \nschool. \nShe had once been afraid to answer questions in class. \nReading aloud made her anxious, and group presentations seemed impossible. \nLittle by little, everything changed. \nHer teachers patiently guided her. \nHer classmates encouraged her to participate. \nShe joined reading activities, accepted leadership roles, and volunteered during \nschool programs. \nEach experience strengthened her confidence. \nWhen Nicole\'s name was called to receive the Leadership Award, the audience \napplauded warmly. \nAs she walked across the stage, she remembered every challenge she had \novercome—not because life had become easier, but because she had become \nstronger. \nAfter the ceremony, a Grade 7 learner approached her. \n\"Ate Nicole,\" he asked, \"what advice can you give someone who is afraid of \nmaking mistakes?\" \nNicole smiled. \n\"Never let fear stop you from trying,\" she replied. \"Every mistake teaches \nsomething valuable. Every challenge prepares you for a greater opportunity.\" \nYears later, Nicole hoped to become a teacher.',1,1,'answer'),
(1347,627,98,'post',1,'Let’s get ready','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next.','Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.',NULL,'[]','Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.',NULL,5,52,'52 \n \n \nPART III \nPOST-ASSESSMENT \nGrade 7 and 8 Posttest Passage \n \nFinding My Voice \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nAt the beginning of the school year, Aaron preferred listening instead of \nspeaking. Whenever teachers asked volunteers to read aloud, he lowered his \nhead and hoped someone else would be chosen. He worried that his classmates \nmight laugh if he mispronounced a difficult word. \nHis English teacher noticed his hesitation and encouraged him to spend fifteen \nminutes each day reading aloud at home. At first, Aaron struggled with \nunfamiliar vocabulary and paused frequently. However, he continued \npracticing every evening. \nAfter several weeks, reading became easier. He learned to recognize \npunctuation marks, read with better expression, and pronounce longer words \ncorrectly. His confidence gradually increased. \nOne day, the school announced a storytelling contest. Although Aaron felt \nnervous, he decided to participate. He practiced faithfully before his parents, \nyounger sister, and even in front of a mirror. \nDuring the contest, Aaron spoke clearly and confidently. He remembered to \npause at commas, change his tone during conversations, and emphasize \nimportant ideas. The audience listened attentively from beginning to end. \nAlthough another learner won first place, Aaron received a special award for \nMost Improved Reader. \nWalking back to his seat, he realized that success is not always measured by \ntrophies. Sometimes it is measured by the courag',1,1,'none'),
(1348,627,98,'post',2,'Read aloud: Finding My Voice','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: Finding My Voice\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 238\n\nAt the beginning of the school year, Aaron preferred listening instead of speaking. Whenever teachers asked volunteers to read aloud, he lowered his head and hoped someone else would be chosen. He worried that his classmates might laugh if he mispronounced a difficult word.\n\nHis English teacher noticed his hesitation and encouraged him to spend fifteen minutes each day reading aloud at home. At first, Aaron struggled with unfamiliar vocabulary and paused frequently. However, he continued practicing every evening.\n\nAfter several weeks, reading became easier. He learned to recognize punctuation marks, read with better expression, and pronounce longer words correctly. His confidence gradually increased.\n\nOne day, the school announced a storytelling contest. Although Aaron felt nervous, he decided to participate. He practiced faithfully before his parents, younger sister, and even in front of a mirror.\n\nDuring the contest, Aaron spoke clearly and confidently. He remembered to pause at commas, change his tone during conversations, and emphasize important ideas. The audience listened attentively from beginning to end.\n\nAlthough another learner won first place, Aaron received a special award for Most Improved Reader.\n\nWalking back to his seat, he realized that success is not always measured by trophies. Sometimes it is measured by the courage to do something that once seemed impossible.\n\nFrom that day forward, Aaron no longer feared reading aloud. Instead, he looked forward to every opportunity to learn, improve, and inspire others through the power of words.',NULL,'[]','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','At the beginning of the school year, Aaron preferred listening instead of speaking. Whenever teachers asked volunteers to read aloud, he lowered his head and hoped someone else would be chosen. He worried that his classmates might laugh if he mispronounced a difficult word. His English teacher noticed his hesitation and encouraged him to spend fifteen minutes each day reading aloud at home. At first, Aaron struggled with unfamiliar vocabulary and paused frequently. However, he continued practicing every evening. After several weeks, reading became easier. He learned to recognize punctuation marks, read with better expression, and pronounce longer words correctly. His confidence gradually increased. One day, the school announced a storytelling contest. Although Aaron felt nervous, he decided to participate. He practiced faithfully before his parents, younger sister, and even in front of a mirror. During the contest, Aaron spoke clearly and confidently. He remembered to pause at commas, change his tone during conversations, and emphasize important ideas. The audience listened attentively from beginning to end. Although another learner won first place, Aaron received a special award for Most Improved Reader. Walking back to his seat, he realized that success is not always measured by trophies. Sometimes it is measured by the courage to do something that once seemed impossible. From that day forward, Aaron no longer feared reading aloud. Instead, he looked forward to every opportunity to learn, improve, and inspire others through the power of words.',20,52,'52 \n \n \nPART III \nPOST-ASSESSMENT \nGrade 7 and 8 Posttest Passage \n \nFinding My Voice \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nAt the beginning of the school year, Aaron preferred listening instead of \nspeaking. Whenever teachers asked volunteers to read aloud, he lowered his \nhead and hoped someone else would be chosen. He worried that his classmates \nmight laugh if he mispronounced a difficult word. \nHis English teacher noticed his hesitation and encouraged him to spend fifteen \nminutes each day reading aloud at home. At first, Aaron struggled with \nunfamiliar vocabulary and paused frequently. However, he continued \npracticing every evening. \nAfter several weeks, reading became easier. He learned to recognize \npunctuation marks, read with better expression, and pronounce longer words \ncorrectly. His confidence gradually increased. \nOne day, the school announced a storytelling contest. Although Aaron felt \nnervous, he decided to participate. He practiced faithfully before his parents, \nyounger sister, and even in front of a mirror. \nDuring the contest, Aaron spoke clearly and confidently. He remembered to \npause at commas, change his tone during conversations, and emphasize \nimportant ideas. The audience listened attentively from beginning to end. \nAlthough another learner won first place, Aaron received a special award for \nMost Improved Reader. \nWalking back to his seat, he realized that success is not always measured by \ntrophies. Sometimes it is measured by the courag',1,1,'answer'),
(1349,628,99,'pre',1,'Let’s get ready','reference','Read or listen, then choose Next.','Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.',NULL,'[]','Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.',NULL,5,8,'8 \n \nPRE-ASSESSMENT \nThe Bridge to Tomorrow \nWhen fourteen-year-old Marco transferred to his new school, everything seemed \nunfamiliar. The classrooms were larger, the teachers spoke faster, and the \nstudents already had their own groups of friends. During his first week, he \npreferred sitting quietly at the back of the room, hoping no one would notice him. \nOne morning, their English teacher announced a reading activity. Each learner \nwould read a passage aloud before the class. Marco\'s heart beat rapidly. He \nenjoyed reading silently, but speaking before others made him nervous. \nWhen his turn came, he stood slowly and held the reading selection with \ntrembling hands. He took a deep breath and began reading. At first, his voice \nwas soft, but after several sentences he noticed that his classmates were \nlistening attentively. His teacher smiled encouragingly whenever he paused \ncorrectly or pronounced a difficult word clearly. \nAfter the activity, several classmates congratulated him. One even asked if they \ncould become partners for the next reading assignment. Marco realized that \nconfidence grows through practice. Every page he read became another step \nacross the bridge toward new friendships and greater opportunities. \n \nWord Count: 181 \n \nOral Reading Record \nTypes of Mis- \ncues \nMispronun- \nciation \nOmis- \nsion \nSubsti- \ntution \nInser- \ntion \nRepe- \ntition \nTranspo- \nsition \nRever- \nsal \nTotal \nNumber of \nMiscues \nNumber of Words in the Passage \n181 \nOral Reading Scor',1,1,'none'),
(1350,628,99,'pre',2,'Read aloud: The Bridge to Tomorrow','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The Bridge to Tomorrow\nWords: 181\n\nWhen fourteen-year-old Marco transferred to his new school, everything seemed unfamiliar. The classrooms were larger, the teachers spoke faster, and the students already had their own groups of friends. During his first week, he preferred sitting quietly at the back of the room, hoping no one would notice him.\n\nOne morning, their English teacher announced a reading activity. Each learner would read a passage aloud before the class. Marco\'s heart beat rapidly. He enjoyed reading silently, but speaking before others made him nervous.\n\nWhen his turn came, he stood slowly and held the reading selection with trembling hands. He took a deep breath and began reading. At first, his voice was soft, but after several sentences he noticed that his classmates were listening attentively. His teacher smiled encouragingly whenever he paused correctly or pronounced a difficult word clearly.\n\nAfter the activity, several classmates congratulated him. One even asked if they could become partners for the next reading assignment. Marco realized that confidence grows through practice. Every page he read became another step across the bridge toward new friendships and greater opportunities.',NULL,'[]','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','When fourteen-year-old Marco transferred to his new school, everything seemed unfamiliar. The classrooms were larger, the teachers spoke faster, and the students already had their own groups of friends. During his first week, he preferred sitting quietly at the back of the room, hoping no one would notice him. One morning, their English teacher announced a reading activity. Each learner would re