he \nclass gardening project. Her classmates eagerly worked beside her, planting \nvegetables and flowering shrubs that later brightened the school grounds. \nLena realized that friendships often begin with shared work rather than shared \nwords. By simply offering her knowledge a',1,1,'answer'),
(1421,695,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: One Honest Decision','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: One Honest Decision\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 238\n\nIt was dismissal time when Joshua noticed a black wallet lying near the school gate. Students hurried past without seeing it. He picked it up and looked around, hoping someone would return to claim it.\n\nInside the wallet were several identification cards, a few receipts, and a considerable amount of money. Joshua immediately realized that someone must be worried about losing it.\n\nHis friends suggested different ideas. One jokingly said, \"Nobody saw you. Just keep it.\"\n\nJoshua smiled but shook his head. \"If this belonged to my father, I would hope someone returned it.\"\n\nInstead of going home immediately, he brought the wallet to the principal\'s office. The school staff checked the identification card and contacted the owner, who happened to be the father of one of the students.\n\nLess than an hour later, the grateful owner arrived. He counted the money and found that nothing was missing.\n\n\"You have saved me from many problems today,\" the man said warmly. \"This money was meant to pay our workers.\"\n\nHe offered Joshua a reward, but the boy politely declined.\n\n\"My parents taught me that honesty is worth more than money,\" Joshua replied.\n\nThe principal later shared Joshua\'s story during the Monday flag ceremony. His classmates applauded as he received a Certificate of Good Citizenship.\n\nJoshua discovered that doing the right thing may not always bring material rewards, but it earns something even greater—the trust and respect of others.',NULL,'[]','One Honest Decision. It was dismissal time when Joshua noticed a black wallet lying near the school gate. Students hurried past without seeing it. He picked it up and looked around, hoping someone would return to claim it. Inside the wallet were several identification cards, a few receipts, and a considerable amount of money. Joshua immediately realized that someone must be worried about losing it. His friends suggested different ideas. One jokingly said, \"Nobody saw you. Just keep it.\" Joshua smiled but shook his head. \"If this belonged to my father, I would hope someone returned it.\" Instead of going home immediately, he brought the wallet to the principal\'s office. The school staff checked the identification card and contacted the owner, who happened to be the father of one of the students. Less than an hour later, the grateful owner arrived. He counted the money and found that nothing was missing. \"You have saved me from many problems today,\" the man said warmly. \"This money was meant to pay our workers.\" He offered Joshua a reward, but the boy politely declined. \"My parents taught me that honesty is worth more than money,\" Joshua replied. The principal later shared Joshua\'s story during the Monday flag ceremony. His classmates applauded as he received a Certificate of Good Citizenship. Joshua discovered that doing the right thing may not always bring material rewards, but it earns something even greater—the trust and respect of others.','It was dismissal time when Joshua noticed a black wallet lying near the school gate. Students hurried past without seeing it. He picked it up and looked around, hoping someone would return to claim it. Inside the wallet were several identification cards, a few receipts, and a considerable amount of money. Joshua immediately realized that someone must be worried about losing it. His friends suggested different ideas. One jokingly said, \"Nobody saw you. Just keep it.\" Joshua smiled but shook his head. \"If this belonged to my father, I would hope someone returned it.\" Instead of going home immediately, he brought the wallet to the principal\'s office. The school staff checked the identification card and contacted the