Joshua \nreplied. \nThe principal later shared Joshua\'s story during the Monday flag ceremony. \nHis classmates applauded as he received a Certificate of Good Citizenship. \nJoshua discovered that doing the right thing may not always bring material \nrewards, but it earns some',1,1,'answer'),
(1422,696,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Forgotten Library Card','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The Forgotten Library Card\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 198\n\nEvery Wednesday afternoon, the Grade 8 learners visited the school library. It had become one of Sophia\'s favorite places because it offered more than books. It provided quiet spaces where she could think, study, and imagine.\n\nOne afternoon, she eagerly searched for a science encyclopedia recommended by her teacher. After finding the book, she walked toward the circulation desk.\n\nThen she realized something.\n\nHer library card was missing.\n\nShe searched every pocket of her bag, checked her notebooks, and even looked beneath the tables. It was nowhere to be found.\n\nFeeling disappointed, Sophia informed the librarian, who calmly smiled.\n\n\"Sometimes,\" the librarian said, \"finding something begins by remembering your last careful moment.\"\n\nSophia retraced her steps. She remembered reviewing her assignment under a large narra tree before entering the library.\n\nWhen she returned outside, she found the card exactly where she had placed it between the pages of her notebook.\n\nRelieved, she borrowed the encyclopedia and thanked the librarian.\n\nThat afternoon she learned that careful habits save both time and worry. Since then, she kept all important school cards inside a small transparent organizer attached to her bag.\n\nSmall acts of organization, she realized, often prevent bigger problems.',NULL,'[]','The Forgotten Library Card. Every Wednesday afternoon, the Grade 8 learners visited the school library. It had become one of Sophia\'s favorite places because it offered more than books. It provided quiet spaces where she could think, study, and imagine. One afternoon, she eagerly searched for a science encyclopedia recommended by her teacher. After finding the book, she walked toward the circulation desk. Then she realized something. Her library card was missing. She searched every pocket of her bag, checked her notebooks, and even looked beneath the tables. It was nowhere to be found. Feeling disappointed, Sophia informed the librarian, who calmly smiled. \"Sometimes,\" the librarian said, \"finding something begins by remembering your last careful moment.\" Sophia retraced her steps. She remembered reviewing her assignment under a large narra tree before entering the library. When she returned outside, she found the card exactly where she had placed it between the pages of her notebook. Relieved, she borrowed the encyclopedia and thanked the librarian. That afternoon she learned that careful habits save both time and worry. Since then, she kept all important school cards inside a small transparent organizer attached to her bag. Small acts of organization, she realized, often prevent bigger problems.','Every Wednesday afternoon, the Grade 8 learners visited the school library. It had become one of Sophia\'s favorite places because it offered more than books. It provided quiet spaces where she could think, study, and imagine. One afternoon, she eagerly searched for a science encyclopedia recommended by her teacher. After finding the book, she walked toward the circulation desk. Then she realized something. Her library card was missing. She searched every pocket of her bag, checked her notebooks, and even looked beneath the tables. It was nowhere to be found. Feeling disappointed, Sophia informed the librarian, who calmly smiled. \"Sometimes,\" the librarian said, \"finding something begins by remembering your last careful moment.\" Sophia retraced her steps. She remembered reviewing her assignment under a large narra tree before entering the library. When she returned outside, she found the card exactly where she had placed it between the pages of her notebook. Relieved, she borrowed the encyclopedia and thanked the librarian. That afternoon she learned that careful habits save both time and worry. Since then, she kept all important school cards inside a small transparent organizer attached to her bag. Small acts of organization, she realized, often prevent bigger problems.',20,27,'27 \n \nActivity 14 \nThe Forgotten Library Card \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nEvery Wednesday afternoon, the Grade 8 learners visited the school library. It \nhad become one of Sophia\'s favorite places because it offered more than books. \nIt provided quiet spaces where she could think, study, and imagine. \nOne afternoon, she eagerly searched for a science encyclopedia recommended \nby her teacher. After finding the book, she walked toward the circulation desk. \nThen she realized something. \nHer library card was missing. \nShe searched every pocket of her bag, checked her notebooks, and even looked \nbeneath the tables. It was nowhere to be found. \nFeeling disappointed, Sophia informed the librarian, who calmly smiled. \n\"Sometimes,\" the librarian said, \"finding something begins by remembering \nyour last careful moment.\" \nSophia retraced her steps. She remembered reviewing her assignment under a \nlarge narra tree before entering the library. \nWhen she returned outside, she found the card exactly where she had placed it \nbetween the pages of her notebook. \nRelieved, she borrowed the encyclopedia and thanked the librarian. \nThat afternoon she learned that careful habits save both time and worry. Since \nthen, she kept all important school cards inside a small transparent organizer \nattached to her bag. \nSmall acts of organization, she realized, often prevent bigger problems.',1,1,'answer'),
(1423,697,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Science Fair Challenge','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The Science Fair Challenge\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 195\n\nThe annual Science Fair was only three weeks away, and excitement filled the campus. Every Grade 8 section had been challenged to present an invention that solved a community problem.\n\nGroup Three decided to create a simple rainwater collection system that could help households conserve water during the dry season.\n\nAt first, everything seemed easy. However, the prototype leaked during testing.\n\nSome members became discouraged.\n\n\"We should change our project,\" one student suggested.\n\nTheir team leader, Camille, disagreed.\n\n\"Real scientists don\'t stop after one failure,\" she said. \"Every mistake teaches us something.\"\n\nThe group carefully examined the design. They discovered that one connector pipe was slightly loose. After making adjustments and sealing the joints properly, they tested the system again.\n\nThis time, the container successfully collected rainwater without leaking.\n\nOn presentation day, the judges praised not only the invention but also the team\'s explanation of the scientific process behind their improvements.\n\nAlthough they placed second overall, the judges selected their project to be displayed during the municipal science exhibit.\n\nCamille smiled as she looked at her teammates.\n\n\"We didn\'t succeed because everything worked the first time,\" she said.\n\n\"We succeeded because we never stopped improving.\"',NULL,'[]','The Science Fair Challenge. The annual Science Fair was only three weeks away, and excitement filled the campus. Every Grade 8 section had been challenged to present an invention that solved a community problem. Group Three decided to create a simple rainwater collection system that could help households conserve water during the dry season. At first, everything seemed easy. However, the prototype leaked during testing. Some members became discouraged. \"We should change our project,\" one student suggested. Their team leader, Camille, disagreed. \"Real scientists don\'t stop after one failure,\" she said. \"Every mistake teaches us something.\" The group carefully examined the design. They discovered that one connector pipe was slightly loose. After making adjustments and sealing the joints properly, they tested the system again. This time, the container successfully collected rainwater without leaking. On presentation day, the judges praised not only the invention but also the team\'s explanation of the scientific process behind their improvements. Although they placed second overall, the judges selected their project to be displayed during the municipal science exhibit. Camille smiled as she looked at her teammates. \"We didn\'t succeed because everything worked the first time,\" she said. \"We succeeded because we never stopped improving.\"','The annual Science Fair was only three weeks away, and excitement filled the campus. Every Grade 8 section had been challenged to present an invention that solved a community problem. Group Three decided to create a simple rainwater collection system that could help households conserve water during the dry season. At first, everything seemed easy. However, the prototype leaked during testing. Some members became discouraged. \"We should change our project,\" one student suggested. Their team leader, Camille, disagreed. \"Real scientists don\'t stop after one failure,\" she said. \"Every mistake teaches us something.\" The group carefully examined the design. They discovered that one connector pipe was slightly loose. After making adjustments and sealing the joints properly, they tested the system again. This time, the container successfully collected rainwater without leaking. On presentation day, the judges praised not only the invention but also the team\'s explanation of the scientific process behind their improvements. Although they placed second overall, the judges selected their project to be displayed during the municipal science exhibit. Camille smiled as she looked at her teammates. \"We didn\'t succeed because everything worked the first time,\" she said. \"We succeeded because we never stopped improving.\"',20,29,'29 \n \nActivity 15 \nThe Science Fair Challenge \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nThe annual Science Fair was only three weeks away, and excitement filled the \ncampus. Every Grade 8 section had been challenged to present an invention \nthat solved a community problem. \nGroup Three decided to create a simple rainwater collection system that could \nhelp households conserve water during the dry season. \nAt first, everything seemed easy. However, the prototype leaked during testing. \nSome members became discouraged. \n\"We should change our project,\" one student suggested. \nTheir team leader, Camille, disagreed. \n\"Real scientists don\'t stop after one failure,\" she said. \"Every mistake teaches \nus something.\" \nThe group carefully examined the design. They discovered that one connector \npipe was slightly loose. After making adjustments and sealing the joints \nproperly, they tested the system again. \nThis time, the container successfully collected rainwater without leaking. \nOn presentation day, the judges praised not only the invention but also the \nteam\'s explanation of the scientific process behind their improvements. \nAlthough they placed second overall, the judges selected their project to be \ndisplayed during the municipal science exhibit. \nCamille smiled as she looked at her teammates. \n\"We didn\'t succeed because everything worked the first time,\" she said. \n\"We succeeded because we never stopped improving.\"',1,1,'answer'),
(1424,698,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: A Letter to My Future Self','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: A Letter to My Future Self\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 194\n\nAs part of their English class activity, each learner was asked to write a letter addressed to themselves ten years in the future.\n\nMost students wrote about the careers they hoped to pursue. Some dreamed of becoming engineers, doctors, teachers, artists, or entrepreneurs.\n\nMiguel stared at his blank paper for several minutes before writing his first sentence.\n\n\"Dear Future Me,\n\nI hope you still remember the promises you made when you were fourteen.\"\n\nHe continued writing about the values he wanted to keep—honesty, kindness, humility, and perseverance.\n\nHe reminded himself that success should never be measured only by money or awards, but also by the number of lives positively influenced.\n\nBefore sealing the letter inside an envelope, he added one final paragraph.\n\n\"If life becomes difficult, remember the dreams that inspired you to begin. Keep learning, keep serving, and never stop believing that every day is another opportunity to become a better person.\"\n\nThe teacher collected all the letters and promised to return them during the learners\' graduation from senior high school.\n\nAs Miguel walked home, he smiled.\n\nPerhaps the most important promise we ever make is the one we make to ourselves.',NULL,'[]','A Letter to My Future Self. As part of their English class activity, each learner was asked to write a letter addressed to themselves ten years in the future. Most students wrote about the careers they hoped to pursue. Some dreamed of becoming engineers, doctors, teachers, artists, or entrepreneurs. Miguel stared at his blank paper for several minutes before writing his first sentence. \"Dear Future Me, I hope you still remember the promises you made when you were fourteen.\" He continued writing about the values he wanted to keep—honesty, kindness, humility, and perseverance. He reminded himself that success should never be measured only by money or awards, but also by the number of lives positively influenced. Before sealing the letter inside an envelope, he added one final paragraph. \"If life becomes difficult, remember the dreams that inspired you to begin. Keep learning, keep serving, and never stop believing that every day is another opportunity to become a better person.\" The teacher collected all the letters and promised to return them during the learners\' graduation from senior high school. As Miguel walked home, he smiled. Perhaps the most important promise we ever make is the one we make to ourselves.','As part of their English class activity, each learner was asked to write a letter addressed to themselves ten years in the future. Most students wrote about the careers they hoped to pursue. Some dreamed of becoming engineers, doctors, teachers, artists, or entrepreneurs. Miguel stared at his blank paper for several minutes before writing his first sentence. \"Dear Future Me, I hope you still remember the promises you made when you were fourteen.\" He continued writing about the values he wanted to keep—honesty, kindness, humility, and perseverance. He reminded himself that success should never be measured only by money or awards, but also by the number of lives positively influenced. Before sealing the letter inside an envelope, he added one final paragraph. \"If life becomes difficult, remember the dreams that inspired you to begin. Keep learning, keep serving, and never stop believing that every day is another opportunity to become a better person.\" The teacher collected all the letters and promised to return them during the learners\' graduation from senior high school. As Miguel walked home, he smiled. Perhaps the most important promise we ever make is the one we make to ourselves.',20,31,'31 \n \n \n \nActivity 16 \nA Letter to My Future Self \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nAs part of their English class activity, each learner was asked to write a letter \naddressed to themselves ten years in the future. \nMost students wrote about the careers they hoped to pursue. Some dreamed of \nbecoming engineers, doctors, teachers, artists, or entrepreneurs. \nMiguel stared at his blank paper for several minutes before writing his first \nsentence. \n\"Dear Future Me, \nI hope you still remember the promises you made when you were fourteen.\" \nHe continued writing about the values he wanted to keep—honesty, kindness, \nhumility, and perseverance. \nHe reminded himself that success should never be measured only by money or \nawards, but also by the number of lives positively influenced. \nBefore sealing the letter inside an envelope, he added one final paragraph. \n\"If life becomes difficult, remember the dreams that inspired you to begin. Keep \nlearning, keep serving, and never stop believing that every day is another \nopportunity to become a better person.\" \nThe teacher collected all the letters and promised to return them during the \nlearners\' graduation from senior high school. \nAs Miguel walked home, he smiled. \nPerhaps the most important promise we ever make is the one we make to \nourselves.',1,1,'answer'),
(1425,699,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Volunteer','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The Volunteer\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 202\n\nA powerful typhoon had passed through the municipality, leaving many families in need of assistance. Several classrooms were damaged, trees blocked the roads, and many homes lost electricity.\n\nThe school organized a volunteer day to help clean the campus and prepare learning spaces for classes to resume.\n\nAmong the volunteers was Aaron, a Grade 7 learner who had never joined a community service activity before.\n\nHe expected the work to be tiring, and it certainly was.\n\nStudents swept classrooms, gathered broken branches, arranged donated books, and repainted faded signboards.\n\nDuring lunch, Aaron noticed an elderly maintenance worker struggling to carry several heavy boxes.\n\nWithout being asked, Aaron immediately helped him.\n\nThe man smiled gratefully.\n\n\"You remind me that kindness is strongest when nobody asks for it,\" he said.\n\nBy late afternoon, the school grounds looked cleaner than they had before the storm.\n\nAlthough Aaron returned home physically exhausted, he felt deeply satisfied.\n\nThat evening, he reflected on the day\'s work.\n\nVolunteering had taught him that serving the community is not measured by the size of one\'s contribution but by the willingness to offer time, effort, and compassion.\n\nSometimes the greatest reward for helping others is discovering that we have grown stronger ourselves.',NULL,'[]','The Volunteer. A powerful typhoon had passed through the municipality, leaving many families in need of assistance. Several classrooms were damaged, trees blocked the roads, and many homes lost electricity. The school organized a volunteer day to help clean the campus and prepare learning spaces for classes to resume. Among the volunteers was Aaron, a Grade 7 learner who had never joined a community service activity before. He expected the work to be tiring, and it certainly was. Students swept classrooms, gathered broken branches, arranged donated books, and repainted faded signboards. During lunch, Aaron noticed an elderly maintenance worker struggling to carry several heavy boxes. Without being asked, Aaron immediately helped him. The man smiled gratefully. \"You remind me that kindness is strongest when nobody asks for it,\" he said. By late afternoon, the school grounds looked cleaner than they had before the storm. Although Aaron returned home physically exhausted, he felt deeply satisfied. That evening, he reflected on the day\'s work. Volunteering had taught him that serving the community is not measured by the size of one\'s contribution but by the willingness to offer time, effort, and compassion. Sometimes the greatest reward for helping others is discovering that we have grown stronger ourselves.','A powerful typhoon had passed through the municipality, leaving many families in need of assistance. Several classrooms were damaged, trees blocked the roads, and many homes lost electricity. The school organized a volunteer day to help clean the campus and prepare learning spaces for classes to resume. Among the volunteers was Aaron, a Grade 7 learner who had never joined a community service activity before. He expected the work to be tiring, and it certainly was. Students swept classrooms, gathered broken branches, arranged donated books, and repainted faded signboards. During lunch, Aaron noticed an elderly maintenance worker struggling to carry several heavy boxes. Without being asked, Aaron immediately helped him. The man smiled gratefully. \"You remind me that kindness is strongest when nobody asks for it,\" he said. By late afternoon, the school grounds looked cleaner than they had before the storm. Although Aaron returned home physically exhausted, he felt deeply satisfied. That evening, he reflected on the day\'s work. Volunteering had taught him that serving the community is not measured by the size of one\'s contribution but by the willingness to offer time, effort, and compassion. Sometimes the greatest reward for helping others is discovering that we have grown stronger ourselves.',20,33,'33 \n \n \nActivity 17 \nThe Volunteer \nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original) \nA powerful typhoon had passed through the municipality, leaving many \nfamilies in need of assistance. Several classrooms were damaged, trees blocked \nthe roads, and many homes lost electricity. \nThe school organized a volunteer day to help clean the campus and prepare \nlearning spaces for classes to resume. \nAmong the volunteers was Aaron, a Grade 7 learner who had never joined a \ncommunity service activity before. \nHe expected the work to be tiring, and it certainly was. \nStudents swept classrooms, gathered broken branches, arranged donated \nbooks, and repainted faded signboards. \nDuring lunch, Aaron noticed an elderly maintenance worker struggling to carry \nseveral heavy boxes. \nWithout being asked, Aaron immediately helped him. \nThe man smiled gratefully. \n\"You remind me that kindness is strongest when nobody asks for it,\" he said. \nBy late afternoon, the school grounds looked cleaner than they had before the \nstorm. \nAlthough Aaron returned home physically exhausted, he felt deeply satisfied. \nThat evening, he reflected on the day\'s work. \nVolunteering had taught him that serving the community is not measured by \nthe size of one\'s contribution but by the willingness to offer time, effort, and \ncompassion. \nSometimes the greatest reward for helping others is discovering that we have \ngrown stronger ourselves.',1,1,'answer'),
(1426,700,NULL,'learn',1,'Read aloud: The Lost Wallet','reading','Tap Start reading and read the whole passage aloud. Tap I’m done when you finish. Your teacher listens for accuracy, speed and expression.','Title: The Lost Wallet\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 246\n\nFriday afternoons were always busy at Manolo Integrated High School. Students hurried toward the school gate while others waited for their parents or rode the last jeepneys home.\n\nAs Claire walked across the covered court, she noticed a brown wallet beneath one of the benches. She picked it up and looked around, but no one seemed to be searching for it.\n\nInside were several identification cards, a driver\'s license, receipts, and enough cash to pay for a week\'s groceries. Claire immediately imagined how worried the owner must be.\n\nHer friend suggested taking the wallet to the nearest store because someone there might recognize its owner. Another classmate thought they should wait outside the gate.\n\nClaire remembered what their Values Education teacher often said: \"When you find something valuable, place it in the hands of someone who is responsible.\"\n\nShe went directly to the Guidance Office. The guidance counselor carefully examined the identification card and discovered that the wallet belonged to the father of a Grade 8 learner.\n\nWithin thirty minutes, the grateful parent arrived.\n\n\"I thought I had lost this forever,\" he said with relief. \"This contains my salary for the week.\"\n\nHe offered Claire a reward, but she politely refused.\n\n\"My reward is knowing everything was returned safely,\" she replied.\n\nDuring the next flag ceremony, the school recognized Claire for her honesty.\n\nHer classmates learned that integrity is demonstrated not only during difficult moments but also through simple decisions that no one may ever see.',NULL,'[]','The Lost Wallet. Friday afternoons were always busy at Manolo Integrated High School. Students hurried toward the school gate while others waited for their parents or rode the last jeepneys home. As Claire walked across the covered court, she noticed a brown wallet beneath one of the benches. She picked it up and looked around, but no one seemed to be searching for it. Inside were several identification cards, a driver\'s license, receipts, and enough cash to pay for a week\'s groceries. Claire immediately imagined how worried the owner must be. Her friend suggested taking the wallet to the nearest store because someone there might recognize its owner. Another classmate thought they should wait outside the gate. Claire remembered what their Values Education teacher often said: \"When you find something valuable, place it in the hands of someone who is responsible.\" She went directly to the Guidance Office. The guidance counselor carefully examined the identification card and discovered that the wallet belonged to the father of a Grade 8 learner. Within thirty minutes, the grateful parent arrived. \"I thought I had lost this forever,\" he said with relief. \"This contains my salary for the week.\" He offered Claire a reward, but she politely refused. \"My reward is knowing everything was returned safely,\" she replied. During the next flag ceremony, the school recognized Claire for her honesty. Her classmates learned that integrity is demonstrated not only during difficult moments but also through simple decisions that no one may ever see.','Friday afternoons were always busy at Manolo Integrated High School. Students hurried toward the school gate while others waited for their parents or rode the last jeepneys home. As Claire walked across the covered court, she noticed a brown wallet beneath one of the benches. She picked it up and looked around, but no one seemed to be searching for it. Inside were several identification cards, a driver\'s license, receipts, and enough cash to pay for a week\'s groceries. Claire immediately imagined how worried the owner must be. Her friend sugg