(1484,757,113,'pre',1,'Pre-test · Days 1-10','open','Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.','1. What is the best definition for \"antidote\"?\n\n2. If something is \"incredible,\" it is:\n\n3. The word \"derange\" most nearly means:\n\n4. A \"sanctuary\" is a place of:\n\n5. To \"squander\" money means to:\n\n6. Which sentence is punctuated correctly?\n\n7. What is the simple subject in this sentence: \"The quick, brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.\"\n\n8. Which word is a synonym for \"happy\"?\n\n9. What part of speech is the word \"run\" in this sentence: \"We will run in the race\"?\n\n10. Which sentence is written in the past tense?\n\n11. How did the library make Maria feel?\n\n12. What did Maria look for in the library?\n\n13. The main idea of this passage is:\n\n14. The person who writes a story is called the:\n\n15. The time and place where a story happen is called the:\n\n16. The main character in a story is called the:\n\n17. \"The wind whispered through the trees\" is an example of:\n\n18. What is the central message or lesson of a story called?\n\n19. What is the primary purpose of a topic sentence in a paragraph?\n\n20. If a story is written from the perspective of \"I,\" what point of view is it?\n\n21. What is the best source to find the definition of a word?\n\n22. What is the first sentence of an essay called that grabs the reader\'s attention?\n\n23. Which of these is a complete sentence?\n\n24. What do we call a word that sounds like what it means (e.g., buzz, pop)?\n\n25. What is the term for a group of lines in a poem?\n\n26. What is the opposite meaning of the word \"severe\"?\n\n27. Which word is an adjective?\n\n28. The phrase \"as brave as a lion\" is an example of a:\n\n29. What is the purpose of a conclusion in an essay?\n\n30. Which of these is a fiction book?\n\n31. What is the term for the most exciting part of a story?\n\n32. Which source would be most useful for finding a synonym?\n\n33. What does the prefix \"un-\" mean in the word \"unhappy\"?\n\n34. Which sentence uses correct subject-verb agreement?\n\n35. What is the term for the dictionary definition of a word?\n\n36. In a story, the problem that the main character faces are called the:\n\n37. Which part of a book lists the chapters and their page numbers?\n\n38. What is the best way to identify the main idea of a paragraph?\n\n39. \"He was a rock, strong and unmoving.\" This is an example of a:\n\n40. Which word is a verb?\n\n41. What is the term for giving human qualities to non-human things?\n\n42. Which of these is a compound word?\n\n43. What is the primary purpose of persuasive writing?\n\n44. Which sentence is a fact?\n\n45. What is the term for the feelings and ideas associated with a word?\n\n46. What do you call the series of events that make up a story?\n\n47. Which of these is a proper noun?\n\n48. What is the best way to check for errors in your writing?\n\n49. What is the name for a word that joins two ideas or clauses (e.g., and, but, or)?\n\n50. The final part of a story where the conflict is resolved is called the:',NULL,'[]','Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.',NULL,10,7,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1485,758,114,'pre',1,'Pre-test · Days 11-20','open','Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.','1. What is the best definition for coax?\n\n2. If someone is distrustful, they are:\n\n3. The word despise most nearly means:\n\n4. A person who is arrogant:\n\n5. To apologize means to:\n\n6. Which sentence is punctuated correctly?\n\n7. What is the simple predicate (verb) in this sentence: \"The greedy lion chased the deer.\"\n\n8. Which word is an antonym for sincerity?\n\n9. What part of speech is the word huge in this sentence: \"The elephant had a huge body.\"?\n\n10. Which sentence is written in the future tense?\n\n11. Why were the ants angry?\n\n12. How did the ants teach the elephant a lesson?\n\n13. What was the result of the ants\' action?\n\n14. The central lesson or message of a story is called the:\n\n15. The main problem that character’s face in a story is the:\n\n16. \"The wind whispered through the trees\" is an example of:\n\n17. A story that uses animals as characters to teach a lesson is called a:\n\n18. The high point or most exciting part of a story is the:\n\n19. What is the primary purpose of a fable?\n\n20. If a character is described as \"cunning,\" they are most likely to be:\n\n21. What is the best source to find a synonym for the word \"huge\"?\n\n22. What is the term for the feeling of sadness or pity you feel for a character?\n\n23. Which of these is a complete sentence?\n\n24. What do we call the narrator of a story who is also a character in the story?\n\n25. What is the term for a word that means the opposite of another word?\n\n26. What is the opposite meaning of the word victory?\n\n27. Which word is a noun?\n\n28. The phrase \"as slow as a tortoise\" is an example of a:\n\n29. What is the purpose of the beginning of a story (exposition)?\n\n30. Which of these is a theme that might be found in a fable?\n\n31. What does the prefix \"re-\" mean in the word \"rewrite\"?\n\n32. Which sentence uses correct subject-verb agreement?\n\n33. What is the term for the actual, dictionary definition of a word?\n\n34. In the story \"The Farmer and His Four Sons,\" what does the bundle of sticks symbolize?\n\n35. Which part of a story comes after the climax?\n\n36. What is the term for a conversation between two or more characters?\n\n37. Which word best describes the fox in \"The Fox and the Crow\"?\n\n38. What is the best way to identify the moral of a fable?\n\n39. \"He was a lion in battle.\" This is an example of a:\n\n40. Which word is a verb?\n\n41. What is the term for an exaggerated statement used for effect?\n\n42. Which of these is a compound word?\n\n43. What is the primary purpose of narrative writing?\n\n44. Which sentence is an opinion?\n\n45. What is the term for the feelings and ideas associated with a word (beyond its dictionary definition)?\n\n46. What do you call the perspective from which a story is told?\n\n47. Which of these is a proper noun?\n\n48. What is the best way to understand a character\'s traits?\n\n49. What is the name for a word that describes a verb (e.g., slowly, quickly)?\n\n50. The final part of a story where the conflict is resolved is called the:',NULL,'[]','Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.',NULL,10,13,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1486,759,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 1 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE JELLYFISH\n\nOur oceans, seas, gulfs, and bays teem with jellyfish.\nThey are found near the shores, especially during the hot summer months. Most common are the transparent jellyfish which are hardly seen in the water. They have an umbrella- shaped body, convex and smooth when seen from above.\nFrom the underside hang four to 20 tentacles with small cup- shaped disks used by the jellyfish to catch its prey of tiny fish, crustaceans, or planktons.\n\nMost of the jellyfish found in Philippine waters are harmless.\nSome species, however, those that have darker shaded bodies, can produce severe itching. Others can cause painful     welts   when        their tentacles attach themselves to the human body.\n\nFound in the Atlantic and the Mediterranean Ocean are a deadly species of the giant jellyfish, called the Portuguese man-of-war. A smaller variety is found in the Pacific Ocean.\nOccasionally, some of these deadly Portuguese men-of-war are carried to our shores by the winds and the sea currents.\n\nThe giant Portuguese man-of-war as well as the smaller variety have the same convex body as the common jellyfish. Atop the body, however, is a crescent-shaped, hollow bladder filled with air serving as a balloon float buoying the jellyfish on top of the water. Trailing away are fishing tentacles that slowly writhe and reach for prey as deep as a hundred feet below the surface of the water. These deadly fishing lines are studded with thousands of stinging cells that contain poison almost as powerful as a cobra\'s venom. Studies made have identified the poison as \"neurotoxia\" - a protein substance that deranges\n\nthe nervous system. To small fishes this poison means instant death. So deadly is the poison that even a dead and dried Portuguese man-of-war stranded on the beach, when stepped on can cause extreme pain. The venom of this jellyfish can be stored for as long as six years without losing its potency.\n\nTo a man in the water, the venom acts with incredible speed. The victim writhes in pain and his breathing becomes difficult. He lapses into shock and his pulse becomes rapid and feeble. There is no known human antidote for the Portuguese man-of-war \'s venom, but common rubbing alcohol seems to neutralize the poison. Natives of the Atlantic shores wash the infected area with strong detergent. If none is handy, they use urine.\n\nSo, when out to sea, when wading or swimming, beware of a balloon- like half-moon floating on the water. It may be the dangerous jellyfish which might have come in with the tide-the Portuguese man-of-war\n\nTHE JELLYFISH VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. convex- a curved or rounded outward like the exterior of a sphere or circle. Example:1. Jellyfish has an umbrella-shaped body, convex, and smooth. 2. This fish has convex, and smooth body. 2.severe- causing a lot of physical pain or suffering. Example:1. The storm caused severe damage to the roof. 2. The patient is in severe pain.\n3. derange- to disturb the operation or functions of something. Example:1. Being stranded at night on a lonely road would derange everyone. 2. The storage room had all been deranged by the earthquake.\n4. incredible- difficult or impossible to believe. Example:1. The movie tells an incredible story. 2. I find his explanation incredible.\n5. antidote- something that corrects or improves the bad effects of something. Example:1. There is no antidote to this poison. 2. For him, racing motorcycle is a great antidote to boredom. THE JELLY FISH ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,21,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1487,759,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 1 · Part 2','open','Read carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. Something that corrects or improves the bad efforts of something.\n\n2. A curved or rounded outward like the exterior of a sphere or circle.\n\n3. Difficult or impossible to believe.\n\n4. Causing a lot of physical pain or suffering.\n\n5. To disturb the operation or functions of something.',NULL,'[]','Read carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,21,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1488,759,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 1 · Part 3','open','Answer in complete sentence.','1. What fishes are usually found near the shores especially during hot summer?\n\n2. What kind of jellyfish are found in the Philippine water?',NULL,'[]','Answer in complete sentence.',NULL,10,21,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1489,760,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 2 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE LION IS DYING\n\nThe lion is the largest and most celebrated of the cat family. It has an apparently large-sized head because of the great mane which covers its head, neck, and shoulders. By the time the lion is five or six years old, it can weigh more than 500 lbs and can be over 9 ½ feet long from the tip of its nose to the tip of its tail.\n\nThe lion has the reputation of being fearless, but it is more accurate to describe it as sly. Its instincts are like those of a house cat, and it hunts like a cat. It quietly stalks its prey, then making a short, quick bound, lands on it. It mauls and bites the head of its victim until its neck is broken, and its veins and arteries are torn out open.\n\nThe lion will eat anything it can find. While in the prime of its strength, it will hunt all sizes of deer, zebra and even buffaloes. However, as it grows older, it may live on rodents, scorpions and tiny creatures, even rotting meat. The lion was at one time known as the king of the jungle. The jungles of Africa, Asia Minor, and India were once the lion\'s domain. But man has been slowly but surely exterminating the species. The lion has disappeared in Asia Minor, Iran and even in many parts of Africa. The remnants of the lion family in Asia are now confined in Northwestern India\'s GIR forest, a reservation. There, some 160 lions remain, a pathetic remnant of a once prolific species.\n\nHowever, even in its sanctuary, the lions are losing their natural prey of deer and wild buffaloes because men have encroached on the sanctuary\n\nand have domesticated the buffaloes. The lions are forced to slaughter and feed on domesticated buffaloes and the herdsmen retaliate by killing the lions.\n\nThe king of the jungle is fast becoming extinct. Unless man would give the lions a place where they can live and multiply peacefully, and where their natural diet of deer and other animals can also live, many may never see a lion again except in museums or in picture books.\n\nTHE LION IS DYING VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. mane -long, thick hair growing from the neck of a lion. Example:1. The lion’s mane covers its head. 2. The lion’s mane is very long.\n2. accurate-free from mistakes and errors. Example:1. The model is accurate down to the tinniest details. 2. Her novel is historically accurate.\n3. prey -an animal that is hunted or killed by another animal for food. Example: 1. The lion stalked its prey. 2. The bird circles above looking for a prey.\n4. exterminate -to destroy or kill (a group of animals, people, etc.) completely. Example: 1. We decided to have the termites exterminated. 2. The invaders nearly exterminated the native people.\n5. sanctuary -a place where someone or something is protected or given shelter. Example:1. The house was a sanctuary of a runaway teen. 2. The sanctuary contains the altar of sacrifice. THE LION IS DYING ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,24,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1490,760,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 2 · Part 2','open','Read the sentences carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. Long, thick hair growing from the neck of a lion.\n\n2. A place where someone or something is protected or given shelter.\n\n3. Free from mistakes or errors.\n\n4. To destroy or kill (a group of animals, people, etc.) completely.\n\n5. An animal that is hunted or killed by another animal for food.',NULL,'[]','Read the sentences carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,24,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1491,760,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 2 · Part 3','open','Answer the following question in complete sentence.','1. What is the largest and most celebrated of the cat family?\n\n2. What animal is considered the king of the jungle, and why?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following question in complete sentence.',NULL,10,24,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1492,761,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 3 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE SMALLEST FISH IN THE WORLD\n\nBefore 1925, the \"tabios\" was thought to have been the smallest fish in the world. A full-grown \"tabios\" measured a fraction over eleven millimeters.\nThe \"tabios\" are found in Lake Buhi, in the Bicol Region of Luzon.\n\nIn 1925, Dr. Albert Herre, an ichthyologist was assigned by the American government to identify and describe Philippine fishes. From a collection of the Philippine National Museum, he found the prototypes of a still smaller type of fish. There were 75 of the tiny fish which he named Pandaka pygmaea in a bottle. They were so small that the \"tabios\" appeared big by comparison.\n\nWhen Dr. Herre went back to the United States, he sent a few specimens to the Stanford National Science Museum. Dr. Herre\'s findings were doubted because this fish remained a rarity. No one had ever seen a live specimen of the fish that Dr. Herre claimed was the smallest fish in the world.\n\nOn February 28, 1951, Ricardo Esguerra, one of the marine biologists working in the saltwater experiments station of the Bureau of Fisheries in Malabon, Rizal, excitedly called Up the Bureau\'s main office and announced that he had found live specimens of the Pandaka.\n\nInocencio Ronquillo, an expert of the Bureau, hurried to the experiment station and studied the tiny fish which had eluded scientists for many years. He measured and weighed them. To confirm his findings, Ronquillo wrote to the curator of Stanford University Museum of Natural Science and asked for some specimen of the fish that Dr. Herre had taken before. Finally, Dr. Leonard Schulz, the museum\'s representative pronounced that they were one and the same type. Thus after 25 years, the Philippines could at least claim to have the world\'s\n\nsmallest fish. The Pandaka has now been found in various parts of the Philippines.\nThe smallest fish is known under the scientific name of Pandaka pygmaea. Samples have been sent to museums and aquariums of cities all over the world.\n\nThe Pandaka\'s average length when full grown is 9.66 millimeters or about the size of a grain of rice. It has a moderately elongated body that is wedge-shaped when viewed from above. Its head is large in proportion to its size. Its eyes protrude. It has two rows of teeth on either side of the jaw, the outer teeth being larger and more widely set than the inner row.\n\nTHE SMALLEST FISH IN THE WORLD VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. specimen-something (such as an animal or plant) collected as an example of a particular kind of thing. Example :1. The church is a magnificent specimen of baroque architecture. 2. Her dance partner is a superb physical specimen.\n2. rarity-a person or thing that is not common or usual. Example :1. In most sports, athletes over the age of 50 are rarities. 2. Snow is a rarity in this part of the world.\n3. museum-a building in which interesting and valuable things (such as paintings and sculptures or scientific or historical objects) are collected and shown to the public. Example :1. This is a museum of natural history. 2. It is a trip to the museum and natural history.\n4. curator-a person who oversees the things in a mesuem, zoo, etc. Example :1. He has been a curator of the museum for ten successive years. 2. The curator of the museum was so strict.\n5. maul-to attack and injure (someone) in a way that cuts or tears skin. Example :1. A bear killed one of its prey and badly mauled the other. 2. The demonstrator claimed that they had been mauled by the police. THE SMALLEST FISH IN THE WORLD ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,27,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1493,761,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 3 · Part 2','open','Read carefully the sentences and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. Something collected as an example of a particular kind of thing.\n\n2. To attack and injure (someone) in a way that cut or tears skin.\n\n3. A person or thing that is not common or usual.\n\n4. A person who oversees the things in a museum, zoo, etc.\n\n5. A building in which interesting things and valuable things (such as paintings and sculptures or historical objects) are collected and shown to the public.',NULL,'[]','Read carefully the sentences and encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,27,'',1,1,'answer'),
