(1501,764,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 6 · Part 1','open','Read the sentences carefully and underline the letter of the correct answer.','NOT THE END OF THE WORLD\n\nWhen       news about a man that had been run over by a train was flashed, a police officer on late night patrol thought after     seeing   the crumpled         body, \"Doctors could not save this man, and even if he survives, what then?\" s\n\nThe man was motionless; his legs were    severed   far above the knees.\nSoon the sound of an ambulance       was heard.\n\nAfter a week, the man named Levy had a hazy memory of the traumatic happening. He remembered having swung into the slow-moving train and fell between the cars.\n\nLevy\'s friends and relatives showered him with love, care, and concern.\nDoctors spoke of surgery that might end up with artificial limbs. Knowing Levy as an independent man all his life, this medical decision hammered a note of pride deep within him.\n\nAfter many years of losing his legs, he said he did not want help from anybody. Speaking with a resolute and powerful voice, he said he wanted to see what he could do on his own- just God and him. He never doubted he was going to be all right.\n\nSince he was living alone, without a wife (because she deserted him) and without children, he did not have to worry about dependents. He was eligible for social security disability, which he turned down. His goal was to live a normal life.\n\nRefusing a wheelchair, Levy\'s kind of traveling has been a source of inspiration and happiness to people in his community. He travels on an 18-inch homemade cart that sits about five inches off the ground. With one wheel on the front and two on the rear, the contraption is put into motion by two\n\nsemicircular wooden and rubber \"walking blocks\" powered by the strong muscles of Levy\'s arms. He sits straight, tightly buckled into his cart.\n\nHe makes his rounds through the streets, waves at people, and chats with them about any topic under the sun.\n\nIf Levy senses any pity coming his way, he is quick to say, \"I am a lot better off than most people who feel sorry for me. I just wish they would look at me and try to understand what is in my heart, what makes me go. I think that is why God kept me around.\"\nWhat are in Levy\'s heart? Courage, faith, and will power. All these are in everybody\'s heart, but some people do not ever find them in themselves; they spend their time worrying about what they do not have or complaining.\n\nHe shows the importance of not giving up. He also shows us all that we can and must take control of our lives, no matter what happens to us.\n\nIt is nice to reflect on these values inspired by Levy. He made a courageous decision when he declared he would do things his way-alone with God\'s help. Against all worldly odds, he has prevailed and shown others something about himself. Most importantly, he has shown them something noble about themselves.\n\nNOT THE END OF THE WORLD VOCABULARY\n\n1.motion-an act or process of moving.\nExample: 1. She has a simple golf swing with no wasted motion.\n2. It is the rhythmic motions of the waves.\n2.hazy-not clear in thought or meaning; not certain.\n\nExample: 1. We had only a hazy view of the mountain.\n2. She has only hazy memories of the accident.\n3.resolute-having or showing a lot of determination.\nExample:1. She is a resolute competitor.\n2. She is a leader with a stern and resolute manner.\n4.contraption-a piece of equipment or machinery that is unusual or strange.\nExample:1. They wondered how the contraption worked.\n2. They built a contraption for easy use.\n5.severe-very bad, serious, or unpleasant.\nExample:1. The storm caused severe damaged to the roof.\n2. The patient is in severe pain.\n\nNOT THE END OF THE WORLD ASSESSMENT\n\n1. an act or process of moving.\n\n2. very bad, serious, or unpleasant.\n\n3. a piece of equipment or machinery that is unusual or strange.\n\n4. not clear in thought or meaning; not certain.\n\n5. having or showing a lot of determination.',NULL,'[]','Read the sentences carefully and underline the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,35,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1502,764,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 6 · Part 2','open','Answer the following question in complete sentence.','1. After being run over by a train, what happened to Levy?\n\n2. How did Levy’s friend treated him after losing his legs?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following question in complete sentence.',NULL,10,35,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1503,765,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 7 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE UNHAPPIEST WOMEN IN THE WORLD\n\nCompared    with     the women of other countries in the civilized world, the Japanese women are said to be the unhappiest women of the world.\nThese graceful, pretty women, who resemble butterflies in their brilliantly colored    kimonos, always looked happy.\n\nThe smile masks their emotions. To them, the highest achievement is to bear sorrow with a smile.\n\nFrom       childhood,      the Japanese woman is trained in this decorative surface happiness.\nEach movement of the head or of the hand is artificial, but it is learned so early in life it becomes second nature.\n\nThe Japanese custom requires that a wife shall know the family duties, etiquette, flower arrangement, and shall have graceful manners. She does not need to be intelligent, but she must be obedient to all her husband\'s wishes. A girl\'s entire education starts on the assumption that she is inferior to man, with no rights of her own.\n\nThere are only two careers open to an average Japanese woman:\nmarriage or being a geisha. She has no legal status in marriage. She cannot divorce her husband, but he can divorce her at will by sending her back to her family. If she is divorced because she is childless, she becomes an outcast.\n\nWhether rich or poor, the Japanese wife is a slave to her husband. She is the first to rise in the morning and the last to retire at night. When her husband comes home, she is obliged to greet him kneeling humbly at the doorway.\nEven in the wealthiest families, the wife serves her husband his breakfast and scrubs his back. She may not sit in a cushion in his presence. When walking with him, she always follows at a distance, carrying his bundles.\n\nWhen it is cold, the husband does not take off his coat to cover his wife; instead, the wife takes off her shawl and covers her husband. It offends the man\'s dignity if he were to hold his wife\'s coat or carry her bag or show her the slightest courtesy.\nEven a mother\'s pleasure on her children is restricted; because, according to law, they belong to the husband. The Japanese world belongs completely to the man; the wife has no place in it except to serve and bear his children.\n\nThe only woman who has a certain degree of freedom is the geisha. It is an old Japanese custom for a man to keep a geisha even immediately after his marriage. He makes her his comrade, and discuss his business with her, a companionship which is denied to his wife. The wife must accept her existence and is forced to adopt her children.\n\nThe Japanese woman\'s only release is suicide. Even in killing herself she is restricted. She may drown herself or leap into a volcano, but she is not worthy to commit hara-kiri. This honorable death is reserved only for the Japanese man:\n\nThe situations discussed here happened in the past. At present, Japan has evolved from the traditional customs and is now fast becoming more democratic especially among the women.\n\nTHE UNHAPPIEST WOMAN IN THE WORLD VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. Resemble -to look or be like (someone or something). Example:1. He strongly resembles his father in appearance and temperament. 2.Terrier dogs closely resemble each other.\n2. etiquette -the rules indicating the proper and polite way to behave. Example:1. Her failure to respond to the invitation was a serious breach of etiquette. 2.The couple exhibited poor etiquette when they left without saying good-bye to the host.\n3. cushion -a soft object or part that is used to make something (such as a seat) more comfortable. Example:1. The pile of leaves helped cushion his fall. 2.The tax cut is meant to cushion the blow of gas prices.\n4. offend -to cause (a person or group) to feel hurt, angry, or upset by something said or done. Example:1. His comments about minority groups offended many of us. 2.It offends me that you would make such a remark.\n5. existence -actual or present occurrence. Example:1. She began to doubt the existence of God. 2.They doubted the existence of the sea mermaids. THE UNHAPPIEST WOMAN IN THE WORLD ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,38,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1504,765,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 7 · Part 2','open','Read the phrases/sentences carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. to look or be like (someone or something).\n\n2. actual or present occurrence.\n\n3. the rules indicating the proper and polite way to behave.\n\n4. to cause (a person or group) to feel hurt or angry, or upset by something said or done.\n\n5. a soft object or part that is used to make something (such as a seat) more comfortable.',NULL,'[]','Read the phrases/sentences carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,38,'',1,1,'answer'),
