(1544,779,115,'post',1,'Post-test · Days 1-10','open','The following questions are based on the reading passages from your lessons. Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.','1. What is the shape of a common jellyfish\'s body?\n\n2. The Portuguese man-of-war is deadly because of its:\n\n3. According to the text, what can neutralize the Portuguese man-of-war\'s venom?\n\n4. Why is it more accurate to describe a lion as \"sly\" rather than \"fearless\"?\n\n5. Where are the last remaining lions in Asia confined?\n\n6. The main reason lions are becoming extinct is:\n\n7. What is the scientific name of the world\'s smallest fish found in the Philippines?\n\n8. Where was the \"tabios,\" once thought to be the smallest fish, found?\n\n9. Who finally confirmed the discovery of the live Pandaka pygmaea after 25 years?\n\n10. Which shark is described as the most vicious killer?\n\n11. Why do fishermen hunt sharks?\n\n12. What method do Sulu fishermen use to drive sharks away?\n\n13. What was Lauro\'s dream?\n\n14. Why did Lauro take the long way to school?\n\n15. What did Lauro do with the bag of money he found?\n\n16. What happened to Levy?\n\n17. How did Levy get around after losing his legs?\n\n18. What qualities did Levy show after his accident?\n\n19. Why were Japanese women of the past considering the unhappiest?\n\n20. What was the only career for a Japanese woman besides being a geisha?\n\n21. How was a Japanese wife expected to greet her husband when he came home?\n\n22. Who was the giant that David fought?\n\n23. What weapon did David use to defeat the giant?\n\n24. Why was David confident he could win the battle?\n\n25. Why was the old man sitting by the bridge?\n\n26. What was the old man\'s job in his town, San Carlos?\n\n27. What animals was the old man worried about?\n\n28. Who was the woman trying to talk to from the street?\n\n29. What was the man being transferred?\n\n30. Why did the man tell the woman to turn the child\'s face to the sun?\n\n31. Based on \"The Jellyfish,\" a convex body is:\n\n32. In \"The Lion is Dying,\" to exterminate a species means to:\n\n33. In \"The Smallest Fish,\" a specimen is:\n\n34. Based on \"Sharks,\" a denizen of the deep is:\n\n35. In \"The Most Wonderful Birthday Gift,\" Lauro\'s father squandered his money, meaning he:\n\n36. In \"Not the End of the World,\" Levy\'s contraption refers to his:\n\n37. In \"The Unhappiest Women,\" etiquette refers to:\n\n38. In \"The Story of David,\" to strangle an animal means to:\n\n39. In \"The Old Man at the Bridge,\" the peasants are:\n\n40. In \"A Conversation from the Third Floor,\" to gaze means to:\n\n41. What is a common theme between \"The Lion is Dying\" and \"Sharks, Monsters of the Deep\"?\n\n42. How are Levy\'s story (\"Not the End of the World\") and Lauro\'s story (\"The Most Wonderful Birthday Gift\") similar?\n\n43. Based on the passages, which statement is a FACT?\n\n44. What was the main cause of the old man\'s worry in \"The Old Man at the Bridge\"?\n\n45. The conversation in \"A Conversation from the Third Floor\" is mostly about:\n\n46. What lesson can be learned from \"The Most Wonderful Birthday Gift\"?\n\n47. In \"The Jellyfish,\" how does the author primarily organize the information about the Portuguese man-of-war?\n\n48. What is the author\'s purpose in \"The Lion is Dying\"?\n\n49. In \"Not the End of the World,\" what is the meaning of the title?\n\n50. Which passage primarily uses a narrative structure to tell a story with characters and a plot?',NULL,'[]','The following questions are based on the reading passages from your lessons. Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.',NULL,10,73,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1545,780,116,'post',1,'Post-test · Days 11-20','open','The following questions are based on the reading passages from your lessons (Days 11-20). Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.','1. Why did the parents call the doctor?\n\n2. What disease was the doctor afraid the child might have?\n\n3. How did the child react when the doctor first tried to examine her?\n\n4. What did the bear do to the man who could not climb the tree?\n\n5. What was the \"advice\" the bear gave?\n\n6. The moral of this story is:\n\n7. Why couldn\'t the crane eat the soup the fox served?\n\n8. How did the crane get back at the fox?\n\n9. The moral of this story is:\n\n10. What news did Mrs. Mallard receive at the beginning of the story?\n\n11. How did Mrs. Mallard initially reacts to the news?\n\n12. At the end of the story, Mrs. Mallard died because of:\n\n13. Why did the ants decide to teach the elephant a lesson?\n\n14. How did the ants punish the elephant?\n\n15. The moral of this story is:\n\n16. What was the problem with the farmer\'s sons?\n\n17. What did the single stick represent in the farmer\'s lesson?\n\n18. The moral of this story is:\n\n19. Why did the tortoise challenge the hare to a race?\n\n20. Why did the hare lose the race?\n\n21. The moral of this story is:\n\n22. Why did Dharma stop Bholaram from taking water?\n\n23. How did Birbal solve the problem?\n\n24. The moral of this story is:\n\n25. How did the fox get the cheese from the crow?\n\n26. What did the fox praise about the crow?\n\n27. The moral of this story is:\n\n28. Why did the lion let the rabbit go?\n\n29. Why couldn\'t the lion catch the deer?\n\n30. The moral of this story is:\n\n31. Based on \"The Use of Force,\" if a person is coaxed, they are:\n\n32. In \"A Friend in Need,\" an oath is a:\n\n33. In \"The Fox and the Crane,\" to humiliate someone means to:\n\n34. Based on \"The Story of an Hour,\" exhaustion means:\n\n35. In \"The Ant and the Elephant,\" an arrogant person is:\n\n36. In \"The Farmer and His Four Sons,\" unity means:\n\n37. In \"The Hare and the Tortoise,\" to mock someone means to:\n\n38. In \"The Farmer and the Well,\" a cunning person is:\n\n39. In \"The Fox and the Crow,\" to persuade someone means to:\n\n40. In \"The Greedy Lion,\" a greedy person:\n\n41. What is a common theme in both \"The Ant and the Elephant\" and \"The Hare and the Tortoise\"?\n\n42. How are the fox in \"The Fox and the Crane\" and the fox in \"The Fox and the Crow\" similar?\n\n43. Based on the passages, which statement is a FACT from \"The Story of an Hour\"?\n\n44. What was the main internal conflict for Mrs. Mallard?\n\n45. The lesson from \"The Farmer and the Well\" and \"The Greedy Lion\" both warn against:\n\n46. What lesson can be learned from both \"A Friend in Need\" and \"The Farmer and His Four Sons\"?\n\n47. In \"The Use of Force,\" the doctor\'s feelings towards the child become increasingly:\n\n48. What is the author\'s primary purpose in a fable like \"The Ant and the Elephant\"?\n\n49. In \"The Story of an Hour,\" what does the \"open window\" symbolize for Mrs. Mallard?\n\n50. Which passage features a character who uses clever wordplay and logic to solve a dispute?',NULL,'[]','The following questions are based on the reading passages from your lessons (Days 11-20). Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.',NULL,10,83,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1546,781,117,'pre',1,'Pre-test · Days 1-10','open','Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.','1. What is the best definition for \"antidote\"?\n\n2. If something is \"incredible,\" it is:\n\n3. The word \"derange\" most nearly means:\n\n4. A \"sanctuary\" is a place of:\n\n5. To \"squander\" money means to:\n\n6. Which sentence is punctuated correctly?\n\n7. What is the simple subject in this sentence: \"The quick, brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.\"\n\n8. Which word is a synonym for \"happy\"?\n\n9. What part of speech is the word \"run\" in this sentence: \"We will run in the race\"?\n\n10. Which sentence is written in the past tense?\n\n11. How did the library make Maria feel?\n\n12. What did Maria look for in the library?\n\n13. The main idea of this passage is:\n\n14. The person who writes a story is called the:\n\n15. The time and place where a story happens is called the:\n\n16. The main character in a story is called the:\n\n17. \"The wind whispered through the trees\" is an example of:\n\n18. What is the central message or lesson of a story called?\n\n19. What is the primary purpose of a topic sentence in a paragraph?\n\n20. If a story is written from the perspective of \"I,\" what point of view is it?\n\n21. What is the best source to find the definition of a word?\n\n22. What is the first sentence of an essay called that grabs the reader\'s attention?\n\n23. Which of these is a complete sentence?\n\n24. What do we call a word that sounds like what it means (e.g., buzz, pop)?\n\n25. What is the term for a group of lines in a poem?\n\n26. What is the opposite meaning of the word \"severe\"?\n\n27. Which word is an adjective?\n\n28. The phrase \"as brave as a lion\" is an example of a:\n\n29. What is the purpose of a conclusion in an essay?\n\n30. Which of these is a fiction book?\n\n31. What is the term for the most exciting part of a story?\n\n32. Which source would be most useful for finding a synonym?\n\n33. What does the prefix \"un-\" mean in the word \"unhappy\"?\n\n34. Which sentence uses correct subject-verb agreement?\n\n35. What is the term for the dictionary definition of a word?\n\n36. In a story, the problem that the main character faces is called the:\n\n37. Which part of a book lists the chapters and their page numbers?\n\n38. What is the best way to identify the main idea of a paragraph?\n\n39. \"He was a rock, strong and unmoving.\" This is an example of a:\n\n40. Which word is a verb?\n\n41. What is the term for giving human qualities to non-human things?\n\n42. Which of these is a compound word?\n\n43. What is the primary purpose of persuasive writing?\n\n44. Which sentence is a fact?\n\n45. What is the term for the feelings and ideas associated with a word?\n\n46. What do you call the series of events that make up a story?\n\n47. Which of these is a proper noun?\n\n48. What is the best way to check for errors in your writing?\n\n49. What is the name for a word that joins two ideas or clauses (e.g., and, but, or)?\n\n50. The final part of a story where the conflict is resolved is called the:',NULL,'[]','Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.',NULL,10,7,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1547,782,118,'pre',1,'Pre-test · Days 11-20','open','Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.','1. What is the best definition for coax?\n\n2. If someone is distrustful, they are:\n\n3. The word despise most nearly means:\n\n4. A person who is arrogant:\n\n5. To apologize means to:\n\n6. Which sentence is punctuated correctly?\n\n7. What is the simple predicate (verb) in this sentence: \"The greedy lion chased the deer.\"\n\n8. Which word is an antonym for sincerity?\n\n9. What part of speech is the word huge in this sentence: \"The elephant had a huge body.\"?\n\n10. Which sentence is written in the future tense?\n\n11. Why were the ants angry?\n\n12. How did the ants teach the elephant a lesson?\n\n13. What was the result of the ants\' action?\n\n14. The central lesson or message of a story is called the:\n\n15. The main problem that characters face in a story is the:\n\n16. \"The wind whispered through the trees\" is an example of:\n\n17. A story that uses animals as characters to teach a lesson is called a:\n\n18. The high point or most exciting part of a story is the:\n\n19. What is the primary purpose of a fable?\n\n20. If a character is described as \"cunning,\" they are most likely to be:\n\n21. What is the best source to find a synonym for the word \"huge\"?\n\n22. What is the term for the feeling of sadness or pity you feel for a character?\n\n23. Which of these is a complete sentence?\n\n24. What do we call the narrator of a story who is also a character in the story?\n\n25. What is the term for a word that means the opposite of another word?\n\n26. What is the opposite meaning of the word victory?\n\n27. Which word is a noun?\n\n28. The phrase \"as slow as a tortoise\" is an example of a:\n\n29. What is the purpose of the beginning of a story (exposition)?\n\n30. Which of these is a theme that might be found in a fable?\n\n31. What does the prefix \"re-\" mean in the word \"rewrite\"?\n\n32. Which sentence uses correct subject-verb agreement?\n\n33. What is the term for the actual, dictionary definition of a word?\n\n34. In the story \"The Farmer and His Four Sons,\" what does the bundle of sticks symbolize?\n\n35. Which part of a story comes after the climax?\n\n36. What is the term for a conversation between two or more characters?\n\n37. Which word best describes the fox in \"The Fox and the Crow\"?\n\n38. What is the best way to identify the moral of a fable?\n\n39. \"He was a lion in battle.\" This is an example of a:\n\n40. Which word is a verb?\n\n41. What is the term for an exaggerated statement used for effect?\n\n42. Which of these is a compound word?\n\n43. What is the primary purpose of narrative writing?\n\n44. Which sentence is an opinion?\n\n45. What is the term for the feelings and ideas associated with a word (beyond its dictionary definition)?\n\n46. What do you call the perspective from which a story is told?\n\n47. Which of these is a proper noun?\n\n48. What is the best way to understand a character\'s traits?\n\n49. What is the name for a word that describes a verb (e.g., slowly, quickly)?\n\n50. The final part of a story where the conflict is resolved is called the:',NULL,'[]','Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.',NULL,10,14,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1548,783,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 1 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE JELLYFISH\n\nOur oceans, seas, gulfs, and bays teem with jellyfish.\nThey are found near the shores, especially during the hot summer months. Most common are the transparent jellyfish which are hardly seen in the water. They have an umbrella- shaped body, convex and smooth when seen from above.\nFrom the underside hang four to 20 tentacles with small cup- shaped disks used by the jellyfish to catch its prey of tiny fish, crustaceans, or planktons.\n\nMost of the jellyfish found in Philippine waters are harmless.\nSome species, however, those that have darker shaded bodies, can produce severe itching. Others can cause painful     welts   when        their tentacles attach themselves to the human body.\n\nFound in the Atlantic and the Mediterranean Ocean are a deadly species of the giant jellyfish, called the Portuguese man-of-war. A smaller variety is found in the Pacific Ocean.\nOccasionally, some of these deadly Portuguese man-of-war are carried to our shores by the winds and the sea currents.\n\nThe giant Portuguese man-of-war as well as the smaller variety have the same convex body as the common jellyfish. Atop the body, however, is a crescent-shaped, hollow bladder filled with air serving as a balloon float buoying the jellyfish on top of the water. Trailing away are fishing tentacles that slowly writhe and reach for prey as deep as a hundred feet below the surface of the water. These deadly fishing lines are studded with thousands of stinging cells that contain poison almost as powerful as a cobra\'s venom. Studies made have identified the poison as \"neurotoxia\" - a protein substance that deranges the nervous system. To small fishes this poison means instant death. So deadly is the poison that even a dead and dried Portuguese man-of-war stranded on\n\nthe beach, when stepped on can cause extreme pain. The venom of this jellyfish can be stored for as long as six years without losing its potency.\n\nTo a man in the water, the venom acts with incredible speed. The victim writhes in pain and his breathing becomes difficult. He lapses into shock and his pulse becomes rapid and feeble. There is no known human antidote for the Portuguese man-of-war \'s venom, but common rubbing alcohol seems to neutralize the poison. Natives of the Atlantic shores wash the infected area with strong detergent. If none is handy, they use urine.\n\nSo when out to sea, when wading or swimming, beware of a balloon- like half moon floating on the water. It may be the dangerous jellyfish which might have come in with the tide-the Portuguese man-of-war\n\nTHE JELLYFISH VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. convex- a curved or rounded outward like the exterior of a sphere or circle. Example:1. Jellyfish has an umbrella-shaped body, convex, and smooth. 2. This fish has convex, and smooth body. 2.severe- causing a lot of physical pain or suffering. Example:1. The storm caused severe damage to the roof. 2. The patient is in severe pain.\n3. derange- to disturb the operation or functions of something. Example:1. Being stranded at night on a lonely road would derange everyone. 2. The storage room had all been deranged by the earthquake.\n4. incredible- difficult or impossible to believe. Example:1. The movie tells an incredible story. 2. I find his explanation pretty incredible.\n5. antidote- something that corrects or improves the bad effects of something. Example:1. There is no antidote to this poison. 2. For him, racing motorcycle is a great antidote to boredom. THE JELLY FISH ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,22,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1549,783,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 1 · Part 2','open','Read carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. Something that corrects or improves the bad efforts of something.\n\n2. A curved or rounded outward like the exterior of a sphere or circle.\n\n3. Difficult or impossible to believe.\n\n4. Causing a lot of physical pain or suffering.\n\n5. To disturb the operation or functions of something.',NULL,'[]','Read carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,22,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1550,783,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 1 · Part 3','open','Answer in complete sentence.','1. What fishes are usually found near the shores especially during hot summer?\n\n2. What kind of jellyfish are found in the Philippine water?',NULL,'[]','Answer in complete sentence.',NULL,10,22,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1551,784,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 2 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE LION IS DYING\n\nThe lion is the largest and most celebrated of the cat family. It has an apparently large-sized head because of the great mane which covers its head, neck, and shoulders. By the time the lion is five or six years old, it can weigh more than 500 lbs and can be over 9 ½ feet long from the tip of its nose to the tip of its tail.\n\nThe lion has the reputation of being fearless, but it is more accurate to describe it as sly. Its instincts are like those of a house cat and it hunts like a cat.\nIt quietly stalks its prey, then making a short, quick bound, lands on it. It mauls and bites the head of its victim until its neck is broken, and its veins and arteries are torn out open.\n\nThe lion will eat anything it can find. While in the prime of its strength, it will hunt all sizes of deer, zebra and even buffaloes. However, as it grows older, it may live on rodents, scorpions and tiny creatures, even rotting meat. The lion was at one time known as the king of the jungle. The jungles of Africa, Asia Minor, and India were once the lion\'s domain. But man has been slowly but surely exterminating the species. The lion has disappeared in Asia Minor, Iran and even in many parts of Africa. The remnants of the lion family in Asia are now confined in Northwestern India\'s GIR forest, a reservation. There, some 160 lions remain, a pathetic remnant of a once prolific species.\n\nHowever, even in its sanctuary, the lions are losing their natural prey of deer and wild buffaloes because men have encroached on the sanctuary\n\nand have domesticated the buffaloes. The lions are forced to slaughter and feed on domesticated buffaloes and the herdsmen retaliate by killing the lions.\n\nThe king of the jungle is fast becoming extinct. Unless man would give the lions a place where they can live and multiply peacefully, and where their natural diet of deer and other animals can also live, many may never see a lion again except in museums or in picture books.\n\nTHE LION IS DYING VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. mane -long, thick hair growing from the neck of a lion. Example:1. The lion’s mane covers its head. 2. The lion’s mane is very long.\n2. accurate-free from mistakes and errors. Example:1. The model is accurate down to the tinniest details. 2. Her novel is historically accurate.\n3. prey -an animal that is hunted or killed by another animal for food. Example: 1. The lion stalked its prey. 2. The bird circles above looking for a prey.\n4. exterminate -to distroy or kill (a group of animals, people, etc.) completely. Example: 1. We made arrangements to have the termites exterminated. 2. The invaders nearly exterminated the native people.\n5. sanctuary -a place where someone or something is protected or given shelter. Example:1. The house was a sanctuary of a runaway teens. 2. The sanctuary contains the altar of sacrifice. THE LION IS DYING ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,25,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1552,784,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 2 · Part 2','open','Read the sentences carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. Long, thick hair growing from the neck of a lion.\n\n2. A place where someone or something is protected or given shelter.\n\n3. Free from mistakes or errors.\n\n4. To distroy or kill (a group of animals, people, etc.) completely.\n\n5. An animal that is hunted or killed by another animal for food.',NULL,'[]','Read the sentences carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,25,'',1,1,'answer'),
