(1554,785,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 3 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE SMALLEST FISH IN THE WORLD\n\nBefore 1925, the \"tabios\" was thought to have been the smallest fish in the world. A full-grown \"tabios\" measured a fraction over eleven millimetres.\nThe \"tabios\" are found in Lake Buhi, in the Bicol Region of Luzon.\n\nIn 1925, Dr. Albert Herre, an ichthyologist was assigned by the American government to identify and describe Philippine fishes. From a collection of the Philippine National Museum, he found the prototypes of a still smaller type of fish. There were 75 of the tiny fish which he named Pandaka pygmaea in a bottle. They were so small that the \"tabios\" appeared big by comparison.\n\nWhen Dr. Herre went back to the United States, he sent a few specimens to the Stanford National Science Museum. Dr. Herre\'s findings were doubted because this fish remained a rarity. No one had ever seen a live specimen of the fish that Dr. Herre claimed was the smallest fish in the world.\n\nOn February 28, 1951, Ricardo Esguerra, one of the marine biologists working in the saltwater experiments station of the Bureau of Fisheries in Malabon, Rizal, excitedly called Up the Bureau\'s main office and announced that he had found live specimens of the Pandaka.\n\nInocencio Ronquillo, an expert of the Bureau, hurried to the experiment station and studied the tiny fish which had eluded scientists for many years. He measured and weighed them. To confirm his findings, Ronquillo wrote to the curator of Stanford University Museum of Natural Science and asked for some\n\nspecimen of the fish that Dr. Herre had taken before. Finally, Dr. Leonard Schulz, the museum\'s representative pronounced that they were one and the same type. Thus after 25 years, the Philippines could at least claim to have the world\'s smallest fish. The Pandaka has now been found in various parts of the Philippines.\nThe smallest fish is known under the scientific name of Pandaka pygmaea. Samples have been sent to museums and aquariums of cities all over the world.\n\nThe Pandaka\'s average length when full grown is 9.66 millimeters or about the size of a grain of rice. It has a moderately elongated body that is wedgeshaped when viewed from above. Its head is large in proportion to its size. Its eyes protrude. It has two rows of teeth on either side of the jaw, the outer teeth being larger and more widely set than the inner row.\n\nTHE SMALLEST FISH IN THE WORLD VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. specimen-something (such as an animal or plant) collected as an example of a particular kind of thing. Example :1. The church is a magnificent specimen of baroque architecture. 2. Her dance partner is a superb physical specimen.\n2. rarity-a person or thing that is not common or usual. Example :1. In most sports, athletes over the age of 50 are rarities. 2. Snow is a rarity in this part of the world.\n3. museum-a building in which interesting and valuable things (such as paintings and sculptures or scientific or historical objects) are collected and shown to the public. Example :1. This is a museum of natural history. 2. It is a trip to the museum and natural history.\n4. curator-a person who is in charge of the things in a mesuem, zoo, etc. Example :1. He has been a curator of the museum for ten successive years. 2. The curator of the museum was so strict.\n5. maul-to attack and injure (someone) in a way that cuts or tears skin. Example :1. A bear killed one of its prey, and badly mauled the other. 2. The demonstrator claimed that they had been mauled by the police. THE SMALLEST FISH IN THE WORLD ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,28,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1555,785,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 3 · Part 2','open','Read carefully athe sentences and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. Something collected as an example of a particular kind of thing.\n\n2. To attack and injure (someone) in a way that cut or tears skin.\n\n3. A person or thing that is not common or usual.\n\n4. A person who is in charge of the things in a museum, zoo, etc.\n\n5. A building in which interesting things and valuable things (such as paintings and sculptures or historical objects) are collected and shown to the public.',NULL,'[]','Read carefully athe sentences and encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,28,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1556,785,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 3 · Part 3','open','Answer the following question in complete sentence.','1. What is the scientific name of the smallest fish found in the Philippines?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following question in complete sentence.',NULL,10,28,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1557,786,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 4 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','SHARKS, MONSTERS OF THE DEEP\n\nTo many, sharks are the most fearful of all denizens of the sea. Some sharks are monsters of more than 30 feet long. They are slow, but powerful and dangerous, too.\n\nAmong the sharks, the tiger shark is the most vicious killer. It is not very big. It is only six to eight feet long. This species hunts in pairs or in packs, and attacks on the slightest excuse. The attack and viciousness of the tiger shark is unbelievably swift and relentless.\n\nMan has discovered that he will never be a match for any shark in the water. But because he had to go to sea for his own livelihood, he had to find a way to keep himself free from the attack of sharks.\n\nHunting sharks is a hazardous operation. Sharks are hunted mostly because of the menace they pose. Fishermen who depend on the sea for their livelihood go on shark hunting expeditions with the sole purpose of ridding their fishing grounds of these monsters.\n\nFishermen go in groups to shark-infested zones. They use decomposed meat as shark baits. As the sharks surface, they are shot with harpoons. The wounded sharks are attacked by other sharks, so the killing continues-man against shark, and shark against shark.\nThe Malaysians have developed a safe but sure method of killing sharks.\nThey toss hundreds of iron spikes wrapped in chunks of meat to the sharks.\nWhen the shark gulps the meat. he is as good as dead. The shark suffers from\n\nstomach ache and while it writhes in pain, its movement will cause balls of spikes to make its way into its innards and make it die.\n\nAnother method used by fishermen is to tow baited hooks behind slow- cruising fishing boats. When the sharks dart in for the bait, they are shot dead by the fishermen safe aboard their boats.\n\nSulu fishermen have discovered that there was one sound that sharks do not like. It is a noise made by rubbing and banging together an iron harpoon head and whetstone under water. When the Sulu skin-divers dive for mother- of-pearls or for underwater seafood in the shark-infested Sulu Sea, they take along with them a harpoon and whetstone, which they rub and bang together to drive sharks away.\n\nSHARKS, MONSTER OF THE DEEP VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. denizen-a person, animal, or plant that lives in or often is found in a particular place or region. Example :1. That animal is one of those muscle-bound denizens of the gym. 2. The polar bear is an iconic denizen of the snowy Arctic.\n2. vicious-very violent and cruel. Example :1. We heard its vicious tone of voice. 2. Although you’re upset with her, but there’s no need to be vicious.\n3. hazardous-involving risk or danger. Example :1. It was a hazardous voyage. 2. These are hazardous chemicals that can cause death if inhaled.\n4. writhe- to twist your body from side to side. Example :1. She lays on the floor, writhing in pain. 2. It is a nest of writhing snakes.\n5. innards-the internal organs of a person or an animal. Example :1. They are going to examine the innards of a frog for science class. 2. Consumers are warned against poking around in the innards of the air conditioner. SHARKS, MONSTER OF THE DEEP ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,31,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1558,786,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 4 · Part 2','open','Read carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. A person, animal, or planet that lives in or often found in a particular place or region.\n\n2. The internal organs of a person or an animal.\n\n3. Very violent and cruel.\n\n4. Involving risk or danger.\n\n5. To twist your body from side to side.',NULL,'[]','Read carefully and encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,31,'',1,1,'answer'),
