(1574,794,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 12 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','A FRIEND IN NEED IS A FRIEND INDEED\n\nOnce upon a time two very close friends lived in a village.   As  time passed          their friendship     grew thicker and thicker.\nNow      they  were grown up men.\nOne day they decided to go and find work. They set off.\nBefore leaving their village, they promised to stand by each other through thick and thin. They took oath of sincerity and assured each other of help in the hour of need. They had to pass through a jungle. They had not gone far into the thick forest when they found themselves face to face with a big bear. The bear began to move towards them. They felt helpless and terrified.\nOne of them knew how to climb a tree. He at once caught hold of a strong branch and swung up the tree. The other could not climb. He had heard that the bear does not eat the dead. So, he lay down on the ground and held his breath pretending to be dead. Meanwhile, the bear came to him and shifted him. The bear left him.\nWhen the bear had gone out of sight, the friend on the tree came down. He asked his friend, \"Dear friend, what did the bear whisper in your ear?\"\nThe other replied quickly, \"The bear advised me not to trust a selfish friend.\"\nSaying this he left him and went away.\nMoral: A Friend in Need is a Friend Indeed A FRIEND IN NEED IS A FRIEND INDEED VOCABULARY\n\n1.village -a small town in the country; a settlement usually larger than a hamlet and smaller than a town.\nExample:       1. The entire villages come to see the parade.\n2.We stayed in a charming bed-and-breakfast in a lakeside village.\n2.oath-a formal and serious promise to tell the truth or to do something.\nExample:       1. It is an oath to defend the nation.\n2.He uttered an oath and walk away.\n3.jungle-an impenetrable thicket or tangled mass of tropical vegetation.\nExample:       1. That is their way of living in the corporate jungle.\n2.Wild animals live in a jungle.\n\nVocabulary\n4. sincerity-the quality or state of being sincere; honesty of mind. Example:       1. His sincerity is the cause of their friendship. 2.Your sincerity will be measured in times of needs.\n5. selfish-having or showing concern only for yourself and not for the needs or feelings of other people. Example:       1. She is interested only in her own selfish concern. 2.Her selfishness could bring her down. A FRIEND IN NEED IS A FRIEND INDEED ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,56,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1575,794,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 12 · Part 2','open','Carefully read the sentences. Look for the synonym of the underlined words. Encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. We stayed in a small house in the village.\n\n2. After the oath taking ceremony, I will go home.\n\n3. This is the entry way to the jungle.\n\n4. His sincerity was measured during the times of his friend’s failures.\n\n5. Her selfishness is the reason why she failed.',NULL,'[]','Carefully read the sentences. Look for the synonym of the underlined words. Encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,56,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1576,794,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 12 · Part 3','open','Answer the following question in complete sentence.','1. Who lived in the village?\n\n2. What happened to the two boys when they met a big bear in the jungle?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following question in complete sentence.',NULL,10,56,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1577,795,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 13 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE FOX AND THE CRANE\nOne day, a wicked fox invited a crane for dinner. The crane was very happy with the invitation. She reached the fox\'s home on time and knocked at the door with her long beak.\nShe has been excited the whole day and was extremely hungry.\nThe fox soon served some soup in shallow bowls for both.\nThe crane was surprised and angry too, as she could not have soup at all. Her long beak could not help her drink from the bowl.\nBut the fox licked up his soup quickly. Feeling cheated and humiliated, she decided to teach the fox a lesson soon.\nShe did not show her anger and behaved politely with the fox. Before leaving she invited him for dinner the next day. She made rice porridge for the dinner and served it in two tall narrow vases.\nShe happily drank her porridge from the vase. The fox tried hard but could not, as the neck of the vessel was narrow. The fox understood everything. He had learned his lesson and had to go home hungry.\nMoral: A selfish act backfires sooner or later.\n\nTHE FOX AND THE CRANE VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. extreme-very great in degree. Example:    1. The plant is sensitive to extreme heat and cold. 2. They are living in extreme poverty\n2. shallow-having a small distance from the bottom from the surface or highest point. Example:     1. The shallow end of the pool is only three feet deep. 2. She could only take shallow breath.\n3. lick-to pass the tongue over (a surface, an object, etc.) Example:      1. He licked the stamp before putting it in an envelope. 2. The dog licked at the plate.\n4. cheat-to break a rule or law usually to gain an advantage at something. Example:     1. The players were accused of cheating. 2. He cheated, that is why she won the contest.\n5. humiliate-to make (someone) feel very ashamed or foolish. Example:     1. I hope I don’t humiliate myself during the presentation. 2.He accused her of trying to humiliate him in public. THE FOX AND THE CRANE ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,58,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1578,795,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 13 · Part 2','open','Read each sentence carefully and put a check (/) mark before each number if it gives the intended meaning and (X) mark if it does not. ___1. Your skin is sensitive to extreme heat. (Your skin cannot survive to extreme heat.) ___2. The shallow end of the river is only two feet deep. (It is very deep.) ___3. The cat licked at the plate. (It eats in a plate.) ___4. The players were accused of cheating. (They were cheaters.) ___5. He accused him of humiliating him in the public. (He is happy with him.) Direction: Answer the following question in complete sentence.','1. What happened when the fox invited the crane for a dinner?\n\n2. What happened when the crane invited the fox for a dinner?',NULL,'[]','Read each sentence carefully and put a check (/) mark before each number if it gives the intended meaning and (X) mark if it does not. ___1. Your skin is sensitive to extreme heat. (Your skin cannot survive to extreme heat.) ___2. The shallow end of the river is only two feet deep. (It is very deep.) ___3. The cat licked at the plate. (It eats in a plate.) ___4. The players were accused of cheating. (They were cheaters.) ___5. He accused him of humiliating him in the public. (He is happy with him.) Direction: Answer the following question in complete sentence.',NULL,10,58,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1579,796,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 14 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE STORY OF AN HOUR\nBy: Kate Chopin (1894)\n\nKnowing       that     Mrs.\nMallard was afflicted with a heart trouble, great care was taken to break to her as gently as possible the news of her husband\'s death.\nIt    was     her     sister Josephine who told her, in broken sentences; veiled hints that       revealed      in    half concealing. Her husband\'s friend Richards was there, too, near her. It was he who had been in the newspaper office when intelligence of the railroad        disaster       was received,        with       Brently Mallard\'s name leading the list of \"killed.\" He had only taken the time to assure himself of its truth by a second telegram, and had hastened to forestall any less careful, less tender friend in bearing the sad message.\nShe did not hear the story as many women have heard the same, with a paralyzed inability to accept its significance. She wept at once, with sudden, wild abandonment, in her sister\'s arms. When the storm of grief had spent itself, she went away to her room alone. She would have no one follow her.\nThere stood, facing the open window, a comfortable, roomy armchair.\nInto this she sank, pressed down by a physical exhaustion that haunted her body and seemed to reach into her soul.\nShe could see in the open square before her house the tops of trees that were all aquiver with the new spring life. The delicious breath of rain was in the air. In the street below a peddler was crying his wares. The notes of a distant song which some one was singing reached her faintly, and countless sparrows were twittering in the eaves.\nThere were patches of blue sky showing here and there through the clouds that had met and piled one above the other in the west facing her window.\nShe sat with her head thrown back upon the cushion of the chair, quite motionless, except when a sob came up into her throat and shook her, as a child who has cried itself to sleep continues to sob in its dreams.\n\nShe was young, with a fair, calm face, whose lines bespoke repression and even a certain strength. But now there was a dull stare in her eyes, whose gaze was fixed away off yonder on one of those patches of blue sky. It was not a glance of reflection, but rather indicated a suspension of intelligent thought.\nThere was something coming to her and she was waiting for it, fearfully.\nWhat was it? She did not know; it was too subtle and elusive to name. But she felt it, creeping out of the sky, reaching toward her through the sounds, the scents, the color that filled the air.\nNow her bosom rose and fell tumultuously. She was beginning to recognize this thing that was approaching to possess her, and she was striving to beat it back with her will--as powerless as her two white slender hands would have been. When she abandoned herself a little whispered word escaped her slightly parted lips. She said it over and over under the breath: \"free, free, free!\"\nThe vacant stare and the look of terror that had followed it went from her eyes.\nThey stayed keen and bright. Her pulses beat fast, and the coursing blood warmed and relaxed every inch of her body.\nShe did not stop to ask if it were or were not a monstrous joy that held her. A clear and exalted perception enabled her to dismiss the suggestion as trivial. She knew that she would weep again when she saw the kind, tender hands folded in death; the face that had never looked save with love upon her, fixed and gray and dead. But she saw beyond that bitter moment a long procession of years to come that would belong to her absolutely. And she opened and spread her arms out to them in welcome.\nThere would be no one to live for during those coming years; she would live for herself. There would be no powerful will bending hers in that blind persistence with which men and women believe they have a right to impose a private will upon a fellow-creature. A kind intention or a cruel intention made the act seem no less a crime as she looked upon it in that brief moment of illumination.\nAnd yet she had loved him--sometimes. Often she had not. What did it matter! What could love, the unsolved mystery, count for in the face of this possession of self-assertion which she suddenly recognized as the strongest impulse of her being! \"Free! Body and soul free!\" she kept whispering.\nJosephine was kneeling before the closed door with her lips to the keyhold, imploring for admission. \"Louise, open the door! I beg; open the door-\n-you will make yourself ill. What are you doing, Louise? For heaven\'s sake open the door.\"\n\"Go away. I am not making myself ill.\" No; she was drinking in a very elixir of life through that open window.\nHer fancy was running riot along those days ahead of her. Spring days, and summer days, and all sorts of days that would be her own. She breathed a quick prayer that life might be long. It was only yesterday she had thought with a shudder that life might be long.\nShe arose at length and opened the door to her sister\'s importunities.\nThere was a feverish triumph in her eyes, and she carried herself unwittingly like a goddess of Victory. She clasped her sister\'s waist, and together they descended the stairs. Richards stood waiting for them at the bottom.\n\nSomeone was opening the front door with a latchkey. It was Brently Mallard who entered, a little travel-stained, composedly carrying his grip-sack and umbrella. He had been far from the scene of the accident, and did not even know there had been one. He stood amazed at Josephine\'s piercing cry; at Richards\' quick motion to screen him from the view of his wife.\nWhen the doctors came they said she had died of heart disease--of the joy that kills.\n\nTHE STORY OF AN HOUR VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. exhaustion -the state of being extremely tired; the state of being exhausted. Example:    1. He worked to the point of completely exhaustion. 2. Our natural resources is exhausted.\n2. haunted -to keep coming back to the mind of (someone) in a way that makes a person sad or upset. Example:    1. Some people',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,60,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1580,796,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 14 · Part 2','open','Choose and underline the word inside the parenthesis that completes the sentence.','1. The lower border of a roof that overhangs the wall is called (exhaustion, aquiver, raves).\n\n2. The state of being extremely tired is called (raves, haunted, exhaustion).\n\n3. To keep coming back to the mind of (someone) in a way that makes a person sad or upset is called ______. (haunted, exhaustion, aquiver)\n\n4. This means shaking or trembling because of strong emotion. . (aquiver, raves, haunted)\n\n5. This means to shake or tremble because of fear or cold. (shudder, raves, exhaustion)',NULL,'[]','Choose and underline the word inside the parenthesis that completes the sentence.',NULL,10,60,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1581,796,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 14 · Part 3','open','Answer the following question in complete sentence.','1. What happened to the husband of Mrs. Mallard?\n\n2. What was the effect of the death of Mr. Mallard to her wife?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following question in complete sentence.',NULL,10,60,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1582,797,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 15 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE ANT AND THE ELEPHANT\nOnce upon a time there lived an elephant in a jungle. He was very arrogant and short-tempered. He was very proud of his strength and huge body. The other animals in the jungle feared him a lot. He enjoyed doing things that showed off his strength and always troubled other small animals in the jungle.\nIn that jungle, there also lived a family of ants in a tiny hole. The ant used to despise the elephant for his haughty nature. One day, when the ants were away to collect food, the elephant stopped them and sprayed a trunk full of water on them.\nThe ants got angry and said\" You should not trouble others for your pleasure. The elephant shouted angrily and said,\" You tiny creatures, keep quiet, or I will crush you to death.\" Now, the ants decided to teach the arrogant animal a lesson for life. They slowly crept under his trunk and spread to his ears and started biting him. The elephant became restless and started crying for help.\nNone of the animals came forward to rescue him. They were happy to see him getting punished for his acts. He kept apologizing to the ants, but they continued to bite him. When they were sure that he has learned his lesson, they let him go and said, \"This is how others feel when you hurt them.\" The ants marched off leaving a wailing elephant there. Then on, the elephant never troubled anyone in the jungle.\nMoral: Pride and arrogance never go unpunished.\n\nTHE ANT AND THE ELEPHANT VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. huge-very large, very great in size, amount or degree. Example:     1.The store is having a huge sale tomorrow. 2.Renovating a house is a huge work.\n2. despise-to dislike (someone or something) very much. Example:     1.She was despised as a hypocrite. 2.I despise salty foods, so I don’t eat them.\n3. arrogant-having or showing the insulting attitude of people who believe that they are better, smarter, or more important than other people. Example:     1.She’s first in her class, but she’s not arrogant about it. 2.The arrogant young lawyer declared himself as the most outstanding person compared to others.\n4. apologize-to express regret for doing or saying something wrong to give or make an apology. Example:     1.He apologized to his wife and children for losing his temper. 2.We apologize for the mistake and promise that it won’t happen again.\n5. wail-to make a loud, long cry of sadness or pain. Example:     1.The child started wailing after she stumbled and fell. 2.No!” I don’t want to go”, she wailed. THE ANT AND THE ELEPHANT ASSESSMENT (LETTER SCRAMBLE)\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,64,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1583,797,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 15 · Part 2','open','Arrange and unscramble the letters to form a word. Remember that the words are taken from the story.','1. e g u h           =____________means very large.\n\n2. e s i p s e d = ______ means to dislike someone or something very much.\n\n3. r o a r g a t n   =____________having or showing the insulting attitude.\n\n4. g i e z o l o p a =____________to express regret for doing or saying something wrong.\n\n5. l a i w         =____________to make a loud, long cry of sadness or pain.',NULL,'[]','Arrange and unscramble the letters to form a word. Remember that the words are taken from the story.',NULL,10,64,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1584,797,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 15 · Part 3','open','Answer the following question in complete sentence.','1. Give the reason why the elephant was arrogant.\n\n2. What was the lesson learned by the elephant?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following question in complete sentence.',NULL,10,64,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1585,798,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 16 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','A FARMER AND HIS FOUR SONS\n\nOnce there lived an old farmer in a village. He had four sons. They were always quarrelling with each other. The farmer tried hard to bring unity among them. He would request them to live with love and peace, but they would never listen to his advice. He was always worried about their future.\nOne day, he fell sick. He thought that it was his chance to bring harmony among his sons. So, he called his sons and asked each of them to bring a few sticks. They brought the sticks to their father. The farmer asked one of them to tie the sticks in a bundle. He then asked each of them to try and break the sticks.\nOne by one, all the four sons tried to break the bundle, but failed. Then, the farmer untied the bundle. He gave one stick to each of them and asked them to break it. This time they were able to do it easily.\nThe farmer said, \"I just defeat you. But you keep quarrelling among yourself; you will be easily broken by anyone. \"The sons learned their lesson and lived happily with their father thereafter. He wanted to show you that if you are united nobody can break you easily.\nMoral: Unity is strength.\n\nA FARMER AND HIS FOUR SONS\n\nVOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. quarrel- a ground of dispute or complaint; usually verbal conflict. Example:      1.The children quarrel all the time. 2.I don’t want to quarrel with you.\n2. unity- the state of being in full agreement. Example:      1.There is a sense of unity in the group. 2.They won the contest because of the unity of their team.\n3. defeat-to win a victory over (someone or something) in a war, contest, game, etc. Example:      1.We must be ready to defeat our enemy in the battle. 2.Our candidate defeated him in the last election.\n4. future-coming after the present time Example:      1.We cannot predict future events. 2.Be able to study very well for your good future.\n5. united-involving people or groups working together to achieve something Example:      1.It is a united campaign against drugs. 2.Your group must be united for you to win. A FARMER AND HIS FOUR SONS ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,66,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1586,798,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 16 · Part 2','open','Arrange and unscramble the scrambled letters to form a word.','1. l e r r q a u   = _________ verbal conflict.\n\n2. y t u i n       =__________ the state of being in full agreement.\n\n3. e a t d f e      =__________to win a victory over (someone or something) in a war, contest, game, etc.\n\n4. d e t i n u  =__________involving people or groups working together to achieve something.\n\n5. e f u r t u     =__________coming after the present time.',NULL,'[]','Arrange and unscramble the scrambled letters to form a word.',NULL,10,66,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1587,798,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 16 · Part 3','open','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.','1. What happened to the old farmer?\n\n2. What did the four brothers do in order to have unity in them?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.',NULL,10,66,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1588,799,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 17 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE HARE AND THE TORTOISE\n\nThere once lived a hare in a jungle. He was very proud of himself. In the same jungle lived an old tortoise.\nThe arrogant hare found him funny and always made fun of his slow pace in front of other animals.\nThe tortoise lost his cool one day            and challenged      the hare for a race.\nEverybody      was surprised, and the hare was laughing loudly. Nevertheless, he accepted the challenge. The race was to take place the next day. The winner had to reach the bank of the river. They started the running race.\nThe hare ran very fast. But the tortoise walked very slowly. Within no time, the proud hare was halfway through the distance. He thought to himself that the tortoise with his slow pace can never match him. So, he decided to rest under a tree for some time. But he soon fell asleep.\nOn the other hand, the tortoise continued to walk towards the finishing point without pausing for a rest. He passed by the sleeping hare. Soon, it was sunset. There was a loud noise of clapping.\nThat day the hare had slept longer than usual. When he woke up, he heard the clapping sound at a distance. He ran fast towards the finishing line.\nHe was shocked to see the old tortoise at the finishing line. All the animals were celebrating his victory. The hare had learned his lesson for life. He never mocked anybody thereafter.\nMoral: Slow and steady wins the race.\nTHE HARE AND THE TORTOISE\n\nVOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. arrogant-having or showing the insulting attitude of people who believe that they are better, smarter, or more important than other people. Example:      1. She’s the most intelligent in their class, but she’s not arrogant about it. 2. The arrogant man declared himself the smartest among others.\n2. challenge-to say or show that (something) may not be true, correct or legal. Example:      1. A number of doctors is challenging a study’s claim. 2.She’s been challenged on her handling of the problem.\n3. halfway-in the middle between two points; not total or complete. Example:      1. She was leading at the halfway of the race. 2.We’re halfway toward completing the project.\n4. victory-success in defeating an opponent; the act of defeating an opponent or enemy. Example:      1. The general led the troops to victory. 2.They had never experienced the thrill of victory.\n5. mock-to laugh at or make fan of (someone or something) especially by copying an action or way of behaving or speaking. Example:      1. The boys mocked him for showing fear. 2.They continue to mock the idea of the new government. THE HARE AND THE TORTOISE ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,68,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1589,799,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 17 · Part 2','open','Choose the synonym of the underlined word in each sentence. Encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. The teacher is challenging the top 5 students in the exam.\n\n2. The athlete is halfway in the race.\n\n3. The arrogant person told the group that he can win the game without his companions.\n\n4. The group mocked him because he is a person with disability.\n\n5. The group celebrated their victory.',NULL,'[]','Choose the synonym of the underlined word in each sentence. Encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,68,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1590,799,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 17 · Part 3','open','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.','1. What made the hare arrogant?\n\n2. Who slept longer than usual? Why?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.',NULL,10,68,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1591,800,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 18 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE FARMER AND THE WELL\nOne day, a farmer named Bholaram, bought a well from his neighbor, Dharma. Dharma was a cunning man. When Bholaram went to fetch water from the well the next day, Dharma stopped him.\nWhen asked why, he said that he had sold only the well to Bholaram, not the water. To use the water, Bholaram must pay for it.\nBholaram was shocked to see the cunningness of his neighbour. He took the matter to the court of Emperor Akbar. There, his minister, Birbal was given the responsibility to solve the matter.\nBirbal called Dharma and asked why he was not letting Bholaram take the water from the well. To this, he replied,\" just sold the well, not the water within it. So, Bholaram has no right over the water\"\nBirbal thought for a second and said, \" Since you have sold the well to the farmer, you have no right to keep your water in it. If you do so, you have to pay him the rent for using his well. Else, take out all your water immediately.\nDharma was caught off guard. He realized his cunning scheme stood no chance before the razor-sharp wit of Birbal. He apologized and went home.\nMoral: Cheating can never lead to happy endings. You have to pay for your mistakes.\n\nTHE\n\nFARMER AND THE WELL VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. cunning-getting what is wanted in a clever and deceptive way. Example:     1. She was cunning enough to fool me. 2.He is cunning to surely win the election.\n2. shock-something that causes disturbance; a state of being so disturbed. Example:     1. She undergoes shock upon knowing that she was defeated. 2.He was shocked when he was not allowed to enter the place.\n3. Emperor-the sovereign supreme male monarch of an empire. Example:     1. The emperor refuses to decide the trials. 2.The emperor is the highest position in their place.\n4. scheme- a clever and often dishonest plan to do or get something. Example:     1. His scheme to fool the people was not successful. 2.His scheme to betray others is very bad and not acceptable.\n5. cheat-to break a rule or law usually to gain an advantage at something. Example:     1.The players were accused of cheating. 2.I had cheat to solve the puzzle. THE FARMER AND THE WELL ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,70,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1592,800,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 18 · Part 2','open','Choose the synonym of the underlined word in each sentence. Encircle the letter of the correct answer.','1. He is cunning to be able to win the race.\n\n2. He was shocked when somebody cheated him.\n\n3. He is the Emperor in that country.\n\n4. His scheme helped a lot in the success of the activity.\n\n5. He cheated in order to survive.',NULL,'[]','Choose the synonym of the underlined word in each sentence. Encircle the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,70,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1593,800,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 18 · Part 3','open','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.','1. Why did Dharma stopped Bholaram when he came to fetch water?\n\n2. What was the decission of Birbal?\n\n3. Did the cunning scheme of Dharma helped him? Why?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.',NULL,10,70,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1594,801,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 19 · Part 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','THE FOX AND THE CROW\nA crow once found a big piece of cheese. He held the cheese in his beak and flew away to a nearby tree. A fox was passing by when he saw the crow with the big piece of cheese in his mouth.\nHe went to the crow and said \" Good morning, dear crow. You have a lovely piece of cheese with you. Could you please share a bit with me?\". The crow refused and looked away.\nThe fox begged a little, but the crow wouldn\'t listen. He just sat there, quietly eating his cheese.\nBut the fox didn\'t give up. He continued persuading the crow. He called the crow handsome and praised his black feathers. He said\" You have a beautiful voice too. Everyone in the forest is praising your voice. Can you sing a sweet song for me?\"\nThe fox saw the crow swelling with pride. The foolish crow believed the cunning fox. He suddenly forgot all about the cheese and began to sing.\nAs soon as the crow began to sing, the piece of cheese fell to the ground. The fox immediately picked it up and ran away. The crow was left crying on the tree all by himself.\nMoral: Beware Of flatterer.\n\nTHE FOX AND THE CROW VOCABULARY\n\nVocabulary\n1. cheese –a food consisting of coagulated, compressed and usually ripened cord of milk. Example:      1.The bread contains cheese. 2.The cheese is often used in making bread.\n2. beg-to ask people for money or food. Example:      1.A homeless man beg on that corner every day. 2.She beg for food to survive.\n3. persuade-to cause (someone) to do something by asking, arguing, or giving reasons. Example:      1.He persuaded his friend to go back to school. 2.She couldn’t be persuaded to go.\n4. voice-the sound that you make with your mouth and throat when you are speaking Example:      1.He has a deep voice. 2.You have a lovely voice.\n5. pride- a feeling that you respect yourself and deserve to be respected by other people Example:      1.Being able to work again gave him his pride back. 2.Pride would not allow him to give up. THE FOX AND THE CROW ASSESSMENT\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,72,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1595,801,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 19 · Part 2','open','Arrange and unscramble the scrambled letters to form a word.','1. e s e e h c            = _______a food consist of coagulated, compressed and usually ripened cord of milk.\n\n2. e g b            =_______to ask people for money or food.\n\n3. e d a u s r e p =________to cause (someone) to do something by asking, arguing, or giving reasons.\n\n4. i v o c e        = _______the sound that you make with your mouth and throat when you are speaking.\n\n5. r p i e d        = _______ a feeling that you respect yourself and deserve to be respected by other people.',NULL,'[]','Arrange and unscramble the scrambled letters to form a word.',NULL,10,72,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1596,801,NULL,'learn',3,'Day 19 · Part 3','open','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.','1. Did the crow share his cheese to the fox? Why?\n\n2. What was the trick of the fox in order to get the cheese from the crow?',NULL,'[]','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.',NULL,10,72,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1597,802,NULL,'learn',1,'Day 20 · Part 1','open','Read carefully the phrases/sentences. Give the antonym of the underlined word.','THE GREEDY LION\nOn a hot summer day, a lion in the forest started feeling hungry. He went hunting for food. Soon he spotted a rabbit roaming around alone. He thought to himself, \"Such a small rabbit as this can\'t satisfy my hunger.\"\nInstead of catching the rabbit, the lion let it go. Just then, a beautiful deer passed by, and the lion decided to hunt it down.\nSo, he ran behind the deer. Since he was weak because of the hunger, he could not keep up with the deer\'s speed. Soon, he was tired and could no longer chase the deer.\nHe let go of the deer and went back looking for the rabbit to fill his stomach for some time. But the rabbit was gone. The lion was sad and remained hungry for a long time.\nMoral: Greed is never a good thing. We should be happy with whatever we get.\n\nTHE GREEDY LION VOCABULARY\n\n1.greedy-having or showing selfish desire to have more of something such as money or food.\nExample:   1. He is greedy, so nobody likes him.\n2.Don’t be greedy so that you will be liked by your classmates.\n\n2.roam-to go to different places without having a particular purpose or plan Example:    1. The cattle roamed in search for water.\n2.Goats roam free in the mountain.\n3.behind-in or toward the back.\n-In the place or situation that is being or has been departed from.\nExample:    1. The cat hid behind the couch.\n2.The sun went behind the cloud.\n4.chase-the hunting of wild animals; to follow rapidly.\n-a sequence in which the characters pursue one another.\nExample:    1. He chased the animal in the forest.\n2.He always chase her sister in the street.\n5.speed-the rate at which someone or something moves or travels.\nExample:    1. This instrument measures wind speed.\n2.The machine is operating at high speed.\n\nTHE GREEDY LION ASSESSMENT\n\n1. Don’t be greedy. You need to share your blessings to others.\n\n2. She roamed in search for her partner.\n\n3. The moon went behind the cloud.\n\n4. His dog chased the other dog.\n\n5. He ran at a very high speed.',NULL,'[]','Read carefully the phrases/sentences. Give the antonym of the underlined word.',NULL,10,74,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1598,802,NULL,'learn',2,'Day 20 · Part 2','open','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.','1. Why did the lion let go of the rabbit?\n\n2. Why did the lion remained sad for a long time? SET A',NULL,'[]','Answer the following questions in complete sentence.',NULL,10,74,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1599,803,119,'post',1,'Post-test · Days 1-10','open','The following questions are based on the reading passages from your lessons. Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.','1. What is the shape of a common jellyfish\'s body?\n\n2. The Portuguese man-of-war is deadly because of its:\n\n3. According to the text, what can neutralize the Portuguese man-of-war\'s venom?\n\n4. Why is it more accurate to describe a lion as \"sly\" rather than \"fearless\"?\n\n5. Where are the last remaining lions in Asia confined?\n\n6. The main reason lions are becoming extinct is:\n\n7. What is the scientific name of the world\'s smallest fish found in the Philippines?\n\n8. Where was the \"tabios,\" once thought to be the smallest fish, found?\n\n9. Who finally confirmed the discovery of the live Pandaka pygmaea after 25 years?\n\n10. Which shark is described as the most vicious killer?\n\n11. Why do fishermen hunt sharks?\n\n12. What method do Sulu fishermen use to drive sharks away?\n\n13. What was Lauro\'s dream?\n\n14. Why did Lauro take the long way to school?\n\n15. What did Lauro do with the bag of money he found?\n\n16. What happened to Levy?\n\n17. How did Levy get around after losing his legs?\n\n18. What qualities did Levy show after his accident?\n\n19. Why were Japanese women of the past considered the unhappiest?\n\n20. What was the only career for a Japanese woman besides being a geisha?\n\n21. How was a Japanese wife expected to greet her husband when he came home?\n\n22. Who was the giant that David fought?\n\n23. What weapon did David use to defeat the giant?\n\n24. Why was David confident he could win the battle?\n\n25. Why was the old man sitting by the bridge?\n\n26. What was the old man\'s job in his town, San Carlos?\n\n27. What animals was the old man worried about?\n\n28. Who was the woman trying to talk to from the street?\n\n29. What was the man being transferred?\n\n30. Why did the man tell the woman to turn the child\'s face to the sun?\n\n31. Based on \"The Jellyfish,\" a convex body is:\n\n32. In \"The Lion is Dying,\" to exterminate a species means to:\n\n33. In \"The Smallest Fish,\" a specimen is:\n\n34. Based on \"Sharks,\" a denizen of the deep is:\n\n35. In \"The Most Wonderful Birthday Gift,\" Lauro\'s father squandered his money, meaning he:\n\n36. In \"Not the End of the World,\" Levy\'s contraption refers to his:\n\n37. In \"The Unhappiest Women,\" etiquette refers to:\n\n38. In \"The Story of David,\" to strangle an animal means to:\n\n39. In \"The Old Man at the Bridge,\" the peasants are:\n\n40. In \"A Conversation from the Third Floor,\" to gaze means to:\n\n41. What is a common theme between \"The Lion is Dying\" and \"Sharks, Monsters of the Deep\"?\n\n42. How are Levy\'s story (\"Not the End of the World\") and Lauro\'s story (\"The Most Wonderful Birthday Gift\") similar?\n\n43. Based on the passages, which statement is a FACT?\n\n44. What was the main cause of the old man\'s worry in \"The Old Man at the Bridge\"?\n\n45. The conversation in \"A Conversation from the Third Floor\" is mostly about:\n\n46. What lesson can be learned from \"The Most Wonderful Birthday Gift\"?\n\n47. In \"The Jellyfish,\" how does the author primarily organize the information about the Portuguese man-of-war?\n\n48. What is the author\'s purpose in \"The Lion is Dying\"?\n\n49. In \"Not the End of the World,\" what is the meaning of the title?\n\n50. Which passage primarily uses a narrative structure to tell a story with characters and a plot?',NULL,'[]','The following questions are based on the reading passages from your lessons. Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.',NULL,10,77,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1600,804,120,'post',1,'Post-test · Days 11-20','open','The following questions are based on the reading passages from your lessons (Days 11-20). Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.','1. Why did the parents call the doctor?\n\n2. What disease was the doctor afraid the child might have?\n\n3. How did the child react when the doctor first tried to examine her?\n\n4. What did the bear do to the man who could not climb the tree?\n\n5. What was the \"advice\" the bear gave?\n\n6. The moral of this story is:\n\n7. Why couldn\'t the crane eat the soup the fox served?\n\n8. How did the crane get back at the fox?\n\n9. The moral of this story is:\n\n10. What news did Mrs. Mallard receive at the beginning of the story?\n\n11. How did Mrs. Mallard initially react to the news?\n\n12. At the end of the story, Mrs. Mallard died because of:\n\n13. Why did the ants decide to teach the elephant a lesson?\n\n14. How did the ants punish the elephant?\n\n15. The moral of this story is:\n\n16. What was the problem with the farmer\'s sons?\n\n17. What did the single stick represent in the farmer\'s lesson?\n\n18. The moral of this story is:\n\n19. Why did the tortoise challenge the hare to a race?\n\n20. Why did the hare lose the race?\n\n21. The moral of this story is:\n\n22. Why did Dharma stop Bholaram from taking water?\n\n23. How did Birbal solve the problem?\n\n24. The moral of this story is:\n\n25. How did the fox get the cheese from the crow?\n\n26. What did the fox praise about the crow?\n\n27. The moral of this story is:\n\n28. Why did the lion let the rabbit go?\n\n29. Why couldn\'t the lion catch the deer?\n\n30. The moral of this story is:\n\n31. Based on \"The Use of Force,\" if a person is coaxed, they are:\n\n32. In \"A Friend in Need,\" an oath is a:\n\n33. In \"The Fox and the Crane,\" to humiliate someone means to:\n\n34. Based on \"The Story of an Hour,\" exhaustion means:\n\n35. In \"The Ant and the Elephant,\" an arrogant person is:\n\n36. In \"The Farmer and His Four Sons,\" unity means:\n\n37. In \"The Hare and the Tortoise,\" to mock someone means to:\n\n38. In \"The Farmer and the Well,\" a cunning person is:\n\n39. In \"The Fox and the Crow,\" to persuade someone means to:\n\n40. In \"The Greedy Lion,\" a greedy person:\n\n41. What is a common theme in both \"The Ant and the Elephant\" and \"The Hare and the Tortoise\"?\n\n42. How are the fox in \"The Fox and the Crane\" and the fox in \"The Fox and the Crow\" similar?\n\n43. Based on the passages, which statement is a FACT from \"The Story of an Hour\"?\n\n44. What was the main internal conflict for Mrs. Mallard?\n\n45. The lesson from \"The Farmer and the Well\" and \"The Greedy Lion\" both warn against:\n\n46. What lesson can be learned from both \"A Friend in Need\" and \"The Farmer and His Four Sons\"?\n\n47. In \"The Use of Force,\" the doctor\'s feelings towards the child become increasingly:\n\n48. What is the author\'s primary purpose in a fable like \"The Ant and the Elephant\"?\n\n49. In \"The Story of an Hour,\" what does the \"open window\" symbolize for Mrs. Mallard?\n\n50. Which passage features a character who uses clever wordplay and logic to solve a dispute?',NULL,'[]','The following questions are based on the reading passages from your lessons (Days 11-20). Read each question carefully and choose the best answer. Encircle the letter of your choice.',NULL,10,87,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1601,805,121,'pre',1,'Pre-test','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','INTERVENTION MATERIALS      Reading        5 Comprehension Skills\n\nActivity 1        MOST DESTRUCTIVE            Literal      6 CREATURES ON EARTH      Comprehension Activity 2      COCONUT, THE TREE OF          Literal      8 LIFE            Comprehension Activity 3      PRISON WITHOUT WALLS       Interpretive   10 Comprehension Activity 4         A MOTHER’S WISDOM       Interpretive   13 Comprehension Activity 5          MIRACLES OF FIRE         Critical     16 Comprehension Activity 6      LIGHTNING AND THUNDER        Critical     19 Comprehension Activity 7         THE GREAT CHINESE         Critical     22 TEACHER          Comprehension Activity 8          MOHAMMEDANISM            Creative     25 Comprehension Activity 9            THE IFUGAOS            Creative     28 Comprehension Activity 10          PLANT THIEVES           Creative     31 Comprehension\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,4,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1602,806,122,'pre',1,'Pre-test','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nHOW A DOG’S LIFE WAS SAVED\n\nBernie, a police dog, loves Boy Scouts and he really has a very good reason for doing so. He would have died were it not for the training of two Boy Scouts who knew how to make a tourniquet. These two boys who were twins were members of the Narra Troop Number\n64.\n\nThe two boys were on their way home from school. They were busy talking about the Boy Scout Jamboree to be held in Japan. They were lucky to be among the Boy Scouts to represent the Philippines in the jamboree.\n\n“Look!” cried Nick, one of the twins. “See the blood trail on the sidewalk.”\n\nBecause Boy Scouts are trained to be of help in time of trouble, they followed the trail. It led them to the backyard of a certain Mr. Gana. They heard a feeble whine. Behind the pile of boxes they found Mr. Gana’s dog, Bernie.\nHe seemed very weak due to loss of blood and looked completely exhausted as he lay.\n\nBernie greeted the two boys with a feeble wag of his tail. The two boys had hopes of saving his life. If only they could stop the bleeding of his wounded foot.\n\n“I will get my handkerchief,” said Pete reaching into his pocket. “Do you have something to bind it with, Nick?”\n\nThe boys did not waste any time. Nick pulled a lead pencil out of his pocket. Pete tore a strip about two or three inches wide from the handkerchief.\nHe folded it over and over three or four times making a strong narrow band.\nPete tied the two ends together while Nick held Bernie. Then Pete skipped the band over the dog’s injured foot. Bernie bore the pain without wincing as the two boys tightened the tourniquet.\n\nWhen the bleeding stopped, the boys went to Mr. Gana and told him what had happened to Bernie. Mr. Gana returned home as soon as possible and found Bernie’s wound well taken care of.\n\nThe boys then tried to find out how it all happened. They followed the trail back to where Bernie met the accident. Apparently, Bernie had accidentally stepped on a broken bottle.\n\nBernie would have bled to death had it not been for the two boys and their tourniquet. This is why Bernie has an excellent reason for loving the Boy Scouts. He never fails to lick Pete’s and Nick’s hands whenever they pass by their home. The twins had a special place in Bernie’s heart.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nLiteral Reading Skill These questions focus on facts explicitly stated in the text.\n\n1. Where was the Boy Scout Jamboree that Nick and Pete were selected to attend?\n\n2. What two specific items did the twins use to stop Bernie’s bleeding?\n\n3. Why did the boys choose to follow the blood trail instead of going straight home?\n\n4. What does the \"feeble wag\" of Bernie’s tail suggest about his condition when the boys found him?\n\n5. How would you evaluate the twins’ decision to use a lead pencil in their medical intervention?\n\n6. What is the author’s primary purpose in mentioning that Bernie is a \"police dog\"?\n\n7. Which statement best analyzes the reliability of the boys\' scout training as presented in the story?\n\n8. If Nick and Pete had not found a pencil in their pockets, which of these items from a student\'s bag would be the best substitute to tighten the tourniquet?\n\n9. Based on the ending of the story, what is the most likely way Bernie would react if he saw Nick and Pete in a large crowd of people?\n\n10. Imagine the \"Narra Troop 64\" wanted to create a commemorative badge for the twins. What symbol would most accurately represent this specific rescue?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,6,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1603,807,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nTHE MOST DESTRUCTIVE CREATURES ON EARTH\nRats are probably the cleverest and the most destructive animals on earth. These animals can survive anywhere. They have also threatened man\'s life; rat-borne diseases have killed more people than all the wars in history.\n\nMost of the 650 species of rats live in forests, fields, and jungles. But a species tries to link their destinies with men.\nThey go where men go, live where they live, eat what they eat, and learn their ways.\n\nThere are two species of common house rats - the brown rat and the black rat. The brown rat has small ears and thick tail that is shorter than its body. It may weigh as much as one-half kilogram and measure nine inches in length, minus its tail. The brown rat is a burrower. It can squeeze through the sewers for miles and enter a man\'s home through the plumbing.\n\nThe black rat is very much smaller than the brown rat. It has an average weight of one-fourth kilogram. It has large eyes and a tail longer than its body.\nThe black rat, clambers up walls, ropes, and wires.\n\nRats are social animals and live in colonies. They wage wars against others and an \"outsider\" is killed by torture known only to rats.\n\nThe average life of a rat is nine months, although some may live up to two years. They are very prolific. It has been estimated that a pair of rats, if allowed to multiply unmolested, could produce up to 350 million descendants in three years.\n\nEverywhere the rats go, they spread contamination. which is a continuing health menace. They carry as many as 35 different diseases. Their fleas spread bubonic plague and typhus. These two diseases have killed more than 200 million people in for countries.\n\nThe rat\'s habits take them from sewer to warehouse to home, spreading disease germs every step of the way. They leave droppings everywhere they go and contaminate everything because in their blood and intestines are organisms that cause dysentery, tularemia, rabies, and many other contagious killer diseases.\nNature has endowed rats with special characteristics to help them survive. They avoid poison but after one or two members had felt its effects.\nThey leave their urine on everything that poison-tainted as a \"keep-off\" sign to other rats.\n\nBut there is one way rats become useful to man; Man makes use of rats in his studies of drugs, medicine, and the treatment of disease.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. According to the text, which physical feature belongs specifically to the black rat?\n\n2. What is the average lifespan of a rat as mentioned in the passage?\n\n3. If left alone to multiply, how many descendants can a single pair of rats produce in three years?\n\n4. How many different diseases are rats known to carry?\n\n5. According to the final section of the text, how are rats useful to humans?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,10,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1604,808,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 2','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nCOCONUT, THE TREE OF LIFE\nThe coconut is a tropical palm. It provides man with almost     everything;   food, clothing,     shelter,  home furnishings, fuel, and many others. On the products of the coconut tree, one can subsist almost fully.\n\nThe coconut grows palm leaf every month. Each leaf bears a cluster of flowers which mature into clusters of fruits. High at the top of the crown of leaves is the coconut heart or bud. If the heart or the bud is removed, the tree dies. The heart itself makes a tasty treat, a \"millionaire\'s salad.\"\n\nThe unopened flowers are protected by a tough sheath that looks like burlap. From this sheath may be fashioned our shoes, caps, and even a kind of helmet.\n\nSugar may be obtained from the sap of the unopened flowers. A gallon of sap may be gathered and boiled to a good syrup which can be crystallized into rich, dark sugar. Or if left standing, the sap ferments quickly into a robust beer-like drink called \"tuba\" in the Philippines, \"toddy\" in India, \"tuwak\" in Indonesia. If left alone for a few weeks; the sap becomes fine vinegar.\n\nThe nut itself is covered by a thick, packed mass of fibers called \"coir\" which can be woven into strong twine or rope. Coir is also used in padding upholstery. The shell of the coconut is hard, fine-grained substance which can be carved into all kinds of objects; cups, ashtrays, button, buckles, and many others. The charcoal from shells is used as air filters in gas masks, submarines, and cigarette tips.\n\nCoconut water contains sugar, minerals, and vitamins, The nut meat is eaten raw or made into salad; coconut milk is used in cooking food. Heated, it yields oil for cooking, lamp fuel, lotions, pomades, and soaps.\n\nGrated nut meats are made into candies, cakes, and pies. Dried coconut meat or \"copra\" is processed for oil and the residue is made into animal food.\n\nThere are many uses of the coconut oil. It is used in shampoos, soaps, toothpaste, lotion, lubricants, hydraulic fluid, paints, synthetic rubber, ice cream, plastics, and others. It is also found in breakfast tables - \"margarine\" as a substitute for butter with all the needed nutrients and vitamins.\n\nThere are plenty of uses for the rest of the tree. The fronds are woven into mats and house furnishings, the midribs made into brooms and brushes.\nEven the roots can be made into dyes.\n\nNo other tree on earth is as useful and as the coconut!\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. According to the text, what will happen to a coconut tree if its heart or bud is removed?\n\n2. What is the name of the beer-like drink made from fermented sap in the Philippines?\n\n3. Which part of the coconut tree can be used to create charcoal for air filters in gas masks?\n\n4. What is \"coir\" specifically defined as in the passage?\n\n5. Which specific part of the coconut tree is used to make brooms and brushes?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,12,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1605,809,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 3','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nPRISON WITHOUT WALLS\nIn 1904, the Iwahig Penal Colony was built. It was a kind of prison where- in long term prisoners, most of them life. The colony was originally, designed to house an overflow of termers, serve    their  sentences     with comparative freedom. convicts from Manila\'s old Bilibid Prison.\nIts basic purpose was to reform and rehabilitate criminals rather than punish them. It is under the supervision of the superintendent of prisons.\n\nThe settlement site was once a 34- square mile of virgin jungle. The first prisoners hacked out roads, prepared the fields and slanted coconuts and other fruit-bearing trees. Through the years, the colony has expanded to cover 160square miles of cultivated land.\n\nOnly prisoners with comparatively long sentences are assigned at the Iwahig Penal Colony. Newly arrived inmates are quartered in disciplinary barracks for at least six months. They do not have the same privileges as the old-time colonial. Later, when they are fully indoctrinated with the rules and regulations of the colony as well as their duties and obligations, they are assigned to various projects where they can put their special skills to good use.\n\nIlliterate inmates attend adult education classes handled by teachers who are fellow convicts. The children of inmates are educated in schools and reading centers in the settlement. Medical treatment is available at the prison\'s hospital.\n\nThe inmates sleep in spacious barrack-rooms with no barred windows to prevent them from breaking out. They are free to marry, but must have permission of the Director of Prisons to do so. If married, arrangements could be made from their families to live in the settlement. They are free to go about within the 160-square mile limits of the penal colony.\n\nLike their free compatriots, the inmates work for a living from P500 to P1,000 a month by farming, fishing, forestry, animal husbandry,\n\nconstruction, jobs in the coconut and coffee industries, and handicrafts. The colony\'s extensive forests provide them with wood which they can sell. They can fish in the coastal waters or raise poultry and meat animals for their consumption. But as inmates, they have to work for the prison industries, too, for certain hours and days of the week.\n\nThe atmosphere in the colony is quiet good such that some convicts prefer to stay there permanently even when their sentences expired. Deserving Ex-convicts who choose to stay in the colony permanently are granted small plots of land for their use. Thus, social derelicts are turned useful citizens.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. Why does the author refer to the Iwahig Penal Colony as a \"prison without walls\"?\n\n2. What is the primary reason new inmates are kept in disciplinary barracks for their first six months?\n\n3. What can you infer about the colony\'s attitude toward the families of the inmates?\n\n4. Based on the description of the colony’s activities (farming, fishing, and handicrafts), what is the most likely way the colony prepares inmates for life after prison?\n\n5. Why do some convicts choose to stay in the colony permanently even after their sentences have expired?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,14,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1606,810,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 4','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nA MOTHER\'S WISDOM\nBarbara was 10 when she was found her first real \"pal\" among the girls in school. Their friendship became all-important to Barbara, and being of a possessive nature, she found herself violently jealous of her friend\'s interests which did not include Barbara. She couldn\'t conceal this attitude until her friend became annoyed with her possessiveness.\n\nBarbara\'s mother understood what was happening. She waited for an occasion to talk to Barbara about it without hurting her.\n\nOne day, Barbara and her mother were admiring some baby chicks.\nBarbara picked up one and held it so tightly that it almost smothered. It struggled to escape from Barbara\'s hold. Casually her mother said, \"If you hold a chick too tightly, it wants only to get away. Try holding one gently.\"\n\nThe next chick nestled quietly in Barbara\'s open palm. Her mother said, \"You know, dear, people are the same as chicks. If we hold those whom we love too closely to us, they\'ll struggle for freedom. Hold them with open hands and they won\'t feel smothered.\"\n\nBarbara understood the message in her mother\'s words. Whenever she felt jealous of her friend\'s attention to others, she always pictures the contented chick nestled in her open palm.\n\nJoseph, Barbara\'s brother was eight. Being young and mischievous, he used to get into jams and then run to his mother for help to get him out of trouble. But Mother never interfered. She would let him untangle his troubles by himself. Joseph never understood why Mother always declined to help him with his problems.\n\nOne day, Joseph saw a cocoon of a moth throbbing and heaving.\nSquealing with anticipation, Joseph began to open the cocoon with his pocket knife to allow the moth to emerge more easily. To his disappointment, the\n\nmoth did not fly; it fell off from the twig on which the cocoon hung, then died that very same moment.\n\nJoseph\'s mother shook him on the shoulders and said, \"Son, the struggle of the moth inside the cocoon was for a purpose, to enable it to gain strength to rise above its surroundings. You did not allow it to mature fully, and when you forcibly opened the cocoon, it can neither walk nor fly. It\'s the same with children. When a boy like you always has somebody to solve his problems for him, he has no chance to develop, Each of the struggles that we go through on our own builds up our strength to meet the next.\"\n\nBarbara and Joseph never forget the lessons they learned from their mother.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What did the mother mean when she told Barbara to hold those she loved with \"open hands\"?\n\n2. In the story of the moth, what does the \"struggle\" inside the cocoon represent for a person\'s life?\n\n3. Why did the mother refuse to \"interfere\" or help Joseph when he got into trouble?\n\n4. What can we infer about the moth\'s death after Joseph opened the cocoon?\n\n5. How did the mother’s teaching style most likely affect Barbara and Joseph’s future?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,17,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1607,811,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 5','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nTHE MIRACLES OF THE FIRE\nDuring the time of Elias, many of the children of Israel honored false gods and false prophets.\n\nOne day, Elias said to the Jews, \"Call together all the people of Israel to Mount Carmel. Bring also the prophets of Baal\" (Baal was the god of the pagans.\n\nWhen all the people gathered together, Elias said, \"If the Lord is God, follow Him. If Baal is God, follow him.\"\n\nThe people did not say a word. Elias continued, \"I am the only prophet of the Lord. Baal has four hundred and fifty prophets. Now, let us see who is God. Let the prophets of Baal prepare a sacrifice. We shall dress the animals and put unlighted wood under them. Let the prophets of Baal call upon their god. I shall call on my Lord. God shall answer by fire.\"\n\nThe people all agreed.\n\n\"O, Baal, hear us!\" This was the prayer of the prophets of Baal. But their prayers were not answered.\n\nThen Elias prayed to the one true God. Fire came down from heaven.\nThe people fell on their faces. They said, \"The Lord is God!\"\n\nThe Lord was always with Elias. Elias talked to the children of Israel.\nHe wanted them to remember the Lord, their God.\n\nOne day the Lord said to Elias. \"You shall make Eliseus a prophet.\"\n\nElias found Eliseus in the field. Eliseus left his father and mother and followed Elias. Eliseus was very kind to Elias. He served him and helped him.\nElias and Eliseus went from place to place reminding the people of the one true God.\n\nOne day, Eliseus and Elias were talking together. A fiery chariot drawn by a fiery horse came between them. A strong wind carried Elias up to heaven.\nEliseus watch until he could see Elias no more.\n\nAfter Elias was brought to heaven in a chariot of fire, his spirit rested upon Eliseus.\n\nThe sons of the prophet at Jericho came to meet Eliseus and bowed down before him. Nevertheless, they organized a searching party of fifty men to look for Elias\' body on the valleys and mountains but failed to find him.\nThey returned to Jericho to see Eliseus and they decided to remain there to heal the soil and purify the waters for drinking.\n\nEliseus performed a miracle by using water for healing.\n\nOne day, Eliseus was leaving Jericho for Bethel, a group of children came out of the city and mocked him. Eliseus turned back and cursed the in the name of the Lord. Suddenly, two bears came out of the wilderness and fore the forty-two boys apart.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. Why did Elias propose the specific contest of calling down fire from heaven to the prophets of Baal?\n\n2. Which statement best evaluates the behavior of the people of Israel when Elias first asked them to choose between God and Baal?\n\n3. What is the author’s primary purpose for including the detail about the \"fiery chariot\" taking Elias to heaven?\n\n4. How does the story justify the harsh punishment of the children who mocked Eliseus at the end of the text?\n\n5. What can we conclude about Eliseus\'s character based on his decision to heal the soil and purify the water at Jericho?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,20,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1608,812,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 6','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nLIGHTNING AND THUNDER\nLightning is the same thing as light in an electric bulb. It is electricity. A flash of lightning has twenty times as much electricity as the world uses in 24 hours.\n\nElectricity is always present in the air. When moisture in the air condenses on a tiny grain of dust, the little drop becomes charged with electricity. Then, when the air gets cold and the drops go together to make raindrops, some of the electricity cannot stay on the drops and has to get off.\nWhen millions of drops of water are pushing off little bits of electricity, they all go together and make one big flash of lightning.\n\nFlashes of lightning usually go from one cloud to another, they sometimes hit the ground. Lightning is hot. When it hits a house, the later gets burned. When it strikes a tree, the heat turns the tree\'s sap into steam which must escape quickly, and thus the tree splits open. It may hit a wire fence and lightning travels along the wire just as electricity travels along a write to a lamp.\n\nTo protect tall buildings from lightning, lightning rods are put into the ground at the side of the building. People used to think that the rod attracts the lightning and when it strikes the rod, the rod carries the lightning through the wire into the ground. Actually, the rod helps to make the lightning strike somewhere else. The rod allows the ground electricity to come up slowly through the rod and into the air. Lightning does not strike in a place where other electricity comes out slowly.\n\nThe safest place during a thunderstorm is in the house. Open windows do not attract lightning as people used to think.\n\nOut of doors, it is not safe to stay under a tree for trees are often struck by lightning. In open fields, the safest thing to do during a thunderstorm is to lie flat on the ground.\n\nLightning does not make any sound. It makes a big flash like a million lamps put together. Along the flash comes a long roll of noise which we call\n\nthunder. It is sometimes so far off that it is just faint mutter; at other times it is so close overhead that it sounds like cannons firing.\n\nThunder sometimes frighten people although it cannot do any harm.\nWhen lightning strikes, air becomes heated so quickly that it explodes into thunder.\n\nLightning and thunder seldom do any damage. There is no reason for people to be afraid of them.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What is the author’s primary goal in the section discussing lightning rods?\n\n2. Which of the following statements from the text is a scientific fact used to disprove a popular \"myth\" or fear?\n\n3. How does the author support the claim that \"there is no reason for people to be afraid\" of lightning and thunder?\n\n4. Based on the text\'s explanation of why trees split open, what is the logical cause-and-effect relationship described?\n\n5. What is the author’s purpose in comparing a flash of lightning to \"a million lamps put together\"?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,23,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1609,813,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 7','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nTHE GREAT CHINESE TEACHER\nConfucius\' teachings had left an indelible mark in the lives of millions of Chinese.\n\nThousands of years ago in a flowery kingdom in the state of Lu lived a soldier Known as Kung, the Tall. He was so-called because he was seven feet tall.\nWhen he reached his seventieth birthday he reflected sorrowfully over his life. He had nine children and all of them were girls. He had no son to carry on his family name and to perform the rites and ceremonies of ancestors worship. This was practiced by the Chinese throughout the centuries to show their reverence to their forefathers. Kung, the Tall came from a noble lineage and he would not want to break this tradition.\n\nTwo years before his death, a son was born to him. His son, named Confucius, became known as a \"Great Sage\" and philosopher to China and the world beyond China. His words and wisdom have such a vast influence on the thoughts and on the ways of life of the Chinese throughout five centuries. He was called Master King.\n\nThe Chinese already lived in beautiful houses with fine furniture, grew crops, used agricultural implements and spun silk for their clothing. They used coins, marked time by the moon and had developed cooking to a fine taste. They already had law courts and schools where history, music and literature were taught. They wrote books by hand on bamboo and they bound the pages together with thongs.\n\nMaster Kung was a scholar in school. He had two special interests: first, how people should live and conduct themselves; and second, how the state should be governed. When he grew up to manhood he traveled all over the civilized world with a band of disciples. Two or three times, he was given positions of importance in the government but he never stayed long in these positions. The officers in the government resented Master Kung\'s honesty.\nConfucius continued teaching not knowing that his ideas were to be passed on from generation to generation, that thousands of books were to be written about him and his sayings, that he was to become one of the most beloved and revered men in history.\n\nHe preached the importance the importance of courtesy, orderliness, loyalty, justice, and devotion to one\'s parents. He emphasized that the government should be governed by men who possessed these virtues. He stressed the importance of happiness in family life and of endurance and consideration for one\'s neighbors.\n\nThese beautiful thoughts had become part of the national character of the Chinese people. They were taught that happiness can be found in cultivating the art of living and in calm and settled existence.\n\nConfucius\' greatest influence in Chinese life was family reverence and bonds of family life. He always believed that reverence for one\'s ancestors was important and desirable.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. Based on the passage, what was the most likely reason the government officers \"resented Master Kung\'s honesty\"?\n\n2. Which of the following statements from the text is an expression of Confucius’s philosophy (opinion/belief) rather than a historical fact?\n\n3. Why does the author include the detail about Kung, the Tall having nine daughters before finally having a son?\n\n4. What is the author’s primary purpose in mentioning that the Chinese already lived in beautiful houses and used coins before Confucius began teaching?\n\n5. How does the author evaluate the long-term impact of Confucius’s life?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,26,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1610,814,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 8','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nMOHAMMEDANISM, THE ISLAM FAITH\nHave you ever wondered why our Muslim brothers in Mindanao have a different religion than ours? For a time, Mohammedanism, or the Muslim religion, threatened all Asia and even a great part of Europe. Here\'s how Mohammedanism came about.\n\nThe Mohammedan religion was founded by Mohammed and for a lime was considered the most powerful religion in the world. There are still two hundred thirty million believers of Mohammedan faith in Asia, Africa, and a group in the Philippines, in Mindanao.\n\nMohammed, the founder of this religion, was born in Mecca in the years 570 A.D. His parents were poor and he received very little education. While still a young boy he worked as a shepherd. At the age of 25, he married a rich widow older than himself.\n\nWhen he was about 40 years old, the Angel Gabriel appeared to him in one of his prayers. The angel declared him as the prophet of God and told him to preach the true religion.\n\nMohammed began to preach. His work was slow. He had only 40 followers after four years of preaching but he continued his work. He wrote verses telling about the divinity of God and how mercy could be gained through prayers, fasting, and alms- giving. These verses could be found at the beginning of the Koran, the sacred book of the Mohammedans.\n\nMohammed was ever patient and never grew tired of preaching his religion, He saw to it that his followers did not eat certain food. Later, he prohibited them from drinking wine, gambling, and indulging is usury, His followers lived by raiding caravans in the deserts and very often they were Successful. Many people of Mecca joined the band of Mohammed.\n\nThen came the years of misunderstanding and strife, War broke out between the followers of Mohammed and the people of Mecca who did not believe in him, Mohammed and his followers attacked Mecca and captured the city in 630 A.D. The people rejoiced and honored him after his victory.\n\nThey considered him a prince and God\'s prophet on earth. He made many conquests after this. His followers swept towards Asia and Africa, and for a time, it was feared that Mohammedanism will succeed to engulf the whole world.\n\nHowever, Christianity triumphed in many places, and only some countries remained Mohammedans — including the Muslims in Mindanao.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. If you were an advisor to Mohammed during his first four years of preaching when he only had 40 followers, what creative strategy based on his character might you suggest to help his message reach more people?\n\n2. Imagine a new city is being built today based strictly on the rules Mohammed established for his followers. Which of these businesses would likely be missing from that city?\n\n3. If the Angel Gabriel had not appeared to Mohammed when he was 40, how might his life have most likely continued based on his background before that event?\n\n4. Based on the \"Five Pillars\" logic often associated with the Koran (prayers, fasting, and alms-giving), how would a follower most likely respond to seeing a person in the desert who has no food or water?\n\n5. If you were tasked with creating a modern \"Peace Treaty\" between the people of Mecca and Mohammed’s followers in 630 A.D., which of Mohammed\'s personal traits mentioned in the text would be most helpful in making the treaty last?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,29,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1611,815,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 9','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nTHE IFUGAOS\nThe Ifugaos inhabiting the Mountain Province of Northern Luzon are among the most interesting people in the Far East. They are direct descendants of the earliest Malay invaders of the Philippines.\nThey have constructed one of the worlds wonders.\n\nIfugao men wear clothes or G-strings; the women wear short skirts reaching from waist to the knees, Men and women alike wear ornaments of gold, beads, brass armlets and legless. They build small but strong thatched houses.\n\nThe Ifugaos have many interesting customs. Once a girl reaches the age of puberty, she is allowed to send the nights with other girls in the house of some spinster or widow called” (ulog in Bontoc). Young men come to the ayamang at night to express their love, The suitors are rejected or admitted to the ayamang depending upon the reactions of the young maidens.\n\nTrial marriage is practiced in the ayamang, A girl can live with the man she prefers. When she is about to become a mother, arrangements are made between their parents for their marriage.\n\nDivorce may be had any time by either party on the Condition that the dissatisfied party pays a fine to the family of the other. All the property of the divorced spouses go to their children.\n\nIfugao customs and traditions abound in taboos, It is taboo for a pregnant woman to wear ornaments forming a circle. It is believed that such ornaments will make child delivery difficult,\n\nPeople from one village cannot pass through another village during rice harvest time. A stranger is not allowed to enter a village during its rest- day celebration. Anyone is prohibited to enter an Ifugao house when the owner is absent. A person doing so is considered a thief even if he has not taken anything from the house.\n\nThe Ifugaos are lovers of law and order. Trial by ordeal by hot water or the hot bolo test -----is practiced in trials for criminal offenses. The trial is a punishment in itself. As a result, there are few crimes in Ifugao land.\n\nFines are assessed in accordance with the wealth of the offending party.\nIn this manner, a poor man Would not be required to pay a fine he cannot afford.\n\nWe have always thought of the Ifugaos as Uncivilized. In the light of their practices, could we still consider them as a society that achieved extraordinary architectural and social milestones independently.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. If you were a modern engineer visiting the Ifugao mountains, which aspect of their ancient \"world wonder\" would you study most to help build sustainable farms in other steep mountain regions?\n\n2. Imagine you are a traveler approaching an Ifugao village during their \"rest-day\" celebration. Based on their taboos, what would be the most respectful and \"creative\" way to handle the situation?\n\n3. If an Ifugao leader wanted to modernize their \"trial by ordeal\" (hot water or hot bolo test) while still keeping their traditional focus on \"law and order,\" what new method might they create?\n\n4. A pregnant Ifugao woman wants to follow the tradition of avoiding circular ornaments but still wants to look beautiful for a ceremony. Based on your creative understanding of the text, what could she wear instead?\n\n5. If the Ifugao \"trial marriage\" system in the ayamang was adapted into a modern school project about \"responsibility,\" what would be the main lesson students would learn?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,32,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1612,816,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 10','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nPLANT THIEVES\nMost plants with green leaves earn their own living. They do not need help from other plants inasmuch as they gather from the soil and air the materials on which they live. Their roots extend deep into the soil seeking out water and the substances the soil supplies.\nMoreover, their leaves get carbon dioxide from the air. With the help of sunshine, they are able to manufacture their food. These plants do not destroy other plants or animals or steal from them to obtain their food. These self- supporting plants live and let other plants live.\n\nBut some plants are dependent on other plants. They also try to obtain all the food they could by robbing their innocent victims. These plants have no green-leaf substances and could not make for themselves. Such plants are supplied entirely by their victims or hosts as they are usually called.\n\nThere are plant thieves that steal from their hosts as much as they can, but because they have green leaves, they manufacture little food for themselves, like all thieves, plant robbers have sneaking ways. They have different ways of attacking their victims, The dodder is a plant that has a tangle of yellow threads. In season, these yellow threads are vine- like sterna twining about the branches of the host plant. The dodder has a bad reputation as a plant thief in the plant world It has no roots nor leaves.\nInstead, there are minute scales. With no roots nor leaves, the dodder grows very well and bears flowers and seeds.\n\nThe secret of its food supply is the slender vines. These thin thread-like stems are firmly fastened by rows of little suckers to the stems and branched of the host plant. The suckers adhere to the host. The dodder sends minute branches right through the bark of its victim into the part where the sap flows.\nThis way, it robs the host of its hard- earned riches supply itself with food for its vigorous growth and for the formation of its cluster of flowers which it shamelessly unfolds over the branches of its victim. The flowers, of course, form abundant seeds to start a new crop of robbers for the next season.\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. If you were an artist creating a \"villain\" character for a comic book based on the dodder plant, which physical feature from the text would be the most accurate \"weapon\" for your character?\n\n2. Imagine you have a garden where a \"plant thief\" has started to grow on your favorite rosebush. Based on how the dodder grows, what would be the most effective way to save your rosebush for the next season?\n\n3. If the \"self-supporting plants\" and the \"plant thieves\" were human characters in a story about a community, which of these scenarios best represents the behavior of the \"Plant Thieves\"?\n\n4. A scientist discovers a way to give \"green-leaf substances\" (chlorophyll) to a dodder plant through a special injection. Based on the logic of the text, how would the dodder\'s life most likely change?\n\n5. If you were writing a \"Safety Guide for Plants,\" what would be the best advice for a \"host\" plant to avoid being attacked by a \"thief\" like the dodder?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,35,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1613,817,123,'post',1,'Post-test','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nPOPE JOHN PAUL II\nPope John Paul II is Karol Wojtyla who was born on May 18, 1920 to Karol and Emilia Kaszrowska. His mother died when he was nine years old. At the age of 21 his father left him, too.\n\nHe graduated at the top of his class when he was 18; began his college studies at the City of Cracow; shifted to Theology in secret during the German occupation, and was ordained priest on November 1, 1946.\nHe was consecrated Bishop on September 28, 1958 and was made an Archbishop on December 30, 1963. Four years later, on May 29, 1967, he became a Cardinal. He then became the Supreme Pontiff a position naming him as the head of the Roman Catholic religion.\n\nMost notable about the Pope is his genuine and sterling love for humanity, his great gearing for peace and love for all men, his keen understanding of human frailties, and most of all, his concern for freedom and human rights.\n\nPope John Paul II is completely averse to the luxury of pomp and grandeur. He is at home in simple surroundings. Named the Head of the church in Poland, he chose a room of Sparta surroundings. When he became a Pope, he reprimanded the personnel staff who removed his belongings to a more comfortable room during his absence. Briefly, he wanted a simple life, sans the trimmings of a luxurious and kingly life.\n\nOn his first day as Supreme Pontiff, Pope John Paul II showed signs of non- confinement within the walls of the Vatican. He disregarded the tradition of seclusion. In less than 24 hours after assumption of office, he was up and about and left the Vatican, much to the concern of the cautious officials, and went to a Roman hospital to pay a visit to an old friend, Polish Bishop Andrzej Maria Deskur. Once more he broke an age-long tradition of talking to a group of hospital officials. As he is desirous to communicate with everyone, he\n\nignores protocol and goes as a politician would, shaking hands and kissing babies.\n\nWhen he came to the Philippines, he was not a stranger in our minds, but a humble brother seeking to find old and new friends, a shepherd tending a flock of sheep. He came as a leader to the only-Christian nation in the Far East back then, the Philippines.\n\nHis message was one of brotherhood, peace, and loving sympathy for the oppressed, the downtrodden, the. unjustly jailed, but most of all intended to families who have a special place in his heart.\n\nHis death on April 2, 2005 was deeply mourned by all people, Catholic and non-Catholic as well.\n\nHe left the legacy of \"Service to others.\"\n\nRead each question thoroughly. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nLiteral Reading Skill These questions test your ability to recall specific facts directly stated in the text.\n\n1. On what date was Karol Wojtyla ordained as a priest?\n\n2. Whom did the Pope visit in a Roman hospital less than 24 hours after assuming office?\n\n3. The text mentions that the Pope \"reprimanded the personnel staff\" for moving his belongings to a more comfortable room. What does this action reveal about his character?\n\n4. Why does the author describe the Pope as acting \"as a politician would\" when he visited people?\n\n5. What is the author\'s primary purpose for mentioning that Karol Wojtyla studied Theology \"in secret\" during the German occupation?\n\n6. Which of the following statements from the text represents an opinion or a metaphor used by the author rather than a historical fact?\n\n7. How does the author support the claim that Pope John Paul II showed signs of \"non-confinement within the walls of the Vatican\"?\n\n8. If Pope John Paul II were to visit a modern school today, which of his core values mentioned in the text would he most likely encourage students to practice?\n\n9. Imagine you are a Vatican official responsible for the Pope\'s safety. Based on the text, what would be your biggest challenge in protecting him?\n\n10. Based on the Pope\'s \"simple life\" philosophy, how would he most likely choose to spend a large donation given to him personally?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,38,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1614,818,124,'pre',1,'Pre-test','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','A GLASS OF MILK\n\nIn a small town, there lived a poor little boy who used to do odd jobs like selling different objects from door to door. The money he earned from those jobs was used to buy his daily food and pay his school expenses. It was a usual day of selling items from house to house when he felt very hungry and sick. He was so famished that he couldn’t even walk any more steps. With enough strength, he decided to ask for food at a house in the neighborhood.\nWhen the poor boy knocked on the door of a house, a beautiful young girl opened the door to his surprise. With much hesitation, he requested the young girl for a glass of water. The boy said, “Can you please give me a glass of water?” The pretty girl was taken aback after seeing the poor boy’s condition and understood how hungry he was. She said, “Sure, wait a minute.” She went into the kitchen, and instead of a glass of water as requested by the little boy, she brought him a glass of milk. “Here, little boy. Drink it!” said the young girl. The boy was astonished to see the glass of milk in front of him. He drank the milk and felt better than ever. After drinking the milk, the boy thanked the kind-hearted girl and asked her, “How much do I owe you for this milk?”\n“Oh dear, I don’t want any money for this”, the girl replied softly. The boy thanked the compassionate girl from the bottom of his heart and then left the place. Years passed by. The young girl grew up into a beautiful grown woman. One day she fell severely ill and was taken to the hospital. There, she had some tests, which unfortunately revealed that she had a rare neurological disorder that required several treatments and great care for many months.\nShe was hospitalized at one of the city’s best hospitals as her health deteriorated day by day. Dr Kevin, a well-known neuro specialist, was assigned the girl’s case by the hospital. Dr Kevin found the girl’s illness hard to cure despite his extraordinary expertise. However, he did not give up. The treatment took months. With determination and much perseverance, Dr Kevin successfully brought the girl’s ailment under control. The girl was finally cured of the disease after careful observation and medication. Everyone in the hospital complimented the doctor for his successful work. But the girl had her own worries about the hospital bills. She thought to herself, “How will I pay for such an extensive treatment when I have only a little money of my own?” The girl’s family did not leave her enough money that could possibly clear her extensive treatment and therapy in that reputed hospital. Finally, the bill arrived. With shaking hands, she opened it and was astonished to see that the bill had been cancelled across. Beneath the amount, there was a note signed by her treatment doctor, Dr Kevin. The note said, “Bill paid in full years ago with a glass of milk!” The girl remembered the young boy she had helped years ago and felt happy.\n\nLiteral Comprehension Questions\n\n1. What kind of work did the little boy do to earn money?\n\n2. What did the young girl give the boy instead of water?\n\n3. Why did the girl give the boy milk instead of water?\n\n4. What does the note “Bill paid in full years ago with a glass of milk” imply?\n\n5. Was it fair for the doctor to cancel the woman’s hospital bill?\n\n6. Which action best shows perseverance in the story?\n\n7. What could have happened if the girl had ignored the hungry boy?\n\n8. If the boy had never met the girl again, what message would the story still teach?\n\n9. If the story were told from the girl’s point of view, what emotion would likely stand out at the end?\n\n10. What modern situation best reflects the message of the story?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,7,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1615,819,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 1','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nA LOST CIVILIZATION\n\nA long, long time ago, thousands of years before there were cities in any place in the world, wandering hunters from the Asia mainland crossed the narrow channel to Alaska.\nThese people who were called Indians slowly spread over the two American continents. Some of them remained hunters, the others became farmers and built great cities. From these Indians descended a distinctive group called the Mayas.\n\nThe Mayas had cities long before any white man landed in the American continent. By the seventh century A.D. they had built many cities in Central America. The ruins of these cities could still be found standing in the jungles of Central America. The most famous of these cities was called \"Chichen Itza\".\nIn its ruins were found many beautiful buildings, terraces, temples, and a great plaza with many courts. The palaces and monasteries were built for their nobles and priests. There was even an observatory for studying the stars and their movements and other meteorological researches:\n\nThe priests carefully watched the movements of the stars. They made a calendar which was as accurate as the one we use today. It was more accurate than the calendars used in Europe and Asia at that time.\n\nThe Mayas dressed in the fine locks. They wore gold ornaments. Most precious among their ornaments were of jade. Jades and rich plume feathers were worn only by the rich and the powerful nobles and priests.\nThey used a system of writing similar to the ancient Babylonians using character symbols because they had no alphabet. They wrote books on their history, astronomy, and religion. Many of these books, however, have been lost. Only three are in existence today.\n\nThe Mayan engineers made fine roads similar to those built by the Romans at the height of their power. They used layers of limestone fill the roadway and gravel to fill in the crevices. They then covered the surface with fine limestone which acted as a sort of cement. A heavy road-roller hewn by hand out of hard stone was rolled over the road over and over again. The rollers were so heavy. It took 15 to 20 men to work them over the roads. The roads they made were very good. With little repairs, they have been used until recent times.\nThe farmers among the Mayas lived in villages outside the cities. They raised crops and built roads, temples and palaces, had no wheeled vehicle nor tamed beasts of burden. They used their own muscles to carry heavy loads.\nHow amazing therefore that they did build magnificent roads and temples and palaces with their own puny hands!\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nRead the selection carefully. Then answer the questions that follow by choosing the correct answer from the given options.\n\n1. Where did the wandering hunters originally come from before crossing to Alaska?\n\n2. What group descended from the early Indians mentioned in the story?\n\n3. What was the most famous Mayan city mentioned in the passage?\n\n4. Who were allowed to wear jade and rich plume feathers according to the story?\n\n5. How did the Mayas carry heavy loads since they had no wheeled vehicles or beasts of burden?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,11,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1616,820,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 2','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nI REFUSE\n\nThe life story of Dr. Jose Rizal has been told and retold many, many times that almost all the intimate details of his life are well known to us. But very little has been written of Jose Rizal\'s mother, Doña Teodora Alonzo. Do many of us know, for example, that she refused to accept pension offered to her by the Philippine government?\n\nDoña Teodora, the mother who gave our nation the neatest Malayan, is a heroine in her own right. She had suffered imprisonment for just being Jose Rizal\'s mother. She suffered more than any other mother in Philippine history for raising and educating her son. Jose.\n\nThe Rizal family had been impoverished because of Dr. Rizal\'s struggles against Spanish tyranny. Doña Teodora found herself in straightened circumstances years after the death of our hero. Therefore, she rightfully deserved the pension offered her by our government.\n\nIn 1908, the Philippine Legislature, aware of Doña Teodora\'s need, unanimously agreed to grant her a life pension as a token of the gratitude of the Filipino people. At that time, she was residing in the house of one of her daughters in Manila. A delegation of lawmakers was appointed to advise her of the pension. The delegation thanked her for the patriotic service to her country and people and told her that the government was giving her a substantial pension which would make her comfortable and secure during her remaining years.\n\n\"A substantial pension is offered to you as a token of the nation\'s gratitude for your patriotism, Doña Teodora, \"the chairman of the delegation said.\n\nDoña Teodora firmly but politely refused to accept the pension. \"Our family is not patriotic because of mercenary motives,\" she told the group of delegation. \"If the government has enough money to offer me a life pension, it should give this money to the poor who deserve it more. I have my children and relatives to take care of me,\" Doña Teodora continued.\n\nThus, the mother of our greatest hero proved herself worthy as a mother of a heroic son. What a contrast to our present-day so-called \"heroes\" and \"patriots\" who not only clamor for pensions and backpays but also, to add insult to injury, claim that they deserve them. Hear howl when action on their application is delayed. They could not wait until their services were given just rewards and recognition.\n\nDoña Teodora\'s self- abnegation should serve as a lesson to our pension-·hungry and so- called \"heroes\" and patriots who mask their greed in the guise of patriotism.\n\nChoose the letter of the best answer.\n\n1. Who is the mother of Jose Rizal mentioned in the story?\n\n2. What did the Philippine government offer Doña Teodora in 1908?\n\n3. Why did Doña Teodora deserve the pension according to the passage?\n\n4. Where was Doña Teodora living when the lawmakers visited her?\n\n5. Why did Doña Teodora refuse the pension?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,14,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1617,821,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 3','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nTHE ENCHANTED TRUNK\n\nThere once lived a fairy in a big balete tree near the mouth of a cave.\nShe was known as Macaban. Unlike other fairies, she was visible most of the time. She had brown eyes and curly hair. Her voice was sweet and was always kind. She was very generous. She was always ready to help the unfortunate, and she comforted them in their sorrows. She gave food and clothing to the poor.\nDuring wedding, she would lend the bridegroom his wedding attire, precious stones, gold tablewares, kitchen utensils, which she kept in storerooms inside an underground city. Anybody could utter his request at the keyhole; the stone door would open at once to let him in and select the things he needed.\n\nThe goodness of Macaban was everywhere. The borrowers came not only from Inabangan but also from neighboring towns.\n\nOne day, a dishonest couple came to borrow some wedding attire, jewels and tableware for their son\'s wedding. They were allowed to enter the city storerooms to get the things they needed. Seeing that nobody kept watch, they picked up several precious objects and put them in their bags and pockets. When they went out of the door, they promised the door keeper to return everything after the wedding, but they did not tell him of the jewels that bulged in their pockets thinking that Macaban would never know.\n\nAfter the wedding, the dishonest couple did not return the things they borrowed. Instead, they went around the town and urged the people to destroy the home of Macaban and to drive the fairy away.\n\nMacaban was angry at what the dishonest couple did to her. So, she thought of teaching them a lesson. She called all the people to enter the city and select a gift for themselves. She gave them 20 minutes to select that they liked best on condition that they must not stay more than the given time.\n\nThe honest people obeyed the command of Macaban for they stayed in the storeroom for only the time allowed. On the other hand, the dishonest couple went into the heart of the city collecting jewels and precious stones.\nWhen the 20minutes were over, Macaban waved her hand. Deep water rushed down the street separating the two high cliffs.\n\n\"Because you are dishonest, you must remain here forever,\" Macaban cried, \"You cannot cross the deep water and escape from the many crocodiles.\"\n\nUntil today, the keyhole, the mouth of the cave, and the stone door can be seen by boat riders going up and down the Inabangan River in Bohol.\nPeople say these parts of the enhanced trunk of Macaban, a splendid city underneath the mountain.\n\nCopy the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. Why did Macaban allow anyone to borrow items from her underground city without strict supervision?\n\n2. What does the dishonest couple’s behavior reveal about their character?\n\n3. Why did Macaban give the people only 20 minutes to choose their gifts?\n\n4. What does the fate of the dishonest couple suggest about the story’s message?\n\n5. What lesson does the story mainly teach readers?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,17,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1618,822,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 4','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nMUTYA NG SAGING\n\nIt was night after supper. The children were gathered around Lolo Hugo waiting for his nightly treat, a story. Lolo Hugo had a trunk load of stories.\nHe had never been at a loss for a story his grandchildren would love so much to hear; and very often, the children were left in wishful thinking after hearing his story.\n\n\"Tell us the story of Juan Tamad,\" said four-year-old Lito, sitting on his grandfather\'s lap.\n\n\"We heard that last night,\" interrupted Imelda. \"Tell us about the aswang or the tikbalang please,\" she continued. She always wanted to hear stories that were shivery and hair-raising. Imelda was Lolo Hugo\'s only granddaughter.\n\n\"Tonight, I\'m going to tell you about the \"Mutya ng Saging”. You must listen very carefully because when you grow up, I would like you to look for it. If you find it, you will be very prosperous and happy all your life.\"\n\nThus goes Lolo Hugo\'s story:\n\n\"The mutya ng saging (magic charm from the banana plant) is considered the strongest and the most charming of all. Only the patient and the stout-hearted could find it.\n\nThe charm cannot be found in all banana plants. This plant grows apart from other banana plants. It produces no young suckling around it; not even weeds would grow within the shadow of Its leaves.\n\nLook for that plant when it is about to bear flowers. You will notice that its purple heart is like that of any other banana plant. But it will open only at midnight when the moon is young. This banana tree will not bear fruit because the heart will fall off when the charm is gone.\n\nSo, you will watch, night after night under this banana tree. At some time before midnight when the heart is about to open, the banana leaves will fly about as if tossed by strong winds, although there is not even the whisper\n\nof a wind animal shapes and shadows shall gather around the banana and frighten you away.\n\nStay beneath the heart and be ready to catch the stone as it falls out. If it falls to the ground, it will sink immediately to the center of the earth. When you catch the stone, swallow it fast because the weird creature will try to wrest it from you. They will bite your hands, tear your mouth. You must fight these creatures until you have swallowed the stone. If you succeed, the creatures, groaning and crying, will leave you alone and go back to where they came from.\n\nHenceforth, love will be yours for the choosing. You will have many friends, and life will come easy and pleasant for as long as you live.\"\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. Why did Lolo Hugo tell the children to listen very carefully to the story of the Mutya ng Saging?\n\n2. What does the difficulty of finding the Mutya ng Saging symbolize?\n\n3. Why do the frightening creatures appear when the banana heart is about to open?\n\n4. What does swallowing the stone before the creatures take it suggest?\n\n5. What is the main message of the story about happiness and prosperity?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,20,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1619,823,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 5','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nTHE WINNING OF ATALANTA\n\nAtalanta, a beautiful and fearless maiden, was the daughter of Scheneus, King of Boetia. What she was most famous for was the fact that she had the fastest pair of feet in the whole world.\n\nBecause of her beauty she had many suitors, but proud Atalanta would not look with favor on any of them.\n\nShe said, \"I will marry only the man who is a faster runner than l.\"\n\nShe told her suitors that whoever really wished to marry her would have to run a race against her. If they failed, they would be put to death. Strangely enough, many of her suitors tried. They lost the race and thus lost their lives.\n\nHippomenes, one of her suitors, watched every race and saw that he could never win against Atalanta. Only a miracle would help him, so he went to Venus, goddess of love and lovers for help.\n\nVenus took pity on him and gave him three golden apples from her garden in the island of Cyprus. She whispered to him the secret of how to use the apples.\n\nHappily, Hippomenes sought Atalanta and asked her permission to run a race with him. Atalanta looked at the young man and her heart filled with pity for him. She tried to persuade him not to try because he might lose his life.\n\n\"I shall gladly die, for without you; life will not be worth living, “Hippomenes said. Finally, with great reluctance, Atalanta agreed to run a race with him.\n\n\"Venus, don\'t forsake me now,\" he whispered as they took their places for the race.\n\nOff they went. Hippomenes was fast but she gained on him and was about to overtake him when he threw one of the golden apples on her path.\nThe girl was attracted by the glitter, so she paused one moment in her flight, scooped up the apple from the ground and ran on.\n\nHippomenes was quite a distance away, but fleet-footed Atalanta easily covered the gap between them. Soon he heard the swift patter of her feet close behind.\n\n\"Venus, kind goddess, do not abandon me now, \"and he tossed the second apple at the feet of Atlanta. For the second time, Atalanta paused, scooped up the apple from the ground then ran on.\n\nHippomenes\' lead did not do him much good for soon Atalanta was close again at his heels and was about to pass him.\n\nThe goal was near His heart beating with joy, Hippomenes threw third golden apple, and it rolled along Atalanta could not resist it. She paused only a brief second and ran again. But before she could catch up with Hippomenes, the young man, with one great bound, reached the goal and won the race.\n\nThey were married and lived happily and had many beautiful and brave descendants.\n\nSelect and copy the letter of the best answer.\n\n1. Was Atalanta’s condition for marriage (racing suitors to death) justified?\n\n2. How should Hippomenes’ use of the golden apples be judged?\n\n3. What does Atalanta’s repeated stopping for the apples suggest about her character?\n\n4. Who bears the greatest responsibility for the deaths of the earlier suitors?\n\n5. What critical lesson does the story suggest about power and pride?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,23,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1620,824,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 6','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nOUR NEIGHBOR,TAIWAN\n\nNorth of the Philippines lies our closest neighbor. It is commonly known as the Republic of China (R.O.C.), or in most international organizations, as \"Chinese Taipei.\" In area, it is about the size of Mindanao.\n\nTaiwan was once a poor, underdeveloped country---enjoy a high standard of living and has a favorable balance of trade. have been put up so fast that new ones are housed under bamboo shelters while permanent buildings are under construction. Farming has been made progressive and modern. Peasants wading in water-filled rice paddies behind water buffaloes have given way to prosperous farms with tractors and other machines.\nBamboo huts have disappeared, replaced by brick stone houses.\n\nTaiwan has succeeded because of its people. The average Taiwanese is industrious and self-reliant and is a willing helper to his neighbor when someone needs help.\n\nTaiwan has realized that if it were to prosper, being basically agricultural country, agriculture must be given primary attention. Instead of first thinking of steelmills;factories,jet, airports, giant buildings and magnificent homes as other underdeveloped countries do, Taiwan began with a program beginning at the grassroots --- improvement of farming and satisfying the people\'s hunger for land.\n\nTaiwan instituted a well-organized land reform program with the aim of providing security and livelihood for the tenants of big landholders. It opened the public land for purchase by tenant-farmers, payable from their crop yield.\nFinally, large private landholdings were brought by/the governments and sold to the tenants at reasonable rates. No landlord could hold more than seven acres of Riceland. The former owners of large tracts of land were encouraged to invest in industrial and business enterprises.\n\nAs a result, Taiwan is self-sufficient in the production of country\'s immediate needs. They produce enough clothing materials. They have an excess of cement production and export over one million tons a year. They have medical and pharmaceutical plants, flour mills, motor cars, and truck factories, chemical fertilizer plants and many other enterprises.\n\nEducational and cultural development in Taiwan is also at a high level.\nAbout 600 billion New Taiwan dollars is spent on education. Taiwan\'s literacy rate as of 2003 is a high 96.1percent of its population.\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. Why did Taiwan choose to prioritize agriculture instead of heavy industries at the start of its development?\n\n2. How did land reform contribute to Taiwan’s economic success?\n\n3. What does the passage suggest about the role of people in Taiwan’s development?\n\n4. Which development approach in the passage is implied to be less effective for poor countries?\n\n5. What lesson can other developing countries learn from the experience of Taiwan?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,26,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1621,825,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 7','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nAFRICA, THE DARK CONTINENT\nWhenever we think of Africa, we seem to picture a large continent covered with either huge desert, high plateau and mountains, and vast impenetrable jungles inhabited by black uncivilized people. We oftentimes overlook the past that Egypt, which is part of Africa, was once cradle of the first great civilization. Egypt had built magnificent temples, palaces, and the pyramids when most on Europe was still in a barbaric stage.\n\nAfrica has often been called the Dark Continent. The true Africa is a continent of vivid contrasts. There are many kinds of landforms and the climate is variable. There are the hot desert Sahara Desert and the high plateau which covers the area south of the Sahara. There are dense jungles, broad fertile plains and high snow-capped mountains. As a result of these differences, many ways of living have developed in Africa\'s varied kinds of lands.\n\nThe areas north of the Sahara have been part of the rich and colorful life of the Mediterranean world. Sahara\'s great expanse is not entirely a waste of shifting sands. Only one-tenth of the desert is covered by sand. The rest are plains, hills, mountains, and thousands of oases where date palms and other palms thrive. Egypt and Ethiopia are regions which are productive, culturally and agriculturally. Theirs is a rich history.\nBoth the eastern and western coastal areas are fertile. Here are cites of the cities and other centers of population. Southern Africa is a pleasant land and is one of the continent\'s most prosperous areas. Central Africa\'s rivers have their source here.\n\nCentral Africa is covered with dense jungles. So much rain falls that trees and plants grow very fast. Thick bushes and tough vines twinning from trees make passage difficult, sometimes impossible. The jungles are inhabited by pygmies who never mingle with other people.\n\nThey depend on fruits, roots, and small animals which they kill with poisoned arrows. Elsewhere, the plateaus are grasslands with only a few trees.\nThe grasslands with all grass are the home of elephants, buffaloes, and rhinoceros. The areas where the grass is not very tall are the roving areas of all kinds of deer, giraffes, and zebras.\n\nThe African plateau is peopled by Negroes (Bantu)who have little contact with civilization. They are very tall men; men seven feet in height are common sight. Many of them earn their living through primitive farming, hording, and hunting, they have a tribal form of government; each village has a headman, usually related to the chief of the tribe. There are many war-like tribes in the heart of Central Africa.\n\nThe civilized people of Africa are mostly Muslims. The Muslim Arabs were the first people to have commercial and cultural contact with Africa.\nChristian missionaries have made inroads in the establishment of Christianity in many parts of Africa.\n\nWith these innovations, could we say then that Africa is no longer the Dark Continent?\n\nSelect the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. Is the term “Dark Continent” fair?\n\n2. Why does the author mention ancient Egypt early in the passage?\n\n3. What weakness appears in the author’s descriptions of some African groups?\n\n4. How should modern readers react to words like “uncivilized” and “primitive”?\n\n5. Based on the passage, what is the strongest reason Africa should no longer be called the “Dark Continent”?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,29,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1622,826,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 8','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nJOSE RIZAL AND BENIGNO AQUINO, JR.\nby: Alberto G. Romulo\n\nIt is often said that it is difficult to make comparisons of two people, even twins, who may have different idiosyncrasies, mannerisms, qualities, principles, and so forth. The opposite to this could be easy.\n\nBenigno Aquino, or Ninoy for one, was likened to Jose Rizal in courage and service to the nation. Rizal had spent years in Europe studying and writing and had incurred the wrath of the friars with his novels Noli Me Tangere and El Filibusterismo.\n\nBenigno Aquino had incurred the intense hatred of Ferdinand Marcos for his political activities including speeches on political platforms and in the Senate highly critical of the then Chief Executive.\n\nThe friars ran after Rizal, but they could not get their hands on him as long as he stayed abroad. Obviously, when he announced that he was coming home, his friends and relatives hastened to tell him to stay abroad.\n\nNinoy was one or only the reason why Marcos declared Martial Law. He was immediately arrested by the military, tried before a military tribunal on charges of murder, possession of prohibited firearms and communism and was finally condemned to die before a firing squad.\n\nFrom the start, Ninoy refused to submit to trial, saying that the military judges\' Commander- in- chief, who was Marcos, had already publicly condemned him on the same charges. He also refused to be represented by counsel.\n\n\"Sirs,\" he addressed the tribunal, \"I know you to be honorable men. You may decide to preserve my life, but Marcos chooses to send me to death. Some people suggest that I beg for mercy. But this, in conscience, I cannot do. l would rather die on my feet with honor than live on bended knees with shame.\"\n\nOne day he suffered terrible chest pain and rushed to the Heart Center.\nThe doctors told him that he needed a coronary bypass of which the Center is incapable of performing. The facilities were inadequate.\n\nNinoy went to Houston, Texas, and received a triple bypass. Three years later, he wanted to come back but Malacañang did everything to keep him out. Marcos and Imelda told him the Communists would assassinate him if he came home. His family and friends also warned him of the danger.\n\nHowever, he managed to come home with a false passport, taking China Airlines. On arrival he was whisked out from his seat and assassinated while going down the stairs.\n\nRizal had likewise defied his family and friends and came home. He was arrested and sentenced to die by firing squad at the Luneta.\n\nRizal and Aquino will live in history with parallel deeds for their countries and similar claims to the gratitude of the Filipinos. Rizal\'s death incensed the revolution against Spain. Aquino\'s death sparked and ignited the peaceful and bloodless revolution that ousted Marcos from power and sent him out the country.\n\nSelect the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. If Jose Rizal and Benigno Aquino Jr. were alive today, how might they express their opposition to injustice?\n\n2. If Rizal had chosen not to return to the Philippines, how might Philippine history have changed?\n\n3. Imagine Ninoy Aquino chose to stay abroad permanently. What effect might this have had on the Filipino people?\n\n4. What modern leader could be compared to Rizal and Ninoy based on the qualities shown in the text?\n\n5. If you were asked to write a new title for the essay, which would best capture its message?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,32,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1623,827,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 9','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nTHE SEA HORSE\n\nNature has many strange creatures, some of them huge, some of them small. One of these strange creatures is the sea horse. This animal of the sea has the arching neck of a wild stallion, the swelling bosom of a pigeon, the grasping tail of a monkey, and the power to change its color that belongs to a chameleon. It has eyes that pivot independently of one another so that while one looks up, the other can look down or around. To top this fantasy, the male-is equipped with a kangaroo style pouch in its front, from which the little ones are born.\n\nThere are more than 40 species, ranging in length from one inch to one foot, and they can be found in the warm seas all over the world. The sea is a master of camouflaging. It can change color to go with its surroundings.\n\nThe sea horse\'s body is sheathed in a tough, Capiz-like armor of bony plates. Because of this rigid armor, it swims standing up by fluttering a tiny yellow, fanlike fin on its back, propelling itself in a stately manner. Straight as a rod, it moves forward or backward, up and down, by manipulating that fin so fast that movement is hardly visible. Two membrane- like fins at the sides of its head wave carelessly.\n\nThe worst thing that can happen to a swimming sea horse is a puncture in its armor. A single puncture in its armor could cause air to escape from its sealed armor and make the sea horse sink helplessly to the bottom. There it remains until he has manufactured enough gas to fill up his tank.\n\nThe courtship of sea horses is strange. The female produces from 500 to 600 eggs and deposits them in the male\'s pouch. For the next 45 days, the father sea horse nourishes the nestling in his swelling incubator pouch. At the end of this period, a baby sea horse the size of a comma swims out, followed by another, then another. This continues until the whole brood is ejected from the incubator. Each baby is an exact replica of its parents, except that the body is transparent. Its tiny heart can be seen beating with aid of magnifying glass.\n\nThe sea horse feeds on plankton and minute plant and animal life. It would not feed on anything that is not alive and moving. The sea horse itself is not fit to be eaten either by man or by fish.\n\nWhat use is a sea horse? Studies made have not revealed any useful purpose in the sea horse. However, everything in nature has a need and a purpose. Perhaps, sea horses were made to keep the level of plankton down so that the latter would not overrun the sea.\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. If the sea horse could speak, what do you think it would complain a bout most?\n\n2. If the sea horse did not have the ability to change color, how might it survive?\n\n3. Which invention could be inspired by the sea horse’s body design?\n\n4. If humans were like sea horses, what part of society would change t he most?\n\n5. If scientists discovered that sea horses suddenly disappeared, what might happen to the ocean?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,35,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1624,828,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 10','open','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.','ACTIVITY 10 Creative thinking)\n\nRead the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nABRAHAM LINCOLN\n\nMany stories have been told of the boy who was born in a log cabin of very poor parents; of how he learned to read by the light of the fireplace; and of how he walked long distances to borrow books. From humble beginnings, he rose and, became the Great Emancipator.\n\nAs a young man, he tended store for a neighbor. On one occasion he unintentionally short-changed a customer. It was long after the customer had departed when he discovered his mistake. He walked miles and miles to the customer\'s house just to return the change. That was how he earned the name \"Honest Abe\".\n\nThen he became President of the United States. When he was troubled by affairs of state, he took long walk to clear his mind. On these walks he was usually dressed in his old clothes. With his long strides he often outdistanced the men assigned to guard him.\n\nAt dusk of one winter, he went on one of these walks. Snow had covered the roads making them slippery. He was walking along an almost deserted street, when a carriage driving fast overtook him. Suddenly, a dog ran across the street frightening the horses. They shied, driving the carriage to a deep ditch, its rear wheel stuck in the deep muddy ditch. The horses were unable to move the carriage no matter how they tired. The colored driver and the footman kept urging the horses, but the carriage-wheel sank deeper.\n\nThe passenger, apparently a very wealthy and important man because of his elegant and rich garb, keep shouting at his men, waving his arms in anger and frustration. He had an appointment to keep at the White House.\n\nThe tall, bearded pedestrian came to the scene, and at a glance saw what had happened. He approached the carriage on the side stuck in the ditch, put his shoulders to the wheel, and signaled the coachman to prod the horses. At first, the carriage would not move. With another mighty heave of the tall pedestrian, the carriage finally moved out of the ditch. The coachman leaped to his place, the passenger sank to his seat, and the carriage drove on to the White House.\n\nThe passenger was just on tome for his appointment. In the President\'s room, he sat, a little impatient. In his country, he was an important man. In this wild. country, he did not relish being kept waiting.\n\nAt last, a door opened. A tall, bearded man entered, his clothes splattered with mud and snow. He gravely took the suddenly limp hand of the dumb- stricken visitor, then went around the desk and, mud and all, sat on the chair of the President of the United States.\n\nEtched on an old-fashioned ink stand on which the tall man dipped a quill pen was the name: A. Lincoln!\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. If Abraham Lincoln lived in today’s world, which action would best show his honesty and humility?\n\n2. How might the wealthy passenger’s view of leadership have changed after meeting Abraham Lincoln?\n\n3. If Lincoln had chosen not to help the stranded carriage, what message would the story send instead?\n\n4. Imagine the story were told from the passenger’s point of view. What feeling would likely dominate the ending?\n\n5. What modern-day situation best parallels Lincoln pushing the carriage out of the ditch?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage and answer the questions that follow.',NULL,10,38,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1625,829,125,'post',1,'Post-test','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Read the selection below and answer the questions that follow.\n\nSTORY OF LOVE AND TIME\n\nA long time ago, there lived on an island different feelings such as happiness, sadness, love, knowledge, etc.\nOne day, they got to know that the island is going to sink soon. All my feelings got worried. The feelings living on the island decided to leave it with their pre-constructed boats. Love was the only one who decided not to leave the island. The day came when the island began to sink. All the feelings were leaving on their boats. Love asked Richness to take him in his grand boat but he said that he already filled his boat with gold and money so he could not take him.\nLove requested Sadness to take him along but he said that he is very sad so he wants to be alone. Next, he called for help to Happiness but he was so happy and joyful that he did not listen to Love and passed in front of him without paying attention to him.\nLove became very sad when no one was taking him in their boat. He heard a voice, “Hey dear friend! Come along with me.” Love came to a safe place in a boat. Love was so overjoyed that he forgot to ask who he was when he asked Knowledge who brought him. He further asked Knowledge if he knew him. Knowledge said, “It was Time who brought you here”. Love asked, “Why did Time help me?” Knowledge replied with great wisdom, “Only Time knows the value of Love.”\n\nLiteral Comprehension Questions\n\n1. Where did the feelings live?\n\n2. Who helped Love escape?\n\n3. Why did Richness refuse to take Love on his boat?\n\n4. What does Happiness passing Love without noticing suggest?\n\n5. Was Love right to stay behind when the island was sinking?\n\n6. Which feeling showed the least responsibility in the story?\n\n7. Why is Time the one who saves Love, according to the story?\n\n8. If Love had left early, how would the story’s message change?\n\n9. If the story were retold today, who might represent “Time”?\n\n10. Which modern situation best reflects the lesson of the story?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,42,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1626,830,126,'pre',1,'Pre-test','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Activity 9    It is not “Why?” But “What?”      Creative      39 Comprehension\n\nActivity 10    Aklan’s Most Illustrious Son     Creative      44 Comprehension\n\nPost Test                                      71\n\nTHE SMALLEST FISH IN THE WORLD\n\nBefore 1925, the \"tabios\" was thought to have been the smallest fish in the world. A full-grown \"tabios\" measured a fraction over eleven millimeters. The \"tabios\" is found in Lake Buhi, in the Bicol Region of Luzon.\n\nIn 1925, Dr. Albert Herre, an ichthyologist, was assigned by the American government to identify and describe Philippine fishes. From a collection of the Philippine National Museum, he found the prototypes of a still smaller type of fish.\nThere were 75 of the tiny fish which he named Pandaka Pygmaea in a bottle. They were so small that the \"tabios\" appeared big by comparison.\n\nWhen Dr. Herre went back to the United States, he sent a few specimens to   the     Stanford    National    Science Museum.       Dr.   Herre’s   findings   were doubted because this fish remained a rarity. No one had ever seen a live specimen of the fish that Dr. Herre claimed was the smallest fish in the world.\n\nOn February 28, 1951, Ricardo Esguerra, one of the marine biologists working in the saltwater experiments station of the Bureau of Fisheries in Malabon, Rizal, excitedly called up the Bureau’s main office and announced that he had found live specimens of the Pandaka. Inocencio Ronquillo, an expert of the Bureau, hurried to the experiment station and studied the tiny fish which had eluded scientists for many years. He measured and weighed them. To confirm his findings, Ronquillo wrote to the curator of Stanford University Museum of Natural Science and asked for some specimens of the fish that Dr. Herre had taken before.\n\nFinally, Dr. Leonard Schulz, the museum’s representative, pronounced that they were one and the same type. Thus after 25 years, the Philippines could at least claim to have the world’s smallest fish. The Pandaka has now been found in various parts of the Philippines.\n\nThe smallest fish is known under the scientific name of Pandaka Pygmaea.\nSamples have been sent to the museums and aquariums of cities all over the world.\n\nThe Pandaka’s average length when full-grown is 9.66 millimeters or about the size of a grain of rice. It has a moderately elongated body that is wedge-shaped when viewed from above. Its head is large in proportion to its size. Its eyes protrude.\nIt has two rows of teeth on either side of the jaw, the outer teeth being larger and more widely set than the inner row.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. Why was the discovery of Pandaka pygmaea in the Philippines in 1925 considered historically important?\n\n2. What was Dr. Albert Herre’s main role in the history of Pandaka pygmaea?\n\n3. How did Ricardo Esguerra contribute to the story of Pandaka pygmaea in 1951?\n\n4. In historical discussions, why is the local fish known as tabios often mentioned alongside Pandaka pygmaea?\n\n5. Which statement best describes the typical habitat historically associated with Pandaka pygmaea in the Philippines?\n\n6. From a biological perspective, why is the tiny adult size of Pandaka pygmaea scientifically intriguing?\n\n7. Historically, how did the \"smallest fish in the world\" label affect public and scientific attention toward Pandaka pygmaea?\n\n8. What does the historical replacement of tabios in popular discussion by Pandaka pygmaea as the \"smallest fish\" mainly illustrate?\n\n9. Why is it important for modern students in the Philippines to learn about the historical discovery and rediscovery of Pandaka pygmaea?\n\n10. What conservation lesson can be drawn from the story of Pandaka pygmaea’s small size and specialized habitat?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,6,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1627,831,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 1','open','Then the mother spoke to the younger one. “Don’t you think you should forgive your brother before you go to bed?” Remember the Bible says, “Don’t let the sun go down on your wrath.”','LET THERE BE NO GRUDGES\n\nThis story is about a young boy who got into a fight with his elder brother. Enoch is his name, and his elder brother would always pick on him to the extent of making him angry and feeling bitter later.\n\nOne morning they were it again. They fought hard and exchanged blows with each other. Somewhat outmatched because Enoch was wanting in size and height, he took quite a beating. His elder brother’s muscles were too much for him. His pride and ego suffered the most. The whole incident left him bitter towards his elder brother. As a matter of fact, he refused to talk or look at his brother. He was gritting his teeth with anger for not being able to put up square fight.\n\nBedtime came. Their mother wanted to pacify them and told them to make up as soon as possible.\n\nEnoch was still grumbling, but his eyes were fixed towards the elder brother’s\n\nThe younger son looked toward his mother with perplexity. He thought for a few seconds and then said, “Mother, how can I keep the sun from going down?” The boy’s question revealed that he had no intention of getting rid of his grudge.\n\nSo, the mother explained to her younger son and made him understand what life would be like when people hold grudges against\n\neach other. It is true that there are people with an unforgiving attitude and true too, that we cannot change another person’s heart.\n\nShe further explained that we cannot keep the sun from going down.\nBut we can keep it from setting on your anger. And that means we must forgive.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nDirections: Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What is the name of the younger boy in the story?\n\n2. Why was Enoch outmatched in the fight?\n\n3. What did Enoch refuse to do after the fight?\n\n4. What Bible quote did the mother reference?\n\n5. What was Enoch\'s literal response to his mother\'s advice?',NULL,'[]','Then the mother spoke to the younger one. “Don’t you think you should forgive your brother before you go to bed?” Remember the Bible says, “Don’t let the sun go down on your wrath.”',NULL,10,13,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1628,832,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 2','open','Choose the letter of the correct answer.','THE VALUE OF WORK\n\nEverything has value, no matter how insignificant or simple it is. At the mere mention of work, one immediately connects with the idea of toil and fatigue, which pertains to a slave who overdoes things in a difficult manner.\nEverything in this world involves work.\n\nIn what light should work be viewed? Anything that comes from something done is achieved by means of labor and work. It is of two-fold nature, either mental or physical. Both are indispensable to the well-being of human society. Our attention is directed towards physical labor.\n\nChristianity teaches us to regard work as something sacred and honorable. Work is duty. You must not only work hard, but you must also love it wholeheartedly. Without it, order and cleanliness can never exist in the house. A state of affairs would be a mess and discomfort would prevail.\n\nPeople who work and stay busy have no time for hanging around, gossiping, or being with bad company. Women who are fond of work are more virtuous and purer.\nThere is no dull moment in their lives, there is so much to gain when they are physically occupied.\n\nBe proud, therefore, and love your work. Do it diligently and make it your best. Treat it as an inseparable friend. Whatever the nature of your work may be,\n\ndo it with care and diligence. If it is a domestic chore, do not find fault and make critical remarks, but put your hand at everything.\n\nIf your work is to be really done well, if it is to gain merit for yourself, perform it with a good intention. Yes, be patient, for without patience, no labor, toil, or work can be meritorious in the eyes of someone above.\n\nThese lines speak well of this: Swiftly time speeds on its way. See that thou use it well. Let each hour of every day be a tale of wisdom to tell.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. What immediate association does the passage make with the mention of \"work\"?\n\n2. According to the passage, work has what kind of nature?\n\n3. What does Christianity teach about work?\n\n4. What do people who work and stay busy avoid, per the passage?\n\n5. What must accompany work to make it meritorious \"in the eyes of someone above\"?',NULL,'[]','Choose the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,16,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1629,833,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 3','open','Choose the letter of the correct answer:','TAJ MAHAL: AN IMMORTAL MONUMENT TO AN EMPEROR’S LOVE\n\nIn Agra, India, stands an immortal monument to an emperor’s love. It was built by Emperor Shah Jahan for his third wife, the beautiful Mumtaz Mahal. Mumtaz, the queen, was born in 1593.\n\nHe was a good emperor, and he ruled wisely. During his reign, magnificent buildings and palaces were built. He had a goldsmith build an elaborate throne of rare gems. It was a long, low couch with golden legs. A canopy, which rested on twelve emerald pillars, was over the throne. A tree of diamonds, rubies, emeralds, and pearls stood beside it.\n\nThen, in 1631, Queen Mumtaz Mahal died in childbirth. The emperor was grief-stricken. He forbade music in his court, gave up wearing the colored imperial robes, and wore only white for mourning. A sepulcher was built for her. Twenty thousand workers labored 20 years to complete it. The best artisans, sculptors, designers, and carvers in India and nearby countries were employed under the most renowned architect of those days, Ustad Ahmad.\n\nThe monument was made of precious stones and valuable materials.\nThe glorious white marble used comes from India; garnets and lapis lazuli come from Ceylon. All in all, more than 40 varieties of precious stones were used, including turquoise mined in Tibet.\n\nOn the north side of the great highway leading to the sepulcher, a tall gateway, 80 feet high, guarded the entrance. The gateway doors were made of silver and studded with semi-precious stones. No less beautiful are the surroundings of the sepulcher. In the waters of the famous reflecting\n\npool, the image of the tall dome floats eternally in blue. Everything around the place is made to draw the eyes to the central figure of the immortal monument to an emperor’s love, the Taj Mahal.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. What can be inferred about Shah Jahan\'s leadership style from the description of his building projects and throne?\n\n2. Why might the emperor\'s choice of white robes after Mumtaz\'s death symbolize more than simple mourning?\n\n3. What does the involvement of artisans from \"India and nearby countries\" suggest about the monument\'s purpose?\n\n4. How does the reflecting pool\'s design infer the Taj Mahal\'s intended emotional impact?\n\n5. What broader inference can be drawn about Mumtaz Mahal\'s role in Shah Jahan\'s life from the monument\'s scale?',NULL,'[]','Choose the letter of the correct answer:',NULL,10,19,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1630,834,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 4','open','Fill in the blanks with the correct words.','NO LOVE, NO VICTORY\n\nLove is an indispensable necessity for every human heart. But it is of great importance, especially for every young person, to have in his heart a true, genuine, and abiding love of God.\n\nIt is said that the decisive battles to be fought are with the devil, the world, and the flesh. However, with the power of love - the love of God - all these can be conquered. It is only when the ardent love of God dwells in one’s heart that he may hope to speak of victories. The history of the world, the pages of sacred history, and the history of each individual alike teach us that without love, there can be no victory.\n\nLove, in a general sense, conquers both good and evil things. Think of what inspired heroes in all ages and led them to achieve immortal deeds of glory. What inflamed the heart of Napoleon the Great, pushing himself forward without rest and conquering countries of Europe? It was love for their fatherland.\n\nWhat causes the miser to suppress the strongest impulse of nature, the desire for food and drink, and literally to die of hunger beside his stock of gold? It is the love of money. What usually compels lovers to commit suicide, conquering even the love of life? Again, it is love which has been rejected.\n\nWhat is the motive which makes a mother overcome her desire for ease and comfort, sacrificing money, time, health, and everything? It is love for her child. What enables good Christian married people to practice self-control,          to    overcome selfishness, and to set aside their own wishes? It is love and conjugal affection for each other. And how was the greatest victory over sin, death, and hell, the victory\n\nwon by the Redeemer triumphant? It was a victory of the love of God for us, human beings. Such is the all-conquering might of love. Without it, there is no victory.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. What can be inferred as the primary source of power needed to win \"decisive battles\" against the devil, world, and flesh?\n\n2. From Napoleon\'s conquests and the miser\'s starvation, what does the passage imply about love\'s influence on human behavior?\n\n3. What can be inferred about a mother\'s sacrifices from the passage\'s example?\n\n4. Based on the lovers\' suicide and spouses\' self-control, what universal truth about love emerges?\n\n5. How does the passage imply Christ\'s victory over sin, death, and hell relates to all other examples?',NULL,'[]','Fill in the blanks with the correct words.',NULL,10,22,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1631,835,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 5','open','Choose the letter of the correct answer.','WHAT IS CADETSHIP?\n\nThere is more to the glamour and prestige that has always been associated with the Philippine Military Academy; that is, the sacrifice and truth. Blood, sweat, and tears are behind all of these.\n\nFirst, it is blood, for it is the individual alone who can make himself survive the continuously fluctuating changes and pressures of Academy life.\n\nSecond, is sweat - it is the painful process of changing from the carefree ways of civilian life to the exacting demands of military life.\n\nThird, are tears for the joy, pride, and feeling of fulfillment of having survived obstacles, something between life and death.\n\nLet it be known, however, that nothing can be a much better source of pride than being able to conquer one’s weaknesses and turn them into strengths. Life begins with a very young man so eager to join the ranks of the “long gray line,” attracted by its glamour, only to be led down the wrong path upon entering and leaving sooner than he came.\n\nThe “Beast Barracks” or Summer Training is something that this same young man feels is too much for him. But it takes some attitude and courage, or guts, so to speak.\nOnce he is in, he is exposed to things he has never dared to think of before. He does things he cannot imagine and wonders how they are done.\n\nAnyway, it is every cadet’s goal as he prays to aspire above the level of common life and to adhere to the patterns of their creed - truth, nobility, and excellence.\nThe Summer Camp Training is a near-perfect program of development and training. One has to survive and surmount it through thick and thin. The feeling to quit is there after a rough time of one month or more.\n\nBut when Incorporation Day comes, every sacrifice is compensated. It is better that one finds out for oneself what it really is like to be a part of this closely-knit big family, that is, the Academy.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. According to the text, what does \"blood\" symbolize in the essence of cadetship?\n\n2. Why does the text describe \"Beast Barracks\" or Summer Training as pivotal for cadets?\n\n3. What is the cadet\'s ultimate goal as per the creed mentioned?\n\n4. How does the text characterize the transformation during cadetship?\n\n5. What compensates for the sacrifices of cadetship, per the text?',NULL,'[]','Choose the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,25,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1632,836,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 6','open','Choose the letter of the correct answer.','HANNIBAL, THE GREAT\n\nCarthage, a great city-state in North Africa, had grown into power. She had extensive colonies in the African continent, conquered Spain, and was in constant warfare with the Roman Empire and its satellite cities. The war, which had been going on between Rome and Carthage for nearly a century, had been indecisive, with the Carthaginians having lost and won as many battles as the Romans.\n\nIn Carthage, there arose a general, Hannibal, who almost destroyed the Roman Empire. No general had ever beaten the famed Roman army in as many battles as Hannibal had, and no army had caused so much fear and destruction as Hannibal’s army did.\n\nHannibal conceived of attacking the Romans in Italy. It was a feat never before made or even dared by any army. He began his invasion with 90,000-foot soldiers, 12,000 horsemen, and 40 elephants. He reached the foot of the Alps and crossed these mountains in nine days. Then, he met the Roman armies.\n\nBy swift maneuvers, Hannibal managed to meet the Roman armies one by one.\nThe sight of the giant elephants charging them brought terror to the Roman cavalry.\nThey fled from the fight and were easily defeated. On one occasion, Hannibal found himself completely surrounded. He ordered his army to fasten burning torches on the cattle horns. At midnight, he drove the animals toward a heavily defended enemy front. The terrible sight frightened the Roman ranks and destroyed them completely.\n\nIn many of these battles, Hannibal’s forces were often outnumbered, but his elephants\' juggernaut, the swiftness of his movements, and his brilliant command\n\ngave him victory. For 16 years, Hannibal roamed the Italian peninsula, defeating all the armies sent against him.\n\nMeanwhile, powerful Roman forces had been sent to Carthage. Hannibal was then recalled defending Carthage. However, his forces had been decimated in the Italian campaign.\n\nAt Zama, near Carthage, the powerful Roman army met Hannibal’s forces. Here, Hannibal met his defeat with a loss of 20,000 men. When peace was concluded, the Romans demanded Hannibal’s surrender to Rome.\n\nTo escape, Hannibal committed suicide.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. What was the primary strategic innovation of Hannibal\'s invasion that shocked the Romans?\n\n2. At Cannae, Hannibal\'s double envelopment tactic succeeded because:\n\n3. Why did Hannibal fail to capitalize on victories like Trebia and Trasimene for total Roman collapse?\n\n4. At Zama, Scipio Africanus defeated Hannibal mainly by:\n\n5. Hannibal\'s suicide in Bithynia (Libyssa) exemplified his:',NULL,'[]','Choose the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,28,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1633,837,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 7','open','Choose the letter of the correct answer.','A GOOD TURN DESERVES ANOTHER\n\nIn life, there are many things that happen which are least expected.\nWhen these things turn to blessings, people’s hearts are softened, and they look up above to praise Him in glorious words. Some may just leave occurrences and daily happenings to fate and may keep everything to themselves.\n\nBut have you ever gone out of your way to do something for someone and had it go unnoticed? Very often it is difficult to believe that doing good to others is worth the effort, especially when we don’t receive a “thank you” in return. And yet, serving without looking for a reward is just like walking with God peacefully. As Christians, we should not display a cash-and-carry attitude, expecting immediate appreciation for the good we do. We must remember that someday he will richly reward us.\n\nA magazine article told of a businessman who facilitated his dealings in the car business with a very young foreigner. The latter bought a new car, and a very fast and honest transaction took place between them. Short of some thousand pesos to complete the full payment, the businessman agreed to collect the amount after two weeks. Two weeks passed, but the buyer still could not produce the amount as his money had not come yet.\nSo a grace period was given and extended to another two weeks. Finally, the deal was over. Everything was paid for.\n\nTen years later, the young man had become the sole purchasing agent for the Saudi Arabian Contractor’s Association. To repay the businessman’s hospitality and to show his gratitude for all the conveniences\n\nafforded to him, he placed a million-dollar order for 500 heavy dump trucks and 200 pickups. The businessman blinked his eyes with disbelief. “It’s unbelievable!” he exclaimed. “I just can’t believe it, but it’s true.” The good he had done was rewarded years later, beyond his wildest imagination.\n\nAs the lines rightfully say: \"Be not weary in your serving; Do your best for those in need. Kindness will be rewarded, By the Lord who prompts the deed.\"\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. What is the primary theological lesson the text draws from the businessman\'s story?\n\n2. How does the text contrast a \"cash-and-carry attitude\" with Christian service?\n\n3. The story of the young foreigner illustrates which key idea about rewards?\n\n4. According to the text, why might people doubt the value of unnoticed good deeds?\n\n5. What does the closing poem reinforce about the overall message?',NULL,'[]','Choose the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,31,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1634,838,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 8','open','Choose the letter of the correct answer.','THE LION IS DYING\n\nThe lion is the largest and most celebrated member of the cat family.\nIt has an apparently large-sized head because of the great mane which covers its head, neck, and shoulders. By the time the lion is five or six years old, it can weigh more than 500 lbs and can be over 9 ½ feet long from the tip of its nose to the tip of its tail.\n\nThe lion has the reputation of being fearless, but it is more accurate to describe it as sly. Its instincts are like those of a house cat, and it hunts like a cat. It quietly stalks its prey, then, making a short, quick bound, lands on it.\nIt mauls and bites the head of its victim until its neck is broken, and its veins and arteries are torn open.\n\nThe lion will eat anything it can find. While in the prime of its strength, it will hunt all sizes of deer, zebras, and even buffaloes. However, as it grows older, it may live on rodents, scorpions, and tiny creatures, even rotting meat.\n\nThe lion was at one time known as the king of the jungle. The jungles of Africa, Asia Minor, and India were once the lion’s domain. But man has been slowly but surely exterminating the species. The lion has disappeared in Asia Minor, Iran, and even in many parts of Africa. The remnants of the lion family in Asia are now confined to Northwestern India’s GIR forest, a reservation. There, some 160 lions remain, a pathetic remnant of a once prolific species.\n\nHowever, even in its sanctuary, the lions are losing their natural prey of deer and wild buffaloes because men have encroached on the sanctuary and have domesticated the buffaloes. The lions are forced to\n\nslaughter and feed on domesticated buffaloes, and the herdsmen retaliate by killing the lions.\n\nThe king of the jungle is fast becoming extinct. Unless man gives the lions a place where they can live and multiply peacefully, and where their natural diet of deer and other animals can also live, many may never see a lion again except in museums or in picture books.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. What does the passage imply about the lion\'s reputation for fearlessness?\n\n2. How does the lion\'s diet as it ages interpret its adaptability?\n\n3. What can be inferred from lions\' confinement to India\'s GIR forest?\n\n4. What does the sanctuary encroachment suggest about human-lion conflict?\n\n5. The passage\'s title and conclusion interpret the lion\'s fate as?',NULL,'[]','Choose the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,34,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1635,839,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 9','open','Fill in the blank with the correct word to complete the sentence.','IT IS NOT \"WHY?\" BUT \"WHAT?\"\n\nWhen any misfortune or ill luck comes our way, we often ask, \"Why me, God?\nWhy should this happen to me?\" Then we examine ourselves as to whether we are right in professing our faith or giving the appearance of being one who believes in the professed faith. Something dictates that perhaps it would be better if we ask, \"What can I learn from this? What is God teaching me?\"\n\nThis story is told by a missionary in China many, many years ago about a woman who boarded a train with him in Europe. The first thing the missionary noticed was her radiant face, so sweet, and troubles seemed far from existence.\nThey talked about anything under the sun, and her face would brighten up when mission and religious work were discussed. Obviously, she had not experienced trying circumstances.\n\nAs the train sped off and their talk turned into intimate conversation, the missionary observed that the woman\'s right hand was missing, and in its place was a steel hook. As their talk continued, the missionary learned that this lady friend had been a missionary in India, had contracted a lung disease, and had been sent home to die. So, she returned to her native country, Sweden, bought a farm, and worked daily on it.\n\nOne day, while she was working on a threshing machine, her right hand was cut. It was a big blow, a great misfortune in her life, but she accepted it as one of life\'s trials. She continued: When my hand was cut off, I immediately looked up and said, \"Lord, what do you really want me to do now that my right hand is gone?\nWhat work? I was not asking why but what?\"\n\nWhat a brave woman! She deserves all the praises this world possesses with her unflinching courage. Her strength of character is really commendable. God used her to turn her farm into a home for the aged, the sick, and the feeble. She is still there, bringing blessings to the unfortunate.\n\nHow many adverse circumstances come into your life? Our nature is to question God\'s designs and workings. He just wants you to show the genuineness of your faith and the adequacy of His grace. So stop asking why. God will show you what.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. What does the missionary initially infer about the woman\'s life from her radiant face?\n\n2. The woman\'s response to losing her hand primarily illustrates which attitude toward misfortune?\n\n3. How does the story interpret the role of adversity in testing faith?\n\n4. What transformation resulted from the woman\'s \"What?\" question after her injury?\n\n5. The story\'s core lesson equates asking \"What?\" with demonstrating:',NULL,'[]','Fill in the blank with the correct word to complete the sentence.',NULL,10,37,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1636,840,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 10','open','Choose the letter of the correct answer.','AKLAN’S MOST ILLUSTRIOUS SON\n\nJaime L. Cardinal Sin was born on August 31, 1928, in New Washington, Aklan, to Juan Sin and Maxima Lachica. He was ordained as a priest on April 3, 1954; served as the first Rector of St. Pius X Seminary in Roxas City from 1957 to 1967; appointed Auxiliary Bishop of Jaro in March 1967 and later Archbishop of Manila on March 19, 1974. Finally, he was named a Cardinal at the Consistory on May 26, 1976. His chosen motto was “Serviam,” which means “to be a servant.” Cardinal Sin generously gave himself in complete service and absolute devotion to God and His flock. He displayed dynamic and militant leadership, which was his inspiration in his deep faith and commitment to lead his people to Jesus Christ.\n\nDuring his elevation to the Cardinalate, he urged everyone to join him in a revolution, a revolution of love. Heeding the needs of his people, he tirelessly fought for the ideals of freedom, truth, and justice. He believed in the separation between the Church and State. Nevertheless, he asserted that the relationship between the two should be collaborative rather than adversarial.\n\nThrough his inspired leadership, he united the Filipino people to make the democratic process work in our country, and thus emerged as “People Power.”\nCardinal Sin’s contagious sense of humor seemed to have become a byword. His wit and wisdom presented to us the partnership between humor and intellectual insight.\nHe thought of himself as an elder brother in the task that the Lord had given him. Full of hope for a more humane citizenry, he turned to everyone on this land to tell them, “Let us choose the way of peace. And the way of peace is the way of justice and truth, the way of deep respect before God for the rights of each one.”\n\nThis was one of his favorite lines: “Man achieves the fullness of his humanity when his dignity is respected, when his freedom to do good is protected. Working hand in hand, the Church and the State can do so much to prevent man’s dehumanization.” Before his death in 2006, he had assailed the continuing graft and corruption in the government and had called for a more vigorous and unrelenting drive against these. His interest called for a change not only in the government but the private sector as well.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. What does Cardinal Sin\'s motto \"Serviam\" primarily reflect about his approach to leadership?\n\n2. During his elevation to Cardinal, what kind of \"revolution\" did Sin urge, and what does it signify about his vision?\n\n3. How did Cardinal Sin view the ideal relationship between Church and State, despite believing in their separation?\n\n4. What interpretive role did Sin play in fostering \"People Power,\" according to the biography?\n\n5. What broader change did Sin advocate before his death, beyond just government graft?',NULL,'[]','Choose the letter of the correct answer.',NULL,10,40,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1637,841,127,'post',1,'Post-test','open','Choose the letter of the best answer.','P a g e | 44\n\nTHE OLYMPIC GAMES\n\nPeople all over the world are thrilled when athletic meets are held in various parts of the globe. World sports bring together athletes from all over. Do you know when and how these Olympic games came about?\n\nThe first Olympic games originated in Ancient Greece. These games were first held during the Panhellenic celebrations. Every four years, patriotic and religious games were held during the celebrations. The year 776 B.C. marked the first of such a recorded festival. The Greeks then began to keep their calendar by \"Olympiads\" from this date or by the four-year span between the games.\n\nSimplicity marked the first Olympic games. It was said that the first consisted of only a foot race of about 200 yards on the plain near the small City of Olympia. As years passed, the kinds of games were increased and led to competitions that brought forward national pride and unity. The competitions were based on fair rules of sportsmanship and justice. However, at first, only Greek citizens were allowed to take part in the games. The winners were crowned with laurel and awarded wild olive and palm wreaths.\nWinners were also given many special privileges in their respective communities.\n\nDuring the reign of the Roman rulers, these games went down to the level of carnivals and circuses. Theodosius, one of the Roman emperors, prohibited the holding of the games in 394 B.C.\nFor almost 2,300 years, no Olympic games were held.\n\nP a g e | 45\n\nThen in 1894, a French educator, Baron Pierre de Coubertin, planned to revive the games. He invited many countries to participate in the athletic meets. The French Baron believed that by   means    of   the   youth’s   love   for   sports,   international understanding would develop.\n\nIn 1896, Baron Pierre de Coubertin was able to enlist nine nations to send their athletes to the first Modern Olympics. This marked the beginning of the participation of thousands of athletes representing nearly all nations to compete in these Olympic games which are held every four years in different countries. The Olympic Games are instrumental in bringing together athletes from.\n\nRepresentatives of different countries compete with each other in games of skill and sportsmanship. The athletes get to know each other very well and learn that all races love the same ideals of fair play, courage, understanding, and friendliness. When the athletes return to their respective countries, they are better world citizens after having participated in activities replete with understanding and brotherhood.\n\nP a g e | 46\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\n1. In what year was the first recorded Olympic festival held?\n\n2. What marked the simplicity of the first Olympic games?\n\n3. Who prohibited the Olympic games in 394 A.D.?\n\n4. What can be inferred about the Greek calendar system from the text?\n\n5. Why were winners awarded special privileges in their communities?\n\n6. What led to nearly 2,300 years without Olympic games?\n\n7. The text critically suggests Roman influence degraded the games by turning them into what? (Critical)\n\n8. Baron de Coubertin\'s belief implies what interpretive goal for modern Olympics? (Interpretive)\n\n9. How does the text interpret the role of Olympic rules? (Interpretive)\n\n10. Critically, what makes athletes \"better world citizens\" per the text? (Critical)',NULL,'[]','Choose the letter of the best answer.',NULL,10,44,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1638,842,128,'pre',1,'Pre-test','open','Read the selection and answer the comprehension check.','More than a million people immigrate to the United States to start new lives every year, and if they are arriving in New York, one of the first sights that they will see is the Statue of Liberty. The Statue of Liberty stands on Liberty Island, near Manhattan in New York. Though she is often thought to be a resident of New York, Liberty Island is actually federal property, which means that the Statue of Liberty belongs to the whole country. The Statue of Liberty is not only the tallest statue in America, but it is also one of the most recognizable American symbols.\n\nThe Statue of Liberty is huge. From the tip of the torch to the pedestal on which she stands, she is just over 151 feet tall. If you include the pedestal in your measurement, she stands more than 305 feet off the ground. That’s more than 30 basketball hoops or an entire football field. Her waist size is 35 feet, which would make it awfully tough to find pants, and the tablet she holds is 23 feet long. Don’t worry though; she hasn’t had any trouble holding that tablet yet with her 8-foot index finger. Talk about heavy-handed…\n\nThough America financed and built the pedestal on which the Statue of Liberty stands, the statue itself was a gift from France. In this way, the complete work, much like the United States, is a product of both American and French contributions. At one time, America was ruled by the British.\nThe founding fathers of America chose to fight against Great Britain for the independence of their country. France supported America by providing money, men, and weapons of war. Had it not been for French contributions during the Revolutionary War, America would not exist in the way that it does today; therefore, it is quite fitting that the Statue of Liberty, which represents freedom, came to being by a joint American and French effort. On October 28th, 1886, just over one hundred years after America declared its independence from Great Britain, the Statue of Liberty was completed and dedicated by its designer, Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi.\n\nPerhaps no person did more to bring the statue into being than Bartholdi. Not only did Bartholdi gain both French and American approval for the project, but he also led the French fundraising efforts   and   designed    the   appearance       of   the   statue.   The appearance of the Statue of Liberty is somewhat derived from Libertas, the Roman goddess of freedom. The torch represents how liberty enlightens the world. The seven points or rays coming from the top of the crown\n\nrepresent the sun, the seven seas, the seven continents, and (as with the torch) how liberty enlightens the world. Though much of the statue was modeled after depictions of goddesses, Bartholdi modeled the face after his mother. Now that’s a Mother’s Day gift that’s hard to top.\n\nThough Bartholdi was responsible for the external appearance of the statue, the internal design can be largely credited to the innovative designer, Gustave Eiffel. Though Mr. Eiffel is best known for his contributions to a tower in Paris that is named after him, he also engineered the internal structure that holds up the Statue of Liberty. Eiffel chose to use a flexible structure so that changes in temperature and strong winds from the ocean would not cause the statue to crack.\nBecause of Eiffel’s crafty design, the Statue of Liberty may sway as much as three inches on a windy day. If the winds exceed 50 miles per hour, the torch may sway five inches. Eiffel is also responsible for including two spiraling staircases on the interior of the statue to help visitors reach the observation point in the crown.\n\nWhile most people appreciate the Statue of Liberty today, during its construction in the 1870s, many Americans were critical of the project. Some took issue with the fact that Bartholdi was French. They believed that American monuments should be designed and constructed by Americans. Others felt that the statue wasn’t much of a gift since it required Americans to foot the bill for the pedestal. After the Panic of 1873, America fell into a deep economic depression that lasted through much of the 1870s. (During an economic depression, people spend less money and it is harder to find jobs.) Because the nation was going through a depression, many Americans thought that money should not be spent to support a giant French statue. I think most Americans would now agree that it was a good inves\n\nCOMPREHENSION CHECK\n\n1. What is the height from the torch tip to the pedestal?\n\n2. When was the Statue dedicated by its designer?\n\n3. What does the waist/tablet size imply about clothing or holding?\n\n4. Why might Americans criticize the \'gift\' during the 1870s?\n\n5. What enables the statue to sway in wind without cracking?\n\n6. What do the crown\'s seven rays critically represent?\n\n7. Critically, why fitting for French-U.S. joint effort?\n\n8. Who credits for face model vs. internal staircases?\n\n9. Interpret text\'s view: Good investment despite criticism?\n\n10. In the late 19th century, some critics argued that funding the Statue of Liberty was a poor investment. From an interpretive, long-term perspective, which reason best explains why it can now be seen as a good investment despite that criticism?',NULL,'[]','Read the selection and answer the comprehension check.',NULL,10,6,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1639,843,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','CARVER, A MAN OF MANY TALENTS\n\nGeorge Washington Carver was an African American. He was a research chemist with many remarkable achievements in chemistry, economics, and industry.\n\nGeorge was born in 1864 to parents who had been slaves. The Civil War in America had set him free, but he chose to stay on the plantation of his former owner, Moses Carver.\nGeorge learned how to care for plants instinctively and collected different plant species, creating his own botanical garden.\n\nAt the age of 8, George wanted to learn how to read, but the nearby schools were only for white children. He was given a blue-backed speller and learned all the words it contained in a short amount of time. Then, young George read the Bible. Perhaps his Bible readings helped him develop the ability to make friends.\n\nAt the age of 10, George left the Carvers to continue his studies. He found a home with the Watkins, who treated him like one of the family, similar to how the Carvers had done. George was a very fast learner and completed the entire primary school course in a single year. He worked his way through upper-grade school, high school, and college, ultimately earning a Bachelor of Science degree in Agriculture.\n\nGeorge was appointed head of the Department of Agricultural Research at a Negro university. There, he conducted an astonishing series of experiments. From peanuts, he developed nearly 300 products, including milk, butter, cheese, shaving lotion, breakfast food, flour, soap, ink, and cosmetics. From sweet potatoes, he developed 118 substances, including starch, vinegar, shoe blacking, library paste, and candies.\n\nHe developed dyes from clay, onions, beans, and tomatoes. He made oil paints from clay, peanuts, and cattle manure. From wood shavings, he created synthetic marble.\nHowever, peanuts were his main interest. Dr. Carver said, \"From peanuts, one can find food and shelter; he can also produce medicine, washing powders, face bleach, candy, wallboards for homes, and hundreds of other things.\"\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the best answer:\n\n1. When was George Washington Carver born?\n\n2. What did George learn to do instinctively as a child on the Carver plantation?\n\n3. What book did young George read after mastering the blue-backed speller?\n\n4. From peanuts, how many products did George Washington Carver develop?\n\n5. From sweet potatoes, how many substances did he develop?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,11,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1640,844,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 2','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','One of the modern world’s intriguing sources of mystery has been airplanes vanishing in mid-flight. One of the more famous of these was the disappearance in 1937 of a pioneer woman aviator, Amelia Earhart.\n\nOn the second last stage of an attempted round the world flight, she had radioed her position as she and her navigator searched desperately for their destination, a tiny island in the Pacific. The plane never arrived at Howland Island. Did it crash and sink after running out of fuel? It had been a long haul from New Guinea, a twenty-hour flight covering some four thousand kilometers.\n\nDid Earhart have enough fuel to set down on some other island on her radioed course? Or did she end up somewhere else altogether? One fanciful theory had her being captured by the Japanese in the Marshall Islands and later executed as an American spy; another had her living out her days under an assumed name as a housewife in New Jersey.         Seventy years after Earhart’s disappearance, ‘myth busters’ continue to search for her. She was the best-known American woman pilot in the world. People were tracking her flight with great interest when, suddenly, she vanished into thin air.\n\nAircraft had developed rapidly in sophistication after World War I, with the 1920s and 1930s marked by an aeronautical record-setting frenzy. Conquest of the air had become a global obsession. While Earhart was making headlines with her solo flights, other aviators like high-altitude pioneer Wiley Post and industrialist Howard Hughes were grabbing some glory of their own. But only Earhart, the reserved tomboy from Kansas who disappeared three weeks shy of her 40th birthday, still grips the public imagination.\n\nHer disappearance has been the subject of at least fifty books, countless magazine and newspaper articles, and TV documentaries. It is seen by journalists as the last great American mystery.\n\nThere are currently two main theories about Amelia Earhart’s fate. There were reports of distress calls from the Phoenix Islands made on Earhart’s radio frequency for days after she vanished. Some say the plane could have broadcast only if it were on land, not in the water.\n\nThe Coast Guard and later the Navy, believing the distress calls were real, adjusted their searches, and newspapers at the time reported Earhart and her navigator were marooned on an island. No one was able to trace the calls at the time, so whether\n\nEarhart was on land in the Phoenix Islands or there was a hoaxer in the Phoenix Islands using her radio remains a mystery.\n\nOthers dismiss the radio calls as bogus and insist Earhart and her navigator ditched in the water. An Earhart researcher, Elgen Long, claims that Earhart’s airplane ran out of gas within fifty-two miles of the island and is sitting somewhere in a 6,000-square-mile area, at a depth of 17,000 feet. At that depth, the fuselage would still be in shiny, pristine condition if ever anyone were able to locate it. It would not even be covered in a layer of silt.\n\nThose who subscribe to this explanation claim that fuel calculations, radio calls, and other considerations all show that the plane plunged into the sea somewhere off Howland Island. Whatever the explanation, the prospect of finding the remains is unsettling to many.\n\nTo recover skeletal remains or personal effects would be a grisly experience and an intrusion.\nThey want to know where Amelia Earhart is, but that’s as far as they would like to go. As one investigator has put it, “I’m convinced that the mystery is part of what keeps us interested.\nIn part, we remember her because she’s our favorite missing person.”\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. In what year did Amelia Earhart disappear during her attempted round-the- world flight?\n\n2. What was the destination island Earhart was searching for when she radioed her position before vanishing?\n\n3. Approximately how long was the flight from New Guinea to Howland Island, covering about 4,000 kilometers?\n\n4. One fanciful theory suggested Earhart was captured by whom and executed as a spy?\n\n5. According to researcher Elgen Long, where is Earhart\'s plane likely located, within 52 miles of Howland Island?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,14,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1641,845,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 3','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Marie Curie was one of the most accomplished scientists in history. Together with her husband, Pierre, she discovered radium, an element widely used for treating cancer, and studied uranium and other radioactive substances. Pierre and Marie’s amicable collaboration later helped to unlock the secrets of the atom.\n\nMarie was born in 1867 in Warsaw, Poland, where her father was a professor of physics. At an early age, she displayed a brilliant mind and a blithe personality. Her great exuberance for learning prompted her to continue her studies after high school. She became disgruntled, however, when she learned that the university in Warsaw was closed to women. Determined to receive a higher education, she defiantly left Poland and in 1891 entered the Sorbonne, a French university, where she earned her master’s degree and doctorate in physics.\n\nMarie was fortunate to have studied at the Sorbonne with some of the greatest scientists of her day, one of whom was Pierre Curie. Marie and Pierre were married in 1895 and spent many productive years working together in the physics laboratory. A short time after they discovered radium, Pierre was killed by a horse-drawn wagon in 1906. Marie was stunned by this horrible misfortune and endured heartbreaking anguish. Despondently, she recalled their close relationship and the joy that they had shared in scientific research. The fact that she had two young daughters to raise by herself greatly increased her distress.\n\nCurie’s feeling of desolation finally began to fade when she was asked to succeed her husband as a physics professor at the Sorbonne. She was the first woman to be given a professorship at the world-famous university. In 1911, she received the Nobel Prize in chemistry for isolating radium. Although Marie Curie eventually suffered a fatal illness from her long exposure to radium, she never became disillusioned about her work. Regardless of the consequences, she had dedicated herself to science and to revealing the mysteries of the physical world.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nRead and understand the questions carefully. Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What can be inferred about Marie Curie\'s personality from her decision to leave Poland for education?\n\n2. Based on the description of her collaboration with Pierre, what inference can be made about their relationship?\n\n3. What does Marie\'s emotional response to Pierre\'s death and her subsequent actions imply about her resilience?\n\n4. From her acceptance of the Sorbonne professorship and Nobel Prize, what can be inferred about her dedication?\n\n5. Despite radium\'s fatal effects, what inference arises about Marie\'s commitment to science?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,17,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1642,846,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 4','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Mount Vesuvius, a volcano located between the ancient Italian cities of Pompeii and Herculaneum, has received much attention because of its frequent and destructive eruptions. The most famous of these eruptions occurred in A.D. 79.\n\nThe volcano had been inactive for centuries. There was little warning of the coming eruption, although one account unearthed by archaeologists says that a hard rain and a strong wind had disturbed the celestial calm during the preceding night. Early the next morning, the volcano poured a huge river of molten rock down upon Herculaneum, completely burying the city and filling the harbor with coagulated lava.\n\nMeanwhile, on the other side of the mountain, cinders, stones, and ash rained down on Pompeii. Sparks from the burning ash ignited the combustible rooftops quickly. Large portions of the city were destroyed in the conflagration. Fire, however, was not the only cause of destruction.\nPoisonous sulfuric gases saturated the air. These heavy gases were not buoyant in the atmosphere and therefore sank toward the earth and suffocated people.\n\nOver the years, excavations of Pompeii and Herculaneum have revealed a great deal about the behavior of the volcano. By analyzing data, much as a zoologist dissects an animal specimen, scientists have concluded that the eruption changed large portions of the area’s geography. For instance, it turned the Sarno River from its course and raised the level of the beach along the Bay of Naples. Meteorologists studying these events have also concluded that Vesuvius caused a huge tidal wave that affected the world’s climate.\n\nIn addition to making these investigations, archaeologists have been able to study the skeletons of victims by using distilled water to wash away the volcanic ash. By strengthening the brittle bones with acrylic paint, scientists have been able to examine the skeletons and draw conclusions about the diet and habits of the residents. Finally, the excavations at both Pompeii and Herculaneum have yielded many examples of classical art, such as jewelry made of bronze, which is an alloy of copper and tin. The eruption of Mount Vesuvius and its tragic consequences have provided everyone with a wealth of data about the effects that volcanoes can have on the surrounding area. Today, volcanologists can locate and predict eruptions, saving lives and preventing the destruction of other cities and cultures.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What can be inferred about the initial warning signs before the A.D. 79 eruption?\n\n2. Why was Herculaneum buried more rapidly than Pompeii?\n\n3. What inference explains deaths in Pompeii beyond fire damage?\n\n4. How do excavation methods imply skeletons were fragile?\n\n5. What broader inference about volcanoes stems from Vesuvius data?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,20,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1643,847,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 5','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','The victory of the small Greek democracy of Athens over the mighty Persian Empire in 490 B.C. is one of the most famous events in history. Darius, the king of the Persian Empire, was furious because Athens had interceded for the other Greek city-states in revolt against Persian domination. In anger, the king sent an enormous army to defeat Athens, thinking it would take drastic steps to pacify the rebellious part of the empire.\n\nPersia was ruled by one man. In Athens, however, all citizens helped to rule. Ennobled by this participation, Athenians were prepared to die for their city-state. Perhaps this was the secret of the remarkable victory at Marathon, which freed them from Persian rule. On their way to Marathon, the Persians tried to fool some Greek city-states by claiming to have come in peace. The frightened citizens of Delos refused to believe this. Not wanting to abet the conquest of Greece, they fled from their city and did not return until the Persians had left. They were wise, for the Persians next conquered the city of Eritrea and captured its people.\n\nTiny Athens stood alone against Persia. The Athenian people went to their sanctuaries. There, they prayed for deliverance and asked their gods to expedite their victory. The Athenians refurbished their weapons and moved to the plain of Marathon, where their little band would meet the Persians. At the last moment, soldiers from Plataea reinforced the Athenian troops.\n\nThe Athenian army attacked, and Greek citizens fought bravely. The power of the mighty Persians was offset by the love that the Athenians had for their city. Athenians defeated the Persians in both archery and hand combat. Greek soldiers seized Persian ships and burned them, and the Persians fled in terror. Herodotus, a famous historian, reports that 6,400 Persians died, compared to only 192 Athenians.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What primarily provoked King Darius to send an army against Athens? A.Athens\' direct conquest of Persian territories\n\n2. How did the citizens of Delos respond to the Persians\' claim of peaceful intentions?\n\n3. What reinforcements arrived to support Athens just before the battle?\n\n4. According to Herodotus, what were the reported casualties in the battle?\n\n5. What factor does the account suggest offset Persian numerical superiority?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,23,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1644,848,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 6','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','One of the most intriguing stories of the Russian Revolution concerns the identity of Anastasia, the youngest daughter of Czar Nicholas II. During his reign over Russia, the czar had planned to revoke many of the harsh laws established by previous czars. Some workers and peasants, however, clamored for more rapid social reform. In 1918, a group of these people known as Bolsheviks overthrew the government. On July 17 or 18, they murdered the czar and what was thought to be his entire family. Although witnesses vouched that all the members of the czar’s family had been executed, there were rumors suggesting that Anastasia had survived. Over the years, a number of women claimed to be Grand Duchess Anastasia. Perhaps the most famous claimant was Anastasia Tschaikovsky, who was also known as Anna Anderson.\n\nIn 1920, 18 months after the czar’s execution, this terrified young woman was rescued from drowning in a Berlin river. She spent two years in a hospital, where she attempted to reclaim her health and shattered mind. The doctors and nurses thought that she resembled Anastasia and questioned her about her background. She disclaimed any connection with the czar’s family.\nEight years later, however, she claimed that she was Anastasia. She said that she had been rescued by two Russian soldiers after the czar and the rest of her family had been killed. Two brothers named Tschaikovsky had carried her into Romania. She had married one of the brothers, who had taken her to Berlin and left her there, penniless and without a vocation. Unable to invoke the aid of her mother’s family in Germany, she had tried to drown herself.\n\nDuring the next few years, scores of the czar’s relatives, ex-servants, and acquaintances interviewed her. Many of these people said that her looks and mannerisms were evocative of the Anastasia that they had known. Her grandmother and other relatives denied that she was the real Anastasia, however.\n\nTired of being accused of fraud, Anastasia immigrated to the United States in 1928 and took the name Anna Anderson. She still wished to prove that she was Anastasia, though, and returned to Germany in 1933 to bring suit against her mother’s family. There she declaimed to the court, asserting that she was indeed Anastasia and deserved her inheritance.\n\nIn 1957, the court decided that it could neither confirm nor deny Anastasia’s identity.\nAlthough it will probably never be known whether this woman was the Grand Duchess Anastasia, her search to establish her identity has been the subject of numerous books, plays, and movies.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What year did the woman later known as Anna Anderson first claim to be Anastasia?\n\n2. According to her story, how did Anna Anderson say she survived the execution of the czar\'s family?\n\n3. What was the outcome of the 1957 court decision regarding Anna Anderson\'s identity?\n\n4. Why did many of the czar\'s relatives, ex-servants, and acquaintances find Anna Anderson convincing at first?\n\n5. What action did Anna Anderson take in 1933 after immigrating to the United States?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,26,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1645,849,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 7','open','This idea was repellent to the Wright brothers, however, and they searched for more efficient methods to control the balance of airborne vehicles. In 1900 and 1901, the Wrights tested numerous gliders and developed control techniques. The brothers’ inability to obtain enough lift power for the gliders almost led them to abandon their efforts.','Many great inventions are initially greeted with ridicule and disbelief. The invention of the airplane was no exception. Although many people who heard about the first powered flight on December 17, 1903, were excited and impressed, others reacted with peals of laughter. The idea of flying an aircraft was repulsive to some people. Such people called Wilbur and Orville Wright, the inventors of the first flying machine, impulsive fools. Negative reactions, however, did not stop the Wrights. Impelled by their desire to succeed, they continued their experiments in aviation.\n\nOrville and Wilbur Wright had always had a compelling interest in aeronautics and mechanics. As young boys, they earned money by making and selling kites and mechanical toys.\nLater, they designed a newspaper-folding machine, built a printing press, and operated a bicycle- repair shop. In 1896, when they read about the death of Otto Lilienthal, the brothers’ interest in flight grew into a compulsion.\n\nLilienthal, a pioneer in hang-gliding, had controlled his gliders by shifting his body in the desired\n\nAfter further study, the Wright brothers concluded that the published tables of air pressure on curved surfaces must be wrong. They set up a wind tunnel and began a series of experiments with model wings. Because of their efforts, the old tables were repealed in time and replaced by the first reliable figures for air pressure on curved surfaces. This work, in turn, made it possible for the brothers to design a machine that would fly. In 1903, the Wrights built their first airplane, which cost less than $1,000. They even designed and built their own source of propulsion—a lightweight gasoline engine. When they started the engine on December 17, the airplane pulsated wildly before taking off. The plane managed to stay aloft for 12 seconds, however, and it flew 120 feet.\n\nBy 1905, the Wrights had perfected the first airplane that could turn, circle, and remain airborne for half an hour at a time. Others had flown in balloons and hang gliders, but the Wright brothers were the first to build a full-size machine that could fly under its own power. As the contributors to one of the most outstanding engineering achievements in history, the Wright brothers are accurately called the fathers of aviation.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. Initial Public Reaction What does the passage imply about why some people ridiculed the Wright brothers\' airplane?\n\n2. Brothers\' Early Interests How did the Wright brothers\' background in kites, printing, and bicycles most directly contribute to their aviation success?\n\n3. Response to Lilienthal\'s Method Why did the Wright brothers reject Lilienthal\'s body-shifting control for gliders?\n\n4. Key Scientific Breakthrough What was the primary outcome of the Wrights\' wind tunnel experiments?\n\n5. Milestone Achievements Which statement best evaluates the Wrights\' progression from 1903 to 1905?',NULL,'[]','This idea was repellent to the Wright brothers, however, and they searched for more efficient methods to control the balance of airborne vehicles. In 1900 and 1901, the Wrights tested numerous gliders and developed control techniques. The brothers’ inability to obtain enough lift power for the gliders almost led them to abandon their efforts.',NULL,10,29,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1646,850,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 8','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','In the 16th century, an age of great marine and terrestrial exploration, Ferdinand Magellan led the first expedition to sail around the world. As a young Portuguese noble, he served the king of Portugal, but he became involved in the quagmire of political intrigue at court and lost the king’s favor. After he was dismissed from service by the king of Portugal, he offered to serve the future Emperor Charles V of Spain.\nA papal decree of 1493 had assigned all land in the New World west of 50 degrees W longitude to Spain and all the land east of that line to Portugal. Magellan offered to prove that the East Indies fell under Spanish authority. On September 20, 1519, Magellan set sail from Spain with five ships. More than a year later, one of these ships was exploring the topography of South America in search of a water route across the continent. This ship sank, but the remaining four ships searched along the southern peninsula of South America. Finally, they found the passage they sought near 50 degrees S latitude. Magellan named this passage the Strait of All Saints, but today it is known as the Strait of Magellan.\n\nOne ship deserted while in this passage and returned to Spain, so fewer sailors were privileged to gaze at that first panorama of the Pacific Ocean. Those who remained crossed the meridian now known as the International Date Line in the early spring of 1521 after 98 days on the Pacific Ocean.\nDuring those long days at sea, many of Magellan’s men died of starvation and disease.\n\nLater, Magellan became involved in an insular conflict in the Philippines and was killed in a tribal battle. Only one ship and 17 sailors under the command of the Basque navigator Elcano survived to complete the westward journey to Spain and thus prove once and for all that the world is round, with no precipice at the edge.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What does the passage imply about the role of political intrigue in Magellan\'s career shift?\n\n2. How does the passage interpret the significance of the papal decree in motivating Magellan\'s voyage?\n\n3. What interpretive insight does the passage offer about the challenges of Pacific crossing?\n\n4. The passage portrays Magellan\'s death as what kind of turning point?\n\n5. What broader interpretation does the passage make about the expedition\'s legacy despite losses?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,32,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1647,851,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 9','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Conflict had existed between Spain and England since the 1570s. England wanted a share of the wealth that Spain had been taking from the lands it had claimed in the Americas.\n\nElizabeth I, Queen of England, encouraged her staunch admiral of the navy, Sir Francis Drake, to raid Spanish ships and towns. Though these raids were on a small scale, Drake achieved dramatic success, adding gold and silver to England’s treasury and diminishing Spain’s supremacy.\n\nReligious differences also caused conflict between the two countries. Whereas Spain was Roman Catholic, most of England had become Protestant. King Philip II of Spain wanted to claim the throne and make England a Catholic country again. To satisfy his ambition and also to retaliate against England’s theft of his gold and silver, King Philip began to build his fleet of warships, the Spanish Armada, in January 1586.\n\nPhilip intended his fleet to be indestructible. In addition to building new warships, he marshaled 130 sailing vessels of all types and recruited more than 19,000 robust soldiers and 8,000 sailors. Although some of his ships lacked guns and others lacked ammunition, Philip was convinced that his Armada could withstand any battle with England.\n\nThe martial Armada set sail from Lisbon, Portugal, on May 9, 1588, but bad weather forced it back to port. The voyage resumed on July 22 after the weather became more stable.\n\nThe Spanish fleet met the smaller, faster, and more maneuverable English ships in battle off the coast of Plymouth, England, first on July 31 and again on August 2. The two battles left Spain vulnerable, having lost several ships and with its ammunition depleted. On August 7, while the Armada lay at anchor on the French side of the Strait of Dover, England sent eight burning ships into the midst of the Spanish fleet to set it on fire. Blocked on one side, the Spanish ships could only drift away, their crews in panic and disorder. Before the Armada could regroup, the English attacked again on August 8.\n\nAlthough the Spaniards made a valiant effort to fight back, the fleet suffered extensive damage. During the eight hours of battle, the Armada drifted perilously close to the rocky coastline. At the moment when it seemed that the Spanish ships would be driven onto the English\n\nshore, the wind shifted, and the Armada drifted out into the North Sea. The Spaniards recognized the superiority of the English fleet and returned home, defeated.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What does the passage primarily imply about the economic roots of the Spain-England conflict?\n\n2. How does the text interpret King Philip II\'s dual ambitions in launching the Armada?\n\n3. What interpretive role does weather play in the Armada\'s early setbacks according to the passage?\n\n4. The passage interprets the English burning ships tactic as what kind of strategic advantage?\n\n5. Overall, what does the narrative suggest about the Armada\'s defeat beyond numerical superiority?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,35,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1648,852,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 10','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Do you know the difference between a submarine and a submersible? A submarine is a watercraft capable of independent operation under the sea. Submarines do not require support ships because they can renew their air and power supplies independently. Submersibles also submerge and operate underwater, but they need the support of a larger vessel. Submersibles cannot renew their air and power supplies without support. For this reason, submersibles are usually smaller and cannot spend as much time underwater as submarines.\n\nThe first documented submersible was constructed in 1620 by Cornelius Drebbel. It was powered by rowing oars underwater. Though this craft was originally designed for underwater exploration, it did not take long for inventors and makers of war to recognize the military potential of the submersible. In 1648, Bishop John Wilkins wrote, “It may be of great advantage against a Navy of enemies, who by this may be undermined in the water and blown up.” Over one hundred years later, the first military submarine was ready to be deployed. The Turtle was the world’s first submarine used in combat. Designed by David Bushnell in 1775, it was deployed by the Continental Army during the American Revolutionary War. Though the Turtle did utilize a support ship in combat, it was fully capable of renewing its air and power supplies independently; therefore, the Turtle is considered to have\n\nbeen a submarine and not a submersible. The Turtle was powered by hand-cranked propellers and was named as such because it resembled a turtle. It held a single person, moved about three miles per hour in calm water, and contained enough air to stay submerged for about thirty minutes.\n\nGeneral Washington authorized an attack using the Turtle on a British flagship blockading New York Harbor. Sgt. Ezra Lee operated the Turtle and attempted to affix underwater explosives to the British ship, but he failed. The Turtle was later destroyed by the British. Despite this failure, the Turtle is still remembered as the first submarine used offensively during war. Another notable submarine originally designed for war was Julius H. Kroehl’s Submarine Explorer. Built between 1863 and 1866, this submarine was created for the North during the American Civil War, but the war ended before it went into use. After the war, it was used commercially to harvest pearls in Panamanian waters during the late 1860s. Unfortunately, the dangers of decompression sickness (a condition that occurs when divers rise to the water’s surface too quickly) were not understood. While\n\nexperimenting with the Submarine Explorer in 1867, Kroehl himself perished from decompression sickness. In 1869, a new engineer put the Submarine Explorer back to the task of harvesting pearls.\nTragically, the use of the Submarine Explorer was discontinued after the entire crew died from decompression sickness. Submarine use increased greatly during World War I. Due to innovations in engineering, such as a dual power system using both diesel and electric sources, submarines had finally developed into effective war machines.\n\nOne watercraft called the U-Boat was put to great effect by the Germans. Some argue that the U-Boat was more of a submersible, since U-Boats operated primarily on the surface using diesel engines and submerged only occasionally to attack using battery power, but the effectiveness of the U-Boat in combat is certain. During World War I, more than 5,000 Allied ships were sunk by U- Boats, including the Lusitania, which is often cited as a reason why America entered the war. U- Boats were again utilized extensively by the Germans during World War II. Though the U-Boats were devastating to British fleets, advances in technology such as radar and sonar reduced their overall effectiveness. Additionally, the U.S. had also developed and deployed a fleet of submarines to great effect. Though the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor destroyed or severely damaged many of the U.S. Navy’s frontline Pacific Fleet ships, U.S. submarines survived the attack and went on to cause great damage. Submarines, though only about 2 percent of the U.S. Navy, destroyed over 30 percent of the Japanese Navy. This made U.S. submarines the most effective anti-ship and antisubmarine weapon in the entire American arsenal. Modern submarines are now powered by a nuclear reactor. The nuclear reactor generates a tremendous amount of power and frees the submarine from the need to occasionally\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. What does the passage imply as the primary reason submersibles like Drebbel\'s 1620 craft were limited compared to later submarines?\n\n2. Why does the passage classify the Turtle (1775) as a submarine despite using a support ship in combat?\n\n3. What interpretive lesson about early submarine risks emerges from the Submarine Explorer\'s pearl harvesting in the 1860s?\n\n4. How does the passage interpret U-Boats\' WWI success (over 5,000 Allied ships sunk) despite surface-primary operation?\n\n5. What broader implication does the passage suggest about modern nuclear submarines from WWII U.S. effectiveness (30% Japanese Navy destruction)?',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,38,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1649,853,129,'post',1,'Post-test','open','Read the selection below and answer the comprehension check.','In 1892, the Sierra Club was formed. In 1908, an area of coastal redwood trees north of San Francisco was established as Muir Woods National Monument. In the Sierra Nevada Mountains, a walking trail from Yosemite Valley to Mount Whitney was dedicated in 1938. It is called the John Muir Trail.\n\nJohn Muir was born in 1838 in Scotland. His family name means “moor,” which is a meadow full of flowers and animals. John loved nature from the time he was small. He also liked to climb rocky cliffs and walls. When John was 11 years old, his family moved to the United States and settled in Wisconsin. John was good with tools and soon became an inventor. He first invented a model of a sawmill. Later, he invented an alarm clock that would cause the sleeping person to be tipped out of bed when the timer sounded.\n\nMuir left home at an early age. He took a 1,000-mile walk south to the Gulf of Mexico in 1867 and 1868. Then he sailed for San Francisco. The city was too noisy and crowded for Muir, so he headed inland for the Sierra Nevadas.\n\nWhen Muir discovered the Yosemite Valley in the Sierra Nevadas, it was as if he had come home. He loved the mountains, the wildlife, and the trees. He climbed the mountains and even climbed trees during thunderstorms in order to get closer to the wind. He put forth the theory in the late 1860s that the Yosemite Valley had been formed through the action of glaciers. People ridiculed him. Not until 1930 was Muir’s theory proven correct.\n\nMuir began to write articles about the Yosemite Valley to tell readers about its beauty. His writing also warned people that Yosemite was in danger from timber mining and sheep ranching interests. In 1901, Theodore Roosevelt became president of the United States. He was interested in conservation. Muir took the president through Yosemite, and Roosevelt helped get legislation passed to create Yosemite National Park in 1906.\n\nAlthough Muir won many conservation battles, he lost a major one. He fought to save the Hetch Hetchy Valley, which people wanted to dam in order to provide water for San Francisco. In late 1913, a bill was signed to dam the valley. Muir died in 1914. Some people say losing the fight to protect the valley killed Muir.\n\nCOMPREHENSION QUESTIONS\n\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n1. In what year was the Sierra Club formed?\n\n2. Where was John Muir born?\n\n3. Why did Muir leave San Francisco after arriving there?\n\n4. What did Muir\'s alarm clock invention do?\n\n5. What does the text suggest Muir felt upon discovering Yosemite Valley?\n\n6. Why was Muir\'s glacier theory initially ridiculed?\n\n7. How did Muir\'s writing contribute to conservation?\n\n8. What major conservation loss is linked to Muir\'s death?\n\n9. The text implies Muir\'s family name \"moor\" connected to his life how?\n\n10. What does the damming of Hetch Hetchy Valley shortly after John Muir\'s death suggest about the limits of his influence on American conservation',NULL,'[]','Read the selection below and answer the comprehension check.',NULL,10,43,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1650,854,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 1','open','Write a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below. The scrambled information is attached below.','Scrambled Facts and Information\n\n▪   An announcement was made by the School Principal Dr. Mary Jean M. Francisquete on Monday that a new program will be implemented by the school.\n\n▪   The Special Program in Journalism (SPJ) will be implemented by Badas National High School for School Year 2021-2022.\n\n▪   After the implementation, the school is expected to receive equipment and facilities related to the resources needed for SPJ.\n\n▪   “We are happy that SPJ will be implemented in Badas NHS because it will give our children the chance to be better writers and journalists in the future,” Linda Jesus, one of the parents said.\n\n▪   The aim of implementing the program is to offer journalism competencies to cater the journalistic skills of students in the school.\n\n▪   Francisquete said that the SPJ implementation became a reality after parents and other stakeholders wrote a letter to the school to open the said program.\n\n▪   “We want to give our students the chance to hone their writing skills and abilities in journalism and also make sure that we can influence them to love writing,” Francisquete said.\n\nTHE LEAD. (Who did what; or What happened? Perhaps When? and Where? might be in this paragraph.)\n\n2ND PARAGRAPH (In many cases this second paragraph will answer the questions Why? or How?)\n\n3RD PARAGRAPH (This is where a quote would often appear. This is viewed by most reporters as essential to keep the story lively.)\n\n4TH PARAGRAPH (Why are you telling me this/what are the implications of this? (significance))\n\n5TH PARAGRAPH (What will other people think about this? (Moving the story on, giving it a human context)\n\n6TH PARAGRAPH (How did the event continue? (Important background)\n\n7TH PARAGRAPH (What is going to happen next? (Again, moving the story on, significance)\n\nWrite a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below. The scrambled information is attached below.',NULL,'[]','Write a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below. The scrambled information is attached below.',NULL,10,6,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1651,855,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 2','open','Write a news article based on the following facts.','What: 1.   government troops on the hunt for Abu Sayyaf chieftain Khadaffy Janjalani and the alleged masterminds of the 2002 Bali bombings clashed with a group of bandits.\n\n2. three marines were injured after the incident Where: in the Southern island province of Sulu When :   on Friday This was according to military reports. Supporting details: Lieutenant Colonel Arnel Caculitan - Marines spokesman. Around 11:30 am, troops from the 7th Marine Battalion clashed with some 40 Abu Sayyaf members. The clash happened in Danag Village. This was in Patikul town. This was according to Caculitan. Caculitan further said that the Abu Sayyaf suffered casualties. He cannot give exact figure. He also said that He could not easily ascertain if Janjalani and two other Jemaah Islamiyah bomb experts Dulmatin and Omar Patik were with the group that engaged with the marines. Sulu province – has been sealed off in the hope of catching the Al Qaeda- linked militants. The Abu Sayyaf and the JI have been responsible for the worst terrorists attack in South east Asia. The Al-Qaeda militants have escaped military dragnet several times. The United States has put up ten million dollar bounty. This was for Dulmatin’s capture. One million dollar each for the capture of Janjalani and Patek.',NULL,'[]','Write a news article based on the following facts.',NULL,10,8,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1652,857,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 4','open','Write 3–4 sentences giving your opinion about whether the school cafeteria should offer more healthy snacks.','●   State your opinion clearly.\n●   Give one reason to support your opinion.\n●   End with a short, convincing sentence.\nExample:\nTopic: Is social media helping or harming students today?\nI think social media is harming students today. Many students spend too much time on apps and games, which can make them tired and distracted from their studies. For example, some students scroll through social media instead of doing homework. Students should use social media less so it does not affect their learning.\n\nWrite 3–4 sentences giving your opinion about whether the school cafeteria should offer more healthy snacks.',NULL,'[]','Write 3–4 sentences giving your opinion about whether the school cafeteria should offer more healthy snacks.',NULL,10,10,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1653,858,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 5','open','Write a short editorial (1 paragraph).','●   Start with a strong opinion (agree or disagree).\n●   Give two reasons to support your viewpoint.\n●   Provide one possible solution (e.g., limit, guidance).\n●   End with a closing statement that urges readers to agree with you.\nExample:\nSocial media is both helping and harming students, but I believe it is mostly harmful. It can help students communicate with friends and learn new things, but many spend too much time online, which affects their schoolwork and sleep. For example, students may stay up late watching videos instead of studying for tests. To reduce these problems, students should set limits on their social media use and focus on important tasks first. Schools and parents can also guide students to use social media responsibly. Using social media in moderation can help students enjoy its benefits without hurting their studies.\n\nWrite a short editorial (1 paragraph).',NULL,'[]','Write a short editorial (1 paragraph).',NULL,10,11,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1654,859,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 6','open','Write a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).','Paragraph 1:\n\n●   Present your opinion clearly.\n●   Explain one positive and one negative effect of social media.\nParagraph 2:\n●   State which effect you think is stronger and why.\n●   Provide evidence or examples (realistic, simple ones).\n●   End with a strong call to action (e.g., encouraging balanced or responsible use)\nExample:\nSocial media is a powerful tool that can both help and harm students today.\nOn the positive side, it allows students to connect with classmates, share ideas, and access educational content online. For example, students can join study groups or watch tutorials on difficult topics. However, social media can also distract students, cause stress, and reduce the time they spend on homework or outdoor activities.\nI believe the negative effects are stronger than the positive ones if social media is not used carefully. Students should be taught to use social media responsibly and set daily time limits. Parents and teachers can also encourage activities like reading, sports, or hobbies to balance screen time. By using social media wisely, students can enjoy its benefits without letting it harm their health or learning.\n\nWrite a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).',NULL,'[]','Write a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).',NULL,10,12,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1655,861,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 8','open','Imagine (or choose) a game your school team played. Write a short recap including:','1. Who played\n\n2. What sport\n\n3. The final score\n\n4. One highlight moment\n\n5. One sentence about how the team felt afterward This helps students build simple sports-story structure.',NULL,'[]','Imagine (or choose) a game your school team played. Write a short recap including:',NULL,10,14,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1656,862,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 9','open','Interview a friend or teammate about a game or practice. Ask questions like:','●   “What was the hardest part of the game?”\n●   “What made you proud?”\n●   “What do you want to improve?”\nThen write three short sentences, each starting with a quote from the person.\nExample:\n“I was nervous at first,” Maya said.\n\nInterview a friend or teammate about a game or practice. Ask questions like:',NULL,'[]','Interview a friend or teammate about a game or practice. Ask questions like:',NULL,10,15,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1657,863,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 10','open','Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.','doctor. Ma. Girlie Pinongan, provincial health officer, said there were 2,211 influenza-like\n\ncases reported from Jan. 1 to Dec. 2 this year in the 31 towns and cities of negros occidental compiared to 616 cases in 2022.\n\nhealth officials urged the public to take precautions during the holidayses as cases as cases of influenza-like illness in Negros Occidental increased\n\nby 258.93 percent this year.\n\nhe highest number of cases were recorded in Isabela town with 1,680 followed by San Carlos\n\nCity with 129; Murcia, 80; Bago City, 57; Talisay City, 50; Hinigaran, 20; Cadiz City, 19;\n\nand Don Salvador Benedicto, Silay City and Victorias City with 16 each.\n\nStill, she said, the public should avoid close contact with persons manifesting influenza-like Symptoms Pinongan said the cases are not severe and are usually triggered by changes in conditions weather/ She also urged people to wear maskes in closed and crowded spaces as a precaution.\n\nCopyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.',NULL,'[]','Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.',NULL,10,16,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1658,864,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 11','open','Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and flush left.','The Lopez family’s energy Developmet Corp. (edc) plans to build additional\n\nsolar power\n\nplants and battery energy storage systems within its geothermal project sites\n\nto further expand\n\nits renewable energy portfolio and increase power supply across the\n\ncountry/\n\nBarcena did not disclose farther details about this undertaking but noted\n\nthat it wood be\n\namong the company’s priority projects in the next 2 years.\n\n“We won’t go far bcoz we already have space within our geothermal project\n\nsites. We’re\n\nlooking at how we can maximize some small solar facilities inside our\n\ngeothermal [power\n\nplants], Allan Barcena, EDC head of corporate social responsibility and\n\npublic relations, told reporters last week.\n\nAt present, EDC has twelve megawatts (MW) of solar power capacity from\n\ntwo facilities in the country.\n\nApart from this, barcena said they were also looking at completing more\n\nbattery energy storage system (BESS) projects in 2024 and 2025.\n\nBESS facilities are used to store excess power from power plants during off-\n\npeak hours. These are tapped for poweeer when electricity demand increases\n\nto augment supply and prevent outages.\n\n“BESS is a concept that’s evolving … I think storage will continues to be an\n\nopportunity to capture additional energy capacity, whether in hydro, solar or\n\ngeothermal,” he said.\n\nEDC signed an agreement in October with First Balfour Inc., the Lopezes’\n\nconstruction and engineering unit, for the the development of three BESS\n\nprojects with a combined capacity of 40 MW.\n\nhese projects will be locate within EDC’s geothermal power plants in\n\nSorsogon, Leyte and negros Oriental provinces\n\nCopyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and flush left.',NULL,'[]','Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and flush left.',NULL,10,17,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1659,865,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 12','open','Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and inverted pyramid.','he provincial cuncil issued Resolution No. 1410-23 on wednesday, declaring a state of\n\ncalamity, following Hinatuan town’s similar declaration the previous day\n\nThe provincial gov’t emphasized declaring a state ofcalamity is a crucial step in aiding the community after the temblor.\n\nThe provincial government of surigao del Sur hasplaced the entire province under a state of\n\ncalamity after a powerful magnitude 7.4 earthquake struck off its coasts onsSaturday evening (December 2).\n\nsurigao del Sur’s Provincial Disaster reduction Risk and Office management said more than 30 percent of the province’s population was affected by the earthquake.\n\nData from the National Disaster Risk reduction and ManagementCouncil (ndrrmc) showed as\n\nof 8 p.m. on Tuesday, the quake reportedly killed 3 and injured 48 individuals\n\nNDRRMC has logged 3,336 aftershocks from the magnitude 7.4 tremor as of 4 p.m. the same day.\n\n“Be it resolved that by the Panlalawigan Sangguniang of Surigao del Sur to diclare the entire\n\nprovince of Surigao del Sur under State of Calamity due to the effects of the magnitude 7.4 offshore earthquake that struck the Province on december 2, 20 23\n\nand the subsequent recurring tremors and big aftershocks,” the resolution read\n\nCopyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and inverted pyramid.',NULL,'[]','Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and inverted pyramid.',NULL,10,19,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1660,866,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 13','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Headline and Lead Writing (Science & Technology Journalism)\nTheme: Technology I. Learning Task Write an effective headline and lead based on ONE (1) of the technology news briefs provided.\nII. Qualities of a Good Headline ✓ Clear and direct ✓ Uses action words ✓ Focused on the main idea ✓ Short (6–10 words)\n✓ Accurate and factual III. Qualities of a Good Lead ✓ 1–2 sentences only ✓ Answers Who, What, Where, When (optional: Why/How)\n✓ Clear, simple language ✓ Focuses on the most important information.\nIV. Topic STUDENT INVENTORS Three senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\nV. Your Output\nA. Write Your Headline (6–10 words only):\n\nB. Write Your Lead (1–2 sentences only):\n\nVI. Reflection (Short Answer)\nWhy is it important for journalists to write clear and accurate headlines and leads in science and technology news?\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,21,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1661,867,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 14','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Developing Context and Main Body Content Theme: Technology\n\nI. Background of the Task You are a student journalist assigned to write a technology news article.\nYour task is to develop the context and main body content of the story based on the information below:\nNews Scenario:\nThree senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\n\nII. Review: What Is Context in News Writing?\nContext provides background information that helps the reader understand:\n\n●   Why the news matters\n●   What problem it addresses\n●   The current situation related to the issue\n\nIII. Review: What Is the Main Body of a News Article?\nThe main body expands the story by adding:\n\n●   Important details\n●   Quotes (real or simulated)\n●   Effects or implications\n●   Supporting data or explanations\n\nIV. Activity Instructions Using the news scenario above, complete the tasks below:\n\nA. Write the Context Paragraph Explain the background of the dengue issue and why a detection app is important. (3–4 sentences)\nYour Answer:\n\nB. Write the Main Body Paragraph Add more details about the app, how it works, who created it, and its possible impact. You may include invented quotes to enrich the story. (4–6 sentences)\nYour Answer:\n\nV. Reflection Question How can technology created by students contribute to solving real community problems?\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,23,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1662,868,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 15','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Adding Supporting Information and Writing the Conclusion Theme: Technology News Scenario: Three senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\nI. Task Overview In this activity, you will strengthen your news article by:\n\n1. Adding supporting information\n\n2. Writing an effective conclusion to the story II. What is Supporting Information? Supporting information includes: - Additional facts or explanations - Expert or user opinions - Research data or statistics - Real or simulated quotes III. What is a News Conclusion? A conclusion: - Wraps up the story - Highlights the importance of the project - Looks at next steps or future impact IV. Activity Instructions',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,25,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1663,869,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 16','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Vocabulary\n5. Add one photo or hand-drawn illustration (optional). Example: Title: A Day in the Life of Mr. Santos, the School Guard Mr. Santos steps through the school gate before the sun has fully risen, his uniform crisp and his flashlight in hand. He smiles warmly as he greets the early students trickling in, locking the gate behind him. “I like being the first one here,” he says. “It gives me time to think and prepare for the day.” Throughout the morning, he patrols the corridors, making sure classrooms are secure and reminding students to put their things away. When someone forgets a book or backpack, he helps them carry it to their classroom. At recess, he watches students play, sometimes pausing to chat he asks about their day, what subjects they like, and even what games they play at home. In the afternoon, Mr. Santos helps guide students out of the school grounds safely. He stands by the gate, waving goodbye, and makes sure no one lags behind. After the last student leaves, he walks around to check every door and window. He says, “My job isn’t just to guard the school — I guard their memories too. I want them to leave knowing they\'re safe.” As he closes up, his face shows a quiet pride: even in the small moments, he knows he’s made a difference.\n\n1. Choose someone to feature: a classmate, teacher, family member, neighbor, or even a school staff member (e.g., librarian, guard, janitor).\n\n2. Interview the person using at least 5 questions (e.g., daily routine, hobbies, challenges, fun facts).\n\n3. Write 3-4 paragraphs feature article titled “A Day in the Life of .”\n\n4. Focus on capturing interesting details, personality, and real experiences.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,27,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1664,870,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 17','open','Answer each question.','1. Explore the school campus and identify a “hidden gem”—a place or feature students may overlook (e.g., a quiet reading corner, an art display, a garden spot).\n\n2. Describe what makes this place special or unique.\n\n3. Interview at least 1 person who knows something about the location (e.g., librarian, teacher, classmate).\n\n4. Write a short feature article (2–3 paragraphs) that includes descriptive details, sensory imagery, and interesting facts.\n\n5. Give the article a catchy title (e.g., “The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab”). Example: Title: The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab Paragraph 1 – Description: Tucked behind the science lab, away from the bustling hallways, lies a small garden that most students pass by without noticing. Sunlight filters through the branches of a tall jacaranda tree, casting dappled shadows on the neatly arranged flowerbeds. The air is fragrant with the scent of blooming marigolds and jasmine, and the gentle sound of rustling leaves creates a peaceful atmosphere. A worn wooden bench sits under the tree, inviting anyone to pause and enjoy the quiet. Paragraph 2 – Stories and Facts: Mrs. Alvarez, the school’s biology teacher, explains that the garden was planted by students five years ago as part of a sustainability project. “It started as a simple idea to grow flowers and herbs for the school, but it became a place where students could relax and observe nature,” she says. Over the years, students have added bird feeders, a small pond, and colorful stepping stones. Many report that spending time here helps them focus and feel calm between classes. Paragraph 3 – Reflection / Significance: Though it may seem small and unassuming, this garden is a hidden treasure in the school. It reminds everyone that even in a busy campus, there are spaces to pause, reflect, and connect with nature. For those who take the time to explore it, the garden offers more than beauty—it offers a moment of calm and inspiration in the middle of a hectic school day.',NULL,'[]','Answer each question.',NULL,10,28,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1665,871,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 18','open','Answer each question.','1. Bring an object from home or choose one from the classroom (e.g., a favorite book, an old photo, a trophy, a toy).\n\n2. Research or reflect on the object’s background: o   Where did it come from? o   Why is it meaningful? o   What memories or events are connected to it?\n\n3. Write a feature-style narrative (3 paragraphs) that tells the “story” of the object in an interesting way.\n\n4. Use sensory details and emotion to make the object come alive. Example: Title: The Old Trophy from the First Math Contest In the trophy case near the principal’s office, there is an old silver cup decorated with ribbons. The plaque reads: “First Inter-School Math Contest – 1985.” It’s a little dusty now, but when you hold it, you can feel its history. Mr. Reyes, one of the oldest teachers, remembers when the school won that first contest. “We practiced every day after school,” he recalls. “We were so poor at first, but we really wanted to prove we could compete.” That math contest was a big deal — students from different schools came, and our school didn’t expect to win. But they did, and when they lifted that trophy, everyone cheered. Now, the trophy stays in the case. Students look at it and talk about who came before them, and what it took. For some, it’s just a shiny cup. For others, it’s a symbol: with hard work, even ordinary students can do extraordinary things.',NULL,'[]','Answer each question.',NULL,10,29,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1666,872,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 19','open','Label each part.','1. Title / Headline – The name of the column, which should be catchy and indicate the topic.\n\n2. Introduction / Lead – The opening paragraph that introduces the topic and grabs the reader’s attention.\n\n3. Opinion / Viewpoint – The writer’s personal opinion or stance on the topic.\n\n4. Body / Supporting Details – Paragraphs that explain, describe, or give examples to support the opinion.\n\n5. Conclusion / Closing – The final paragraph that summarizes the main idea and reinforces the writer’s opinion. 1. Books, magazines, and even online articles can teach useful lessons and provide fun stories. Students who read regularly often perform better in school and feel more confident in class discussions. 2. The Importance of Reading 3. In conclusion, reading is more than just a school activity; it is a skill that helps students grow. Developing a daily reading habit will benefit students now and in the future. 4. Reading is an important habit for students because it helps them learn new things and expand their imagination. It can also make schoolwork easier and more enjoyable. 5. I believe all Grade 7 students should read every day because it improves vocabulary, thinking skills, and creativity. Reading also helps students understand the world better and connect with others.',NULL,'[]','Label each part.',NULL,10,30,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1667,873,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 20','open','Put the sentences in the correct order and rewrite them.','Students who participate in sports also build confidence through regular practice. They learn to cooperate with teammates and value hard work.\nSchool Sports: More Than Just Games Sports are an important part of school life because they help students stay active and healthy. They also make school more enjoyable through fun and energetic activities.\nIn conclusion, school sports help students grow physically, mentally, and socially. They build character and prepare young people for future challenges.\nI believe Grade 7 students should join school sports because they learn more than just playing the game. Sports teach teamwork, discipline, and responsibility.\n\nPut the sentences in the correct order and rewrite them.',NULL,'[]','Put the sentences in the correct order and rewrite them.',NULL,10,31,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1668,875,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 22','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','“River in Crisis: Plastic Waste Threatens Local Fish Population”\nSan Isidro, Laguna — Residents of Barangay Malinaw were shocked this week after local scientists released a report revealing dangerously high levels of plastic waste in the Maliit River, a waterway once known for its clear water and abundant fish.\nAccording to the team of researchers from Laguna State Environmental Laboratory, plastic pollution in the river has increased by 63% in the last five years. The most concerning part of the study shows that if the trend continues, the community may lose up to 60% of its fish population in the next five years.\n“We found plastic bags, sachets, straws, and even broken toys trapped in the riverbed,” said Dr. Elisa Ramos, lead scientist of the study. “Fish are mistaking microplastics for food. Many are getting sick or dying.”\nFisherfolk in the area have also noticed the alarming decline.\nMang Tonyo, a fisherman of 30 years, shared that his usual three kilos of fish per day has dropped to less than one kilo. “Minsan wala pa nga. Puno ng basura ang lambat namin,” he said, showing nets tangled with plastic waste.\nLocal officials admitted that the river cleanup programs have not been enough. Barangay Captain Lucia Hernandez urged residents to reduce single-use plastics and participate in the upcoming community clean-up next month.\nEnvironmental groups warn that without immediate action, the river may become biologically dead—unable to support fish or other aquatic life.\n“This river feeds families,” Dr. Ramos added. “If we lose it, we lose part of our community.”\n\nTask:\n\n●    Draw a cartoon showing how the river looks now, full of plastic waste.\n●    Include fish, humans, and trash in a visual story:\no   Fish struggling or sick from microplastics. o   Mang Tonyo trying to catch fish in trash-filled nets. o   Residents or children holding signs: “Save Maliit River!”\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,33,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1669,876,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 23','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','“Luna and the Sad River”\n\nIn the town of Malinaw, there flowed a river that everyone loved. It was called the Maliit River, and it sparkled under the sun like a long, silver ribbon. Children played by its shore, fishermen caught fresh fish every morning, and families enjoyed picnics on weekends.\nBut one day, Luna, a curious 12-year-old girl, noticed something different.\nWhen she sat by the river, she frowned.\n“Why is the water… darker?” she whispered.\nInstead of smooth stones and shiny fish, Luna saw plastic bags, sachets, straws, and even an old broken toy floating in the water.\nThe fish looked unhappy too. One small fish swam near her, bumping into bits of plastic.\n“Oh no,” Luna said. “Are you okay?”\nThe fish couldn’t speak, of course, but Luna imagined it saying, “We’re trying… but the trash is making it hard to breathe.”\nThat afternoon, Luna went to visit Mang Tonyo, the friendly fisherman who always told funny stories. She saw him pulling up his fishing net, but instead of fish, it was full of plastic.\n“Mang Tonyo, what happened?” Luna asked.\nHe sighed. “The river is sick, anak. There’s too much trash. I used to catch three kilos of fish every day. Now… sometimes I catch none.”\nLuna felt a tight squeeze in her chest. How could a river get sick?\nThe next day at school, Dr. Ramos, a scientist from the community, visited their class.\n“Children,” she said gently, “we studied the Maliit River, and we found that plastic waste has increased a lot. If nothing changes, we might lose more than half of the fish in just a few years.”\n\nThe whole class gasped.\n“But it’s not too late,” Dr. Ramos continued with a hopeful smile. “If we work together, we can help the river heal.”\nLuna’s eyes brightened. She had an idea.\nThat weekend, with help from her classmates, she organized a River Rescue Day. They brought gloves, sacks, and posters that said, “A Clean River is a Happy River!” Even parents and neighbors joined in.\nThey picked up trash, sorted recyclables, and planted new plants along the riverbank. It was hard work, but Luna felt proud.\nAt the end of the day, she sat by the river again. This time, she saw something wonderful—small fish swimming freely in a cleaner spot.\nLuna smiled.\n“Don’t worry, little river,” she whispered. “We’re here to help.”\nAnd from that day on, the people of Malinaw promised to take better care of the Maliit River—so it could sparkle once more and stay alive for future generations.\n\nTask:\n\n●    Draw a cartoon showing Luna discovering the polluted river.\n●    Include:\no   Dark, plastic-filled water o   Sad or struggling fish o   Luna’s worried expression\n\n●    Optional: A thought bubble for the fish: “We’re trying… but the trash is making it hard to breathe.”\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,34,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1670,877,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 24','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Article for Students Title: Schools Debate Cell Phone Use in Class Several schools in the region are debating whether to ban cell phones during class. Teachers say phones distract students and cause lower performance.\nStudents argue that phones help them research quickly and communicate during emergencies. Some schools are considering a “limited use” policy to balance both sides.\n\nTask:\n\n    Create a cartoon showing both advantages and disadvantages of cell phones in school.\n    Include at least two characters.\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,36,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1671,878,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 25','open','Write a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements:','● A warm greeting\n● Station name\n● A short welcome message Example:\n“Good morning! You’re listening to One Voice News Radio, bringing you the latest news and updates all day! Stay tuned for today’s top stories. We’ve got important reports and interviews lined up just for you!”\n\nWrite your greeting here:\n\nWrite a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements:',NULL,'[]','Write a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements:',NULL,10,37,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1672,879,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 26','open','Write a short weather report about the information provided below.','● Greeting\n● Today’s weather\n● Temperature\n● A simple forecast (e.g. “Sunny this afternoon”, “cloudy skies”)\n● Advice for listeners (e.g. “Don’t forget your jacket!”)\nExample:\n“Good day, everyone! Today in Bayabason, Maramag, Bukidnon, expect cloudy skies with temperatures around 22 degrees. Don’t forget to bring your umbrellas, be safe!\n\nWrite your report here:\n\nWrite a short weather report about the information provided below.',NULL,'[]','Write a short weather report about the information provided below.',NULL,10,38,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1673,880,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 27','open','Write a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions). Requirements:','● Greeting\n● What the announcement is about\n● Important details (date, time, place)\n● A short closing line Example:\n“Good afternoon, students! Don’t forget that the Art Club meets tomorrow at 3 p.m. in Room 12. Everyone is welcome to join. This is FIS News Radio reminding you to stay creative!”\n\nWrite your short school announcement script here:\n\nWrite a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions). Requirements:',NULL,'[]','Write a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions). Requirements:',NULL,10,39,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1674,881,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 1','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Write a news article based on the following facts which are given at random:\n-     The incident happened shortly after midnight yesterday.\n-     unidentified men sprayed the casualties with bullets.\n-     two men died in the said incident. Another was seriously wounded.\n-     The unidentified men were in a sport utility vehicle.\n-     A service crew of a fast-food chain named Sonny Cagaoan was declared dead on arrival at the Mary Johnston Hospital. He is 22 years old.\n-     Cagaoan died due to gunshot wounds in the head and body.\n-      Investigation showed that the victims were having a drinking spree in front of a house along Panday Pira Street. Here, the suspects in a dark blue Mitsubishi Pajero repeatedly shot at the group. Afterwards, they drove away.\n-     The car’s plate number is ERY-929.\n-    Danilo Fajardo- the companion of the two. He is 19 years old and is under observation at the Jose Reyes Memorial Medical Center for gunshot wounds in the arm and foot.\n-     Chief Inspector Alberto Peco – head of the Homicide Section of the Manila Police District. According to him, revenge could be the motive for the attack.\n-      Another who also succumbed to gunshot wounds in the head is Mar Jerkin Tan. Tan is 24 years old and a maintenance crew of the Ninoy Aquino Int’l Airport.\n-     The suspects were backed up by unidentified men on board another sports utility vehicle resembling an Isuzu Crosswind.\n-     Police are verifying the identities of the owners of the vehicles used in the shooting.\n-     Peco said his follow-up team failed to find Quan and the rest of his gang at their residences.\n-      Peco gathered from the witnesses that several days ago, Tan and his group figured in a fight with five residents of the nearby Dandan Street resulting in injuries. The injured here are the cousins Francis Quan and Edward Basilio.\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _______________________________________________________