(1738,947,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 13','open',' Read the short news story below.  Write a catchy headline summarizing the main point in a few words.  Write a lead paragraph (1-2 sentences) answering: What happened? Why is it important?  Environmental News Story: A recent study found that urban trees significantly improve air quality by reducing harmful pollutants like nitrogen dioxide and particulate matter. The research highlights the importance of green spaces in cities for public health and climate resilience. Tasks: Task 1: Write a catchy headline for the story here. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________.','Task 2: Write a lead paragraph for the article here.\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________.\n\n Read the short news story below.  Write a catchy headline summarizing the main point in a few words.  Write a lead paragraph (1-2 sentences) answering: What happened? Why is it important?  Environmental News Story: A recent study found that urban trees significantly improve air quality by reducing harmful pollutants like nitrogen dioxide and particulate matter. The research highlights the importance of green spaces in cities for public health and climate resilience. Tasks: Task 1: Write a catchy headline for the story here. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________.',NULL,'[]',' Read the short news story below.  Write a catchy headline summarizing the main point in a few words.  Write a lead paragraph (1-2 sentences) answering: What happened? Why is it important?  Environmental News Story: A recent study found that urban trees significantly improve air quality by reducing harmful pollutants like nitrogen dioxide and particulate matter. The research highlights the importance of green spaces in cities for public health and climate resilience. Tasks: Task 1: Write a catchy headline for the story here. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________.',NULL,10,24,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1739,948,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 14','open',' Read the following medical breakthrough news summary: \"Scientists have developed a new gene therapy that shows promise in treating a rare genetic disorder called spinal muscular atrophy (SMA). The therapy uses a modified virus to deliver healthy copies of the defective gene, resulting in improved muscle function in early clinical trials.\" Tasks: Task 1: Answer the following questions:','1. What is the medical breakthrough or discovery? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n2. What disease or condition does it target? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n3. How does the therapy work in simple terms? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n4. Why is this breakthrough important? What impact could it have? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n5. Identify any challenges or limitations mentioned. ___________________________________________________________________ Task 2: Write a short science news article (250-350 words) based on the information above. ___________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________.',NULL,'[]',' Read the following medical breakthrough news summary: \"Scientists have developed a new gene therapy that shows promise in treating a rare genetic disorder called spinal muscular atrophy (SMA). The therapy uses a modified virus to deliver healthy copies of the defective gene, resulting in improved muscle function in early clinical trials.\" Tasks: Task 1: Answer the following questions:',NULL,10,25,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1740,949,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 15','open','PART I – HEADLINE ANALYSIS  Study the sample space-related headlines: A. “New Ion Thruster Cuts Travel Time to Mars by 40%” B. “James Webb Detects Possible Water Vapor on Distant Exoplanet” C. “Philippine CubeSat Maps Atmospheric Changes from Low Earth Orbit”','Composing an opinion paragraph using evidence and reasoning.\n\nAnswer the following:\n\n1. Which headline shows the greatest technological advancement? Explain. __________________________________________________________________\n\n2. Which headline suggests a discovery about conditions beyond Earth? Why? __________________________________________________________________\n\n3. Which headline has the most impact for the Philippines? Support your answer. __________________________________________________________________ PART II – ADVANCED LEAD WRITING (5W1H) Choose ONE headline from Part I.  Write a 4–5 sentence lead paragraph. Write your lead here: ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _____________________________. PART III – SCIENCE ACCURACY CHECK  Identify whether the statement is SCIENTIFICALLY ACCURATE or MISLEADING. Provide a short explanation. “Astronauts float in the International Space Station because there is no gravity in space.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _________________________________________________ “Ion propulsion pushes spacecraft forward by shooting out charged particles at high speed.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _________________________________________________ “The dark side of the Moon is always dark because sunlight never reaches it.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _________________________________________________ PART IV – SPACE DATA INTERPRETATION NASA scientists tested three propulsion systems for a future Mars mission. Here are the results: Propulsion Type   Speed (km/s)        Fuel Efficiency (%)Travel Time to Mars (month) Chemical Rocket         5.4                 30%               7.0 Ion Thruster                   12.1               78%               4.2 Nuclear Engine          18.7                65%               2.9 Answer the following:\n\n1. Which propulsion type is the fastest? ________________________________________________________________\n\n2. Which propulsion type uses fuel most efficiently? _________________________________________________________________\n\n3. Which propulsion system would be most newsworthy for a Sci-Tech article? Explain why. __________________________________________________________________\n\n3. If you were writing the lead paragraph, what is the single most important fact to include from the table? Why? PART V – OPINION COLUMN  Write a one-paragraph Sci-Tech opinion column (8–10 sentences) answering: “Should countries invest more in space exploration or focus on Earth-based problems? Support your argument using scientific facts and real or realistic space technologies.” Write your paragraph here: ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _____________________________.',NULL,'[]','PART I – HEADLINE ANALYSIS  Study the sample space-related headlines: A. “New Ion Thruster Cuts Travel Time to Mars by 40%” B. “James Webb Detects Possible Water Vapor on Distant Exoplanet” C. “Philippine CubeSat Maps Atmospheric Changes from Low Earth Orbit”',NULL,10,26,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1741,950,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 16','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Human Interest Story “A Day in the Life”\nStep-by-Step Instructions:\n\n1. Choose a subject: Select a person in your school or community whose daily life or experiences are interesting. Examples: a teacher, a school staff member, a local artist, or a peer with a unique hobby.\n\n2. Plan your approach: Think of 5–7 questions to ask during an interview, focusing on their daily routine, challenges, achievements, and personal interests.\n\n3. Observe: Spend time observing your subject in their environment. Take detailed notes about their actions, expressions, and interactions.\n\n4. Conduct an interview: Use your prepared questions. Record or write down their responses verbatim to include direct quotes in your article.\n\n5. Write your feature article (400–500 words): o    Lead: Begin with an engaging anecdote or scene from their day. o    Body: Share their story, including quotes, descriptive details, and personal insights. o    Conclusion: Reflect on what makes this person’s life inspiring, interesting, or unique. EXAMPLE: Mr. Daniel, the school janitor Lead: Every morning at 5:30 a.m., while most of the town is still asleep, Mr. Daniel is already sweeping the empty corridors of Riverside High School. The faint smell of disinfectant fills the air as he hums a soft tune, preparing the school for another day of learning. Body: Mr. Daniel has worked at Riverside High for over 15 years. “I enjoy seeing the students smile when the school is clean,” he says with a warm grin. His day begins with sweeping and mopping floors, followed by restocking classrooms and checking that all facilities are in order. Despite his busy schedule, he always finds time to greet students by name. “It’s the small things that make my day worthwhile,” he explains. Outside work, Mr. Daniel volunteers at the local community center, helping children with homework and organizing weekend sports activities. Conclusion: Mr. Daniel’s dedication reminds everyone that every role in the school community matters. Through his hard work and kindness, he quietly makes Riverside High a brighter place for students and staff alike.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,29,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1742,951,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 17','open','Answer each question.','1. Select a trend: Identify a current trend among teenagers (e.g., social media challenge, fashion style, hobby, or app).\n\n2. Research: Use the internet, social media, and news articles to gather information about the trend. Note its origin, popularity, and impact.\n\n3. Interview peers: Ask classmates or friends why they follow this trend and what they like about it. Record at least 2–3 quotes.\n\n4. Plan your article: Create an outline: o    Introduction: Hook the reader with a fun fact, question, or statistic about the trend. o    Body: Explain what the trend is, why it is popular, and who participates. Include quotes and interesting details. o    Impact: Discuss how it affects teens or society (positively or negatively). o    Conclusion: Summarize the trend’s importance or speculate about its future.\n\n5. Write your feature article (400–500 words): Use clear, engaging language and maintain a friendly, lively tone.\n\n6. Edit and polish: Check for grammar, smooth flow, and correct facts. Make sure quotes and data are accurate. EXAMPLE: DIY Resin Art Lead: Over the past year, a colorful wave of creativity has taken over social media: DIY resin art. From glittery keychains to miniature landscapes, teenagers are turning their free time into artistic masterpieces. Body: Resin art involves mixing resin with pigments, glitter, and molds to create small decorative items. Teens like 16-year-old Maya explain why they love it: “It’s relaxing, and I love seeing the final product shine.” The trend has gained attention on Instagram and TikTok, with thousands of posts under #ResinArt. Schools and online communities now host workshops where beginners can learn to craft their own resin pieces. Experts note that resin art encourages creativity and patience, giving teenagers a productive way to spend their time. Impact: While resin art is mostly positive, some teens spend large amounts on supplies, which can be costly. However, many view it as a hobby that boosts confidence and skills. Conclusion: From glitter-filled coasters to custom jewelry, DIY resin art shows how a simple craft can inspire a generation of young creators. As more teens experiment with colors and shapes, the trend is likely to grow even further.',NULL,'[]','Answer each question.',NULL,10,31,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1743,952,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 18','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Place Feature – “Hidden Gems in Our Community”\nStep-by-Step Instructions:\n\n1. Choose a location: Find a lesser-known or interesting place in your community or school (park, café, library corner, historical site).\n\n2. Visit and observe: Spend 15–30 minutes noting details about the surroundings, atmosphere, people, sounds, smells, and activities.\n\n3. Talk to people: If possible, ask visitors or staff about their experiences at the place. Record at least 1–2 quotes.\n\n4. Plan your article: Organize your observations: o    Lead: Open with a vivid description or scene-setting that immediately immerses the reader. o    Body: Describe the place in detail, include quotes, history (if relevant), and why it is unique. o    Conclusion: Suggest why readers should visit or appreciate this hidden gem.\n\n5. Write your feature article (400–500 words): Use descriptive language, imagery, and personification. Make the reader feel as if they are visiting the place themselves.\n\n6. Revise and proofread: Ensure your article flow well, has correct grammar, and creates a; strong impression of the location. Example: Maple Grove Park Lead: Tucked behind the main road in downtown Oakville, Maple Grove Park is a serene escape from the city’s hustle. The soft rustling of leaves and the scent of blooming flowers greet visitors as they step onto winding cobblestone paths. Body: The Park is dotted with wooden benches, perfect for reading or quiet reflection. Children play near the small fountain, while joggers circle the walking trails. “I come here every morning; it’s peaceful and refreshing,” says local resident Mr. Cruz. Historically, Maple Grove Park was established in 1920 and still retains its century-old oak trees. Birdsong fills the air, and butterflies often flutter past the flowerbeds, adding to the park’s charm. Conclusion: Whether you’re seeking a quiet spot to read, a place to exercise, or a peaceful escape from the busy city, Maple Grove Park is a hidden gem worth exploring. It proves that sometimes, the most beautiful places are the ones we least expect.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,33,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1744,953,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 19','open','Read the sample column below. Then identify and explain at least five persuasive techniques used (e.g., emotional appeal, rhetorical questions, statistics, anecdote, tone, call to action). Sample Column: \"Every year, students complain that school events feel repetitive—and they’re right. While traditions are important, repeating the same activities without improvement only drains student interest. Last year’s fair saw fewer participants, lower sales, and even feedback from parents saying it felt ‘exactly the same as 2019.’ If schools want to build excitement and creativity, they must start listening to student suggestions. Isn’t it time for a fresh, student-led approach that reflects what today’s learners actually enjoy?\" Tasks:','Identify persuasive techniques used in a professional-style column.\n\n1. Identify 5 persuasive techniques.\n\n2. Explain how each technique strengthens the argument.',NULL,'[]','Read the sample column below. Then identify and explain at least five persuasive techniques used (e.g., emotional appeal, rhetorical questions, statistics, anecdote, tone, call to action). Sample Column: \"Every year, students complain that school events feel repetitive—and they’re right. While traditions are important, repeating the same activities without improvement only drains student interest. Last year’s fair saw fewer participants, lower sales, and even feedback from parents saying it felt ‘exactly the same as 2019.’ If schools want to build excitement and creativity, they must start listening to student suggestions. Isn’t it time for a fresh, student-led approach that reflects what today’s learners actually enjoy?\" Tasks:',NULL,10,35,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1745,956,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 22','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','“Twist the Satire”\n\nTask:\n     Change the symbols, characters, or exaggerations      Keep the issue but show a different or deeper angle\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,38,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1746,957,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 23','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Select a relevant social or political issue and create a high-satire cartoon showing:\n   Irony or sarcasm    Symbolic metaphors    Exaggeration    Social commentary Possible Themes:\n   Fake news    Political responsibility    Environmental destruction    Education inequality    Data privacy    Consumerism\n\n✔ Producing layered, analytical editorial cartoons\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,39,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1747,958,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 24','open','Read the passage, then answer the questions.','Draw a cartoon following the instructions below:\n“Silent but Deadly” – Zero Text Cartoon No words allowed:\n   No caption    No labels    No speech bubbles\n\nOnly symbols, pose, and imagery may be used to express the message.\n\nWrite your answer.',NULL,'[]','Read the passage, then answer the questions.',NULL,10,40,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1748,959,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 25','open','Write a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements:  A warm greeting  Station name  A short welcome message Example: “Good morning! You’re listening to One Voice News Radio, bringing you the latest news and updates all day! Stay tuned for today’s top stories. We’ve got important reports and interviews lined up just for you!”','Write your greeting here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________\n\nWrite a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements:  A warm greeting  Station name  A short welcome message Example: “Good morning! You’re listening to One Voice News Radio, bringing you the latest news and updates all day! Stay tuned for today’s top stories. We’ve got important reports and interviews lined up just for you!”',NULL,'[]','Write a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements:  A warm greeting  Station name  A short welcome message Example: “Good morning! You’re listening to One Voice News Radio, bringing you the latest news and updates all day! Stay tuned for today’s top stories. We’ve got important reports and interviews lined up just for you!”',NULL,10,41,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1749,960,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 26','open','Write a short weather report about the information provided below.  Greeting  Today’s weather  Temperature  A simple forecast (e.g. “Sunny this afternoon”, “cloudy skies”)  Advice for listeners (e.g. “Don’t forget your jacket!”) Example: “Good day, everyone! Today in Bayabason, Maramag, Bukidnon, expect cloudy skies with temperatures around 22 degrees. Don’t forget to bring your umbrellas, be safe!','Write your report here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________\n\nWrite a short weather report about the information provided below.  Greeting  Today’s weather  Temperature  A simple forecast (e.g. “Sunny this afternoon”, “cloudy skies”)  Advice for listeners (e.g. “Don’t forget your jacket!”) Example: “Good day, everyone! Today in Bayabason, Maramag, Bukidnon, expect cloudy skies with temperatures around 22 degrees. Don’t forget to bring your umbrellas, be safe!',NULL,'[]','Write a short weather report about the information provided below.  Greeting  Today’s weather  Temperature  A simple forecast (e.g. “Sunny this afternoon”, “cloudy skies”)  Advice for listeners (e.g. “Don’t forget your jacket!”) Example: “Good day, everyone! Today in Bayabason, Maramag, Bukidnon, expect cloudy skies with temperatures around 22 degrees. Don’t forget to bring your umbrellas, be safe!',NULL,10,42,'',1,1,'answer'),
(1750,961,NULL,'learn',1,'Activity 27','open','Write a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions). Requirements:  Greeting  What the announcement is about  Important details (date, time, place)  A short closing line Example: “Good afternoon, students! Don’t forget that the Art Club meets tomorrow at 3 p.m. in Room 12. Everyone is welcome to join. This is FIS News Radio reminding you to stay creative!”','Write your short school announcement script here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________\n\nWrite a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions). Requirements:  Greeting  What the announcement is about  Important details (date, time, place)  A short closing line Example: “Good afternoon, students! Don’t forget that the Art Club meets tomorrow at 3 p.m. in Room 12. Everyone is welcome to join. This is FIS News Radio reminding you to stay creative!”',NULL,'[]','Write a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions). Requirements:  Greeting  What the announcement is about  Important details (date, time, place)  A short closing line Example: “Good afternoon, students! Don’t forget that the Art Club meets tomorrow at 3 p.m. in Room 12. Everyone is welcome to join. This is FIS News Radio reminding you to stay creative!”',NULL,10,43,'',1,1,'answer');
