rs.\n\n\n\n3. Guided Practice (15 minutes)\n\n- Activity 1: “Syllable Sort Relay”\n\n - In small groups, pupils pick a picture card, say the word, clap/tap syllables, then\nplace it on the correct Syllable Sorting Mat (1, 2, 3, or 4).\n\n- Activity 2: “Syllable Count Orchestra”\n\n - Pupils use simple instruments (or clap/tap) to “play” the syllable beats of given\nwords (e.g., “butterfly” = tap-tap-tap).\n\n4. Independent Practice (10 minutes)\n\n- Distribute Worksheet 1: How Many Syllables?.\n\n- Pupils look at pictures, say the word, clap syllables, and circle the correct number.\n\n\n\nIV. Evaluation (10 minutes)\n\n- Distribute Worksheet 2: Syllable Cut and Sort.\n\n- Pupils cut out pictures, sort by syllable count, glue in correct columns, and write the\nword broken into syllables.\n\nPDF page 139\nWorksheet 1: How Many Syllables?\nName: __________________________________Level: ____________\n\n\nInstructions: Say each word. Clap the syllables. Circle the correct\nnumber.\n1. 🐱 (cat)\n ○1 ○2 ○3\n2. 🐰 (rabbit)\n ○1 ○2 ○3\n3. 🐘 (elephant)\n ○1 ○2 ○3\n4. � (butterfly)\n ○2 ○3 ○4\n5. 🍉 (watermelon)\n ○3 ○4 ○5\n6. ✏️ (pencil)\n ○1 ○2 ○3\n\nPDF page 140\nWorksheet 2: Syllable Cut and Sort\nName: ____________________________________ Level: ______________\nInstructions: Cut out the pictures. Say each word. Clap the syllables.\nGlue in the correct column. Then write the word with dots or dashes\nbetween syllables.\n\n\n1 Syllable               2 Syllables           3 Syllables\n__________              ____________          ____________\n__________              ____________          ____________\n__________              ____________          ____________\n\n\n\n\nPictures to cut:\n🐱 (cat)\n\n🐶 (dog)\n\n🐰 (rabbit)\n\n🍎 (apple)\n\n🐘 (elephant)\n\n🍌 (banana)\n� (butterfly)\n🚗 (car)\n\n👑 (tiara)\n\n\nExample: rabbit = rab•bit\n\nPDF page 141\nV. Agreement / Assignment (5 minutes)\n\n- Homework: “Go on a Syllable Scavenger Hunt at home! Find one object with 1, 2,\nand 3 syllables. List them and clap the syllables for your family.”\n\n\n\n**VI. Teacher’s Notes**\n\n- Key Concept: Emphasize that each syllable must contain a vowel sound.\n\n- Support struggling learners with one-on-one repetition, exaggerated mouth\nmovements, and physical prompts (hand under chin).\n\n- Challenge advanced pupils with longer words (e.g., “alligator,” “helicopter”) or\ncompound words.\n\n- Use consistent language: “Let’s break the word into word parts.”\n\n- Ensure inclusive participation by allowing responses in chorus or individually.\n\nPDF page 142\nPhonics 14: Segmenting Words into Syllables\n\nI.Lesson Objectives:\n\nBy the end of this 60-minute lesson, students will be able to:\n\n1. Count and clap the number of syllables in spoken words and picture-named\nitems.\n\n2. Sort picture cards into categories based on their number of syllables (1-4).\n\n3. Segment and represent each syllable in 2-4 syllable words using manipulatives.\n\nII. Focus Skill: Phonological Awareness - Segmenting spoken words into syllables\n\nMaterials: Whiteboard/marker, picture cards, syllable sorting mats (1-4 syllables),\nclapping hands cutouts, \"Syllable Detective\" badges, interactive slide deck, bingo\nchips/counters, syllable counting worksheets, self-assessment exit tickets, drum or\ntambourine (optional).\n\nIII. Lesson Procedure:\n\nPart 1: Warm-Up & Introduction (10 minutes)\n\nActivity: Name Cheer (5 min)\n\n  * Teacher models: \"My name is Mr./Ms. [Two-syllable name]. Let\'s clap it: [Teach-\ner]. 2 claps!\"\n\n  * Ask for volunteers: \"Who has a name we can clap?\" Choose students with 1, 2,\nand 3 syllable names.\n\n  * Introduce vocabulary: \"Each clap is called a **syllable**! It\'s a word part with\none vowel sound.\"\n\n   * Show visual anchor chart with the word **Syllables** broken into claps: **Syl** 👏\n**la** 👏 **bles** 👏.\n\n\n\n* **Introduce the \"Syllable Detective\" Mission (5 min)**\n\n  * Give each student a paper \"Syllable Detective\" badge to wear.\n\n   * Explain mission: \"Today, we are detectives finding how many syllables are hiding\nin our words!\"\n\n  * Teach hand motions: Hand under chin to feel jaw drops, clapping, or using\n\"chin bumps.\"\n\nPart 2: Guided Practice & Varied Activities (35 minutes)\n\n* **Activity 1: \"Clap It Out\" Whole Group Practice (10 min)**\n\n  * Show large picture cards one at a time. Model thinking: \"**Sunflower**. Let\'s\nclap: **sun** 👏 **flow** 👏 **er** 👏. 3 syllables!\"\n\nPDF page 143\n* Use varied methods: **Clapping, Stomping, Drum Taps, \"Robot Talk\"** (say word\nbroken into syllables).\n\n  * **Picture Examples:**\n\n    * 1 syllable: 👟 (shoe), 🐝 (bee), 🌞 (sun)\n\n    * 2 syllables: 🍎 (ap-ple), 📚 (book), � (but-ter-fly)\n\n    * 3 syllables: � (gor-il-la), � (broc-co-li), � (fla-min-go)\n\n\n\n* **Activity 2: \"Syllable Sort\" Center Activity (15 min)**\n\n  * Set up 4 tables or mats labeled with numbers 1-4 and corresponding clapping\nhands visuals.\n\n  * Divide students into small groups. Give each group a set of 8-12 picture cards.\n\n  * Task: Work together to clap each word, then place the card on the correct\nsorting mat.\n\n   * Teacher circulates, asking: \"How did you figure out that \'elephant\' has 3\nsyllables?\"\n\n  * **Picture Card Examples for Sorting:**\n\n    * 1-Syllable: 🚗 (car), � (pie), 🌙 (moon)\n\n    * 2-Syllable: 🍪 (cookie), � (fox), 🖍️ (crayon)\n\n    * 3-Syllable: 🍍 (pine-ap-ple), � (liz-ard), � (av-o-ca-do)\n\n    * 4-Syllable: � (di-no-saur), 🍓 (straw-ber-ry), 🐊 (al-li-ga-tor)\n\n\n\n* **Activity 3: \"Syllable Smash\" with Play Dough or Counters (10 min)**\n\n  * Each student gets a small ball of play dough or 4 bingo chips.\n\n   * Teacher says a word (or shows a picture). Students repeat the word, segment it\ninto syllables, and smash one ball/flatten one chip for each syllable.\n\n  * Example: \"**Butterfly**.\" Students say \"but-ter-fly\" while smashing 3 dough balls.\n\"How many parts? Three!\"\n\n  * **Word List for Activity:** 📞 (tel-e-phone), 🚒 (fire truck), 👑 (ti-a-ra), 🚜 (trac-tor),\n� (par-rot).\n\nPart 3: Assessment & Wrap-Up (15 minutes)\n\n\n\n* **Independent Assessment Worksheet (8 min):**\n\n  * Students work individually on a two-part worksheet.\n\nPDF page 144\nName: __________________________ Level: _____________________\n\n\n  Part A: Syllable Count (Formative)\n   Look at each picture. Clap the word. Color the number of claps\n(syllables).\n  1. 🍭 (lollipop) - Color: ☐1 ☐2 ☑3\n\n  2. 🐱 (cat) - Color: ☑1 ☐2 ☐3\n\n  3. � (li-on) - Color: ☐1 ☑2 ☐3\n\n  4. 🚁 (hel-i-cop-ter) - Color: ☐2 ☐3 ☑4\n\n\n  Part B: Syllable Sort (Summative)\n  Cut and paste the pictures into the correct column.\n\n\n 1 Syllable          2 Syllables            3 Syllables\n_____________        _____________         __________\n_____________        ______________        __________\n\n\n\n\n  Pictures to cut:\n\n\n🍰 (cake - 1),\n    (di-no-saur - 3),\n\n    🌷 (tulip - 2),\n✏️ (pen-cil - 2).\n\nPDF page 145\nV. Agreement: Simple half-slip with two pictures and blank lines.\n\n      1. 🐘 (elephant) _____ syllables\n\n      2. 🍌 (banana) _____ syllables\n\n  * Students write the number, hand it to teacher on their way to the next activity.\nThis provides quick, individual data.\n\n\n\n---\n\nPDF page 146\n','[40, 137, 138, 139, 140, 141, 142, 143, 144, 145, 146]','assets/images/level2b/page-040.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:44'),
(46,3,12,'Activity 11 · Blend and complete words','Blend and complete words','Activity 11 · Blending Syllables into Words. By the end of this lesson, pupils will be able to: 1. Orally blend separately spoken syllables to identify the whole word. 2. Connect blended words to their corresponding pictures through matching activities. 3. Create and illustrate a new word by blending given syllables.','PDF page 41\n26\n\n\n\n                       Blending Syllables into Words\n\n\n\n  Name                                                Grade\n\nDirections: Draw a line along the letters that blend to form a word.\nWrite your answer in the box.\n     Example:                 c i t\n                              h a m        _c _a _t_\n\n                             h       a        n\n     1.                      p       e        p           ___      ___       ___\n                             b          o m\n     2.                      c          I w              ___      ___       ___\n                             g          i y\n     3.                      b          o l              ___      ___       ___\n                             m           i     t\n     4.                      b          a     n          ___      ___       ___\n                             k          i    g\n     5.                      b          a    l           ___      ___       ___\n                             c          o         y\n     6.                      m          a         p      ___      ___       ___\n                             l      a         t\n                             b      o         n          ___      ___       ___\n     7.\n                             i      i        c\n     8.                      p          e     l          ___      ___       ___\n                             g       i       y\n     9.                      r      a        t           ___      ___       ___\n\n\n                         Department of Education ● Republic of the Philippines\n                                                                                   32\n\nPDF page 42\n27\n\n\n                                  m        i       d\n       10.                        b        u       l          ___      ___       ___\n\n                      Blending Syllables into Words\n\nName                                                 Grade\n\nAct. #1\n\nDirections: Choose the correct blend to complete the word. Write it\nin the blank.\n\n\n\n\n  1.                  ______ab            6.                        _____uit\n\n\n\n  2.                  ______og            7.                        _____ush\n\n\n\n  3.                  ______ink           8.                        _____um\n\n\n\n  4.                  ______ies           9.                        _____ee\n\n\n\n                                          10.                   _____oom\n  5.                  ______oss\n\n                              Department of Education ● Republic of the Philippines\n                                                                                       33\n\nPDF page 147\nLesson 15: Phonological Awareness – Blending Syllables into Compound Words\n\n\n\nI. Objectives\n\nAt the end of the lesson, pupils should be able to:\n\n1. Orally blend two separate word parts to form a complete compound word (e.g.,\nrain + bow = rainbow).\n\n2. Identify and match picture cards with their corresponding two-word parts to create\ncompound words.\n\n3. Write and illustrate at least three compound words formed by blending two words.\n\nII. Subject Matter\n\nSubject: English (Phonological Awareness & Vocabulary)\n\nDuration: 60 minutes\n\nTopic: Blending Syllables into Compound Words (Two Words into One)\n\nSkills: Compound Word Recognition, Oral Blending, Vocabulary Building, Early\nDecoding\n\nReference: K to 12 Curriculum Guide (English Grade 1)\n\nMaterials: Picture cards of compound words and their parts (rainbow: rain + bow;\ncupcake: cup + cake; butterfly: butter + fly; toothbrush: tooth + brush; sunflower: sun\n+ flower), word part cards, pocket chart, whiteboard/markers, “Compound Word\nPuzzles” (two-piece puzzles), worksheets, scissors, glue, crayons, “Blending Binoculars”\n(cardboard tubes to “see” the two parts come together), interactive digital slide\n(optional)\n\nStrategies Used\n\n- Visual and tactile learning: puzzles, picture cards, cut-and-paste activities.\n\n- Explicit modeling of blending two meaningful word parts into one.\n\n- Play-based exploration with props like binoculars and puzzles.\n\n- Differentiated support through visual aids and peer collaboration.\n\n- Formative assessment via observation of oral blending and completed worksheets.\n\n\n\nIII. Procedure\n\nA. Preliminary Activities (5 minutes)\n\n- Phonemic Warm-up: “Word Part Clap” – Teacher says two separate words (e.g.,\n“foot” – clap, “ball” – clap), pupils blend and clap once for the compound word\n(“football”).\n\nPDF page 148\n- Review: Quick recall of blending syllables into multisyllabic words.\n\n- Motivation: Show a split picture of a “sun” and a “flower” separately, then combine\nto make “sunflower.” Ask: “What new word do we make when we put these\ntogether?”\n\nB. Lesson Proper (40 minutes)\n\n1. Introduction (5 minutes)\n\n- Define compound words: “Two small words put together to make one big word.”\n\n- Demonstrate with physical objects or props: a toy **box** + a **car** = **boxcar**\n(or similar).\n\n- Use hand motions: hold up two fingers for the two words, then bring hands together.\n\n2. Modelling (10 minutes)\n\n- Use picture cards and word part cards in a pocket chart.\n\n- Model blending for familiar compound words:\n\n - rain   + bow    → rainbow\n\n - cup    + cake   → cupcake\n\n - butter + fly    → butterfly\n\n - tooth + brush   → toothbrush\n\n - sun    + flower → sunflower\n\n- Emphasize that both parts are real words on their own.\n\n\n\n3. Guided Practice (15 minutes)\n\n- Activity 1: “Compound Word Puzzles”\n\n - In pairs, pupils solve two-piece puzzles. Each piece has one word part and half of a\npicture. They join them, say both parts, then blend into the compound word.\n\n- Activity 2: “Blending Binoculars”\n\n - Pupils use cardboard “binoculars” to “spot” two separate picture cards (e.g., “pan”\n+ “cake”), then lower binoculars to “see” them blend into one word (“pancake”).\n\n\n\n4. Independent Practice (10 minutes)\n\n- Distribute **Worksheet 1: Make a Compound Word**.\n\n- Pupils draw lines to match two word parts to make a compound word, then match\nit to a picture.\n\nPDF page 149\nIV. Evaluation (10 minutes)\n\n- Distribute Worksheet 2: Compound Word Creation.\n\n- Pupils cut out word parts, blend them to form compound words, glue them, and write\nthe new word.\n\nPDF page 150\nWorksheet 1: Blend the syllables\nName:______________________________Level: ____________________\n\n\nInstructions: Write the word when you blend the two words.\n\n\nPicture           syllable 1       syllable 2          Word\n                    rain             bow             ________\n                    cup              cake            ________\n                    tooth            brush           ________\n                    but               ter            ________\n                    flo              wer             ________\n\nPDF page 151\nV. Agreement / Assignment (5 minutes)\n\n- Homework: “Be a Compound Word Detective at home! Find two objects that can\nbe combined to make a compound word (e.g., hair + brush = hairbrush). Draw them\nand write the new word.”\n\n\n\n** Teacher’s Notes**\n\n- **Key Concept:** Emphasize that each part of a compound word is a standalone\nword (e.g., “rain” is a word, “bow” is a word).\n\n- Support struggling learners with physical manipulation of puzzle pieces and\nexaggerated oral blending.\n\n- Challenge advanced pupils to think of their own compound words or identify them\nin classroom labels.\n\n- Reinforce throughout the week with a “Compound Word of the Day” and classroom\nword hunts.\n\n- Ensure pupils understand the meaning of the new compound word, not just the\nblending skill.\n\nPDF page 152\nPhonics 16: Blending Syllables into Words\n\n\n\nI.Lesson Objectives:\n\nBy the end of this 60-minute lesson, students will be able to:\n\n1. Orally blend separately spoken syllables to identify the whole word.\n\n2. Connect blended words to their corresponding pictures through matching\nactivities.\n\n3. Create and illustrate a new word by blending given syllables.\n\nII. Subject Matter:\n\nFocus Skill: Phonological Awareness - Blending spoken syllables to form complete\nwords\n\nMaterials: Whiteboard/marker, picture cards, \"Syllable Mystery Box,\" syllable blending\npuzzles, interactive slide deck, blending sound cards, bingo chips/counters, blending\nworksheets, student whiteboards/dry erase markers, \"Blending Binoculars\" (paper\ntowel tubes), drum or rhythm sticks (optional).\n\nIII. Procedure:\n\nA. Part 1: Warm-Up & Introduction (10 minutes)\n\n         Activity: Syllable Segmentation Review (5 min)\n\n      Quick game: Teacher says a word (e.g., popcorn), students clap syllables\n(pop👏corn👏) and hold up fingers to show the count.\n\n        Use pictures from previous lesson for support: 🍿 (popcorn), � (butterfly), �\n(dinosaur).\n\n         Introduce \"Syllable Blending\" with a Puppet (5 min)\n\n         Use a puppet that speaks in \"broken\" syllables. \"Hello! I... for... get... how... to...\nsay... words... to... geth... er!\"\n\n         Teacher models: \"Listen: win... dow. What word am I saying? Window!\"\n\n        Explain: \"Today we\'re doing the OPPOSITE of clapping words apart. We\'re\nputting word parts TOGETHER! This is called blending.\"\n\n        Show visual anchor chart: Syllable + Syllable = Whole Word with picture of\npuzzle pieces coming together.\n\nB. Part 2: Guided Practice & Varied Activities (35 minutes)\n\n         Activity 1: \"Mystery Word\" Listening Game (10 min)\n\n         Teacher says syllables with a pause between, using a consistent pace.\n\n        Students use \"Blending Binoculars\" (hands shaped like circles or paper tubes)\nto \"zoom in\" and put the sounds together.\n\nPDF page 153\nExample: \"Listen: pan... da. Use your blending binoculars! What\'s the word?\"\nStudents: Panda! Show picture: 🐼\n\n         Gradual Release: Start with 2-syllable compound words, move to 3-syllable\nwords.\n\n    **Word List with Pictures:**\n\n    * 🍰 (cup... cake)\n\n    * 🌈 (rain... bow)\n\n    * 🚒 (fire... truck)\n\n    * 🍍 (pine... ap... ple)\n\n    * 🎻 (vi... o... lin)\n\n\n\n* **Activity 2: \"Syllable Puzzle Match\" Center (15 min)**\n\n  * Prepare puzzle cards where one piece has syllables (e.g., **ham** / **bur** /\n**ger**) and the matching piece has the picture (🍔).\n\n  * Students work in pairs to blend the syllables orally, then find the matching picture.\n\n  * Extension: Some puzzles have the whole word written for early readers.\n\n  * **Puzzle Sets:**\n\n    * Set 1 (2 syllables): 🚁 (hel... i... cop... ter), 🐸 (tad... pole), � (cup... cake)\n\n    * Set 2 (3 syllables): � (li... on), � (gir... af... fe), � (pre... tz... el)\n\n* **Activity 3: \"Beat the Blend\" Rhythm Activity (10 min)**\n\n  * Use drum or rhythm sticks to tap out syllables, then blend.\n\n  * Teacher: *Tap-tap* (say \"bas\") *tap* (say \"ket\") *fast continuous taps* (students\nshout \"basket!\")\n\n  * Students then create their own: One student taps syllable beats, class blends.\n\n  * **Picture Prompts for Rhythm:** 🏀 (basketball), � (drum), 🎹 (piano), 🐧 (penguin).\n\n: Assessment & Wrap-Up (15 minutes)\n\n\n\n* **Independent Assessment Worksheet (8 min):**\n\n  * Students complete a two-part worksheet individually.\n\nPDF page 154\nIV. Evaluation:\n\nPart A: Picture Match (Formative)\n\n  *Listen to your teacher say the word in parts. Blend the syllables to make a word.\nCircle the matching picture.*\n\n\n\n  1. Teacher says: \"can... dy\"\n\n    Options: 🍬 (candy) 🐕 (dog) 📘 (book)\n\n\n\n  2. Teacher says: \"tel... e... phone\"\n\n    Options: 🐘 (elephant) 📞 (telephone) 🌮 (taco)\n\n\n\n  3. Teacher says: \"win... dow\"\n\n    Options: � (window) 💨 (wind) 🏮 (lantern)\n\n\n\n  **Part B: Create a Word & Illustrate (Summative)**\n\n  *Blend the syllables to discover a secret word. Draw it in the box!*\n\n\n\n  1. \"sand... witch\" = ____________\n\n    📦 (Box for drawing a sandwich)\n\n\n\n  2. \"but... ter... fly\" = ____________\n\n    📦 (Box for drawing a butterfly)','[41, 42, 147, 148, 149, 150, 151, 152, 153, 154]','assets/images/level2b/page-041.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:44'),
