a Doctor\nAnd\nShe is intelligent\nThis is the future\nThat we\'re building\nHere today\nWhole world\nThey will be amazed\nYeah\nI am\nYou are\nHe is third Person\nShe is\nIt is\nWe are awesome\nYou are\nThey are\nsubject pronouns\nWith the verb to be\nWell this is the\nVerb to be\nAnd my friend\nYou\'re on your way','[17]','assets/images/level4/g3-p17-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(118,7,9,'Practice Passage 8','Verb To Be II','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 8: “Verb To Be II”\nModule PDF page 18 · Number of words in the passage (module): 123\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nVerb To Be II\n\nIt\'s easy as\nOne two three\nJust practice\nEveryday\nThis is the future\nThat we\'re building\nHere today\nYou\'ll show the\nWhole world\nThey will be amazed\nYeah\nI am\nYou are\nHe is third person\nShe is\nIt is\nWe are awesome\nYou are\nThey are\nsubject pronouns\nWith the verb to be\nWhen I say subject\nYou say pronoun\nSubject (pronoun)\nSubject (pronoun)\nWhen I say verb to\nYou say be\nVerb to (Be)\nVerb to (Be)\nThis is the future\nThat we\'re building\nHere today\nYou\'ll show the\nWhole world\nThey will be amazed\nI am\nYou are\nHe is third person\nShe is\nIt is\nWe are awesome\nYou are\nThey are\nsubject pronouns\nWith the verb to be','[18]','assets/images/level4/g3-p18-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(119,7,10,'Practice Passage 9','Daisies','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 9: “Daisies” by Frank Dempster Sherman\nModule PDF page 19 · Number of words in the passage (module): 80\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nDaisies\n\nAt evening when I go to bed\nI see the stars shine overhead;\nThey are the little daisies white\nThat dot the meadow of the Night.\nAnd often while I\'m dreaming so,\nAcross the sky the Moon will go;\nIt is a lady, sweet and fair,\nWho comes to gather daisies there.\nFor, when at morning I arise,\nThere\'s not a star left in the skies;\nShe picked them all and dropped them down\nInto the meadows of the town.','[19]','assets/images/level4/g3-p19-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(120,7,11,'Practice Passage 10','A Robot Dog','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 10: “A Robot Dog”\nModule PDF pages 20, 21 · Number of words in the passage (module): 403\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nA Robot Dog\n\nwalked and walked. After a little bit, they walked past a big tree. Poof! Rocky was a robot dog. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog. “What is going on?” asked Andrew. “Who turned my dog into a robot dog?” Andrew looked all around him. He looked up the street. He looked down the street. He looked behind the tree. He looked up into the tree. No one was there. He stopped and stood still. “Hee, hee, hee,” he heard. It came from the tree. “Woof, woof,” barked Rocky the robot dog at the tree. Andrew looked at the tree. He could not see anything different about this tree. He could not think of what to do. “Tree, are you a real tree?” asked Andrew. The tree did not say anything. Rocky the robot dog came over and smelled the tree. “Woof, woof,” he barked at it again. Andrew was mad. He did not like it that Rocky was a robot dog. Andrew walked over to the tree and tickled it. “Stop that,” said the tree. “Why did you tickle me?” “I think you turned my dog into a robot dog,” said Andrew. “Maybe I did, and maybe I didn’t,” said the tree who was holding his branches in front of himself so Andrew could not tickle him again. “It is kind of funny to see a dog become a robot.” Andrew got real mad this time and went up to the tree. He pushed away the tree’s branches and tickled him some more. “Oh, stop, stop,” said the tree. “I don’t want to be tickled anymore.” “I still think you turned Rocky into a robot dog,” said Andrew who was about to tickle the tree again. “Okay, okay,” said the tree. “I turned your dog into a robot dog. Just don’t tickle me anymore.” “Well, I don’t think it is funny,” said Andrew. “Now turn Rocky the robot dog back into Rocky the real dog.” “Okay,” said the tree. Poof! Rocky was Rocky the real dog again. “Now don’t do that anymore,” said Andrew. “Someone might get mad and tickle you again. You would not like that.” “Okay, I won’t turn dogs into robot dogs anymore,” said the tree. Andrew and Rocky walked home. The tree never turned any more dogs into robots, but every now and then, Andrew would see a robot butterfly go flying past his home.','[20, 21]','assets/images/level4/g3-p20-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(121,7,100,'Post-test Passage','The Ant and The Fly','Read the passage aloud again to show how much you have grown.','Post-test Passage: “The Ant and The Fly” by Aesop Fable\nModule PDF page 23 · Number of words in the passage (module): 199\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Ant and The Fly\n\nOnce, an Ant and a Fly were fighting over who was more important. The Fly said, “Ant! What do you think you are? How can you even think about comparing yourself to me? Look at me! You have to work hard but I pass my time with the rich and the learned. I fly into the temples and taste the offerings. I can sit on the King’s crown and kiss the Queen’s forehead. I do not work, yet have the best life!” The Ant replied, “Don’t be so proud! You are always hated when you enter the temples. You are driven away as soon as you sit on the King’s crown or the Queen’s forehead. You do not have anything left for the difficult times as you are lazy. In winters, you feed yourself on a pile of cow dung. Look at me! I work hard and gather a store of grain for the winter. Later on, when you shiver in the cold, I am safe and at peace in my cozy home. I am prepared to live in any season of the year.”\n\nLook at your own faults, before finding faults in others.','[23]','assets/images/level4/g3-p23-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(122,8,1,'Pre-test Passage','My First Baseball Game','Read the passage aloud so your teacher can see how you read now.','Pre-test Passage: “My First Baseball Game” by Randy Ryan\nModule PDF page 9 · Number of words in the passage (module): 255\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nMy First Baseball Game\n\nI remember my first Cubs game. My uncle came to take my sister and me to it. It was a sunny Saturday. He drove all the way from Indiana to get us. Then we took the train to the ballpark. He said it would be hard to find a parking space there, so he didn’t drive there. He was right. When we got there it was so crowded. We had tickets that were for seats far back. It seemed like I climbed about 300 steps to get to them. But when we got there and I turned around I could see everything. I saw the whole field. It was much better than watching it on TV. Then the game started. Everyone cheered. The man next to me said, “This is going to be the year. They are playing really well.” My uncle said to him, “Let’s hope this is the one.” But then he whispered to us, “Jerome and Felicia, we’ll have to wait and see. Remember last year? They started out well and then they lost at the end.” “Hey, Hey, Hey!” The Cub batter hit a ball far to the wall.\n\nJust then I heard “Hot dogs!” “Peanuts!” I looked and saw a man with a big tray hanging from his shoulders. It was full of hotdogs in plastic wrap and bags of peanuts. “Three hot dogs,” my uncle shouted. Then he gave the man next to us $6. He passed it to the man with the hot dogs.','[9]','assets/images/level4/g4-p09-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(123,8,2,'Practice Passage 1','The Singing Plant','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 1: “The Singing Plant”\nModule PDF page 12 · Number of words in the passage (module): 209\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Singing Plant\n\nTodd awoke to singing. The singing was light and airy. It reminded him of a sunrise. Well that’s appropriate, thought Todd, it’s so early the sun is probably just rising. As his mind grew more awake, Todd realized that he didn’t know where the music was coming from. He checked his radio, as well as his phone. Nothing. He stretched and looked around his room. Nothing in here would be singing, he thought. Todd got out of bed and walked toward his window, thinking that maybe one of his neighbors was playing music. The music didn’t get louder as he walked toward the window, and when he opened the window he could tel l that the music did not come from outside. Todd walked toward his bedroom door, but was hesitant to go out into the hallway. It was still pretty dark, and the rest of his family was still asleep. That always made him nervous to leave his room. It’s only singing, though, there’s nothing scary about that, Todd thought. He opened the door, and could hear the music coming from downstairs. He walked downstairs and followed the music into the kitchen. There, he found the source of the music, but he couldn’t believe his eyes!','[12]','assets/images/level4/g4-p12-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(124,8,3,'Practice Passage 2','The Singing Plant II','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 2: “The Singing Plant II”\nModule PDF page 13 · Number of words in the passage (module): 195\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Singing Plant II\n\nThe plants on the windowsill above the sink were singing! Singing plants! How is this possible? There were 3 of them, all gently swaying and singing together. Todd sat and watched them for a while, trying to understand how and why the plants had all of a sudden become musical plants. Their song faded away after a few minutes, and the plants became still. Todd stayed to watch, just in case they started singing again. His Dad found him sitting in the kitchen watching the plants. “What’s going on, Todd?” his dad asked. “What kind of plant are these?” Todd asked in return. “They’re just little houseplants, they won’t grow too much bigger than they are now. Why?” “They were singing. I didn’t know plants could sing!” exclaimed Todd. “Todd, plants don’t sing. Were you dreaming?” Dad asked, concerned. “No, really, they were! That’s what woke me up this morning!” “Hmmm…” Dad sighed. He walked over to the sink, grabbed a cup, and began to water the plants. He jumped back in surprise when the flowers started singing a happy little tune! “Holy cow! They must have been thirsty! I’ve never seen anything like this!”','[13]','assets/images/level4/g4-p13-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(125,8,4,'Practice Passage 3','A Glass of Cold Water','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 3: “A Glass of Cold Water”\nModule PDF page 14 · Number of words in the passage (module): 258\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nA Glass of Cold Water\n\nEverybody calls me young, beautiful, wonderful. Am I? Look at my hair, my lips, my red rosy cheeks and a pair of blinkering eyes. I remember, somebody says that I look like my mother that I look like my mother. But that when she was young. Now, I am much lovelier than she is. I’m a mortal Venus. Oops! What time is it? I must get ready for the party! Beep-beep…!A-huh! Here they are! Yes, I’m coming! “Child, are you still there?”“Hmp! That’s my mama” “Child, are you still there? Will you please get me a glass of cold water?” “Mama, I’m in a hurry!” “Please child, try to get me a glass of cold water.” “Mama, please, try to get it on your own.” “Please child, try to get me a glass of cold water!”At the party, I danced and danced the whole night. You see, I can’t leave the party at once. I have to dance with everybody who proposed to me. At last, the party is over. I’m very tired. Very, very tired. So, I went home to tell mama what happened. “Mama, I’m home! It’s very quiet. “Mama, I’m home!” Nobody answers. Where is she? I look for her in the sala, but she’s not there. Where is she? A-huh! In the kitchen! I saw my mama, lying down on the floor, dead. With a glass on her hand. I remember, she tried to get it. Oh, God, just for the glass of cold water! Mama! Ma-','[14]','assets/images/level4/g4-p14-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(126,8,5,'Practice Passage 4','The Elephant and the Crocodile','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 4: “The Elephant and the Crocodile”\nModule PDF page 15 · Number of words in the passage (module): 379\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Elephant and the Crocodile\n\nAn Elephant and a Crocodile were once standing beside a river. They were disputing as to which was the better animal. “Look at my strength,” said the Elephant. “I can tear up a tree, roots and all, with my trunk.” “Ah! but quantity is not quality, and your skin is not nearly so tough as mine,” replied the Crocodile, “for neither spear, arrow, nor sword can pierce it.” Just as they were coming to blows, a Lion happened to pass. “Heyday, sirs!” said His Majesty, going up to them, “let me know the cause of your quarrel.” “Will you kindly tell us which is the better animal?” cried both at once. “Certainly,” said the Lion. “Do you see that soldier’s steel helmet on yonder wall?” pointing at the same time across the river. “Yes!” replied the beasts. “Well, then,” continued the Lion, “go and fetch it, and bring it to me, and I shall be able then to decide between you.” Upon hearing this, off they started. The Crocodile, being used to the water, reached the opposite bank of the river first, and was not long in standing beside the wall. Here he waited till the Elephant came up. The latter, seeing at a glance how matters stood, extended his long trunk, and reached the helmet quite easily. They then made their way together back again across the river. The Elephant, anxious to keep up with the Crocodile in the water, forgot that he was carrying the helmet on his back, and a sudden lurch caused the prize to slip off and sink to the bottom. The Crocodile noticed the accident, so down he dived, and brought it up in his capacious mouth. They then returned, and the Crocodile laid the helmet at the Lion’s feet. His Majesty took up the helmet, and addressing the Elephant, said: “You, on account of your size and trunk, were able to reach the prize on the wall but, having lost it, you were unable to recover it. And you,” said the Lion, turning to the Crocodile, “although unable to reach the helmet, were able to dive for it and save it. You are both wise and clever in your respective ways. Neither is better than the other.”','[15]','assets/images/level4/g4-p15-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(127,8,6,'Practice Passage 5','Songs of the Witches','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 5: “Songs of the Witches”\nModule PDF page 16 · Number of words in the passage (module): 202\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nSongs of the Witches\n\nRound about the cauldron go:\nIn the poisoned entrails throw.\nToad, that under cold stone\nDays and nights has thirty-one\nSweated venom sleeping got,\nBoil thou first i’ the charmed pot\nDouble, double toil and trouble;\nFire burn and cauldron bubble.\nFillet of a fenny snake,\nIn the cauldron boil and bake;\nEye of newt and toe of frog,\nWool of bat and tongue of dog,\nAdder\'s fork and blind-worm\'s sting,\nLizard\'s leg and owlet\'s wing.\nFor a charm of powerful trouble,\nLike a hell-broth boil and bubble.\nDouble, double toil and trouble;\nFire burn and cauldron bubble.\nScale of dragon, tooth of wolf,\nWitch\'s mummy, maw and gulf\nOf the ravin\'d salt-sea shark,\nRoot of hemlock digg\'d i’ the dark,\nLiver of blaspheming Jew;\nGall of goat; and slips of yew\nSliver\'d in the moon\'s eclipse;\nNose of Turk, and Tartar\'s lips;\nFinger of birth-strangled babe\nDitch-deliver\'d by a drab,\nMake the gruel thick and slab:\nAdd thereto a tiger\'s chaudron,\nFor the ingredients of our cauldron.\nDouble, double toil and trouble,\nFire burn and cauldron bubble.\nCool it with a baboon\'s blood,\nThen the charm is firm and good.','[16]','assets/images/level4/g4-p16-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(128,8,7,'Practice Passage 6','My First Baseball Game II','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 6: “My First Baseball Game II” by Randy Ryan\nModule PDF page 17 · Number of words in the passage (module): 237\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nMy First Baseball Game II\n\nHe sent back three hot dogs. “This is great,” I said to my uncle. “Not so great,” he said. “We just struck out.” While I had been watching the hotdogs, the team had struck out. It went on that way for the rest of the game. We got peanuts. We even got another hot dog. And the Cubs kept losing. We’d all hope every time a batter came to bat.\n\nOne by one they struck out. Still, everyone was staying happy. Even at the end. The score was 7 to 0. The Cubs never got a run. “Tomorrow, they’ll win,” one person said. “Right. This was just a bad day for them,” another said. “The pitcher tomorrow is really good.” When we were on the way home, I asked my uncle, “Why was everyone so sure they will win next time?” “That’s what it means to be a Cubs fan.\n\nWe believe in tomorrow. When they win, it’s the best. Every year, we hope. Every game we hope. That’s loyalty. It’s about supporting your team no matter what. Cubs fans are loyal. And it’s about having fun as a family. Did you have fun?” As our uncle was about to drive home to Indiana, Felicia said, “It was great. I see what you meant. Even when they lose, they are like a family, they stick together. Thanks for taking us. And thanks for being loyal to us.”','[17]','assets/images/level4/g4-p17-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(129,8,8,'Practice Passage 7','The Boy Who Cried Wolf','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 7: “The Boy Who Cried Wolf” by Aesop Fable\nModule PDF page 18 · Number of words in the passage (module): 162\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Boy Who Cried Wolf\n\nThere once was a shepherd boy who was bored as he sat on the hillside watching the village sheep. To amuse himself he took a great breath and sang out, \"Wolf! Wolf! The Wolf is chasing the sheep!\"\n\nThe villagers came running up the hill to help the boy drive the wolf away.\n\nBut when they arrived at the top of the hill, they found no wolf.\n\nThe boy laughed at the sight of their angry faces.\n\n\"Don\'t cry \'wolf\', shepherd boy,\" said the villagers, \"when there\'s no wolf!\" They went grumbling back down the hill.\n\nLater, the boy sang out again, \"Wolf! Wolf! The wolf is chasing the sheep!\" To his naughty delight, he watched the villagers run up the hill to help him drive the wolf away. When the villagers saw no wolf they sternly said, \"Save your frightened song for when there is really something wrong! Don\'t cry \'wolf\' when there is NO wolf!\"','[18]','assets/images/level4/g4-p18-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(130,8,9,'Practice Passage 8','The Boy Who Cried Wolf II','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 8: “The Boy Who Cried Wolf II”\nModule PDF page 19 · Number of words in the passage (module): 148\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Boy Who Cried Wolf II\n\nBut the boy just grinned and watched them go grumbling down the hill once more. Later, he saw a REAL wolf prowling about his flock. Alarmed, he leaped to his feet and sang out as loudly as he could, \"Wolf! Wolf!\" But the villagers thought he was trying to fool them again, and so they didn\'t come. At sunset, everyone wondered why the shepherd boy hadn\'t returned to the village with their sheep. They went up the hill to find the boy. They found him weeping. \"There really was a wolf here! The flock has scattered! I cried out, \"Wolf!\" Why didn\'t you come?\" An old man tried to comfort the boy as they walked back to the village. \"We\'ll help you look for the lost sheep in the morning,\" he said, putting his arm around the youth, \"Nobody believes a liar...even when he is telling the truth!\"','[19]','assets/images/level4/g4-p19-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(131,8,10,'Practice Passage 9','A Day at the Beach','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 9: “A Day at the Beach” by Author unknown\nModule PDF page 20 · Number of words in the passage (module): 58\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nA Day at the Beach\n\nThe sun on my face,\nthe sand in my toes,\nmakes any day a delight.\nHunting for seashells,\nand surfing the waves,\nmakes everything seem alright.\nBuilding a castle,\nfit for a king,\nmakes all my dreams within reach.\nWearing my shades\nand getting a tan,\nmakes a great day at the Beach.','[20]','assets/images/level4/g4-p20-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(132,8,11,'Practice Passage 10','I Wanna Do Right','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 10: “I Wanna Do Right”\nModule PDF page 21 · Number of words in the passage (module): 100\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nI Wanna Do Right\n\nI wanna do right, wanna do what\'s right\nI\'m gonna give You all of my life\nI put my trust in You, Jesus\nAnd You\'ll show me, show me how to do right\nHey, hey, every word I say\nOoh, ooh, I wanna be like You\nOh, my, my, every day goes by\nI wanna be more like You\nI wanna be more like You\nBridge:\nI was made to give You praise\nMake me more like You\nI was made to give You praise\nMake me more like You\nIn all I do\n(Repeat Chorus)','[21]','assets/images/level4/g4-p21-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(133,8,100,'Post-test Passage','My First Baseball Game','Read the passage aloud again to show how much you have grown.','Post-test Passage: “My First Baseball Game” by Randy Ryan\nModule PDF page 23 · Number of words in the passage (module): 255\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nMy First Baseball Game\n\nI remember my first Cubs game. My uncle came to take my sister and me to it. It was a sunny Saturday. He drove all the way from Indiana to get us. Then we took the train to the ballpark. He said it would be hard to find a parking space there, so he didn’t drive there. He was right. When we got there it was so crowded. We had tickets that were for seats far back. It seemed like I climbed about 300 steps to get to them. But when we got there and I turned around I could see everything. I saw the whole field. It was much better than watching it on TV. Then the game started. Everyone cheered. The man next to me said, “This is going to be the year. They are playing really well.” My uncle said to him, “Let’s hope this is the one.” But then he whispered to us, “Jerome and Felicia, we’ll have to wait and see. Remember last year? They started out well and then they lost at the end.” “Hey, Hey, Hey!” The Cub batter hit a ball far to the wall.\n\nJust then I heard “Hot dogs!” “Peanuts!” I looked and saw a man with a big tray hanging from his shoulders. It was full of hotdogs in plastic wrap and bags of peanuts. “Three hot dogs,” my uncle shouted. Then he gave the man next to us $6. He passed it to the man with the hot dogs.','[23]','assets/images/level4/g4-p23-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(134,9,1,'Pre-test Passage','The Crocodile and the Monkey','Read the passage aloud so your teacher can see how you read now.','Pre-test Passage: “The Crocodile and the Monkey”\nModule PDF page 9 · Number of words in the passage (module): 491\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Crocodile and the Monkey\n\nOnce upon a time, there lived a monkey on a mango tree on the banks of a river. The mangoes of that tree were the sweetest of all. The tree bore fruits year-round. The monkey used to live very happily on the tree eating the tasty fruits and playing on the tree.\n\nOn a particular day, a crocodile came near that mango tree. He seemed very exhausted. The monkey offered him some delicious mangoes from the tree. The crocodile loved those mangoes and thanked the monkey for the same. From then on, the crocodile used to visit the monkey every day and they became good friends.\n\nOne day the crocodile thought to take some mangoes for his wife. When his wife ate the mangoes, she demanded something very absurd from the crocodile. She said that these fruits are so tasty, then how delicious will be the heart of that monkey who regularly eats these mangoes!\n\nShe ordered the crocodile to bring the monkey’s heart for her. The crocodile was shocked at her demand. The monkey was his friend. How could he betray him? He declined to kill the monkey and bring his heart to her.\n\nThe crocodile’s wife was adamant about getting the monkey’s heart. She told the crocodile that she is not keeping well and the doctor has recommended her to eat a monkey’s heart to recover. She even threatened him that if the crocodile didn’t get the monkey’s heart for her, she would surely die. The crocodile had to give in to her order.\n\nWith a heavy heart, the crocodile left to get the monkey. He went to the monkey and said, “Friend, my wife loved the mangoes sent by you. She has invited you to our house to thank you.” The monkey agreed and sat on the crocodile’s back so that the crocodile could take him to his house.\n\nIn the middle of the river, the crocodile began sinking in. The monkey got frightened and he asked the crocodile as to why he was sinking. The crocodile, knowing that now there was no escape for the monkey, replied, “Forgive me, my friend. My wife needs to eat your heart to save her life. That’s why I am taking you with me.”\n\nThe monkey was outraged. The monkey was smart. He kept calm and told the crocodile that he would be more than happy to save his wife’s life but he left his heart at the mango tree. The monkey told him that they can quickly go and get his heart from the tree.\n\nThe crocodile accepted it at once and turned back. As soon as they reached the tree, the monkey jumped onto the tree and said, “O foolish crocodile, how can someone take out his heart and keep it somewhere else? You have deceived me as a friend. Now go and never come back. The ashamed crocodile went back to his home.','[9]','assets/images/level4/g5-p09-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(135,9,2,'Practice Passage 1','Fight Song','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 1: “Fight Song” by Rachel Platten\nModule PDF page 11 · Number of words in the passage (module): 332\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nFight Song\n\nLike a small boat, On the ocean, Sending big waves, Into motion\nLike how a single word , Can make a heart open\nI might only have one match, But I can make an explosion\nAnd all those things I didn\'t say, Wrecking balls inside my brain\nI will scream them loud tonight, Can you hear my voice this time?\nThis is my fight song, Take back my life song\nProve I\'m alright song, My power\'s turned on\nStarting right now I\'ll be strong, I\'ll play my fight song\nAnd I don\'t really care if nobody else believes\n\'Cause I\'ve still got a lot of fight left in me\nLosing friends and I\'m chasing sleep\nEverybody\'s worried about me In too deep\nSay I\'m in too deep (in too deep)\nAnd it\'s been two years I miss my home\nBut there\'s a fire burning in my bones\nStill believe. Yeah, I still believe\nAnd all those things I didn\'t say\nWrecking balls inside my brain\nI will scream them loud tonight\nCan you hear my voice this time?\nThis is my fight song, Take back my life song\nProve I\'m alright song, My power\'s turned on\nStarting right now I\'ll be strong, I\'ll play my fight song\nAnd I don\'t really care if nobody else believes\n\'Cause I\'ve still got a lot of fight left in me\nA lot of fight left in me, Like a small boat\nOn the ocean, Sending big waves Into motion\nLike how a single word, Can make a heart open\nI might only have one match\nBut I can make an explosion\nThis is my fight song, Take back my life song\nProve I\'m alright song, My power\'s turned on\nStarting right now I\'ll be strong (I\'ll be strong)\nI\'ll play my fight song\nAnd I don\'t really care if nobody else believes\n\'Cause I\'ve still got a lot of fight left in me\nKnow I\'ve still got a lot of fight left in me','[11]','assets/images/level4/g5-p11-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(136,9,3,'Practice Passage 2','You Can','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 2: “You Can”\nModule PDF page 12 · Number of words in the passage (module): 112\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nYou Can\n\nIf you think you’re beaten, you are…\nIf you think you dare not, you don’t…\nIf you like to win…but think you can’t…\nIt’s almost a cinch, YOU WON’T!!\nIf you think you’ll lose, you’re lost….\nFor out in the world, you’ll find…\nSuccess begins with a fellow’s will….\nIt’s all in a state of mind.\nIf you think you’re outclassed, you are….\nYou’ve got to think high to rise…\nYou’ve got to be sure of yourself before….\nYou can ever win the prize.\nLife’s battles don’t always go…\nTo the stronger or faster man…\nBut sooner or later the one who wins…\nIS THE ONE WHO THINKS HE CAN','[12]','assets/images/level4/g5-p12-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(137,9,4,'Practice Passage 3','Print Your Own Medicine','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 3: “Print Your Own Medicine” by Lee Cronin\nModule PDF page 13 · Number of words in the passage (module): 472\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nPrint Your Own Medicine\n\nOrganic chemists make molecules, very complicated molecules, by chopping up a big molecule into small molecules and reverse engineering. And as a chemist, one of the things I wanted to ask my research group a couple of years ago is, could we make a really cool universal chemistry set? In essence, could we \"app\" chemistry? Now what would this mean, and how would we do it? Well to start to do this, we took a 3D printer and we started to print our beakers and our test tubes on one side and then print the molecule at the same time on the other side and combine them together in what we call reactionware. And so, by printing the vessel and doing the chemistry at the same time, we may start to access this universal toolkit of chemistry. Now what could this mean? Well, if we can embed biological and chemical networks like a search engine, so if you have a cell that\'s ill that you need to cure or bacteria that you want to kill, if you have this embedded in your device at the same time, and you do the chemistry, you may be able to make drugs in a new way. So how are we doing this in the lab? Well, it requires software, it requires hardware and it requires chemical inks. And so the really cool bit is, the idea is that we want to have a universal set of inks that we put out with the printer, and you download the blueprint, the organic chemistry for that molecule and you make it in the device. And so, you can make your molecule in the printer using this software. So, what could this mean? Well, ultimately, it could mean that you could print your own medicine. And this is what we\'re doing in the lab at the moment. But to take baby steps to get there, first of all we want to look at drug design and production, or drug discovery and manufacturing. Because if we can manufacture it after we\'ve discovered it, we could deploy it anywhere. You don\'t need to go to the chemist anymore. We can print drugs at point of need. We can download new diagnostics. Say a new super bug has emerged. You put it in your search engine, and you create the drug to treat the threat. So, this allows you on-the-fly molecular assembly. But perhaps for me the core bit going into the future is this idea of taking your own stem cells, with your genes and your environment, and you print your own personal medicine. And if that doesn\'t seem fanciful enough, where do you think we\'re going to go? Well, you\'re going to have your own personal matter fabricator. Beam me up, Scotty. (Applause)','[13]','assets/images/level4/g5-p13-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(138,9,5,'Practice Passage 4','When You Are Old','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 4: “When You Are Old” by William Butler Yeats\nModule PDF page 14 · Number of words in the passage (module): 104\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nWhen You Are Old\n\nWhen you are old and grey and full of sleep,\nAnd nodding by the fire, take down this book,\nAnd slowly read, and dream of the soft look\nYour eyes had once, and of their shadows\ndeep;\nHow many loved your moments of glad grace,\nAnd loved your beauty with love false or true,\nBut one man loved the pilgrim soul in you,\nAnd loved the sorrows of your changing face;\nAnd bending down beside the glowing bars,\nMurmur, a little sadly, how Love fled\nAnd paced upon the mountains overhead\nAnd hid his face amid a crowd of stars.','[14]','assets/images/level4/g5-p14-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(139,9,6,'Practice Passage 5','Jimmy Jet and His TV Set','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 5: “Jimmy Jet and His TV Set” by Shel Silverstein\nModule PDF page 15 · Number of words in the passage (module): 150\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nJimmy Jet and His TV Set\n\nI\'ll tell you the story of Jimmy Jet--\nAnd you know what I tell you is true.\nHe loved to watch his TV set\nAlmost as much as you.\nHe watched all day, he watched all night\nTill he grew pale and lean,\nFrom \"The Early Show\" to \"The Late Show\"\nAnd all the shows in between.\nHe watched till his eyes were frozen wide,\nAnd his bottom grew into his chair.\nAnd his chin turned into a tuning dial,\nAnd antennae grew out of his hair.\nAnd his brains turned into TV tubes,\nAnd his face to a TV screen.\nAnd two knobs saying \"vert.\" and \"horiz.\"\nGrew where his ears had been.\nAnd he grew a plug that looked like a tail\nSo we plugged in little Jim.\nAnd now instead of him watching TV\nWe all sit around and watch him.','[15]','assets/images/level4/g5-p15-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(140,9,7,'Practice Passage 6','Pygmalion: Henry and Eliza','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 6: “Pygmalion: Henry and Eliza”\nModule PDF page 16 · Number of words in the passage (module): 523\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nPygmalion: Henry and Eliza\n\nGeorge Bernard Shaw’s play Pygmalion was the basis for the famous musical My Fair Lady. First performed in 1913, Pygmalion is the story of Eliza Doolittle, a poor and unsophisticated flower seller on the streets of London, who wants to learn to speak “like a lady.” In this passage from Act I, she has met by chance Professor Henry Higgins, a grumpy professor of phonetics, near a church during a rainstorm. They are amongst a group of people who have gathered under the church’s portico to stay dry. Henry has been listening to the other people as part of his research. Eliza was afraid for a time that he was a policeman trying to arrest her and is just beginning to recover from her fear. Henry is “The Note Taker” below. Eliza is “The Flower Girl,” and “The Gentleman” is Colonel Pickering, who was part of the group. The rain has stopped.\n\nThe Flower Girl: Poor girl! Hard enough for her to live without being worried and chivied.\n\nThe Gentleman: [returning to his former place on the note taker’s left] How do you do it, if I may ask? The Note Taker: Simply phonetics. The science of speech. That’s my profession; also my hobby. Happy is the man who can make a living by his hobby! You can spot an Irishman or a Yorkshireman by his brogue. I can place any man within six miles. I can place him within two miles in London. Sometimes within two streets.\n\nThe Flower Girl: Ought to be ashamed of himself, unmanly coward!\n\nThe Gentleman: But is there a living in that?\n\nThe Note Taker: Oh yes. Quite a fat one. This is an age of upstarts. Men begin in Kentish Town with 80 pounds a year, and end in Park Lane with a hundred thousand. They want to drop Kentish Town; but they give themselves away every time they open their mouths. Now! can teach them— The Flower Girl: Let him mind his own business and leave a poor girl— The Note Taker: [explosively] Woman: cease this detestable boohooing instantly; or else seek the shelter of some other lace of worship.\n\nThe Flower Girl: [with feeble defiance] I’ve a right to be here if I like, same as you.\n\nThe Note Taker: A woman who utters such depressing and disgusting sounds has no right to be anywhere—no right to live. Remember that you are a human being with a soul and the divine gift of articulate speech: that your native language is the language of Shakespeare and Milton and The Bible; and don’t sit there crooning like a bilious pigeon.\n\nThe Flower Girl: [quite overwhelmed, and looking up at him in mingled wonder and deprecation without daring to raise her head] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow—oo!\n\nThe Note Taker: [whipping out his book] Heavens! what a sound! [He writes; then holds out the book and reads, reproducing her vowels exactly] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow— ow— oo!\n\nThe Flower Girl: [tickled by the performance, and laughing in spite of herself] Garn!','[16]','assets/images/level4/g5-p16-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(141,9,8,'Practice Passage 7','Old McDonald had a Farm','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 7: “Old McDonald had a Farm”\nModule PDF page 17 · Number of words in the passage (module): 315\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nOld McDonald had a Farm\n\nOld MacDonald had a farm E-i-e-i-o\nAnd on that farm he had some cows\nE-i-e-i-o\nWith a moo-moo here\nAnd a moo-moo there\nHere a moo, there a moo\nEverywhere a moo-moo\nOld MacDonald had a farm E-i-e-i-o\nAnd on that farm he had some chickens\nE-i-e-i-o\nWith a cluck-cluck here\nAnd a cluck-cluck there\nHere a cluck, there a cluck\nEverywhere a cluck-cluck\nMoo-moo here\nAnd a moo-moo there\nHere a moo, there a moo\nEverywhere a moo-moo\nOld MacDonald had a farm E-i-e-i-o\nAnd on that farm he had some sheeps\nE-i-e-i-o\nWith a baa-baa here And a baa-baa there\nHere a baa, there a baa\nEverywhere a baa-baa\nA cluck-cluck here\nAnd a cluck-cluck there\nHere a cluck, there a cluck\nEverywhere a cluck-cluck\nMoo-moo here And a moo-moo there\nHere a moo, there a moo\nEverywhere a moo-moo\nOld MacDonald had a farm E-i-e-i-o\nAnd on that farm he had some pigs E-i-e-i-\nWith a oink-oink here And a oink-oink there\nEverywhere a oink-oink Baa-baa here\nAnd a baa-baa there\nHere a baa, there a baa\nEverywhere a baa-baa\nA cluck-cluck here\nAnd a cluck-cluck there\nHere a cluck, there a cluck\nEverywhere a cluck-cluck Moo-moo here\nAnd a moo-moo there\nHere a moo, there a moo\nEverywhere a moo-moo\nOld MacDonald had a farm E-i-e-i-o\nAnd on that farm he had some ducks\nE-i-e-i-o\nWith a quack-quack here\nAnd a quack-quack there\nHere a quack, there a quack\nEverywhere a quack-quack\nA oink-oink here And a oink-oink there\nHere a oink, there a oink\nEverywhere a oink-oink\nBaa-baa here And a baa-baa there\nHere a baa, there a baa\nEverywhere a baa-baa A cluck-cluck here\nAnd a cluck-cluck there\nHere a cluck, there a cluck\nEverywhere a cluck-cluck\nMoo-moo here And a moo-moo there\nHere a moo, there a moo\nEverywhere a moo-moo\nOld MacDonald had a farm\nE-i-e-i-o','[17]','assets/images/level4/g5-p17-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(142,9,9,'Practice Passage 8','The Crocodile and the Monkey','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 8: “The Crocodile and the Monkey”\nModule PDF page 18 · Number of words in the passage (module): 495\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Crocodile and the Monkey\n\nOnce upon a time, there lived a monkey on a mango tree on the banks of a river. The mangoes of that tree were the sweetest of all. The tree bore fruits year-round. The monkey used to live very happily on the tree eating the tasty fruits and playing on the tree.\n\nOn a particular day, a crocodile came near that mango tree. He seemed very exhausted. The monkey offered him some delicious mangoes from the tree. The crocodile loved those mangoes and thanked the monkey for the same. From then on, the crocodile used to visit the monkey every day and they became good friends.\n\nOne day the crocodile thought to take some mangoes for his wife. When his wife ate the mangoes, she demanded something very absurd from the crocodile. She said that these fruits are so tasty, then how delicious will be the heart of that monkey who regularly eats these mangoes!\n\nShe ordered the crocodile to bring the monkey’s heart for her. The crocodile was shocked at her demand. The monkey was his friend. How could he betray him? He declined to kill the monkey and bring his heart to her.\n\nThe crocodile’s wife was adamant about getting the monkey’s heart. She told the crocodile that she is not keeping well and the doctor has recommended her to eat a monkey’s heart to recover. She even threatened him that if the crocodile didn’t get the monkey’s heart for her, she would surely die. The crocodile had to give in to her order.\n\nWith a heavy heart, the crocodile left to get the monkey. He went to the monkey and said, “Friend, my wife loved the mangoes sent by you. She has invited you to our house to thank you.” The monkey agreed and sat on the crocodile’s back so that the crocodile could take him to his house.\n\nIn the middle of the river, the crocodile began sinking in. The monkey got frightened and he asked the crocodile as to why he was sinking. The crocodile, knowing that now there was no escape for the monkey, replied, “Forgive me, my friend. My wife needs to eat your heart to save her life. That’s why I am taking you with me.”\n\nThe monkey was outraged. The monkey was smart. He kept calm and told the crocodile that he would be more than happy to save his wife’s life but he left his heart at the mango tree. The monkey told him that they can quickly go and get his heart from the tree.\n\nThe crocodile accepted it at once and turned back. As soon as they reached the tree, the monkey jumped onto the tree and said, “O foolish crocodile, how can someone take out his heart and keep it somewhere else? You have deceived me as a friend. Now go and never come back. The ashamed crocodile went back to his home.','[18]','assets/images/level4/g5-p18-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(143,9,100,'Post-test Passage','The Crocodile and the Monkey','Read the passage aloud again to show how much you have grown.','Post-test Passage: “The Crocodile and the Monkey”\nModule PDF page 20 · Number of words in the passage (module): 491\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Crocodile and the Monkey\n\nOnce upon a time, there lived a monkey on a mango tree on the banks of a river. The mangoes of that tree were the sweetest of all. The tree bore fruits year-round. The monkey used to live very happily on the tree eating the tasty fruits and playing on the tree.\n\nOn a particular day, a crocodile came near that mango tree. He seemed very exhausted. The monkey offered him some delicious mangoes from the tree. The crocodile loved those mangoes and thanked the monkey for the same. From then on, the crocodile used to visit the monkey every day and they became good friends.\n\nOne day the crocodile thought to take some mangoes for his wife. When his wife ate the mangoes, she demanded something very absurd from the crocodile. She said that these fruits are so tasty, then how delicious will be the heart of that monkey who regularly eats these mangoes!\n\nShe ordered the crocodile to bring the monkey’s heart for her. The crocodile was shocked at her demand. The monkey was his friend. How could he betray him? He declined to kill the monkey and bring his heart to her.\n\nThe crocodile’s wife was adamant about getting the monkey’s heart. She told the crocodile that she is not keeping well and the doctor has recommended her to eat a monkey’s heart to recover. She even threatened him that if the crocodile didn’t get the monkey’s heart for her, she would surely die. The crocodile had to give in to her order.\n\nWith a heavy heart, the crocodile left to get the monkey. He went to the monkey and said, “Friend, my wife loved the mangoes sent by you. She has invited you to our house to thank you.” The monkey agreed and sat on the crocodile’s back so that the crocodile could take him to his house.\n\nIn the middle of the river, the crocodile began sinking in. The monkey got frightened and he asked the crocodile as to why he was sinking. The crocodile, knowing that now there was no escape for the monkey, replied, “Forgive me, my friend. My wife needs to eat your heart to save her life. That’s why I am taking you with me.”\n\nThe monkey was outraged. The monkey was smart. He kept calm and told the crocodile that he would be more than happy to save his wife’s life but he left his heart at the mango tree. The monkey told him that they can quickly go and get his heart from the tree.\n\nThe crocodile accepted it at once and turned back. As soon as they reached the tree, the monkey jumped onto the tree and said, “O foolish crocodile, how can someone take out his heart and keep it somewhere else? You have deceived me as a friend. Now go and never come back. The ashamed crocodile went back to his home.','[20]','assets/images/level4/g5-p20-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(144,10,1,'Pre-test Passage','\"Bad Girl\"','Read the passage aloud so your teacher can see how you read now.','Pre-test Passage: “\"Bad Girl\"”\nModule PDF page 9 · Number of words in the passage (module): 269\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\n\"Bad Girl\"\n\nHey! Everybody seems to be staring at me..\nYou! You! All of you!\nHow dare you to stare at me?\nWhy? Is it because I\'m a bad girl?\nA bad girl I am, A good for nothing teenager, a\nproblem child?\nThat\'s what you call me!\nI smoke. I drink. I gamble at my young tender\nage.\nI lie. I cheat, and I could even kill, If I have too.\nYes, I\'m a bad girl, but where are my parents?\nYou! You! You are my good parents?\nMy good elder brother and sister in this society\nwhere I live?\nLook…look at me…What have you done to me?\nYou have pampered and spoiled me, neglected me when I needed you\nmost!\nEntrusted me to a yaya, whose intelligence was much lower than mine!\nWhile you go about your parties, your meetings and gambling session…\nThus… I drifted away from you!\nLonging for a father\'s love, yearning for a mother\'s care!\nAs I grew up, everything changed!\nYou too have changed!\nYou spent more time in your poker, mahjong tables, bars and night\nclubs. You even landed on the headlines of the newspaper as crooks,\npeddlers and you racketeers.\nNow, you call me names, accuse me of everything I do to myself?\nTell me! How good are you?\nIf you really wish to ensure my future…\nThen hurry….hurry back home! Where I await you, because I need you…\nProtect me from all evil influences that will threaten at my very own\nunderstanding…\nBut if I am bad, really bad…then, you\'ve got to help me!\nHelp me! Oh please…Help me.','[9]','assets/images/level4/g6-p09-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(145,10,2,'Practice Passage 1','It is Raining','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 1: “It is Raining”\nModule PDF page 11 · Number of words in the passage (module): 241\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nIt is Raining\n\nWhere would you like to be in the rain?\nWhere would you like to be?\nI\'d like to be on a city street\nWhere the rain comes driving down\nTrying to make things neat\nAs it washes the houses, roof and wall\nThe taxis, buses, cars, and all.\nThat’s where I’d like to be in the rain\nThat’s where I’d like to be.\nIt is raining.\nWhere would you like to be in the rain?\nWhere would you like to be?\nI\'d like to be in a tall tree top\nWhere the rain comes dripping drop, drop, drop, drop,\nAround on every side -\nwhere it wets the farmers, the barns, the pig.\nthe cows, the chickens, both little and big.\nWhere it batters and beats on a field of grain.\nAnd makes the little birds hide from the rain.\nThat’s where I\'d like to be in the rain.\nThat’s where I\'d like to be.\nIt is raining.\nWhere would you like to be in the rain?\nWhere would you like to be?\nI\'d like to be on ship at sea\nWhere everything’s wet as can be\nAnd the waves are rolling high\nWhere sailors are pulling the ropes and singing\nAnd winds in the rigging and salt’s sprays\nstinging And round us sea gulls cry\nOn a dipping, skimming ship at sea.\nThat’s where I\'d like to be in the rain.\nThat’s where I\'d like to be.','[11]','assets/images/level4/g6-p11-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(146,10,3,'Practice Passage 2','You Learn','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 2: “You Learn” by Alanis Morissette\nModule PDF page 12 · Number of words in the passage (module): 220\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nYou Learn\n\nI recommend getting your heart trampled on to anyone\nI recommend walking around naked in your living room Swallow it down\n(what a jagged little pill)\nIt feels so good (swimming in your stomach) Wait until the dust settles\nYou live you learn, You love you learn\nYou cry you learn, You lose you learn\nYou bleed you learn, You scream you learn\nI recommend biting off more than you can chew to anyone certainly do\nI recommend sticking your foot in your mouth at anytime\nFeel free\nThrow it down (the caution blocks you from the wind)\nHold it up (to the rays)\nYou wait and see when the smoke clears\nYou live you learn, You love you learn\nYou cry you learn, You lose you learn\nYou bleed you learn, You scream you learn\nWear it out (like a three-year-old would do)\nMelt it down (you\'re gonna have to eventually anyway)\nThe fire trucks are coming up around the bend\nYou live you learn, You love you learn\nYou cry you learn, You lose you learn\nYou bleed you learn, You scream you learn\nYou grieve you learn, You choke you learn\nYou laugh you learn, You choose you learn\nYou pray you learn, You ask you learn\nYou live you learn','[12]','assets/images/level4/g6-p12-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(147,10,4,'Practice Passage 3','The House on the Hill','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 3: “The House on the Hill” by Edwin Arlington Robinson\nModule PDF page 13 · Number of words in the passage (module): 107\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe House on the Hill\n\nThey are all gone away,\nThe House is shut and still,\nThere is nothing more to say.\nThrough broken walls and gray\nThe winds blow bleak and shrill:\nThey are all gone away.\nNor is there one to-day, To speak them good or ill:\nThere is nothing more to say.\nWhy is it then we stray, Around the sunken sill?\nThey are all gone away,\nAnd our poor fancy-play, For them is wasted skill:\nThere is nothing more to say.\nThere is ruin and decay, In the House on the Hill:\nThey are all gone away, There is nothing more to say.','[13]','assets/images/level4/g6-p13-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(148,10,5,'Practice Passage 4','The Golden Egg','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 4: “The Golden Egg”\nModule PDF page 14 · Number of words in the passage (module): 218\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Golden Egg\n\nOnce upon a time, there lived a cloth merchant in a village with his wife and two children. They were indeed quite well-off. They had a beautiful hen which laid an egg every day. It was not an ordinary egg, rather, a golden egg. But the man was not satisfied with what he used to get daily. He was a get rich-trice kind of a person.\n\nThe man wanted to get all the golden eggs from his hen at one single go. So, one day he thought hard and at last clicked upon a plan. He decided to kill the hen and get all the eggs together.\n\nSo, the next day when the hen laid a golden egg, the man caught hold of it, took a sharp knife, chopped off its neck and cut its body open. There was nothing but blood all around & no trace of any egg at all. He was highly grieved because now he would not get even one single egg.\n\nHis life was going on smoothly with one egg a day but now, he himself made his life miserable. The outcome of his greed was that he started becoming poorer & poorer day by day and ultimately became a pauper. How jinxed and how much foolish he was.','[14]','assets/images/level4/g6-p14-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(149,10,6,'Practice Passage 5','To the River','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 5: “To the River” by Edgar Allan Poe - 1809-1849\nModule PDF page 15 · Number of words in the passage (module): 78\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nTo the River\n\nFair river! in thy bright, clear flow\nOf crystal, wandering water,\nThou art an emblem of the glow\nOf beauty—the unhidden heart—\nThe playful maziness of art\nIn old Alberto’s daughter;\nBut when within thy wave she looks—\nWhich glistens then, and trembles—\nWhy, then, the prettiest of brooks\nHer worshipper resembles;\nFor in my heart, as in thy stream,\nHer image deeply lies—\nHis heart which trembles at the beam\nOf her soul-searching eyes.','[15]','assets/images/level4/g6-p15-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(150,10,7,'Practice Passage 6','Be Glad Your Nose is on Your Face','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 6: “Be Glad Your Nose is on Your Face” by Jack Prelutsky\nModule PDF page 16 · Number of words in the passage (module): 140\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nBe Glad Your Nose is on Your Face\n\nBe glad your nose is on your face, not pasted on some other place,\nfor if it were where it is not, you might dislike your nose a lot.\nImagine if your precious nose, were sandwiched in between your toes,\nthat clearly would not be a treat, for you\'d be forced to smell your feet.\nYour nose would be a source of dread were it attached atop your head,\nit soon would drive you to despair, forever tickled by your hair.\nWithin your ear, your nose would be\nan absolute catastrophe,\nfor when you were obliged to sneeze,\nyour brain would rattle from the breeze.\nYour nose, instead, through thick and thin,\nremains between your eyes and chin,\nnot pasted on some other place--\nbe glad your nose is on your face.','[16]','assets/images/level4/g6-p16-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(151,10,8,'Practice Passage 7','The Crocodile and the Monkey','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 7: “The Crocodile and the Monkey”\nModule PDF page 17 · Number of words in the passage (module): 435\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nThe Crocodile and the Monkey\n\nOnce upon a time, there lived a monkey on a mango tree on the banks of a river. The mangoes of that tree were the sweetest of all. The tree bore fruits year-round. The monkey used to live very happily on the tree eating the tasty fruits and playing on the tree. On a particular day, a crocodile came near that mango tree. He seemed very exhausted. The monkey offered him some delicious mangoes from the tree. The crocodile loved those mangoes and thanked the monkey for the same. From then on, the crocodile used to visit the monkey every day and they became good friends. One day the crocodile thought to take some mangoes for his wife. When his wife ate the mangoes, she demanded something very absurd from the crocodile. She said that these fruits are so tasty, then how delicious will be the heart of that monkey who regularly eats these mangoes! She ordered the crocodile to bring the monkey’s heart for her. The crocodile was shocked at her demand. The monkey was his friend. How could he betray him? He declined to kill the monkey and bring his heart to her. The crocodile’s wife was adamant about getting the monkey’s heart. She told the crocodile that she is not keeping well and the doctor has recommended her to eat a monkey’s heart to recover. She even threatened him that if the crocodile didn’t get the monkey’s heart for her, she would surely die. The crocodile had to give in to her order. With a heavy heart, the crocodile left to get the monkey. He went to the monkey and said, “Friend, my wife loved the mangoes sent by you. She has invited you to our house to thank you.” The monkey agreed and sat on the crocodile’s back so that the crocodile could take him to his house. In the middle of the river, the crocodile began sinking in. The monkey got frightened and he asked the crocodile as to why he was sinking. The crocodile, knowing that now there was no escape for the monkey, replied, “Forgive me, my friend. My wife needs to eat your heart to save her life. That’s why I am taking you with me.” The monkey was outraged. The monkey was smart. He kept calm and told the crocodile that he would be more than happy to save his wife’s life but he left his heart at the mango tree. The monkey told him that they can quickly go and get his heart from the tree. The crocodile accepted it at once and turned back. As soon as they reached the tree, the monkey jumped onto the tree and said, “O foolish crocodile, how can someone take out his heart and keep it somewhere else? You have deceived me as a friend. Now go and never come back. The ashamed crocodile went back to his home.','[17]','assets/images/level4/g6-p17-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(152,10,9,'Practice Passage 8','Pygmalion: Henry and Eliza','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 8: “Pygmalion: Henry and Eliza”\nModule PDF page 18 · Number of words in the passage (module): 522\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nPygmalion: Henry and Eliza\n\nGeorge Bernard Shaw’s play Pygmalion was the basis for the famous musical My Fair Lady. First performed in 1913, Pygmalion is the story of Eliza Doolittle, a poor and unsophisticated flower seller on the streets of London, who wants to learn to speak “like a lady.” In this passage from Act I, she has met by chance Professor Henry Higgins, a grumpy professor of phonetics, near a church during a rainstorm. They are amongst a group of people who have gathered under the church’s portico to stay dry. Henry has been listening to the other people as part of his research. Eliza was afraid for a time that he was a policeman trying to arrest her and is just beginning to recover from her fear. Henry is “The Note Taker” below. Eliza is “The Flower Girl,” and “The Gentleman” is Colonel Pickering, who was part of the group. The rain has stopped.\n\nThe Flower Girl: Poor girl! Hard enough for her to live without being worried and chivied.\n\nThe Gentleman: [returning to his former place on the note taker’s left] How do you do it, if I may ask? The Note Taker: Simply phonetics. The science of speech. That’s my profession; also my hobby. Happy is the man who can make a living by his hobby! You can spot an Irishman or a Yorkshireman by his brogue. I can place any man within six miles. I can place him within two miles in London. Sometimes within two streets.\n\nThe Flower Girl: Ought to be ashamed of himself, unmanly coward!\n\nThe Gentleman: But is there a living in that?\n\nThe Note Taker: Oh yes. Quite a fat one. This is an age of upstarts. Men begin in Kentish Town with 80 pounds a year, and end in Park Lane with a hundred thousand. They want to drop Kentish Town; but they give themselves away every time they open their mouths. Now! can teach them— The Flower Girl: Let him mind his own business and leave a poor girl— The Note Taker: [explosively] Woman: cease this detestable boohooing instantly; or else seek the shelter of some other place of worship.\n\nThe Flower Girl: [with feeble defiance] I’ve a right to be here if I like, same as you.\n\nThe Note Taker: A woman who utters such depressing and disgusting sounds has no right to be anywhere—no right to live. Remember that you are a human being with a soul and the divine gift of articulate speech: that your native language is the language of Shakespeare and Milton and The Bible; and don’t sit there crooning like a bilious pigeon.\n\nThe Flower Girl: [quite overwhelmed, and looking up at him in mingled wonder and deprecation without daring to raise her head] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow—oo!\n\nThe Note Taker: [whipping out his book] Heavens! what a sound! [He writes; then holds out the book and reads, reproducing her vowels exactly] Ah—ah—ah—ow—ow— ow— oo!\n\nThe Flower Girl: [tickled by the performance, and laughing in spite of herself] Garn!','[18]','assets/images/level4/g6-p18-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(153,10,10,'Practice Passage 9','My First Baseball Game','Read the passage aloud accurately and at a good speed.','Practice Passage 9: “My First Baseball Game” by Randy Ryan\nModule PDF page 19 · Number of words in the passage (module): 257\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\nMy First Baseball Game\n\nI remember my first Cubs game. My uncle came to take my sister and me to it. It was a sunny Saturday. He drove all the way from Indiana to get us. Then we took the train to the ballpark. He said it would be hard to find a parking space there, so he didn’t drive there. He was right. When we got there it was so crowded. We had tickets that were for seats far back. It seemed like I climbed about 300 steps to get to them. But when we got there and I turned around I could see everything. I saw the whole field. It was much better than watching it on TV. Then the game started. Everyone cheered. The man next to me said, “This is going to be the year. They are playing really well.” My uncle said to him, “Let’s hope this is the one.” But then he whispered to us, “Jerome and Felicia, we’ll have to wait and see. Remember last year? They started out well and then they lost at the end.” “Hey, Hey, Hey!” The Cub batter hit a ball far to the wall.\n\nJust then I heard “Hot dogs!” “Peanuts!” I looked and saw a man with a big tray hanging from his shoulders. It was full of hotdogs in plastic wrap and bags of peanuts. “Three hot dogs,” my uncle shouted. Then he gave the man next to us $6. He passed it to the man with the hot dogs.','[19]','assets/images/level4/g6-p19-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(154,10,100,'Post-test Passage','\"Bad Girl\"','Read the passage aloud again to show how much you have grown.','Post-test Passage: “\"Bad Girl\"”\nModule PDF page 21 · Number of words in the passage (module): 269\n\nHOW TO ADMINISTER THE FLUENCY PASSAGE (module pages 6–8, Phil-IRI)\n\n1. Both you and the pupil need a copy of the passage. The pupil reads it aloud once. Record the time the pupil starts and stops reading.\n\n2. Mark every miscue while the pupil reads:\n   • Mispronunciation – underline the word and write how it was said above it. 1 error each (dialect variation is not an error).\n   • Omission – circle the word or phrase left out. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Substitution – underline the word and write the word said above it. 1 error each.\n   • Insertion – use a caret (^) and write the added word/s above it. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Repetition – underline the part repeated. 1 error each word or phrase.\n   • Transposition – mark the words or letters that changed places. 1 error each.\n   • Reversal – write the word/non-word said above the correct word. 1 error each.\n   • Self-correction – write S above the word. NOT an error.\n\n3. Oral Reading Score = (number of words − number of miscues) ÷ number of words × 100\n   Independent 97–100% · Instructional 90–96% · Frustration 89% and below\n   Example: 65 words, 15 miscues → (65 − 15) ÷ 65 × 100 = 76.9%\n\n4. Reading Speed = number of words ÷ reading time in seconds × 60 = words per minute\n   Example: 103 words read in 90 seconds → 69 words per minute\n\nIn BULIG the pupil can press Start reading and I’m done to time the reading; the time and words per minute are saved with the pupil’s response. Speak Answer shows a word-match guide only. Count the miscues yourself from listening.\n\nPASSAGE TEXT\n\n\"Bad Girl\"\n\nHey! Everybody seems to be staring at me..\nYou! You! All of you!\nHow dare you to stare at me?\nWhy? Is it because I\'m a bad girl?\nA bad girl I am, A good for nothing teenager, a\nproblem child?\nThat\'s what you call me!\nI smoke. I drink. I gamble at my young tender\nage.\nI lie. I cheat, and I could even kill, If I have too.\nYes, I\'m a bad girl, but where are my parents?\nYou! You! You are my good parents?\nMy good elder brother and sister in this society\nwhere I live?\nLook…look at me…What have you done to me?\nYou have pampered and spoiled me,neglected me when I needed you\nmost!\nEntrusted me to a yaya, whose intelligence was much lower than mine!\nWhile you go about your parties, your meetings and gambling session…\nThus… I drifted away from you!\nLonging for a father\'s love, yearning for a mother\'s care!\nAs I grew up, everything changed!\nYou too have changed!\nYou spent more time in your poker, mahjong tables, bars and night\nclubs. You even landed on the headlines of the newspaper as crooks,\npeddlers and you racketeers.\nNow, you call me names, accuse me of everything I do to myself?\nTell me! How good are you?\nIf you really wish to ensure my future…\nThen hurry….hurry back home! Where I await you, because I need you…\nProtect me from all evil influences that will threaten at my very own\nunderstanding…\nBut if I am bad, really bad…then, you\'ve got to help me!\nHelp me! Oh please…Help me','[21]','assets/images/level4/g6-p21-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(155,11,1100,'Pre-test','Pre-assessment','Show what you already know about these words and stories.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nPre-assessment · module PDF pages 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 7\nPRETEST (Activity 1-10)\nVocabulary Development (1-10)\nA. Instructions: Draw a line to match the word to the\ncorrect picture.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. Nose\na.\n2. Teddy bear\nb.\n3. Mother\nc.\n4. Shovel\nd.\n5. Chair\ne.\n\nPDF page 8\nB. Instructions: Draw a line to match the meaning to\nthe correct picture.\n1. An animal that barks\na.\n2. Used for writing\nb.\n3. Comes from chicken\nc.\n4. The father of your mother\nd.\nor father\n5. A dish used for holding\ne.\nliquids often with handle\n\nPDF page 9\nA. Listening Comprehension:\nInstructions: Listen carefully as your teacher reads the\nsentences/passage. Then, answer the questions below.\nChoose the letter of the correct answer.\n(Read Aloud) “My Toys” These are my toys. My mother and\nfather gives it to me! I can name my favorite toys.\n1. “My parents gave me something I can throw and catch.\nIt’s round and bouncy. What is it?”\na. Kite\nb. Ball\nc. Doll\nd. Teddy Bear\n2. “I love to play with my toy on the water. It has a sail and\nhelps me pretend I’m a sailor. What am I playing with?\na. Car\nb. Train\nc. Boat\nd. Yoyo\n3. (Read Aloud) “My Pets” These are my pets. I will take\ncare of my pets. I will give them food. I love my pets.\nWrite T if it is true and F if it is false.\n______ A dog can bark.\n______ A fish can fly in the sky\n4. (Read Aloud) “My Things” These are my things. I will take\ncare of my things. I will keep it clean.\n\nPDF page 10\nWhat should you do with your things?\na. Break them\nb. Take care of them\nc. Make it tidy\nWhat does “keep it clean” mean?\na. Make it dirty\nb. Make it new\nc. Make it tidy\n5. (Read Aloud) “My Food” These are the foods I eat. My\nfather buys them for me. My mother cook these for me.\nWho cooks the food in the story?\na. Father\nb. Mother\nc. Friend\n6. (Read Aloud) “My Family” This is my family. My mother\nand my father. My brother and my sister. And our baby\nboy. Who is in the family?\na. Friends\nb. Relatives\nc. Mother, Father, Brother, Sister, and Baby\n7. (Read Aloud) “My Home” This is my home. We have\ntables and chairs in our home. We have rooms and\n\nPDF page 11\nbeds in our home. We have the kitchen and the\ncomfort rooms too. What is the story about?\na. My Garden\nb. My Home\nc. My Kitchen\n8. (Read Aloud) “Our Tools” This is our tools. We have\nshovel and rake. We have to take care of our tools. Tools\nare very important for us. What is the main idea of the\nstory?\na. Tools are fun\nb. Tools are useful and we should take care of\nthem\nc. Tools are expensive\n\nPDF page 12\nPRETEST (Activity 1-10)\nA. Vocabulary Development\nInstructions: Draw a line to match the words in Column A\nwith their meanings/uses /color in Column B.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. Pencil\na. Used to carrying things.\n2. Bag\nb. Cooked bananas on a\nstick\n3. Carrot\nc. Used for writing.\n4. Banana cue\nd. A person who\nworks in a library\n5. Librarian\ne. Its color is orange\n\nPDF page 13\nf. A container for waste\n6. Swing\n7. Trash can\ng. Takes care of sick or\ninjured people in a\nhospital or clinic\nh. A seat hanging from\n8. Nurse\nropes or chains that\nmoves back and forth\n9. Building\ni. A hard board used for\ncutting goods\n10. Chopping board\nj. A structure with a roof\nand walls, such as a\nhouse or School.\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13]','assets/images/level5/g1/p007-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(156,11,2201,'Activity 1','My Body','Listen, learn and use new words about my body.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nMy Body · module PDF pages 15, 16, 17, 18\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 15\nMe and Myself\nlam happy\nlam glad\n| truly feel\nThat | am loved\n\nPDF page 16\nMy Body\nThis is my body.\nGod give this to me!\n| can name the parts of my body.\n\nPDF page 17\nAssessment:\nA. Vocabulary\nInstructions: Draw a line to match the word to the correct\npicture\n1. Eye\n2. Nose\n3. Arm\n4. Head\n5. Mouth\n\nPDF page 18\nA.\nListening Comprehension- Body Part Identification\nInstructions: Point to the following body parts on yourself\nwhen I say them:\n1. Nose\n2. Head\n3. Arm\n4. Eye\n5. Mouth\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[15, 16, 17, 18]','assets/images/level5/g1/p015-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(157,11,3202,'Activity 2','My Toys','Listen, learn and use new words about my toys.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nMy Toys · module PDF pages 19, 20, 21, 22\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 19\nMy Toys\nThese are my toys.\nMy mother and father give it to me!\nI can name my favorite toys\n\nPDF page 20\nA. Vocabulary Assessment\nInstructions: Draw a line to match each word/picture\nto the correct meaning.\n1. Ball\na. A toy with wheels you can ride\n2. Doll\nb. A round toy you can throw and\ncatch\n3. Kite\nc. A toy that looks like a person\n4. Car\nd. A toy that can fly in the wind\n5. Teddy\nBear e. A soft toy you can hug\n\nPDF page 21\nB. Listening Comprehension:\nListen to each question. Write the letter of the correct\nanswer.\n1. “My parents gave me something I can throw and\ncatch. It’s round and bouncy. What is it?”\na. Kite\nb. Ball\nc. Doll\nd. Teddy Bear\n2. “I love to play with my toy on the water. It has a sail\nand helps me pretend I’m a sailor. What am I\nplaying with?\na. Car\nb. Train\nc. Boat\nd. Yoyo\n3. “I have a toy that goes ‘choo-choo’ and runs on\ntracks. What toy is this?”\na. Car\nb. Train\nc. Kite\nd. Yoyo\n4. “This toy is soft and furry, and I love to cuddle with it\nwhen I sleep. Which toy is it?”\na. Ball\nb. Doll\nc. Teddy Bear\nd. Kite\n\nPDF page 22\n5. “I have a toy that I can make go up in the sky when\nthe wind blows. What is it?”\na.\nCar\nb.\nYoyo\nc.\nKite\nd.\nDoll\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[19, 20, 21, 22]','assets/images/level5/g1/p019-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(158,11,4203,'Activity 3','My Pets','Listen, learn and use new words about my pets.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nMy Pets · module PDF pages 23, 24, 25\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 23\nMy Pets\nThese are my pets.\n| will take care of my pets.\n| will give them food.\nllove my pets.\nrabbit fish\ncat\nturtle |\n\nPDF page 24\nAssessment:\nA. Vocabulary\nInstructions: Write the correct letter to match the\npicture to its meaning.\na. A small animal that likes\n______ 1.\nto eat carrots\n.\nb. An animal that barks\n______ 2.\n______ 3.\nc. A pet that swims\n______ 4.\nd. A pet that chirps and\ncan fly\ne. An animal with soft\n______ 5.\nfur that says “meow”\n\nPDF page 25\nB. Listening Development\nInstructions: Listen as your teacher reads each\nsentence. Write T if it is true and F if it is false.\n______ 1. A dog can bark.\n______ 2. A fish can fly in the sky.\n______ 3. A bird can chirp.\n______ 4. A rabbit likes to hop.\n______ 5. A cat can swim in the ocean.\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[23, 24, 25]','assets/images/level5/g1/p023-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(159,11,5204,'Activity 4','My Things','Listen, learn and use new words about my things.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nMy Things · module PDF pages 26, 27, 28\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 26\nMy Things\nThese are my things.\n| will fake care of my things.\n| will keep it clean.\nbook eraser\nsharpener pencil\nruler\n\nPDF page 27\nA. Vocabulary:\nInstruction: Match the school items in column A with\ntheir uses in column B. Write the letter of your answer on\nthe line.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. ______\nBook\na. Used for writing\n2. ______\nEraser\nb. Used for reading stories\n3. ______\nSharpener\nc. Used for measuring lines\n4. ______\nPencil\nd. Used to sharpen pencils\n5. ______\nRuler\ne. Used for erasing mistakes\nB. Listening Comprehension:\nListen carefully as your teacher reads the story “My Things”.\nThen, answer the questions below. Circle the letter of the\ncorrect answer.\n1. What are the things in the story?\na. Toys\nb. School supplies\nc . Fruits\n2. What should you do with your things?\na. Break them\nb Take care of them\nc. Make it tidy\n\nPDF page 28\n3. What does “keep it clean” mean?\na. Make it dirty\nb. Make it new\nc. Make it tidy\n4. Which of these is used for writing?\na. Book\nb. Pencil c. Eraser\n5. Which of these is used for measuring?\na. Bag\nb. Ruler c. Sharpener\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[26, 27, 28]','assets/images/level5/g1/p026-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(160,11,6205,'Activity 5','My Food','Listen, learn and use new words about my food.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nMy Food · module PDF pages 29, 30, 31\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 29\nMy Food\nThese are the foods | eat.\nMy father buys them for me.\nMy mother cook these for me.\n~— Se =e S\nrice banana fish soup\negg meat chicken com\nee\nmango cake bread vegetables\n\nPDF page 30\nA.\nVocabulary:\nInstruction: Match the food in column A with where it\ncomes from in column B. Write the letter of your answer on\nthe line.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. ______\nRice\na. Sea\n2. ______\nBanana\nb. Farm\n3. ______\nFish\nc. Tree\n4. ______\nEgg\nd. Plant\n5. ______\nMeat\ne. Chicken\nB Listening Comprehension:\nListen carefully as your teacher reads the story “My\nFood”. Then, answer the questions below. Circle the\nletter of the correct answer.\n1. Who buys the food in the story?\n\nPDF page 31\na. Mother\nb. Father\nc. Sibling\n2. Who cooks the food in the story?\na. Father\nb. Mother\nc. Friend\n3. Which of these is a fruit?\na. Rice\nb. Banana\nc. Soup\n4. Which of these is a vegetable?\na. Meat\nb. Corn\nc. Cake\n5. Which of these is eaten with rice?\na. Cake\nb. Egg\nc. Mango\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[29, 30, 31]','assets/images/level5/g1/p029-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(161,11,7206,'Activity 6','My Family','Listen, learn and use new words about my family.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nMy Family · module PDF pages 32, 33, 34, 35\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 32\nMe and My Family\nWe are happy\nWe are glad\nWe truly feel good\nWe are loved\n\nPDF page 33\nMy Family\nThis is my family.\nMy mother and my father.\nMy brother and my sister.\nAnd our baby boy.\nbaby\na as\nrm a wh\nbrother father mother sister\n\nPDF page 34\nVocabulary:\nA.\nInstruction: Match the family member in Column A\nwith their description In Column B. Write the letter of your\nanswer on the line.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. ______\nMother\na. A male sibling\n2. ______\nFather\nb. A female sibling\n3. ______\nBrother\nc. A female parent\n4. ______\nSister\nd. A male parent\n5. ______\nBaby\ne. A very young child\n\nPDF page 35\nB. Listening Comprehension:\nListen carefully as your teacher reads the story “My Family”.\nThen, answer the questions below. Circle the letter of the correct\nanswer.\n1. Who is in the family?\na. Friends\nb. Relatives\nc. Mother, Father, Brother, Sister, and Baby\n2. Who are the parents?\na. Brother and Sister\nb. Mother and Father\nc. Baby and Brother\n3. What are the siblings?\na. Mother and Father\nb. Brother and Sister\nc. Baby and Parents\n4. Who is the youngest member of the family?\na. Brother\nb. Sister\nc. Baby\n5. What is the story about?\na. School\nb. Family\nc. Toys\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[32, 33, 34, 35]','assets/images/level5/g1/p032-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(162,11,8207,'Activity 7','My Relatives','Listen, learn and use new words about my relatives.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nMy Relatives · module PDF pages 36, 37, 38\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 36\nMy Relatives\nThese are my relatives.\nMy uncle and my aunts.\nMy grandfather and grandmother.\nMy nephew and my niece.\n\nPDF page 37\nA. Vocabulary:\nInstruction: Match the family member in Column A\nwith their description In Column B. Write the letter of your\nanswer on the line.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. ______\nUncle\na. The father of your\nmother or father\n2. ______\nAunt\nb. The mother of your\nmother or father\n3. ______\nGrandfather\nc. The son of your\nbrother or sister\n4. ______\nGrandmother\nd. The brother of your\nmother or father\n5. ______\nNephew\ne. The sister of your\nmother or father\nB. Listening Comprehension:\nListen carefully as your teacher reads the story “My\nRelatives”. Then, answer the questions below. Circle the\nletter of the correct answer.\n1. Who are the relatives mentioned in the story?\na. Friends\nb. Neighbors\n\nPDF page 38\nc. Uncle, Aunt, Grandfather, Grandmother,\nNephew, Niece\n2. Who are the grandparents?\na. Uncle and Aunt\nb. Grandfather and Grandmother\nc. Nephew and Niece\n3. What is an uncle?\na. Your parent’s brother\nb. Your parent’s sister\nc. Your sibling’s child\n4. What is an aunt?\na. Your parent’s brother\nb. Your parent’s sister\nc. Your sibling’s child\n5. What are the nephews and nieces?\na. Grandparents\nb. Parents\nc. Children of your siblings\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[36, 37, 38]','assets/images/level5/g1/p036-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(163,11,9208,'Activity 8','My Home','Listen, learn and use new words about my home.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nMy Home · module PDF pages 39, 40, 41\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 39\nMy Home\nThis is my home.\nWe have tables and chairs in our home.\nWe have rooms and beds in our home.\nWe have the kitchen and the comfort\nrooms too.\ncomfort room kitchen\nbed\ntable chair\n\nPDF page 40\nA. Vocabulary:\nInstructions: Draw a line to match each word to the correct\npicture.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. Table\na.\n2. Chair\nb.\n3. Comfort room\nc.\n4. Bed\nd.\n5. Kitchen\ne.\n\nPDF page 41\nB. Listening Comprehension:\nListen carefully as your teacher reads the story “My\nHome”. Then, answer the questions below. Circle the\nletter of the correct answer.\n1. What is the story about?\na. My Garden\nb. My Home\nc. My Kitchen\n2. What furniture is mentioned in the story?\na. Comfort room\nb. Bedroom\nc. Tables and chairs\n3. What rooms are mentioned in the story?\na. Bedroom, comfort room,\nb. Chair\nc. Table\n4. What do we have in our rooms?\na. Beds\nb. Comfort room\nc. Kitchen\n5. Where do we cook food?\na. Bedroom\nb. Chair\nc. Kitchen\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[39, 40, 41]','assets/images/level5/g1/p039-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(164,11,10209,'Activity 9','Our Tools','Listen, learn and use new words about our tools.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nOur Tools · module PDF pages 42, 43, 44\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 42\nOur Tools\nThis is our tools.\nWe have shovel and rake.\nWe have fo take care of our tools.\nTools are very important for us.\nreilen Clippers\nhoe\nGarden hose\naN\nSeed packet pot\n\nPDF page 43\nA. Vocabulary:\nInstructions: Draw a line to match each word to\nthe correct picture.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1. Shovel\na.\n2. Rake\nb.\n3. Tools\nc.\nB. Listening Comprehension:\nListen carefully as your teacher reads the story\n“Our Tools”. Then, answer the questions below.\nCircle the letter of the correct answer.\n1. What is the story about?\na. Our toys\nb).Our tools\nc. Our books\n\nPDF page 44\n2. What tools are mentioned in the story?\na. Shovel and hammer\nb. Rake and saw\nc. Shovel and rake\n3. What should we do with our tools?\na. Break them\nb. Take care of them\nc. Lose them\n4. Why are tools important?\na. They are not important\nb. They are very important for us\nc. They are for playing\n5. . What is the main idea of the story?\na. Tools are fun\nb. Tools are useful and we should take care of\nthem\nc. Tools are expensive\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[42, 43, 44]','assets/images/level5/g1/p042-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(165,11,11210,'Activity 10','Our Kitchen','Listen, learn and use new words about our kitchen.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 1\nOur Kitchen · module PDF pages 45, 46, 47, 48\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 45\nOur Kitchen\nThis is our kitchen.\nWe cook our food in our kitchen.\nWe have plates, forks and spoons.\nWe have pans, bowls, and cups too.\neS; =\nspatula\n\nPDF page 46\nA. Vocabulary:\nInstructions: Draw a line to match the kitchen item in\nColumn A with its correct description in Column B.\nColumn A\nColumn B\n1.\nPan\na. Used for stirring or\nserving food\n2.\nSpatula\nb. A dish used for holding\nliquids, often with a handle\n3.\nBowl\nc. A flat utensil used for\nlifting or flipping food\n4.\nCup\nd. A round shallow\ndish used for Cooking\n5.\nSpoon\ne. A deep, round dish\nused for mixing or\nholding food\n\nPDF page 47\nB. Listening Comprehension:\nInstructions: Listen carefully as your teacher reads the\nstory “Our Kitchen”. Then, answer the questions below.\nCircle the letter of the correct answer.\n1. Where are we?\na. Bedroom\nb. Kitchen\nc. Garden\nd. Living room\n2. What do we do in the kitchen?\na. Sleep\nb. Cook food\nc. Play games\nd. Watch TV\n3. Which of these items do we have?\na. Plates, forks, and knives\nb. Plates, spoons, and cups\nc. Plates, forks, and spoons\nd. Bowls, cups, and knives\n4. Besides plates, forks, and spoons, what else do\nwe have?\na. Pans, bowls, and cups\nb. Tables, chairs, and beds\nc. Books, toys, and games\nd. Cars, bikes, and scooters\n\nPDF page 48\n5. What is Not mentioned as being in the kitchen?\na. Spoons\nb. Bowls\nc. Knives\nd. Cups\n\nThe module has no answer key for this grade.','[45, 46, 47, 48]','assets/images/level5/g1/p045-1.webp',1,'2026-1