Tug \nstrap \nMoney \nCash \nsource \nPaint \nTint \nattempt \nMovie \nShow \ndinner \nTry \nAttempt \ntime \nSupper \nDinner \ntug \nCause \nSource \nshow \nWhen \nTime \ntint \nCrop \nYield \nyield \n \n \n \nActivity 13: Antonyms \n \nSet A \nSet B \nalways \nthrough \nsometimes \nconstantly \nmorning \nthe past \nafternoon \nsunrise \neveryday \nby-and-by \nfinally \ndaily \ntomorrow \nlastly \nStart \noccasionally \nyesterday \nafter lunch \nlater \nfuture \nmorning \nadvanced \n5\nPDF page 67\nActivity 14: Word Puzzle \n \nP \nC \nW \nA \nN \nS \nW \nE \nR \nA \nR \nH \nA \nM \nO\nU \nN \nT \nR \nM \nO \nS \nQ \nU \nA \nR \nE \nT \nE \nL \nB \nX \nT \nZ \nW \nV \nL \nD \nE \nG \nL\nH \nU \nG \nE \nC \nI \nR \nC \nL \nE \nJ \nI \nQ \nO \nU \nC \nO \nE \nH \nM \nK \nU \nI \nM \nF \nS \nL \nO \nD \nS \nA \nN \nO \nI \nT \nC \nA \nR \nF \nL \n \nActivity 15: Arranging Letters \n \n1 \nr e s f o t \nforest \n2 \nr e r o c t c \ncorrect \n3 \ns o w d r \nwords \n4 \nk r i d n \ndrink \n5 \nr a g r p h a p a \nparagraph \n6 \ne n s t e n e c \nsentence \n7 \ne d a r a p \nparade \n8 \nt h r a e \nearth \n9 \nt a c h \nchat \n10 \nw r a d \ndraw \n11 \nn o s e ra \nreason \n12 \ny o s t r \nstory \n \nActivity 16: Correctly Spelled \n \n1. Ant \n6. animal \n2. Bug \n7. world \nDuring \nbegin\nPDF page 68\n3. kitten \n8. home \n4. everybody \n9. teeth \n5. deer \n10. Tongue \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nActivity 20: Sentence Completion \n \n \nfavorite \nbeautiful \nnotice \nsince \nnecessary \npleasure \nthought \nbetter \nsuppose \nwritten \nperhaps \ngeneral \n \n1. My favorite fruit is mango. \n2. Teachers notice to the parents about the meeting. \n3. It is better to take care of ourselves. \n4. Watching a game gave me a great pleasure \n5. He has two works since he graduated. \n6. You’d better back off. \n7. The book provide general information of the subject. \n8. Farmers perhaps not interested in the new Farming. \n9. My name is written on the blackboard. \n10. I suppose that they are not ready for the class. \n11. Do you have any thought for the lesson? \n12. She has the most beautiful  smile. \n \n \nPre - Assessment (Activity 11-20) \n \nVocabulary Development \nListening Comprehension \n \n1. Body \n11. lap \n2. feather \n12. hand \n3. Bill \n13. east \n4. eye \n14. pie \n5. finger \n15. tail \n \nB. \nContext Clues \nListening Comprehension(Passage) \n \n6. ant \n16. Lila goes to the park with her dog, Max . \n \n7. kitten \n17. Max is brown. \n \n8. everybody \n18. Lila swings on the swings and plays on \nthe slide.\nPDF page 69\n9. deer \n19. She brings a small snack and a bottle of \nwater. \n10. umbrella \n20. She watches birds in the trees and feeds \nthe ducks near the pond . \n \n \n \n \nPost - Assessment (Activity 11-20) \n \n1. bill \n11. pie \n2. body \n12. hand \n3. fingers \n13. east \n4. eye \n14. lap \n5. feather \n15. tail \n6. ant \n16. Lila swings on the swings and plays on the \nslide \n7. umbrella \n17. she watches birds in the trees and feeds \nthe ducks near the pond \n8. everybody \n18. Lila goes to the park with her dog, Max \n9. deer \n19. Max is brown \n10. kitten \n20. She brings a small snack and a bottle of \nwater','[11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16]','assets/images/level5/g4/p011-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(226,14,3201,'Activity 1','Point Act Game','Listen, learn and use new words about point act game.','Listening Comprehension and Vocabulary Development · Level 5 · Grade 4\nPoint Act Game · module PDF page 18\n\nPupils see each module page with its text and pictures. Listen reads the page aloud; for listening items read the passage yourself if you prefer. Pupils type or say their numbered answers; check them with the answer key below and give feedback.\n\nPDF page 18\nPoint Act Game\nActivity 1\nDirections: Pick a word card inside the box or bag. Read the\nword. Find the words inside the classroom. Read, point, act\nand write them on the board (e.g. half ½, 9, 11, etc.) Do it again\nand again.\nwhite\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 50\nGrade 4 Lesson Guide \n \nTitle \nPoint Act Game \nDay \n1 \nObjective/s \nBe able to understand words of numbers and colors. \nMaterials \nwordcard, box/bag, pen/pointer, others \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \nPick square word card from a box or bag. Read the word card and \ndo it by pointing them inside the classroom or write them on the \nboard (e.g. half ½, 9, 11, etc.) Do it again and again. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \ncolor \ngray \neight \neleven \nhalf \nhuge \nhundred \nlarge \neven \nnumber \nnine \npiece \nwhite \n \n \nTitle \nFind and Color Me \nDay \n2 \nObjective/s \nBe able pupils to understand adverbs through color and finding \nthem out \nMaterials \nworksheet, coloring materials \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to Do: \nThe teacher  prepares  the words in the worksheet. \nLet the learners find or search the words in the worksheet. Let \nthem figure out the word base on the list. Inform them that the \nwords may appear vertically, diagonally and horizontally, and \ncolor theses crayon or pen. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nleft \n  else \n  gone \n  full \ncount \n clean \n right \nheavy \nside \nenough \ndifferent \n page \n \n \nTitle \nNaming Me \nDay \n3 \nObjective/s \nBe able to search noun words to improve vocabulary and \nlanguage learning .\nPDF page 51\nMaterials \nsearch puzzles/Worksheets \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \n1. Have the teachers prepare a word search worksheet . \nDirection: Instruct the learners to find and encircle the words in \nthe worksheet which they are found in the box. The words \nappear horizontally,diagonally or vertically. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nplace \n address \n city \n country \ncamp \nriver \nfield \nland \npond \nsand \nsea \nsky \n \n \nTitle \nHidden Letters \nDay \n4 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help pupils search and practice person words in a \nrepetitive fashion by filling in the missing letters and slow down \nand focus on the task at hand. . \nMaterials \nWorksheets \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \n1. Have teachers prepared a worksheet . \n2. Instruct learners to fill in the missing letter in the repetitive \nwords in the activity sheet. Words to write can be seen below \nof the activity sheet. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \npeople \n  family \n himself \n king \nMiss \nbrother \nchildren \nfriend \nmother  neighbour   queen\nPDF page 52\nTitle \nJumbled Letters \nDay \n5 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students foster their persistence, working memory \nand extend their vocabulary and their skills in asking them to \nunscramble the jumbled letters of things using the word definitions \nas clues. \nMaterials \nWorksheets \nDirection/ \nProcedure/ \nInstruction \nWhat to do: \n1. Worksheets will be provided by the teacher. \n2. Have learners unscramble the words given in the worksheet. \n3. Have the learners refer their answer on the word definition \nbelow. \n4. Write your answer in the blank provided \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nwords bowl \nbrick \ncap \ncase \ncup \ndesk \nknife \npail \npen \npencil \npie \nplate \npot \n \n \nTitle \nLink the Letters \nDay \n6 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students boost context fluency and improve their \nunderstanding of the meaning of words by finding vocabulary \nwords in a grid of letters, using the definitions of words as clues \nMaterials \nWorksheets \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \n1. The teacher provides the worksheet. \n2. Have the learners solve the vocabulary words in a grid of \nletters. \n3. For each question, a grid letter is presented. \n4. Let them locate the words within the grid and draw a line \nfrom square to square to link the letters of the words. \n5. \nUse the definition of words as clues. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nbeans \nbanana \nleaf \nleaves  peas \nplant \ngift \ngold \nradio \nrock \nroof \ndust \n \nTitle \nSpiral Puzzle \nDay \n7\nPDF page 53\nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words by spinning on the classic crossword, students fill \nin these puzzles using definitions and words as clues. \nMaterials \nWorksheets/ crossword \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: Instruct learners to; \n1. Use the clues to solve the spiral puzzle. \n2. Use this different spin on the classic crossword \n3. Students fill in these puzzles using definitions and words as \nclues below the Worksheet. \n4. Worksheet will be provided by teacher \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nlisten nap \nshut \nslip \nshine \nslow \nmark \nlearn \nquite \nkiss \nknock \nsend \nshake \n \n \nTitle \nCrossword \nDay \n8 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words by practicing their vocabulary and spelling skills \nwith the past verb. \nMaterials \nWorksheets/ crossword \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \nTeachers instruct learners to; \n1. Solve the crossword puzzles \n2. Crossword puzzle are created to help students practice their \nvocabulary skills by shading the words. \n3. Use color pen or crayons to answer. \n4. Answer sheets are provided. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nflew \n hid \nbit  \ndead  \nshook \nseen \nsent \nfought \ngrew \nkept \nhung \nfelt \n \nTitle \nOpposite Words \nDay \n9 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words by choosing words of opposite meanings from a \nword bank to match against a list of words. \nMaterials \nWorksheets, opposite meaning words \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nWhat to do: \n1. Teachers have list of words with the opposite meaning. \n2. Instruct learners to choose words of opposite meanings from a \nword bank to match against a list of words in the worksheets\nPDF page 54\nInstructions \n3. Write your answer in the blank provided. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nplant \n press \n \npay  \nmarch \n kill \nlead \nkick \nhang \nborn \ndry  care \nbusy \ndream \n \n \nTitle \nPrep Paired \nDay \n10 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words by completing the sentences with words from a \nword bank. \nMaterials \nVocabulary worksheets, sentences \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \nThe teacher lets the learners; \n1. Read the words listed in the word bank. \n2. Complete the sentences with words from a word bank \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nabove \nalmost \nbegan \nbeside \noutside east \nago \n across \n between center  \nfront  under \n \nTitle \nFill in the Blanks \nDay \n11 \nObjective/s \nBe able to understand the meaning of different parts of animals \nand human. \nMaterials \nWorksheets, Activity Sheet \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \nTeacher gives to learners the following directions: \n1. Read the given words in the worksheet. \n2. Instruct the learners to answer the activity. \n3. Write the correct answer in the blank. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nbody \nbone \neye \nbill feather chin \nfoot \nhair \nlap \nmeat \nmouth \nfinger \nskin\nPDF page 55\nTitle \nSynonyms \nDay \n12 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words by matching the words synonyms a word bank. \nMaterials \nWorksheets: match the words \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \nInstruction given by teacher to learners; \n1. Choose the synonyms of words with similar meanings. \n2. In the worksheet, students match the words from the word \nbank with their synonyms.. \n4.  Connect the words with a line. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nbelt \n money picture  paint \ncover  \npicture \nwhen \nmovie \npull \ntried supper \ncause \n \nTitle \nAntonyms \nDay \n13 \nObjective/s \nBe able learners to understand adverbs. \nMaterials \nFlashcards/Chart, activity sheet, pen, etc. \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \nTeacher presents the following words in a chart or flashcard. \n1. Have the children read. \n2. Have another set of words (synonym of the given words). \n3. Let them read. Do the reading two times. \n4. Give them an activity sheet; let them connect the antonym of \nthe words. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nAlways yesterday during later tomorrow morning after everyday \nsometimes afternoon finally start \n \n \nTitle \nWord Puzzle \nDay \n14 \nObjective/s \nBe able to understand words related to mathematics through \nword puzzle. \nMaterials \nActivity Sheet word puzzle\nPDF page 56\nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \nTeacher instruct the following to learners; \n1. Find the words listed below. \n2. Circle the words from the list below. \n3. Words may appear horizontally, vertically but appear forward. \n4. Do it in the activity sheet. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nstart south  \narea \nproblem medium \nhuge \nfraction \nequal  cost \n answer Circle \n whole \namount square \n \n \nTitle \nArranging Letter \nDay \n15 \nObjective/s \nBe able learners to understand words with jumble and forming it \nby arranging letters to give correct word. \nMaterials \nFlashcards/Chart, activity sheet, pen, etc. \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \n1. The teacher prepared worksheet. \n2. The learners do the activity. \n3. The letters (words) in the worksheet are jumbled. \n4. Let the learners figure out the word base on the list below. \n5. Write the word in the blank provided. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nstory forest parade \nwords  earth \nChat \nsentence \ncorrect draw  \nreason drink \nparagraph \n \n \nTitle \nCorrectly Spelled \nDay \n16 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words by writing correct spelling of words order from a \nword bank. \nMaterials \nWorksheets: list the words \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \n1. Teacher prepare the activity sheet. \n2. Children learn to spell words correctly dictate by the teacher. \nwords are given ahead. \n3. In the worksheet, students are asked to write words taken from \na word bank in correct spelling. \n4. Activity sheet will be provided.\nPDF page 57\nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nAnt \nbug \nkitten everybody \ndeer \nanimal \nworld \nhome  teeth \ntongue umbrella company \n \nTitle \nWord Roll \nDay \n17 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words through game \nMaterials \nWorksheets: list the words, 3 dice with words \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \n1. Materials are prepared by teacher. \n2. Children are given worksheet. \n3. The list of words can be seen on the worksheet. \n4. Roll the dice and look for the number and letters. \n5. Have them read the words and check or cross them out. \n6. Do the game over and over until all words will be checked. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nPen \n follow \nanother \nthink together toward experience \nthank pretty \n happy however because practice condition \n \n \nTitle \nPair a Word \nDay \n18 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words through word pairing. \nMaterials \nWord in worksheet with word in a card - pairing (wordcard – \n2 set \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \n1. List of words are seen in the worksheet that the teacher \nprepared. \n2. Set of word cards from a box is also use. \n3. Have learners read each words and pair it with a word in the \ncard. \n4. Do it again and again until all words are paired. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \nair \nairplane \ncreek  early \nhall \npath \nlake \ncloud \ncool \nfresh  breakfast chimney \nbuilding \n \nTitle \nBe Bingo \nDay \n19\nPDF page 58\nObjective/s \nBe able to help students improve their understanding of the \nmeaning of words through a bingo game. \nMaterials \nBingo Word cards, dicer, pen \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \n1. Teacher gives a a copy of poster for the vocabulary as well as \na new bingo card to the learners. \n2. Instruct them to pick a particular number and attach them to \ntheir blank bingo card. \n3. Alternatively, they could either writes or draws the vocabulary. \n4. Store the entire set of posters into a hat and pick them out one \nby one. \n5. The first child to tick all the items on their card shouts ‘Bingo’ \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary) \ndivision \ndevelop \ndirect \ndescribe \ndeal \neffect \nseveral \nexample important \nplease question \nremember \nnothing \n \n \nTitle \nSentence Complete \nDay \n20 \nObjective/s \nBe able to help students apply their vocabulary learning than \nlearning words by dictionary definitions in context in sentence. \nMaterials \nVocabulary worksheets (completing sentences \nDirections/ \nProcedures/ \nInstructions \nWhat to do: \nThe teacher gives the children a copy of posters for the \nvocabulary Learners are given the instruction below; \n1. Read the sentences in the worksheet. \n2. Perform the activity by putting words in context in the \nsentences. \n3. In the worksheets, select the correct word of three options for \neach sentence. \n4. Write the correct answer in the blank. \nActivity Words \n(Vocabulary \nfavorite beautiful notice \nsince  necessary pleasure \nthought better suppose written \nperhaps genera \n \n \n \n \n \nAnswer Key \nActivity 2: Find and Color it. \n \nq k X h \ne \na \nv \ny \ns\nPDF page 59\nf \nu L l \nl \ng \nr \no \nw \no h E y \ns \nb k \nu \na \nd i \nF f \ne \nr \ne \nn \nt \nm b T g \ns \ni \nd \ne \np \nw e N o \nu \ng \nh \nt \na \nc \no U n \nt \nh o \nR \ng \nc \nl \nE e n \nt \nt \ns \ne \n \n \nleft \nelse \ngone \nfull \ncount \nclean \nright \nheavy \nside \nenough \ndifferent \npage \n \n \nActivity 3: Naming Me \n \na l p w h i d j \nd c o u n t r y \nd i n l o b i k \nr t d a t e v s \ne y a n e c e s \ns a n d g a r e \ns m f i e l d a\nPDF page 60\no v c a m p f t \n \n \n \nplace city \ncountry camp \nriver \nfield \nland \npond \nsand \nsea \nsky \naddress \n \n \n \n \nActvity 4:Hidden Letters \n \n \n \n \n \n \nC \nh \ni \nl \nd \nr \ne \nn\nC \nh \ni \nl \nd \nr \ne \nn\nC \nh \ni \nl \nd \nr \ne \nn\nC \nh \ni \nl \nr \ne \nn\nC \nh \ni \nl \nd \nr \ne \nn\nn \ne \ni g h \nb \no \nu \nr \nn \ne \ni g h \nb \no \nu \nr \nn \ne \ni g h \nb \no \nu \nr \nn \ne \ni g h \nb \no \nu \nr \nn \ne \ni g h \nb \no \nu \nr \np \ne \no \np \nl \ne \np \ne \no \np \nl \ne \np \ne \no \np \nl \ne \np \ne \no \np \nl \ne \np \ne \no \np \nl \ne \nh \ni \nm \ns \ne \nl \nf \nh \ni \nm \ns \ne \nl \nf \nh \ni \nm \ns \ne \nl \nf \nh \ni \nm \ns \ne \nl \nf \nh \ni \nm \ns \ne \nl \nf \nf \na \nm \ni \nl \ny \nf \na \nm \ni \nl \ny \nf \na \nm \ni \nl \ny \nf \na \nm \ni \nl \ny \nf \nm \ni \ny \nb \nr \no \nt \nh \ne \nr \nb \nr \no \nt \nh \ne \nr \nb \nr \no \nt \nh \ne \nr \nb \nr \no \nt \nh \ne \nr \nb \nr \no \nt \nh \ne \nr \nm o \nt \nh e r \nm o \nt \nh e r \nf \nr \ni \ne \nn \nd \nf \nr \ni \ne \nn \nd \nf \nr \ni \ne \nn \nd \nk \ni \nn \ng \nK \ni \nn \ng \nk \ni \nn \ng\nPDF page 61\nfruit with soft and \npulpy flesh \n \n \n