(376,19,21220,'Activity 20','One Sunday Morning · Just Pray','Predicting Outcomes: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 3\nActivity 20: One Sunday Morning · Just Pray · skill: Predicting Outcomes · module PDF pages 53, 54\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nOne Sunday Morning (PDF page 53)\nDirection: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nOne Sunday Morning\nIt was Sunday Morning. The church bells were ringing.\n\n“There is no need to hurry anyway,” Benilda said to herself as she tossed in bed. “I’ll just get up when I like. Sunday is a rest day,” she thought.\n\nSuddenly she got up. She remembered that she should be in church early. She would see Linda there. They were going to a party together.\n\n“Oh my, why did I forget?” Benilda said looking at the big clock.\n\nWhat do you think did Linda do when Benilda did not come?\nA. She attended the mass alone.\nB. She went to a party instead.\nC. She played in the church.\n\nHow do you think did Benilda feel?\nA. She was happy.\nB. She was sorry.\nC. She was sleepy.\n\nWhat was she likely to do?\nA. Go to mass alone, too.\nB. See a show.\nC. Go back to sleep.\n\nJust Pray (PDF page 54)\nDirection: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nJust Pray\nMrs. Soriano’s house was very quiet. The people inside seemed to be talking in whispers. But everyone seemed to be actively moving around.\n\nSuddenly an ambulance stopped in front of the house. With it came a doctor and a nurse. Soon the eldest girl in the family came out holding two pillows. She was followed by her brother holding a bag and a blanket. Then a patient in the stretcher was placed in the ambulance.\n\nwhat was the ambulance for?\nA. to distribute medicines\nB. to give free injections\nC. to get a sick person\n\nWho was the sick person?\nA. the eldest girl\nB. Mother\nC. the boy\n\nHow did this story end?\nA. Mother was brought to the hospital.\nB. the eldest girl died.\nC. the ambulance stayed in front of the house\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 64\n60  \nReferences:  \nDeveloping Reading Power 3  \nJack\'s Birdhouse - Level D Children\'s Story | K5 Learning The \nMissing Doll (k5learning.com)  \nMarty the Clownfish (k5learning.com)  \nlevel-g-childrens-story.pdf (k5learning.com) \nAnswer Key:  \nA Rainy Day  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. C  \n4. B  \n5. B  \nA Gift from Uncle \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. C  \n4. B  \n5. B  \nTaking Care of  \nAnimals  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. C  \n4. B  \n5. C  \nLost and Found \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. C  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nThe Banana   \nPeelings  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. A  \n4. C  \n5. C  \nDay 6 – Getting  \nthe General  \nSignificance  \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. C  \nDay 7 - Getting  \nthe General  \nSignificance  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \nDay 8 - Getting  \nthe General  \nSignificance  \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \nDay 9 - Getting  \nthe General  \nSignificance  \n1. A   \n1. B  \n2. B   \n2. B  \n3. A   \n4. B  \nDay 10 - Getting  \nthe General   \nSignificance  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. C  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. B\n61  \nDay 14- Drawing   \nDay 18- Predicting  \nDay 11 – Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. A  \n1. A  \n2. B\n\nPDF page 65\n3. A  \nDay 12 - Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. C  \nDay 13 - Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. C  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. A  \n3. C \nConclusion  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. A  \n2. C  \n3. C  \nDay 15 - Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. B  \nDay 16 – Predicting  \nOutcomes  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. A  \n3. C  \nDay 17 - Predicting  \nOutcomes  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. A  \nOutcomes  \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. B  \nDay 19 - Predicting  \nOutcomes  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. B  \nDay 20 - Predicting  \nOutcomes  \n1. A  \n2. A  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. B \n62  \nPre Test Answer Key (   \nPre and Post Answer   \nNothing Details)  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n4. C  \n5. B  \nPre test SKILL B- Getting the  \nGeneral Significance  \nAnswer Key  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nPre test SKILL C- Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nPre test SKILL D  \nPredicting Outcomes   \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nKey ( Nothing Details) \n1.B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n4. C  \n5. B  \nPre and Post test SKILL B\n\nPDF page 66\nGetting the General   \nSignificance  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nPre and Post test SKILL C \nDrawing Conclusion  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nPre and Post test SKILL D \nPredicting Outcomes   \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A','[53, 54]','assets/images/level6/g3/p053-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(377,19,22300,'Post-test','Reading comprehension test','Show how much your reading comprehension has grown.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 3\nPost-test: Post-test · skill: Noting Details · module PDF pages 56, 57, 58, 59, 60, 61, 62, 63\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nGrowing Vegetables (PDF pages 56, 57)\nDirections: Read the story carefully. Choose the correct answer for each question and encircle the letter.\n\nGrowing Vegetables\nLino went with mother to market. They bought some vegetables. Mother said, “I was able to buy only some with my money. Vegetables cost much now.” Lino was sorry for Mother. They did not have much money. So, he said, “Let’s plant vegetables in our backyard. Then we will not have to buy them in the market.”\n\nSo, Lino planted three plots of tomatoes, two plots of eggplants and a plot of pechay. He had some ampalaya climbing on the bamboo fence. Lino worked in his garden after class every afternoon. He grew more vegetables than they could eat. Mother sold some of them in the market.\n\nWhere did Lino and Mother go?\nA. To the farm\nB. To the market\nC. To school\nD. To the park\n\nWhat did they buy at the market?\nA. Fruits\nB. Clothes\nC. Vegetables\nD. Toys\n\nWhy did Lino suggest planting vegetables in their backyard?\nA. To make their backyard look nice\nB. Because vegetables were expensive at the market\nC. To sell vegetables to neighbors\nD. To have fun in the garden\n\nHow many plots of tomatoes did Lino plant?\nA. One\nB. Two\nC. Three\nD. Four\n\nWhich vegetable was climbing on the bamboo fence?\nA. Pechay\nB. Ampalaya\nC. Eggplant\nD. Tomato\n\nFowls (PDF pages 58, 59)\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice.\n\nFowls\nFowls are animals. They are animals with two feet and two wings. Their bodies are covered with feathers. They give us eggs. Their meat is good to eat. Chickens, ducks, turkeys and geese are fowls. They eat grains such as palay and corn. Chickens like worms. Ducks and geese like to swim. They like to stay in the water They are water birds, so they have webbed feet. Turkeys have beautiful feathers. The male turkey spreads its tail like a fan. Geese are good watchers. They make loud noises when people come near. Fowls are good to raise. Many people get rich because of poultry raising.\n\nWhat is the main idea of the passage?\nA. Fowls are animals that give eggs and meat.\nB. Fowls only eat grains.\nC. Turkeys have beautiful feathers.\n\nWhy are fowls considered useful to humans?\nA. They make loud noises.\nB. They give eggs, meat, and can make people rich through poultry raising.\nC. They like to swim in water.\n\nWhat type of birds are ducks and geese?\nA. Land birds\nB. Water birds with webbed feet\nC. Birds with colorful feathers\n\nWhy do geese make loud noises?\nA. To call other geese\nB. To warn people or act as watchers\nC. To scare chickens\n\nWhat is the general significance of raising fowls?\nA. It provides food and livelihood for people\nB. It helps fowls grow beautiful feathers\nC. It teaches birds to swim\n\nThe First Monkey (PDF pages 60, 61)\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice.\n\nThe First Monkey\nLong time ago, there was a farmer who had a wide corn field. He grew big and sweet corn. One day he saw some of his corn gone. Many corn plants had no more fruits. He wanted to catch whoever stole his corn.\n\nThe next day he hid behind a big mango tree. He carried a stick. He watched and watched until he saw a man coming. The man passed through a broken part of the fence. The man began to pick some corn.\n\nThe farmer went from behind the mango tree and walked towards the man. The farmer ran after him, but he could not catch up with him. Then he threw the stick at the man. The stick hit the man’s back and became a tail. The man went up the tree. He became smaller and smaller until he became a monkey.\n\nWhen did the story most likely take place?\nA. Last week\nB. Long time ago\nC. Tomorrow\n\nWhat did the farmer have?\nA. A mango tree\nB. A corn field\nC. A garden\n\nWhat words describe the farmer’s corn?\nA. Small and sour\nB. Big and sweet\nC. Rotten and old\n\nWhat happened to the farmer’s corn?\nA. It was eaten by animals\nB. Some of it was gone and plants had no fruits\nC. It was harvested for sale\n\nWhere did the farmer hide to catch the thief?\nA. Behind a mango tree\nB. In a corn plot\nC. On the roof\n\nThe Bees (PDF pages 62, 63)\nDirections: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the tree with the hive, what could be the result for the bees?\nA. They would continue making honey in the same place.\nB. They might have to find a new place to live.\nC. The queen bee would sting the young bees.\nD. The workers would become lazy.\n\nIf the bees did not make honey from nectar, what might happen?\nA. The young bees might go hungry.\nB. The drones would start collecting nectar.\nC. The hive would produce more nectar.\nD. The workers would leave the swarm.\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 64\n60  \nReferences:  \nDeveloping Reading Power 3  \nJack\'s Birdhouse - Level D Children\'s Story | K5 Learning The \nMissing Doll (k5learning.com)  \nMarty the Clownfish (k5learning.com)  \nlevel-g-childrens-story.pdf (k5learning.com) \nAnswer Key:  \nA Rainy Day  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. C  \n4. B  \n5. B  \nA Gift from Uncle \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. C  \n4. B  \n5. B  \nTaking Care of  \nAnimals  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. C  \n4. B  \n5. C  \nLost and Found \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. C  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nThe Banana   \nPeelings  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. A  \n4. C  \n5. C  \nDay 6 – Getting  \nthe General  \nSignificance  \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. C  \nDay 7 - Getting  \nthe General  \nSignificance  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \nDay 8 - Getting  \nthe General  \nSignificance  \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \nDay 9 - Getting  \nthe General  \nSignificance  \n1. A   \n1. B  \n2. B   \n2. B  \n3. A   \n4. B  \nDay 10 - Getting  \nthe General   \nSignificance  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. C  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. B\n61  \nDay 14- Drawing   \nDay 18- Predicting  \nDay 11 – Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. A  \n1. A  \n2. B\n\nPDF page 65\n3. A  \nDay 12 - Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. C  \nDay 13 - Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. C  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. A  \n3. C \nConclusion  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. A  \n2. C  \n3. C  \nDay 15 - Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. B  \nDay 16 – Predicting  \nOutcomes  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n1. B  \n2. A  \n3. C  \nDay 17 - Predicting  \nOutcomes  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. A  \n3. A  \nOutcomes  \n1. C  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. B  \nDay 19 - Predicting  \nOutcomes  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. B  \nDay 20 - Predicting  \nOutcomes  \n1. A  \n2. A  \n3. B  \n1. C  \n2. B  \n3. B \n62  \nPre Test Answer Key (   \nPre and Post Answer   \nNothing Details)  \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n4. C  \n5. B  \nPre test SKILL B- Getting the  \nGeneral Significance  \nAnswer Key  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nPre test SKILL C- Drawing  \nConclusion  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nPre test SKILL D  \nPredicting Outcomes   \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nKey ( Nothing Details) \n1.B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n4. C  \n5. B  \nPre and Post test SKILL B\n\nPDF page 66\nGetting the General   \nSignificance  \n1. A  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nPre and Post test SKILL C \nDrawing Conclusion  \n1. B  \n2. B  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A  \nPre and Post test SKILL D \nPredicting Outcomes   \n1. B  \n2. C  \n3. B  \n4. B  \n5. A','[56, 57, 58, 59, 60, 61, 62, 63]','assets/images/level6/g3/p056-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(378,20,1100,'Pre-Assessment','Reading comprehension test','Show how well you understand what you read before the lessons.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nPre-Assessment: Pre-Assessment · skill: Noting Details · module PDF pages 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nAna’s Pet (PDF pages 7, 8)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nAna’s Pet\nMother bought two piglets. She had a pigpen ready for them. “These pigs should be given good care,” she told Ana. “Can you help me take care of them?” “Of course, Mother,” answered Ana. “l’ll help you take care of them.” So, Ana became the caretaker of the piglets. She cleaned the pen early in the morning and gave them baths. She fed them swill which she got from their neighbors. Soon the piglets grew big and fat. When they were ready for sale, Ana felt sad. She knew Mother would get a good price for them but she would lose her pet.\n\nWhat did Ana’s Mother buy?\nA. Two piglets\nB. Three piglets\nC. Four piglets\n\nWhere did she keep them?\nA. In their yard\nB. In a pigpen\nC. Under the house\n\nWhom did she ask to help her take care of them?\nA. Ana\nB. her maid\nC. her neighbor\n\nDid Ana like to take care of the pigs?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Perhaps\n\nWhat new job did Ana have?\nA. nurse of the pigs\nB. friend of the pigs\nC. caretaker of the pigs\n\nAt what time of the day did she clean the pigpen?\nA. early in the morning\nB. late in the afternoon\nC. at noon\n\nWho gave baths to the pigs?\nA. Ana herself\nB. Ana’s sister\nC. Ana’s mother\n\nWhere did Ana get the swill for the pigs?\nA. from the market\nB. from a nearby store\nC. from their neighbors\n\nWhat happened with the pigs?\nA. Became sick\nB. Grew big and fat\nC. Did not grow at all\n\nHow did Ana feel when the pigs were ready for sale?\nA. very happy\nB. angry\nC. sad\n\nExercise (PDF pages 9, 10)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nExercise\nCarlos is a fourth grader. His books are not covered. He loses his pencil very often. Every day, when he arrives from school he throws his bag and books around. One day, he left his schoolbag on the floor. His dog, Liksi, found the bag. It bit and tore the bag. It bit one book and tore the pages, too. Carlos’s mother saw what happened. She quickly drove the dog away. She picked up the bag. It was torn to pieces. She picked up a book. Many pages were also torn.\n\nWho is the boy in the story?\nA. Jhon\nB. Karla\nC. Carlos\n\nWhat pet is Liksi?\nA. Cow\nB. Dog\nC. Cat\n\nWhat is the story about?\nA. a careless boy\nB. Carlos’s mother\nC. a dog named Liksi\n\nHow did Carlos’s mother feel when she saw what happened?\nA. surprised\nB. gladdened\nC. angered\n\nWhat will this incident teach Carlos?\nA. to be more polite\nB. to be more obedient\nC. to be more careful with his things\n\nWhy does Carlo seem to be a careless boy?\nA. because he always listens to her Mom.\nB. because he doesn’t care\nC. because he is a good boy.\n\nWhose bag was torn by pet Liksi?\nA. Carlo\nB. Mom\nC. Father\n\nGuava Jam (PDF pages 11, 12)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nGuava Jam\nThe cook had just prepared a kettle of guava jam. She placed the kettle on the table to cool. Some guava jam spilled on the table. In a short time, ants gathered at the jam on the table. “What fine guava jam!” they all said. The ants ate and ate. They continued eating even when they were already full. They did not want to leave the jam. They thought they would sleep on it so that when they wake up they could eat and eat again. But alas, the guava jam became thicker and thicker as it cooled.\n\nWhat fruit is the jam?\nA. Strawberry\nB. Guava\nC. Tamarind\n\nWhere these ants gathered to ate the jam?\nA. In the table.\nB. In the bed.\nC. In the floor\n\nWhat happened to the guava when it cooled?\nA. It became sticky\nB. It became watery\nC. It became thicker\n\nWhat happened to the ants?\nA. They became healthy.\nB. They stayed on the jam for a vacation.\nC. Their legs got stuck on the table and they died there.\n\nWhat should they have done?\nA. They should have eaten just enough and left.\nB. They should not have eaten any of the jam.\nC. They should have brought home the jam.\n\nWhat lesson did you get from his story?\nA. Don’t be too slow.\nB. Don’t be too greedy.\nC. Don’t be too industrious.\n\nWhy ants decide to sleep on the jam?\nA. to play there\nB. to study there\nC. to eat there\n\nPlaying with Circles (PDF page 13)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nPlaying with Circles\n\nDraw five circles of the same size. Draw a straight line joining the circles through the middle.\n\nDraw a big circle. Divide it into four equal parts. Shade one part of it with your pencil.\n\nWrite what part of your circle in number 2 is not shaded.\n\nDraw a big circle. Write the word circle inside it.\n\nDraw another big circle. Draw aa smaller circle inside it.\n\nThe Horse and the Snail (PDF page 14)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nThe Horse and Snail\nOne morning the horse and the snail met. The horse made fun of how the snail walked. This made the snail very angry. He decided to run a race with the horse. The snail walked slowly. But walked and walked. He did not look back. The horse laughed. He could easily beat the snail, the horse thought. He ran for a while. He saw the snail for behind him. He rested under a mango tree. Soon he fell asleep. The snail walked past the horse. He did not rest. When the horse woke up, he ran as fast as he could. But the snail was the first at the finish line.\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                         GRADE \nSpeed Rate Grade 4 Level \n• \nNeeds Intervention: below 70 WPM \n• \nDeveloping: 70–110 WPM \n• \nProficient: 111–139 WPM \n• \nAdvanced: 140 WPM and above \nReferences: \n                    Developing Reading Power Grade 4 \n  Project: I Can Read \n                    GOOGLE PICTURES \n                    Rasinski, T. & Padak, N. (2005). 3-Minute Reading   \n                          Assessments. New York, NY: Scholastic Inc. \n 2018 Updated Phil-IRI Manual, by Mark Anthony Llego','[7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14]','assets/images/level6/g4/p007-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(379,20,2201,'Activity 1','Pepe and the Guavas','Noting Details: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 1: Pepe and the Guavas · skill: Noting Details · module PDF pages 16, 17\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nPepe and the Guavas (PDF pages 16, 17)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nPepe and the Guavas\nPepe climbed the guava tree near his house. It had many ripe fruits. He picked as many as he could and put them in his pockets. Then he sat on a branch and began eating them. While he was eating, he felt the branch break. He did not know it was a dead branch.\n\nDown, down he fell! After that, he did not know what happened. When he woke up, he was in a hospital bed. His two arms were in cast.\n\nWho climbed the guava tree?\nA. Pepe\nB. Pepe’s friend\nC. Pepe’s brother\n\nWhere was the guava tree?\nA. Near Pepe’s house\nB. In his neighbor’s yard\nC. Near his friend’s house\n\nWhat was the fruit on the tree?\nA. Ripe guava fruits\nB. green fruits\nC. Many birds\n\nWhere did Pepe put the fruits he picked?\nA. In his hat\nB. In a basket\nC. In his pocket\n\nWhy did Pepe fall down at the guava tree?\nA. He did not know it was a died branch\nB. He did not know what happened\nC. He felt the branch break\n\nWhen did he know that he fell down?\nA. His two arms were in cast\nB. When he woke up he was in a hospital bed\nC. He woke up at the branch of a tree\n\nHow did Pepe get on the top of the tree?\nA. He climbed the tree\nB. He sat on the branch\nC. He fall down on the tree\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                         GRADE \nSpeed Rate Grade 4 Level \n• \nNeeds Intervention: below 70 WPM \n• \nDeveloping: 70–110 WPM \n• \nProficient: 111–139 WPM \n• \nAdvanced: 140 WPM and above \nReferences: \n                    Developing Reading Power Grade 4 \n  Project: I Can Read \n                    GOOGLE PICTURES \n                    Rasinski, T. & Padak, N. (2005). 3-Minute Reading   \n                          Assessments. New York, NY: Scholastic Inc. \n 2018 Updated Phil-IRI Manual, by Mark Anthony Llego','[16, 17]','assets/images/level6/g4/p016-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(380,20,3202,'Activity 2','At the Public Playground','Noting Details: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 2: At the Public Playground · skill: Noting Details · module PDF pages 18, 19\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nAt the Public Playground (PDF pages 18, 19)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nAt the Public Playground\nThe public playground was full of children. They were shouting with joy. The smaller children were playing in the sand boxes. They were digging the sand and filling their pails. The bigger boys and girls were having fun riding on swings.\n\nSome big boys were chinning the bar. Others were climbing the big tower and were sliding down. Some of them were busy playing on the seesaws, going up and going down. Some girls in bathing suits were swimming in the pool.\n\nWhere were the children?\nA. In school\nB. At the Rizal Park\nC. At the public playground\n\nHow did they show they were happy?\nA. They shouted with joy.\nB. They ran and jumped.\nC. They ran here and there.\n\nWho played in the sand boxes?\nA. small girls\nB. small boys\nC. smaller children\n\nWhat did they do?\nA. Made houses of sand.\nB. Filled their pails with sand.\nC. Walked without shoes on the sand.\n\nHow did the bigger boys and girls have fun?\nA. Rode in bicycles.\nB. Danced together.\nC. Rode on the swings.\n\nWho chinned the bar?\nA. A big boy.\nB. Some big girls.\nC. Some big boys.\n\nHow did the children go down the tower?\nA. Sliding\nB. Running\nC. Jumping\n\nWhat went up and down?\nA. Slides\nB. Seesaw\nC. Swings\n\nWho were swimming in the pool?\nA. Some girls\nB. Some boys\nC. Little children\n\nWhat were they wearing?\nA. Shorts\nB. Play suits\nC. Bathing suits\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                         GRADE \nSpeed Rate Grade 4 Level \n• \nNeeds Intervention: below 70 WPM \n• \nDeveloping: 70–110 WPM \n• \nProficient: 111–139 WPM \n• \nAdvanced: 140 WPM and above \nReferences: \n                    Developing Reading Power Grade 4 \n  Project: I Can Read \n                    GOOGLE PICTURES \n                    Rasinski, T. & Padak, N. (2005). 3-Minute Reading   \n                          Assessments. New York, NY: Scholastic Inc. \n 2018 Updated Phil-IRI Manual, by Mark Anthony Llego','[18, 19]','assets/images/level6/g4/p018-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(381,20,4203,'Activity 3','A Family of Music Lovers','Noting Details: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 3: A Family of Music Lovers · skill: Noting Details · module PDF pages 20, 21\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nA Family of Music Lovers (PDF pages 20, 21)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nA Family of Music Lovers\nThe Santos family are fond of music. All of them have good singing voices and all can play the piano. Mr. Santos is their teacher and Mrs. Santos is their pianist. People enjoy listening to them when they are practicing, more so when they sing Filipino songs. The songs make the listeners happy. Lita, Raul, Emil, Nina and Anita sing with feeling. While singing they watch Mr. Santos’s hand, so they know when to sing louder or softer.\n\nHow many children are in the family?\nA. Five\nB. Six\nC. Seven\n\nWhat kind of family are the Santoses?\nA. Poetic\nB. Dramatic\nC. Music lovers\n\nWhat do all of them have?\nA. Good singing voices\nB. Weak singing voices\nC. Good speaking voices\n\nWhat instrument can they play?\nA. Guitar\nB. Violin\nC. Piano\n\nWho is their teacher?\nA. Their father\nB. Their mother\nC. Their brother\n\nWho is their pianist?\nA. Their father\nB. Their brother\nC. Their mother\n\nHow do people feel while they are listening to the Santoses?\nA. Sad\nB. Angry\nC. Happy\n\nHow do the children sing?\nA. Noisily\nB. With feeling\nC. Loud and clear\n\nWhat kind of songs make the listeners feel happier?\nA. English songs\nB. Filipino songs\nC. American songs\n\nWhat is the children’s sign in signing louder or softer?\nA. Mr. Santos’s eyes\nB. Mr. Santos’s hands\nC. Mrs. Santos’s body movement\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                         GRADE \nSpeed Rate Grade 4 Level \n• \nNeeds Intervention: below 70 WPM \n• \nDeveloping: 70–110 WPM \n• \nProficient: 111–139 WPM \n• \nAdvanced: 140 WPM and above \nReferences: \n                    Developing Reading Power Grade 4 \n  Project: I Can Read \n                    GOOGLE PICTURES \n                    Rasinski, T. & Padak, N. (2005). 3-Minute Reading   \n                          Assessments. New York, NY: Scholastic Inc. \n 2018 Updated Phil-IRI Manual, by Mark Anthony Llego','[20, 21]','assets/images/level6/g4/p020-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(382,20,5204,'Activity 4','The Artesian Well','Noting Details: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 4: The Artesian Well · skill: Noting Details · module PDF pages 22, 23\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nThe Artesian Well (PDF pages 22, 23)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nThe Artesian Well\nNonoy lived in a little barrio far from the town. He had to wake up early every morning to fetch water from a well. He had to make several trips before he could fill a big jar.\n\nOne day men came to the barrio to drill an artesian well. In ten days, the artesian well was ready. It was like there was a fiesta. Barrio people lined up to get water. When Nonoy’s turn came, he took a long drink. How cool and satisfying the water tasted!\n\nWhere did Nonoy live?\nA. In a barrio\nB. In the town\nC. In a city\n\nWhat did he do every morning?\nA. Fetched water from a well.\nB. Fetched his sister from school.\nC. Bought bread from the store.\n\nHow long did it take him to get water?\nA. Two trips\nB. Several trips\nC. Several hours\n\nWhere did he put the water?\nA. On a big jar\nB. In a big can\nC. In a big bottle\n\nWhat did the men do in the barrio?\nA. Drilled an artesian well.\nB. Fetched water for the people.\nC. Dug a deep well in the ground.\n\nHow did the barrio people feel about it?\nA. Angry\nB. Happy\nC. Ashamed\n\nWhat would the well give them?\nA. Unsafe water\nB. Clean, safe water\nC. Water for the fields\n\nHow long did the men finish the work?\nA. Ten days\nB. Four days\nC. One week\n\nWhat did the barrio people do when it was ready?\nA. Lined up to get water\nB. Took bath near the well.\nC. Danced along the tree\n\nHow did the water taste to Nonoy?\nA. He did not like it.\nB. It was not so good.\nC. It tasted cool and satisfying.\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                         GRADE \nSpeed Rate Grade 4 Level \n• \nNeeds Intervention: below 70 WPM \n• \nDeveloping: 70–110 WPM \n• \nProficient: 111–139 WPM \n• \nAdvanced: 140 WPM and above \nReferences: \n                    Developing Reading Power Grade 4 \n  Project: I Can Read \n                    GOOGLE PICTURES \n                    Rasinski, T. & Padak, N. (2005). 3-Minute Reading   \n                          Assessments. New York, NY: Scholastic Inc. \n 2018 Updated Phil-IRI Manual, by Mark Anthony Llego','[22, 23]','assets/images/level6/g4/p022-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(383,20,6205,'Activity 5','Raindrops','Getting the General Significance: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 5: Raindrops · skill: Getting the General Significance · module PDF page 24\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nRaindrops (PDF page 24)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nRaindrops\n“I’m going down to do my turn,”\nOnce said a drop of rain;\n“I hear a sigh, a thirsty sigh,\nI’ll go with you.” “Me, too! Me, too!”\nCried all the raindrops near;\nSo down they fell, in merry mood,\nThe whole wide field to cheer.\n\nWhat is the best tittle for this poem?\nA. The field\nB. The rain\nC. The flowers\n\nWho are talking in the poem?\nA. The flower\nB. The raindrops\nC. The field\n\nWhich sentence tells about the rain?\nA. It waters plants to make them grow.\nB. It causes floods which destroy crops.\nC. It prevents children from going to school.\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                         GRADE \nSpeed Rate Grade 4 Level \n• \nNeeds Intervention: below 70 WPM \n• \nDeveloping: 70–110 WPM \n• \nProficient: 111–139 WPM \n• \nAdvanced: 140 WPM and above \nReferences: \n                    Developing Reading Power Grade 4 \n  Project: I Can Read \n                    GOOGLE PICTURES \n                    Rasinski, T. & Padak, N. (2005). 3-Minute Reading   \n                          Assessments. New York, NY: Scholastic Inc. \n 2018 Updated Phil-IRI Manual, by Mark Anthony Llego','[24]','assets/images/level6/g4/p024-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(384,20,7206,'Activity 6','Carlos','Getting the General Significance: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 6: Carlos · skill: Getting the General Significance · module PDF pages 25, 26\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nCarlos (PDF pages 25, 26)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nCarlos\nCarlos is a fourth grader. His books are not covered. He loses his pencil very often. Every day, when he arrives from school he throws his bag and books around. One day, he left his schoolbag on the floor. His dog, Liksi, found the bag. It bit and tore the bag. It bit one book and tore the pages. It bit another book and tore the pages too.\n\nCarlos’s mother saw what happened. She quickly drove the dog away. She picked up the bag. It was torn to pieces. She picked up a book. Many pages were also torn.\n\nWhat is the story about?\nA. A careless boy.\nB. Carlos’s mother\nC. A dog named Liksi.\n\nHow did Carlos’s mother feel when she saw what happened?\nA. Surprised\nB. Gladdened\nC. Angered\n\nWhat will this incident teach Carlos?\nA. To be more polite.\nB. To be more obedient.\nC. to be more careful with his things.\n\nHow did Liksi ruin the bag and book?\nA. stepped on it\nB. bit and tore it\nC. throw it away\n\nWhat happened to the other book bitten by Liksi?\nA. it was wet\nB. it was lost\nC. it was to torn into pieces\n\nWhat do you think fits to describe the attitude of Liksi?\nA. playful\nB. naughty\nC. well-behaved\n\nIs Carlos’s behavior on taking care of his things is very good?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                       