(386,20,9208,'Activity 8','Flag','Getting the General Significance: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 8: Flag · skill: Getting the General Significance · module PDF page 29\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nFlag (PDF page 29)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nFlag\nI. Way up there,\nWaving so high!\nRed and blue, side by side\nWith a triangle of white.\n\nII. Way up there,\nWaving so high!\nThe sun, a golden bright\nOn a triangle of white.\n\nIII. Way up there,\nWaving so high,\nKeep it up right\nFor it we should fight.\n\nThe best little for the poem is______.\nA. our flag\nB. Let’s fight\nC. Up in the sky\n\nThe flag described is a___________.\nA. Philippine flag\nB. American flag\nC. Japanese flag\n\nThe poem teaches us_________.\nA. To look at the flag.\nB. To fight for our flag.\nC. How to draw a flag.\n\nWhat are the colors of flag side by side on it?\nA. black and red\nB. pink and blue\nC. red and blue\n\nWhat is the shape of color white in the flag\nA. circle\nB. triangle\nC. square\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                         GRADE \nSpeed Rate Grade 4 Level \n• \nNeeds Intervention: below 70 WPM \n• \nDeveloping: 70–110 WPM \n• \nProficient: 111–139 WPM \n• \nAdvanced: 140 WPM and above \nReferences: \n                    Developing Reading Power Grade 4 \n  Project: I Can Read \n                    GOOGLE PICTURES \n                    Rasinski, T. & Padak, N. (2005). 3-Minute Reading   \n                          Assessments. New York, NY: Scholastic Inc. \n 2018 Updated Phil-IRI Manual, by Mark Anthony Llego','[29]','as