(389,20,12211,'Activity 11','The Pet Bird','Predicting Outcomes and Making Inferences: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 11: The Pet Bird · skill: Predicting Outcomes and Making Inferences · module PDF page 32\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nThe Pet Bird (PDF page 32)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nThe Pet Bird\nDolor had a pet bird which she loved very much. Every day she gave it ripe bananas to eat and fresh water to drink.\n\nOne day Dolor got sick and had to stay in bed. She asked her brother Jun to feed her pet every day. But Jun was very different from Dolor. He was not found of the bird.\n\nOne morning Jun hurriedly gave the bird a ripe banana and some water. Then, away he ran to his waiting friends.\n\nWhen Dolor woke up, she saw a little red feather on her blanket. She thought her bird was beside her. Then she found other feathers scattered on the floor. Slowly she got up and looked at her bird in the cage. The cage was empty.\n\nDolor cried bitterly. She cried and cried and would not eat. Her mother was worried. Jun was very sorry.\n\nThen Jun got his savings and went out. “I must do something for Dolor,” he said.\n\nWhat happen with the bird?\nA. It flew away.\nB. It got sick and die.\nC. It was eaten by the cat.\n\nWhat might happen to dolor due to the disappearance of her bird?\nA. She might get well very soon.\nB. She might get worse.\nC. She might become kinder.\n\nWhat will June do with his savings?\nA. give it to Dolor.\nB. buy doll for Dolor.\nC. buy another bird for Dolor.\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE                