(390,20,13212,'Activity 12','Banana Peelings','Predicting Outcomes and Making Inferences: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 12: Banana Peelings · skill: Predicting Outcomes and Making Inferences · module PDF page 33\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nBanana Peelings (PDF page 33)\nInstructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nBanana Peelings\nBen and Lito always pray and eat lunch in school together. One day after eating several ripe bananas which Lito brought, the two boys played war. They threw banana peeling at each other. After playing they forgot about banana peelings which they threw on the cemented porch. Then, the school bell rang. The boys and girls formed their lines. As they passed by the porch to their room, something happened. Three boys helped Lisa to the school clinic. Lisa was then brought to a hospital because of a broken knee. Her knee was then placed in plaster cast. How sorry Ben and Lito were. How they wished they did not play war after eating those bananas! How they wish they throw those bananas peelings in th garbage can at once.\n\nWhat happened when the boys and girls passed by the porch?\nA. some girls and boys ate bananas, too.\nB. some boys played war, too.\nC. a girl slipped when she stepped on the banana peelings.\n\nWhy boys and girls formed their lines?\nA. because they’ll buy food to canteen.\nB. because it’s flag ceremony time.\nC. because it is time to get inside their room.\n\nHow long do you think would it take before Lisa could move about again?\nA. one day\nB. one-month\nC. one week\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n7. A \n7.A \n7.C \n7.B \n8.B \n8.B \n8.A \n9.A \n9.B \n9.A \n10.C \n10.B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nRAINDROPS \nCARLOS \nPAL IS SICK \nFLAG \n1. B \n1.A \n1.C \n1.A \n2. B \n2.C \n2.A \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.B \n3.B \n4.B \n4.C \n4.C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.B \n6.A \n6.C \n7.B \n7.C\n\nPDF page 52\n52 \nA JOKE \nTHE BIRDLINGS \nTHE PET BIRD \nBANANA \nPEELING \n1. B \n1.B \n1.C  \n1.C \n2. A \n2.B \nB2.  \n2.A \n3. C \n3.A \n3.C \n3.B \n4. A \nMONTHS \nOF \nTHE \nYEAR \nENGLISH ALPHABET \nUNDERSTANDING \nDIRECTIONS \n1. JANUARY \n1.A \n1.LIBRARY \n2. MAY \n2.Z \n2.CAT  \n3. AUGUST \n3. \nELEPHANT \n4. JULY \n4.COIL \nTIGER \n5. DECEMBER \n5.N \n3.DIRTY \n4.OIL \n5.PHILIPPINES \nC\n\nPDF page 53\n53 \nHOW TO COMPUTE SPEED RATE \nSpeed Rate Formula \n• \nTime Consumed (in minutes) = Time Finished − Time Started, then \nconvert the result to minutes. \n• \nSpeed Rate (words per minute) = Number of words in the passage ÷ Time \nConsumed (in minutes). \nSample: \n            9:00                                    9:01:30                           1:30 \n   TIME STARTED                      TIME FINISHED           TIME CONSUMED \nExample Time consumed in Minutes: \n1 \nminute \n30 \nseconds \n→ \n 1+30÷60=1.51+30÷60=1.5 minutes \n       100 WORDS                              66.66                     Needs Intervention \n      1.5 MINUTES     \n  SPEED RATE     