(396,20,19218,'Activity 18','A Dream That Became a Reality','Reading Exercises for Speed: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 4\nActivity 18: A Dream That Became a Reality · skill: Reading Exercises for Speed · module PDF page 39\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nA Dream That Became a Reality (PDF page 39)\nInstructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Dream That Became a Reality\nFor many, many years, people had always dreamed of going to the moon. This seemed impossible because the moon is very far from Earth.\n\nNot even the biggest, fastest and most powerful airplane could ever reach the moon. Then, very powerful rocket ships were made. These rocket ships could travel fast enough to escape the earth’s gravity, withstand the sun’s heat and land on the moon. On July 20, 1969, an almost impossible dream came true. Neil Armstrong, together with two other American astronauts, made a trip to the moon aboard Apollo 11, a spacecraft. Neil Armstrong became the very first man to land on the moon.\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 51\n51 \nPre-Assessment/Post-Assessment Answer Key \nANSWER KEY \nANA’S PET \nEXERCISE \nGUAVA JAM \n1. A \n1.C \n1.B \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. A \n3.A \n3. C \n4. A \n4.C \n4. C \n5. C \n5.C \n5. A \n6. A \n6.B \n6. B \n7. A \n7.A \n7. C \n8. C \n9. B \n10. C \nANSWER KEY \nPEPE \nAND \nTHE GUAVA \nAT THE PUBLIC \nPLAYGROUND \nA FAMILY OF \nMUSICLOVERS \nTHE ARTESIAN \nWELL \n1. A \n1.C \n1.A \n1.A \n2. A \n2.A \n2.C \n2.A \n3. A \n3.C \n3.A \n3.B \n4. C \n4.B \n4.C \n4.A \n5. C \n5.C \n5.A \n5.A \n6. B \n6.C \n6.C \n6.B \n