(426,22,4203,'Activity 3','How the Romans Cooked Their Food','Noting Details: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 6\nActivity 3: How the Romans Cooked Their Food · skill: Noting Details · module PDF pages 22, 23\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nHow the Romans Cooked Their Food (PDF pages 22, 23)\nDirections: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nHow the Romans Cooked Their Food\nLong ago the Romans used to cook their food just as the way hunters today sometimes do. They dug out an over in the ground, lined it with stones and made a fire in it. When the improvised oven was well heated, they raked out the ashes, put in the food and covered it. We learned about this from excavations in the city of Pompeii. Pompeii was destroyed and buried during the eruption of a volcano hundreds of years ago. When the city found excavated, ovens with wood and ashes were found among the ruins. It is believed that just as the cooks were about to bake, the fiery volcano rained out ashes and boiling lava and burned down the city.\n\nIn ancient times, how did the Romans cook their food?\nA. just as the cooks do today\nB. just as the people do today\nC. just as some hunters do today\n\nWhat did they use to line the ground where they built the ovens?\nA. lime\nB. clay\nC. stones\n\nWhat did they do with the oven before putting in their food?\nA. They cooled it.\nB. They heated it.\nC. They poured water in it.\n\nWhere did they dig their ovens?\nA. in the ground\nB. in the cement\nC. in the tree trunks\n\nWhat did they rake out of the oven before putting in their food?\nA. sand\nB. ashes\nC. wood\n\nWho cooked their food in underground ovens?\nA. the Jews\nB. the Italians\nC. the Romans\n\nWho had ovens with food ashes in them?\nA. the British\nB. the Romans\nC. the people of Pompeii\n\nWhat destroyed Pompeii?\nA. a great fire\nB. a terrible flood\nC. a volcanic eruption\n\nWhat were the cooks about to do when the volcano erupted?\nA. eat\nB. bake\nC. wash dishes\n\nWhat was done with the buried city?\nA. rebuilt\nB. forgotten\nC. excavated\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 58\n51 \nAnswer Key: \nPre-Test \nSkill-A  \nNoting \nDetails \nSkill-B \nGetting \nSignificance \nof a \nSelection \nSkill-C \nPredicting \nOutcomes \nof a given \nevent \nSkill-D \nReading to \nFollow \nPrecise \nDirections \nSkill-E \nReading for \nSpeed and \nComprehension \nThe Face \nin the \nPool1 \nJay’s \nCuriosity \nRice \nWally’s Kite \nA Fairy Tale \nfrom Japan \n1. A \n1. B \n1. C \n1. answers \nmay vary \n1. B \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. A \n2. A \n3. A \n3. C \n3. A \n3. answers \nmay vary \n3. C \n4. A \n4. \n4.  \n4. \n4. C \n5. B \n5. \n5. \n5. \n5. A \n6. \n6. \n6. \n6. \n6. B \n7. \n7. \n7. \n7. \n7. C \n8. \n8. \n8. \n8. \n8. A \n9. \n9. \n9. \n9. \n9. B \n10. \n10. \n10. \n10. \n10. A \nSkill-A Noting Details \nRules to Follow \nDaphne \nHow the \nRomans \nCooked their \nFood \nFirst Night in the \nCity \n1. A \n1. A \n1. C \n1. A \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. A \n3. A \n3. B \n3. B \n3. B \n4. C \n4. B \n4. A \n4. B \n5. A \n5. B \n5. B \n5. B \n6. A \n6. B \n6. C \n6. C \n7. A \n7. C \n7. C \n7. C \n8. C \n8. B \n8. C \n8. B \n9. C \n9. A \n9. B \n9. B \n10. B \n10. C \n10. C \n10. A\n\nPDF page 59\n52 \nSkill- B Getting the General Significance of a Selection \nExercise 5 \nExercise 6 \nExercise 7 \nExercise 8 \n1. B \n1. B \n1. C \n1. C \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. B \n3. C \n3. B \n3. B \n3. C \nSkill-C Predicting Outcome of a Given Event and Making Inferences \nExer \nExercise 10 \nExercise 11 \nExercise 12 \n1. anwers may \nvary \n1. anwers may \nvary \n1. anwers may \nvary \n1.anwers may \nvary \n2. anwers may \nvary \n2. anwers may \nvary \n2. anwers may \nvary \n2. anwers may \nvary \n3. anwers may \nvary \n3. anwers may \nvary \n3. anwers may \nvary \n3.. anwers may \nvary \nSkill-D Reading to Follow Precise Directions \nThe Earthworm \nRizal and the \nFilipino Youth \nChristmas Eve \nHints for \nKeeping Friends \n1. \n1. \n1. anwers may \nvary \n1. B \n2. \n2. B \n2. \n2. C \n3. \n3. \n3. A \n3. A \n4. \n4. C \n4. C \n5. A \n5. \n5. A \nSkill E Reading Exercise for Speed and Comprehension \nBETHLEHEM \nBIRDS OF \nPARADISE \nBIRTHSTONES \nAPPLES \n1. B \n1. C \n1. C \n1. C \n2. C \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. B \n3. A \n3. B \n3.  A \n4.  C \n4. B \n4. A \n4. B \n5.  C \n5. A \n5. B \n5. A \n6. A \n6. A \n6. A \n6. A \n7. B \n7. C \n7. A \n7. B \n8. C \n8. B \n8. A \n8. C \n9. C \n9. B \n9.  \n9. C \n10. C \n10. B \n10. B \n10.\n\nPDF page 60\n53 \nPost Test \nThe Face in \nthe Pool \nJay’s \nCuriosity \nRice \nWally’s Kite \nA Fairy Tale \nfrom Japan \n1. A \n1. B \n1. C \n1. \n1. B \n2. B \n2.C \n2. C \n2. \n2. A \n3. A \n3. C \n3. A \n3. \n3. C \n4. A \n4. \n4. C \n5. B \n5. \n5. A \n6. B \n7. C \n8. A \n9. B \n10. A','[22, 23]','assets/images/level6/g6/p022-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(427,22,5204,'Activity 4','First Night in the City','Noting Details: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 6\nActivity 4: First Night in the City · skill: Noting Details · module PDF pages 24, 25\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nFirst Night in the City (PDF pages 24, 25)\nDirections: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nFirst Night in the City\nIt was Lynn’s first night in the city. She woke up with a stun and then she remembered where she was. She had never spent the night in a city before. She lay still and listened. Cars and jeepneys seemed to race like ants heading straight towards here. A new house was being built next to her aunt’s house and the cement mixer was roaring angrily. Downstairs, in the kitchen, she heard the clattering of pans. Then, she heard the chime of the big clock. She turned over and fell asleep again.\n\nWhat did Lynn remember when she woke up?\nA. She was in the city.\nB. She was in their house.\nC. She was in her country.\n\nHas Lynn ever spent a night before in the city?\nA. No\nB. Yes\nC. Maybe\n\nWhere did Lynn imagine the cars and jeeps going?\nA. heading to the city\nB. heading towards her\nC. heading towards the house\n\nWhere did the roaring sound come from?\nA. a bulldozer\nB. a cement mixer\nC. a street sweeper\n\nHow did she know the time?\nA. from the radio\nB. from the big clock\nC. from the position of the sun\n\nWhy did Lynn wake up with a stun?\nA. She was afraid.\nB. She had a dream.\nC. She felt she was in a strange place.\n\nWhat did she do when she woke up?\nA. She ran out to play.\nB. She washed her face.\nC. She lay still and listened\n\nWhat did Lynn hear passing in front of the house?\nA. buses and trains\nB. cars and jeepneys\nC. streetcars and carts\n\nWhere did she hear the clattering of the pans?\nA. from the streets\nB. from the kitchen\nC. from the living room\n\nDid Lynn fall asleep again?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 58\n51 \nAnswer Key: \nPre-Test \nSkill-A  \nNoting \nDetails \nSkill-B \nGetting \nSignificance \nof a \nSelection \nSkill-C \nPredicting \nOutcomes \nof a given \nevent \nSkill-D \nReading to \nFollow \nPrecise \nDirections \nSkill-E \nReading for \nSpeed and \nComprehension \nThe Face \nin the \nPool1 \nJay’s \nCuriosity \nRice \nWally’s Kite \nA Fairy Tale \nfrom Japan \n1. A \n1. B \n1. C \n1. answers \nmay vary \n1. B \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. A \n2. A \n3. A \n3. C \n3. A \n3. answers \nmay vary \n3. C \n4. A \n4. \n4.  \n4. \n4. C \n5. B \n5. \n5. \n5. \n5. A \n6. \n6. \n6. \n6. \n6. B \n7. \n7. \n7. \n7. \n7. C \n8. \n8. \n8. \n8. \n8. A \n9. \n9. \n9. \n9. \n9. B \n10. \n10. \n10. \n10. \n10. A \nSkill-A Noting Details \nRules to Follow \nDaphne \nHow the \nRomans \nCooked their \nFood \nFirst Night in the \nCity \n1. A \n1. A \n1. C \n1. A \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. A \n3. A \n3. B \n3. B \n3. B \n4. C \n4. B \n4. A \n4. B \n5. A \n5. B \n5. B \n5. B \n6. A \n6. B \n6. C \n6. C \n7. A \n7. C \n7. C \n7. C \n8. C \n8. B \n8. C \n8. B \n9. C \n9. A \n9. B \n9. B \n10. B \n10. C \n10. C \n10. A\n\nPDF page 59\n52 \nSkill- B Getting the General Significance of a Selection \nExercise 5 \nExercise 6 \nExercise 7 \nExercise 8 \n1. B \n1. B \n1. C \n1. C \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. B \n3. C \n3. B \n3. B \n3. C \nSkill-C Predicting Outcome of a Given Event and Making Inferences \nExer \nExercise 10 \nExercise 11 \nExercise 12 \n1. anwers may \nvary \n1. anwers may \nvary \n1. anwers may \nvary \n1.anwers may \nvary \n2. anwers may \nvary \n2. anwers may \nvary \n2. anwers may \nvary \n2. anwers may \nvary \n3. anwers may \nvary \n3. anwers may \nvary \n3. anwers may \nvary \n3.. anwers may \nvary \nSkill-D Reading to Follow Precise Directions \nThe Earthworm \nRizal and the \nFilipino Youth \nChristmas Eve \nHints for \nKeeping Friends \n1. \n1. \n1. anwers may \nvary \n1. B \n2. \n2. B \n2. \n2. C \n3. \n3. \n3. A \n3. A \n4. \n4. C \n4. C \n5. A \n5. \n5. A \nSkill E Reading Exercise for Speed and Comprehension \nBETHLEHEM \nBIRDS OF \nPARADISE \nBIRTHSTONES \nAPPLES \n1. B \n1. C \n1. C \n1. C \n2. C \n2. B \n2. B \n2. A \n3. B \n3. A \n3. B \n3.  A \n4.  C \n4. B \n4. A \n4. B \n5.  C \n5. A \n5. B \n5. A \n6. A \n6. A \n6. A \n6. A \n7. B \n7. C \n7. A \n7. B \n8. C \n8. B \n8. A \n8. C \n9. C \n9. B \n9.  \n9. C \n10. C \n10. B \n10. B \n10.\n\nPDF page 60\n53 \nPost Test \nThe Face in \nthe Pool \nJay’s \nCuriosity \nRice \nWally’s Kite \nA Fairy Tale \nfrom Japan \n1. A \n1. B \n1. C \n1. \n1. B \n2. B \n2.C \n2. C \n2. \n2. A \n3. A \n3. C \n3. A \n3. \n3. C \n4. A \n4. \n4. C \n5. B \n5. \n5. A \n6. B \n7. C \n8. A \n9. B \n10. A','[24, 25]','assets/images/level6/g6/p024-1.webp',1,'2026-11-15 11:14:45'),
(428,22,6205,'Activity 5','Ed’s Day','Getting the General Significance: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 6\nActivity 5: Ed’s Day · skill: Getting the General Significance · module PDF pages 26, 27\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nEd’s Day (PDF pages 26, 27)\nDirections: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nEd’s Day\nAs Ed passed in his neighbor’s yard, he saw Tiger, his neighbor’s dog, stumble and fall across the live fallen electric wire. Tiger gave one wild yell of pain and lay on the ground, kicking and struggling to set himself loose from the wire.\n\nEd stopped his neighbor’s two sons from going near the dog. He saw one of the boys rush towards the dog, crying. He ran to prevent the boy from touching the dog.\n\n“Oh, poor Tiger!” Ed said with tears in his eyes as he saw Tiger, helplessly freeing himself. Then he turned to the boys and shouted, “Keep back there or that wire will kill you, too.”\n\n“Run home quickly and call up the Electrical Company,” Ed told one of the brothers. “Tell them a live wire has killed a dog and may kill others too if they don’t hurry. Give the address. Run!”\n\nWhat did Ed see?\nA. how a fallen electric wire killed the passerby\nB. how a dog was killed by a live, fallen electric wire\nC. how a boy was killed by a live, fallen electric wire\n\nWho did Ed save from harm?\nA. Tiger\nB. the two boys\nC. the man from the Electric Company\n\nFrom whom would the boys ask for help?\nA. from the people\nB. from their parents\nC. from the Electric Company\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 58\n51 \nAnswer Key: \nPre-Test \nSkill-A  \nNoting \nDetails \nSkill-B \nGetting \nSignificance \nof a \nSelection \nSkill-C \nPredictin