(437,22,15214,'Activity 14','Rizal and the Filipino Youth','Reading to Follow Precise Directions: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 6\nActivity 14: Rizal and the Filipino Youth · skill: Reading to Follow Precise Directions · module PDF page 37\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nRizal and the Filipino Youth (PDF page 37)\nRead the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nRizal and the Filipino Youth\nJose Rizal loved his people especially the youth.\n\nJose Rizal believed in the young people of his country. He wrote a patriotic poem entitled, “To the Filipino Youth”, dedicated to his countrymen. In this poem, he called the young Filipino the “fair hope of his fatherland”.\n\nRizal also wrote “Noli Me Tangere”. In this novel, he called on the youth to serve their country. He called on them to study harder and do the best they could to achieve peace and progress for the Philippines.\n\nCopy the name of the poem Rizal wrote.\n\nTo whom did he dedicate the poem?\nA. to his mother\nB. to his countrymen\nC. to the Filipino youth\n\nWrite the name of the book Rizal wrote.\n\nCopy the word that describes what kind of poem “To The Filipino Youth” is.\n\nWhat was Rizal’s counsel to the youth?\nA. to study hard\nB. to write books\nC. to take up arms\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 58\n51 \nAnswer Key: \nPre-Test \nSkill-A  \nNoting \nDetails \nSkill-B \nGetting \nSignificance \nof a \nSelection \nSkill-C \nPredicting \nOutcomes \nof a given \nevent \nSkill-D \nReading to \nFollow \nPrecise \nDirections \nSkill-E \nReading for \nSpeed and \nComprehension \nThe Face \nin the \nPool1 \nJay’s \nCuriosity \nRice \nWally’s Kite \nA Fairy Tale \nfrom Japan \n1. A \n1. B