(441,22,19218,'Activity 18','Birds of Paradise','Reading Exercises for Speed: read the story and answer the questions.','Graded Reading Comprehension · Level 6 · Grade 6\nActivity 18: Birds of Paradise · skill: Reading Exercises for Speed · module PDF pages 43, 44\n\nPupils read the story on its own card (Listen reads it aloud), then answer one question per card. Choices, Reality/Fantasy, ordering of events and cause-and-effect matching follow the module. Speed-reading stories have a timer that records the reading time and words per minute.\n\nBirds of Paradise (PDF pages 43, 44)\nDirection: Fill in the blanks in each sentence with the correct answer.\n\nBirds of Paradise\nNo bird in the world has a brighter color than the birds of paradise. No one can guess from their color that they belong to the crow family.\n\nMost of the feathers of the birds of paradise have queer shapes. Some are used in making beautiful plumes. The male birds have bright colored feathers while the females’ feathers are dull in color. Some male birds of paradise do a great deal of dancing and strutting when they are courting.\n\nThere are about forty-five kinds of birds of paradise. They live mostly in the hot rainforest of the island of New Guinea. A few kinds are found in Australia.\n\nFor a time, there was a danger that all birds of paradise would be killed for their feathers. But now there are laws to protect them. So, we expect the species of the birds of paradise to be multiplied in the future.\n\nThe story is about __________.\nA. children\nB. animal\nC. birds\n\nThey are called ___________.\nA. birds of prey\nB. birds of paradise\nC. birds of feathers\n\nThey belong to the ___________ family.\nA. crow\nB. maya\nC. owl\n\nThe male bird’s feathers have ___________.\nA. dull colors\nB. bright colors\nC. green color\n\nThe female birds’ feathers have ___________.\nA. dull colors\nB. bright colors\nC. green color\n\nThe male birds of paradise do a lot of ___________ when courting.\nA. dancing and strutting\nB. jumping and bending\nC. kicking and prancing\n\nBirds of paradise are of __________.\nA. twenty kinds\nB. fifteen kinds\nC. forty-two kinds\n\nThey live mostly in the rainforest of ___________.\nA. New Zealand\nB. New Guinea\nC. New Jersey\n\nA few kinds of birds of paradise are found in ___________.\nA. America\nB. Australia\nC. Amsterdam\n\nWith the passage of laws protecting birds, we can expect them to ______.\nA. become household pets.\nB. multiply rapidly\nC. disappear in no time at all\n\nANSWER KEY (teacher only)\n\nPDF page 58\n51 \nAnswer Key: \nPre-Test \nSkill-A  \nNoting \nDetails \nSkill-B \nGetting \nSignificance \nof a \nSelection \nSkill-C \nPredicting \nOutcomes \nof a given \nevent \nSkill-D \nReading to \nFollow \nPrecise \nDirections \nSkill-E \nReading for \nSpeed and \nComprehension \nThe Face \nin the \nPool1 \nJay’s \nCuriosity \nRice \nWally’s Kite \nA Fairy Tale \nfrom Japan \n1. A \n1. B \n1. C \n1. answers \nmay vary \n1. B \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. A \n2. A \n3. A \n3. C \n3. A \n3. answers \nmay vary \n3. C \n4. A \n4. \n4.  \n4. \n4. C \n5. B \n5. \n5. \n5. \n5. A \n6. \n6. \n6. \n6. \n6. B \n7. \n7. \n7. \n7. \n7. C \n8. \n8. \n8. \n8. \n8. A \n9. \n9. \n9. \n9. \n9. B \n10. \n10. \n10. \n10. \n10. A \nSkill-A Noting Details \nRules to Follow \nDaphne \nHow the \nRomans \nCooked their \nFood \nFirst Night in the \nCity \n1. A \n1. A \n1. C \n1. A \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. A \n3. A \n3. B \n3. B \n3. B \n4. C \n4. B \n4. A \n4. B \n5. A \n5. B \n5. B \n5. B \n6. A \n6. B \n6. C \n6. C \n7. A \n7. C \n7. C \n7. C \n8. C \n8. B \n8. C \n8. B \n9. C \n9. A \n9. B \n9. B \n10. B \n10. C \n10. C \n10. A\n\nPDF page 59\n52 \nSkill- B Getting the General Significance of a Selection \nExercise 5 \nExercise 6 \nExercise 7 \nExercise 8 \n1. B \n1. B \n1. C \n1. C \n2. B \n2. C \n2. C \n2. B \n3. C \n3. B \n3. B \n3. C \nSkill-C Predicting Outcome of a Given Event and Making Inferences \nExer \nExercise 10 \nExercise 11 \nE