(964,964,'Sentence Complete\nActivity 19\nDirections:\nSelect the correct word from the list to complete each\nsentence. Write the correct answer in the blank.\namber\ndominion\nsundry\ncapillary\nimpetuous\nblight\nwrest\nenumerate\ndaunted\ncondescend\nimprison\nadjust\nbacteria\noccurrence\naccess\ncalendar\ndivision\ndevelop\ndirect\ndescribe\n1. My mother bought a ______\noranges.\n2. Ants ______\nis situated in the ground.\n3. It is ______\nto take care of ourselves.\n4. Watching basketball is my great ______\n.\n5. He works using the two ______\n.\n6. You’re so ______\n, it’s better to back off.\n7. The book ______\nhas huge information of the subject\nmatter.\n8. Farmers ______\nthe interested ways in Farming.\n9. I am ______\nwith my name on the blackboard.\n10.\nMy ______\nare not ready doing something.\n11.\nDo you have some ______\nin your body?\n12.\nHer smile can ______\na heart.\n13.\nI have a ______\nin my arm.\n14.\nPlease ______\nthe echo of the sound.\n15.\nOh my! I don’t have ______\nin the net.','teacher'),
(965,965,'Paragraph Filling\nActivity 20\nDirections:\nComplete the paragraph by filling on the blank. The first and\nlast letters of each word are given. Use/Select the words\ninisde the box.\nadvance\ndirection\nchilly\nweird\ngallery\nimpossible\nbridge\ngeneral\nabolish\ntrucker\napparatus comment\nelementary perhaps\nnecessity relativity\ncommercial favorite\nbeautiful\nimprint\nAn a ______\ne used of technolog\nis\nvery w ______ d but c ______\ny\nthings\nhappened nowadays. It gives us good\nd ______\nn in c ______\nl and g ______\ny\nof a ______\ns.\nB ______\ne were a ______\nh in very i\n______ e reasons and in easy way that t\n______ r had no c ______\ns.\nAn e ______\ny learners has lots of\nn ______\ny needed that were b\n______ l and their f ______\ne.\nIn g ______\nl these things needed to i\n______ t and p ______\ns required r\n______ y.','teacher'),
(966,966,'POST TEST\nPart A — Numbers (1–4)\n1. Write the number word: 3 = ______\n2. Write the number: “five” = ______\n3. Which is a number?\na) red b) six c) cat\n4. What number comes after 2? ______\nPart B — Colors (5–6)\n5. Which is a color?\na) blue b) run c) book\n6. The sun is ______\n.\na) yellow b) green c) black\nPart C — Adverbs (7–8)\n7. Choose the adverb:\na) quickly b) dog\nc) chair\n8. “She sings softly.”\nThe adverb is: ______\nPart D — Antonyms (Opposites) (9–10)\n9. Opposite of hot is ______\n.\n10. Opposite of big:\na) small b) tall c) long\nPart E — Synonyms (Same meaning) (11–12)\n11. “Happy” means the same as:\na) sad b) glad\nc) angry\n12. “Start” means the same as ______\n.\na) begin\nb) stop c) sleep\nPart F — Nouns (13–14)\n13. Which is a noun?\na) pencil\nb) run c) quickly\n14. Which is a place?\na) school b) blue c) jump','teacher'),
(967,967,'Part G — Body Parts (15–16)\n15 You hear with your ______\na) tail\nb) nose c) wing\n16.An animal has a ______\nto move (example: horse).\nb) tail b) nose c) wing\nPart H — Mini Crossword (17–18)\n17. Across (3 letters): A pet that barks:\n18. Down (5 letters): Color of grass:\nPart I — Word Hunt (19)\n19. Find two animals with wings in this string:\nCATDOGHORSEBIRDSUN\nPart J — Matching (20)\n20. Match:\nc) red\n1. a color\nd) run\n2. to move fast\nWrite: a– ______\n, b– ______','teacher'),
(968,968,'Read the stories. Then read the questions carefully and encircle the correct answer.\n\nPre-test\nI am Miko. I have a friend. His name is Tito.\nTito is a smart cat. I feed him rice and fish.\nTito and I play together.\n\nDana’s father is a farmer. He goes to farm every day.\nHe works on the farm. He plants rice and corn on the farm.\n\nDolly is a doll. She has long and curly hair.\nIsn’t she pretty? She has a nice dress.\nI bring her to school. I love Dolly so much!\n\nTito is a smart ______.\na. cat\nb. dog\nc. pig\n\nTito eats ______.\na. rice and fish\nb. rice and meat\nc. rice and fruits\n\nDana’s father is a ______?\na. farmer\nb. driver\nc. teacher\n\nWhat did he plant on the farm?\na. Ubi and gabi\nb. Rice and corn\nc. Sugar cane\n\nWho is Dolly?\na. a cat\nb. a doll\nc. a rat','teacher'),
(969,969,'Direction: Write R for Reality and F for Fantasy on the space provided.\n\nA cow can give milk.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe baby goats drink milk from their mother goat.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe grass eats the mother goat.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nHorse Lino, goat Lucy and cow Lita are animals.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nCows and goats can give milk and meat too.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(970,970,'Direction: Put the sentences in order by arranging the events listed below. Write numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 on the blank provided.\n\nItems 11–15. Write 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event to show the order.\n11. Bear finds a den.\n12. Bear eats a lot.\n13. Bear eats even more!\n14. Bear fills the den with leaves, so he will stay warm.\n15. Bear needs to get ready for winter.','teacher'),
(971,971,'Direction: Draw a line to match each cause on the left with its effect on the right.\n\nColumn A\n16. Matt did not study\n17. To ease the pain\n18. Jean went inside her house\n19. Jean was unhappy\n20. Emmy got a wound\nA. because she fell off her bike.\nB. because the wind blows her balloon.\nC. Emmy eats an ice cream.\nD. so he failed the Math test.\nE. because she was tired.','teacher'),
(972,972,'Read the story. Then choose the correct answer.\n\nMy Friend\nI am Miko.\nI have a friend.\nHis name is Tito.\nTito is a smart cat.\nI feed him rice and fish.\nTito and I play together.\n\nWho has a friend?\nA. Bobby\nB. Miko\nC. Peter\n\nWhat is the name of his friend?\nA. Dodo\nB. Sano\nC. Tito\n\nTito is a smart ______.\na. cat\nb. dog\nc. pig\n\nTito eats ______.\na. rice and fish\nb. rice and meat\nc. rice and fruits\n\nWho plays together?\na. Tita and all\nb. Tito and you\nc. Tito and I','teacher'),
(973,973,'Read the story. Then choose the correct answer.\n\nThe Farmer\nDana’s father is a farmer.\nHe goes to the farm every day.\nHe works on the farm.\nHe plants rice and corn on the farm.\n\nWhat is the work of Dana’s father?\nA. driver\nB. farmer\nC. teacher\n\nWhere does her father work?\nA. hospital\nB. office\nC. farm\n\nWhen does my father go to the farm?\nA. everyday\nB. yesterday\nC. today\n\nWhat does he plant on the farm?\nA. ubi and gabi\nB. rice and corn\nC. sugar cane\n\nWhat animals were fed by father?\nA. chicken and goat\nB. cows and pigs\nC. dogs and cats','teacher'),
(974,974,'Read the story. Then choose the correct answer.\n\nMy Dolly\nThis is Dolly.\nDolly is a doll.\nShe has long and curly hair.\nIsn’t she pretty?\nShe has a nice dress.\nI bring her to school.\nI love Dolly so much!\n\nWho is Dolly?\nA. a cat\nB. a doll\nC. a rat\n\nHow will you describe Dolly’s hair?\nA. curly\nB. red\nC. short\n\nWhat does Dolly look like?\nA. lazy\nB. pretty\nC. ugly\n\nWhich nice thing does Dolly have?\nA. cap\nB. dress\nC. shoes\n\nWhere does she bring Dolly?\nA. to market\nB. to school\nC. to plaza','teacher'),
(975,975,'Read the story. Then choose the correct answer.\n\nJoy’s Aya\nJoy has a pet. Her pet is a goat.\nShe calls her Aya. Aya’s color is brown.\nShe is 2 years old. Aya is so cute.\nJoy plays with Aya every day.\nShe eats healthy grasses.\nAya is very healthy. Joy likes Aya.\n\nWhat was Joy’s pet?\nA. a cat\nB. a dog\nC. a goat\n\nWhat is the name of her pet?\nA. Aya\nB. Dina\nC. Mary\n\nWhat was Aya’s color?\nA. brown\nB. red\nC. red\n\nWhat does Aya like to eat?\nA. fruits\nB. healthy grasses\nC. vegetables\n\nHow old is Aya?\nA. 1\nB. 2\nC. 3','teacher'),
(976,976,'Direction: Read the story. Write R for Reality and F for Fantasy on the space provided.\n\nLita the Cow\nLita is a cow.\nShe lived with her family on the farm.\nShe’s a mother to a cute calf.\nHer partner is a strong and big bull.\nLita eats grass.\nShe gives milk.\nShe spends time playing with her friends.\nHorse Lino and goat Lucy were her friends.\n\nA cow can give milk.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nCow Lita is a goat.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nCow Lita is a strong horse.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nHorse Lino, goat Lucy and cow Lita are animals.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nCow Lita eats grass.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(977,977,'Direction: Read the selection. Write R for Reality and F for Fantasy on the space provided.\n\nRosa’s Goat\nRosa has a goat. It is a mother goat.\nIt has two baby goats.\nMother goat eats grass.\nThe mother goat is black.\nThe babies are black and white.\nThe baby goats drink milk from their mother goat.\nRosa has three goats.\n\nRosa is a goat.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe mother goat has five baby goats.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe baby goats drink milk from their mother goat.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe grass eats the mother goat.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nMother goat has milk.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(978,978,'Direction: Read the selection. Write R for Reality and F for Fantasy on the space provided.\n\nBenny’s Happy Day\nBenny the bear loved to play.\nHe played in his backyard.\nHe saw a bright sun shining in the sky.\nBenny picked a colorful flower for his mom.\nHe felt happy and danced with his friends.\nBenny said, “Today is the best day ever”.\n\nBenny was playing in his backyard.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nHe picked a golden crown for his mom.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nHe was playing with animals and fairies.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nPicking flowers for Benny’s mother made him happy.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nFinally, Benny said, “Today is the best day ever”.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(979,979,'Direction: Read the selection. Write R for Reality and F for Fantasy on the space provided.\n\nLily’s Magical Pet\nLily had a stuffed rabbit named Fluffy.\nOne day, she wished Fluffy could talk and play with her.\nSuddenly, in her dream, Fluffy spoke and said, “Let’s play hide-and-seek!”\nThey played together in a magical garden.\nBut when Lily woke up, Fluffy was just a stuffed animal again.\n\nLily had a stuffed rabbit.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nShe wished Fluffy to talk and play with her.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nLily and Fluffy played in a magical garden.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nLily dreamed of playing Fluffy in a magical garden.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nFluffy was talking in real life.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(980,980,'Direction: Write the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\n\nA Cold Bear\nBrrrr! It is getting cold.\nBear needs to get ready for winter.\nFirst, he eats a lot.\nNext, he finds a den.\nThen, he fills the den with leaves, so he will stay warm.\nLast, he eats even more.\nIs Bear ready for winter?\nYes, he is. Winter is here!\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\nBear finds a den.\nBear eats a lot.\nBear eats even more!\nBear fills the den with leaves, so he will stay warm.\nBear needs to get ready for winter.','teacher'),
(981,981,'Direction: Write the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\n\nDessert Time!\nYum! It is time for dessert.\nFirst, Tim gets a cookie.\nNext, Jan gets some cake.\nThe, Ben gets a donut.\nLast, Deb gets some pie.\nOh no! There is nothing left for Sam.\nWhat will he do?\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\nJan gets some cake.\nTim gets a cookie.\nBen gets a donut.\nIt is time for dessert.\nDeb gets some pie.','teacher'),
(982,982,'Direction: Read the story and write the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\n\nHarry and the Crayon\nHarry was dreaming.\nOne day, he went for a walk.\nHe took his bag with crayons.\nHe sat under the trees.\nHe laid his bag and drew.\nHe uses his crayons to draw.\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\nHe took his bag with crayons.\nHe sits under the trees.\nHarry was dreaming.\nHe lays his bag and draws.\nHe uses his crayons to draw.','teacher'),
(983,983,'Direction: Read the story and write the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\n\nMy Pet\nI have a pet.\nMy pet is a dog.\nHis name is Whitey.\nMy pet’s color is white.\nIt eats fish and bones.\nHe likes to play hide and seek.\nI love my pet dog.\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\nI love my pet dog.\nIt eats fish and bones.\nI have a pet.\nHis name is Whitey.\nHe likes to play hide and seek.','teacher'),
(984,984,'Direction: Read the story. Draw a line to match each cause on the left with its effect on the right.\n\nHappenings\nEmmy got a wound because she fell off her bike.\nTo ease the pain, she eats ice cream.\nHer brother Matt did not study so he failed the Math test.\nJean, her younger sister was unhappy because the wind blew her balloons away.\nShe also went inside the house because she was tired.\n\nColumn A\n1. Matt did not study.\n2. To ease the pain.\n3. Jean went inside her house.\n4. Jean was unhappy.\n5. She fell off her bike.\nA. so Emmy got a wound.\nB. because the wind blows her balloon.\nC. Emmy eats ice cream.\nD. there he failed the Math test.\nE. because she is tired.','teacher'),
(985,985,'Direction: Read the story. Draw a line to match each cause on the left with its effect on the right.\n\nActivities\nIt rained all night, so the classes were cancelled.\nThe children are played all day long.\nLuis went biking with his friends and fell off his bike, so his knee got wounded.\nMary ran fast to Luis, so she slipped.\nHector got scared so he went home.\nBut Letty studied for the next test, so she got an A grade.\n\nColumn A\n1. Hector got scared\n2. Letty studied for the next test.\n3. Luis fell off his bike\n4. It rained all night\n5. Mary ran fast to Luis\nA. so she slipped.\nB. so his knee got wounded.\nC. so the classes are cancelled.\nD. so she got an A grade.\nE. so he went home.','teacher'),
(986,986,'Direction: Read the story. Draw a line to match each cause on the left with its effect on the right.\n\nGood Ways\nOne Sunday, Lino went to the garden to check the vegetables he planted.\nWhile walking fast, a branch hit his forehead.\nHe picked malunggay leaves, crushed them, and rubbed them on his forehead.\nHe continued gathering vegetables to bring them home.\nHe cooked them to have a delicious viand.\n\nColumn A\n1. Lino went to the garden.\n2. He picked malunggay leaves\n3. Lino cooked the vegetables\n4. He was walking fast\n5. To be able to bring home some vegetables\nA. so they had a delicious viand.\nB. when a branch hit his head.\nC. and rubbed it on his forehead\nD. he gathered it.\nE. to check the vegetables.','teacher'),
(987,987,'Direction: Read the story. Draw a line to match each cause on the left with its effect on the right.\n\nSnowball Fight\nIt was winter. The snow fell and it was very cold.\nTim and Mark put on their mittens and boots then went outside to make snowballs.\nJan throw a snowball, but he was not fast enough. Mark saw him and threw one first.\nIt hit Jan and knock him down.\n\nColumn A\n1. Jan was hit by a snowball\n2. Mark throws a snowball\n3. They went outside\n4. The snow fell\n5. They saw a bear\nA. the snowball knocks Jan down.\nB. they ran fast.\nC. because he was not fast enough.\nD. it was very cold.\nE. to make snowballs.','teacher'),
(988,988,'Read the sentence in each number and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nAlex and His Mother\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nAlex’s mother checked her list while she carried a basket. They went up from one lane to another. This perhaps means that:\n☐ Alex and his mother went to a picnic.\n☐ Alex and his mother went to a store.\n\nThe basket was getting full. They now had vegetables, fruits, and bananas in the basket. They still needed to get milk, bread, eggs, and rice. This perhaps means that:\n☐ Alex and his mother were buying.\n☐ Alex and his mother were eating.\n\nMany people are waiting in a lane, with baskets full of groceries. Then they stood in line and waited from one customer to another. This probably means that:\n☐ Alex and his mother were paying for their groceries.\n☐ Alex and his mother were choosing from the shelves.\n\nAlex hoped they would get home in time as they waited for a ride for him to go outside and play for a while. This probably means that:\n☐ Alex and his mother are at the park.\n☐ Alex and his mother are at the bus terminal.\n\nHis mother put their groceries on top of the table. He went to his room and immediately changed his clothes. This probably means that:\n☐ Alex and his mother are now inside their house.\n☐ Alex and his mother are now inside the bus.','teacher'),
(989,989,'Read the sentence in each number and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nKaren’s Puzzle\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nKaren opened a puzzle box and put all the pieces out on the table. This perhaps means that:\n☐ the box was full of fruits.\n☐ the box was full of pictures.\n\nAll the pieces would go together to take a picture of a beautiful butterfly. There were 15 different pieces to put together. This perhaps means that:\n☐ Karen is getting annoyed with the puzzle.\n☐ Karen is starting to put together the puzzle.\n\nKaren looked for pieces that looked like the butterfly’s wings. Soon she had four pieces put together. This probably means that:\n☐ Karen is starting to put together a puzzle.\n☐ Karen is coloring a picture with crayons.\n\nShe was very happy with the result. She was proud of herself and she couldn’t wait to show her mom. This probably means that:\n☐ Karen has completed the puzzle.\n☐ Karen did not finish the puzzle.\n\nKaren asked her mother for another puzzle. This time she wants to have a flower. This probably means that:\n☐ Karen liked playing the puzzle.\n☐ Karen does not want to play puzzle again.','teacher'),
(990,990,'Read the sentence in each number and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nWatching a Circus Show\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nFrankie’s dad bought snacks for everyone before they went in to watch the show. This probably means that:\n☐ They will watch a movie.\n☐ They will watch a circus.\n\nFirst, clowns were running around doing tricks and beeping little horns. This probably means that:\n☐ Frankie’s family is at the birthday party.\n☐ Frankie’s family is at the circus show.\n\nThen there was a parade of elephants, horseback riders, and of course, the clowns. Frankie could not stop clapping his hands. This probably means that:\n☐ Frankie is enjoying the show.\n☐ Frankie is scared.\n\nThen there were men and women walking on high wires far up above the ground. Then came the event that Frankie had been waiting for — the lion tamers! This probably means that:\n☐ Frankie’s family is at the circus park.\n☐ Frankie’s family is at the concert hall.\n\nFrankie told his classmates about his Circus experience. His classmates wanted to watch too. This probably means that:\n☐ Frankie is in school.\n☐ Frankie is in his house.','teacher'),
(991,991,'Read the sentence in each number and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nFishing\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nDad and Eric prepare a hook. This probably means that:\n☐ Father and Eric will go farming.\n☐ Father and Eric will go fishing.\n\nEric’s dad helped him put bait on his hook. This probably means that:\n☐ His dad does not want Eric to get hurt.\n☐ His dad does not care about Eric.\n\nHe doesn’t want Eric to be hurt. He showed Eric how to toss his hook out into the water. Then they waited. This probably means that:\n☐ They started fishing.\n☐ They started swimming.\n\nThe hook started to move. When his father took it, it caught a big fish. This probably means that:\n☐ Eric is happy.\n☐ Eric is sad.\n\nWhen they had caught enough fish, Eric’s father started to light a fire. This probably means that:\n☐ Eric’s father will swim.\n☐ Eric’s father will cook the fish they catch.','teacher'),
(992,992,'Direction: Read the questions/sentence carefully and encircle the correct answer.\n\nTito is a smart ______.\na. cat\nb. dog\nc. pig\n\nTito eats ______.\na. rice and fish\nb. rice and meat\nc. rice and fruits\n\nDana’s father is a ______?\na. farmer\nb. driver\nc. teacher\n\nWhat did he plant on the farm?\na. Ubi and gabi\nb. Rice and corn\nc. Sugar cane\n\nWho is Dolly?\na. a cat\nb. a doll\nc. a rat','teacher'),
(993,993,'Direction: Write R for Reality and F for Fantasy on the space provided.\n\nA cow can give milk.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe baby goats drink milk from their mother goat.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe grass eats the mother goat.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nHorse Lino, goat Lucy and cow Lita are animals.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nCows and goats can give milk and meat too.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(994,994,'Direction: Put the sentences in order by arranging the events listed below. Write numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 in the blank provided.\n\nItems 11–15. Write 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event to show the order.\n11. Bear finds a den.\n12. Bear eats a lot.\n13. Bear eats even more!\n14. Bear fills the den with leaves, so he will stay warm.\n15. Bear needs to get ready for winter.','teacher'),
(995,995,'Direction: Draw a line to match each cause on the left with its effect on the right.\n\nColumn A\n16. Matt did not study\n17. To ease the pain\n18. Jean went inside her house\n19. Jean was unhappy\n20. Emmy got a wound\nA. because she fell off her bike.\nB. because the wind blows her balloon.\nC. Emmy eats an ice cream.\nD. so he failed the Math test.\nE. because she was tired.','teacher'),
(996,996,'Direction: Read carefully and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nI have ______.\na. a car\nb. a friend\nc. a pen\n\nHis name is ______.\na. Ben\nb. Tim\nc. Tom\n\nI went to the ______.\na. farm\nb. park\nc. zoo\n\nWho lived in the palace?\na. King\nb. Princess\nc. Queen\n\nWhere does the king sit to watch people?\na. balcony\nb. inside the palace\nc. office','teacher'),
(997,997,'Direction: On the space provided, write R if it is Reality, F if it is Fantasy.\n\nBirds can be seen in the park.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe birds hop in the trees.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nBirds read books in the library.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe birds drive cars to school.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nBirds fluff their feathers.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(998,998,'Direction: Arrange the events below in the correct order. Write numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 in the blank before each event.\n\nItems 11–15. Write 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event to show the order.\n11. They is stories about brave kids.\n12. She loved to read about Sherlock Holmes, who was a brave detective.\n13. She loves to read.\n14. She is quiet girl.\n15. She loved adventures stories.','teacher'),
(999,999,'Direction: After reading through each cause, put a check (✓) on the effect that appears most likely to happen.\n\nCause: A boy fell off his bike so…\n☐ he rode his bike again.\n☐ he got wounds on his knee.\n\nCause: It snowed at night so…\n☐ the classes were cancelled.\n☐ they had a picnic.\n\nCause: Jan studied hard so…\n☐ she passed the test.\n☐ she failed the test.\n\nCause: She fell off her bike so…\n☐ she bruised her arm.\n☐ she ate ice cream.\n\nCause: Amora did not study so…\n☐ she failed the exam.\n☐ she got the highest score.','teacher'),
(1000,1000,'Direction: Read the selection and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nIn the Barn\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nThe animals came to the barn. They told each other about their moms. “My mom lay me in the nest,” the chick said. This perhaps means that…\n☐ The chick came out of an egg.\n☐ The chick came out of a shell.\n\n“My mom is a big animal,” the calf said. “She eats plenty of grass to produce milk for me.” This perhaps means that…\n☐ The calf’s mom gives him milk.\n☐ The calf’s mom gives him an egg.\n\n“My mother stays in the pen. She says oink, oink, oink,” said the piglet. This perhaps means that…\n☐ The sound the piglet makes is also oink, oink, oink.\n☐ The sound the piglet makes is also meow, meow, meow.\n\n“My mom loves to eat bones. She guards the master’s house.” This perhaps means that…\n☐ Her mom is a dog who barks at strangers.\n☐ Her mom is a cat who calls at strangers.\n\n“My mother pulls carriages and can be used for transportation,” said the baby horse. This perhaps means that…\n☐ His mother is hardworking.\n☐ His mother just sleeps all day.','teacher'),
(1001,1001,'Activity 1.1 · Noting details is answering questions from the story read. Direction: Read the story and figure it out for yourself. Choose the correct answer of the questions.\n\nA Friend\nI have a friend.\nHis name is Tim.\nTim is nice and cool.\nTim has a remote-control car.\nIt is big and blue.\nWe play with the car.\n\nI have ______.\na. a car\nb. a friend\nc. a pen\n\nHis name is ______.\na. Tim\nb. Tom\nc. Ben\n\nTim is nice and ______.\na. bad\nb. cool\nc. friendly\n\nTim has ______.\na. a car\nb. a friend\nc. a pen\n\nThe remote-control car is ______.\na. big and blue\nb. big and green\nc. big and red','teacher'),
(1002,1002,'Activity 1.2 · Noting details is answering questions from the story read. Direction: Read the selection and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nMy Pet Dog\nI have a pet dog.\nHis name is Sam.\nHe was given to me by my cousin.\nSam likes to run and catch a ball.\nI take Sam to the park every day.\n\nI have a pet ______.\nA. cat\nB. pig\nC. dog\n\nMy pet’s name is ______.\nA. Cam\nB. Dan\nC. Sam\n\nSam was given by my ______.\nA. Aunt\nB. cousin\nC. lola\n\nSam likes to ______.\nA. catch a ball\nB. walk in the park\nC. swim in the pond\n\nI take Sam to the ______.\nA. farm\nB. market\nC. park','teacher'),
(1003,1003,'Activity 1.3 · Noting details is answering questions from the story read. Direction: Read the selection and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nAt the Farm\nI went to the farm with my family.\nI saw a big horse at the farm.\nThe horse was brown.\nThe horse was eating grass.\nThe horse can run fast.\n\nI went to the ______.\nA. farm\nB. park\nC. zoo\n\nI was with my ______.\nA. classmates\nB. family\nC. friends\n\nI saw a ______ horse.\nA. big\nB. cute\nC. small\n\nThe horse was eating ______.\nA. fruits\nB. grass\nC. vegetables\n\nThe horse can ______.\nA. jump\nB. run fast\nC. sing','teacher'),
(1004,1004,'Activity 1.4 · Noting details is answering questions from the story read. Direction: Read the selection and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nThe King\nIn a small Kingdom, a good King lives.\nHis palace is very big.\nIt is on top of the hill.\nEvery morning, he would sit on his balcony and look down at his kingdom.\nHis people make him happy.\n\nWhat is the story all about?\na. The King\nb. The man\nc. The Queen\n\nThe King lives in a very big ______.\na. cave\nb. house\nc. palace\n\nThe palace is on top of the ______.\na. hill\nb. mountain\nc. volcano\n\nWhere does the king sit to watch people?\na. balcony\nb. inside the palace\nc. office\n\nWhat makes the King happy?\na. foods\nb. money\nc. people','teacher'),
(1005,1005,'Activity 2.1 · Fantasy or Reality tells if the events in the story are real or not. Reality is something that is real and can happen in everyday life; fantasy is imaginary and not real. Direction: Write R for reality and F for fantasy on the space provided.\n\nAt the Park\nI love to go bird watching at the park. I see a lot of birds there and they are all so colorful! Their little feet are so funny, too. They hop along and then they stretch out their wings and fluff up their feathers.\n\nBirds can be seen in the park.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe birds hop in the trees.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nBirds read books in the library.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nI watch birds at the farm.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nBirds fluff their feathers.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(1006,1006,'Activity 2.2 · Fantasy or Reality tells if the events in the story are real or not. Direction: Write R for reality and F for fantasy on the space provided.\n\nMolly Reads Stories\nWhen Molly is reading a story, she is looking for clues in the text to help her visualize what is happening. She looks at the pictures and uses them to create a mental image of the story.\n\nShe also reads quickly, which helps her to understand what is happening in the story. When she has finishes reading, she is able to give a detailed description of the story. This shows that Molly is a good reader.\n\nMolly is reading a story.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nShe looks at the picture to create an image of the story.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe pictures in the book laughed at Molly.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nAfter reading, the books quickly jumped with Molly.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nMolly reads quickly.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(1007,1007,'Activity 2.3 · Fantasy or Reality tells if the events in the story are real or not. Direction: Write R for reality and F for fantasy on the space provided.\n\nRex’s New Dog\nRex got a new dog.\nHe loves to play with his dog.\nSometimes Rex takes his dog to the park.\nThey run and jump and chase each other.\nRex throws a ball for his dog.\nRex has fun with his new dog.\n\nRex got a new dog.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nHis dog could fly over the clouds.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nRex takes his dog to the park.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nRex and his dog barked each other.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe dog went shopping at the mall.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(1008,1008,'Activity 2.4 · Fantasy or Reality tells if the events in the story are real or not. Direction: In the space provided, put a (✓) in the box if it is reality and (X) for fantasy.\n\nMy New Pet\nMy dad bought me a new pet.\nShe is a cute little kitten.\nShe is always sleepy.\nMy dad and mom help me keep the kitten clean.\nWe have a box house where she stays.\nI always play with her in the evening when I come home from school.\nShe is growing quickly. I love my new pet.\n\nHer dad bought her a new pet cat.\n✓ Reality\nX Fantasy\n\nHer pet clean the house with her parents.\n✓ Reality\nX Fantasy\n\nThe cat keeps on barking loudly.\n✓ Reality\nX Fantasy\n\nShe loves her cat’s long horn.\n✓ Reality\nX Fantasy\n\nShe plays with her cat in the evening.\n✓ Reality\nX Fantasy','teacher'),
(1009,1009,'Activity 3.1 · Sequencing/Arranging Events. Direction: Put the sentences in order by arranging the events listed below. Before each event, write the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 in the blank.\n\nPushing and Pulling\nWe move things by pushing them or by pulling them. Heavy things are hard to move. We want to move things; we push them and move them away from us. Wheels make it easier to push heavy things. Engines help to push big and heavy things. When we want to move things, we pull them, we move them towards us. Wheels made it easier to pull heavy things. A cart has wheels to make it easier to pull the cart. Trucks have engines and wheels.\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\nEngines help to push big and heavy things.\nHeavy things are hard to move.\nWheels make it easier to push heavy things.\nTrucks have engines and wheels.\nWe move things by pushing them or by pulling them.','teacher'),
(1010,1010,'Activity 3.2 · Sequencing/Arranging Events. Direction: Put the sentences in order by arranging the events listed below. Before each event, write the number 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 in the blank.\n\nAnn\nAnn rides her bicycle. Ann rides her little bicycle to work. Ann rides her little red bicycle to work every day. Nina rides the bus. Nina rides the big bus to work. Nina rides the big slow bus to work every day.\n\nLuis rides the train. Luis rides the big train work. Luis rides the big fast train to work every day.\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\nLuis rides the big fast train to work every day.\nNina rides the big bus to work.\nAnn rides her bicycle.\nLuis rides the train.\nNina rides the big slow bus to work every day.','teacher'),
(1011,1011,'Activity 3.3 · Sequencing/Arranging Events. Direction: Put the sentences in order by arranging the events listed below. Before each event, write the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 in the blank.\n\nMy Mom\nI am George and she is my mother Franklin. My mother is a doctor. She plays with me and tells me bedtime stories. She takes good care of everyone in the family. She is the best mother in the world.\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\nShe takes good care of everyone in the family.\nHer name is George, and she is her mother, Franklin.\nShe is the best mother in the world.\nMy mother is a doctor.\nShe plays with George and tells her bedtime stories.','teacher'),
(1012,1012,'Activity 3.4 · Sequencing/Arranging Events. Direction: Read the story. Then write 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\n\nPearl\nThis is Pearl. She is a quiet girl.\nShe loves to read.\nShe loves adventure stories.\nThey are stories about brave kids.\nShe loves to read about Sherlock Holmes, who is a brave detective.\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event as they happen in the story.\nIt is stories about brave kids.\nShe loved to read about Sherlock Holmes, who was a brave detective.\nShe loves to read.\nShe is a quiet girl.\nShe loved adventures stories.','teacher'),
(1013,1013,'Activity 4.1 · Cause and Effect. A cause is WHY something happens. The effect is WHAT happens as a result. Read and check the most likely effect of each cause.\n\nCause: The dog saw a squirrel…\n☐ it barked and chased it.\n☐ it fell asleep.\n\nCause: It’s Gab’s birthday so…\n☐ her mom baked a cake.\n☐ Her mom scolded him.\n\nCause: The kids are on a field trip at the zoo so…\n☐ they took a Math test.\n☐ they saw a crocodile.\n\nCause: When Ana was little, she was afraid of the chickens on her grandpa’s farm. Because Ana was afraid of the chickens,…\n☐ Her grandpa no longer brings her to the farm.\n☐ Her grandpa showed him how to shoo the chickens away.\n\nCause: Ana learned to shoo the chickens away by waving her hand, saying “Shoo chicks, shoo chicks!” Because Ana learned to shoo the chickens away,…\n☐ She’s no longer afraid of the chickens anymore.\n☐ she played with his grandpa’s dog.','teacher'),
(1014,1014,'Activity 4.2 · Cause and Effect. After reading through each cause, put a check (✓) on the effect that appears most likely to happen.\n\nCause: A boy fell off his bike so…\n☐ he rode his bike again.\n☐ he got wounds on his knee.\n\nCause: It snowed last night thus…\n☐ the classes are cancelled.\n☐ they had a picnic.\n\nCause: Jane studied hard therefore…\n☐ she passed the test.\n☐ she failed the test.\n\nCause: Mary bruised her arm because…\n☐ she fell off her bike.\n☐ she ate a lot of ice cream.\n\nCause: Amara did not study so…\n☐ she failed the Exam.\n☐ she got the highest score.','teacher'),
(1015,1015,'Activity 4.3 · Cause and Effect. After reading through each cause, put a check (✓) on the effect that appears most likely to happen.\n\nCause: Because the wind blew away her balloon,…\n☐ little Cherry was unhappy.\n☐ little Cherry was tired.\n\nCause: Bell’s shirt was dirty because…\n☐ she played outside.\n☐ she watched too much TV.\n\nCause: Arman’s car is in the shop because…\n☐ It needs a new tire.\n☐ It needs to eat.\n\nCause: The bottle is empty so…\n☐ I will throw it in the garbage.\n☐ I will put it inside the cabinet.\n\nCause: Because Luis practiced playing basketball every day,…\n☐ he made the winning shot during the game.\n☐ he lost the game.','teacher'),
(1016,1016,'Activity 4.4 · Cause and Effect. After reading through each cause, put a check (✓) on the effect that appears most likely to happen.\n\nCause: Joy got tired after joining the race so…\n☐ she continued running.\n☐ she rested under the tree.\n\nCause: Zen helped her grandpa clean the house so…\n☐ her grandpa bought her a toy.\n☐ Her grandpa scolded her.\n\nCause: Because someone threw a stone at his window,…\n☐ he went to the window and looked.\n☐ he did not mind and continued sleeping.\n\nCause: Since Grandma is not feeling well,…\n☐ she went to the zoo.\n☐ she went to the hospital.\n\nCause: The teacher started to talk which is why…\n☐ the learners run away.\n☐ The learners listened.','teacher'),
(1017,1017,'Activity 5.1 · Drawing conclusions means figuring something out for yourself. To draw conclusions, you need to think about what makes the most sense. Direction: Read the selection and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nGoing to the Movies\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nMy class is going to the movies on the field trip next week. We have to get permission slips signed before we go. This perhaps means that…\n☐ They must get permission from their parents.\n☐ They must ride a bus to go to movies.\n\nWe also need to ask our parents to drive us to the movie theater. This perhaps means that…\n☐ Their parents should not mind them.\n☐ Their parents should accompany them.\n\nWe love it when movies are made from books. It is fun to compare the movie to the book. This perhaps means that…\n☐ They usually like to watch movies based on books.\n☐ They usually liked to read books based on movies.\n\nWe get the movie early so we can buy popcorn. Some of us buy candy too. This perhaps means that…\n☐ They want to eat while watching movie.\n☐ They want to play.\n\nThe movie ended and everyone in the audience clapped. This perhaps means that…\n☐ They wanted to see another movie.\n☐ They all liked the movie.','teacher'),
(1018,1018,'Activity 5.2 · Drawing conclusions means figuring something out for yourself. Direction: Read the selection and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nReward\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nIda was a kind-hearted heart girl. One day when she’s crossing a bridge, she saw two boys on the stream, trying to drown a little dog. This perhaps means that…\n☐ She wanted to join the boys in drowning the dog.\n☐ She ran to the bank to help the dog.\n\nIda was crying loudly, “Oh! How can you be so cruel to that poor little dog?” This perhaps means that…\n☐ She feels sorry for the dog.\n☐ She wants to hit the boys.\n\nOne of the boys said, “Father told us to drown it.” This perhaps means that…\n☐ The boys only followed their father.\n☐ The boys willingly wants to drown the dog.\n\nThe dog reached the bank and crept toward Ida. “Poor little fellow,” she said, patting him. This perhaps means that…\n☐ She wants to throw the dog away.\n☐ She wants to adopt the dog.\n\n“Yes,” said the boys, “You may have him.” Ida thanked the boys and ran home. This perhaps means that…\n☐ Ida is going home happy.\n☐ Ida is going home sad.','teacher'),
(1019,1019,'Activity 5.3 · Drawing conclusions means figuring something out for yourself. Direction: Read the selection and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nIn the Barn\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nThe animals came to the barn. They told each other about of their moms. “My mom laid me in the nest,” the chick said. This perhaps means that…\n☐ The chick came out of an egg.\n☐ The chick came out of a shell.\n\n“My mom is a big animal,” the calf said. “She eats plenty of grass to produce milk for me.” This perhaps means that…\n☐ The calf’s mom gives him milk.\n☐ The calf’s mom gives him an egg.\n\n“My mother stays in the pen. She says oink, oink, oink,” said the piglet. This perhaps means that…\n☐ The sound the piglet makes is also oink, oink, oink.\n☐ The sound the piglet meow, meow, meow.\n\n“My mom loves to eat bones. She guards the master’s house.” This perhaps means that…\n☐ Her mom is a dog who barks at strangers.\n☐ Her mom is a cat who calls at strangers.\n\n“My mother pulls carriages and can be used for transportation,” said the baby horse. This perhaps means that…\n☐ His mom is hardworking.\n☐ His mother just sleeps all day.','teacher'),
(1020,1020,'Activity 5.4 · Drawing conclusions means figuring something out for yourself. Direction: Read the selection and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nRod and Wena\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nRod is a farmer. This perhaps means that…\n☐ He lives on the farm.\n☐ He lives in the sea.\n\nHe has a pet cow. His pet’s name begins with W. This perhaps means that…\n☐ His pet cow is Wena.\n☐ His pet cow is Mimo.\n\nWena gives him milk. She also pulls his cart. This perhaps means that…\n☐ Wena is giver and helpful.\n☐ Wena is bad and ugly.\n\nWena helps him till his land. She is an industrious cow. This perhaps means that…\n☐ Rod is cruel to his pet cow Wena.\n☐ Rod loves his pet cow Wena.\n\nRod feeds Wena. And she loves to eat grass, hay, and pellets. This perhaps means that…\n☐ Rod takes good care of Wena.\n☐ Rod does not care about Wena.','teacher'),
(1021,1021,'Direction: Read carefully and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nI have ______.\na. a car\nb. a friend\nc. a pen\n\nHis name is ______.\na. Ben\nb. Tim\nc. Tom\n\nI went to the ______.\na. farm\nb. park\nc. zoo\n\nWho lived in the palace?\na. King\nb. Princess\nc. Queen\n\nWhere does the king sit to watch people?\na. balcony\nb. inside the palace\nc. office','teacher'),
(1022,1022,'Direction: On the space provided, write R if it is Reality, F if it is Fantasy.\n\nBirds can be seen in the park.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe birds hop in the trees.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nBirds read books in the library.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nThe birds drive cars to school.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy\n\nBirds fluff their feathers.\nR – Reality\nF – Fantasy','teacher'),
(1023,1023,'Direction: Arrange the events below in the correct order. Write numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 in the blank before each event.\n\nItems 11–15. Write 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 before each event to show the order.\n11. They is stories about brave kids.\n12. She loved to read about Sherlock Holmes, who was a brave detective.\n13. She loves to read.\n14. She is quiet girl.\n15. She loved adventures stories.','teacher'),
(1024,1024,'Direction: After reading through each cause, put a check (✓) on the effect that appears most likely to happen.\n\nCause: A boy fell off his bike so…\n☐ he rode his bike again.\n☐ he got wounds on his knee.\n\nCause: It snowed at night so…\n☐ the classes were cancelled.\n☐ they had a picnic.\n\nCause: Jan studied hard so…\n☐ she passed the test.\n☐ she failed the test.\n\nCause: She fell off her bike so…\n☐ she bruised her arm.\n☐ she ate ice cream.\n\nCause: Amora did not study so…\n☐ she failed the exam.\n☐ she got the highest score.','teacher'),
(1025,1025,'Direction: Read the selection and put a check (✓) in the box for the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nIn the Barn\nRead each part of the story on the next cards. Then check (✓) the conclusion that makes the most sense.\n\nThe animals came to the barn. They told each other about their moms. “My mom lay me in the nest,” the chick said. This perhaps means that…\n☐ The chick came out of an egg.\n☐ The chick came out of a shell.\n\n“My mom is a big animal,” the calf said. “She eats plenty of grass to produce milk for me.” This perhaps means that…\n☐ The calf’s mom gives him milk.\n☐ The calf’s mom gives him an egg.\n\n“My mother stays in the pen. She says oink, oink, oink,” said the piglet. This perhaps means that…\n☐ The sound the piglet makes is also oink, oink, oink.\n☐ The sound the piglet makes is also meow, meow, meow.\n\n“My mom loves to eat bones. She guards the master’s house.” This perhaps means that…\n☐ Her mom is a dog who barks at strangers.\n☐ Her mom is a cat who calls at strangers.\n\n“My mother pulls carriages and can be used for transportation,” said the baby horse. This perhaps means that…\n☐ His mother is hardworking.\n☐ His mother just sleeps all day.','teacher'),
(1026,1026,'Directions: Read the story carefully. Choose the correct answer for each question and encircle the letter.\n\nGrowing Vegetables\nLino went with his mother to market. They bought some vegetables. Mother said, “I was able to buy only some with my money. Vegetables cost much now.” Lino was sorry for Mother. They did not have much money. So, he said, “Let’s plant vegetables in our backyard. Then we will not have to buy them in the market.”\n\nSo, Lino planted three plots of tomatoes, two plots of eggplants and a plot of pechay. He had some ampalaya climbing on the bamboo fence. Lino worked in his garden after class every afternoon. He grew more vegetables than they could eat. Mother sold some of them in the market.\n\nWhere did Lino and Mother go?\nA. To the farm\nB. To the market\nC. To school\nD. To the park\n\nWhat did they buy at the market?\nA. Fruits\nB. Clothes\nC. Vegetables\nD. Toys\n\nWhy did Lino suggest planting vegetables in their backyard?\nA. To make their backyard look nice\nB. Because vegetables were expensive at the market\nC. To sell vegetables to neighbors\nD. To have fun in the garden\n\nHow many plots of tomatoes did Lino plant?\nA. One\nB. Two\nC. Three\nD. Four\n\nWhich vegetable was climbing on the bamboo fence?\nA. Pechay\nB. Ampalaya\nC. Eggplant\nD. Tomato','teacher'),
(1027,1027,'Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice.\n\nFowls\nFowls are animals. They are animals with two feet and two wings. Their bodies are covered with feathers. They give us eggs. Their meat is good to eat. Chickens, ducks, turkeys and geese are fowls. They eat grains such as palay and corn. Chickens like worms. Ducks and geese like to swim. They like to stay in the water They are water birds so they have webbed feet. Turkeys have beautiful feathers. The male turkey spreads its tail like a fan. Geese are good watchers. They make loud noise when people come near. Fowls are good to raise. Many people get rich because of poultry raising.\n\nWhat is the main idea of the passage?\nA. Fowls are animals that give eggs and meat.\nB. Fowls only eat grains.\nC. Turkeys have beautiful feathers\n\nWhy are fowls considered useful to humans?\nA. They make loud noises.\nB. They give eggs, meat, and can make people rich through poultry raising.\nC. They like to swim in water.\n\nWhat type of birds are ducks and geese?\nA. Land birds\nB. Water birds with webbed feet\nC. Birds with colorful feathers\n\nWhy do geese make loud noises?\nA. To call other geese\nB. To warn people or act as watchers\nC. To scare chickens\n\nWhat is the general significance of raising fowls?\nA. It provides food and livelihood for people\nB. It helps fowls grow beautiful feathers\nC. It teaches birds to swim','teacher'),
(1028,1028,'Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice.\n\nThe First Monkey\nLong time ago, there was a farmer who had a wide corn field. He grew big and sweet corn. One day he saw some of his corn gone. Many corn plants had no more fruits. He wanted to catch whoever stole his corn.\n\nThe next day he hid behind a big mango tree. He carried a stick. He watched and watched until he saw a man coming. The man passed through a broken part of the fence. The man began to pick some corn.\n\nThe farmer went from behind the mango tree and walked towards the man. The farmer ran after him, but he could not catch up with him. Then he threw the stick at the man. The stick hit the man’s back and became a tail. The man went up the tree. He became smaller and smaller until he became a monkey.\n\nWhen did the story most likely take place?\nA. Last week\nB. Long time ago\nC. Tomorrow\n\nWhat did the farmer have?\nA. A mango tree\nB. A corn field\nC. A garden\n\nWhat words describe the farmer’s corn?\nA. Small and sour\nB. Big and sweet\nC. Rotten and old\n\nWhat happened to the farmer’s corn?\nA. It was eaten by animals\nB. Some of it was gone and plants had no fruits\nC. It was harvested for sale\n\nWhere did the farmer hide to catch the thief?\nA. Behind a mango tree\nB. In a corn plot\nC. On the roof','teacher'),
(1029,1029,'Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the tree with the hive, what could be the result for the bees?\nA. They would continue making honey in the same place.\nB. They might have to find a new place to live.\nC. The queen bee would sting the young bees.\nD. The workers would become lazy.\n\nIf the bees did not make honey from nectar, what might happen?\nA. The young bees might go hungry.\nB. The drones would start collecting nectar.\nC. The hive would produce more nectar.\nD. The workers would leave the swarm.','teacher'),
(1030,1030,'Direction: Read the story below and encircle the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Rainy Day\nIt was a rainy day. The children could not go out to play. So Lucy, Zeny and Perla stayed in the house.\n\n“Let’s cut out paper fruits for our store,” said Lucy.\n\nSo, the girls got their papers, scissors and crayons. Lucy cut out 5 apples. She colored them red. Zeny cut out 6 mangoes. She colored them yellow. Perla cut out 4 guavas. She colored them green. Then they counted their paper fruits.\n\n“Now we have many pretty paper fruits for our store,” said Perla.\n\nWhat kind of day was it?\nA. Sunny\nB. rainy\nC. bright\n\nHow many girls are in the story?\nA. two\nB. three\nC. four\n\nWhere did the girls stay?\nA. In school\nB. In the yard\nC. In the house\n\nWhat did Lucy cut out?\nA. 5 apples\nB. 6 mangoes\nC. 4 guavas\n\nWhat color were the apples?\nA. Green\nB. yellow\nC. red\n\nWhy did the girls couldn’t go out?\nA. Because it’s raining.\nB. Because it’s so hot .\nC. Because it’s stormy.\n\nHow did the girls make use of their time on a rainy day?\nA. cut out paper fruits\nB. played hide-and-seek\nC. cooked soup\n\nIf you were the girls, how would you spend a rainy day?\nA. play under the rain\nB. sleep\nC. read stories','teacher'),
(1031,1031,'Direction: Read the story below and encircle the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Gift from Uncle\nDo you have an uncle living far away? Miriam has. His name is Max. Miriam calls him Uncle Max. He lives in New York.\n\nLast Christmas, Uncle Max sent Miriam a gift. It came in a big box. Father helped her open the box.\n\nThen they saw something very pretty. It had round blue eyes. It had a small red mouth. It had a blue lace dress and curly black hair. Miriam called her Curly. She loved to play with it. What Miriam liked best was the way Curly walked. Miriam would hold one of its hands and they would walk together around the sala. Curly made Miriam happy.\n\nWho is Curly?\nA. a dog\nB. a doll\nC. a cat\n\nWho gave curly to Miriam?\nA. Father\nB. Mother\nC. Uncle Max\n\nWhere does Uncle Max live?\nA. Manila\nB. New York\nC. Cebu\n\nOn what occasion was Curly given?\nA. Christmas\nB. Birthday\nC. Graduation\n\nWho helped Miriam open the box?\nA. Mother\nB. Father\nC. Uncle Max\n\nWhy did Uncle Max send Miriam a gift?\nA. It was Christmas\nB. It was her birthday\nC. It was New Year\n\nWhat would you do if someone will gave you a gift?\nA. say thank you\nB. just smile\nC. do nothing\n\nWhat do you think Miriam felt about the gift she received?\nA. happy\nB. sad\nC. angry','teacher'),
(1032,1032,'Direction: Read the story below and encircle the letter of the correct answer.\n\nTaking Care of Animals\nVictor lives on a farm. He lives with his parents in a little nipa house. Victor has an elder sister named Nora. Victor and Nora help their parents. They help take care of their animals. They have a carabao, a horse, a goat and some chickens. Father takes care of the horse. Victor takes care of the carabao. Mother takes care of the chickens. Nora takes care of the goat. Their animals are all fat and healthy. Each one takes care of his animal very well.\n\nWhere does Victor live?\nA. in the city\nB. on the farm\nC. near the sea\n\nWhich word tells about their house?\nA. Wooden\nB. Cement\nC. Brick\n\nWho is Victor’s sister?\nA. Nina\nB. Nora\nC. Nilda\n\nHow many kinds of animals do they have?\nA. 5\nB. 4\nC. 3\n\nWhich animal do they not have?\nA. carabao\nB. horse\nC. cow\n\nHow do the characters show their love to their animals?\nA. take care of them\nB. hurt them\nC. ignore them\n\nHow can they show their cooperation in the family?\nA. by helping each other in taking care of the animals\nB. by not helping in taking care of the animals\nC. by selling away all the animals\n\nWhat can the family members do to ensure that the animals are healthy?\nA. give them vitamins\nB. give them water\nC. do not feed them','teacher'),
(1033,1033,'Direction: Read the story below and encircle the letter of the correct answer.\n\nLost and Found\nPedro and Jose came to school early. There were no other pupils in the school yet.\n\n“Look,” said Pedro. “There is a handbag under the stairs. It is a girl’s handbag.”\n\nJose got the handbag. He opened it. There was a red pencil, a pink handkerchief and fifty centavos in it.\n\n“Let’s look for the owner of this handbag.”\n\n“Good morning, Mr. Cruz. We found this bag under the stairs,” said Jose.\n\nMr. Cruz smiled and said, “Thank you boys. I shall help you find the owner.”\n\nWhat time was it?\nA. Morning\nB. afternoon\nC. noon\n\nWho are the boys in the story?\nA. Pedro and Juan\nB. Juan and Jose\nC. Pedro and Jose\n\nWhat did the boys find?\nA. a book\nB. a handbag\nC. a box\n\nWhere was the handbag found?\nA. Under the building\nB. Under the tree\nC. Under the stairs\n\nWhat kind of handbag was it?\nA. A girl’s handbag\nB. A man’s handbag\nC. A teacher’s hand bag\n\nWhy do you think finding it was so important to Jose and Pedro?\nA. to show greediness\nB. to show honesty\nC. to show dishonesty\n\nWhat would you do if you found something that was lost?\nA. Keep it.\nB. Tell nobody about it.\nC. Return it to the owner.\n\nWhat other way can they do to find the owner of the bag?\nA. announce it during flag ceremony\nB. keep it\nC. do nothing','teacher'),
(1034,1034,'Direction: Read the story below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nThe Banana Peelings\nCelso was eating bananas one evening. He threw the peelings out of the window. In the morning, Celso got ready for school. He did not want to be late for school. He walked fast.\n\n“Ouch!” Celso cried as he stepped on some banana peelings. Down he fell. His clothes became dirty. His books dropped. “Are you hurt?” Mother asked running to him.\n\n“No, Mother, I’m not hurt,” Celso said as he got up. He looked at the banana peelings.\n\n“Somebody did not use the garbage can,” Mother said. “I threw these peelings last night, Mother. Next time I’ll use the garbage can,” said Celso.\n\nWhat did Celso eat?\nA. Mango\nB. bananas\nC. oranges\n\nWhen did he eat the fruit?\nA. One evening\nB. One morning\nC. One afternoon\n\nWho stepped on the banana peelings?\nA. Father\nB. Mother\nC. Celso\n\nWhere was Celso going?\nA. to church\nB. to market\nC. to school\n\nWho saw Celso fall?\nA. Father\nB. Mother\nC. Sister\n\nHow did Celso feel when he stepped the banana peelings and fell?\nA. regretful\nB. happy\nC. angry\n\nIf you saw banana peelings on the floor, what would you do?\nA. Ignore it .\nB. Call someone to pick it up\nC. Put it in the trash bin\n\nWhat could Celso do next time after eating a banana?\nA. throw it anywhere\nB. put it under the chair\nC. put it in the trash bin','teacher'),
(1035,1035,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nFather who was sitting near the children, stopped reading the newspaper. “Today is Sunday. Two days from now your Uncle Fred will arrive from America. What day will it be?” Father asked.\n\n“It will be Wednesday,” Nestor said.\n\n“No, it will be Friday,” little Nenita said.\n\n“That will be on Tuesday,” ten-year-old Vita said.\n\nWho had the correct answer?\nA. Nestor\nB. Nenita\nC. Vita\n\nWhat is the story about?\nA. the coming of Uncle Fred\nB. the days of the week\nC. on a Sunday\n\nWhich sentence is correct?\nA. All the children have the same answer.\nB. Uncle Fred will come home soon\nC. The children do not like to see Uncle Fred.','teacher'),
(1036,1036,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nNida combed her hair and tied it with a ribbon. She changed her sleeping clothes with working clothes. She got a bolo and went to the garden.\n\n“Are you going to help me today?” Father asked.\n\n“Yes, Father. It’s Saturday and I have nothing to do. Lilia is helping Mother. I would like to help you, too.”\n\nWhat time of the day was it?\nA. night\nB. afternoon\nC. morning\n\nWhich is the best title for the story?\nA. Saturday morning\nB. Helping at home\nC. How to Comb\n\nWhy were Nida and Lilia at home?\nA. It was Saturday\nB. They were sick\nC. To play all day','teacher'),
(1037,1037,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nCarling and his baby brother were by the window. They were watching Father get ready for the market.\n\nFather put a big basket of camote and a sack of corn in the calesa. There was also another basket of fresh vegetables and fruits. “Father, please buy us something to eat when you come home,” Carling said.\n\n“Take good care of your brother and I’ll buy you some.” Father said as he got into his calesa.\n\nWhat was Father’s work?\nA. a carpenter\nB. a fisherman\nC. a farmer\n\nHow did Carling help at home?\nA. by raising chickens\nB. by taking care of his brother\nC. by raising vegetables\n\nWhich sentence is true?\nA. Father got ready for the market in a jeep.\nB. Carling and his baby brother were by the window.\nC. Carling helped Father get ready for the market.','teacher'),
(1038,1038,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n“All flowers are red,” six-year-old Rosa said.\n\n“No, all flowers are yellow,” four-year-old Jose said.\n\n“Come here, you two,” ten-year-old Tina said. “There are white, red, yellow, pink, violet, orange and green flowers. Sampaguita, our national flower is white. Ilang-ilang is green and some daisies are orange. There are red and yellow roses.\n\n“Now I know why flowers are beautiful,” Rosa said. “They are not of the same color.”\n\nWhich is a good title for the story?\nA. Our National Flower\nB. Colors of Flowers\nC. Care of Flowers\n\nWhich is true about flowers?\nA. They have the same color.\nB. They have different colors.\nC. All are white.\n\nWho knows best about flowers?\nA. Jose\nB. Tina\nC. Rosa','teacher'),
(1039,1039,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n“My pet can bark. It guards our house,” Pablo said.\n\n“My pet lives in a cage. It has brightly colored feathers. It can sing and dance,” Paula said.\n\n“Mine has soft hair. It walks lightly. It catches mice,” Nena said. “I have three pets. One barks, another catches mice and the other lives in a cage,” Merla said.\n\nWhat is the story about?\nA. pets\nB. mouse\nC. dogs\n\nWhat is Pablo’s pet?\nA. dog\nB. cat\nC. bird\n\nWhat are Merla’s pets?\nA. dog, bird, cat\nB. fish, bird, hen\nC. cat, carabao, goat','teacher'),
(1040,1040,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nMarina got out of her bed in the middle of the night. She went near mother and said, “Mother, please get up. Let’s go to the toilet.\n\nMother, got up and saw Marina holding her stomach. “I told you not to eat much ice cream yesterday. Did you eat santol and guavas, too?\n\nI did, Mother,” Marina replied.\n\nWhat did Marina do the day before?\nA. ate cake and candies\nB. ate fruits and ice cream\nC. ate peanuts and chicos\n\nWhat does the story tell about Marina?\nA. She got up to study.\nB. She got up to eat santol\nC. She got up to go to the toilet.\n\nWhy was Marina holding her stomach?\nA. It was painful.\nB. She was hungry.\nC. It was full','teacher'),
(1041,1041,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nPr---r---r a k !\n\nMother went running to the sala. She saw Pina very pale and trembling. She was holding a broom. On the floor was a broken vase. “I am very sorry, Mother. The vase fell when I moved the table,” Pina said.\n\n“It’s all right. Now be careful in sweeping all the broken pieces away,” Mother said. “Be sure not a piece is left.”\n\nWhich is a good title for the story?\nA. An Honest Girl\nB. Helping At Home\nC. How Pina Trembled\n\nWhat word best describes Pina?\nA. honest\nB. kind\nC. bright\n\nWhat was Pina doing in the house?\nA. playing\nB. cleaning\nC. sleeping','teacher'),
(1042,1042,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\n“I will buy an ice cream cone for the one who can tell the name of the fruit I will describe.” Father said to two-year-old Ofelia and Malu. “Go ahead and describe it,” his daughters replied.\n\n“It’s a fruit and seed at the same time. It has water inside surrounded by white meat,” Father described.\n\n“I don’t know,” Lety and Ofelia replied almost at the same time. “It’s coconut,” Malu, the eldest of the three, replied.\n\nWho got an ice cream cone?\nA. Lety\nB. Ofelia\nC. Malu\n\nWhat game was Father and his daughters playing?\nA. fruit game\nB. guessing game\nC. story game\n\nWhy did Lety and Ofelia not know the answer?\nA. They were still small.\nB. They were old.\nC. They were as old as Malu','teacher'),
(1043,1043,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nNardo stopped in front of a small store and counted his money. “Five pesos and thirty centavos!” he said with a smile. He rested for a while. Soon he was on the streets again. “Journal, Bulletin, Inquirer!” he shouted. As he walked calling out his sales, he thought of the birthday gift he would buy his mother. “I will bring home a box of chocolate for her,” he thought.\n\nWhat does Nardo sell?\nA. chocolate\nB. newspapers\nC. cakes\n\nWhy does Nardo want to give his mother a gift?\nA. It was her birthday\nB. It was Mother’s Day\nC. It was Christmas Day\n\nWhat was Nardo’s work?\nA. a newsboy\nB. a bootblack\nC. a janitor','teacher'),
(1044,1044,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nAling Ines was very happy watching her children. Rosita was cleaning the house. Anita was taking care of her baby sister. Lino was watering the plants in the garden. Petra went to the market.\n\n“I told the doctor that you have fever, Mother. He will be here soon,” Petra said when she came from the market.\n\n“Thank you, Petra. You’re a good girl,” Mother said.\n\nWhy was Aling Ines not working?\nA. She was lazy.\nB. She had a visitor.\nC. She was sick.\n\nWhat was the story about?\nA. helpful children\nB. how to clean the house\nC. how to go to market\n\nWhat lesson does the story teach?\nA. Love your neighbor\nB. Love you pets\nC. Love your family','teacher'),
(1045,1045,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nHearing Mass\nOne Sunday morning, Father, Mother, Teresita and Marivic were attending mass. They could not wait any longer for the mass to end. During consecration, Marivic began to cry loudly. Everybody turned to look at her.\n\nHow did Mother feel when Marivic started to cry in the church?\nA. excited\nB. angry\nC. embarrassed\n\nIf you were Mother and Father, what would you do?\nA. Spank Marivic\nB. Take Marivic out of the church\nC. Scold Marivic and finish the mass\n\nWhat should Father and Mother do with Marivic next time they go to mass?\nA. Remind Marivic to behave well at church\nB. Let her play in the church patio\nC. Bring her along and not mind if she annoys the other people in the church','teacher'),
(1046,1046,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nThe Big, Pink Bundle of Joy\nMother had been in the hospital for five days. She could hardly wait to bring the big, pink bundle of joy to the children. Mother held the pink bundle lovingly to her bosom while Father called for a taxi.\n\nWhere were Father and Mother?\nA. in the hospital\nB. in the supermarket\nC. in the store\n\nWhy were they there?\nA. Mother was sick\nB. Mother delivered a baby\nC. Mother met an accident\n\nWhere were they going?\nA. home\nB. to the hospital\nC. to the park','teacher'),
(1047,1047,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Bookworm\nVicente is a bookworm. He likes to read about almost everything. He likes to read adventure stories, nature stories, fairy tales, and biographies. Whenever he gets hold of a book, nothing could stop him from finishing it. He overworks his eyes by reading. He reads even when light is dim.\n\nWhat do you think will happen to Vicente?\nA. He will get tired of reading\nB. He will lose interest in books\nC. His eyes will become poor so he will soon need eyeglasses\n\nWhat should Vicente do to save his eyes?\nA. He should not read at all\nB. He should read with a good light on\nC. He should read only books with big letters.\n\nDoes Vicente know how to take care of his eyes?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe','teacher'),
(1048,1048,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nThe Ferris Wheel Ride\nMyrna was scared to ride on the Ferris wheel. One fiesta day, her cousin asked her to ride on the Ferris wheel. When the Ferris wheel turned around, Myrna felt dizzy. She couldn’t look down. She felt as if she was falling.\n\nWhat do you think Myrna wished for?\nA. She wished the ferris wheel would soon stop turning.\nB. She wished that the ferris wheel would never stop turning.\nC. She wished that she had not ridden on a ferris wheel.\n\nDid Myrna enjoy her ride on the ferris wheel?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe\n\nWhat would Myrna do next time?\nA. She would ride on a ferris wheel again.\nB. She would not go to the carnival again.\nC. She would never ride on a ferris wheel again.','teacher'),
(1049,1049,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nAnti-Decay Vaccine\nMost people have had dental problems which start with simple toothache. No matter how well one brushes his teeth, sooner or later cavities are formed which later cause tooth decay.\n\nToday, scientists are busy experimenting to discover a vaccine that would make people immune to tooth decay.\n\nWhy do you think do scientists want to develop the vaccine?\nA. to help children and youth\nB. to help babies\nC. to help people, young and old alike\n\nWhat is the vaccine for?\nA. to prevent bad breath\nB. to prevent deformed teeth\nC. to prevent tooth decay\n\nWhat will be the effect of the vaccine?\nA. Many people will no longer need dentists\nB. People will not brush their teeth anymore\nC. Many people will be saved from pain of having tooth decay','teacher'),
(1050,1050,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nScared Stiff\nMario watched a horror movie that afternoon. That night when he turned off the light to sleep, he suddenly remembered the ghost in the movie. The ghost seemed to be in every corner of his room. It seemed to be peeping at him from the ceiling from the wall, from the door lock, or from the broken windowpane.\n\nSuddenly, he heard something go clink-clank! Then there was a loud “CRUSH” sound from the kitchen.\n\nWhat could it be?\nA. A real ghost\nB. A robber\nC. A glass shaved by the cat\n\nHow did Mario feel?\nA. thrilled\nB. surprised\nC. scared\n\nWhat was Mario likely to do?\nA. watch another horror movie\nB. read a book\nC. sleep with his brother','teacher'),
(1051,1051,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nCaught in the Rain\nThe sky was very dark. With his books, Jose ran as fast as he could. He was panting and perspiring. He was only about three blocks away from home when the rain fell. That night, Jose lay shivering on his bed. He was wrapped in thick blankets with a thermometer in his mouth.\n\nWhat happened to Jose?\nA. He was scared of ghosts.\nB. He caught a cold and developed a fever.\nC. He ate an ice\n\nWhen did the story happen?\nA. on a Sunday\nB. on a holiday\nC. on a school day\n\nWhere did Jose come from?\nA. from school\nB. from church\nC. from the market','teacher'),
(1052,1052,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Visit to the City\nMang Tibo had not visited the city lately. Every time he came to the city, he would write his son to pick him up at the station. Because he wanted to surprise his son, he did not write him about his coming this time.\n\nMang Tibo was surprised to see the many changes in the city. When he got down the bus, he did not know where to go.\n\nWhat happened to Mang Tibo?\nA. He got lost.\nB. He enjoyed looking around\nC. He took a walk around the place\n\nWhat should Mang Tibo have done?\nA. gone to the nearest radio and TV station\nB. gone back to the province at once\nC. looked for a policeman and asked for help\n\nWhat was Mang Tibo likely to decide?\nA. Never come to the city again\nB. Never leave the province\nC. Never come to the city without asking his son to meet him','teacher'),
(1053,1053,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nFirst Elevator Ride\nIt was Marcelo’s first trip to Manila. It was his first time to see a train, an electric light, a telephone, a television set and big buildings. It was his first time to ride in a car, an elevator and on an escalator. Marcelo rode in an elevator on his third day in the city. His mother took him to the office of his eldest brother. His brother’s office was in the seventh floor so they rode in the elevator. Inside the elevator Marcelo cried and said, “Let me out of this box!”\n\nWhat do you think did the people in the elevator do?\nA. They did nothing.\nB. They laughed at him.\nC. They told him not to be afraid.\n\nWhy did Marcelo want to go out?\nA. he could not breathe inside.\nB. He was afraid.\nC. He wanted to play.\n\nHow did Marcelo’s mother feel?\nA. proud of Marcelo\nB. ashamed of Marcelo\nC. happy about Marcelo','teacher'),
(1054,1054,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nFalling in Line\nA little boy was walking on the street. He was holding a pail. Also walking were an old man who was holding a can and a little girl holding a bottle. They would all join a line with people at the corner of the street. Some of the people were holding cans, others, pails and still others, bottles and pitchers. They seemed to be waiting for something.\n\nThen a big red truck was seen coming. How happy the people were! They would hardly keep still on the line.\n\nWhat was the content of the truck?\nA. rice\nB. water\nC. fish\n\nWhat would happen if the truck did not come?\na. the people would have nothing to drink\nb. the people would have nothing to eat\nc. the people would have nothing to sell\n\nHow did the people feel afterwards?\nA. angry\nB. happy\nC. proud','teacher'),
(1055,1055,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nAn Accident\nLety heard a jeep stopped very suddenly in front of their house. Then there was a loud cry. She ran to the window to see what happened.\n\nThere was already a crowd of people. Many more people were running to see what happened. Someone was crying loudly.\n\nWhat do you think happened?\nA. The jeep picked up a passenger.\nB. The jeep bumped somebody.\nC. A child got into the jeep.\n\nWhy was an ambulance needed?\nA. Somebody was dead.\nB. Somebody was hurt.\nC. For a joy ride\n\nHow did the story end?\nA. Nobody got an ambulance.\nB. the people did not help at all.\nC. An ambulance came to help.','teacher'),
(1056,1056,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Surprise Gift\nNorma’s fever was very high. She kept tossing in bed. After a while, she fell asleep. Then she began talking in her sleep.\n\n“Oh, Mother, please buy me a box of chocolate,” she said. Mother heard her. She went to the store as fast as she could. She was back before Norma woke up.\n\n“Is this mine, Mother?” Norma asked when she woke up. She was holding something.\n\nWhat was Norma holding?\nA. a bunch of flowers\nB. a box of chocolate\nC. a beautiful dress\n\nWhat was she likely to do with it?\nA. keep it\nB. eat it\nC. play with it\n\nHow do you think did Norma feel?\nA. She felt happy.\nB. She felt angry.\nC. She felt sad.','teacher'),
(1057,1057,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nRico\nRico is in the third grade. He goes to school every day. He listens well to his teacher. He tries to answer all questions asked of him. He works well with his classmates. He finished his school projects on time. At home he studies his lessons before going to bed. He dies his homework neatly and well. When he finishes all his school assignments he tries to help his mother. In this way he makes his mother happy.\n\nWhat grade will Rico likely receive?\nA. poor grades\nB. high grades\nC. failing grades\n\nWhat is likely to happen to Rico?\nA. will not finish his studies\nB. graduate with honors\nC. stop going to school\n\nWhat else will likely happen to Rico?\nA. becomes successful\nB. become lazy\nC. become sickly','teacher'),
(1058,1058,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nWhy Lina Cried\nFour-year-old Lina came out of the room crying. She just woke up.\n\nMother, still holding needle and thread asked, “What do you want Lina?” Lina did not answer but continued crying.\n\nMother touched Lina. Her hands were hot. Her cheeks were red. Her eyes were red too.\n\n“Call a taxi at once,” Mother told father as she was changing Lina’s clothes.\n\nWhat was Mother doing when Lina woke up?\nA. cooking\nB. reading\nC. sewing\n\nWhy was Lina crying?\nA. She lost her money.\nB. She was sick.\nC. She was hungry.\n\nWhy did Mother need a taxi?\nA. She was taking Lina to a doctor.\nB. She was going to party with Lina.\nC. She was going to the market with Lina.','teacher'),
(1059,1059,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Letter from Father\n“Come, Leah, come, eric,” Mrs. Mislang said. “I have here a letter from your father.”\n\n“What does it say, Mother?” little Eric asked.\n\n“He says that Mindanao is really a land of promise. He wants us to go there soon.” Mrs. Mislang said. We will build our house there and stay there for some years.\n\n“Why did father go there?” Leah asked.\n\n“To earn money for us. He loves us that’s why,” Mrs. Mislang explained.\n\nWhere do you think the father is?\nA. Philippines\nB. America\nC. China\n\nWhat will Mrs. Mislang do?\nA. stay in Manila\nB. plan to go abroad\nC. prepare to live in Mindanao\n\nHow will this story end?\nA. Mrs. Mislang and her children went to Mindanao\nB. Mrs. Mislang and her children stayed in Manila\nC. Mrs. Mislang and her children went to Japan.','teacher'),
(1060,1060,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nA Boy’s Wish\nAngel has been sick for a week. He is not getting any better. A good doctor visits him every day but he is still very ill. His mother is always near him.\n\nShe is not happy. The doctor said that angel’s sickness cannot be cured. In a few days he will die.\n\n“I want a beautiful schoolbag,” Angel said in his sleep one time. Mother heard it. While angel was still sleeping Mother went to the nearest store. She went to buy something for Angel.\n\nWhat do you think did Mother buy?\nA. ice cream for Angel\nB. schoolbag for Angel\nC. shirt for Angel\n\nHow did Angel feel about it?\nA. happy\nB. angry\nC. sad\n\nHow did this story end?\nA. Angel got mad at Mother.\nB. Angel died.\nC. Angel was happy','teacher'),
(1061,1061,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nMina’s Father\nMina and mother were on their way to the bank. “What shall we do at the bank, Mother?” Mina asked.\n\n“We’ll deposit the money your father sent us,” Mother said.\n\n“What does father do to earn money?” Mina asked.\n\n“He checks if all letters mailed have correct stamps. He says that he works with friendly Arabs. He said, too, that he is the only Filipino in the office where he works.\n\nWhat is the work of Mina’s father?\nA. teacher\nB. doctor\nC. office worker\n\nWhere do you think does Mina’s father work?\nA. school\nB. post office\nC. hospital\n\nWhat will Mother likely do after depositing the money?\nA. write Father she received the money\nB. write Father she is sick\nC. write Father that she did not receive the money.','teacher'),
(1062,1062,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nGetting Ready\nHow busy they were! Liza was wiping pieces of furniture. Maya was hanging new curtains. Nilo was sweeping the yard and Pepe was busy borrowing chairs from their neighbors. Mother with three other women were cooking in the kitchen. Father was roasting a pig at the backyard. “How delicious the litson smells!” Pepe said.\n\n“Your friends will enjoy your party, Maya,” Nilo said.\n\n“When I’ll be eighteen years old, will I have a big party like this, Ate Maya?” Liza asked.\n\nWhat was the family preparing for?\nA. a fiesta\nB. a birthday party\nC. a family reunion\n\nWhat is likely to happen?\nA. Many visitors would come.\nB. No visitor would come.\nC. Only father’s friends would come.\n\nHow did the story end?\nA. Liza received many gifts.\nB. Maya received many gifts\nC. father received many gifts.','teacher'),
(1063,1063,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nOne Sunday Morning\nIt was Sunday Morning. The church bells were ringing.\n\n“There is no need to hurry anyway,” Benilda said to herself as she tossed in bed. “I’ll just get up when I like. Sunday is a rest day,” she thought.\n\nSuddenly she got up. She remembered that she should be in church early. She would see Linda there. They were going to a party together.\n\n“Oh my, why did I forget?” Benilda said looking at the big clock.\n\nWhat do you think did Linda do when Benilda did not come?\nA. She attended the mass alone.\nB. She went to a party instead.\nC. She played in the church.\n\nHow do you think did Benilda feel?\nA. She was happy.\nB. She was sorry.\nC. She was sleepy.\n\nWhat was she likely to do?\nA. Go to mass alone, too.\nB. See a show.\nC. Go back to sleep.','teacher'),
(1064,1064,'Direction: Read the selections below and choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nJust Pray\nMrs. Soriano’s house was very quiet. The people inside seemed to be talking in whispers. But everyone seemed to be actively moving around.\n\nSuddenly an ambulance stopped in front of the house. With it came a doctor and a nurse. Soon the eldest girl in the family came out holding two pillows. She was followed by her brother holding a bag and a blanket. Then a patient in the stretcher was placed in the ambulance.\n\nwhat was the ambulance for?\nA. to distribute medicines\nB. to give free injections\nC. to get a sick person\n\nWho was the sick person?\nA. the eldest girl\nB. Mother\nC. the boy\n\nHow did this story end?\nA. Mother was brought to the hospital.\nB. the eldest girl died.\nC. the ambulance stayed in front of the house','teacher'),
(1065,1065,'Directions: Read the story carefully. Choose the correct answer for each question and encircle the letter.\n\nGrowing Vegetables\nLino went with mother to market. They bought some vegetables. Mother said, “I was able to buy only some with my money. Vegetables cost much now.” Lino was sorry for Mother. They did not have much money. So, he said, “Let’s plant vegetables in our backyard. Then we will not have to buy them in the market.”\n\nSo, Lino planted three plots of tomatoes, two plots of eggplants and a plot of pechay. He had some ampalaya climbing on the bamboo fence. Lino worked in his garden after class every afternoon. He grew more vegetables than they could eat. Mother sold some of them in the market.\n\nWhere did Lino and Mother go?\nA. To the farm\nB. To the market\nC. To school\nD. To the park\n\nWhat did they buy at the market?\nA. Fruits\nB. Clothes\nC. Vegetables\nD. Toys\n\nWhy did Lino suggest planting vegetables in their backyard?\nA. To make their backyard look nice\nB. Because vegetables were expensive at the market\nC. To sell vegetables to neighbors\nD. To have fun in the garden\n\nHow many plots of tomatoes did Lino plant?\nA. One\nB. Two\nC. Three\nD. Four\n\nWhich vegetable was climbing on the bamboo fence?\nA. Pechay\nB. Ampalaya\nC. Eggplant\nD. Tomato','teacher'),
(1066,1066,'Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice.\n\nFowls\nFowls are animals. They are animals with two feet and two wings. Their bodies are covered with feathers. They give us eggs. Their meat is good to eat. Chickens, ducks, turkeys and geese are fowls. They eat grains such as palay and corn. Chickens like worms. Ducks and geese like to swim. They like to stay in the water They are water birds, so they have webbed feet. Turkeys have beautiful feathers. The male turkey spreads its tail like a fan. Geese are good watchers. They make loud noises when people come near. Fowls are good to raise. Many people get rich because of poultry raising.\n\nWhat is the main idea of the passage?\nA. Fowls are animals that give eggs and meat.\nB. Fowls only eat grains.\nC. Turkeys have beautiful feathers.\n\nWhy are fowls considered useful to humans?\nA. They make loud noises.\nB. They give eggs, meat, and can make people rich through poultry raising.\nC. They like to swim in water.\n\nWhat type of birds are ducks and geese?\nA. Land birds\nB. Water birds with webbed feet\nC. Birds with colorful feathers\n\nWhy do geese make loud noises?\nA. To call other geese\nB. To warn people or act as watchers\nC. To scare chickens\n\nWhat is the general significance of raising fowls?\nA. It provides food and livelihood for people\nB. It helps fowls grow beautiful feathers\nC. It teaches birds to swim','teacher'),
(1067,1067,'Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice.\n\nThe First Monkey\nLong time ago, there was a farmer who had a wide corn field. He grew big and sweet corn. One day he saw some of his corn gone. Many corn plants had no more fruits. He wanted to catch whoever stole his corn.\n\nThe next day he hid behind a big mango tree. He carried a stick. He watched and watched until he saw a man coming. The man passed through a broken part of the fence. The man began to pick some corn.\n\nThe farmer went from behind the mango tree and walked towards the man. The farmer ran after him, but he could not catch up with him. Then he threw the stick at the man. The stick hit the man’s back and became a tail. The man went up the tree. He became smaller and smaller until he became a monkey.\n\nWhen did the story most likely take place?\nA. Last week\nB. Long time ago\nC. Tomorrow\n\nWhat did the farmer have?\nA. A mango tree\nB. A corn field\nC. A garden\n\nWhat words describe the farmer’s corn?\nA. Small and sour\nB. Big and sweet\nC. Rotten and old\n\nWhat happened to the farmer’s corn?\nA. It was eaten by animals\nB. Some of it was gone and plants had no fruits\nC. It was harvested for sale\n\nWhere did the farmer hide to catch the thief?\nA. Behind a mango tree\nB. In a corn plot\nC. On the roof','teacher'),
(1068,1068,'Directions: Read each question carefully. Choose the correct answer from the options provided and encircle the letter of your choice\n\nThe Bees\nThe bees live in a big family. There are many bees in the family. We cannot count them. The bee family is called a swarm. The swarm lives in the hive. The hive hangs on the tree. Each swarm has a mother bee. She is called the queen bee. She is the mother of all the bees in the swarm. There are two kinds of bees. The busy bees are called workers. The lazy bees are called drones. The workers do not like the drones. They sting the lazy drones. The workers gather nectar from flowers. They put the nectar in the hive. The bees make the nectar into honey. Honey is sweet. It is the food of the young bees.\n\nIf the queen bee were not in the hive, what might happen to the swarm?\nA. The swarm would gather more nectar than usual.\nB. The young bees might not have a mother to care for them.\nC. The workers would become lazy drones.\nD. The hive would fall from the tree.\n\nIf the worker bees stopped gathering nectar, what could happen to the hive?\nA. The hive would become stronger.\nB. The hive would be full of honey.\nC. The young bees might not have food to eat.\nD. The drones would start stinging the queen.\n\nIf the drones were suddenly very hardworking, what might happen?\nA. The workers would still sting them.\nB. There would be more honey in the hive.\nC. The queen bee would leave the hive.\nD. The swarm would split into two.\n\nIf a storm knocked down the tree with the hive, what could be the result for the bees?\nA. They would continue making honey in the same place.\nB. They might have to find a new place to live.\nC. The queen bee would sting the young bees.\nD. The workers would become lazy.\n\nIf the bees did not make honey from nectar, what might happen?\nA. The young bees might go hungry.\nB. The drones would start collecting nectar.\nC. The hive would produce more nectar.\nD. The workers would leave the swarm.','teacher'),
(1069,1069,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nAna’s Pet\nMother bought two piglets. She had a pigpen ready for them. “These pigs should be given good care,” she told Ana. “Can you help me take care of them?” “Of course, Mother,” answered Ana. “l’ll help you take care of them.” So, Ana became the caretaker of the piglets. She cleaned the pen early in the morning and gave them baths. She fed them swill which she got from their neighbors. Soon the piglets grew big and fat. When they were ready for sale, Ana felt sad. She knew Mother would get a good price for them but she would lose her pet.\n\nWhat did Ana’s Mother buy?\nA. Two piglets\nB. Three piglets\nC. Four piglets\n\nWhere did she keep them?\nA. In their yard\nB. In a pigpen\nC. Under the house\n\nWhom did she ask to help her take care of them?\nA. Ana\nB. her maid\nC. her neighbor\n\nDid Ana like to take care of the pigs?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Perhaps\n\nWhat new job did Ana have?\nA. nurse of the pigs\nB. friend of the pigs\nC. caretaker of the pigs\n\nAt what time of the day did she clean the pigpen?\nA. early in the morning\nB. late in the afternoon\nC. at noon\n\nWho gave baths to the pigs?\nA. Ana herself\nB. Ana’s sister\nC. Ana’s mother\n\nWhere did Ana get the swill for the pigs?\nA. from the market\nB. from a nearby store\nC. from their neighbors\n\nWhat happened with the pigs?\nA. Became sick\nB. Grew big and fat\nC. Did not grow at all\n\nHow did Ana feel when the pigs were ready for sale?\nA. very happy\nB. angry\nC. sad','teacher'),
(1070,1070,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nExercise\nCarlos is a fourth grader. His books are not covered. He loses his pencil very often. Every day, when he arrives from school he throws his bag and books around. One day, he left his schoolbag on the floor. His dog, Liksi, found the bag. It bit and tore the bag. It bit one book and tore the pages, too. Carlos’s mother saw what happened. She quickly drove the dog away. She picked up the bag. It was torn to pieces. She picked up a book. Many pages were also torn.\n\nWho is the boy in the story?\nA. Jhon\nB. Karla\nC. Carlos\n\nWhat pet is Liksi?\nA. Cow\nB. Dog\nC. Cat\n\nWhat is the story about?\nA. a careless boy\nB. Carlos’s mother\nC. a dog named Liksi\n\nHow did Carlos’s mother feel when she saw what happened?\nA. surprised\nB. gladdened\nC. angered\n\nWhat will this incident teach Carlos?\nA. to be more polite\nB. to be more obedient\nC. to be more careful with his things\n\nWhy does Carlo seem to be a careless boy?\nA. because he always listens to her Mom.\nB. because he doesn’t care\nC. because he is a good boy.\n\nWhose bag was torn by pet Liksi?\nA. Carlo\nB. Mom\nC. Father','teacher'),
(1071,1071,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nGuava Jam\nThe cook had just prepared a kettle of guava jam. She placed the kettle on the table to cool. Some guava jam spilled on the table. In a short time, ants gathered at the jam on the table. “What fine guava jam!” they all said. The ants ate and ate. They continued eating even when they were already full. They did not want to leave the jam. They thought they would sleep on it so that when they wake up they could eat and eat again. But alas, the guava jam became thicker and thicker as it cooled.\n\nWhat fruit is the jam?\nA. Strawberry\nB. Guava\nC. Tamarind\n\nWhere these ants gathered to ate the jam?\nA. In the table.\nB. In the bed.\nC. In the floor\n\nWhat happened to the guava when it cooled?\nA. It became sticky\nB. It became watery\nC. It became thicker\n\nWhat happened to the ants?\nA. They became healthy.\nB. They stayed on the jam for a vacation.\nC. Their legs got stuck on the table and they died there.\n\nWhat should they have done?\nA. They should have eaten just enough and left.\nB. They should not have eaten any of the jam.\nC. They should have brought home the jam.\n\nWhat lesson did you get from his story?\nA. Don’t be too slow.\nB. Don’t be too greedy.\nC. Don’t be too industrious.\n\nWhy ants decide to sleep on the jam?\nA. to play there\nB. to study there\nC. to eat there','teacher'),
(1072,1072,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nPlaying with Circles\n\nDraw five circles of the same size. Draw a straight line joining the circles through the middle.\n\nDraw a big circle. Divide it into four equal parts. Shade one part of it with your pencil.\n\nWrite what part of your circle in number 2 is not shaded.\n\nDraw a big circle. Write the word circle inside it.\n\nDraw another big circle. Draw aa smaller circle inside it.','teacher'),
(1073,1073,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nThe Horse and Snail\nOne morning the horse and the snail met. The horse made fun of how the snail walked. This made the snail very angry. He decided to run a race with the horse. The snail walked slowly. But walked and walked. He did not look back. The horse laughed. He could easily beat the snail, the horse thought. He ran for a while. He saw the snail for behind him. He rested under a mango tree. Soon he fell asleep. The snail walked past the horse. He did not rest. When the horse woke up, he ran as fast as he could. But the snail was the first at the finish line.','teacher'),
(1074,1074,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nPepe and the Guavas\nPepe climbed the guava tree near his house. It had many ripe fruits. He picked as many as he could and put them in his pockets. Then he sat on a branch and began eating them. While he was eating, he felt the branch break. He did not know it was a dead branch.\n\nDown, down he fell! After that, he did not know what happened. When he woke up, he was in a hospital bed. His two arms were in cast.\n\nWho climbed the guava tree?\nA. Pepe\nB. Pepe’s friend\nC. Pepe’s brother\n\nWhere was the guava tree?\nA. Near Pepe’s house\nB. In his neighbor’s yard\nC. Near his friend’s house\n\nWhat was the fruit on the tree?\nA. Ripe guava fruits\nB. green fruits\nC. Many birds\n\nWhere did Pepe put the fruits he picked?\nA. In his hat\nB. In a basket\nC. In his pocket\n\nWhy did Pepe fall down at the guava tree?\nA. He did not know it was a died branch\nB. He did not know what happened\nC. He felt the branch break\n\nWhen did he know that he fell down?\nA. His two arms were in cast\nB. When he woke up he was in a hospital bed\nC. He woke up at the branch of a tree\n\nHow did Pepe get on the top of the tree?\nA. He climbed the tree\nB. He sat on the branch\nC. He fall down on the tree','teacher'),
(1075,1075,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nAt the Public Playground\nThe public playground was full of children. They were shouting with joy. The smaller children were playing in the sand boxes. They were digging the sand and filling their pails. The bigger boys and girls were having fun riding on swings.\n\nSome big boys were chinning the bar. Others were climbing the big tower and were sliding down. Some of them were busy playing on the seesaws, going up and going down. Some girls in bathing suits were swimming in the pool.\n\nWhere were the children?\nA. In school\nB. At the Rizal Park\nC. At the public playground\n\nHow did they show they were happy?\nA. They shouted with joy.\nB. They ran and jumped.\nC. They ran here and there.\n\nWho played in the sand boxes?\nA. small girls\nB. small boys\nC. smaller children\n\nWhat did they do?\nA. Made houses of sand.\nB. Filled their pails with sand.\nC. Walked without shoes on the sand.\n\nHow did the bigger boys and girls have fun?\nA. Rode in bicycles.\nB. Danced together.\nC. Rode on the swings.\n\nWho chinned the bar?\nA. A big boy.\nB. Some big girls.\nC. Some big boys.\n\nHow did the children go down the tower?\nA. Sliding\nB. Running\nC. Jumping\n\nWhat went up and down?\nA. Slides\nB. Seesaw\nC. Swings\n\nWho were swimming in the pool?\nA. Some girls\nB. Some boys\nC. Little children\n\nWhat were they wearing?\nA. Shorts\nB. Play suits\nC. Bathing suits','teacher'),
(1076,1076,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nA Family of Music Lovers\nThe Santos family are fond of music. All of them have good singing voices and all can play the piano. Mr. Santos is their teacher and Mrs. Santos is their pianist. People enjoy listening to them when they are practicing, more so when they sing Filipino songs. The songs make the listeners happy. Lita, Raul, Emil, Nina and Anita sing with feeling. While singing they watch Mr. Santos’s hand, so they know when to sing louder or softer.\n\nHow many children are in the family?\nA. Five\nB. Six\nC. Seven\n\nWhat kind of family are the Santoses?\nA. Poetic\nB. Dramatic\nC. Music lovers\n\nWhat do all of them have?\nA. Good singing voices\nB. Weak singing voices\nC. Good speaking voices\n\nWhat instrument can they play?\nA. Guitar\nB. Violin\nC. Piano\n\nWho is their teacher?\nA. Their father\nB. Their mother\nC. Their brother\n\nWho is their pianist?\nA. Their father\nB. Their brother\nC. Their mother\n\nHow do people feel while they are listening to the Santoses?\nA. Sad\nB. Angry\nC. Happy\n\nHow do the children sing?\nA. Noisily\nB. With feeling\nC. Loud and clear\n\nWhat kind of songs make the listeners feel happier?\nA. English songs\nB. Filipino songs\nC. American songs\n\nWhat is the children’s sign in signing louder or softer?\nA. Mr. Santos’s eyes\nB. Mr. Santos’s hands\nC. Mrs. Santos’s body movement','teacher'),
(1077,1077,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nThe Artesian Well\nNonoy lived in a little barrio far from the town. He had to wake up early every morning to fetch water from a well. He had to make several trips before he could fill a big jar.\n\nOne day men came to the barrio to drill an artesian well. In ten days, the artesian well was ready. It was like there was a fiesta. Barrio people lined up to get water. When Nonoy’s turn came, he took a long drink. How cool and satisfying the water tasted!\n\nWhere did Nonoy live?\nA. In a barrio\nB. In the town\nC. In a city\n\nWhat did he do every morning?\nA. Fetched water from a well.\nB. Fetched his sister from school.\nC. Bought bread from the store.\n\nHow long did it take him to get water?\nA. Two trips\nB. Several trips\nC. Several hours\n\nWhere did he put the water?\nA. On a big jar\nB. In a big can\nC. In a big bottle\n\nWhat did the men do in the barrio?\nA. Drilled an artesian well.\nB. Fetched water for the people.\nC. Dug a deep well in the ground.\n\nHow did the barrio people feel about it?\nA. Angry\nB. Happy\nC. Ashamed\n\nWhat would the well give them?\nA. Unsafe water\nB. Clean, safe water\nC. Water for the fields\n\nHow long did the men finish the work?\nA. Ten days\nB. Four days\nC. One week\n\nWhat did the barrio people do when it was ready?\nA. Lined up to get water\nB. Took bath near the well.\nC. Danced along the tree\n\nHow did the water taste to Nonoy?\nA. He did not like it.\nB. It was not so good.\nC. It tasted cool and satisfying.','teacher'),
(1078,1078,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nRaindrops\n“I’m going down to do my turn,”\nOnce said a drop of rain;\n“I hear a sign, a thirsty sigh,\nI’ll go with you.” “Me, too! Me, too!”\nCried all the raindrops near;\nSo down they fell, in merry mood,\nThe whole wide field to cheer.\n\nWhat is the best tittle for this poem?\nA. The field\nB. The rain\nC. The flowers\n\nWho are talking in the poem?\nA. The flower\nB. The raindrops\nC. The field\n\nWhich sentence tells about the rain?\nA. It waters plants to make them grow.\nB. It causes floods which destroy crops.\nC. It prevents children from going to school.','teacher'),
(1079,1079,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nCarlos\nCarlos is a fourth grader. His books are not covered. He loses his pencil very often. Every day, when he arrives from school he throws his bag and books around. One day, he left his schoolbag on the floor. His dog, Liksi, found the bag. It bit and tore the bag. It bit one book and tore the pages. It bit another book and tore the pages too.\n\nCarlos’s mother saw what happened. She quickly drove the dog away. She picked up the bag. It was torn to pieces. She picked up a book. Many pages were also torn.\n\nWhat is the story about?\nA. A careless boy.\nB. Carlos’s mother\nC. A dog named Liksi.\n\nHow did Carlos’s mother feel when she saw what happened?\nA. Surprised\nB. Gladdened\nC. Angered\n\nWhat will this incident teach Carlos?\nA. To be more polite.\nB. To be more obedient.\nC. to be more careful with his things.\n\nHow did Liksi ruin the bag and book?\nA. stepped on it\nB. bit and tore it\nC. throw it away\n\nWhat happened to the other book bitten by Liksi?\nA. it was wet\nB. it was lost\nC. it was to torn into pieces\n\nWhat do you think fits to describe the attitude of Liksi?\nA. playful\nB. naughty\nC. well-behaved\n\nIs Carlos’s behavior on taking care of his things is very good?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe','teacher'),
(1080,1080,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nPal Is Sick\nPal was crying in his bed. He did not like the things that happened that day. “You will have to take this with a glass of water. It tastes fine, like candy.” Said a nurse by his bedside. “Take it and you’ll see that you’ll start feeling fine.” “No! No! I don’t want anything, I don’t even want to stay here. I want to go home,” cried Pal.\n\n“I will stay with you here, Pal,” said mother. “We shall go home as soon as you get well. Take what the nurse is giving you so you will get well soon. Then we can go home.\n\nWho is in the bed crying?\nA. Dante\nB. Carlo\nC. Pal\n\nWhat do you think happened to the boy?\nA. Sick\nB. Fine\nC. Playing\n\nDoes the boy like to stay in that place?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. I don’t know\n\nWhere was Pal?\nA. In the school\nB. In the church.\nC. In the hospital\n\nWhat was the matter with him?\nA. He was sick.\nB. He was playing.\nC. He was studying\n\nWhat was the nurse giving him?\nA. Toys\nB. Books\nC. Medicine\n\nwhom you will ask help if you are sick?\nA. Teacher\nB. Friend\nC. Doctor','teacher'),
(1081,1081,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nFlag\nI. Way up there,\nWaving so high!\nRed and blue, side by side\nWith a triangle of white.\n\nII. Way up there,\nWaving so high!\nThe sun, a golden bright\nOn a triangle of white.\n\nIII. Way up there,\nWaving so high,\nKeep it up right\nFor it we should fight.\n\nThe best little for the poem is______.\nA. our flag\nB. Let’s fight\nC. Up in the sky\n\nThe flag described is a___________.\nA. Philippine flag\nB. American flag\nC. Japanese flag\n\nThe poem teaches us_________.\nA. To look at the flag.\nB. To fight for our flag.\nC. How to draw a flag.\n\nWhat are the colors of flag side by side on it?\nA. black and red\nB. pink and blue\nC. red and blue\n\nWhat is the shape of color white in the flag\nA. circle\nB. triangle\nC. square','teacher'),
(1082,1082,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nA Joke\nCarlito enjoys playing jokes on his brothers, sisters and friends. One day he saw a black rubber mouse for sale in a store. An idea came into his mind so he bought the rubber mouse at once.\n\nWhen he went home, he placed the rubber mouse on one of the steps of their stairs. Pretty soon his sister came down the stairs carrying a big bundle of dirty clothes. When she was on the step where the rubber mouse was, Carlito shouted, “Look! A mouse!”\n\nWhat must have happened to his sister?\nA. She got sick.\nB. She was frightened.\nC. She laughed and laughed.\n\nWhat happened with the bundle of dirty clothes?\nA. It was scattered all around.\nB. It became lighter.\nC. It fell on Carlito’s arms.\n\nWhat do you think did Mother say later?\nA. How we enjoyed your joke!\nB. Play the same joke again to others.\nC. Do not play such a kind of joke anymore.\n\nWhy is that Carlito shouted?\nA. Because he wants to scare her sister.\nB. Because He was surprised seeing the mouse.\nC. Because He was angry seeing the mouse.','teacher'),
(1083,1083,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nThe Birdlings\nHigh on the tree top, four hungry bird lings lay chirping in their nest.\n\nMother bird was hunting food for its young ones. It was in the wide-open yard looking for fat worms.\n\nA boy with a slingshot came along. Slowly he went near mother bird and shot at it.\n\nWhat a good aim! Mother bird lay on the ground with a broken wing. “I’ll have my catch fried for my supper,” said the boy to himself as he went home whistling with the bird on his hand.\n\nWhat do you think happened to the birdlings?\nA. They learned to look for their own food.\nB. They died of hunger.\nC. The boy took care of them.\n\nWhat happened with Mother bird?\nA. It’s broken wing was treated and it was returned to the birdlings.\nB. It was killed and fried for supper.\nC. It was thrown away.\n\nWhat do you think did the boy have for his supper that evening?\nA. Fried bird\nB. Fried fish\nC. Pancit and soup','teacher'),
(1084,1084,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nThe Pet Bird\nDolor had a pet bird which she loved very much. Every day she gave it ripe bananas to eat and fresh water to drink.\n\nOne day Dolor got sick and had to stay in bed. She asked her brother Jun to feed her pet every day. But Jun was very different from Dolor. He was not found of the bird.\n\nOne morning Jun hurriedly gave the bird a ripe banana and some water. Then, away he ran to his waiting friends.\n\nWhen Dolor woke up, she saw a little red feather on her blanket. She thought her bird was beside her. Then she found other feathers scattered on the floor. Slowly she got up and looked at her bird in the cage. The cage was empty.\n\nDolor cried bitterly. She cried and cried and would not eat. Her mother was worried. Jun was very sorry.\n\nThen Jun got his savings and went out. “I must do something for Dolor,” he said.\n\nWhat happen with the bird?\nA. It flew away.\nB. It got sick and die.\nC. It was eaten by the cat.\n\nWhat might happen to dolor due to the disappearance of her bird?\nA. She might get well very soon.\nB. She might get worse.\nC. She might become kinder.\n\nWhat will June do with his savings?\nA. give it to Dolor.\nB. buy doll for Dolor.\nC. buy another bird for Dolor.','teacher'),
(1085,1085,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully and silently. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nBanana Peelings\nBen and Lito always pray and eat lunch in school together. One day after eating several ripe bananas which Lito brought, the two boys played war. They threw banana peeling at each other. After playing they forgot about banana peelings which they threw on the cemented porch. Then, the school bell rang. The boys and girls formed their lines. As they passed by the porch to their room, something happened. Three boys helped Lisa to the school clinic. Lisa was then brought to a hospital because of a broken knee. Her knee was then placed in plaster cast. How sorry Ben and Lito were. How they wished they did not play war after eating those bananas! How they wish they throw those bananas peelings in th garbage can at once.\n\nWhat happened when the boys and girls passed by the porch?\nA. some girls and boys ate bananas, too.\nB. some boys played war, too.\nC. a girl slipped when she stepped on the banana peelings.\n\nWhy boys and girls formed their lines?\nA. because they’ll buy food to canteen.\nB. because it’s flag ceremony time.\nC. because it is time to get inside their room.\n\nHow long do you think would it take before Lisa could move about again?\nA. one day\nB. one-month\nC. one week','teacher'),
(1086,1086,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nMonths of the Year\nDo you know the months of the year in their proper order? You may use a scratch paper if you like, in following the directions below.\n\nWrite the first month of the year.\n\nWhat month has the shortest name? Write it down.\n\nWrite the month which comes after July.\n\nWrite the month which comes before September?\n\nWrite the name of the month when Christmas is celebrated.','teacher'),
(1087,1087,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nThe English Alphabet\nHow well do you know the English alphabet? Use a scratch paper if you think you need one in following these directions.\n\nWrite down the first letter in the English alphabet.\n\nWrite down the last letter in the English alphabet and encircle it.\n\nDraw a square. Inside it, write the third letter of the alphabet.\n\nWrite the word “boil”. Cross out its, first letter. In its place, write the third letter of the alphabet.\n\nWrite the letter in the alphabet that comes after “m”.','teacher'),
(1088,1088,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nOur Nipa Hut\nSee our nipa hut, It’s home to all of us; It’s here that we live, Father, Mother and I. Inside our nipa hut, There’s not much to see; A chair, a table, a bed, Which to visitors we give. Behind our nipa hut, Are fruit trees big and small; Duhat, santol, balimbing, Guava, chico, and atis too.\n\nDraw a nipa hut.\n\nCopy the title of the poem in printed form.\n\nCopy the line that tells who lives in the nipa hut.\n\nDraw one of the things that can be seen inside the nipa hut.\n\nWrite at least 3 fruit trees that can be found behind the nipa hut.','teacher'),
(1089,1089,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nUnderstanding Directions\n\nWrite the name of the place in your school where children borrow and read books, newspapers and magazines.\n\nCopy the three words in the list below that refer to animals. Words in the list: vegetable, kitchen, cat, bark, fruit, kettle, tiger, elephant, bag, castle\n\nCopy the word below and write its opposite. Clean-\n\nWrite the word “boil”. Cross out its first letter.\n\nWrite down the name of our country. Underline its first letter.','teacher'),
(1090,1090,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Kingfisher and the Cat\nOne fine morning, a kingfisher sat on a branch of a tree. She was busy eating a fresh fish which she caught in the river. A hungry cat ran under the tree and asked a piece of it. The kingfisher did not want to share the fish.\n\nThe cat was very hungry so she thought of a way to get the fish from the kingfisher. She asked the kingfisher to sing.\n\nThe silly kingfisher could not sing at all. But she was so flattered that she opened her mouth and tried. Of course, she dropped the fish.\n\nThe cat gulped it down hurriedly. Then she ran away laughing.','teacher'),
(1091,1091,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Dream That Became a Reality\nFor many, many years, people had always dreamed of going to the moon. This seemed impossible because the moon is very far from Earth.\n\nNot even the biggest, fastest and most powerful airplane could ever reach the moon. Then, very powerful rocket ships were made. These rocket ships could travel fast enough to escape the earth’s gravity, withstand the sun’s heat and land on the moon. On July 20, 1969, an almost impossible dream came true. Neil Armstrong, together with two other American astronauts, made a trip to the moon aboard Apollo 11, a spacecraft. Neil Armstrong became the very first man to land on the moon.','teacher'),
(1092,1092,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Ant and the Grasshopper\nThe ant and the grasshopper were good friends. The ant was industrious but the grasshopper was lazy. While the ant was busy storing food for the rainy days, the grasshopper did nothing all day but sing and play. When the rain came, the lazy grasshopper shivered with the cold and hunger. He went to the ant’s house and begged for some food. The ant gave him food but he kept on coming back for more.\n\nThe ant lost her patience and said, “You did nothing but sing and play during the sunny days. Now you can sing and play in the rain. I have nothing more to give you”','teacher'),
(1093,1093,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nA Medal on the Barong Filipino\nManuel is a young artist. He is good in drawing almost anything. Some of his works have been shown at school exhibit. One day, he joined an art contest. His painting won the first prize. He was invited to attend a program where he would be given the prize. One of his friends said, “In this program you will be given a medal. It will be nice to wear coat and tie.” “What? Wear coat and tie for that medal? I’m going to wear a Barong Filipino. The Barong Filipino is just as good as any other clothes for a medal of honor.” The medal was pinned on Manuel’s Barong Filipino.','teacher'),
(1094,1094,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nAna’s Pet\nMother bought two piglets. She had a pigpen ready for them. “These pigs should be given good care,” she told Ana. “Can you help me take care of them?” “Of course, Mother,” answered Ana. “l’ll help you take care of them.” So Ana became the caretaker of the piglets. She cleaned the pen early in the morning and gave them baths. She fed them swill which she got from their neighbors. Soon the piglets grew big and fat. When they were ready for sale, Ana felt sad. She knew Mother would get a good price for them but she would lose her pet.\n\nWhat did Ana’s Mother buy?\nA. Two piglets\nB. Three piglets\nC. Four piglets\n\nWhere did she keep them?\nA. In their yard\nB. In a pigpen\nC. Under the house\n\nWhom did she ask to help her take care of them?\nA. Ana\nB. her maid\nC. her neighbor\n\nDid Ana like to take care of the pigs?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Perhaps\n\nWhat new job did Ana have?\nA. nurse of the pigs\nB. friend of the pigs\nC. caretaker of the pigs\n\nAt what time of the day did she clean the pigpen?\nA. early in the morning\nB. late in the afternoon\nC. at noon\n\nWho gave baths to the pigs?\nA. Ana herself\nB. Ana’s sister\nC. Ana’s mother\n\nWhere did Ana get the swill for the pigs?\nA. from the market\nB. from a nearby store\nC. from their neighbors\n\nWhat happened with the pigs?\nA. Became sick\nB. Grew big and fat\nC. Did not grow at all\n\nHow did Ana feel when the pigs were ready for sale?\nA. very happy\nB. angry\nC. sad','teacher'),
(1095,1095,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nExercise\nCarlos is a fourth grader. His books are not covered. He loses his pencil very often. Every day, when he arrives from school he throws his bag and books around. One day, he left his schoolbag on the floor. His dog, Liksi, found the bag. It bit and tore the bag. It bit one book and tore the pages, too. Carlos’s mother saw what happened. She quickly drove the dog away. She picked up the bag. It was torn to pieces. She picked up a book. Many pages were also torn.\n\nWho is the boy in the story?\nA. Jhon\nB. Karla\nC. Carlos\n\nWhat pet is Liksi?\nA. Cow\nB. Dog\nC. Cat\n\nWhat is the story about?\nA. a careless boy\nB. Carlos’s mother\nC. a dog named Liksi\n\nHow did Carlos’s mother feel when she saw what happened?\nA. surprised\nB. gladdened\nC. angered\n\nWhat will this incident teach Carlos?\nA. to be more polite\nB. to be more obedient\nC. to be more careful with his things\n\nWhy is Carlos a careless boy?\nA. because he always listens to her Mom.\nB. because he doesn’t care\nC. because he is a good boy.\n\nWho owns the bag which was torn by Liksi?\nA. Carlos\nB. Mom\nC. Father','teacher'),
(1096,1096,'Instructions: Read the given story carefully. After reading, answer the questions based on what you understood. Write only the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet. Make sure to read each question before answering.\n\nGuava Jam\nThe cook had just prepared a kettle of guava jam. She placed the kettle on the table to cool. Some guava jam spilled on the table. In a short time, ants gathered at the jam on the table. “What fine guava jam!” they all said. The ants ate and ate. They continued eating even when they were already full. They did not want to leave the jam. They thought they would sleep on it so that when they wake up they could eat and eat again. But alas, the guava jam became thicker and thicker as it cooled.\n\nWhat fruit is the jam?\nA. Strawberry\nB. Guava\nC. Tamarind\n\nWhere these ants gathered to ate the jam?\nA. In the table.\nB. In the bed.\nC. In the floor\n\nWhat happened to the guava when it cooled?\nA. It became sticky\nB. It became watery.\nC. It became thicker\n\nWhat happened to the ants?\nA. They became healthy.\nB. They stayed on the jam for a vacation.\nC. Their legs got stuck on the table and they died there.\n\nWhat should they have done?\nA. They should have eaten just enough and left.\nB. They should not have eaten any of the jam.\nC. They should have brought home the jam.\n\nWhat lesson did you get from his story?\nA. Don’t be too slow.\nB. Don’t be too greedy.\nC. Don’t be too industrious.\n\nWhy ants decided to sleep on the jam?\nA. to play there\nB. to study there\nC. to eat there','teacher'),
(1097,1097,'Instructions: Read each direction carefully and follow the instructions exactly.\n\nPlaying with Circles\n\nDraw five circles of the same size. Draw a straight line joining the circles through the middle.\n\nDraw a big circle. Divide it into four equal parts. Shade one part of it with your pencil.\n\nWrite what part of your circle in number 2 is not shaded.\n\nDraw a big circle. Write the word circle inside it.\n\nDraw another big circle. Draw a smaller circle inside it.','teacher'),
(1098,1098,'Instructions: Read the words or sentences aloud as quickly and correctly as you can. Start when your teacher says “Go.” Read clearly and do not skip any word.\n\nThe Horse and Snail\nOne morning the horse and the snail met. The horse made fun of how the snail walked. This made the snail very angry. He decided to run a race with the horse. The snail walked slowly. But walked and walked. He did not look back. The horse laughed. He could easily beat the snail, the horse thought. He ran for a while. He saw the snail far behind him. He rested under a mango tree. Soon he fell asleep. The snail walked past the horse. He did not rest. When the horse woke up, he ran as fast as he could. But the snail was the first at the finish line.','teacher'),
(1099,1099,'Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nMagnets\nMagnets have the power of attracting objects made of steel, iron, and other metals.\n\nThe discovery of the magnet and the many things that have been made out of it have brought about many discoveries and useful things. The magnet makes possible the compass that was used and still being used by travelers - pilots, sailors, boy scouts, soldiers, engineers, and others. The magnet which makes use of electricity has made all types of electric and industrial motors.\n\nMagnets can attract objects made of ___________.\nA. plastic\nB. metals\nC. wood\nD. rubber\n\nThe discovery of magnets led to many ______________.\nA. inventions\nB. revolutions\nC. problems\nD. effects\n\nMagnets made possible the inventions of the ____________.\nA. compass\nB. computer\nC. camera\nD. cellphone\n\nPilots, sailors, boy scouts, soldiers and engineers depend much on ______.\nA. compass\nB. electricity\nC. motors\nD. laptop','teacher'),
(1100,1100,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nOur body remains at a certain temperature all the time. When we get very warm, little beads of water called sweat form on our skin. Sweat is mostly water mixed with salt from the body. This is also called perspiration. Perspiring is a quick method of cooling off the body. As a little bead of sweat evaporates, it carries away a little heat from the body, and we feel a little cooler.\n\nWhat happens to our body when we feel hot?\nA. Our body perspires\nB. Our body evaporates\nC. Our body burns\nD. Our body dies\n\nWhat is the paragraph about?\nA. It is about maintaining the body temperature.\nB. It is about keeping the body warm.\nC. It is about keeping the body cool.\nD. It is about living a meaningful life.\n\nWhat does the selection explain?\nA. perspiring is not good for the body.\nB. perspiring is good for the body.\nC. perspiring is a sign of illness.\nD. Perspiring can make us tired.','teacher'),
(1101,1101,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe body should be kept clean at all times. Plenty of soap and warm water help remove excess oil, dirt, and germs from the skin. Rubbing the body with a towel after bathing stimulates the flow of the oil from oil glands and keep the skin smooth and soft.\n\nDirty hands may not actually look dirty, but it is always safe to wash them before handling food, before and after eating.\n\nThe ears, the eyes, the nose, the mouth and teeth need special cleaning Attention so, with the hair.\n\nThe feet should be washed and dried before putting on one’s shoes or slippers.\n\nA clean body keeps sickness away!\n\nWhat is a good title for the selection?\nA. Keeping the body clean\nB. Dirty hands and feet\nC. How to clean One’s Feet\nD. How to be dirty\n\nWhen should the body be kept clean?\nA. before sleeping\nB. at all times\nC. before and after eating\nD. when we wake up\n\nWhich sentence is true according to the selection?\nA. A clean body keeps sickness away.\nB. Keeping the body clean takes little effort.\nC. Cleaning the body takes a lot of time and effort.\nD. A clean foot can attract other people.','teacher'),
(1102,1102,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nAnti-Decay Vaccine\nMost people have had dental problems which start with a simple toothache. No matter how well one brushes his teeth, sooner or later cavities are formed which later cause tooth decay.\n\nToday, scientists are busy experimenting to discover a vaccine that would make people immune to tooth decay.\n\nWhy do scientists want to discover a vaccine for tooth decay?\nA. To help people avoid tooth decay.\nB. To help babies grow stronger.\nC. To improve people\'s eyesight.\nD. To help medicine companies earn money.\n\nWhat is the vaccine for?\nA. To prevent bad breath\nB. To prevent deformed teeth\nC. To prevent tooth decay\nD. To prevent skin diseases\n\nWhat will be the effect of the vaccine?\nA. Many people will be saved from the pain of having tooth decay.\nB. People will not brush their teeth anymore.\nC. There will be no more need for dentists.\nD. Many people will have skin allergies.','teacher'),
(1103,1103,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nWhen Rizal Was a Boy\nOne Monday morning, Jose woke up early to get ready for school. His mother told him to wear his new “camisa” made of abaca. Jose looked quiet and sad. He didn’t like to wear camisa.\n\n“But I want you to wear it,” his mother insisted.\n\nJose looked at his mother and politely answered, “Yes Mother”.\n\nBox the word that described Jose.\nA. Intelligent\nB. Obedient\nC. Helpful\n\nBox the phrase that tells when the story happened.\nA. when Rizal was a boy\nB. when Rizal was a grown-up man\nC. when Rizal was dead\nD. when he was a teenager\n\nWhat word that starts with letter P that tells how Jose answered his mother?\n\nCopy the name of the plant mentioned in the selection and draw a box around it.','teacher'),
(1104,1104,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nInsects\nThere are more than half a million kinds of insects. The kinds of insects outnumber the kinds of all animals put together.\n\nInsects live in almost any kind of habitat. There are many in the sea, but they are found almost everywhere in freshwater and on land.\n\nAll insects have six legs and one pair of feelers. All insects are covered with a waterproof substance called chitin. Most insects have wings. Their bodies are divided into three parts: the head, the thorax, and the abdomen.\n\nMost insects crawl, hop, swim, jump or walk. But flying is their usual way of getting about. Some can fly very fast. Dragonflies one are among the fastest fliers.\n\nSome insects have two pairs of wings. Some have one pair. Some have none at all. It is safe, therefore, to think that insects differ in shape, size and color.\n\nThere are more than ____________ kinds of insects.\nA. thousand\nB. half a million\nC. hundred\nD. millions\n\nIn what ways do insects differ?\nA. In form\nB. in height\nC. in shape, size and color\nD. in movement\n\nWhy can insects fly?\nA. they have legs\nB. they have antenna\nC. they have wings\nD. they have feelers','teacher'),
(1105,1105,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nThe Eyebrows\nEyebrows are part of the face which serve two purposes--for beauty and protection.\n\nEyebrows make the face look more beautiful by calling attention to the eyes. They make the eyes look healthier and brighter.\n\nEyebrows not only beautify the face but also protect the eyes from sweat. Without the eyebrows, drops of sweat may run into the eyes and blur the sight. Sweat itself is a waste product and should be gotten rid of.\n\nThe part of the body mentioned in the selection is the ______________.\nA. eyelashes\nB. eyelids\nC. eyebrows\nD. eyeliner\n\nThis part makes the face look ____________.\nA. beautiful\nB. sad\nC. sleepy\nD. happy\n\nAside from beauty, they also serve the purpose of _______.\nA. communication\nB. conservation\nC. protection\nD. transportation\n\nThe eyebrows call attention to the ___________.\nA. nose\nB. eye s\nC. cheeks\nD. lips\n\nThe eyebrows make the face look _________.\nA. uglier\nB. healthier\nC. funnier\nD. younger\n\nHow do eyebrows serve its purpose for the eyes?\nA. The eyebrows protect the eyes from sweat.\nB. The eyebrows protect the eyes from glare.\nC. The eyebrows protect the eyes from diseases.\nD. The eyebrows protect the eyes from enemies.\n\nSweat is ________.\nA. sweet\nB. useful\nC. poisonous\nD. useless\n\nSweat can blur the sense of __________.\nA. sight\nB. smell\nC. taste\nD. touch\n\nSweat should be__________.\nA. conserved\nB. gotten rid of\nC. licked\nD. saved\n\nWhy do we have eyebrows?\nA. They make the face beautiful.\nB. They protect the eyes from sweat.\nC. They make the eyes look brighter.\nD. They help the face look healthier.','teacher'),
(1106,1106,'Choose the letter of the correct answer.\n\nCalla Lilies\nThe stately flowers that are commonly used for decorating the church during weddings are the Calla lilies. Calla lilies are also called Baguio lilies because they grow abundantly there.\n\nCalla lilies grow singly on long slender stalks. They may either be yellow or white. They are made into bridal bouquets or even wreaths.\n\nCalla lilies are commonly used for ____________.\nA. corsages\nB. garlands\nC. church decoration\nD. birthdays\n\nCalla lilies are also called ___________________.\nA. Water lily\nB. Rose lily\nC. Baguio lilies\nD. Lily rose\n\nWhy are calla lilies also called Baguio lilies?\nA. It is because these lilies grow abundantly there.\nB. It is because these lilies grow scarcely there.\nC. It is because these lilies grow singly there.\nD. It is because these lilies grow nicely there.\n\nCalla lilies grow ______________.\nA. in clusters\nB. singly\nC. in pairs\nD. in group\n\nThe selection is about ___________.\nA. gumamela\nB. calla lilies\nC. sampaguita\nD. roses\n\nHow will you describe the stalks of calla lilies?\nA. The stalks of calla lilies are slender.\nB. The stalks of calla lilies are stout.\nC. The stalks of calla lilies are wide.\nD. The stalks of calla lilies are short.\n\nCalla lilies may either be _______________.\nA. white or red\nB. red or yellow\nC. yellow or white\nD. red or white\n\nBrides used Calla lilies in their ___________.\nA. wreaths\nB. corsage\nC. bouquets\nD. headdress\n\nCalla lilies are also used for____________________.\nA. corsage for the head\nB. corsage for the dress\nC. wreath for the dead\nD. corsage for the dead\n\nCalla lilies are ___________.\nA. stately\nB. fancy\nC. unattractive\nD. cheap','teacher'),
(1107,1107,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nCounting the Rain\nI love counting the rain, Mother.\n\nAs they patter and as I see Do not all the great men like Rizal Count rain, too, when still young like me?\n\nSee the numerous dropping rains How hard it is for little brains.\n\nAnd I recall the children’s riddle About the old man’s countless canes.\n\nHow I love the fast falling rain Like a thousand pounding pestles And the little rushing water Down the stream to see it nestles.\n\nThe child is talking to his __________.\nA. father\nB. mother\nC. sister\nD. grandmother\n\nThe child loves to ___________ the rain.\nA. count\nB. drink\nC. catch\nD. splash\n\nThe child wants to know if counting the rain has also been an activity of ______.\nA. great men\nB. old men\nC. sickly men\nD. women\n\nThe rain nestles in the ____________.\nA. dream\nB. stream\nC. street\nD. River\n\nThe old man countless canes is a ___________.\nA. riddle\nB. fiddle\nC. peddler\nD. needle\n\nThe water rushes to the ___________.\nA. brook\nB. stream\nC. street\nD. Ocean\n\nIn the riddle, the countless canes refer to the __________.\nA. little brain\nB. dropping rains\nC. stream\nD. little hands\n\nHow does the rain move?\nA. withers\nB. patters\nC. gathers\nD. fades\n\nThe child loves the ________ rain.\nA. fast\nB. slow\nC. tumbling\nD. cold\n\nWhy does the child like the fast-falling rain?\nA. Because, it is like a rushing water.\nB. Because it is like a big waves.\nC. Because it is like a pounding pestle.\nD. Because it is like a silent water.','teacher'),
(1108,1108,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nThe Enchanted Deer\nOnce there were two warring tribes whose kingdoms were divided only by a wide river. The king on the land north of the river had a beautiful daughter named Mayumi; while the King on the land south of the river had a brave handsome son named Bayani.\n\nBoth kings hated each other so much that they even wanted their children to hate each other, too.\n\nBut fate played a trick on them. Mayumi and Bayani fell so madly in love with each other that they defied their parents’ wish. They often met secretly in the nearby forest.\n\nOne early morning, Mayumi went to meet Bayani in the forest. She had some enchanted power so she changed herself into a deer. She did not want anybody to see her.\n\nBayani waited for her. When he saw the deer, he was attracted to it. He quickly shot an arrow not knowing it was Mayumi.\n\nThe deer fell bleeding. Bayani ran to see his prize, but he saw Mayumi instead. He wept and, in his grief, he stabbed himself to death.\n\nThe two kingdoms were divided by ____________.\nA. mountains\nB. seas\nC. a river\nD. clouds\n\nMayumi was a daughter of the king of the ___________.\nA. South\nB. North\nC. East\nD. West\n\nBayani was the son of the king of the _________.\nA. South\nB. North\nC. East\nD. West\n\nHow will you describe the kings’ relationship?\nA. good friends\nB. enemies\nC. very old\nD. happy\n\nMayumi and Bayani _________________________.\nA. hated each other\nB. loved each other\nC. envied each other\nD. do not know each other\n\nMayumi and Bayani met in the forest ______________.\nA. openly\nB. secretly\nC. very often\nD. sadly\n\nHow will you describe Mayumi?\nA. Mayumi possessed some enchanted power .\nB. Mayumi was a witch.\nC. Mayumi was a magician.\nD. Mayumi was a warrior.\n\nMayumi could change herself into a ___________.\nA. dog\nB. horse\nC. deer\nD. cat\n\nWhat did Bayani do to the deer?\nA. Bayani shot the deer.\nB. Bayani just looked at the deer.\nC. Bayani ignored the deer.\nD. Bayani played with the deer.\n\nHow did the story end?\nA. happily\nB. sadly\nC. foolishly\nD. fearfully','teacher'),
(1109,1109,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nGreen Plant\nA green plant is the only living thing that can make its own food. The green leaves make food for the plant.\n\nTo make food, the leaves combine water and carbon dioxide together. With the help of the sunlight, water and carbon dioxide are made into sugar or starch. Sugar and starch are the food of the plants.\n\nWhat is the selection mainly about?\nA. what plants give us\nB. kinds of food\nC. how the green plant make its food\nD. The importance of plants\n\nWhat is true according to the selection?\nA. All plants can make their own food.\nB. Only green plants can make their own food.\nC. Animals cook food for the plants.\nD. All green plants can be eaten.\n\nWhat is needed by plants in making their food?\nA. water and sunlight\nB. oxygen\nC. water, carbon dioxide, and sunlight\nD. air and fertilizer','teacher'),
(1110,1110,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nThe Lost Sheep\nOne day, a Shepherd was tending his flock of one hundred sheep. As night drew near, he counted his flock. He found out that one sheep was missing. He was very much worried, so he immediately left the ninety-nine sheep to look for the missing one. He did not stop until he found the lost one. When he found it, he was very happy.\n\nWhat is a good title for this selection?\nA. The Good Shepherd\nB. The Lost Sheep\nC. The Foolish Shepherd\nD. The Lazy Sheep\n\nWhich sentence is true?\nA. The Shepherd did not mind the lost sheep.\nB. The Shepherd left the other sheep to look for the lost one.\nC. It is foolish to look for one lost sheep.\nD. The shepherd was not worried about the missing sheep.\n\nWhich of the following words best describe the shepherd?\nA. responsible\nB. irresponsible\nC. obedient\nD. generous','teacher'),
(1111,1111,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nInsects\nSome insects are very destructive to plants. The locust is a destructive insect. It is an enemy of the farmer. A swarm of locusts descending on a rice field is considered bad luck. It is because these insects can clear a rice field in one hour and eat all the grains of palay which are ready for harvest. When this happens, there will be a failure of rice crop, and shortage of food or hunger for the people.\n\nWhat is a good title for the selection?\nA. Destructive Insect\nB. Shortage of Food\nC. The Farmer’s Enemy\nD. The Lovely Insects\n\nWhy are locusts destructive?\nA. They can clear a rice field in an hour.\nB. They attack the poor farmers.\nC. They eat other insects and small animals.\nD. They kill people\n\nThe above paragraph is mainly about ___________.\nA. How to eradicate locusts\nB. How locusts multiply\nC. How locusts cause food shortage\nD. How to kill plants','teacher'),
(1112,1112,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nLinda Saves\nLinda did not have enough money most of the time. But she knew that others had much less . So every time she had money and was tempted to buy an extra candy or another cold drink, she would stop herself and put the money inside a box which was marked , “ For the Poor.”\n\nAt the end of the year, she was surprised to see that her box was filled and was very heavy. She sent the box filled with money to an orphanage.\n\nWhat kind of girl is Linda?\nA. hospitable\nB. charitable\nC. cooperative\nD. industrious\n\nDid she pity herself for being poor?\nA. No.\nB. Yes.\nC. The story does not tell\nD. maybe\n\nFor what was the money given by Linda to the orphanage?\nA. To buy decoration for the orphanage\nB. To buy medicine for the orphans\nC. To buy Christmas gifts for the orphan\nD. To buy appliances','teacher'),
(1113,1113,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nHearing Mass\nOne Sunday morning, Father, Mother, Teresita and Marivic went to mass. Marivic could not wait any longer for the mass to end. During consecration, she began to cry loudly. Everybody turned to look at her.\n\nHow did Mother feel when Marivic started to cry in the church?\nA. excited\nB. angry\nC. embarrassed\nD. Sad\n\nIf you were Mother and Father, what would you do?\nA. Spank Marivic.\nB. Take Marivic out of the church.\nC. Scold Marivic and finish the mass.\nD. Never mind Marivic.\n\nWhat should Father and Mother do with Marivic next time they go to mass?\nA. Leave her in the house with somebody.\nB. Let her play in the church patio.\nC. Bring her along and not mind if she annoys the other people in the church.\nD. Leave Marivic at home alone.','teacher'),
(1114,1114,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nThe Big, Pink Bundle of Joy\nMother has been in the hospital for five days. She could hardly wait to bring the big, pink bundle of joy to the children. Mother held the pink bundle lovingly to her bosom while Father called for a taxi.\n\nWhere were father and mother?\nA. in the hospital\nB. in the supermarket\nC. in the store\nD. at home\n\nWhy were they there?\nA. Mother was sick\nB. Mother delivered a baby\nC. Mother met an accident\nD. Mother had a check-up\n\nWhat bundle of joy were they bringing home?\nA. pig\nB. baby\nC. bag\nD. gift','teacher'),
(1115,1115,'Write the letter of the correct answer in the blank.\n\nWhy We Cook the Food We Eat\nWe cook our food for three reasons- to make food palatable and appetizing, to soften hard and tough foods, and to kill any microbe that may happen to be in the food.\n\nThere are many ways in preparing food. Food can be fried, boiled, broiled, roasted, baked, steamed, stewed, or sauteed. They can be well cooked or half cooked.\n\nIt is easier to digest food that is well cooked. But there are foods that are easier to digest when half cooked than when well cooked. Meat and liver for example, are easier to digest when half cooked. But certainly, they taste better when well cooked.\n\nMany vegetables are also easier to digest when well-cooked but some are eaten raw.\n\nWe cook our food _______________.\nA. For no reason at all\nB. For one main reason\nC. For three good reasons\n\nHard food becomes ____________ when cooked.\nA. Harder\nB. soft\nC. Indigestible\n\nBy cooking, the microbes in the food _____________.\nA. Multiply\nB. Are killed\nC. Disappear\n\nCooked food looks _____________.\nA. Appetizing\nB. Frozen\nC. Rotten\n\nGenerally, cooked foods are more ____________.\nA. Digestible\nB. Indigestible\nC. Nutritious\n\nMeat and liver are easier to digest when ___________.\nA. Not cooked\nB. Half cooked\nC. Thoroughly cooked\n\nMeat and pork certainly taste better when ___________.\nA. Raw\nB. Well-cooked\nC. Overdone\n\nVegetables are usually easier to digest when ____________.\nA. Raw\nB. half cooked\nC. Well cooked\n\nFrying, boiling, broiling, roasting, baking, steaming, sautering, and stewing are ways of ________________.\nA. Preparing food\nB. Buying food\nC. Eating food\n\nLettuce is a vegetable that is eaten ____________.\nA. Raw\nB. Well-cooked\nC. Ripe','teacher'),
(1116,1116,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nNestor and Serafin were both thrown together inside a prison cell which had only one small window. One night, they both looked out of their barred window.\n\n“What do you see, Nestor?” asked Serafin to his companion.\n\n“Oh, nothing except the mud right under this window,” answered Nestor.\n\n“Ah, but I see a star!” exclaimed Serafin looking at the same muddy water reflecting a twinkling light.\n\nWhat does Serafin feel in his heart?\nA. hope\nB. fear\nC. disgust\n\nWhat is a good title for the selection?\nA. What Men Do in Prison\nB. One Lonely Night\nC. Beauty is in the Eyes That See\n\nWho has a better outlook in life?\nA. Nestor\nB. Serafin\nC. none','teacher'),
(1117,1117,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nWhy Horses Need Iron Shoes\nWild horses do not need iron shoes to be able to run fast. But for work horses, shoes are necessary because the hard roads along which they are driven wear away the horn of the hoofs. The hoofs become dry and unhealthy through lack of moisture.\n\nOn their native pastures, the wild horses bathe their feet every morning with dew on the grass and the moisture soaks in the horn of the hoofs, and keeps them healthy and tough. Dew is a good ointment for horses’ hoofs and it has been found that horses which are kept at night in dewy fields have such healthy hoofs that they can do a great deal of work without shoes. But as long as horses continue to be driven or ridden along hard roads and stony streets, they will need iron shoes.\n\nWhat will happen if work horses do not wear iron shoes?\nA. They will run faster.\nB. They will work better.\nC. Their hoofs will be worn out.\n\nWhat should be done to horses at night?\nA. Let them work overtime.\nB. Let them roam on the grass.\nC. Let them stay in their stables.\n\nIf horses are well taken care of, what will become of them?\nA. They will work better.\nB. They will become wild.\nC. They will grow longer manes.','teacher'),
(1118,1118,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nFather Was Late\nMother and the children had been expecting Father for supper. They watched TV while waiting for him. When the program was over, it was already nine o’clock. Mother and the children looked at each other. Father had never been late for supper before.\n\nHow do you think they feel?\nA. Sad\nB. worried\nC. excited\n\nWho was late? Write his name in a square.\n\nWho waited for him? Underline your answer. Mother the children mother and the children\n\nDraw a clock and copy the time the story happened?\n\nWrite the word which tells how many times Father had been late before.','teacher'),
(1119,1119,'Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nIce Cream\nNo one knows when or where ice cream was first made. Today, ice cream is one of the best liked foods in the Philippines.\n\nIce cream is one of the nourishing foods we eat. It also tastes good. It is more than three-fourths milk and cream. It may have fruits like mango, atis, langka, and coconut or pineapple. It may have nuts, too. Egg and gelatin make ice cream smooth. Some ice cream flavors are vanilla, chocolate and strawberry. Ice cream makers advertise and make different flavors of ice cream every month. Nuts, fruits and flavorings are bought from all over the world.\n\nMuch ice cream is eaten as plain dessert at meals. A great deal of ice cream goes into ice cones, milk shakes, ice cream sodas, ice cream bars and ice cream cups. Ice cream is also sold by gallons and half gallons. It is served in almost all parties.\n\nWhat is ice cream mostly made of?\nA. fruits and eggs\nB. milk and cream\nC. nuts and gelatin\n\nWhat is usually used to make the ice cream creamy and smooth?\nA. water\nB. sugar\nC. gelatin\n\nIce cream was first made in the Philippines\nA. True\nB. False\nC. Does not tell\n\nWhat can you say about the ice cream?\nA. one of the nourishing foods we eat\nB. eaten as one of Filipino dishes\nC. best-liked food only in the Philippines\n\nWhich of the following is not listed as a flavoring for ice cream?\nA. chocolate\nB. strawberry\nC. tea\n\nwhat is the proportion of milk and cream in an ice cream?\nA. one-fourth\nB. three-fourths\nC. one-half\n\nWhy do you think do ice cream makers put fruits and flavoring to ice cream?\nA. to make it more expensive\nB. to make it thick\nC. to make it more palatable\n\nHow is ice cream eaten most of the time?\nA. as plain dessert after meals\nB. as melted drink on glass or cups\nC. as part of breakfast\n\nIn what form is ice cream bought?\nA. ice cream bars and scoops\nB. melted on glass or cups\nC. mixed in fruit salad\n\nWhat is the selection all about?\nA. dessert\nB. ice cream\nC. best Filipino food','teacher'),
(1120,1120,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nA Bookworm\nVicente is a bookworm.\n\nHe likes to read about almost everything. He likes to read adventure stories, nature stories, fairy tales, and biographies.\n\nWhenever he gets hold of a book, nothing could stop him from finishing it. However, he overworks his eyes by reading. He reads even when the light is dim.\n\nWhat do you think will happen to Vicente if he continues reading in dim light?\nA. He will get tired of reading\nB. He will lost interest in books\nC. His eyesight will become poor, so he will soon need eyeglasses\nD. He will have a very clear eyesight.\n\nWhat should Vicente do to save his eyes?\nA. He should not read at all\nB. He should read with a good light on\nC. He should read only books with big letters.\nD. He should always read in the dark\n\nWill he have a good eyesight if he continue to overwork his eyes and read in the dark?\nA. Yes\nB. no\nC. maybe\nD. not sure','teacher'),
(1121,1121,'Choose the letter of the correct answer:\n\nThe Ferris Wheel Ride\nMyrna was scared to ride on the ferris wheel. One fiesta day, her cousin asked her to ride on the ferris wheel. When the ferris wheel turned around, Myrna felt dizzy. She couldn’t look down. She felt as if she was falling.\n\nWhat do you think Myrna wished for?\nA. She wished the ferris wheel would soon stop turning.\nB. She wished that the ferris wheel would never stop turning.\nC. She wished that she had not ridden on a ferris wheel.\nD. She wished that the people will also shout.\n\nDid Myrna enjoy the ride on the Ferris wheel?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. maybe\nD. not sure\n\nWhat would Myrna most likely do next time?\nA. She would ride on a ferris wheel again.\nB. She would never ride on a ferris wheel again.\nC. She would not go on a carnival again.\nD. She will be excited to ride on a ferris wheel again.','teacher'),
(1122,1122,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Drawing Game\n\nDraw a big circle between two small circles. Draw your face in the big circle.\n\nDraw you mother and mother’s faces in the two small circles.\n\nDraw a square around the three circles.\n\nDraw a rectangle under the square. Write your name, grade and section inside the rectangle.\n\nBelow the rectangle, print in capital letters the name of your school and draw a rectangle around it.','teacher'),
(1123,1123,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nOne Market Day\nOne Saturday morning, Mother went to market. She brought two large baskets with her. She bought 2-1/2 kilos of beef, 1 kilo of pork and 2 kilos of fresh fish. She also bought vegetables like cabbage, beans, tomatoes, onions, and carrots. She bought bananas, pineapple, and papaya, too. Answer the following questions:\n\nBox the word that tells when the story happened.\nA. Saturday\nB. Sunday\nC. Monday\nD. Tuesday\n\nUnderline the word which tells where Mother went.\nA. church\nB. school\nC. market\nD. office\n\nWrite the number of things she bought.\n\nEncircle the number of baskets she brought to market.\nA. 10\nB. 11\nC. 2\nD. 5\n\nDraw the vegetables she bought','teacher'),
(1124,1124,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nDrawing Shapes\n\nDraw a big rectangle. Draw a triangle over the big rectangle.\n\nIn the middle of the rectangle, draw two vertical lines.\n\nIn the middle of two vertical lines, draw a small circle inside a bigger circle.\n\nDraw a square on each side of the vertical lines. Draw four horizontal lines inside each square.\n\nWhat have you drawn? Label the picture.','teacher'),
(1125,1125,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nGolden Shower\nOne of the most beautiful flowering trees is the golden shower. It is found in parks and in the gardens. It got its name from its golden-yellow flowers that bloom in clusters. This tree is useful as well as beautiful. Its bark is used for tanning leather and for medicine. It also bears a tubelike fruit. The pulp of the tubelike pod is also used for medicine.\n\nCopy the name of the tree.\n\nWrite the color of its flowers and draw a square around it.\n\nWhat word best describes how it blooms? Underline the correct answer. Singly pairs clusters\n\nName the parts that are used for medicine.\n\nThe golden shower is beautiful, but not useful. Answer with True or False.','teacher'),
(1126,1126,'Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nOur Forefathers\nIn the olden days there were no churches in the Philippines. Although there were no churches, our forefathers knew how to pray. They prayed in many places. Most often they prayed in their homes. They believed in a great good called Bathala, they believed that Bathala was the creator of all things. They worshipped him. They prayed to him.\n\nOur forefathers held religious feasts in the house of their headman or chief. Usually, a feast lasted for several days. All the people of the barangay attended. They would hang a large lamp at the center of the house and many small ones on the posts. Drums and gongs provided music. As part of the celebration, the people danced. They would beat drums for as long as the feast lasted. The people, young or old, enjoyed the religious festivities.\n\nWhen were there no churches in the Philippines according to the selection?\nA. for many years\nB. in the olden days\nC. for years\n\nWhat is the selection about?\nA. religion of our forefathers\nB. the musical instruments of our forefathers\nC. the enjoyment of our forefathers\n\nWhere were the feasts held?\nA. in the forest\nB. in the chief’s house\nC. in the plaza\n\nWho attended the feasts?\nA. all the children\nB. all the soldiers\nC. all the people\n\nHow long did their feasts last?\nA. a day\nB. a night\nC. several days\n\nWhat instruments were used to provide music?\nA. flutes\nB. gongs and drums\nC. guitars\n\nWhat were hung on the posts?\nA. pretty pictures\nB. small flowers\nC. small lamps\n\nWhat can you say about our forefathers?\nA. lazy\nB. religious\nC. godless\n\nCopy the letter of the word that tells to whom our forefathers prayed.\nA. anitos\nB. Bathala\nC. spirits of dead ancestors\n\nWhere did our forefathers often pray?\nA. in the church\nB. in their homes\nC. on the streets','teacher'),
(1127,1127,'Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nEarly Manhood of Father Gomez\nIt had been raining very hard for three days and the streets were flooded. Mariano, as Father Gomez was then called, had to stay home. His father looked at the water and said, “The roads are not safe to travel on. The children must stay home today.\n\nThe rain continued up to Sunday. The people could not go out to the streets. It was very hard to go to the church. They could not even go to the market to buy the things they needed.\n\n“Where is Mariano?” Mother asked. Mariano’s parents looked for him in the house. Finally, his father exclaimed, “There he is!”\n\nMother looked out of the window. She saw Mariano on a wooden raft.\n\n“Your young man is going to the church.” Father said. “Rain will not stop him from doing his duty.”\n\nWhen did the story happen?\nA. A day during the Christmas season\nB. A day during the rainy season\nC. A day during the summer month\n\nWhich were not safe?\nA. the buses\nB. the jeeps\nC. the roads\n\nHow long did the heavy rain last?\nA. one day\nB. two days\nC. three days\n\nWho could not be found in the house?\nA. Mariano\nB. Father\nC. Mother\n\nWhere did they find Mariano?\nA. on a bicycle\nB. on a raft\nC. in the kitchen\n\nWhere was Mariano going?\nA. to market\nB. to church\nC. to school\n\nWhy must the children stay home?\nA. The streets were flooded.\nB. The rain was strong.\nC. They were sick.\n\nWho looked for Mariano?\nA. his mother\nB. his father\nC. his parents\n\nWhat kind of a young man was Mariano according to the story?\nA. sickly\nB. strong\nC. religious\n\nWhat did Mariano become later?\nA. doctor\nB. lawyer\nC. priest','teacher'),
(1128,1128,'Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nInsects\nThere are more than half a million kinds of insects. The kinds of insects outnumber the kinds of all animals put together.\n\nInsects live in almost any kind of habitat. There are many in the sea, but they are found almost everywhere in freshwater and on land.\n\nAll insects have six legs and one pair of feelers. All insects are covered with a waterproof substance called chitin. Most insects have wings. Their bodies are divided into three parts: the head, the thorax, and the abdomen.\n\nMost insects crawl, hop, swim, jump or walk. But flying is their usual way of getting about. Some can fly very fast. The dragonflies one of the fast fliers.\n\nSome insects have pair of wings. Some have one pair. Some have none at all. It is safe, therefore, to think that insects differ in shape, size and color.\n\nThere are more than ____________ kinds of insects.\nA. thousand\nB. half a million\nC. hundred\nD. millions\n\nThe selection is mainly about ____________\nA. Uses of Insects\nB. Dangers of Insects\nC. Nature of Insects\nD. Insects food\n\nInsects live ___________________.\nA. in dark places\nB. in almost every place\nC. in trees\nD. in soil\n\nInsects usually move about by ____________.\nA. crawling\nB. hopping\nC. flying\nD. walking\n\nNo insects move about by ___________\nA. jumping\nB. slithering\nC. swimming\nD. Flying\n\nThe bodies of insects are divided into _________ parts.\nA. four\nB. two\nC. three\nD. six\n\nThe dragonfly _______very fast.\nA. jumps\nB. flies\nC. runs\nD. swims\n\nAll insects have _____________ of feelers.\nA. one pair\nB. two pairs\nC. three pairs\nD. four pairs\n\n____________ insects have two pairs of wings.\nA. All\nB. Some\nC. Young\nD. Old\n\nWhich of these is not true about insects? Copy the letter of your answer and encircle it.\nA. All insects have wings\nB. The body of insects have 3 parts.\nC. Insects have six wings.\nD. All insects can swim.','teacher'),
(1129,1129,'Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nMan’s First Clothes\nThe earliest true clothing of man was made of animal skin, grass or bark of trees.\n\nAbout 100,000 years ago man used the skin of bears, goats or buffaloes for his clothing. He scraped the inside of the skin and hung it dry. When the skin was dry, he made it soft by rubbing it with fat and by beating it with his hand. Then he cut the soft skin to pieces with sharp stone knife. He used rough needles made of bones and thread made of strong plant fiber to sew the pieces together.\n\nThe prehistoric man in warm countries, where plants and trees grew in abundance, fashioned his clothing out of grass and bark of trees. Clothes made of animal skin lasted longer than those made of grass and bark of trees. But the clothes made of grass were cooler, more comfortable and lighter.\n\nThe story is mainly about ______________________.\nA. animal skin\nB. man’s first clothes\nC. bark of trees\nD. food\n\nThe uncivilized prehistoric man wore clothing maybe mainly for _______.\nA. attraction\nB. modesty\nC. protection\nD. fashion\n\nThe animal skin was hung to _________________.\nA. dry\nB. soften\nC. harden\nD. melt\n\nThe skin was made soft by __________________.\nA. drying\nB. beating\nC. cooking\nD. soaking\n\nthe needles were made of ______________.\nA. stone\nB. bone\nC. glass\nD. wood\n\nThreads used were made of _________________.\nA. plant fiber\nB. plant roots\nC. plant seeds\nD. plant leaves\n\nWhere do you think clothes made of animal skin mostly worn?\nA. in cold countries\nB. in warm countries\nC. in all countries\nD. in all countries\n\nClothes made of animal skin ____________.\nA. was weak\nB. was durable\nC. was cool\nD. was warm\n\nAnimal was cut to pieces by a ___________________.\nA. sharp bolo\nB. sharp razor\nC. sharp stone knife\nD. scissors\n\nAbout how many years ago was this period? Write your answer in words.','teacher'),
(1130,1130,'Write the letter of the correct answer in the blank.\n\nThe Legend of the Poinsettia\nWhen the poinsettia begins to bloom, we are reminded that Christmas is near. As such it has become a Christmas symbol. Poinsettias begin to bloom in November until March.\n\nThe poinsettia is an odorless flower with brilliant red color. Its flowers are actually leaves. How the poinsettia came about is an interesting legend.\n\nIt happened during the time of the Holy Wars or Crusades when the Christian knights were fighting the Mohammedans to regain the Holy Land.\n\nIn one of the holy wars, a brave knight climbed the walls surrounding the Holy City to open the gates so that the other Christian soldiers could enter. A Moorish soldier saw him and shot him with an arrow. As he fell over the wall, his blood dropped on a shrub which later bore red flowers.\n\nPoinsettias bloom during the __________.\nA. Christmas season\nB. dry season\nC. summer months\n\nPoinsettias have become a _________.\nA. Christmas symbol\nB. bridal shower\nC. national flower\n\nThe poinsettia is _________.\nA. odorous\nB. odorless\nC. fragrant\n\nIt begins to bloom in __________.\nA. December\nB. March\nC. November\n\nThe brilliant red petals are actually __________.\nA. leaves\nB. seeds\nC. stems\n\nThe poinsettia came to have red color during ___________.\nA. World War I\nB. the Holy War\nC. the Japanese-Filipino War\n\nAnother name for those wars was the ___________.\nA. Civil War\nB. World War\nC. Crusades\n\nThe Christians were fighting against the ___________.\nA. pagans\nB. Mohammedans\nC. Protestants\n\nA Moorish soldier shot the knight with a/an ___________.\nA. dart\nB. arrow\nC. gun\n\nThe poinsettia is a _________.\nA. vine\nB. shrub\nC. tree','teacher'),
(1131,1131,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe food of fishes is as varied as the food of land animals. Many fishes eat other fish. Some eat only crustaceans such as crabs, shrimps, and sea mantis.\n\nMany other fishes have great strong teeth for crushing the shells of oysters, clams, and barnacles which they feed on. Some kinds of fish eat water plants called “lumot” and “digman”.\n\nYoung fishes and some species of large fishes feed on plankton. Plankton is the name given to microscopic plants and animals that live in rivers, lakes, and seas. Plankton is also called “pasture of the sea”.\n\nWhat is the selection about?\nA. kinds of fish\nB. food of fishes\nC. food value of fish\n\nWhich sentence is true?\nA. Fishes have different kinds of food.\nB. Fishes do not eat other fishes.\nC. Fishes eat only small plants and animals.\n\nWhy is plankton called “pasture of the sea”?\nA. They look like grazing meadows on the land.\nB. They serve as food for people.\nC. They serve as food for many kinds of fish','teacher'),
(1132,1132,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Mother’s Expectation\nMrs. Cruz was very proud of her son, Juanito. Since Juanito was in Grade One, he had always topped his class. He had been a gold medalist for four years. Now, Mother was expecting another gold medal for Juanito.\n\nA few weeks before the closing of school, Juanito got sick. He missed most of his lessons. Luckily though, he passed all his tests.\n\nOn Awards Day, Mother was waiting to be invited. But she was not.\n\nHow did Mother feel?\nA. embarrassed\nB. surprised\nC. disappointed\n\nWhy was Mother not invited?\nA. Juanito would not receive any medal.\nB. Father would pin the medal on Juanito\nC. Juanito would pin the medal on himself.\n\nDid Juanito pass the tests?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe','teacher'),
(1133,1133,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Surprise for Rolando\nIt was Wednesday morning, Mother prepared some nice dishes while Ariel cleaned the house. Armin arranged the furniture and Cecille arranged the curtains. They helped each other cheerfully. They wanted to surprise their youngest brother, Rolando, who’s celebrating his eleventh birthday.\n\nIn the afternoon, they all waited at the porch. Soon Rolando’s friends started to arrive.\n\nWrite the name and age of the boy who was celebrating his birthday.\n\nBox the name of the person who prepared the dishes.\n\nWrite the number of children in the family and their names.\n\nIs Rolando the eldest child? If not, write the opposite word of eldest.\n\nWrite the part of the house where they waited.','teacher'),
(1134,1134,'Copy the letter of the correct answer.\n\nThe Octopus\nThe octopus is one of the strangest-looking animals that ever lived. It has two large eyes and snake-like arms that made it frightful to look at. Its name, octopus, means “eight” arms. These eight powerful arms, called tentacles are joined around its mouth.\n\nThis sea animal is also called “devil fish”. However, it is not a fish. The sea animal belongs to a class called mollusks. It feeds on other sea animals like crabs, oysters and shrimps. On the under sides of its tentacles are suckers. Using these suckers, the octopus grasps other sea animals and takes them to the mouth.\n\nOctopuses are different from each other in size. Some are just as small as a one-peso coin. Others are as large as a beach umbrella.\n\nBeneath the head of the octopus is a funnel. This funnel is very useful to the octopus. When attacked by other animals, this funnel gives off a black liquid which hides the octopus.\n\nThe octopus is an animal that lives _________________\nA. in the air\nB. on land\nC. in the water\n\nThe octopus is ______________________________.\nA. funny\nB. nice-looking\nC. strange-looking\n\nThe arms of the octopus are like _________________________.\nA. snakes\nB. stairs\nC. stems\n\nThe word octopus means ________________________.\nA. five arms\nB. eight arms\nC. ten arms\n\nAnother name for it is ___________________________________.\nA. angel fish\nB. devilfish\nC. snake fish','teacher'),
(1135,1135,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Face in the Pool\nNarcissus was out of breath from running when he came to a clear pool. He stopped to drink. Just as he was about to sip the cool water, he saw a face in the pool. It was handsome and he was attracted to it.\n\n“Who are you?” he asked.\n\n“Who are you?” echoed someone.\n\n“Why are you mocking me?” asked Narcissus.\n\n“Mocking me,” said the voice.\n\nNarcissus was puzzled. He wanted to talk with the face in the pool but it would not answer. He tried to catch it but to no avail. At last, he lost hope and plunged into the pool hoping to see the owner of the face.\n\nWhy was Narcissus out of breath?\nA. He was running.\nB. He was Jumping.\nC. He was skipping.\n\nWhich of these sentences is true?\nA. Narcissus came to a clear lake.\nB. Narcissus came to a clear pool.\nC. Narcissus came to a clear path.\n\nHe stopped to ____.\nA. drink\nB. swim\nC. bathe\n\nHe saw something which ____.\nA. attracted him\nB. frightened him\nC. drove him away\n\nWhat did he see in the pool?\nA. a boat\nB. a face\nC. a flower','teacher'),
(1136,1136,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nJay’s Curiosity\n“Is Father going hunting?” Jay asked his mother when he saw his father get his gun and go out of the house.\n\n“Didn’t you listen to the radio, Jay?” asked her mother. “Every citizen who owns a gun without license is asked to surrender it to the government.”\n\n“Will Father be punished for owning a gun?” asked Jay.\n\n“No, Jay,” answered Mother. “No questions will be asked to citizens who surrender their guns. They will also be issued receipts.”\n\n“That is a very good move to prevent crimes,” said Jay. “Ordinary citizens like us will have nothing to be afraid of anymore.”\n\n“You’re right, Jay,” agreed Mother. “There is peace around us now.”\n\nWhich word best describe what Mother thinks about our present society?\nA. fearful\nB. peaceful\nC. same as before\n\nWhich of these statements is true?\nA. Citizen fear for their lives.\nB. Citizen cannot rest easily.\nC. Citizen are not afraid anymore.\n\nWhat is expected of every citizen who possesses firearms without license?\nA. to keep it\nB. to fight with it\nC. to surrender it','teacher'),
(1137,1137,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRice\nRice is a cereal native in Asia and Africa. It is the staple food of more than half of the people in the world.\n\nRice is a plant that grows the whole year round. It grows from two to four feet tall. It has round jointed stems, long-pointed leaves and seeds borne in dense heads on separate stalks.\n\nBrown rice with bran layer is rice in vitamin B, a cure for beriberi. Polished white rice is rich in starch but low in protein, fat, and vitamin B. In the Orient, the fine soft straw of the rice plant is used for weaving hats and slippers. Powdered grain is used for making laundry starch and distilled liquor.\n\nWhat would happen if rice would suddenly disappear?\nA. People would rejoice.\nB. People would not mind.\nC. Many people would starve.\n\nWhat will happen to a person with beriberi if he eats brown rice with the bran layer?\nA. He will get weaker.\nB. He will die of beriberi.\nC. He will be cured of beriberi.\n\nWhich do you think is more nutritious, brown rice, or white polished rice?\nA. brown rice\nB. neither of the two\nC. white polished ric','teacher'),
(1138,1138,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nWally’s Kite\nIt was Saturday morning and Wally was getting ready to fly his kite. Soon his friends Dennis and Berto came along. Together they went to the field. They tossed their kites up to the air. The kites flew up into the sky until they look like birds. The boys had a fine time. Suddenly the kite flew away up to the sky. He ran fast to follow his kite. Dennis ran to follow Wally. At last Wally’s kite landed on a treetop. Wally and Dennis reached the tree where the kite landed. Wally climbed the tree and brought his kite down, but it could not fly anymore. Its wings and tail were broken.\n\nWhat day was it?\n\nWrite the words that tell who came along while Wally was flying his kite.\n\nWhich of these phrases tell what the boys had. Copy the phrase.\nA. had a fine time\nB. had a bad time\nC. had an accident\n\nDraw the things that looked like birds.\n\nWhy Wally’s kite could not fly?\n\nIf you have a kite what design do you like to have and why?\n\nWhat object you can compare with a kite? Explain why?','teacher'),
(1139,1139,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nA Fairy Tale from Japan\nOnce upon a time there lived an old couple in one small village in Japan. They had no children so they went from temple to temple to offer prayers to their gods so that they might be blessed with a child. But their prayers were in vain.\n\nOne morning, after working in the garden, the couple sat down under a peach tree to rest. The husband was telling his wife how much he wanted to have a son to help him in his garden when a very big peach fell on his wife’s lap. She got the fruit and put it in her pocket.\n\nAfter a while, they went home for breakfast. As the husband was about to cut the peach, he heard a cry. “Let me out!” said the voice. When the wife looked into the fruit, she saw a child as big as her little finger. The woman lifted the child and kissed him.\n\n“I heard your prayers up there,” the child said, “So I came to be your son.”\n\n“We will love you very much,” the couple said.\n\nThe couple called the tiny child Peaching. They devoted their time in making him happy. They treated him like a real son. But Peaching did not grow big like other children. Yet he could help his father plow the field by sitting on the right ear of the plow horse and directing him where to go. He could also go marketing for his mother by carrying the coins in a small bag. He would sit in the market basket carried by a dog.\n\nHe always did his marketing early to avoid curios people who might notice him. The couple found a good helper in their tiny son. They loved him more each day.\n\nOne night as Peaching’s parents were sleeping, they both have similar dreams. They dreamed that if they would dip their tiny son into the brook that was in their yard, he would grow up like other children.\n\nThe next morning the happy parents told each other about their dreams They tiptoed to their tiny son’s room. The husband carried the bed where their tiny son was sleeping soundly. He immersed the bed with the tiny boy into the brook.\n\nThey were both surprised when the bed seemed to have disappeared from their hands. They were so frightened that they began looking for it among the waterlilies near the bank of the brook.\n\nThey heard a merry laughter behind them. When they turned, the couple saw a young man holding the tiny bed in his hands.\n\n“Father, Mother, I am Peaching,” he said as he rushed and embraced the couple.\n\n“A wicked fairy bewitched me and placed me inside a peach,” Peaching explained. “Because you loved me and baptized me in this brook, the enchantment was broken. Now I will really be a son to you.”\n\nThe more he became a great help to the couple. He loved his foster parents dearly. Not long after, Peaching met a lass from another barrio. He fell in love with her and they were married.\n\nThe old couple lived happily with their dear son and his family ever after.\n\nThe Japanese couple prayed for a ____.\nA. daughter\nB. son\nC. child\n\nThey sat down to rest under a ____.\nA. peach tree\nB. orange tree\nC. mango tree\n\nA big peach fell on the woman’s ____.\nA. shoulder\nB. head\nC. lap\n\nThe child they saw was no bigger than the couple’s little ____.\nA. head\nB. toes\nC. fingers\n\nThey called him ____.\nA. Peaching\nB. Orangeling\nC. Ting-a-ling\n\nThe tiny boy went marketing early to avoid ____.\nA. cars\nB. curious people\nC. his parents\n\nOne night, the couple dreamed that they should dip their tiny son into a ______.\nA. river\nB. well\nC. brook\n\nThe tiny boy was bewitched by a ____.\nA. fairy\nB. giant\nC. kapre\n\nThe enchantment was broken because the couple the boy ____.\nA. hated\nB. loved\nC. killed\n\nThe old couple lived with their dear son ____.\nA. happily\nB. sadly\nC. poorly.','teacher'),
(1140,1140,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRules to Follow\nThe Grade Six pupils decided to make their room clean, orderly and beautiful. Here are some of the rules everyone needs to follow: Wipe your shoes on the doormat before entering the room.\n\nPut the wastepaper, fruit peelings and candy wrappers in the wastebasket.\n\nKeep the walls, desks and tables free from pencil and chalk marks and scratches.\n\nPut the cleaning materials in a box.\n\nArrange the furniture properly.\n\nPut away your pencils, books, notebooks after using them.\n\nHang umbrellas and raincoats in one corner of the room.\n\nKeep books clean and well- covered.\n\nWhat did the pupils decide to do?\nA. make their room clean, orderly and beautiful\nB. make their homes clean, orderly and beautiful\nC. make their school surroundings clean, orderly and beautiful\n\nWhat did they agreed upon?\nA. to form a society\nB. to follow the rules\nC. to work on the school ground\n\nWhat will they do with their shoes?\nA. wipe on the doormat\nB. leave on the doormat\nC. put under the doormat\n\nFrom what will they keep the furniture free?\nA. books\nB. cover\nC. scratches\n\nWhere should the cleaning materials be kept?\nA. box\nB. drawer\nC. bookcase\n\nHow should the furniture be arranged?\nA. properly\nB. carelessly\nC. artistically\n\nWhere should one keep his pencils, books and notebooks after using them?\nA. in this bag\nB. on the desk\nC. on the teacher’s table\n\nWhere should wastepaper be thrown?\nA. in the desk\nB. in the drawer\nC. in the wastebasket\n\nWhere should one hang raincoats and umbrellas?\nA. on the desk\nB. in the cabinet\nC. on one corner of the room\n\nWhat should be kept clean and well-covered?\nA. desks\nB. books\nC. tables','teacher'),
(1141,1141,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nDaphne\nDaphne was a beautiful nymph. The sun god, Apollo, fell in love with her but she rejected him. Once, Apollo saw here and pursued her. Daphne sought shelter behind a big tree. She was out of breath from running. Now, as she leaned on the tree, she looked back with threw herself upon her knees and prayed to be saved from her pursuer. She rose with outstretched arms. Then her fingers fluttered gently, as if blown by a gentle breeze. Little by little she was changed from a beautiful nymph to a laurel tree. Slowly she broke into a smile. Now, she was safe. Apollo could not carry her away anymore.\n\nDaphne sought shelter behind ____.\nA. a big tree\nB. a laurel tree\nC. a large tree\n\nAs she leaned on the tree, she looked back with ____.\nA. round eyes\nB. smiling eyes\nC. frightened eyes\n\nShe was out of breath from ____.\nA. talking\nB. running\nC. laughing\n\nShe prayed to be saved from her ____.\nA. father\nB. pursuer\nC. husband\n\nSlowly she stretched out her ____.\nA. feet\nB. arms\nC. hands\n\nShe threw herself upon her ____.\nA. bed\nB. knees\nC. pillow\n\nShe was changed from a beautiful nymph into ____.\nA. an oak tree\nB. a narra tree\nC. a laurel tree\n\nHer fingers fluttered ____.\nA. slowly\nB. gently\nC. rapidly\n\nShe felt safe, after she became a ____.\nA. tree\nB. nymph\nC. goddess\n\nShe could not be carried away anymore by ____.\nA. Theus\nB. Midas\nC. Apollo','teacher'),
(1142,1142,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nHow the Romans Cooked Their Food\nLong ago the Romans used to cook their food just as the way hunters today sometimes do. They dug out an over in the ground, lined it with stones and made a fire in it. When the improvised oven was well heated, they raked out the ashes, put in the food and covered it. We learned about this from excavations in the city of Pompeii. Pompeii was destroyed and buried during the eruption of a volcano hundreds of years ago. When the city found excavated, ovens with wood and ashes were found among the ruins. It is believed that just as the cooks were about to bake, the fiery volcano rained out ashes and boiling lava and burned down the city.\n\nIn ancient times, how did the Romans cook their food?\nA. just as the cooks do today\nB. just as the people do today\nC. just as some hunters do today\n\nWhat did they use to line the ground where they built the ovens?\nA. lime\nB. clay\nC. stones\n\nWhat did they do with the oven before putting in their food?\nA. They cooled it.\nB. They heated it.\nC. They poured water in it.\n\nWhere did they dig their ovens?\nA. in the ground\nB. in the cement\nC. in the tree trunks\n\nWhat did they rake out of the oven before putting in their food?\nA. sand\nB. ashes\nC. wood\n\nWho cooked their food in underground ovens?\nA. the Jews\nB. the Italians\nC. the Romans\n\nWho had ovens with food ashes in them?\nA. the British\nB. the Romans\nC. the people of Pompeii\n\nWhat destroyed Pompeii?\nA. a great fire\nB. a terrible flood\nC. a volcanic eruption\n\nWhat were the cooks about to do when the volcano erupted?\nA. eat\nB. bake\nC. wash dishes\n\nWhat was done with the buried city?\nA. rebuilt\nB. forgotten\nC. excavated','teacher'),
(1143,1143,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nFirst Night in the City\nIt was Lynn’s first night in the city. She woke up with a stun and then she remembered where she was. She had never spent the night in a city before. She lay still and listened. Cars and jeepneys seemed to race like ants heading straight towards here. A new house was being built next to her aunt’s house and the cement mixer was roaring angrily. Downstairs, in the kitchen, she heard the clattering of pans. Then, she heard the chime of the big clock. She turned over and fell asleep again.\n\nWhat did Lynn remember when she woke up?\nA. She was in the city.\nB. She was in their house.\nC. She was in her country.\n\nHas Lynn ever spent a night before in the city?\nA. No\nB. Yes\nC. Maybe\n\nWhere did Lynn imagine the cars and jeeps going?\nA. heading to the city\nB. heading towards her\nC. heading towards the house\n\nWhere did the roaring sound come from?\nA. a bulldozer\nB. a cement mixer\nC. a street sweeper\n\nHow did she know the time?\nA. from the radio\nB. from the big clock\nC. from the position of the sun\n\nWhy did Lynn wake up with a stun?\nA. She was afraid.\nB. She had a dream.\nC. She felt she was in a strange place.\n\nWhat did she do when she woke up?\nA. She ran out to play.\nB. She washed her face.\nC. She lay still and listened\n\nWhat did Lynn hear passing in front of the house?\nA. buses and trains\nB. cars and jeepneys\nC. streetcars and carts\n\nWhere did she hear the clattering of the pans?\nA. from the streets\nB. from the kitchen\nC. from the living room\n\nDid Lynn fall asleep again?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe','teacher'),
(1144,1144,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nEd’s Day\nAs Ed passed in his neighbor’s yard, he saw Tiger, his neighbor’s dog, stumble and fall across the live fallen electric wire. Tiger gave one wild yell of pain and lay on the ground, kicking and struggling to set himself loose from the wire.\n\nEd stopped his neighbor’s two sons from going near the dog. He saw one of the boys rush towards the dog, crying. He ran to prevent the boy from touching the dog.\n\n“Oh, poor Tiger!” Ed said with tears in his eyes as he saw Tiger, helplessly freeing himself. Then he turned to the boys and shouted, “Keep back there or that wire will kill you, too.”\n\n“Run home quickly and call up the Electrical Company,” Ed told one of the brothers. “Tell them a live wire has killed a dog and may kill others too if they don’t hurry. Give the address. Run!”\n\nWhat did Ed see?\nA. how a fallen electric wire killed the passerby\nB. how a dog was killed by a live, fallen electric wire\nC. how a boy was killed by a live, fallen electric wire\n\nWho did Ed save from harm?\nA. Tiger\nB. the two boys\nC. the man from the Electric Company\n\nFrom whom would the boys ask for help?\nA. from the people\nB. from their parents\nC. from the Electric Company','teacher'),
(1145,1145,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Crayfish\nThe most striking thing in the appearance of the crayfish is the pair of nippers at the tips of its front legs. Joined together are its “thumb and finger”. The “thumb” is jointed so that it can move back and forth freely. Both nippers are armed with saw teeth and a sharp claw at the tip so that they can get a firm grip of any object. The claws can be bent in any direction.\n\nWhat is the story about?\na. the crab\nb. the crayfish\nc. a water animal\n\nWhich is true about the animal described?\na. It has a great pair of nippers on its sides.\nb. It has a great pair of nippers on its hind legs.\nc. It has a great pair of nippers on each tip of his front legs.\n\nWhich sentence describes their claws?\na. It can be bent sideways.\nb. It can be bent in any direction.\nc. It can be bent forward and back','teacher'),
(1146,1146,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nGreat Mind\nNot all great deeds are done in time of war. Here is the story of a man whose greatness was achieved in time of peace. He was Thomas Alva Edison. He was the greatest inventor that ever lived. He invented the talking machine and the electric light. He made instruments to measure the heat of far-off stars. He invented hundreds of things that gave comfort to the lives of millions of people. From childhood he showed an inquiring mind. He looked closely at things around him. His mind looked for causes and reasons. His mother had to answer and explain things to him. But Thomas was not contented. He tried to find out about things himself. So, he began performing experiments one after another.\n\nWhat is the story about?\nA. the greatest painter of all times\nB. the greatest sculpture of all times\nC. the greatest inventor of all times\n\nWhat distinguishing trait had Thomas Edison?\nA. He was very playful.\nB. He was always busy.\nC. He had an inquiring mind.\n\nChoose the best title of the story?\nA. The Experiment\nB. The Great Thinker\nC. The Greatest Inventor','teacher'),
(1147,1147,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Bible\nThe Bible is the word of God. The evangelists who wrote the Bible did so at the command and inspiration of God Himself. What God wants to say to us, He has indicated in the Bible, in the Old and New Testament, we learn what God wants of us: to make us know Him, love Him, serve Him and be forever happy with Him in heaven. It is in the Bible where we learn His exact will for us, His commandments, His love and reward for us, and the Last Judgment.\n\nWhat is the selection about?\nA. God’s physical appearance\nB. The Old and New Testaments\nC. The Bible as the Word of God\n\nWhich sentence is true?\nA. It is man’s word that is written in the Bible.\nB. We learn the exact will of God for us from the Bible.\nC. God will appear to us so that we may know what He expects of us\n\nWhat is the ultimate goal of God’s wishes for us?\nA. to punish us\nB. to appear before us\nC. to be forever happy with Him in heaven','teacher'),
(1148,1148,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Fox and the Stork\nThe fox and the stork were formerly good friends. They often visited each other. One day, the fox invited the stork to dinner because he wanted to play a joke on her. At the dinner table, the fox put soup in a very shallow dish before the stork. The fox could easily lap the soup, but the poor stork could only wet the end of her long bill. So, she left the dinner table as hungry as when she came.\n\n“I’m sorry you did not like the soup,” said the fox.\n\n“Oh, please, don’t apologize,” said the stork sweetly. “I hope you will come and dine with me soon.”\n\nSo came the day when the fox visited the stork. The stork served their dinner in a jar with a very long neck and a narrow mouth. All the fox could do was lick the outside of the jar.\n\nHow did the fox feel when he saw where the stork served dinner?\nA. He was angry.\nB. He was happy.\nC. The story does not say.\n\nWhat do you think happened?\nA. The fox thanked the stork.\nB. The fox went away satisfied.\nC. The fox went away as hungry as when he came.\n\nWas the stork right in what she did?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe','teacher'),
(1149,1149,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nPete’s Catch\nPete considered himself a fine fisherman. He stood on the riverbank as he held his fishing line with the patience only a fisherman knew. His heart beat faster as he felt a tug on the line. He was excited as he planted his feet firmly on the ground. He gave a big pull expecting to find a big fish at the hook of his fishing line. Disappointment clouded his face as he stared at his catch.\n\nWhat do you think did Pete catch?\nA. a big fish\nB. a big turtle\nC. a boot\n\nWhat kind of fisherman was Pete?\nA. He was an old fisherman.\nB. He was a good fisherman.\nC. He was an impatient fisherman.\n\nWhat did Pete do?\nA. went away\nB. sat on the riverbank\nC. threw his fishing line again into the water','teacher'),
(1150,1150,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Red Sweater\nLourdes opened her piggy bank. She counted her money carefully and went straight to a downtown store. She looked with longing at the bright new sweater on display at the store window. She wanted to inquire about the price but as she was about to go in, she stopped and hesitated. She counted her money again, then shook her head. At last, she looked at the sweater with regret and returned the money into her pocket.\n\nHow did Lourdes feel about the sweater?\nA. She disliked it.\nB. She liked it very much.\nC. She just wanted to look at it.\n\nWhat did she intended to do?\nA. She wanted to buy it.\nB. She wanted to ask for it.\nC. She wanted to ask Mother to buy it for her.\n\nDid she finally get the sweater?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Maybe','teacher'),
(1151,1151,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Graduation Gift\n“Thank you! O thank you, Father!” cried Jun embracing his father tightly. “This is the best gift I have received in my whole life. I have prayed and longed to own one and my prayer has been answered.”\n\nI am very glad about that,” Father said putting his arms around his son. “I waited for your graduation to buy one.”\n\n“I am not very happy about the bicycle,” put in Mother. “I hope it will not be a source of trouble.”\n\n“You don’t know it, Mother, but I have been riding Rene’s bike every now and then,” said Jun. “I can ride very well now.”\n\nOne afternoon, as Mother was tending her garden, she saw Jun passed by on his bicycle. Suddenly there was a crash! People were looking at the direction where Jun has turned on a curb.\n\nWhat do you think happened?\nA. Jun jumped out of his bicycle.\nB. Jun made fancy driving on his bicycle.\nC. The bicycle bumped against something.\n\nWhat made Jun happy on his graduation day?\nA. his graduation\nB. his Mother’s advice to him\nC. the bicycle his father gave him\n\nWhat do you think happened to Jun’s bicycle?\nA. It had a flat tire.\nB. It became twisted.\nC. The bicycle had some scratches.','teacher'),
(1152,1152,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Earthworm\nHave you seen an earthworm? Children are afraid of the earthworm. It lives in moist soil. It has no distinct head and it breathes through its skin. If you cut an earthworm in two, the parts will still live.\n\nThe earthworm is harmless. It helps the farmers and plant growers. The earthworm bores many holes in the soil. This way the soil becomes soft and well- mixed. Then more air goes into the soil. Soft, well-mixed and airy soil is good for the plants. Earthworms help the plants grow well.\n\nIn what kind of soil does the earthworm live?\n\nDoes the earthworm have a head? Write Yes or No.\n\nWrite the word that tells where the earthworm breathes through.\n\nWrite the earthworm have a head? Write Yes or No.\n\nCopy the correct sentence.\nA. The earthworm is harmful\nB. The earthworm kills the plants\nC. The earthworm helps the plants','teacher'),
(1153,1153,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nRizal and the Filipino Youth\nJose Rizal loved his people especially the youth.\n\nJose Rizal believed in the young people of his country. He wrote a patriotic poem entitled, “To the Filipino Youth”, dedicated to his countrymen. In this poem, he called the young Filipino the “fair hope of his fatherland”.\n\nRizal also wrote “Noli Me Tangere”. In this novel, he called on the youth to serve their country. He called on them to study harder and do the best they could to achieve peace and progress for the Philippines.\n\nCopy the name of the poem Rizal wrote.\n\nTo whom did he dedicate the poem?\nA. to his mother\nB. to his countrymen\nC. to the Filipino youth\n\nWrite the name of the book Rizal wrote.\n\nCopy the word that describes what kind of poem “To The Filipino Youth” is.\n\nWhat was Rizal’s counsel to the youth?\nA. to study hard\nB. to write books\nC. to take up arms','teacher'),
(1154,1154,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nChristmas Eve\nIt was Christmas Eve. Mr. Cruz’s family had finished their Noche Buena. “Come on children,” Mother called. “Hang your stockings and go to bed.” Sonia hung one for her mother, one for her father and a very small one for her baby brother. Tess put hers on one corner of her mirror. Dennis pinned his on the curtain of his bedroom window and Edmer found a place near his Cabinet.\n\nWrite the name of the holiday that the children were preparing for.\n\nWhat did the children do before going to bed? Copy the answer.\n\nCopy the phrase that tells what the family would do.\nA. go to bed\nB. go to a party\nC. go to the movies\n\nCopy the sentence that is true.\nA. Sonia hung one stocking for herself.\nB. Sonia hung stockings for herself and her mother.\nC. Sonia hung stockings for her mother, father and for her baby brother.\n\nWrite the name of the boy who pinned his stockings on the curtain of his bedroom.','teacher'),
(1155,1155,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nHints for Keeping Friends\nTo have friends, you must genuinely like people and consider their feelings. Here are some hints for keeping friends: Be true to your word. Get out of your way to be friendly and help others. Be a good listener. Avoid talking about people behind their backs. Avoid laughing at the mistakes of others and going around correcting people. Do your share of the work – don’t be lazy. Be neat and clean. Don’t insist on having your way. Don’t feel that you are better than others, and be careful not to give that impression. Treat others as you, yourself, would like to be treated.\n\nCopy the phrase that tells what the selection is about.\nA. how to be popular\nB. how to make and keep friends\nC. how to be at ease in a gathering\n\nWhat should one avoid? Draw a circle around the letter of the correct answer.\nA. Avoid talking to people.\nB. Avoid participating in a conversation.\nC. Avoid talking about people behind their backs.\n\nCopy the sentence that is true.\nA. Be neat\nB. Talk every time\nC. Wear new clothes every time.\n\nWrite the sentence that tells what you should not do.\nA. Do your share of the work.\nB. Be friendly and help others.\nC. Laugh at mistakes of others.\n\nShould you always insist on having your way? Write your answer.\nA. No\nB. Yes\nC. Maybe','teacher'),
(1156,1156,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions. Direction: Write the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet.\n\nBethlehem\nSince the birth of Christ, there have been a number of beautiful carols sung all over the world. One of them is the “O Little Town of Bethlehem” and it is the best loved of all Christmas carols.\n\nChristians know that our Lord Jesus Christ was born in the little, quiet town of Bethlehem which is not far from the city of Jerusalem. A long street leads to the Church of the Nativity. It is believed that Jesus was born on his very spot. Every year thousands of people all over the world visit Bethlehem, which is considered the most sacred place in the whole world. Beautiful gifts and offerings have been given to this famous Church of the Nativity.\n\nToday, most people who come to Bethlehem are disappointed to see that it has greatly changed. Bethlehem today seems to have lost its solemnity.\n\nWhich of the following is true about the Christmas carol, “O Little Town of Bethlehem”?\nA. It is loved by a selected few.\nB. It is the best loved of all Christmas carols.\nC. It is the least known of all Christmas carols.\n\nWhere was Jesus born?\nA. Palestine\nB. Jerusalem\nC. Bethlehem\n\nWhat marks now the very spot where Jesus was born?\nA. a stable\nB. a church\nC. a museum\n\nFrom where are the people who visited Bethlehem yearly?\nA. Jerusalem\nB. Bethlehem\nC. all over the world\n\nHow do people show their reverence to Jesus’ birthplace?\nA. by visiting it often\nB. by living near the place\nC. by bringing gifts and offerings\n\nWhat disappoints people who visit the place nowadays?\nA. its modernization\nB. its austere simplicity\nC. its poor accommodation\n\nWhat could be the religion of the people who come to visit Bethlehem?\nA. Hinduism\nB. Christianity\nC. Mohammedanism\n\nWhat do you think do many people visit Bethlehem?\nA. For no reason at all.\nB. To satisfy their curiosity.\nC. To show their reverence to Jesus.\n\nHow is Bethlehem known today? Write the phrase of your choice in capital letters.\nA. the richest city\nB. most modern city\nC. most sacred place\n\nWhat is the selection about?\nA. Christmas all over the world\nB. the birthplace of Jesus Christ\nC. the life and sufferings of Jesus','teacher'),
(1157,1157,'Direction: Fill in the blanks in each sentence with the correct answer.\n\nBirds of Paradise\nNo bird in the world has a brighter color than the birds of paradise. No one can guess from their color that they belong to the crow family.\n\nMost of the feathers of the birds of paradise have queer shapes. Some are used in making beautiful plumes. The male birds have bright colored feathers while the females’ feathers are dull in color. Some male birds of paradise do a great deal of dancing and strutting when they are courting.\n\nThere are about forty-five kinds of birds of paradise. They live mostly in the hot rainforest of the island of New Guinea. A few kinds are found in Australia.\n\nFor a time, there was a danger that all birds of paradise would be killed for their feathers. But now there are laws to protect them. So, we expect the species of the birds of paradise to be multiplied in the future.\n\nThe story is about __________.\nA. children\nB. animal\nC. birds\n\nThey are called ___________.\nA. birds of prey\nB. birds of paradise\nC. birds of feathers\n\nThey belong to the ___________ family.\nA. crow\nB. maya\nC. owl\n\nThe male bird’s feathers have ___________.\nA. dull colors\nB. bright colors\nC. green color\n\nThe female birds’ feathers have ___________.\nA. dull colors\nB. bright colors\nC. green color\n\nThe male birds of paradise do a lot of ___________ when courting.\nA. dancing and strutting\nB. jumping and bending\nC. kicking and prancing\n\nBirds of paradise are of __________.\nA. twenty kinds\nB. fifteen kinds\nC. forty-two kinds\n\nThey live mostly in the rainforest of ___________.\nA. New Zealand\nB. New Guinea\nC. New Jersey\n\nA few kinds of birds of paradise are found in ___________.\nA. America\nB. Australia\nC. Amsterdam\n\nWith the passage of laws protecting birds, we can expect them to ______.\nA. become household pets.\nB. multiply rapidly\nC. disappear in no time at all','teacher'),
(1158,1158,'Direction: Write the letter of the correct answer on your answer sheet.\n\nBirthstones\nSuperstitions about the precious stones originated during the early times. Anyone who wore any of these stones was supposed to become strong and brave. There were those who believed that when worn, these stones protect the wearer from fire and lightning. Still many believed that they were supposed to bring good luck and good fortune.\n\nOur present beliefs about birthstones are believed on these old superstitions. Wearing a birthstone is believed to bring good fortune especially when it is given by somebody as present.\n\nAlmost everybody nowadays do appreciate wearing birthstones. Although not everyone agrees on what particular birthstone is intended for each month, there is a grouping often followed in connection with birthstones. There are calendars that include the different birthstones for each month. We take interest in reading the meaning of the birthstones in our horoscopes. For who would not want to be protected and helped by his particular birthstone.\n\nSuperstitions about precious stones originated.\nA. a very recently\nB. a long time ago\nC. in the modern times\n\nWearer of these stones believed that they would.\nA. meet bad luck\nB. meet good luck\nC. have supernatural powers\n\nIt is believed that birthstones bring best luck when.\nA. they are costly\nB. they are given as presents\nC. they match the birth month of the wearer\n\nWhich of these is believed as an exemption from birthstones’ magical influence?\nA. Wearer can make an enemy sick.\nB. Wearer will become strong and brave.\nC. Wearer will be protected from fire and lightning.\n\nAll these good things attributed to birthstones are.\nA. not true\nB. very true\nC. somewhat true\n\nPeople nowadays wear birthstones for.\nA. good luck\nB. showing off\nC. their beauty\n\nIn buying birthstones, the first thing one should consider in his.\nA. birth month\nB. favorite color\nC. favorite design\n\nAs time goes by, do you think more and more people will believe in the good luck associated with birthstones?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. It depends\n\nThe birthstone for June is pearl. Draw a ring with a single pearl.\n\nThis selection as a whole tells us that good luck and birthstones.\nA. cannot be separated\nB. are closely associated\nC. has nothing to do with each other','teacher'),
(1159,1159,'Direction: Select the letter of your answer and write on your answer sheet.\n\nApples\nApples are valuable fruits. They provide important minerals and vitamins to people’s diet.\n\nThe first settlers in America brought apple trees with them to plant in the New World. The United States today grows more apples than any other country in the world.\n\nApple Trees can be grown from seeds as Johnny Appleseed or John Chapman in real life, did when he spread the trees across the United States in the 1800s.\n\nToday, most apple trees are grown by budding. Buds are cut from a healthy apple tree. Then they are inserted into a slit in the bark near the roots of another tree.\n\nThe tree grows to about 40 feet high. The fragrant, pink, and white apple blossoms appear late in the spring. The beauty and the taste of the fruit have made the apple one of the most popular fruits. Apples have different beautiful colors, like dark red, yellow green, and green.\n\nWhy apples are considered valuable fruits?\nA. They are rare and costly.\nB. They come in attractive colors.\nC. They are rich in vitamins and minerals.\n\nWho brought the apple trees to America?\nA. the first settlers\nB. the late settlers\nC. the immigrants from other countries\n\nHow did the apple trees fare in America?\nA. They grew abundantly.\nB. They almost did not survive.\nC. They disappeared eventually.\n\nWhat does this show?\nA. The Americans are not interested in apples.\nB. The soil and climate in America is suited for apples.\nC. The soil and climate in America is not suited for apples.\n\nIn what kind of climate do you think will apple thrive?\nA. cold climate\nB. warm climate\nC. any kind of climate\n\nHow did Johnny Appleseed grow apples in America?\nA. from buds\nB. from seeds\nC. from cuttings\n\nWhen did Johnny Appleseed spread apples in the United States?\nA. about 1600\nB. about 1800\nC. about 1700\n\nWhy do you think was Johnny called Johnny Appleseed?\nA. He wanted to be called Appleseed.\nB. His father’s surname was Appleseed.\nC. People began calling him Appleseed because he grew apples from seeds.\n\nHow are most apples grown now?\nA. by seeds\nB. by cutting\nC. by budding\n\nDraw an apple. Inside your drawing, write how many feet high the apple trees grow.','teacher'),
(1160,1160,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nThe Face in the Pool\nNarcissus was out of breath from running when he came to a clear pool. He stopped to drink. Just as he was about to sip the cool water, he saw a face in the pool. It was handsome and he was attracted to it.\n\n“Who are you?” he asked.\n\n“Who are you?” echoed someone.\n\n“Why are you mocking me?” asked Narcissus.\n\n“Mocking me,” said the voice.\n\nNarcissus was puzzled. He wanted to talk with the face in the pool but it would not answer. He tried to catch it but to no avail. At last, he lost hope and plunged into the pool hoping to see the owner of the face.\n\nWhy was Narcissus out of breath?\nA. He was running.\nB. He was Jumping.\nC. He was skipping.\n\nWhich of these sentences is true?\nA. Narcissus came to a clear lake.\nB. Narcissus came to a clear pool.\nC. Narcissus came to a clear path.\n\nHe stopped to ____.\nA. drink\nB. swim\nC. bathe\n\nHe saw something which ____.\nA. attracted him\nB. frightened him\nC. drove him away\n\nWhat did he see in the pool?\nA. a boat\nB. a face\nC. a flower','teacher'),
(1161,1161,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nJay’s Curiosity\n“Is Father going hunting?” Jay asked his mother when he saw his father get his gun and go out of the house.\n\n“Didn’t you listen to the radio, Jay?” asked her mother. “Every citizen who owns a gun without a license is asked to surrender it to the government.”\n\n“Will Father be punished for owning a gun?” asked Jay.\n\n“No, Jay,” answered Mother. “No questions will be asked to citizens who surrender their guns. They will also be issued receipts.”\n\n“That is a very good move to prevent crimes,” said Jay. “Ordinary citizens like us will have nothing to be afraid of anymore.”\n\n“You’re right, Jay,” agreed Mother. “There is peace around us now.”\n\nWhich word best describe what Mother thinks about our present society?\nA. fearful\nB. peaceful\nC. same as before\n\nWhich of these statements is true?\nA. Citizen fear for their lives.\nB. Citizen cannot rest easily.\nC. Citizen are not afraid anymore.\n\nWhat is expected of every citizen who possesses firearms without license?\nA. to keep it\nB. to fight with it\nC. to surrender it','teacher'),
(1162,1162,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nRice\nRice is a cereal native in Asia and Africa. It is the staple food of more than half of the people in the world.\n\nRice is a plant that grows the whole year round. It grows from two to four feet tall. It has round jointed stems, long-pointed leaves and seeds borne in dense heads on separate stalks.\n\nBrown rice with bran layer is rice rich in vitamin B, a cure for beriberi. Polished white rice is rich in starch but low in protein, fat, and vitamin B. In the Orient, the fine soft straw of the rice plant is used for weaving hats and slippers. Powdered grain is used for making laundry starch and distilled liquor.\n\nWhat would happen if rice would suddenly disappear?\nA. People would rejoice.\nB. People would not mind.\nC. Many people would starve.\n\nWhat will happen to a person with beriberi if he eats brown rice with the bran layer?\nA. He will get weaker.\nB. He will die of beriberi.\nC. He will be cured of beriberi.\n\nWhich do you think is more nutritious, brown rice, or white polished rice?\nA. brown rice\nB. neither of the two\nC. white polished rice','teacher'),
(1163,1163,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nWally’s Kite\nIt was Saturday morning and Wally was getting ready to fly his kite. Soon his friends Dennis and Berto came along. Together they went to the field. They tossed their kites up to the air. The kites flew up into the sky until they looked like birds. The boys had a fine time. Suddenly the kite fly way up to the sky. He ran fast to follow his kite. Dennis ran to follow Wally. At last Wally’s kite landed on a treetop. Wally and Dennis reached the tree where the kite landed. Wally climbed the tree and brought his kite down, but it could not fly anymore. Its wings and tail were broken.\n\nWhat day was it?\n\nWrite the words that tell who came along while Wally was flying his kite.\n\nWhich of these phrases tell what the boys had. Copy the phrase.\nA. had a fine time\nB. had a bad time\nC. had an accident\n\nDraw the things that looked like birds.\n\nWhy could Wally’s kite not fly?','teacher'),
(1164,1164,'Directions: Read the passages and answer the questions.\n\nA Fairy Tale from Japan\nOnce upon a time there lived an old couple in one small village in Japan. They had no children so they went from temple to temple to offer prayers to their gods so that they might be blessed with a child. But their prayers were in vain.\n\nOne morning, after working in the garden, the couple sat down under a peach tree to rest. The husband was telling his wife how much he wanted to have a son to help him in his garden when a very big peach fell on his wife’s lap. She got the fruit and put it in her pocket.\n\nAfter a while, they went home for breakfast. As the husband was about to cut the peach, he heard a cry. “Let me out!” said the voice. When the wife looked into the fruit, she saw a child as big as her little finger. The woman lifted the child and kissed him.\n\n“I heard your prayers up there,” the child said, “So I came to be your son.”\n\n“We will love you very much,” the couple said.\n\nThe couple called the tiny child Peaching. They devoted their time in making him happy. They treated him like a real son. But Peaching did not grow big like other children. Yet he could help his father plow the field by sitting on the right ear of the plow horse and directing him where to go. He could also go marketing for his mother by carrying the coins in a small bag. He would sit in the market basket carried by a dog.\n\nHe always did his marketing early to avoid curios people who might notice him. The couple found a good helper in their tiny son. They loved him more each day.\n\nOne night as Peaching’s parents were sleeping, they both have similar dreams. They dreamed that if they would dip their tiny son into the brook that was in their yard, he would grow up like other children.\n\nThe next morning the happy parents told each other about their dreams They tiptoed to their tiny son’s room. The husband carried the bed where their tiny son was sleeping soundly. He immersed the bed with the tiny boy into the brook.\n\nThey were both surprised when the bed seemed to have disappeared from their hands. They were so frightened that they began looking for it among the waterlilies near the bank of the brook.\n\nThey heard a merry laughter behind them. When they turned, the couple saw a young man holding the tiny bed in his hands.\n\n“Father, Mother, I am Peaching,” he said as he rushed and embraced the couple.\n\n“A wicked fairy bewitched me and placed me inside a peach,” Peaching explained. “Because you loved me and baptized me in this brook, the enchantment was broken. Now I will really be a son to you.”\n\nThe more he became a great help to the couple. He loved his foster parents dearly. Not long after, Peaching met a lass from another barrio. He fell in love with her and they were married.\n\nThe old couple lived happily with their dear son and his family ever after.\n\nThe Japanese couple prayed for a.\nA. daughter\nB. son\nC. child\n\nThey sat down to rest under a ___.\nA. peach tree\nB. orange tree\nC. mango tree\n\nA big peach fell on the woman’s ___.\nA. shoulder\nB. head\nC. lap\n\nThe child they saw was no bigger than the couple’s little ___.\nA. head\nB. toes\nC. fingers\n\nThey called him ___.\nA. Peaching\nB. Orangeling\nC. Ting-a-ling\n\nThe tiny boy went marketing early to avoid ___.\nA. cars\nB. curious people\nC. his parents\n\nOne night, the couple dreamed that they should dip their tiny son into a ______.\nA. river\nB. well\nC. brook\n\nThe tiny boy was bewitched by a ____.\nA. fairy\nB. giant\nC. kapre\n\nThe enchantment was broken because the couple the boy ___.\nA. hated\nB. loved\nC. killed\n\nThe old couple lived with their dear son ___.\nA. happily\nB. sadly\nC. poorly.','teacher'),
(1165,1165,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nIndependence Day\n“Tomorrow is June 12. We will celebrate our Independence Day. We don’t have classes, Mother,’’ Norma told her mother happily.\n\n“What do you want to do tomorrow then?’’ Mother asked.\n\n“Please, Mother, let us go to the Rizal Park. There will be a parade. Many of my friends will be there,’’ Norma said.\n\n“Alright. Tell Nelly and Bella to get ready for tomorrow. We shall all go and see the parade,’’ Mother said.\n\nWhat is celebrated on June 12?\nA. All Soul’s Day\nB. Christmas Day\nC. Independence Day\n\nWho will have no classes?\nA. Mother\nB. Norma\nC. Father\n\nWhere does Norma want to go?\nA. To the mall\nB. To the Rizal Park\nC. To Nayong Pilipino\n\nWho else will go to the Rizal Park?\nA. Uncle Ben\nB. Brother Jun\nC. Nelly and Bella\n\nWhat will they see?\nA. A show\nB. A dance\nC. A parade','teacher'),
(1166,1166,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nMoney from America\n“A letter from America,’’ the postman said as he gave the letter to Minda. “Thank you,’’ Minda said then called loudly,” Mother!\n\nFather! A letter! It is from America.’’\n\nFather looked at the letter. It was from their son, Norman. As Father opened the letter, something fell from it. Mother picked it up and said happily, “It is a one-hundred-dollar bill!’’\n\nHow happy Father and Mother were! Minda was happy, too. “Now, Mother, you can buy me a new dress,’’ Minda said.\n\nWhere did the letter come from?\nA. Pasay\nB. Manila\nC. America\n\nWhat fell out of the letter?\nA. Ring\nB. Money\nC. Picture\n\nWhere was Norman?\nA. In Manila\nB. In America\nC. In Mindanao\n\nHow did Father and Mother feel?\nA. Sad\nB. Angry\nC. Happy\n\nWhat did Minda want Mother to buy?\nA. Pants\nB. Dress\nC. Hat','teacher'),
(1167,1167,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nSchool Needs\nSamuel has two twenty-peso bills. Mother gave it to him. He will buy a bottle of paste, and several pieces of manila paper. He will cover his books at school He will cover his notebooks, too. His teacher said that all books and notebooks should be covered.\n\n“May I buy chocolate if I have some money left, Mother?’’ Samuel asked. “Why not a banana or any fruit instead of chocolate?’’ Mother asked.” Okay, I will buy banana.’’ Samuel said.\n\nWhich of the following choices might Samuel said?\nA. A kilo of meat\nB. A big notebook\nC. A bottle of paste\n\nWho said that all books should be covered?\nA. Samuel’s friend\nB. Samuel’s Mother\nC. Samuel’s Teacher\n\nWhat will Samuel do with the things he will buy?\nA. Cover books\nB. Cover posters\nC. Cover albums\n\nHow much did Mother give him?\nA. Fifty pesos\nB. Forty pesos\nC. Twenty pesos\n\nWhat food did mother want Samuel to eat?\nA. Fruits\nB. Candy\nC. Fried Chicken','teacher'),
(1168,1168,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nPlaymates\nEster and Elena are good friends. They are in Grade Two. They go to school together and go home together. On Saturdays they help their mothers in their homes. On Saturdays they go to church together. In the afternoon of Sundays, they play at Elena’s Grandmother’s yard.\n\n“I like to play here at your grandmother’s place,’’ Ester said. “It is nice to play under the trees.’’ “I like to play here, too. I enjoy picking some guava fruits after playing,”\n\nElena said as she climbed a guava tree.\n\nIn what grade were the girls?\nA. Grade One\nB. Grade Two\nC. Grade Three\n\nWhat do they do on Saturdays?\nA. Play all day\nB. Help their mothers\nC. Work in the garden\n\nWhen do they play?\nA. On Sunday nights\nB. On Sunday mornings\nC. On Sunday afternoons\n\nWhere do they play?\nA. In Elena’s house\nB. On the school playground\nC. At Elena’s Grandmother’s yard\n\nWhat does Elena enjoy doing in her grandmother’s yard?\nA. Picking flowers\nB. Playing games\nC. Picking guava fruits','teacher'),
(1169,1169,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nGoing to Doctor\nNestor felt very sick. His fever was very high. His mother brought him to a doctor.” You will have to be injected to get well,” the doctor said. “It would not hurt at all.”\n\n“Come on, Nestor. Be a good boy,” Mother said.\n\nNestor smiled at the doctor. Then he looked away as the doctor gave him the injection. “Now, tell me, did it hurt?” asked the doctor.\n\nNestor said, “Not at all. Thank you.”\n\nWho was sick?\nA. Nestor\nB. Mother\nC. The nurse\n\nWho brought him to the doctor?\nA. Father\nB. Mother\nC. The nurse\n\nWho injected Nestor?\nA. Mother\nB. The nurse\nC. The doctor\n\nWhy should Nestor be given an injection?\nA. To get well\nB. To get a prize\nC. To please his mother\n\nWas Nestor hurt with the injection?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Perhaps','teacher'),
(1170,1170,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nSchool Materials\n“Tomorrow, we shall go out to buy your school materials. You must all wake up early,” Mother said. The next morning, Pina Mario and Lorna woke up early. They went to the bookstore with their mother. “This store is very big. It’s full of school materials,” Mario said. Pina bought notebooks, pad papers and bullpens. Mario bought a box of crayons, a bottle of paste and a pencil. Lorna bought notebooks and a ballpen.\n\nWhat were the children going to buy?\nA. Shoes\nB. Uniforms\nC. School materials\n\nWhere did they buy them?\nA. In the market\nB. In the drugstore\nC. In the bookstore\n\nWhat were in the store?\nA. School materials\nB. Fruits and vegetables\nC. Eggs and canned goods\n\nWho bought pencil and crayons?\nA. Pina\nB. Mario\nC. Lorna\n\nWho went with the children?\nA. Sister\nB. Father\nC. Mother','teacher'),
(1171,1171,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nMeat and Milk\n“Meat is good for us. It helps the body. But meat is very expensive now.\n\nWhat food can take the place of meat?” Miss Domingo asked.\n\n“Mongo, fish and beans,” Helen said. “Very good. So, you see, children, there are a lot of food that we can eat in place of meat, Miss Domingo explained. “Is coffee good for us?” Melba asked. “No, coffee is not good for you. Milk is the best for children. Drinking milk helps make you healthy and strong,” Miss Domingo said.\n\nWhich one of these can take the place of meat?\nA. Candy\nB. Mongo\nC. Bananas\n\nWhich one of these drinks is good for children?\nA. Tea\nB. Milk\nC. Coffee\n\nWhat helps build the body?\nA. Meat\nB. Soft drinks\nC. Chocolate\n\nWhat food is very expensive?\nA. Meat\nB. Beans\nC. Mongo\n\nIs coffee good for children?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Always','teacher'),
(1172,1172,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nParty Dresses\nFour girls were talking about what to wear at Rosie’s birthday party. The party will be on the next day. “I will wear my pretty red dress to the party,” Myrna said. “I will wear my new blue dress,” Ana said.\n\nLucy was not talking. She had no new dress. She did not have a pretty dress either. “What will you wear, Lucy?” Agnes asked. “I will just wear our school uniform. I do not have any party dress. My mother cannot afford to buy a new dress.” Lucy replied. “I will wear our school uniform, too,” Agnes said.\n\nWho will wear a new dress?\nA. Ana\nB. Myrna\nC. Agnes\n\nWho will wear a red dress?\nA. Ana\nB. Lucy\nC. Myrna\n\nWhose party will the girls go to?\nA. Lucy’s\nB. Rosie’s\nC. Agnes’s\n\nWhat will Lucy wear?\nA. A nice, new dress\nB. Her school uniform\nC. Her sister’s new dress\n\nWhose mother cannot buy a new dress?\nA. Lucy’s\nB. Myrna’s\nC. Agnes’s','teacher'),
(1173,1173,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nAndres Bonifacio\nAndres Bonifacio was born in Tondo, Manila on November 30, 1863. His parents were very poor. He was very young when his parents died. He had three younger brothers and two sisters. He worked hard so that he could go to school. He made and sold paper fans on the streets together with his brothers. With the money that they have earned, they bought food to eat and clothes to wear.\n\nBonifacio was also a very good and brave soldier. He fought hard for the country. He is one of our country’s Heroes.\n\nWhere was Bonifacio born?\nA. Pasay\nB. Manila\nC. Baguio\n\nHow many brothers did he have?\nA. Two\nB. Four\nC. Three\n\nHow many sisters did he have?\nA. Two\nB. Four\nC. Three\n\nHow old was he when his parents died?\nA. Very young\nB. Old enough\nC. Ten years old\n\nWhat was Andres Bonifacio best known for?\nA. Being a brave soldier\nB. Being a good farmer\nC. Being a good teacher','teacher'),
(1174,1174,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nAt the Zoo\n“We are going to the zoo! We are going to the zoo! “Cynthia said happily as she got in the jeepney. Father, Mother and Norman were in the jeepney, too.\n\nAt the zoo, the children saw many monkeys. There were many kinds of birds, too.\n\n“Look at the big lion,” Norman said. “I am afraid of the lion. Let us not go near it,” Cynthia said. “We better go and see the elephant.”\n\n“Animals in the zoo do not harm you. Just do not go very near them,” Mother said.\n\nWhich animals did the children see?\nA. Bears\nB. Fishes\nC. Monkeys\n\nWho was afraid of the lion?\nA. Mother\nB. Cynthia\nC. Norman\n\nWhat animal did Cynthia want to see?\nA. Bird\nB. Lion\nC. Elephant\n\nWhat should the children not do?\nA. See the animals\nB. Move around the zoo\nC. Go very near the animals\n\nWhere did the family ride?\nA. In a car\nB. In a taxi\nC. In a jeepney','teacher'),
(1175,1175,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nCare of Books\nMiss Luna looked at the books of her children. Some books were covered with manila paper. Others were covered with newspaper. Others were not covered at all. A coin was inside Lorna’s book. A pencil was in Nestor’s book. Merlita’s book was covered very neatly.\n\nAfter Miss Luna has seen all the books, she said, “Tomorrow I shall look at your books again. All books should be covered. Do not put anything inside your books. The books are not yours. Take good care of them. Do not write anything on the pages.”\n\nWere all books covered?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Perhaps\n\nWhat was in Nestor’s book?\nA. A pen\nB. A coin\nC. A pencil\n\nWhat was in Lorna’s book?\nA. A pen\nB. A coin\nC. A pencil\n\nWhose book was neatly covered?\nA. Lorna’s\nB. Nestor’s\nC. Merlita’s\n\nWhat should the children do with their books?\nA. Write on them\nB. Play with them\nC. Take good care of them','teacher'),
(1176,1176,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nAn Honest Boy\nThe group leaders were checking the attendance when Miss Jose came in. She came from the office of the principal.\n\n“No one is absent in my group,” Renato said.\n\n“We’re all present today,” Pablo said. “Nobody is late, and nobody is absent.” Norma said.\n\n“I’m glad that you are all present today. And nobody is late,” Miss Jose said. Norberto stood up and said, “I came late today, Ma’am. You were in the office when I came.”\n\nMiss Jose was glad about Norberto. She said to him, “You’re honest. I’m proud of you.”\n\nWho were checking the attendance?\nA. The teachers\nB. The principals\nC. The group leaders\n\nWhere did Miss Jose come from?\nA. From the playground\nB. From the school clinic\nC. From the principal’s office\n\nWas everybody present?\nA. Yes\nB. No\nC. Perhaps\n\nHow many were late?\nA. One\nB. Two\nC. Three\n\nWhy was Miss Jose glad about Norberto?\nA. He was brave.\nB. He was honest.\nC. He was present.','teacher'),
(1177,1177,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nSelling Rice Cakes\nNora had just put her baby sister to sleep. She had boiled some water. She had cleaned the house. How happy she was when she saw her mother coming!\n\nMother put her basket down. There were eggs, bananas, a bottle of fish sauce, and meat in the basket.\n\n“I was able to sell all the rice cakes,” Mother said. “I bought a nice bag for you. Here it is. You have been a good girl,” Mother said.\n\n“Thank you, Mother,” Nora said.\n\nWhat would be a good title for the story?\nA. A Helpful Girl\nB. Selling Rice Cakes\nC. A Bottle of Fish Sauce\n\nWhich is true about Nora?\nA. She is a good pupil.\nB. She is a good dancer.\nC. She is a good daughter.\n\nWhat does Mother do to earn money?\nA. She sells in school.\nB. She sells in a big store.\nC. She sells in the market','teacher'),
(1178,1178,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nGoing to a Picnic\nSome girls were in front of the school. They were very happy. They were in Girl Scout uniform. Each girl had a bag. A bus was waiting for them.\n\n“My Mother put adobo in my bag,” Lucy said. “I have fried chicken,” Helen said.\n\n“I have eggs and tomatoes for lunch,” Zeny said.\n\n“Is everybody here now? Let us get on the bus,” Miss Dizon said. “The picnic place is far from here,”\n\n“Yes, Ma’am,” the girls said happily.\n\nWhat was true about the girls?\nA. They were all prepared.\nB. They were in school uniform.\nC. They were in Girl Scout uniform.\n\nWhat would be a good title for the story?\nA. Kinds of Food\nB. Going to School\nC. Going to a Picnic\n\nWhy was a bus waiting for the girls?\nA. To take them to school\nB. To take them to the church\nC. To take them to the picnic place','teacher'),
(1179,1179,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nNorma\nMrs. Abraham and Miss Carlos were Grade Five teachers. One day, they were talking about Norma when the principal came. “I have heard a lot about Norma,” the principal said.\n\n“Norma works well even if I am not in the room. She is a good leader,” Miss Carlos said.\n\n“Norma is the best in my sewing class. She is now sewing a dress for her little sister,” Mrs. Abraham said.\n\nWhat would be a good title for the story?\nA. Norma\nB. Miss Carlos\nC. Mrs. Abraham\n\nWhat is Mrs. Abraham?\nA. A teacher in music\nB. A teacher in sewing\nC. A teacher in reading\n\nWhat word best tells about Norma?\nA. Brave\nB. Healthy\nC. Wonderful','teacher'),
(1180,1180,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nRazon’s Children\nThe Razon family has three children. They are Gina, Bella and Bess. They are healthy children.\n\nOne Saturday afternoon, their mother had a visitor. Gina got all her toys and played near her mother. She made a lot of noise. Bess turned on the radio loudly. She danced and danced. Bella went to her room to study her lessons. She kept quiet. She heard her mother say, “Gina and Bess, please keep quiet.”\n\nWho among the girls did what was right?\nA. Bess\nB. Gina\nC. Bella\n\nWhat lesson should the two girls learn?\nA. How to dance well\nB. How to play carefully\nC. How to behave when there are visitors\n\nWhich is true about Bella?\nA. She dances well.\nB. She is a noisy girl.\nC. She is a good girl.','teacher'),
(1181,1181,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Vendor\nAling Doray calls out, “Vegetables, fish and meat! Meat, vegetables and fish!”\n\nOn her head is a big basket of vegetables and meat. On her right hand is a smaller basket of fish.\n\nOn the streets she calls out loud and clear, “Vegetables, fish and meat!” She wants people to hear her. If they hear her, they will buy.\n\nAling Doray goes from one street to the other. If someone calls her, she stops to sell.\n\nWhat do you call Aling Doray?\nA. A vendor\nB. A teacher\nC. A dressmaker\n\nWhich group of words makes you think Aling Doray is a vendor?\nA. Has basket of vegetables\nB. Goes from one street to another\nC. Calls out “Vegetables, fish and meat!”\n\nWhich is true?\nA. She sells in the school.\nB. She sells in the market.\nC. She sells from house to house','teacher'),
(1182,1182,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nLazy Carlito\nMiss Luna was giving her pupils a test when she was called by the principal.\n\n“Work on the test even when I am out,” Miss Luna said to her pupils.\n\nAs soon as Miss Luna was out, the children began to copy from their notebooks.\n\nBut Carlito did not copy from his notebook.\n\n“Why are you not copying from your notebook, Carlito?” Benito asked. “You had better copy like all of us,” Perlita added.\n\n“No, I will not copy even if I get zero,” Carlito said.\n\nWhat kind of a boy is Carlito?\nA. Kind\nB. Brave\nC. Honest\n\nWhich is true about Perlita?\nA. She did not copy.\nB. She told Carlito not to copy.\nC. She copied from her notebook.\n\nWhich is true about Carlito?\nA. He asked Perlita to help him.\nB. He copied from his notebook.\nC. He did not copy from his notebook.','teacher'),
(1183,1183,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Storm\n“We will stop our recitation now, children. You are going home right away,” Mr. Cruz said. “Why are we going home so early?” Pacito asked. “There will be a storm. You go home as fast as you can so that you will not get wet.” “What is a storm, Sir?” Josefina asked. “It is a strong rain. Strong winds go with the rains,” Mr. Cruz said. Soon the bell rang. The children went home as fast as they could.\n\nWhat is the story all about?\nA. The coming of rain.\nB. The coming of storm.\nC. The coming of strong winds.\n\nWhich sentence is correct?\nA. The children were sent home.\nB. The children stayed home.\nC. The children waited for the storm.\n\nWhat is true about a storm?\nA. It brings cool breeze.\nB. It sometimes has no rain.\nC. It has strong winds.','teacher'),
(1184,1184,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nLolita Had a Fever\n“You are very warm, Lolita. I think you are sick. Let me take you to the school clinic.” Nita said after feeling Lolita’s forehead.\n\nThe two girls went to the clinic. Miss Robles, the school nurse, asked, “How do you feel, Lolita?”\n\n“I am feeling cold. My head aches,” Lolita said.\n\n“I think you have fever, that is why you are feeling cold and your head aches,” Miss Robles said. “Take this medicine.”\n\nLolita took the medicine. She thanked Miss Robles. Then Nita took her home.\n\nHow did Nita help Lolita?\nA. She gave her medicine.\nB. She took her to the clinic.\nC. She bought candy for her.\n\nHow did Nita know that Lolita was sick?\nA. Lolita was sad.\nB. Lolita was crying.\nC. Lolita’s forehead was hot.\n\nWhich is the best title for the story?\nA. Giving medicine\nB. Going to the clinic\nC. Helping a sick friend','teacher'),
(1185,1185,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nLola’s White Hair\nHonesto stopped pulling his Lola’s white hair. His Lola said, “Count all of the hairs you pulled out. I will give you fifty centavos for every white hair you got.”\n\nHonesto counted the hair he pulled out. “I have pulled five white hairs, Lola,” he said happily.\n\n“How much will I pay you then?” Lola asked.\n\n“Fifty centavos for each white hair. I pulled out five white hairs. That makes two pesos and fifty centavos,” Honesto said. “So, you should pay me two pesos and fifty centavos.” Lola gave Honesto two pesos and fifty centavos. How happy Honesto was!\n\nWhat does the story tell about?\nA. How to count money.\nB. How to pull out white hairs.\nC. How Honesto earned two pesos and fifty centavos.\n\nWhy was Honesto happy?\nA. Lola gave him money.\nB. He pulled out Lola’s hair.\nC. He stopped pulling out Lola’s white hair.\n\nWhich sentence is correct?\nA. Lola’s hair was all white.\nB. Lola’s hair was all black.\nC. Some of Lola’s hair were white.','teacher'),
(1186,1186,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Wedding Day\nThe church bells were ringing. Linda and Rosie ran to see what was in the church. There were many people. They saw a girl dressed in a long, white gown. She had a long white veil on her head. She was very beautiful. They saw a man in barong Filipino near the girl.\n\nThen the girl and a man went inside the car.\n\nThe people followed the car. “They are all going to eat breakfast in the girl’s house,” Rosie said. “The girl is beautiful, isn’t she?”\n\n“Yes, she is, Linda said.\n\nWhat was going on in the church?\nA. A wedding\nB. A baptismal\nC. A communion\n\nWhat made Linda and Rosie go to the church?\nA. The people\nB. The beautiful girl\nC. The ringing of the church bells\n\nWhat word tells that it is morning?\nA. Church\nB. Beautiful\nC. breakfast','teacher'),
(1187,1187,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nHonesty\nLino was walking to school one early morning. He saw a small purse on the way. He picked it up. He opened it and saw money inside.\n\nWhen Lino reached the school, he went to the principal. He said, “This purse is not mine, Sir. I saw it on my way to school. There is a hundred-peso-bill inside.”\n\nThe principal got the purse and said, “You are an honest boy, Lino. I will try to find the owner of this purse.”\n\nThe next morning, Lino was called to the office. The principal said, “I found the owner of the purse. It is Miss Salvador’s purse.”\n\nWhat is true about Lino?\nA. He is kind.\nB. He is brave.\nC. He is honest.\n\nWhat happened to the purse?\nA. It was given to Lino.\nB. The principal kept it.\nC. It was given back to the owner.\n\nWhat lesson does the story teach?\nA. Be kind\nB. Be polite\nC. Be honest','teacher'),
(1188,1188,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nIn School\nIt was Monday morning. Myrna was early in school. She went to their room and saw her teacher writing on the blackboard. “Good morning, ma’am,” Myrna greeted. “Good morning,” Miss Castro said.\n\nMyrna put her schoolbag on her desk. She saw that the windows were not yet opened. The desks were not all arranged. She also saw that her teacher’s table was dirty.\n\nWhat did Myrna do?\nA. Went out to play\nB. Sat down and studied her lesson\nC. Opened the windows and cleaned the room\n\nWhat else did Myrna do?\nA. Ate some candy\nB. Fixed her schoolbag\nC. Cleaned the teacher’s table\n\nWhat do you think would Miss Castro tell Myrna?\nA. “You go away, Myrna.”\nB. “You are brave, Myrna.”\nC. “You are helpful Myrna','teacher'),
(1189,1189,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nEverybody Was Busy\nMother was busy cooking in the kitchen. Lola was helping her. Father was busy cooking Lechon. Annie was putting flowers in the flower vases. Luz was cleaning the house. Cynthia was washing the cups, plates and glasses. Helen was putting out the spoons and forks. Everybody was busy.\n\n“They are about to arrive,” Rudy said.\n\nWhy was everybody busy?\nA. The family was going to have a party.\nB. The family was going to have a program.\nC. The family was going to play some games.\n\nFor how many people were they preparing?\nA. one family\nB. many families\nC. about two people\n\nHow did the story end?\nA. No visitors came.\nB. The visitors had plenty to eat.\nC. The visitors did not like the food.','teacher'),
(1190,1190,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nPlaying in the Rain\nThe streets were flooded. The rain was strong. But Wilfredo and Lety were outside playing paper boats.\n\n“Do not stay very long in the rain. Come inside now,” Mother called.\n\nWilfredo and Lety did not obey their mother. They went on playing in the water.\n\nThat night Father and Mother did not sleep. They stayed near Lety, Lety could not sleep, either. She stayed awake in bed until morning I had better call a doctor,” Father said.\n\nWhat happened to Lety?\nA. She became sick.\nB. She had a toothache.\nC. She had a stomach ache.\n\nWhat would have happened had Lety obeyed her mother?\nA. She would not have gotten sick.\nB. She would not have gotten tired,\nC. Mother would have bought her a doll.\n\nWhat do you think happened next?\nA. Lety never got well.\nB. No doctor came for Lety.\nC. Lety got well after the doctor treated her.','teacher'),
(1191,1191,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nWatching Birds\nElvira and Helen were looking at the nest on the branch of a big tree. “The little birds are learning to fly,” Elvira said. “Look at them fly.”\n\n“One, two, three,” Helen counted. There are three little birds in all.” “No there are two more coming out,” Elvira said.\n\n“How pretty the birds are! They fly a little then go back to their nest,”\n\nHelen said.\n\nHow many birds were there in all?\nA. There were five\nB. There were eight\nC. There were three\n\nWhat did the girls do with the birds?\nA. Took them home\nB. Played with them\nC. Just looked at them\n\nHow did the story end?\nA. The birds died.\nB. The birds grew big.\nC. A cat ate them all.','teacher'),
(1192,1192,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Raincoat\nNorman ate his breakfast of eggs, milk, bread and banana. After eating, he brushed his teeth. He took a bath and combed his hair. He changed his clothes then put on his shoes.\n\nThen Norman got his school bag. He looked inside the bag to see if everything he needed in school was there. Before he left, mother said.” Bring your raincoat. The clouds are dark.”\n\nWhy did mother tell Norman to bring his raincoat?\nA. It was going to rain.\nB. The day was very hot.\nC. The sun was very bright.\n\nWhere was Norman going?\nA. To a party\nB. To a school\nC. To the market\n\nWhat time of a day was it?\nA. Morning\nB. Afternoon\nC. Noontime','teacher'),
(1193,1193,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nLate in Church\nFather and mother were already dressed up. Father was in barong Filipino. Mother was in a nice blue dress.\n\n“Come now, children. The church bells are ringing.” Father called. Narla and Zeny came out wearing pretty dresses like their mother’s.\n\nThere were already many people in the church when they arrived. Many people were standing outside the church.\n\nWhat day was it?\nA. Monday\nB. Sunday\nC. Tuesday\n\nWhat would they do in the church?\nA. dance\nB. pray\nC. play\n\nWhat do you think happened?\nA. They did not hear mass.\nB. They did not go to church\nC. They heard mass outside the church.','teacher'),
(1194,1194,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nDanilo’s School Things\n“It is almost about time, Danilo. What is keeping you so long?” Mother called. “You should have placed them in your bag last night. I always tell you that.” Then she helped Danilo look for his ballpen and notebook.\n\nJust then the school bell rang. “There is the bell.” Mother said, “And you have not found your ballpen yet.”\n\nWhich of these was Danilo not able to find?\nA. His ballpen\nB. His notebook\nC. His ballpen and notebook\n\nWhich happened to Danilo?\nA. He was late in school.\nB. He did not go to school.\nC. He came to school on time.\n\nWhat lessen does the story teach?\nA. To be clean.\nB. To wake up early.\nC. To have school things ready at night.','teacher'),
(1195,1195,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Tree\nFather came home with a pretty pine tree. “Let us put the tree in this corner,” Mother said.\n\nWhen the tree was put in place, Lilia and Remy started to put decorations on it. Lilia placed a big silver star on top of the tree. Remy placed bells and little stars on its branches. Cards with pictures of angels, flowers, candles, and bells were also hung on its branches.\n\nAt the base of the tree were beautiful boxes. There were gifts for everyone in the family.\n\nWhat do you think was the tree used for?\nA. Tree house\nB. Family tree\nC. Christmas tree\n\nWhat month of the year was it?\nA. June\nB. November\nC. December\n\nWhich is the best ending for the story?\nA. The tree was not beautiful.\nB. The family did not like the gift.\nC. The tree made everyone happy.','teacher'),
(1196,1196,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nPlaying at Night\nMany children were playing on the street. They were happy. It was night but they did not need any lamp. They could see each other very well.\n\nWhen the children got tired playing, they counted the stars. There were so many of them. An airplane passed by. It had beautiful lights. Later on, the children felt something fall.\n\n“Let us go home before we get wet,” Melinda said.\n\nWhat kind of a night was it?\nA. Dark night\nB. Bright night\nC. Cloudy night\n\nWhat do you think were falling?\nA. Stars\nB. Leaves\nC. Raindrops\n\nHow did the story end?\nA. The children went home.\nB. The children played in the rain.\nC. The children ran to their school.','teacher'),
(1197,1197,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nFlowers\nMarina brought flowers to school. She picked them their garden. She gave each of her classmates a flower.\n\n“How sweet these sampaguita flowers smell! Edna said.\n\n“Roses smell sweet and are pretty, too, “Tina said showing the rose Marina gave her.\n\nMarina’s classmates wanted to see Marina’s flower garden. They wanted to see the roses, dahlias, sampaguitas and gumamelas.\n\nWhy did Marina have a beautiful garden?\nA. She picks flowers every day.\nB. She takes good care of her plants.\nC. She waters her plants once a week.\n\nWhat will the children do one day?\nA. Visit Marina’s Garden.\nB. Buy flowers from Marina.\nC. Ask Marina for a picture of the garden.\n\nWhat did the children do?\nA. They helped Marina water the plants.\nB. They gave a party in school for Marina.\nC. They asked some flowers from Marina.','teacher'),
(1198,1198,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Christmas Gift\nIt was Christmas Day in school. All the children were happy except Nora. All her classmates had new dress. She wanted very much to have a new dress but her mother had no money to buy one for her. She no longer has a father.\n\nMiss Lozano said, “Before we begin our program, I am going to ask all those who have no mothers and Fathers to write what Christmas gifts they want. A rich man will give you the gift you will write down.”\n\nWhat do you think did Nora write down?\nA. A doll\nB. A book\nC. A dress\n\nHow did Nora feel after writing down the gift she wanted?\nA. Happy\nB. Sad\nC. Angry\n\nHow did the story end?\nA. Nora got a doll.\nB. Nora got a book\nC. Nora got a dress','teacher'),
(1199,1199,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nPlaying Soldiers\nIt was Saturday. Jun, Gerry, Samy, Ismael and Dindo were playing happily under a mango tree. Samy had a drum. As he beat the drum, Jun, Gerry and Dindo marched.\n\nIsmael was the leader. He gave the orders.\n\n“Left, left, left, right, left,” Ismael called aloud. To stop them he said, “Halt! One, two.”\n\nWrite the day when they were playing.\n\nDraw what Samy had.\n\nWrite the names of the boys who marched.\n\nWrite the name of the leader.\n\nCopy what Ismael said when he ordered the boys to stop.','teacher'),
(1200,1200,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nPaper Boats\nLito, Jose and Dario were playing in the rain. They were sailing paper boats. Lito’s boat was red and Jose’s was blue, Dario’s boat was green. They made their boats race. After thirty minutes the green boat was the first. The last was the blue boat. It was fun looking at the paper boats.\n\nCopy all the names of the boys in the story.\n\nDraw three boats and color them.\n\nWrite inside a box the number of minutes the boats ran.\n\nWhose boat was red? Copy and underline his name.\n\nWas Dario’s boat red? Answer with Yes or No.','teacher'),
(1201,1201,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nAt the School Canteen\nMarita had twenty pesos. She went to the school canteen to buy something to eat. Below was a list of the food sold at the canteen.\n\nBananas ....................... P3. 00\nSoup ........................... P5.00\nCookies ....................... P2. 00\nGinataan ...................... P5. 00\nBoiled corn ................... P5. 00\n\nWhich of the foods below are not sold at the school canteen? Copy them on your paper.\nbananas\nice drop\negg soup\n\nList down the foods that are sold in cups.\n\nCopy the name of the cheapest food.\n\nCopy the name of the fruit.\n\nWhere did Marita see the foods? Write your answer and underline it.','teacher'),
(1202,1202,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nAntonyms\nPlaying with words is fun. Jose and Lito enjoy playing with words.\n\nOne day the game they played was. “Giving the Opposite of Words.”\n\nLito said, “Boy.”\n\nJose replied “Girl” Words that have opposite meaning are antonyms. You can play the game with your pencil and paper. Just do what the directions tell.\n\nWrite inside a circle the opposite of in.\n\nWrite inside a box the opposite of night.\n\nWrite and underline the opposite of small.\n\nWrite the word down then write its opposite after it.\n\nWrite the opposite of father in capital letters.','teacher'),
(1203,1203,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Happy Family\nNorma and Cielo are Mr. and Mrs. Villa’s children. They study at Gomez Elementary School. Norma is seven years old and Cielo is ten.\n\nEvery night, Mrs. Villa plays the piano. Norma and Cielo sing. Mr. Villa just listens. Sometimes Norma and Cielo dance as their mother plays the piano. They are a happy family.\n\nCopy the word that tells about the family.\n\nWrite the name of the children.\n\nDraw two triangles like these.\n\nWrite Norma’s age in the first triangle and Cielo’s age in the second.\n\nWrite two things that Norma and Cielo do as their mother plays the piano.\n\nCopy the name of the school in the story.','teacher'),
(1204,1204,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nTrees\nTrees are our friends. They give us food. Some trees that give us food are santol, guava, Chico, mango, coconut and many more.\n\nTrees near homes are helpful. They give us shade. They make houses cool and beautiful.\n\nTrees also give materials for clothing. Some trees also give medicine. It is good to plant trees.\n\nCopy the name of the story.\n\nCopy the names of trees that give food.\n\nWhich is true about trees?\nA. Trees make cloth.\nB. Trees are our friends.\nC. Trees make medicine for us.\n\nWhich of the following tells what we should do?\nA. Burn trees\nB. Plant trees\nC. Cut down trees\n\nCopy the sentence that tells what trees do to houses.','teacher'),
(1205,1205,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nWater\nWater is very useful. Man, animals and plants need water.\n\nEvery living thing will die without water. What are the uses of water?\n\nWater is for drinking. Water is for washing. Water is for cooking.\n\nWater is for watering plants. Water is for cleaning. Many animals live in water. We should use water properly in school, at home and everywhere.\n\nCopy the sentence that tells who need water.\n\nWhich of the words below tells what will happen to us if there is no water?\n\nCopy the word in the first sentence that talks about water.\n\nCopy four of the uses of water.\n\nHow should we use water? Write your answer on your paper.','teacher'),
(1206,1206,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA-1 Child\nAre you neat and clean? If you do the things below, you are neat and clean. You are also strong and healthy. Then you are an A-1 child. Take a bath every day. Brush your teeth after each meal. Use a clean comb for combing your hair. Wear clean clothes. Eat the right kind of food. Sleep at least eight hours a day. Exercise in the open air.\n\nCopy the sentence that tells what you should wear.\n\nCopy the word that tells what you should do with the right kinds of food.\n\nWhere should you exercise? Copy and draw a box around your answer.\n\nWrite the part of your body that you should brush.\n\nWrite the number of hours you should sleep.','teacher'),
(1207,1207,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nTelling Time\nHere is a picture of a clock. When both of the hands of the clock point to the number 12, it is twelve o’clock. When the short hand of the clock points to number 6 and the long hand points to 12, it is six o’clock.\n\nWrite all the numbers you see in the clock.\n\nWrite the time that you see in the clock above.\n\nDraw a clock that shows twelve o’clock time.\n\nDraw a clock that shows three o’clock time.\n\nWhat time does this clock show? Write your answer inside a box.','teacher'),
(1208,1208,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThank You\nGerry is looking out of the window one night. He saw the moon so round and bright. He saw many stars shining in the sky. He saw children playing in the moonlight.\n\nThat night, before Gerry went to bed, he prayed. “Dear God, thank You for the night. Thank you, too, for the moon and stars. Please help me sleep well tonight. Please watch over me until morning. Amen”\n\nName three things which Gerry saw that night. Write them on your paper.\n\nWhere was Gerry? Copy your answer from the words below.\n\nWhat did Gerry want that night? Copy and underline the correct answer.\n\nCopy the word that tells to whom Gerry prayed.\n\nCopy the name of the things Gerry thanked God for.','teacher'),
(1209,1209,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nSchoolbags\nAling Rosa looked inside her children’s schoolbags. In Lety’s bag, she saw books, notebooks, ballpen and a box of crayons. In Dani’s bag, she saw books, a notebook, a ballpen and a pair of scissors. In little Annie’s bag, she saw a coloring book, box of crayons and a bottle of paste.\n\nAll the schoolbags were clean neat. Aling Rosa put one sandwich in each schoolbag.\n\nHow many children has Aling Rosa? Write your answer inside a circle.\n\nWhich of these was not in Lety’s bag? Copy and underline your answer.\nA bottle of paste\na box of crayons\nA coloring book\nbooks\n\nWhich of these was in Dani’s bag? Copy and underline your answer.\nA bottle of paste\na box of crayons\nA coloring book\nbooks\n\nCopy two words that tell about all of the schoolbags.\n\nCopy the word that names the thing Aling Rosa put in each schoolbag','teacher'),
(1210,1210,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nJeepneys\nJeepneys are fast rides. There are many of them in the century. Jeepneys come in different colors. There are blue, yellow, green and red jeepneys. There are also multicolored ones.\n\nSome jeepneys have stereo sets. There are those with brightly colored curtains. Students who go to school like to ride in curtained jeepneys with stereos.\n\nCopy the sentence that tells what kind of rides jeepneys are.\n\nCopy the different colors of jeepneys.\n\nWhat kind of jeepneys do students like to ride in? Copy the correct answer from the words below.\nRed jeepneys\nGreen jeepneys\nBlue jeepneys\nJeepneys with stereo sets\n\nCopy the word that tells what some jeepneys have.\nTelevision\nstereo sets\nRefrigerator\n\nWrite one word that tells what the story is about. Underline the word.','teacher'),
(1211,1211,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nFowls\nFowls are animals. They are animals with two feet and two wings. Their bodies are covered with feathers. They give us eggs. Their meat is good to eat. Chickens, ducks, turkeys, and geese are fowls. They eat grains such as palay and corn. Chickens like worms. They scratch on the ground to look for worms. Ducks and geese like to swim. They like to stay in the water. They are water birds so they have webbed feet. Turkeys have beautiful feathers. The male turkey spreads its tail like a fan. Geese are good watchers. They make loud noises when people come near. Fowls are good to raise. Many people get rich because of poultry raising.\n\nWhat are fowls?\na. Plant\nb. Trees\nc. Animals\n\nHow many feet do fowls have?\na. Two\nb. Four\nc. Many\n\nWhat covers their bodies?\na. Hair\nb. Hide\nc. Feathers\n\nWhat makes them fly?\na. Feet\nb. Wings\nc. Feathers\n\nWhich is not a grain?\na. Corn\nb. Meat\nc. Palay\n\nWhich does not have webbed feet?\na. Duck\nb. Chicken\nc. Goose\n\nWhich one do fowls do not give us?\na. Milk\nb. Eggs\nc. Meat\n\nWhere do ducks and geese like to stay?\na. In the sand\nb. In the water\nc. On the branches of trees\n\nWhy do chickens scratch on the ground?\na. To look for fish\nb. To look for water\nc. To look for worms\n\nWhich are good watchers?\na. Ducks\nb. Geese\nc. Turkeys','teacher'),
(1212,1212,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe First Monkey\nA long time ago, there was a farmer who had a wide corn field. He grew big and sweet corn. One day, he saw some of his corn was gone. Many corn plants had no more ears. He wanted to catch whoever was stealing his corn.\n\nThe next day, he hid behind a big mango tree. He carried a stick. He watched carefully until he saw a man coming. The man passed through a broken part of the fence and began to pick some corn. The farmer came out from behind the mango tree and walked toward the man. When the man saw the farmer, he started to run. The farmer ran after him but he could not catch him. Then he threw the stick at the man’s back, and it became a tail. The man went up the tree. He became smaller and smaller until he became a monkey.\n\nWhen did the story happen?\na. Last year\nb. A few years ago\nc. a long time ago\n\nWhat did the farmer have?\na. A rice field\nb. A camote field\nc. a corn field\n\nWhat words will tell about his corn?\na. big and sweet\nb. small and sweet\nc. sweet and yellow\n\nWhat happened to his corn?\na. were stolen\nb. destroyed by insects\nc. destroyed by the wind\n\nWhere did the farmer hide one day?\na. up the mango tree\nb. behind the guava tree\nc. behind the mango tree\n\nWhat did he carry?\na. a stick\nb. some fruits\nc. some stones\n\nWho did he see come into the corn field?\na. a dog\nb. a man\nc. a Monkey\n\nWhat did the farmer do to the man?\na. caught him\nb. threw the stick at him\nc. beat him with the stick\n\nWhat happened to the stick?\na. was lost\nb. was broken\nc. became a tail\n\nWhat happened to the man?\na. became sick\nb. became a monkey\nc. became a corn plant','teacher'),
(1213,1213,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nNo Carnival for Ben\nIt was Saturday. Andres, Anita, and Ben were at home. ‘’Father and I are going to the city,’’ Mother said. “We shall come home this afternoon. What will you do in the house?’’ ‘’I shall clean the living room,’’ said Anita. ‘’ I shall clean the yard,’’ said Andres. Ben was quiet. He wanted to swim in the river. ‘’Ben will clean the garden,’’ Father said. ‘’I will take you all to the carnival tonight.” But you must help in the house today.’’ Andres and Anita finished their work. Ben swam in the river. He forgot his work in the garden. In the evening, Father took Andres and Anita to the carnival. “Ben will not go to the carnival. He was not helpful today.’’ said Father.\n\nWhy were the children at home?\na. It was holiday\nb. It was Sunday\nc. It was Saturday\n\nWhere were father and mother going?\na. To the city\nb. To the farm\nc. To the show\n\nWhen were they coming back?\na. In the morning\nb. In the evening\nc. In the afternoon\n\nWhat did Anita do?\na. cleaned the kitchen\nb. cleaned the living room\nc. cleaned the bedroom\n\nWhat did Andres clean?\na. Yard\nb. Garden\nc. Pig pen\n\nwhat did Ben do?\na. swam in the river\nb. played in the yard\nc. played with friends\n\nwhat did father tell to Ben to clean?\na. Yard\nb. Garden\nc. Pig pen\n\nWho finished their work?\na. Anita and Ben\nb. Anita and Andres\nc. Ben and Andres\n\nWho was not helpful?\na. Ben\nb. Andres\nc. Anita\n\nWho went with father to the carnival?\na. Anita and Ben\nb. Andres and Ben\nc. Andres and Anita','teacher'),
(1214,1214,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Butterfly\nOne afternoon, Luisa took her little sister, Ann, to the garden. They saw a butterfly. The butterfly was resting on a flower. Ann ran to the butterfly. She wanted to catch it. The butterfly flew away. It rested again on another flower.\n\n“Do not disturb the pretty butterfly,” said Luisa. “It is sucking the nectar of the flower. Look at its pretty wings. It folds its wings when it rests. It opens its wings when it flies.”\n\n“Does the butterfly fly at night?” asked Ann. “No, the butterfly sleeps at night. It flies from flower to flower during the day. It likes the flowers. It likes the nectar of the flowers. Nectar is sweet,” said Luisa. Then Luisa picked a flower. She showed Ann the nectar of the flower.\n\nWhat time did the story happen?\na. morning\nb. afternoon\nc. night\n\nWhere did Luisa and Ann go?\na. to school\nb. to the garden\nc. to the playground\n\nWhat did they see in the garden?\na. bird\nb. nest\nc. butterfly\n\nWhere was the butterfly resting?\na. on a leaf\nb. on a fruit\nc. on a flower\n\nWho wanted to catch the butterfly?\na. Ann\nb. Luisa\nc. Mother\n\nWhat does the butterfly suck from the flowers?\na. milk\nb. water\nc. nectar\n\nHow does the nectar taste?\na. sour\nb. bitter\nc. sweet\n\nWhat does the butterfly do at night?\na. flies\nb. eats\nc. sleeps\n\nWhat does the butterfly do with its wings when it rests?\na. It folds them.\nb. It opens them.\nc. It moves them.\n\nWhere can we find the nectar?\na. in the fruit\nb. in the trunk\nc. in the flower','teacher'),
(1215,1215,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Moth\nIt was getting dark. Isabel lit a lamp. She put the lamp on the table. Soon a moth flew into the room. It flew through the window, then it flew around the lamp.\n\n“Look at the little butterfly. It goes around and around the lamp,” said Isabel.\n\n“No, it is not a butterfly. It is a moth,” Mother said.\n\n“But it looks like a butterfly,” Isabel said.\n\n“Yes, it looks like a butterfly, but it is not a butterfly. Look at its body and wings. They are covered with dust. It does not fold its wings when it rests. The moth sleeps at daytime. It flies toward the light at night. The moth likes the light,” Mother said.\n\n“Does the flame not burn the moth?” asked Isabel.\n\n“It does. It burns the moth when it gets too near it.” said Mother.\n\nWhat did Isabel light?\na. lamp\nb. candle\nc. flashlight\n\nWhere did she put the lamp?\na. on the floor\nb. on the wall\nc. on the table\n\nWhat flew into the room?\na. a bird\nb. a moth\nc. a butterfly\n\nWhere did the moth pass through?\na. door\nb. stairs\nc. window\n\nWhat covers the body and wings of the moth?\na. hair\nb. dust\nc. feathers\n\nDoes the moth fold its wings when it rests?\na. yes\nb. no\nc. Not stated in the story\n\nWhat does the moth like?\na. light\nb. dark\nc. flowers\n\nWhen does the moth sleep?\na. never\nb. at night\nc. at daytime\n\nWhere did the moth fly?\na. around Isabel\nb. around the lamp\nc. around the moon\n\nWhat time did our story happen?\na. noon\nb. morning\nc. evening','teacher'),
(1216,1216,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nKeeping Healthy\nNestor was not a healthy boy. He was pale and thin. His parents were worried about him. One day, Mother took him to the doctor. The doctor examined him.\n\nHe said, “You are not sick, Nestor. But you are not strong and healthy. You will be strong and healthy if you follow the health rules. Do you know the health rules?”\n\n“Yes, Doctor,” said Nestor. “I learned them in school. But I do not follow them.”\n\n“It is easy to follow them,” said the doctor.\n\nAt home Mother said, “What will you do to become healthy? Let’s see if you know.”\n\nNestor said, “I will drink milk. I will sleep ten hours every night. I will eat fruits and vegetables. I will play under the sun. Then I will be healthy.”\n\nWhich describes Nestor?\na. a weak boy\nb. a strong boy\nc. a healthy boy\n\nWho were worried about him?\na. his sister\nb. his parents\nc. his friends\n\nWho took him to a doctor?\na. father\nb. mother\nc. teacher\n\nWho examined Nestor?\na. Mother\nb. the doctor\nc. the teacher\n\nWas Nestor sick?\na. yes\nb. no\nc. Not stated in the story\n\nWhere did Nestor learn the health rules?\na. at home\nb. in school\nc. in the clinic\n\nWho knew that Nestor did not follow the health rules?\na. Father\nb. Mother\nc. the doctor\n\nWhat will he drink?\na. milk\nb. coffee\nc. soft drink\n\nHow many hours will he sleep?\na. six\nb. eight\nc. ten\n\nWhere will he play?\na. in the house\nb. in the school\nc. under the sun','teacher'),
(1217,1217,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Truthful Boy\nJuan was alone at home. His mother and sister went to the dentist. His Father was still in his office. So, Juan played with his dog. He ran after his dog, and his dog ran after him. Juan ran so fast that he ran into the table in the middle of the living room. The table was knocked down. The flower vase on the table was broken. How frightened Juan was! He picked up the broken pieces and put them on the table.\n\nSoon Mother and Pacita arrived. Pacita saw the broken vase first. “Mother, look! The vase is broken,” said Pacita. “Who broke it?” asked Mother. “Perhaps the cat,” said Pacita. Juan heard what Pacita said. He went to Mother and said, “It is not fair to blame the cat. I broke the vase, Mother, I am sorry.” “It is good you told me the truth. Next time, be careful,” said Mother.\n\nWho was alone in the house?\na. Juan\nb. Pacita\nc. Mother\n\nWhere did Mother go?\na. to the market\nb. to the river\nc. to the dentist\n\nWhere was father?\na. in school\nb. on the farm\nc. in his office\n\nWho went with Mother?\na. Juan\nb. Father\nc. Pacita\n\nWith whom did Juan play?\na. cat\nb. dog\nc. sister\n\nWhat was knocked down?\na. the bed\nb. the chair\nc. the table\n\nWhat happened to the vase?\na. fell down\nb. was broken\nc. was thrown away\n\nWho saw the broken vase first?\na. Father\nb. Pacita\nc. Mother\n\nDid Juan tell the truth?\na. yes\nb. no\nc. not stated in the story\n\nWhat kind of boy was Juan?\na. a kind boy\nb. a polite boy\nc. a truthful boy','teacher'),
(1218,1218,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Lantern Show\nChristmas was soon coming. The school was going to have a lantern show on Christmas Eve.\n\nOne day, Miss Villa brought to class a beautiful lantern. “This is a Christmas lantern,” said Miss Villa. “It is like a star. It is made of paper and bamboo. It can hold a candle. I made this lantern.”\n\n“Let us make lanterns like that for the lantern show,” said Pedro.\n\n“Let us make them of different colors,” Paz said.\n\n“My lantern will be red,” said Pedro.\n\n“Mine will be green,” said Paz.\n\n“Mine will be orange,” said Rene.\n\nEveryone wanted a different color. The children worked and worked. Then the lanterns were finished. How happy the children were to see their lanterns in the lantern show.\n\nWhat was the school going to have?\na. a lantern show\nb. a Christmas show\nc. a Christmas program\n\nWhat did Miss Villa bring to class?\na. a lantern\nb. a book\nc. a bag\n\nWhat does the lantern look like?\na. a star\nb. a moon\nc. a fruit\n\nWhat was the lantern made of?\na. wood and paper\nb. bamboo and cloth\nc. paper and bamboo\n\nWhat can the lantern hold?\na. a bulb\nb. a lamp\nc. a candle\n\nWho made the lantern?\na. a boy\nb. a girl\nc. Miss villa\n\nWhen will the lantern show be?\na. on Christmas day\nb. on Christmas Eve\nc. on the last day of school\n\nWho will make a red lantern?\na. Paz\nb. Pedro\nc. Rene\n\nWhose lantern will be green?\na. Paz’s\nb. Rene’s\nc. Pedro’s\n\nWhose lantern will be orange?\na. Paz’s\nb. Rene’s\nc. Pedro’s','teacher'),
(1219,1219,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Chinese Boy\nMother told Nilo to buy some bananas. So, Nilo went to the market. On his way he saw many children playing. Then he saw his friends, Celso and Ben. They were looking inside a room in Sana’s store. Sana was Chinese. He had a son named Sen Lu. “What are you looking at?” Nilo asked Celso and Ben. “We are watching Sen Lu,” answered Ben. “Why are you watching him?” asked Nilo. “He is eating rice. He is eating with chopsticks,” said Celso. “That is the way Chinese eat. It is clean to eat with chopsticks,” said Nilo. “Why do you say it’s clean?” asked Ben. “Chopsticks can be washed with boiling water. It is like eating with spoons,” said Nilo. Then Nilo went on to buy bananas.\n\nWhere did Nilo go?\na. to the river\nb. to the school\nc. to the market\n\nWhat was he going to buy?\na. rice\nb. fish\nc. bananas\n\nWho told him to buy bananas?\na. Father\nb. Mother\nc. Sister\n\nWho were Nilo’s friends?\na. Ben and Pablo\nb. Pablo and Celso\nc. Ben and Celso\n\nWho was Sana?\na. an African\nb. a Chinese\nc. an American\n\nWho was Sen Lu?\na. Sana’s son\nb. Ben’s friend\nc. Sana’s brother\n\nWhat were Ben and Celso doing?\na. watching Sana\nb. watching Sen Lu\nc. looking at the things for sale\n\nWhat was Sen Lu doing?\na. eating rice\nb. eating cakes\nc. eating candies\n\nWhy is it clean to eat with the chopsticks?\na. They can be kept\nb. They can be thrown away\nc. They can be washed with boiling water\n\nWhat is eating with chopsticks like?\na. eating with forks\nb. eating with hands\nc. eating with spoons','teacher'),
(1220,1220,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Monkey and the Crocodiles\nA Monkey wanted to cross a river. But there was no bridge and he could not swim. Then a crocodile swam to the river bank. The monkey asked the crocodile if it could take him to the other side of the river. He wanted to visit his friends.\n\nThe crocodile told the monkey to jump on its back. When they were in the middle of the river, the crocodile stopped swimming. “You, foolish monkey,” said the crocodile. “Do you not know that I am very hungry? I’m going to eat you.\n\n“Wait, I have a friend at the river bank, let us get him and you will have two for lunch,” said the monkey.\n\n“Very well,” said the crocodile. “Let us get him.” So, the crocodile swam fast. When they reached the bank, the monkey jumped away and said, “Goodbye, crocodile. Next time use your head.”\n\nWho wanted to cross the river?\na. Crocodile\nb. a friend\nc. a monkey\n\nWhy could he not walk across?\na. He had sore feet\nb. He didn’t like to walk\nc. There was no bridge\n\nWhy could he not cross the river?\na. He could not swim\nb. The water was cold\nc. The water was deep\n\nWhy did he want to cross the river?\na. He wanted to go home\nb. He wanted to look for good\nc. He wanted to visit his friends\n\nWho took him to the other side?\na. A banana\nb. a crocodile\nc. Another monkey\n\nWhere did the crocodile stop?\na. At the bank\nb. On the other side\nc. In the middle of the river\n\nWhy did crocodile stop?\na. He was tired\nb. He wanted to rest\nc. He wanted to eat the monkey\n\nWho was hungry?\na. The friend\nb. The monkey\nc. The crocodile\n\nDid the crocodile eat the monkey?\na. yes\nb. no\nc. The story does not tell\n\nWho used his head in the story?\na. The friend\nb. The monkey\nc. The crocodile','teacher'),
(1221,1221,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 11\nJulio dressed early. He did not want to be late to the program. He was going to recite a poem. He borrowed his big brother’s new pair of shoes and blue shirt. He was wearing black short pants. He combed his hair very neatly. “Are you ready for your part, Julio?” Miss Garcia asked.\n\n“Yes, Ma’am, I am,” Julio replied.\n\nMario and Lito were ready, too. They were dressed neatly. They looked at Julio. They smiled and began to whisper to each other. They looked at Julio from head to foot. Then they laughed.\n\nWhy did Julio borrow his brother’s new pair of shoes?\na. to win in the contest\nb. to be able to recite well\nc. to look nice in the program\n\nWhat did Mario and Lito do which is a bad manner?\na. They looked at Julio.\nb. They laughed at Julio.\nc. They whispered to each other.\n\nWhat would be a good title for the story?\na. Mario Laughed\nb. A School Program\nc. Julio’s Borrowed Clothes','teacher'),
(1222,1222,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 12\n“Always keep right,” Mother told Marina. “Look to your left and to your right before crossing the street.”\n\nOff went Marina with a basket in her hand. She was thinking of the things she would buy at the market.\n\n“I will buy a kilo of meat, a dozen eggs, fish, and vegetables. On my way home, I will buy a bar of soap at Aling Tinay’s store. Suddenly, a jeep stopped in front of her.\n\n“Get in. Ride with us. We are going to the same place anyway,” Chita, her friend, said.\n\nWhere was Marina going?\na. to the store\nb. to the market\nc. to the church\n\nWhat kind of a friend was Chita?\na. selfish\nb. generous\nc. obedient\n\nWhat did Mother remind Marina before she left the house?\na. what to buy\nb. to ride in a jeep\nc. some safety rules','teacher'),
(1223,1223,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 13\n“A picnic! A picnic!” shouted Amelia and Romy. “We will all go to a picnic tomorrow. We will ride on the school bus.”\n\n“May we go, Mother, please?” Amelia asked.\n\n“If the weather is fine, you will go. The newspaper says that there will be a storm tomorrow,” Mother said.\n\nThat night Romy prayed. “Dear God, please do not make it rain tomorrow. Make the day fair and let the sun shine.”\n\n“Did you enjoy the picnic, children?” Mother asked Amelia and Romy the next day. “We sure did,” they replied happily.\n\nDid the storm come?\na. No\nb. Yes\nc. It rained hard\n\nHow did Romy show his trust in God?\na. He did not pray.\nb. He prayed to God.\nc. He asked Mother to pray.\n\nWhat is a good title for the story?\na. The Storm\nb. A Rainy Day\nc. An Answered Prayer','teacher'),
(1224,1224,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 14\nAling Rosa lives in a small nipa hut with her three children. Her husband died a year ago. She earns money by washing clothes. Tony, her eldest child, helps by selling newspaper and magazines. Her two other children are too young yet to earn.\n\nOne day, Tony came home with one hundred and fifty pesos. He gave it all to his mother and said, “When I grow bigger, Mother, I will not let you work anymore. I will buy you beautiful clothes like those of Mrs. Arce’s, our rich neighbor. That night, Aling Rosa thanked God for her good son, Tony.\n\nWhat is the story about?\na. earning money\nb. Aling Rosa’s family\nc. Aling Rosa’s good son\n\nWhich word best describes Tony?\na. loving\nb. polite\nc. responsible\n\nWhich sentence is true about Tony?\na. Tony helps Mother earn money.\nb. Tony shines shoes to earn money.\nc. Tony brought Mother beautiful dresses.','teacher'),
(1225,1225,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 15\nMillions of years ago, whales used to walk on land. But as time passed, they have changed a lot. They became big and their hind legs disappeared. Their fronts legs have turned into flippers. They can’t live on land anymore but they are still mammals. This means that they breathe air and feed their babies with milk.\n\nTo fill its lungs with fresh air, a whale surfaces on the water. When it exhales, warm air escapes from its blowhole which looks like a spout. It is called a blow.\n\nThis story tells that whales live ____.\na. On land\nb. In the air\nc. In the water\n\nWhales are mammals because_____.\na. They live in the water\nb. They breathe through their lungs\nc. They can swim with their flippers\n\nWhy can’t whales live on land anymore?\na. They eat only fishes.\nb. They don’t have legs anymore.\nc. They get air they need from the water.','teacher'),
(1226,1226,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 16\nMaria’s mother was very ill last week. She had a very high fever. She kept tossing in bed. Once in her sleep she said, “I want to see Victor, I want to see Victor!” Maria heard what her mother said. She was by her bedside at that time.\n\nVictor is Maria’s brother in America. He went there ten years ago and has never come home for a visit ever since.\n\nNow Maria’s mother is very well and happy. Her wish in her sleep came true. Victor came home when he received Maria’s letter.\n\nWhat did maria do when she heard her mother’s wish?\na. Went to America\nb. Did not care at all\nc. Sent victor a letter\n\nWhat was maria’s mother’s wish that was granted?\na. To get well\nb. To see victor\nc. to go to America\n\nWhat would be a good title for the story?\na. A letter\nb. Maria’s mother\nc. A dream come true','teacher'),
(1227,1227,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 17\nTen-year-old Jose and his one-year-old brother were playing. How happy they were. “Boo,” Jose said as he nodded his head. “Boo, “his little brother repeated as he nodded his head. Jose clapped his hands. His little brother clapped, too. Jose laughed and so did his brother. “Mother, look at him. He does everything I do. He says everything I say,” Jose said. “Will he always be like that? “Yes, little brothers and sisters follow their older brothers or sisters. So always be a good example,” Mother said.\n\nWhy was Jose happy?\na. He was working alone.\nb. He was a good brother.\nc. He was playing with his brother.\n\nWhat did Jose’s little brother do?\na. Laugh and cried.\nb. Clapped his hands and cried\nc. Followed what Jose said and did.\n\nWhat lesson does the story teach?\na. To be kind\nb. To be playful\nc. To be a good example','teacher'),
(1228,1228,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 18\nThat night Dindo could not sleep. He was thinking of Pastor. He boxed him hard on his left eye when they had a fight. Pastor had a black eye. Dindo was very sorry for what he did.\n\nThe following morning Dindo saw pastor standing alone by the school gate. He went near him and said, “I am sorry, Pastor, for what I did, I hope you will forgive me.” “Forget it, Dindo. I am sorry as you are, Pastor said. Then, the school bell rang. Both boys went to their classroom together.\n\nWhich sentence is correct?\na. Dindo had a black eye.\nb. Dindo could sleep well after the fight.\nc. Dindo and Pastor became friends again.\n\nWhich sentence tells about the boys?\na. They had a fight.\nb. They rang the school bell.\nc. They went to sleep together.\n\nWhat is a good name for the story?\na. Pastor’s friend\nb. Two sorry boys\nc. going to school','teacher'),
(1229,1229,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 19\nVicente wanted to buy a new basketball. He had only ten pesos. He needed seventy-five pesos more for a new one. The whole night he had been thinking of what to do to raise the money he needed.\n\n“May I help you, Mother?” Vicente said the next morning while his mother was cleaning the house. “You better go to the garden and help your father,” Mother replied. The whole day Vicente and his father worked in the garden. They planted seven plots of Okra, Eggplant and Amargoso. When the family was resting that night, father said,” I know you want to buy a new basketball. Take this money and buy one.” Vicente was very happy. He will have a new basketball and save also his ten pesos.\n\nWhat is the story about?\na. How to plant\nb. How to help mother?\nc. How Vicente got a new basketball?\n\nWhat word best describes about Vicente?\na. Great\nb. Honest\nc. Helpful\n\nWhy did Vicente need money?\na. To buy seeds\nb. To buy a shirt\nc. To buy a basketball','teacher'),
(1230,1230,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 20\nThe clock struck eleven. “It is eleven o’clock sharp and mother have not come home from the market. Aunt Martha and Uncle Leon are coming for lunch,” Mila said to herself.\n\nMila went to the kitchen. She washed a kettle. Then she placed three cups of rice in it. She was washing the rice when she heard a tricycle stop in front of their house. She looked out of the window. Her face turned pale.\n\n“Oh, my God, they are here. No food is ready yet,” Mila exclaimed as she ran to meet the visitors. Just then another calesa arrived. Mila’s face turned bright and happy. “What took you so long, Mother?” Mila asked.\n\nWhich sentence is correct?\na. Aunt Martha did not come\nb. Mila did not help cook rice\nc. Mother stayed long in the market\n\nWho arrived first?\na. Mother\nb. Aunt Martha with Uncle Leon\nc. Aunt Martha\n\nWhich word best describes Mila?\na. Helpful\nb. Brave\nc. Kind','teacher'),
(1231,1231,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nActivity 21\nSchool children work and play with many people. Communicable diseases spread easily in a school if health rules are not followed. How do schools try to prevent the spread of diseases?\n\nSchools see to it that children are examine regularly by the school doctor and school dentist. Lessons on how to take care of one’s health are taught. Children are served good food during recess time. They get fresh air and exercise during their physical education classes. They are given immunization against cholera, typhoid and dysentery.\n\nWhat will happen if school children will obey all health rules?\na. They will be tall\nb. They will become rich\nc. They will be healthy and strong\n\nWhy do schools teach children how to take care of their health?\na. To make them honest\nb. To make them happy\nc. To prevent the spread of diseases\n\nWhat is the story about?\na. How schools help prevent the spread of flies?\nb. How schools help prevent the spread of diseases\nc. How schools help prevent the spread of mosquitoes','teacher'),
(1232,1232,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nForgetful Lucia\nLucia put all her things in her bag. She looked under the table. She looked inside her box. “Mother, I cannot find it. I need it for cutting pictures for my album,” Lucia said.\n\n“You look around some more,” Mother said. Then suddenly Mother laughed. She was looking at Lucia’s hands. “Look at what you have in your hands,” Mother said.\n\nLucia laughed loudly. “How forgetful I am!” she said. “Is it not funny looking for something that I am holding?”\n\nWhat was Lucia looking for?\na. Needle\nb. Pair of scissors\nc. Pictures\n\nWhat made Mother laugh?\na. Lucia was about to cry.\nb. Lucia found what she was looking for\nc. Lucia was holding what she was looking for.\n\nHow did the story end?\na. Lucia cried.\nb. Lucia went to sleep.\nc. Lucia cut pictures for her album.','teacher'),
(1233,1233,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nJust Pray\nMrs. Soriano’s house was very quiet. The people inside seemed to be talking in whispers. But everyone seemed to be actively moving around. Suddenly an ambulance stopped in front of the house. With it came a doctor and a nurse. Soon the eldest girl in the family came out holding two pillows. She was followed by her brother who was holding a bag and a blanket. Then a patient in a stretcher was placed in the ambulance. “Do not cry,” the girl said to her little sister. “Just pray that Mother will get well soon.”\n\nWhat was the ambulance for?\na. to get a sick person\nb. to give free injections\nc. to distribute medicines\n\nWho was the sick person?\na. Mother\nb. the boy\nc. the eldest girl\n\nHow did the story end?\na. The eldest girl died.\nb. Mother was brought to the hospital.\nc. The ambulance stayed in front of the house.','teacher'),
(1234,1234,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nAn Early Morning Fight\nOne early morning, Roel peeped through a partly opened window. He saw two men fighting. One of them was holding a stick while the other was holding a stick while other was waving a bolo. A woman was trying hard to hold back one man from getting near the other.\n\n“Please, Roman,” the woman said, “do not go near. You might get killed. Think of our children.”\n\nMore people were gathering around. Then a mobile patrol car came screeching in. The noisy crowd became very quiet.\n\nWho came out of the mobile patrol?\na. farmers\nb. policeman\nc. teachers\n\nWhat was the police mobile patrol for?\na. to give Roel money\nb. to arrest the persons who were fighting\nc. to stop the people from gathering around\n\nWhat was likely to happen?\na. The people fought against one another.\nb. Ramon and Roel quarreled.\nc. The men stopped fighting.','teacher'),
(1235,1235,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe King’s Little Girl\nIt was a wonderful day and everyone was happily waiting to see the king. He had come home from war. His people were lined along the street to honor and welcome him for he had won the battle.\n\nSuddenly, a little girl broke through the crowd and ran towards the marching soldiers. An angry guard grabbed her arm before she could go near the king and said, “Look here, child, do you not know that he is our king?”\n\n“I am sorry, Sir,” the little girl said politely. “He may be the king to you, but he is my father.”\n\nWhat happened to the girl?\na. The guard put her in prison.\nb. The guard let her go to the king.\nc. The guard kept her away from the king.\n\nHow did the king feel?\na. Glad\nb. Sorry\nc. Hungry\n\nHow did the story end?\na. The girl went home alone.\nb. The king went home alone.\nc. The king went home to the girl.','teacher'),
(1236,1236,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThree Friends\nAna, Cynthia and Angelina were talking as they were walking; each girl was holding something. Ana was holding a bunch of flowers; Cynthia, a box of chocolates; and Angelina, some oranges.\n\n“Do you think Editha will ever get well?” Ana asked.\n\n“I hope she will get well,” Cynthia replied.\n\n“She is really very ill. Her doctor is doing all he can to make her well,” Angelina said.\n\n“Well, let us better hurry up,” Ana said. “I guess it is going to rain. We cannot stay long with Editha.”\n\nWhere do you think the girls are going?\na. to visit a sick friend\nb. to a birthday party\nc. to visit a new friend\n\nFor whom were the things they were holding?\na. for their sick friend\nb. for their new friend\nc. for their friend who is having a birthday\n\nHow will their friend feel?\na. sad\nb. happy\nc. angry','teacher'),
(1237,1237,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nTired Boy Scouts\nT-r-r-rum! T-r-r-rum! T-r-r-rum! went the beating of the drum. Boys in Boy Scout uniform were marching on the street. There were many of them. They came from different schools. They belonged to different troops, too.\n\nEvery boy had a knapsack on his back. The smaller boy seemed very tired. All of the boys were wet with perspiration. They could hardly carry their feet any farther. “Keep on marching, boys,” the scout Master said. “We will reach the camp soon.”\n\nWhy were the boys tired?\na. They had been sitting\nb. They had just begun.\nc. They had been walking for a long time.\n\nWhat would the boys do in a camp?\na. sell newspaper\nb. make kites and boats\nc. sing songs and play games\n\nWhat do you think did the boys do as soon as they reached the camp?\na. rested\nb. played hard\nc. walked around','teacher'),
(1238,1238,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Program\nThere were many people waiting for the program to begin. They started to arrive as early as two thirty. It was already ten minutes past three but the program had not started. It was supposed to start at three o’clock. At first, the sun shone brightly. Soon it was covered by dark clouds. The day became dark. “I better hurry home. The clothes on the line might get wet,” Aling Rosa said. “I have to go home, too. I did not close our windows,” Mrs. Rosal said as she walked away. Soon most of the people started to go home.\n\nWhat is going to happen?\na. It was going to rain\nb. It was going to be a fine day\nc. It was going to be a sunny day\n\nWhat was likely to happen next?\na. The program will go on\nb. The program will not be held that day\nc. Many people will stay for the program\n\nWhat else would likely happen?\na. The program will be cancelled\nb. The program will go on rain or shine\nc. The program will be held the next day','teacher'),
(1239,1239,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nBerto Jokes No More\nHave you ever played a joke on someone? Berto was fond of playing jokes on others. But he does not play jokes anymore. This story will tell you why. One morning, in their science class, Berto sat behind Carlos, his best friend. The teacher asked Carlos to answer a question. When Carlos stood up to answer, Berto pulled Carlos’s chair backward. Suddenly there was a loud thud! The class saw Carlos down on the floor. He tried to stand up but he could not.\n\nWhy did Carlos fall on the floor?\na. He stumbled\nb. He had no chair to sit on\nc. He stepped on a banana peeling\n\nWhy do you think was Carlos unable to stand?\na. He was lazy to stand up\nb. He have hurt a bone badly\nc. He wanted someone to pull him up\n\nWhat happen next?\na. Carlos just laughed\nb. Carlos and Berto went out to play\nc. Carlos was taken to the school doctor','teacher'),
(1240,1240,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe “green Revolution”\nMiss Lozano was telling her pupils the meaning of “Green Revolution.” “Let us plant vegetables in every open space or vacant lot near our homes”, Miss Lozano said. “If we have no vacant space we can plant in cans, pots and wooden boxes. We can plant tomatoes, pechay, and mustard.” “I can plant in the empty milk cans of my baby sister. We have many at home,” Elena said. “We have many empty cans, too,” Ruela said. On her way home, Helena was thinking of the space where she plays with her sister.\n\nWhat will Helena do?\na. Tell her sister not to plant.\nb. Play all day with her sister\nc. Make a garden out of the space\n\nWhat will happen if all will help in the “Green Revolution”?\na. We will have more food\nb. We will have more houses\nc. We will have more schools\n\nWhat will happen next?\na. People will be poorer\nb. People will be healthier\nc. People will fight one another','teacher'),
(1241,1241,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Dog’s Bite\n“How many of you have dogs at home?” Miss Angeles asked. Several hands were raised. About one-fourth of the class did not. “You should have your dogs injected against rabies. Rabies is a disease coming from dogs. The bite of a dog which is not injected against rabies will kill a man if he is not treated by a doctor,” Miss Angeles said. “Our little dog gave me a playful bite before I came to school,” Avelina said. But it is only a scratch.”\n\nWhat should Miss Angeles tell Avelina?\na. Go to sleep\nb. See a doctor\nc. Take a bath\n\nWhat should Avelina do?\na. Obey Miss Angeles\nb. Just go home and rest\nc. Forget what Miss Angeles said\n\nWhat will children likely do?\na. Sell their dogs\nb. Eat their dogs\nc. Have their dogs injected against rabies','teacher'),
(1242,1242,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nA Bus Ride\nA tired woman carrying a big bundle got in the bus and hurriedly look for a seat. Finding no vacant seat, she stood on the aisle, one hand holding tightly on one of the seats and the other clutching her bundle. Six-year-old Mario and his mother were on the same bus. Before mother could ask Mario to ask the woman, he has already offered his seat to her. “You looked so tired and seem to be in a hurry.” Mario said politely. “I can stand for the rest of the way while you take my seat.” How glad the woman was! All she could say was,” Thank you very much.”\n\nHow do you think did Mario feel after offering his seat?\na. Sorry\nb. Happy\nc. Very tired\n\nHow would you think did Mother feel about Mario?\na. Proud of him\nb. Angry with him\nc. Ashamed of him\n\nHow will you end the story?\na. Mario got back his seat\nb. The woman reached her home happily\nc. Mario and his mother did not talk to the woman','teacher'),
(1243,1243,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nSets\nA Grade Two class was studying about sets. They learned that a set is a group of things. They knew that an empty set has nothing in it. They also learned that equivalent sets have equal members in them. They were taught how to add and subtract sets.\n\nThe children liked their lessons on sets very much. In three months’, time they learned many things about sets and set operations. Get your pencil and paper, do what these sentences tell.\n\nWrite the title of the story.\n\nDraw a set of three apples.\n\nDraw a set of 2 tables and 1 chair.\n\nDraw two equivalent sets, each having 4 members.\n\nWrite the sum of the equivalent sets you made.','teacher'),
(1244,1244,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nOpposites\nOne day, Miss Carlos said, “Let us play a game. Let us have two rows A and B, facing each other.\n\n“What game is this?”. A child in one row will say a word and a child in the opposite row will give the opposite word,” said Miss Carlos.\n\nThey started the game.\n\n“Day,” said the boy in A row.\n\n“Night,” answered a girl in a row B.\n\n“Correct,” said Miss Carlos. “Night is the opposite of day.”\n\nThe children had much fun playing the game. They learned many words. Play the game with your pencil and paper.\n\nWrite the opposite of the boy.\n\nWrite the opposite of clean.\n\nWrite the opposite of small.\n\nWhat is the opposite of good? Write and encircle it.\n\nWhat is the opposite of new?','teacher'),
(1245,1245,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nOur National Symbols\nOur country is the Philippines. We love our country. Every Filipino must know his country very well. “Do you know the national symbols?” Miss Luna asked the children in Grade Three. “Yes, Miss Luna,” said Fe, “We learned them in Grade II.” “Can you name some?” said Miss Luna. “Our national flower is sampaguita,” said Lucy. “Our national fruit is mango,” said Vic. “Our national tree is Narra,” “We have many national symbols. You must learn them all. We named only a few.\n\nWrite the name of our country.\n\nWrite the name of our national flower.\n\nWhat is the national tree? Write it.\n\nWhat is our national fruit?\n\nAre there other national symbols? Write Yes or No.','teacher'),
(1246,1246,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nHow Nestor Earns Money\nNestor goes to Mrs. Magno’s house every Saturday afternoon. He helps Mrs. Magno clean her garden and water her plants. He also carries water for her. Mrs. Magno pays Nestor for his services.\n\nNestor earns money by working for other people. He goes from one house to another. although he has very few clothes, he is always neat and clean. Many people like him to work for them.\n\nOne day Mrs. Magno placed all her son’s old clothes in a box. “I know of a boy who would like these clothes. They are still good to wear, “she thought.\n\nWhat will Mrs. Magno do with the box of clothes?\na. Sell it\nb. Give it away\nc. Throw it away\n\nWho does Mrs. Magno have in mind?\na. Nestor\nb. her son\nc. her nephew\n\nWhat do you think will the boy do?\na. Sell the box\nb. Throw the box away\nc. Thank Mrs. Magno and get the box.','teacher'),
(1247,1247,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nFiesta Time\nAll the streets were decorated with little flags of different colors. There was a band going around. Many people lined up the street. They were waiting for something. “My sister’s dress is yellow. Her crown is very lovely. “Marina said “If you will see my sister’s dress you will be surprised. It is pure white. Her crown is as white as her dress,” Yolanda said. “Well, let us find out who has the prettier dress.” Minda said. “Let us wait and see.” “They are coming! They are almost here now! “Shouted the people with delight.\n\nWho do you think were coming?\na. The governor and his wife.\nb. The president and his wife.\nc. The fiesta queen with her princess.\n\nWhat was the town celebrating?\na. Independence Day\nb. The town fiesta\nc. Bataan Day\n\nWhy were the people lined up on the street?\na. They were watching a funeral.\nb. They were watching a parade.\nc. They were watching a procession.','teacher'),
(1248,1248,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nFirst Elevator Ride\nIt was Marcelo’s first trip to Manila. It was his first time to see a train, an electric light, a telephone, a television set and big buildings. It was his first time to ride in a car, in an elevator and on an escalator.\n\nMarcelo rode in an elevator on his third day in the city. His mother took him to his elder brother’s office. His brother’s office was in the seventh floor so they took an elevator. Inside the elevator, Marcelo cried and said, “Let me out! Let me out this room!”\n\nWhat do you think did the people in the elevator do?\na. They laughed at him\nb. They sent Marcelo out.\nc. They told him not to be afraid.\n\nWhy did Marcelo want to go out?\na. He was afraid.\nb. He wanted to play.\nc. He could not breathe inside.\n\nHow did Marcelo’s mother feel?\na. Proud of Marcelo\nb. Ashamed of Marcelo\nc. Happy about Marcelo','teacher'),
(1249,1249,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nShare a Toy\nIt was the first week of December. “Christmas is coming soon,” said Miss Villa. “Would you like to make a poor child happy during Christmas?” “Yes, Miss Villa,” answered the children. “Some of you have many toys. You can give away one of them. We will give your toys to the very poor children who cannot buy a toy this Christmas. Will you share a toy with a poor child?” asked Miss Villa.\n\n“I will tell my mother about it,” said Samuel. “Of course. Tell your mother about our ‘Share a Toy’ campaign. Do not bring anything if your mother does not know about it,” said Miss Villa.\n\nWrite down the name of the month.\n\nWrite the word that tells what was coming.\n\nWho was the teacher? Write the name.\n\nWhom must they tell about the campaign? Write it.\n\nCopy the words that tell with whom they will share a toy.\na. A rich child\nb. A good friend\nc. A poor child','teacher'),
(1250,1250,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Toys\nA big SHARE A TOY poster was posted on the wall. Miss Villa told the children to read it. “Are you ready with your toys?” Miss Villa asked. “Some of us are ready,” said Samuel. “Let us put our toys in the big box. The box is on the corner,” said Miss Villa.\n\nSome children took out their toys. Samuel stood up and put his toy truck in the box. Mila came next and put a doll. Other children also put their toys in the box. Soon the box was half full of toys. Miss Villa counted the toys. She was very happy. The children were very happy, too. It was the first day of the campaign and they had fifteen toys.\n\nWrite what they read on the poster.\n\nWrite what Samuel gave.\n\nWrite what Mila gave.\n\nCopy the box below. Put a circle around the word that tells where they put the toys.\nBasket\nBox\nTable\n\nWrite the number that tells how many toys they had.','teacher'),
(1251,1251,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nGrowing Roses\nAling Doray grows roses in her garden. She takes good care of her roses. Every morning and afternoon we see Aling Doray in her rose garden.\n\nThe rose plants are very healthy. They have big, beautiful flowers. They are of different colors. There are red, white, pink and yellow roses.\n\nEarly in the morning, Aling Doray cut some flowers. She takes them to the flower shop. She sells the roses to the shop owner. Aling Doray is happy. She earns much money selling her roses.\n\nWho grows roses? Write the name.\n\nWrite the word that tells about the rose plants.\n\nList the different colors of the flowers.\n\nWrite two words that tell about the flowers.\n\nWhen does Aling Doray cut flowers?','teacher'),
(1252,1252,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nThe Fishermen\nOne Saturday morning, Father and Lito went to the river. They went to catch fish. They brought their fishing lines. The fishing lines were made of bamboo. They had long strings. The used small worms as baits.\n\nLito sat on a big rock on the river bank. Father stayed a few meters away. After a while, Lito pulled his line hard. A big fish was at the end of the line. “Father, I caught a big fish,” Lito said.\n\nSoon Father pulled his line, too. “See what I caught,” Father said. It was a very small fish. How Father and Lito laughed.\n\nWrite the day when Father and Lito went fishing.\n\nWrite the word that tells where they went.\n\nWrite the word that tells where Lito was.\n\nWho caught a big fish?\n\nWho caught a small fish? Write the name.','teacher'),
(1253,1253,'Read the story carefully, then answer the questions.\n\nDays of the Week\nOne day Ben and Ric had a contest. “Let us see who can write the names of the days of the week correctly,” said Ben. “Let us write them in order,” said Ric.\n\nBen wrote only five days. Ric wrote all the days correctly. He wrote these on his paper:\n\nSunday\nMonday\nTuesday\nWednesday\nThursday\nFriday\nSaturday\n\nCopy the names of the days of the week.\n\nNumber them from 1 to 7.\n\nPut a cross after the days that you do not come to school.\n\nDraw a line under the first school day of the week.\n\nDraw a circle around the last school day of the week.','teacher'),
(1254,1254,'Directions: Arrange the jumbled letters inside the parenthesis to complete the sentence. Write the answer on the space provided.\n\nNewsletters should (r n m o i f) ______________ parents of special school activities such as field trips, meetings and volunteer opportunities.\n\nNewsletters are also a great way to (m e n d r i) ______________ families when there are special activities in school.\n\nWrite about specific happenings around the school, this will (c c n e t n o) ______________ the parents and the school.\n\nNewsletters serve as (e a n l c n h) ______________ of communication between home and school.\n\nCampus newsletters provide an opportunity for pupil-writer to (r e s x p s e) ______________ themselves.','teacher'),
(1255,1255,'Directions: Write True if the statement is correct, False if it is incorrect.\n\nHaving a school newspaper empowers students to showcase their writing skills and voice their opinions.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nPupil-run newspapers are great way to build communication between all groups in the school.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nThe purpose of newsletter is to provide information to parents only.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nBrainstorming and producing a school newspaper can provide pupils with valuable experience in collaboration and teamwork.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nCampus newsletter helps pupils build confidence.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nNews found in the campus newsletter are just copied from well-known newspapers in the country.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nCampus newsletter may not publish national and local news.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nPupils can express their thoughts and ideas in school newsletter.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nCampus newsletter strengthens the relationship of the school and community.\nTrue\nFalse\n\nPupils are trained to manage school paper guided by school newsletter advisers.\nTrue\nFalse','teacher'),
(1256,1256,'Directions: Read the sentences carefully. If the statement implies importance to pupils/students, draw a star (★). If it implies importance to the school/community, draw a circle (●).\n\nCampus newsletter develops qualities of cooperation, tact, accuracy, tolerance, responsibility and leadership.\n★ Star – pupils/students\n● Circle – school/community\n\nPublish school and community news.\n★ Star – pupils/students\n● Circle – school/community\n\nProvides an opportunity for interesting writing.\n★ Star – pupils/students\n● Circle – school/community\n\nEncourages and stimulates worthwhile activities.\n★ Star – pupils/students\n● Circle – school/community\n\nMake known the achievements of the school.\n★ Star – pupils/students\n● Circle – school/community','teacher'),
(1257,1257,'Directions: Based on the diagram below, choose 3 words and explain how it is related to the topic: Importance of campus newsletter in the school and community.\n\nImportance of Campus Newsletter\n\nFirst word you chose: write the word and explain how it is related to the importance of the campus newsletter.\n\nSecond word you chose: write the word and explain how it is related to the importance of the campus newsletter.\n\nThird word you chose: write the word and explain how it is related to the importance of the campus newsletter.','teacher'),
(1258,1258,'Directions: As a journalist, make a simple news informing the community about important activity/activities in your school.\n\nWrite your simple news here.','teacher'),
(1259,1259,'Directions: Find and encircle each word related to news values in the puzzle. Words may be written horizontally, vertically or diagonally. They also may be written in reverse order, so you may have to read them from right to left.','teacher'),
(1260,1260,'Directions: Write FACT if the statement is correct and BLUFF if it is not.\n\nJournalists are looking out for news stories that attract a significant audience or readership.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nConflict is something out of the ordinary has more news value than an everyday event.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nAn event is more newsworthy the sooner it is reported.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nRecent events have higher news value than earlier happenings.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nNews values are general guidelines or criteria which determine how much prominence a media outlet gives a news story.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nProminence means events are more newsworthy when it focuses on war, conflict or struggle.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nEach news value describes a different quality that can be found in a newsworthy story.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nCurrency includes events which are already popular or trending.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nMore value is attributed to stories pertaining to issues or topics that are in the spotlight of public concern.\nFACT\nBLUFF\n\nConflict and disagreement add excitement to a story.\nFACT\nBLUFF','teacher'),
(1261,1261,'Directions: In the blank beside each of these topics for stories in the campus newspaper, write its main news value: conflict, timeliness, proximity, prominence, impact, bizarreness and currency.\n\nNews Values\n\nThe Russian navy has conducted major war games near Alaska involving dozens of ships and aircraft, the military says, the biggest such drills in the area since Soviet times.\n\nQueen Elizabeth II on Sunday thanked healthcare workers on the frontline of the fight against the coronavirus outbreak, promising that a united effort would help defeat the disease.\n\nThe Philippine economy plunged by much more than expected in the second quarter, falling into recession for the first time in 29 years, as economic activity was hammered by one of the world’s longest and strictest coronavirus lockdowns.\n\nMIAMI—A Florida candidate for Congress said Monday she stands by her claims she was abducted to an alien spaceship at age 7 but that the incident shouldn’t detract from what she’s done here on Earth since then.\n\nMANILA, Philippines — Mobile wallet application GCash has decided to delay the placement of fees for bank transfers, moving it to a month after the initial announcement.\n\nThe Department of Health in Region 2 (DOH-2) confirmed Monday afternoon a new case of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) in the Cagayan Valley region. According to DOH Officer-in-Charge Director Leticia Cabrera, the newest case was a 40-year-old woman from Cauayan City, Isabela. Identified as PH4805, the patient had no travel history to a COVID-19- infected area in the country, but had an encounter with a coronavirus- positive patient, PH3987.\n\nMANILA, Philippines — Malacañang shortened work hours in government offices under the executive branch on Monday to allow employees to celebrate the Kainang Pamilya Mahalaga Day. Work hours will be suspended by 3:30 p.m., according to a memorandum circular issued by Executive Secretary Salvador Medialdea.','teacher'),
(1262,1262,'Directions: Cite three (3) news stories from a newspaper or the internet. Copy the lead paragraph of each story in the spaces provided and identify their news values.\n\nA. Lead paragraph of the first news story — then write its News Value.\n\nB. Lead paragraph of the second news story — then write its News Value.\n\nC. Lead paragraph of the third news story — then write its News Value.','teacher'),
(1263,1263,'Directions: Determine the SCOPE or ORIGIN of the news in the articles presented. Write the letter of the correct answer.\n\nScope or Origin of the News\n\nMANILA, Philippines — Sen. Francis Tolentino on Friday proposed that the government look into partnering with ice cream manufacturers in order to address the Philippines\' current lack of freezers needed to stock up on the much-awaited coronavirus vaccine.\nA. Local news\nB. National news\nC. Foreign news\nD. Dateline news\n\nJapan\'s Maritime Self-Defense Force conducted anti-submarine drills in the South China Sea on Oct. 9, deploying three vessels including a helicopter aircraft carrier and a submarine, according to the Japanese defense ministry.\nA. Local news\nB. National news\nC. Foreign news\nD. Dateline news\n\nA former labor official on Saturday called for stronger monitoring and protection mechanisms for overseas Filipino workers following the death of another Filipina allegedly in the hands of her employers in Kuwait.\nA. Local news\nB. National news\nC. Foreign news\nD. Dateline news\n\nA 2-week enhanced community quarantine (ECQ) has been placed in Ilagan City, Isabela amid a rise in COVID-19 cases, its mayor said Monday. Ilagan City Mayor Jose Marie Diaz told Teleradyo the stricter lockdown was imposed starting Friday, Oct. 16 until Oct. 30 to curb the spread of the virus. He attributed the increase of COVID-19 infections to local transmission and arrival of locally-stranded individuals (LSIs).\nA. Local news\nB. National news\nC. Foreign news\nD. Dateline news\n\nThe Department of Science and Technology (DOST) Region 2 has already inked an agreement with the Local Government Units of Cagayan and Isabela for the production of ready-to-eat food packs that can be distributed to families affected by calamities and emergencies. Regional Director Sancho Mabborang said the project is part of the food innovation program of the department which is aimed at developing nutritious food packs, either meals or snacks, for emergencies.\nA. Local news\nB. National news\nC. Foreign news\nD. Dateline news','teacher'),
(1264,1264,'Directions: Identify the structure of news stories indicated in each example below. Write the answer on the blank.\n\nExample\nThey say a child’s smile is the sweetest thing in the world. It could be a source of warmth and joy not only to the child but those blessed with its presence.\n\nFor children with cleft deformities, however, this proves to be different as they are sometimes teased and bullied for their smiles.\n\nBut through courage and kindness, these children are being given hope.\n\nOver a hundred children with cleft lips and palates in Tacloban City were given the gift of a beautiful smile this November.\n\nFrom Nov. 11 to 16, children and young adults with cleft lips, cleft palates and other facial deformities were given free and safe surgery by a team of medical volunteers from the civic group Operation Smile.\n\nThe mission was held at the Eastern Visayas Regional Medical Center in Tacloban City. (feature)\n\nThe Department of Health (DOH) Region 2 has opened a 51-bed capacity Community Isolation Unit (CIU) facility at the Regional Government Center, this city. Dr. Rio Magpantay, regional director, said the building was intended for the Regional Blood Center, however, since it is not yet operational, the rooms were converted into isolation units for the meantime. The two-storey facility is equipped with isolation rooms provided with sanitary and food supplies, with comfort rooms and medical staff who will man the CIU on a 24/7 basis.\n\nRobots will destroy 85 million jobs at mid-sized to large businesses over the next five years as the COVID-19 pandemic accelerates changes in the workplace likely to exaggerate inequalities, a World Economic Forum (WEF) study has found.\n\nThe situation is harrowing enough. But it becomes profoundly horrifying when news about millions of Filipinos enduring daily hunger pangs comes on the heels of a staggering revelation in a recent Senate hearing: that the Department of Social Welfare and Development still has in its office some P10 billion that was supposed to aid four million poor Filipinos under the social amelioration program (SAP), but that has remained undistributed.\n\nThe Department of Education has allowed its division offices and schools to hire learning support aides (LSAs) that would help teachers and parents facilitate the schooling of students at home. Alliance of Concerned Teachers (ACT) Secretary General Raymond Basilio, however, said LSAs do not provide learning opportunities for disadvantaged learners since they have no teaching function and would simply assist teachers in class management and clerical work.','teacher'),
(1265,1265,'Directions: Identify the type of content (e.g., crime, health, science, sports, or public safety) shown in each article. Write your answer in the space provided.\n\nThree persons in Cagayan Valley’s provincial and municipal lists of most wanted persons were arrested in separate police operations in provinces of Cagayan and Isabela. Personnel of Abulug Police, Cagayan Provincial Police Office arrested Ronald Anglioan, 30, and the No.1 most wanted in the provincial level, in Barangay Pugis, La Trinidad, Benguet, last May 15. He was wanted for two counts of rape.\n\nA trio of new studies published in JAMA Internal Medicine on Tuesday examining the efficacy of an arthritis drug against COVID-19 showed inconsistent results. Tocilizumab has been used by doctors since the start of the pandemic with the hope of tamping down an abnormal immune response known as a \"cytokine storm\" that causes severe organ damage in some hospitalized COVID-19 patients.\n\nVirgin coconut oil can help destroy the novel coronavirus, according to Filipino researchers, following months of experiments on the product as a potential antiviral agent. \"The results are very promising, as not only does it show that the VCO, by itself, can destroy the virus, but it also has a key mechanism in upregulating the immune response against COVID-19,” Dr. Jaime C. Montoya, executive director of the Philippine Council for Health Research and Development (PCHRD), said in a statement. The Philippine General Hospital (PGH) is conducting a separate clinical trial for virgin coconut oil among severe patients battling with the virus.\n\nUnbeaten Teofimo Lopez upset Vasiliy Lomachenko by a unanimous decision on Saturday to become the undisputed world lightweight champion in Las Vegas. Lopez defended his 135-pound IBF lightweight title and seized the WBC, WBO and WBA belts from Ukraine’s Lomachenko who suffered his first loss in six years.\n\nMANILA, Philippines — With more people expected to head outdoors during the Christmas season amid the easing of quarantine restrictions, the Philippine National Police (PNP) yesterday said it would double the deployment of personnel in public places to thwart criminals. The PNP is wary of a possible spike in crimes and terrorism in Metro Manila during the holidays. “We will deploy more police personnel. That’s automatic, but we have to rationalize the deployment of our forces. When we say this, we mean that we need to secure places where police are needed,” PNP chief Gen. Camilo Cascolan told reporters.','teacher'),
(1266,1266,'Directions: Determine the treatment of news story depicted in each item. Write the correct answer on the space provided.\n\n“To be given the same Lifetime Contributor award that was given to titans such as former president Fidel Ramos and the late SM chairman Henry Sy Sr. is a distinction I will always be grateful for.”, said Tycoon Ramon Ang, president and COO of San Miguel Corp., after he received the prestigious Lifetime Contributor Award at the 2020 Asia CEO Awards, touted to be the largest business awards in Asia. Ang was recognized for “his visionary leadership, significant and long-term contributions to the country, and unprecedented response to the COVID-19 pandemic.” “His success in business is not only found in the Philippines but also globally. His company is instrumental in the various crucial infrastructure projects that (will) help our country not just recover but also leads us to soar even higher in terms of creating jobs, stimulating economic activities,” the board of judges said.\n\nThe Miami Heat refused to let their amazing postseason run end in Game 5 of the 2020 NBA Finals. Behind another superb outing from Jimmy Butler, the Heat overcame the Los Angeles Lakers, 111-108, to stay alive and extend the series to a Game 6. Butler had his second triple-double of the Finals, with 35 points, 12 rebounds, 11 assists and five steals, as the Heat came out on top after a thrilling back-and-forth encounter. Duncan Robinson added 26 points, making seven of his 13 three-pointers in the game. But Butler was the clear hero of the game for Miami, as he drew a foul against Anthony Davis with 16.8 seconds left and drained the go-ahead free throws for a 109-108 count. Before Butler\'s charities, Davis put the Lakers ahead with a layup of an offensive rebound, doing so despite having injured his ankle in the first quarter of the game.\n\n\"Ladies and gentlemen, I hereby declare Marawi City liberated from the terrorist influence that marks the beginning of rehabilitation,\" President Rodrigo Duterte said in a speech before troops 148th day into the crisis. Duterte’s announcement came after Marawi siege leaders Isnilon Hapilon and Omar Maute were killed in a military operation on Monday dawn. His announcement drew cheers from troops present during his speech. The president declared martial law in the whole of Mindanao to neutralize the local terror group and contain violence in the region. He also suspended the privilege of the writ of habeas corpus in the region. The Armed Forces of the Philippines reported that as of October 16, 824 terrorists were killed, 827 firearms were recovered and 1,771 hostages were rescued. On the part of the government, 162 soldiers were killed and 1,000 others were wounded in action.\n\nThe United Nations Educational, Scientific, and Cultural Organization (UNESCO) this month launched a photo exhibition which featured World Heritage marine sites including Philippines’ Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park. UNESCO’s photo exhibition titled “Protecting UNESCO’s Marine World Heritage through scientific research” was launched at the UNESCO headquarters in Paris, France last October 7. The photo exhibit particularly showcased photographs taken by the Monaco Explorations during a scientific exploration in UNESCO World Heritage marine sites. Aside from the Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park, Malpelo Fauna and Flora Sanctuary in Colombia, Rock Islands Southern Lagoon in Palau, and Lagoons of New Caledonia were also included in the list. Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park is a UNESCO World Heritage Site located in the center of the Sulu Sea and covers the Tubbataha and Jessie Beazley Reefs. It covers 96.828 hectares including the North and South Reefs. The natural park is home to a great diversity of marine life, including whales, dolphins, sharks, turtles, and Napoleon wrasse. The reef ecosystems support over 360 species of corals and almost 700 species of fish. The reserve likewise protects one of the few remaining colonies of breeding seabirds in the region.\n\nThe Department of Education (DepEd) has appealed to teachers to make some learning module exercises and activities optional to prevent students from experiencing “burnout” due to the overwhelming subject requirements they must accomplish after just one week of remote classes. Diosdado San Antonio, DepEd undersecretary, reminded them to gauge what students should accomplish in a day and that the agency had a policy of not requiring daily homework for every subject. “These should be discussed by school teams because they are the ones who agree how many tasks are reasonable [for students]. We do not want the students’ excitement in learning converted into burnout,” San Antonio said at a press briefing. According to Jesus Mateo, DepEd undersecretary for planning, LSAs will be employed to assist teachers in lesson preparation and delivery, guide learners and households on their chosen learning delivery modalities, and monitor students’ progress and achievements.\n\n1. What part of the activity do you find difficult? Why?','teacher'),
(1267,1267,'Directions: Study the letters in column A and connect the missing letters in column B to complete the News Values in each number.\n\nColumn A\n1. Curr\n2. Time\n3. Prom\n4. Con\n5. Biza\n6. Imp\n7. Prox\na. imity\nb. act\nc. rreness\nd. ency\ne. liness\nf. inence\ng. flict','teacher'),
(1268,1268,'Directions: Identify the news values through the sentences below. Fill in the boxes with each number.\n\nMore value is attributed to stories pertaining to issues or topics that are in the spotlight of public concern rather than to issues or topics about which people care less. Stories come and stories go.\n\nStrife is newsworthy. The old newspaper adage “if it bleeds it leads” rules. For example the conflict of the policemen and the political protest in Metro Manila during the EDSA revolution.\n\nThe weirdness of an event can make it newsworthy. Dog bites a child” usually is not a news, but a child bites dog is more bizarre.\n\nThe weirdness of an event can make it newsworthy. Dog bites a child” usually is not a news, but a child bites dog is more bizarre.\n\nThe weirdness of an event can make it newsworthy. Dog bites a child” usually is not a news, but a child bites dog is more bizarre.\n\nNews gets out of date quickly. Recent events have higher than earlier happenings.\n\nThe number of people whose lives will be influenced in some way by the subject of the story.','teacher'),
(1269,1269,'Read the quotations below, in one to two sentences write your own opinion or understanding on the space provided.\n\n“A child educated only in school is an uneducated child.”\n\n“There is no elevator to success. You have to take the stairs.”\n\n“Your future is created by what you do today not tomorrow.”\n\n“Mistakes are proof that you are trying.”\n\n“The greatest amount of wasted time is the time not getting started.”\n\n“Education is the passport to the future, for tomorrow belongs to those who prepare for it today.”','teacher'),
(1270,1270,'Write two paragraph opinion articles about the topic, “Will you go to school this summer?\n\n“Will you go to school this summer?\n\nEducation in time of a pandemic: Academic Freeze pushed or rejected','teacher'),
(1271,1271,'Directions: Fill in the graphic organizer with a news report using the given facts.\n\nBackground Information for Learners · News Report\nA news report is a report on current or recent events to inform readers of what is happening in the world or in the local area. It contains facts and detailed information to general questions like who, what, when, where, and how.\n\nParts of the News Report\n1. Headline tells what the news is about.\n2. Lead has the most important facts and is placed at the beginning of the news report.\n3. Body paragraph has the less significant details but the readers may want to know.\n\nNews Reports\nIn writing the lead, at least four of the Ws and one H questions are answered.\n\nNews reports are found in newspapers and their purpose is to inform readers of what is happening in the world around them. They are found in the news section.\n\nNews reports follow the inverted pyramid structure. Let us study the diagram below.\n\nThe Inverted Pyramid\nThe primary lead is the most important information. It answers at least four of the WH- Questions.\n\nThe secondary lead is supporting detail that supports the primary lead.\n\nBackground details support the secondary lead.\n\nThe least important information links the background details. Sometimes it consists a quote from a person involved s in the news.\n\nThe Given Facts\n● The residents living near the shores were advised to evacuate to designated evacuation facilities by the Local Government Unit as the strong typhoon is approaching.\n● Strong typhoon washed out plenty of houses and other properties near the shores.\n● Strong typhoon affects several families in the barangay.\n● Many extended help to the affected residents by donating food, clothing, and other necessities.\n\nFill in the graphic organizer: write the Primary Lead, Secondary Lead, Background Details and Least Important Information of the news report.','teacher'),
(1272,1272,'Directions: Arrange the given facts as they are written in a news report. Write numbers 1- 4 on the line, 1 as the first detail and 4 as the last.\n\nWrite 1, 2, 3, 4 on the line: 1 as the first detail and 4 as the last.\nBig waves hit a fisherman\'s boat while fishing during stormy weather.\nBig waves capsized the boat.\nNo fatality reported but the fishermen’s boat was left distracted.\nDue to financial difficulty and necessities, the fishermen sometimes were forced to go fishing even in bad weather which put their lives in danger.','teacher'),
(1273,1273,'Directions: Write a news report using the given facts below. Write your answers on your answer sheets.\n\nThe Given Facts\nFACT #1 Barangay Mayumi receives Php 10 million worth of food packs from Neutralizer Foundation to be given to the poorest of the poor families at the height of the dengue fever epidemic.\n\nFACT #2 Each family recipient will receive three sacks of rice, Php 10,000 worth of grocery items, and Php 5,000 worth of medicine. Lactating mothers will also receive supplementary milk for their babies and Php 5000 worth of vitamins.\n\nFACT #3 Family recipients will be identified through surprise home visits and interviews to be conducted by the Neutralizer Foundation.\n\nWrite your news report using the facts.','teacher'),
(1274,1274,'Activity 6 · Feature Writing Exercises\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Feature Writing\nFeature writing is an art that combines factual reporting with creative storytelling to produce an engaging and informative piece. By following these guidelines, writers can craft stories that resonate with their audience, providing depth and insight into a wide range of topics.\n\nKey Components\n1. Headline: Captivating and summarizing the essence of the story.\n2. Lead (Lede): An opening paragraph that hooks the reader, often with an anecdote, striking fact, or question.\n3. Nut Graf: A paragraph that outlines the central theme or purpose of the story, providing a roadmap for what’s ahead.\n4. Body:\n○ Background Information: Essential details that set the scene or provide context.\n○ Development: The story’s main content, including arguments, descriptions, and narratives.\n○ Supporting Elements: Quotes, statistics, anecdotes, and examples that enrich the narrative.\n5. Conclusion: Wraps up the story, often tying back to the lead or looking forward to the future.\n6. Call to Action: (Optional) Directs readers toward further engagement or exploration of the topic.\n\nDescribe vividly your seatmate in a two-sentence paragraph.\n\nWrite a short (at most five paragraphs) feature article about abortion. It doesn’t matter if you are for or against it. What is important is you remain factual yet entertaining, convincing and informative. Provide your own title. Show your work to your group mates.\n\nYou are the feature editor of your school paper. This morning while you were on your way to school, you saw a Grade 4 pupil helping an old woman cross the street. Fourth year boys, who were playing nearby saw the old woman but did not care to help her.\n\nYou interviewed the young boy. Now write a feature story on the boy or on the incident. Supply the facts you gathered from the interview. Remember, you are not writing an editorial. Note also that like an editorial, a feature article is hung on a natural or artificial news peg. Give an interesting and entertaining title.','teacher'),
(1275,1275,'Activity 7 · Copyreading and Headline Writing\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Copyreading\nCopyreading is editing or correcting errors made by the reporter and proofreading the errors made by the typesetter or printer.\n\n• It is the art of arranging, correcting, and selecting the quality and type of news\n• It is also called copyediting.\n• One who edits copies is called a copyreader or copyeditor\n\nWhat to copyread?\n1. Errors in fact\n2. Errors in grammar\n3. Errors in structure\n4. Errors in style\n5. Libelous and derogatory statements\n6. Seditious and rebellious matter\n7. Opinion, editorializing statements in news\n8. Verbal deadwoods, redundancy\n9. Errors in unity, coherence and emphasis\n\nProcedure in Copyreading\n1. Read the whole article first to determine what kind of story it is.\n2. Having determined the type of story, read the article again. This time determine what the lead paragraph is.\n3. Take note of errors in fact. Check on the accuracy of the statements and information. Verify when in doubt.\n4. Read the story again and mark all paragraph beginnings. Then, correct errors in grammar, spelling, punctuations, and style using copyreading symbols.\n5. Improve the organization of the story. Make sure the lead features the lead fact.\n6. Go over the whole story and again. Remove all unnecessary and irrelevant materials.\n7. Check the length of the story and then prepare the clean copy.\n8. Write a very catchy and fitting headline for the story.\n9. Mark the copy for the printer (size, type, column, width, etc.)\n10. Encircle the slug line (short title, name of reporter),\n11. End page with a paragraph. Do not cut paragraph and continue on the next page.\n12. Write “more” at the bottom of the page of unfinished story and continue the next sheet. Do not use the back page of the paper.\n13. On the second page, instead of numbering as page 2, label it “first add” or “add one” followed by the slug. For example, “first add nabbed” or “add one nabbed”.\n14. End the story with number 30 or sharp (#). Encircle it.\n15. If the copy could hardly be read because of so many corrections, retype it if time permits to facilitate editing and typesetting.\n\nHeadline Writing\nHeadline is the title of the news story. It is the display window of the newspaper. It also serves as a source of information for busy newspaper readers.\n\nSteps in Headline Writing\n1. Read the whole story to understand its message.\n2. Underline the key words from the lead.\n3. Using these key words, write a short telegraphic sentence summary of the news (omit articles, unnecessary auxiliary verbs, prepositions, details).\n4. Divide according to units of thought into the number of lines required.\n5. Substitute simple, effective synonyms to reduce width within the limits of space allotted.\nExample: Charter polls / must go on, say experts\n\nRules in Writing Headline\n1. The headline must have a verb and it must be in the present tense. Wrong: Ang Limbukad copped 5 trophies in DSPC. Correct: Ang Limbukad cops 5 trophies in DSPC\n2. The first letter of the first word and proper nouns are generally capitalized. Example: Iran offers more oil in RP\n3. Do not use period.\n4. Minimize punctuation marks. Wrong: Rolling stores to sell rice and groceries. Correct: Rolling stores to sell rice, groceries\n5. Don’t use the articles a, an, the. Wrong: A fire hits the squatters’ area in Tondo. Correct: Fire hits squatters’ area in Tondo\n6. Don’t use names unless the person is well-known. Wrong: Santos electrocuted. Correct: Carpenter electrocuted\n7. Use the infinitives for future events. Wrong: City Hall will push anti-squatting drive. Correct: City Hall to push anti-squatting drive\n8. Use specific terms instead of generalities. Wrong: School official gets scholarship. Correct: Principal gets US scholarship\n9. Assertions in a headline should have a source or attribution. Poor: Principal orders suspension of classes. Strong: Classes suspended today, says principal. Good: Even SC can err-Cuevas. Good: Cuevas: Even SC can err\n10. Use the shorter word whenever possible, e.g., use “cut” for decrease, or “hike” for increase. Example: School hikes tuition fee / Studes urge class boycott\n\nPart A: Copy Reading\nDirections: Read the following news article carefully. Underline and correct any errors in spelling, grammar, punctuation, or fact. Write your corrections on the lines below the paragraph.\n\nArticle 1: Yesterday morning, a fire broke out in barangay San roque due to faulty electric wirings. Many houses was damage and the residence quickly evacuated. Firetruck from nearby city arrived twenty minutes after the call. Thankfully, there were no injury reported.\n\nWrite your corrections on the lines below the paragraph.\n\nArticle 1\nThe local government of Tagumpay launched a tree-planting campaign last Friday. Over 500 volunteers planted trees along the highway to fight climate change.\n\nYour Headline:\n\nArticle 2\nA Grade 4 pupil from Rizal Elementary School won the Regional Spelling Bee. She will represent the region in the national contest.\n\nYour Headline:\n\nArticle 3\nDue to heavy rains, classes in several schools in the province were suspended. Parents were informed early in the morning.\n\nYour Headline:','teacher'),
(1276,1276,'Sports Vocabulary Matching Worksheet 1 · Choose the correct words from the list below and write them under the correct pictures.\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Sports Writing\nSports Writing is a form of journalism that reports on sports topics and events. While the sports department within some newspapers has been mockingly called the toy department, because sports journalists do not concern themselves with the \'serious\' topics covered by the news desk, sports coverage has grown in importance as sport has grown in wealth, power and influence.\n\nHow to Write a Sports Writing:\n1. Have a strong lead. Like any article, you want to start your sports article out with a strong lead, one that encapsulates the available information on \"who, what, where, when, why and how.\" This is basic for how to write articles of any topic. Look at sports articles in almost any paper to see how they introduce the game or event they are covering with their lead paragraph. They get the reader\'s attention with a strong but concise summary of the story to follow.\n\n2. Write clearly and concisely. If you\'ve ever read Sports Illustrated, you know that some sports articles can be what you might call \"literary non-fiction:\" lengthy, poetic, filled with metaphor and digressions into back story. If your particular assignment requires that kind of writing, go for it. But if you read the daily sports section of your city paper, you will also notice that most sports articles reporting on the sporting events of the past day are concisely written. Yes, sports articles include context and metaphor and technical sports terms, but they\'re also to-the-point and generally stick to basic vocabulary. Being specific is one of the more basic tips for article writing.\n\n3. Know the context. Like any news article, how to write a sports article will require you to have a basic working knowledge of the universe on which you\'re reporting. This may mean not only knowing all about the current players, coaches and standings but knowing some history, as well. This may be common knowledge to you, but if not, you may need to do some research.\n\nYou will also need to know about the sport itself including rules, history, league standings, current controversies and other information. You may already know much of this if you\'re a sports enthusiast--but be aware that a journalist may need more in-depth knowledge than a casual fan. Be sure to have a solid grasp on the sport you\'re covering before you start to cover it.\n\nAlso, keep in mind that many sports teams have press departments that will provide journalists with extensive information about their organizations including current players and team history.\n\nWord List\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.\n\nWrite the correct sport word for this picture.','teacher'),
(1277,1277,'Draw and tell to the teacher what is your favorite sport or which sport don´t you like and describe them.\n\nDraw and tell to the teacher what is your favorite sport or which sport don´t you like and describe them.','teacher'),
(1278,1278,'Directions: Find and encircle each word related to news values in the puzzle. Words may be written horizontally, vertically or diagonally. They also may be written in reverse order, so you may have to read them from right to left.','teacher'),
(1279,1279,'Arrange the story in the correct sequence. Label each part: Headline, Byline, Lead, Body, Conclusion.\n\nResidents of Barangay Maligaya gathered early Sunday morning for a clean-up drive after heavy rains caused flooding and scattered debris across several streets.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBy Jericho Dela Cruz, Campus Journalist\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nThe barangay plans to hold monthly clean-ups and educational talks on prop er waste disposal in local schools.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nVolunteers, including students and parents, helped clear garbage from sidewalks and drains. Barangay officials said this is part of a larger campaign to prevent future floods. “We need to keep our surroundings clean to protect our health and homes,” said Barangay Captain Liza Ramos.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBarangay Holds Clean-Up Drive After Flood\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion','teacher'),
(1280,1280,'DIRECTIONS : Write a sports coverage using the following facts.\n\nThe Facts\nWhat: Municipal Meet volleyball eliminations\nWhere: Arayata Sports Complex, Tanza, Cavite\nWhen : September 9 at 2 p.m.\nBetween : Saint John School (SJS) and Saint Mark Academy (SMA)\nScores: 25-19, 23-25, 25-20, 25-20\n\nSignificance of the game:\nSJS on 2-0 lead; routed Saint Joseph Institute (SJI) in previous outing\n\nHeroes of the game:\nJerome Arbis and Noli Cruz combined for 17 points in last set\n\nQuotes from SJS coach Rody Santos:\n“My boys are pumped up from their victory over the Saint Joseph Institute Tossers.”\n“This is a good start as we are gunning for the championship like our basketball varsity posting a 6-0 winning streak.”\n\nWeather condition:\nHeavy rains and thunderstorms brought by Typhoon Maring but the Arayata gym was filled to the rafter.\n\nSpectators’ attitude:\nSJS crowd favorite, predominately SJS crowd chanting cheers; SJS red and white clad mascot dancing in front of SJS contingent\n\nReported injury:\nSMA’s Jin Rivas sprained ankle after a bad fall in the second set; carried out of playing court and brought to Family General Hospital\n\nHighlights of the game:\nSMA came on strong in second set with Joseph Rom’s mounting strong wallops\nRom’s heroics aided by Dan Sibal’s stopping ball on a service by SJS Richard Montes, also in second set.\n\nWrite your sports coverage using the facts.','teacher'),
(1281,1281,'Based on the information presented below, write a sports game story of at least 350 words. Statements as presented on this fact sheet may be treated as direct quotations or paraphrases. You are writing for the Sept. 17 issue of the Hays High Guidon.\n\nBackground\nThe Varsity Girls’ Volleyball team began season- Sept. 15- v. Salina South. Hays lost 15-7, 13- 15, 11-15.\nThe next game: Sept. 19, at Junction City.\n\nThe Team\nThe team includes six returners,\nThree returners made all-district last year\nTwo returners advanced to the all-region roster, Amie Roberts and Leah Claycamp, both juniors.\nRhonda Ames, a senior, is predicted to be one of the district’s star players.\n\nSignificant Plays\nFirst game\nAmie Roberts scored the first point of the game, an off-speed spike, after only 10 seconds of play. She scored five points in the first game.\nKenda Von Feldt scored six points. Leah Claycamp scored four points\nThe Salina South team scored seven points.\nSecond game\nRoberts scored six points and blocked 10 spikes.\nClaycamp scored five points. Her most significant play was a dive that set up a point. She also made two fouls.\nVon Feldt scored two points-one after she made a dig to another player who returned it to her for the score.\nHays hit the ball out of bounds four times. The officials called three thrown ball penalties on Hays.\nSalina South won this game.\nThird game\nRhonda Ames scored the first five points of this game. She was injured while making a double block and did not play the rest of the game.\nClaycamp scored four points, one after a spin serve that Salina could not even return.\nVon Feldt scored two points.\nThe score was tied almost the entire way through the game. The winning four points all came within one minute of play when the Hays team looked exhausted.\n\nQuotations\nLarry Schmidt, varsity coach\nThe first game of the season didn’t go as well as I hoped. We didn’t accomplish the team goals that we set. We didn’t serve and pass as well as I hoped to.\nWe need to work on our movement and getting into the right position when it comes to playing defense.\nJessica Bird, sophomore\nI think we were playing very well. We need to work on our fundamentals. Overall, I think that we are playing great and the season should be successful.\nRhonda Ames, senior\nI think if we work hard and use our potential, we’ll do all right in state. Salina just beat us by playing good clean volleyball.\nLori Billinger, senior\nI need to work on my passing, but otherwise, everything is going great for me so far this year. I think that out team should talk more to each other when we are on the courts.\n\nWrite your sports game story (at least 350 words).','teacher'),
(1282,1282,'Direction: Match the items on Column B to the items in Column A to answer the mystery question. Write your answers on the space provided for.\n\nColumn A\n1. Be precise, choose every word carefully.\n2. A journalist must safeguard his sources.\n3. We speak more slowly on air than in real life, but we need to describe, to tell stories, to create pictures.\n4. Stick to the facts.\n5. The anchor and the editor need to know how you are doing so as to properly work on the broadcast.\n6. Use simple language so the audience can get the focus.\n7. Everybody is owed privacy and respect.\n8. Try to show all sides of the argument.\nA. On-air Delivery – the right tone\nR. Respecting Others\nD. Accuracy\nS. Balance\nW. Clarity\nH. No Commenting\nL. Protect Your Sources\nP. Keep in Touch with Newsroom\n\nMystery Question:\nWho is the American newspaper publisher, known for introducing sensationalism, who cabled this to Frederic Remington in 1898?\n\nWrite the answer to the mystery question.','teacher'),
(1283,1283,'Given the editorial cartoon below. Discuss the different characters and how does it symbolize.\n\nDiscuss the different characters and how does it symbolize.','teacher'),
(1284,1284,'Write a news article · Choose an interesting school club and write an article describing its activities, members, goals and achievements.\n\nBackground Information for Learners\nNews is an account of an event, a fact or an opinion that interests people. It is a presentation of current events.\n\nThe Inverted Pyramid Style\nLead: The “key” to the story. One to no more than two sentences. Includes the most important of the five Ws and H.\nSecondary information: Vital statistics and description. Extend on five Ws and H.\nBackground information: Incidents leading up to or following the event.\nAdditional information\n\nInverted Pyramid Format\nWhen you write a story in inverted pyramid format, you put the most newsworthy information at the beginning and the least newsworthy information at the end. The inverted pyramid helps readers scan news stories quickly and efficiently, and it also helps you write them with ease. Once you train yourself to organize facts this way, you can apply the formula to almost any breaking news event—this is why the inverted pyramid has been a cornerstone of news writing for the past century.\n\nWho\n\nWhat\n\nWhen\n\nWhere\n\nWhy\n\nHow\n\nQuestion: · Interviewee: · Response:\n\nQuestion: · Interviewee: · Response:\n\nQuestion: · Interviewee: · Response:\n\nWrite your newspaper article with a headline.','teacher'),
(1285,1285,'Directions: Read the following examples of journalistic writing, then identify whether each one is News, Opinion, Feature or Sports news.\n\nEating chocolates not only satisfies your taste buds, it also improves your brain function. The flavonoids found in chocolate have been proven to increase blood flow to the brain. They can help boost memory and attention span, and it also inhance problem-solving skills.\nNews\nOpinion\nFeature\nSports news\n\nA retired police colonel died after being trapped inside one of the structures that collapsed in his family’s resort, as the town of Cataingan in Masbate bore the brunt of a 6.6 magnitude earthquake that suck the province on Tuesday morning. By: Nino Luces\nNews\nOpinion\nFeature\nSports news\n\nManny Pacquiao scored a unanimous decision over the no. 1 pound-for-pound fighter, Floyd Mayweather Jr., in an epic boxing rematch on Sunday in Philippine Arena.\nNews\nOpinion\nFeature\nSports news\n\nIndeed, frontliners must be regarded as modern-day heroes. From battling the dreaded COVID-19 in the hospitals to ensuring safety in public spaces, our brave frontliners deserve commendation and respect from the people.\nNews\nOpinion\nFeature\nSports news\n\nThe Magician, Efren “Bata” Reyes, has once again proven his magic on the pool table. Reyes captured the gold medal in the men’s 9-ball singles event at the 2020 Manila Billiard Tournament.\nNews\nOpinion\nFeature\nSports news','teacher'),
(1286,1286,'Direction: Match the types of journalistic writing in Column A with their corresponding contents in Column B.\n\nColumn A\n1. News\n2. Feature\n3. Sports News\n4. Opinion\nA. sporting topics or competition\nB. editorial\nC. current or recent events\nD. in-depth about a certain topic','teacher'),
(1287,1287,'Directions: Find any of the following: News, Features, Opinion and Sports News. Copy the first paragraph in your notebook, then identify its type.\n\nCopy the first paragraph, then identify its type (News, Feature, Opinion or Sports News).','teacher'),
(1288,1288,'I. Identifying the 5 Ws · Instruction/s: Read the scrambled information, identify the WHO, WHAT, WHY, WHEN, WHERE. Write your answers on the space provided. A sample is given for your guidance.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year.\nwill resume in the voter’s registration at the City Hall\nCommission of Election on Monday\n\nExercise No. 1 · WHO: · WHAT: · WHEN: · WHY: · WHERE:\n\nExercise No. 2\nyesterday.\nThree people riding on a motorcycle\nalong Maharlika Highway, Barangay San Nicolas\nafter their vehicle collided head-on with a passenger bus were killed\n\nExercise No. 2 · WHO: · WHAT: · WHEN: · WHY: · WHERE:','teacher'),
(1289,1289,'Instruction/s: Write a news story of 5 to 7 paragraphs using the structure below.\n\nThe Structure\nTHE LEAD. Who did what; or What happened? Perhaps When? and Where? might be in this paragraph.\n2ND PARAGRAPH In many cases this second paragraph will answer the questions Why? or How?\n3RD PARAGRAPH This is where a quote would often appear. This is viewed by most reporters as essential to keep the story lively.\n4TH PARAGRAPH Why are you telling me this/what are the implications of this? (significance)\n5TH PARAGRAPH What will other people think about this? (Moving the story on, giving it a human context)\n6TH PARAGRAPH How did the event continue? (Important background)\n\nThe Lead\n\n2nd Paragraph\n\n3rd Paragraph\n\n4th Paragraph\n\n5th Paragraph\n\n6th Paragraph','teacher'),
(1290,1290,'Direction: Do the following tasks:\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Feature Writing\nFeature writing is an art that combines factual reporting with creative storytelling to produce an engaging and informative piece. By following these guidelines, writers can craft stories that resonate with their audience, providing depth and insight into a wide range of topics.\n\nKey Components\n1. Headline: Captivate readers and summarizes the essence of the story.\n2. Lead: An opening paragraph that hooks the reader, often with an anecdote, striking fact, or question.\n3. Nut Graf: A paragraph that outlines the central theme or purpose of the story, providing a roadmap for what follows.\n4. Body:\n› Background Information: Provides Essential details that set the scene or give context.\n› Development: Present the story’s main content, including arguments, descriptions, and narratives.\n› Supporting Elements: Quotes, statistics, anecdotes, and examples that enrich the narrative.\n5. Conclusion: Wraps up the story, often tying back to the lead or looking forward to the future.\n6. Call to Action: (Optional) Directs readers toward further engagement or exploration of the topic.\n\nEnumerate the elements of a feature article. Describe each element based on your understanding.\n\nAfter enumerating the elements, write a feature article on your English notebook. Choose one that interests you the most from the given topics below.\na. A day at a beach\nb. A Mother’s Day Celebration\nc. A Surprise for Father\nd. Life While staying at Home e. Rizal Our National Hero\n\nRecall an unforgettable experience travelling out of town or abroad. Where was it, and why did you consider the experience as memorable? Write a feature article that observes the five elements f feature writing. The final output should be written on one whole intermediate paper.','teacher'),
(1291,1291,'This activity will serve your opportunity to expressing your unique perspective on the editorial. Read the editorial article carefully to understand the author\'s viewpoint, then provide the expected details as instructed. Grab your pens and let your thoughts flow!\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Editorial Cartooning\nCartooning is an illustration that depicts humorous events, whether serious or not. The word cartooning comes from two words related terms: Caricature and Lampoon.\n\n› Caricature is an exaggerated illustration of someone or something, often enlarging certain body parts to create humorous effect.\n› Lampoon is a form of writing that publicly criticizes someone or something.\n\nEditorial Cartoon\nAn editorial cartoon (also called Political Cartoon) is an opinionated drawing about political or social issues. It may be created to attack, criticize, entertain or praise. Editorial cartoons are usually paired with editorials but may also appear in features, literary sections, or other parts of a publication wgen no appropriate photograph is available.\n\nDOs\n• Do deal with a single topic and be original.\n• Do have humor and be entertaining if possible.\n• Do use symbols which are generally known by people in order to avoid confusion.\n• Drawings that represents something.\nEx. A crocodile represents a greedy person or corrupt politician.\n• Do have less texts like captions, labels, and dialogs.\n• Do have a balance between the artwork created and the opinion of the cartoonist.\n\nDONTs\n• Don’t over-exaggerate so as not to make it look corny.\n• Don’t or avoid hurting someone.\n• Don’t attack the person himself instead of his actions.\n• Don’t use several illustrations for it may confuse the reader. Around 3 – 5 illustrations will do.\n\nSteps in Doing Editorial Cartooning\n1. Issue – Know the issue you are concerned with and know the facts related to it.\n2. Stand – Questions like “Which side do you agree or disagree with?” and “What elements affect the issue?”\n3. Sketching – The creation of the guide or draft of your cartoon.\n4. Outlining/Sketching – The detailed part of the cartoon is created by using a pen to trace the shape of the sketch.\n5. Shading – An optional step where in you give depth to your drawing making it more realistic.\n\nEditorial: Stop Bullying, Schools Must Be a Safe Place\nSchools are places where we learn and build meaningful relationships with fellow students we meet along the way. At their core, they must be safe spaces place for learners.\n\nHowever, there lurks a disease in schools that needs to be cured -- bullying.\n\nThroughout the years, we have heard countless stories of students being bullied. Some were severely bullied and it led them to do unimaginable things for someone their age. Some committed suicide while others harmed fellow students.\n\nDavao City Councilor Enzo Villafuerte said schools have failed to address bullying cases within their premises.\n\n\"Nag-interview ko og mga parents kung naa ba silay experiences of bullying diri sa schools nato sa Davao, and daghan kog na-estoryahan nga naa gyud cases of bullying and wala\'y ginabuhat ang mga teachers nato and principals nato para i-address ang bullying (I have talked to some parents and asked them if they have experiences of bullying here in the schools in the city, and I found out that there really existing cases but neither teachers nor principals have acted on it),\" Villafuerte said during his interview on Davao City Disaster Radio on January 5, 2022.\n\nBullying takes a huge toll on the mental health of young people. Victims tend to show signs of depression, anxiety, stress, and detachment. They would harm themselves and be in a very dark place.\n\nWhat is worse is the mental and emotional stress on these young people is being seen as just a phase. Parents would seldom seek professional health for their children who are victims of bullying. Others would just ask the victim to \"pray the depression away.\"\n\nFor some, what some of these kids post on social media is just seen as trivial posts. We have to start taking their posts on depression, anxiety, and sadness more seriously. These posts should be seen as red flags and interventions must be made.\n\nThe responsibility of protecting students from being bullied or preventing the birth of bullies also lies with the school officials and the parents themselves. At home, parents must teach their kids the right values like being respectful of others. Schools themselves must have safeguards in place to protect students from bullies and reprimand the bullies.\n\nMaybe it is time that we intensify topics on good manners and right conduct in schools.\n\nLikewise, schools must truly and intentionally capacitate all teaching and non-teaching personnel with the knowledge on identifying students who are being bullied and those who are having mental or emotional issues.\n\nSchools must be safe places. That\'s why it falls upon the responsibility of the schools and the parents that where their children go is also psychologically, mentally, and emotionally safe for other kids.\n\nGetting Started: Dive into the article and check out how the author introduces the topic. What caught your attention in the beginning?\n\nWhat\'s Inside: Take a stroll through the main points in the article. What are the key things the author is talking about? Identify any details that stood out to you?\n\nThe Wrap-up: Look at how the article wraps things up. How did the author close the conversation? Is it a strong finish or something that leaves you thinking?\n\nAuthor\'s Take: What\'s the author\'s stand on the topic? Do they seem like they\'re sharing their personal take, giving us the facts, or maybe a bit of both?\n\nImpact on you: Let\'s get real for a moment. How did the article hit you? Do you think it might change how you see things or act in the future?\n\nHEART-TO-HEART: Now, spill the beans. What\'s your take on all of this? Do you agree with the author, or do you see things differently now? How did the article play with your thoughts?','teacher'),
(1292,1292,'Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:\n\nWaste Segregation\n\nThe Rise of Generative AI and Its Impact on Everyday Work\n\nThe Science Behind Earthquakes and Disaster Preparedness\n\nGamification in Learning and Workplace Productivity\n\nIndigenous Knowledge and Modern Scientific Collaboration','teacher'),
(1293,1293,'Directions: Write article on each of the following topics:\n\nWhy Society is Becoming Increasingly Dependent on Technology?\n\nClassroom Technologies That Actually Make Learning Harder\n\nHow Online Misinformation Divides Communities?\n\nWhat I Learned After Moving Away From Home\n\nThe Fear and Beauty of Starting Over\n\nHow a Simple Routine Changed My Life?\n\nLessons From a Year Of Uncertainty\n\nRediscovering Childhood Passions as an Adult\n\nMy Journey Toward Better Mental Health\n\nThe Joy of Ordinary Days','teacher'),
(1294,1294,'Topic: Is social media helping or harming students today? · Instructions: Write 3–4 sentences giving your opinion about whether the school cafeteria should offer more healthy snacks.\n\nRemember\n• State your opinion clearly.\n• Give one reason to support your opinion.\n• End with a short, convincing sentence.\n\nExample:\nI think social media is harming students today. Many students spend too much time on apps and games, which can make them tired and distracted from their studies. For example, some students scroll through social media instead of doing homework. Students should use social media less so it does not affect their learning.\n\nWrite 3–4 sentences giving your opinion.','teacher'),
(1295,1295,'Direction: Write a well-developed editorial (2 paragraphs).\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Editorial Writing\n› Editorials usually appear on the editorial or opinion page to differentiate them from news stories.\n› They comment on issues reported in the news articles. By weighing and presenting arguments, an editorial reaches a conclusion and makes a recommendation regarding an issue.\n› The editorial represents the official stand of a newspaper. Because of this, it is written in a way that conveys the opinion of the entire publication\n› “I” and “my” are not used because they indicate personal opinion of a single writer.\n› The editorial always includes a title.\n\nParts of Editorial Writing · 1. Introduction\nIt is the opening paragraph. It may contain the news upon which the editorial is based, in such in case, the introduction briefly states the background and topic.\n\nLast week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Thrash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food were piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.\n\n2. Body\nThis section contains the facts about the news or issues including the writer’s opinion or viewpoints. It builds up the case through a logical arrangement of ideas supported by facts • Reaction – sets the reader up to receive the opinion The PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria cause health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.\n\n• Details – support your reaction and lead to a conclusion The problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.\n\nAfter the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.\n\nThe canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.\n\n3. Conclusion\nThis is the final of the editorial. It either summarizes or drives home the point. It ties up the ideas and arguments presented in the body. It is sometimes referred to as the clincher.\n\nWe support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.\n\nParagraph 1:\n• Present your opinion clearly.\n• Explain one positive and one negative effect of social media.\n\nParagraph 2:\n• State which effect you think is stronger and why.\n• Provide evidence or examples (realistic, simple ones).\n• End with a strong call to action (e.g., encouraging balanced or responsible use)\n\nExample;\nSocial media is a powerful tool that can both help and harm students today. On the positive side, it allows students to connect with classmates, share ideas, and access educational content online. For example, students can join study groups or watch tutorials on difficult topics. However, social media can also distract students, cause stress, and reduce the time they spend on homework or outdoor activities.\n\nI believe the negative effects are stronger than the positive ones if social media is not used carefully. Students should be taught to use social media responsibly and set daily time limits. Parents and teachers can also encourage activities like reading, sports, or hobbies to balance screen time. By using social media wisely, students can enjoy its benefits without letting it harm their health or learning.\n\nParagraph 1\n\nParagraph 2','teacher'),
(1296,1296,'Direction: Copyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.\n\nBackground Information for Learners: Copyreading\nCopyreading is editing or correcting errors made by the reporter and proofreading the errors made by the typesetter or printer.\n\n• It is the art of arranging, correcting, and selecting the quality and type of news\n• It is also called copyediting.\n• One who edits copies is called a copyreader or copyeditor\n\nWhat to copyread?\n1. Errors in fact\n2. Errors in grammar\n3. Errors in structure\n4. Errors in style\n5. Libelous and derogatory statements\n6. Seditious and rebellious matter\n7. Opinion, editorializing statements in news\n8. Verbal deadwoods, redundancy\n9. Errors in unity, coherence and emphasis\n\nProcedure in Copyreading\n1. Read the whole article first to determine what kind of story it is.\n2. Having determined the type of story, read the article again. This time determine what the lead paragraph is.\n3. Take note of errors in fact. Check on the accuracy of the statements and information. Verify when in doubt.\n4. Read the story again and mark all paragraph beginnings. Then, correct errors in grammar, spelling, punctuations, and style using copyreading symbols.\n5. Improve the organization of the story. Make sure the lead features the lead fact.\n6. Go over the whole story and again. Remove all unnecessary and irrelevant materials.\n7. Check the length of the story and then prepare the clean copy.\n8. Write a very catchy and fitting headline for the story.\n9. Mark the copy for the printer (size, type, column, width, etc.)\n10. Encircle the slug line (short title, name of reporter),\n11. End pagewith a paragraph. Do no tcut paragraph and continue on the next page.\n12. Write “more” at the bottom of the page of unfinished story and continue the next sheet. Do not use the back page of the paper.\n13. On the second page, instead of numbering as page 2, label it “first add” or “add one” followed by the slug. For example, “first add nabbed” or “add one nabbed”.\n14. End the story with number 30 or sharp (#). Encircle it.\n15. If the copy could hardly be read because of so many corrections, retype it if time permits to facilitate editing and typesetting.\n\nHeadline Writing\nHeadline is the title of the news story. It is the display window of the newspaper. It also serves as a source of information for busy newspaper readers.\n\nSteps in Headline Writing\n1. Read the whole story to understand its message.\n2. Underline the key words from the lead.\n3. Using these key words, write a short telegraphic sentence summary of the news (omit articles, unnecessary auxiliary verbs, prepositions, details).\n4. Divide according to units of thought into the number of lines required.\n5. Substitute simple, effective synonyms to reduce width within the limits of space allotted.\nExample: Charter polls / must go on, say experts\n\nRules in Writing Headline\n1. The headline must have a verb and it must be in the present tense. Wrong: Ang Limbukad copped 5 trophies in DSPC. Correct: Ang Limbukad cops 5 trophies in DSPC\n2. The first letter of the first word and proper nouns are generally capitalized. Example: Iran offers more oil in RP\n3. Do not use period.\n4. Minimize punctuation marks. Wrong: Rolling stores to sell rice and groceries. Correct: Rolling stores to sell rice, groceries\n5. Don’t use the articles a, an, the. Wrong: A fire hits the squatters’ area in Tondo. Correct: Fire hits squatters’ area in Tondo\n6. Don’t use names unless the person is well-known. Wrong: Santos electrocuted. Correct: Carpenter electrocuted\n7. Use the infinitives for future events. Wrong: City Hall will push anti-squatting drive. Correct: City Hall to push anti-squatting drive\n8. Use specific terms instead of generalities. Wrong: School official gets scholarship. Correct: Principal gets US scholarship\n9. Assertions in a headline should have a source or attribution. Poor: Principal orders suspension of classes. Strong: Classes suspended today, says principal. Good: Even SC can err-Cuevas. Good: Cuevas: Even SC can err\n10. Use the shorter word whenever possible, e.g., use “cut” for decrease, or “hike” for increase. Example: School hikes tuition fee / Studes urge class boycott\n\nThe Article to Copyread\ndoctor. Ma. Girlie Pinongan, provincial health officer, said there were 2,211 influenza-like cases reported from Jan. 1 to Dec. 2 this year in the 31 towns and cities of negros occidental compared to 616 cases in 2022.\nhealth officials urged the public to take precautions during the holiday as cases as cases of influenza-like illness in Negros Occidental increased by 258.93 percent this year.\nhe highest number of cases were recorded in Isabela town with 1,680 followed by San Carlos City with 129; Murcia, 80; Bago City, 57; Talisay City, 50; Hinigaran, 20; Cadiz City, 19; and Don Salvador Benedicto, Silay City and Victorias City with 16 each.\nStill, she said, the public should avoid close contact with persons manifesting influenza-like symptoms\nPinongan said the cases are not severe and are usually triggered by changes in conditions weather/\nShe also urged people to wear mask in closed and crowded spaces as a precaution.\n\nWrite the copyread article, the slug and the printers’ direction.\n\nHeadline 1 (barline)\n\nHeadline 2 (droplines)','teacher'),
(1297,1297,'Developing Context and Main Body Content · Theme: Technology\n\nI. Background of the Task\nYou are a student journalist assigned to write a technology news article. Your task is to develop the context and main body content of the story based on the information below:\n\nNews Scenario:\nThree senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\n\nII. Review: What Is Context in News Writing?\nContext provides background information that helps the reader understand:\n• Why the news matters\n• What problem it addresses\n• The current situation related to the issue\n\nIII. Review: What Is the Main Body of a News Article?\nThe main body expands the story by adding:\n• Important details\n• Quotes (real or simulated)\n• Effects or implications\n• Supporting data or explanations\n\nA. Write the Context Paragraph · Explain the background of the dengue issue and why a detection app is important. (3–4 sentences)\n\nB. Write the Main Body Paragraph · Add more details about the app, how it works, who created it, and its possible impact. You may include invented quotes to enrich the story. (4–6 sentences)\n\nV. Reflection Question · How can technology created by students contribute to solving real community problems?','teacher'),
(1298,1298,'A Day in the Life of ….\n\nInstructions:\n1. Choose someone to feature: a classmate, teacher, family member, neighbor, or even a school staff member (e.g., librarian, guard, janitor).\n2. Interview the person using at least 5 questions (e.g., daily routine, hobbies, challenges, fun facts).\n3. Write a 3-4 paragraph feature article titled “A Day in the Life of ______.”\n4. Focus on capturing interesting details, personality, and real experiences.\n5. Add one photo or hand-drawn illustration (optional).\n\nExample: Title: A Day in the Life of Mr. Santos, the School Guard\nMr. Santos steps through the school gate before the sun has fully risen, his uniform crisp and his flashlight in hand. He smiles warmly as he greets the early students trickling in, locking the gate behind him. “I like being the first one here,” he says. “It gives me time to think and prepare for the day.”\n\nThroughout the morning, he patrols the corridors, making sure classrooms are secure and reminding students to put their things away. When someone forgets a book or backpack, he helps them carry it to their classroom. At recess, he watches students play, sometimes pausing to chat he asks about their day, what subjects they like, and even what games they play at home.\n\nIn the afternoon, Mr. Santos helps guide students out of the school grounds safely. He stands by the gate, waving goodbye, and makes sure no one lags behind. After the last student leaves, he walks around to check every door and window. He says, “My job isn’t just to guard the school — I guard their memories too. I want them to leave knowing they\'re safe.” As he closes up, his face shows a quiet pride: even in the small moments, he knows he’s made a difference.\n\nA Day in the Life of ______ (3-4 paragraphs)\n\nInstructions:\n1. Explore the school campus and identify a “hidden gem”—a place or feature students may overlook (e.g., a quiet reading corner, an art display, a garden spot).\n2. Describe what makes this place special or unique.\n3. Interview at least 1 person who knows something about the location (e.g., librarian, teacher, classmate).\n4. Write a short feature article (2–3 paragraphs) that includes descriptive details, sensory imagery, and interesting facts.\n5. Give the article a catchy title (e.g., “The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab”).\n\nExample: Title: The Secret Garden Behind the Science Lab\nParagraph 1 – Description: Tucked behind the science lab, away from the bustling hallways, lies a small garden that most students pass by without noticing. Sunlight filters through the branches of a tall jacaranda tree, casting dappled shadows on the neatly arranged flowerbeds. The air is fragrant with the scent of blooming marigolds and jasmine, and the gentle sound of rustling leaves creates a peaceful atmosphere. A worn wooden bench sits under the tree, inviting anyone to pause and enjoy the quiet.\n\nParagraph 2 – Stories and Facts: Mrs. Alvarez, the school’s biology teacher, explains that the garden was planted by students five years ago as part of a sustainability project. “It started as a simple idea to grow flowers and herbs for the school, but it became a place where students could relax and observe nature,” she says. Over the years, students have added bird feeders, a small pond, and colorful stepping stones. Many report that spending time here helps them focus and feel calm between classes.\n\nParagraph 3 – Reflection / Significance: Though it may seem small and unassuming, this garden is a hidden treasure in the school. It reminds everyone that even in a busy campus, there are spaces to pause, reflect, and connect with nature. For those who take the time to explore it, the garden offers more than beauty—it offers a moment of calm and inspiration in the middle of a hectic school day.\n\nWrite your feature article with a catchy title (2–3 paragraphs).','teacher'),
(1299,1299,'Instructions: Arrange the story in a correct sequence. Label each part: Headline, Byline, Lead, Body, Conclusion.\n\nResidents of Barangay Maligaya gathered early Sunday morning for a clean-up drive after heavy rains caused flooding and scattered debris across several streets.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBy Jericho Dela Cruz, Campus Journalist\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nThe barangay plans to hold monthly clean-ups and educational talks on proper waste disposal in local schools.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nVolunteers, including students and parents, helped clear garbage from sidewalks and drains. Barangay officials said this is part of a larger campaign to prevent future floods. “We need to keep our surroundings clean to protect our health and homes,” said Barangay Captain Liza Ramos.\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion\n\nBarangay Holds Clean-Up Drive After Flood\nHeadline\nByline\nLead\nBody\nConclusion','teacher'),
(1300,1300,'Instructions: Read the scrambled information, identify the WHO, WHAT, WHY, WHEN, WHERE. Write your answers on the space provided.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year. will resume in the voter’s registration\nat the City Hall Commission of Election on Monday\n\nWho\n\nWhat\n\nWhy\n\nWhen\n\nWhere','teacher'),
(1301,1301,'Instructions: Read the scrambled information. Identify first the Ws. Next, WRITE the WHO, WHAT, WHEN, and WHERE in a basic News Lead. The WHY will be written in the next paragraph. Write your answers on the space provided.\n\nExercise No. 1 Scrambled Information\nin preparation for the barangay and Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) elections next year.\nwill resume in the voter’s registration\nat the City Hall on Monday\nCommission of Election\n\nNews Lead: ______ (WHO) + ______ (WHAT) + ______ (WHEN) + ______ (WHERE).\n\nSecond Paragraph (the WHY)','teacher'),
(1302,1302,'Write an editorial using the following data:\n\nA Dirty Cafeteria\nLast week the Parents Teachers Association (PTA) decided to close the school cafeteria because the place remained dirty despite repeated calls for cleaning up. Trash left from lunches, used bottles and plastic cans were found on tables, chairs, floors. Even left-over food were piled to rot in a corner. PTA members had complained about these in the past to no avail.\n\nThe PTA decision was correct and necessary. Not only does the dirty cafeteria causes health problems to students, it gives a bad reflection of the school as well.\n\nThe problem surfaced several months ago when the PTA inspected the school laboratories and facilities. The PTA members found the cafeteria messy and unclean that time.\n\nAfter the inspection, Mr. Rene Ramirez, the PTA president, held a dialog with the cafeteria manager and appealed for the maintenance of canteen cleanliness.\n\nThe canteen became clean for about a month. After that, the place went back to its old dirty state.\n\nWe support the closing of the canteen in the hope that when it opens again, a cleaner, safer cafeteria will serve the school. More than our support however, we believe that maintaining the cafeteria clean and safe is everybody’s duty. It is not the duty of the management alone, not the duty of the PTA alone, but the duty of all of us.\n\nWrite an editorial using the following data:\n• New policies of the administration\n• New grading system\n• K-12 Curriculum\n\nNote\n-describes the event, action, or proposal, then explains the causes and reasons involved. This is followed by an assessment of importance, a look at possible effects, then a statement of support or a call for further study.\n\nWrite your editorial.','teacher'),
(1303,1303,'Write an editorial using the following data:\n\nSolution: Return to the Basics\nLengthening the elementary school up to Grade VII is not the solution to the poor quality of instruction in the public school system.\n\nCongress has deplored the proliferation of half-baked products of the present school system. Sen. Edgardo Angara has proposed to add one curriculum year each to both elementary and high schools.\n\nWe believe that this is not the solution to the deteriorating educational system. The best we should do is return to the basics-the mastery of the three Rs- Reading, Writing and Arithmetic.\n\nOnly competent and committed teachers should be employed. They should be given higher pay and more incentives and benefits.\n\nNo amount of frequent seminars, workshops and conferences will help. Let us do away with unnecessary innovations learned by educators abroad which are not applicable to Philippine setting. There are so many “approaches” but as one President of the Philippine Normal University said, “We never reach.”\n\nAlso, the added years to the elementary and high school would mean more expenses on the part of the parents\n\nWrite an editorial using the following data:\n• Policies which the staff considers unfair to the student\n• Management of a school facility that needs to be improved such as library, canteen\n\nNote\n-begins with a brief explanation of a problem and states the need for change. A discussion of weaknesses, errors, or causes follows. Then a solution, suggestion, or alternative is offered, along with supportive reasons for the proposed action.\n\nWrite your editorial.','teacher'),
(1304,1304,'Topic: RH Bill · Stand: Favor\n\nThe Way of Most Desks\nStudent-judges who inspected every room in connection with “Operation Cleanliness” found out that most of the desks had scratches and/or ink spots. One can hardly write on them without a thick sheet of paper for a pad.\n\nOutside the classrooms, some desks are placed for the students to sit on. Students really take advantage of them. They sit on top of the desks and place their muddy shoes on the seats instead.\n\nWhen the 220 new desks for Pasig Line will be made available for the students’ use, will they end up the way most desks go?\n\nTopic: RH Bill Stand: Favor\n1. Cite first some major arguments against the issue. Cite sources/authorities/church leaders\n2. Then, build your case (points) from strong to strongest\n3. Title,\n4. Intro (thesis)/ main point /stand (1-2 par)\n5. Body (3-5 par.)\n6. Conclusion (1 par)\n\nTitle\n\nIntro (thesis)/ main point /stand (1-2 par)\n\nBody (3-5 par.)\n\nConclusion (1 par)','teacher'),
(1305,1305,'Instructions : Write a sports coverage using the following facts.\n\nThe Facts\nWhat: Municipal Meet volleyball eliminations\nWhere: Arayata Sports Complex, Tanza, Cavite\nWhen : September 9 at 2 p.m.\nBetween : Saint John School (SJS) and Saint Mark Academy (SMA)\nScores: 25-19, 23-25, 25-20, 25-20\n\nSignificance of the game:\nSJS on 2-0 lead; routed Saint Joseph Institute (SJI) in previous outing\n\nHeroes of the game:\nJerome Arbis and Noli Cruz combined for 17 points in last set\n\nQuotes from SJS coach Rody Santos:\n“My boys are pumped up from their victory over the Saint Joseph Institute Tossers.”\n“This is a good start as we are gunning for the championship like our basketball varsity posting a 6-0 winning streak.”\n\nWeather condition:\nHeavy rains and thunderstorms brought by Typhoon Maring but the Arayata gym was filled to the rafter.\n\nSpectators’ attitude:\nSJS crowd favorite, predominately SJS crowd chanting cheers; SJS red and white clad mascot dancing in front of SJS contingent\n\nReported injury:\nSMA’s Jin Rivas sprained ankle after a bad fall in the second set; carried out of playing court and brought to Family General Hospital\n\nHighlights of the game:\nSMA came on strong in second set with Joseph Rom’s mounting strong wallops\nRom’s heroics aided by Dan Sibal’s stopping ball on a service by SJS Richard Montes, also in second set.\n\nWrite your sports coverage using the facts.','teacher'),
(1306,1306,'Instructions: Based on the information presented below, write a sports game story of at least 350 words. Statements as presented on this fact sheet may be treated as direct quotations or paraphrases. You are writing for the Sept. 17 issue of the Hays High Guidon.\n\nBackground\nThe Varsity Girls’ Volleyball team began season-Sept. 15- v. Salina South. Hays lost 15-7, 13- 15, 11-15.\nThe next game: Sept. 19, at Junction City.\n\nThe Team\nThe team includes six returners,\nThree returners made all-district last year\nTwo returners advanced to the all-region roster, Amie Roberts and Leah Claycamp, both juniors.\nRhonda Ames, a senior, is predicted to be one of the district’s star players.\n\nSignificant Plays\nFirst game\nAmie Roberts scored the first point of the game, an off-speed spike, after only 10 seconds of play. She scored five points in the first game.\nKenda Von Feldt scored six points. Leah Claycamp scored four points\nThe Salina South team scored seven points.\nSecond game\nRoberts scored six points and blocked 10 spikes.\nClaycamp scored five points. Her most significant play was a dive that set up a point. She also made two fouls.\nVon Feldt scored two points-one after she made a dig to another player who returned it to her for the score.\nHays hit the ball out of bounds four times. The officials called three thrown ball penalties on Hays.\nSalina South won this game.\nThird game\nRhonda Ames scored the first five points of this game. She was injured while making a double block and did not play the rest of the game.\nClaycamp scored four points, one after a spin serve that Salina could not even return.\nVon Feldt scored two points.\nThe score was tied almost the entire way through the game. The winning four points all came within one minute of play when the Hays team looked exhausted.\n\nQuotations\nLarry Schmidt, varsity coach - “The first game of the season didn’t go as well as I hoped. We didn’t accomplish the team goals that we set. We didn’t serve and pass as well as I hoped to.We need to work on our movement and getting into the right position when it comes to playing defense.”\nJessica Bird, sophomore - “I think we were playing very well. We need to work on our fundamentals. Overall, I think that we are playing great and the season should be successful.”\nRhonda Ames, senior - “I think if we work hard and use our potential, we’ll do all right in state. Salina just beat us by playing good clean volleyball.”\nLori Billinger, senior - “I need to work on my passing, but otherwise, everything is going great for me so far this year. I think that out team should talk more to each other when we are on the courts.”\n\nWrite your sports game story (at least 350 words).','teacher'),
(1307,1307,'Instructions: From the following reporter’s notes, prepare a summary story of at least 350 words for the February 18 issue of the Central High School News.\n\nReporter’s Notes\nFebruary 5: Basketball game between Central High Bears and East High Wildcats in East High gymnasium, won by Central 44-36. John Gunnison of Central was high point man with 15 points. East was always within 5 points of Central until the last few minutes of the game. Game was marred by many fouls on both sides.\n\nGunnison: “They played us tough and stayed with us throughout, but we were able to get by them. They are a scrappy team. . .I don’t remember having this much trouble with them before. Still, we were able to pull it out.”\n\nCoach Turner: “Gunnison did the job tonight. When he is on his game, he can do some really good things. Tonight was his night.”\n\nFebruary 12: Central High v. Midvale Astronauts, at Central. Won by Midvale 54-52. High scoring game, with Central ahead at half time, 32-22, and keeping lead until last 10 minutes. Joe Small was hero of game for Midvale, making many scores in last part of game and encouraging his teammates. Guninson and Jimmy Smith of Central were outstanding, but team just wasn’t good enough, according to Coach Ed Turner.\n\nTurner: “A game like this one breaks your heart. We seemed to be on as far as our scoring was concerned. We scored more points than in any other game this year. Still, we have to play defense. Without defense, we are sitting ducks.”\n\nFebruary 19: Central High v. West High Gophers at West. West hasn’t won a game this year but has put up a good fight every time and almost defeated Midvale. Gunnison twisted his leg in practice early this week but should be able to play.\n\nFebruary 26: Central High v. River City Scorpions at Central. River City hasn’t been defeated yet but meets Midvale Feb. 19 in a game that may decide league championship. river City has excellent defensive team and a high-scoring center, Jack Sorrenson.\n\nTurner: “These two games are both going to be a challenge. People think that West is going to be an easy win, but they play tough despite their record. River City has been great all year. They just seem to be in a groove.\n\nMaybe we can trip them up, but we’ll see. A lot of it depends on how strong Gunnison is this week. He is taking it one day at a time, so hopefully things will pan out.”\n\nWrite your summary story (at least 350 words).','teacher'),
(1308,1308,'Instructions: 1.Copyread the article using the copyreading symbols. 2.Write a headline. 3. Indicate the slugline and printer spec.\n\nThe Article\n“Another isue which Japan & the Philippines agre ed on today is Japan’s (welcoming) house hold workers to Japanise economic zones, Yasuhisa Kawamura, jjapan’s press sec. and Abe’s spokersperson told raporters.\n\nIn a billateral meeting on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific Economy Cooperation (Apec) summit in manila on hursday, japanese Prime minister Shinzo Abe and President Aquino agreed on the highring of filipino domesticated workers in the very specialty economic seen zones in Japan’s kanagawa and osaka Prefectures.\n\nThe awesome agreement, however, was still vague on tha number of worker from the Philippines Japan will need.\n\nIt deppends on how many people is wanted on the Japanese’s side. Based on the needs, we will had the apropriate worker of number, Kawamura said.\n\n“With PM Abe’s approval, the system to refuse Finoy workers is almost [a done deal],” said Kawamura said.\n\nThe spokespersons said Japan to be wanted to begin hiring generously by the end of the year at the earliest and soonest. wo “Abe requested from the side of the Ph that that the neccessary preperations must be made so the agreement could be implemented moothly implemented,” Waka-waka said.\n\n1. Copyread the article using the copyreading symbols.\n\n2. Write a headline.\n\n3. Indicate the slugline and printer spec.','teacher'),
(1309,1309,'Instructions: 1.Copyread the article using the copyreading symbols. 2.Write a headline. 3. Indicate the slugline and printer spec.\n\nThe Article\n“The die is cast. I have already crossed the Rubicon.”\n\nDavao city mayor Rodrigo Duterte make the statesment on Monday as he confirmed reports over the week end that he have changed his mined and would runs for President in next year’s elections.\n\nYes, I am running, he told reporters in San Juan City (metro manila). ““But the formal declaration will be made by the PDP.”\n\nThe tuogh-talking mayor was referreng to Partido Demokratiko Pilipino-Lakas ng Bayan or PDP-Laban, the over thirty-year-old political party that has been pushing him to gun for the presidency.\n\nPDP-Laban traces its roots to davao city and cagayan de oro city and was organized in the early 1980s primarily to gulvanized anti-Marcos forces in the region. Duterte is a member of the party.\n\nSen. Aquilino “Koko” Pimentel 3rd, president of the PDP-Laban, said he spoken with Duterte last Saturday and the mayor told him she is now decinclined to run in 2016.\n\n“This is the ac cumulation of his [Duterte] frustrations or concerns due to complaints given to him like tanim-bala [bullet-planting], citizenship and others. Maybe he’s had enough,” Pimentel added.\n\n1. Copyread the article using the copyreading symbols.\n\n2. Write a headline.\n\n3. Indicate the slugline and printer spec.','teacher'),
(1310,1310,'Instructions: 4.Copyread the article using the copyreading symbols. 5.Write a headline. 6. Indicate the slugline and printer spec.\n\nThe Article\nThe Philippine Information Agency will recognize this year’s best performing school paper advisers in Western Visayas.\n\nFour school paper advisers from Capiz will be cited as among the region’s “best” during the gathering of about 400 student writers for the College Press Conference and Awards on November 26, Included in the awardees are Mrs. Mary Gretchel Montorio of Jagnaya National High School in Jagnaya, Jamindan town, Mr. Joey Balatayo of Capiz State University-Roxas City main campus, Mr. Leonardo Bayadog of “Ang Kasanag” and Ms. Shanika Mae Isagan of “The Script,” both of Capiz National High School.\n\nMontorio and Balatayo will also be recipients of the Hall of Fame award for having consistently won the award for three consecutive years, that is, from 2013 to 2015.\n\nThe “Ang Kasanag” will also be cited as Best High school Newsletter for 2015 and First Place and Second Place\n\n4. Copyread the article using the copyreading symbols.\n\n5. Write a headline.\n\n6. Indicate the slugline and printer spec.','teacher'),
(1311,1311,'Instructions: Compose a feature story using the information from this article.\n\nWidespread Tree Planting as a Solution to Flooding – DA\nAgriculture Secretary William Dar believes that widespread tree planting in watersheds is the solution to prevent massive flooding in many cities and towns across the country.\n\nAccording to Dar, planting trees is a timely and long-term solution against flooding, aside from other government plans.\n\n“We need engineering interventions here. At the same time, we need to reforest in a big way our watersheds. Otherwise, we will have this problem year in, year out,” Dar explained.\n\n“I am hoping that as the engineering interventions would start now, like dredging, we should also start reforesting in a big way all our watersheds,” the secretary added.\n\nBased on data, 18 watersheds in Luzon with healthy tree cover are needed to provide strong protection against floodwaters.\n\nIt is noticeable that when successive typhoons struck the country, particularly Luzon, many areas could no longer escape the torrents of rain and mud coming from the mountains.\n\nAccording to affected residents, there are almost no trees left in the forests because of unchecked illegal logging.\n\nIn Cagayan, for example, many trees that could have absorbed floodwaters during storms have disappeared, causing numerous towns in the province to be submerged when Typhoon Ulysses struck.\n\nOnly a few trees remain in San Mateo and Montalban in Rizal, which is why powerful floods mixed with mud regularly flow down toward Marikina City.\n\nResidents also reported that quarrying is rampant in San Mateo and Montalban, but it seems the mayors of these towns have neglected the issue.\n\nWrite your feature story.','teacher'),
(1312,1312,'Instructions: Compose a feature story using the information from this article\n\nDeforestation in the Philippines: Challenges and Solutions\nThe Philippines, home to two-thirds of the planet’s biodiversity and up to four-fifths of its flora and fauna, continues to face severe deforestation. The nation loses an estimated 47,000 hectares of rainforest annually and has already lost around 60% of its forest cover in the last 80 years. As of 2020, only 24.1% of its 30 million-hectare land area remained forested. With the country experiencing an average of 20 major storms each year due to its location in the typhoon belt, deforestation has worsened soil erosion, landslides, flash floods, food and water insecurity, and the displacement of indigenous tribes.\n\nIn 2019, the Department of Environment and Natural Resources (DENR) classified 55 mammals, 184 birds, 53 reptiles, 30 amphibians, and 784 invertebrates as threatened species. Anna Varona of the Haribon Foundation warned that clearing forests destroys habitats, food sources, and breeding environments, increasing the risk of extinction. Much of the forest loss stemmed from indiscriminate logging between 1935 and 1991, while agricultural expansion, mining, urbanization, and infrastructure projects further contributed. Climate-induced disasters, such as Typhoon Haiyan in 2013, which killed more than 6,000 people and decimated 86% of mangrove forests in eastern Philippines, have also accelerated forest degradation.\n\nThe country’s population of over 110 million has intensified demand for forest resources. According to the Philippine Master Plan for Climate Resilient Forestry Development, pressure continues to rise for wood, fuelwood, and other forest products. The plan, first formulated in 1990 after a government ban on log and lumber exports, was updated in 2003 and again in 2013 to address climate change impacts. In 2011, two major policies were introduced: a moratorium on tree cutting in natural forests with the creation of an Anti-Illegal Logging Task Force, and the National Greening Program (NGP), which aimed to plant 1.5 billion trees on 1.5 million hectares between 2011 and 2016. Extended to 2028, the NGP now covers 7.1 million hectares, with 1.83 billion seedlings planted on 2.18 million hectares as of 2022.\n\nThese interventions have yielded modest gains. Data from the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) show a 0.5% increase in forest cover between 2010 and 2020, averaging 34,900 hectares added annually. However, Varona cautioned that planting exotic trees does not promote Philippine biodiversity. She stressed the importance of prioritizing native species to restore ecosystems and preserve biodiversity. This focus has inspired other initiatives such as BINHI, the greening project of the Energy Development Corporation (EDC), the world’s largest vertically integrated geothermal power producer. Established in 2008, BINHI seeks to rescue, preserve, and propagate native species, with 47% of the country’s indigenous trees already threatened with extinction.\n\nBINHI has conducted tree population surveys and partnered with stakeholders to protect forests. To date, it has assessed 1,470 tree species through Botanic Garden Conservation International’s Global Tree Assessment, identifying 145 priority species. Discoveries include red lauan (Shorea negrosensis), once thought confined to Negros, and Mapilig (Xanthostemon bracteatus), previously found only in Camarines Norte. The program also identified dwindling species such as kaladis narig (Vatica elliptica), where only two trees were found in 2016. Through partnerships, 11 trees were later discovered, bearing fruit during the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\nBINHI also plants trees outside their natural habitats to nurture “mother trees” that will produce viable seeds. Plans for in-situ conservation are underway, planting species back into their native environments. While undoing decades of forest damage will take years, reforestation efforts are making progress. As a result of BINHI’s initiatives, 10 of the 145 priority species have been removed from the list, while 21 were downgraded from “critically endangered.” These efforts highlight the importance of combining science, policy, and community action to protect the Philippines’ forests and biodiversity.\n\nTomorrow\nBy Pamela Joy Go\nHeal the world,\nMake if a better place\nFor you and for me\nAnd the entire human race\nThere are people dying,\nIf you care enough for the living\nMake a better place\nFor you and for me\n\nTomorrow (continued)\nI could only stare at the third-grade pupils as they interpret this Michael Jackson classic for their presentation during the flag ceremony. They seem to be so full of life, so vibrant. Nothing in this world could seem to break the unbeatable character they have glowing brightly inside them.\n\nI sigh as my thoughts fall on the degrading society that we are having now. How long will these naïve tots have the overwhelming spirit in them? How will they manage to smile?\n\nLook around you, human. What do you see? The pressing issues concerning the destruction of Mother Earth is too real…too hard to ignore. The huge forest fires that terrorize countless of species both here and abroad. The perennial garbage problem that embraces the government like a cold pack of harsh ice. The dwindling population of all sorts of mammals and reptiles…the endangered species. The red list is getting longer and longer. Soon, I doubt if we’ll ever be able to keep track of all the creatures that have disappeared from the face of the earth forever.\n\nYes, we want to escape from these enigmas. We really want to. But listen. It’s also we who are keeping ourselves chained to the enslaving powers of material riches. We are blinded by the glitter of gold every time we cut down trees.\n\nHowever, there are better realities in this issue our ancient mentality should just think about. The contemporary ambience of people all over the world getting more humane, more environment-friendly, more real is embracing us. Any maybe, just maybe, we could start from here.\n\nA year ago, I came across this Reader’s Digest article that suggested a different view of this dilemma. While countless others would rather stick to the old, hopeless sense of presenting these inevitable facts, the article examined the optimism that, surprisingly, never left us.We still have a chance. At least, that’s what the article said. People are becoming civilized.\n\nWe are no longer a heartless breed of warm bodies out for nothing but earthly wealth. Through time, we have evolved to be persons with some sense. And Mother Earth is pretty much depending on it. Her whole life, her whole existence is actually relying on this so-called sensitivity that we are starting to acquire.\n\nThese are not made-up facts, ladies and gentlemen. No, this isn’t escapism. We are not merely creating this news for the sake of lessening the parables that grasp us by the throat. This is reality.\n\nAnd this is where we could start. Instead of being a wet blanket, why not take a positive paradigm shift? This maybe is the breakthrough we have been waiting for. And this could take us somewhere in renovating our home, to say the least.\n\nYes, the predicaments around us are more than enough to last us a lifetime. There’s no way they’ll be solved the moment we put our acts together. They may not even be solved in the entire life of this generation. They may not even be solved at all. But let us begin. Let us start now.\n\nIn John F. Kennedy’s words, “United there’s little we cannot do. Divided, there’s little we can do.” If we hold hands now, if we open our hearts to one another and disregard those barriers that keep us from being who we truly are, then maybe we could still hang on to life.\n\nAs I watch the children hold hands and dance about the stage, I recognize in their eyes what we almost lost—hope. The smiles pasted on their innocent faces spell a promise that there will be a tomorrow. And that tomorrow will surely be better.\n\nTomorrow, the world will be healed. Tomorrow, the world will be a better place. For you and for me and the entire human race.\n\nWrite your feature story.','teacher'),
(1313,1313,'Instructions: Compose a feature story using the information below.\n\nDescribe vividly your seatmate in a two-sentence paragraph.\n\nYou are the feature editor of your school paper. This morning while you were on your way to school, you saw a Grade 4 pupil helping an old woman cross the street. Fourth year boys, who were playing nearby saw the old woman but did not care to help her.\n\nYou interviewed the young boy. Now write a feature story on the boy or on the incident. Supply the facts you gathered from the interview. Remember, you are not writing an editorial. Note also that like an editorial, a feature article is hung on a natural or artificial newspeg. Give an interesting and entertaining title.\n\nNEXT TO BORACAY · Why Libuacan Cold Spring also needs a shutdown?\nEarlier this year, President Rodrigo Duterte ordered the temporary closure of the Boracay Island, a renowned tourist destination in the country, following reports of poor waste disposal mechanisms and environmental abuse. In the local context, I believe the most famous tourist spot in Tagbina – Libuacan Cold Spring – also needs a break from the daily tourism grind.\n\n-Subjective description on how Libuacan changed over the years, statement from a local supporting my description\n-Interview with barangay council on the ESWM implementation in Libuacan\n-Interview with the Municipal Tourism focal person regarding waste disposal issues\n-Transition from waste disposal issue to lack of regular employee to maintain cleanliness of the spring\n-Interview with Mayor on plans for rehabilitation, ownership issues\n-Transition to the temporary closure of the Enchanted River, interview with the management on the changes in the operation, effects of the closure\n-Go back to the expected results of Boracay closure\n- Conclusion reinforcing the idea presented in the title and lead\nFirst Place, In-depth Analysis, 2018 Regional TOT on Campus Journalism\n\nCyber pornography\nAs the pandemic takes its toll, more women have lost their jobs and are lured into cyber pornography. Even more alarming is the case of women in the flesh trade who expose themselves not only to sexually transmitted diseases and COVID-19, but also to sexual abuse, particularly rape, to earn money and make ends meet.\n\nWorld Wide Web Foundation co-founder Tim Berners-Lee warned in the early 2020 that the web is too often not safe for women. In its survey, 52% of young women and girls said they had experienced online abuse, including threatening messages, sexual harassment, and the sharing of private images without consent. Also, 87% said they think the problem is getting worse.\n\nThrough people\'s high dependence on digital interactions, the pandemic also seems to have increased the risk of cyber violence. Non-consensual sharing of images designed to threaten, shame, and control women facilitates cyber violence. Distribution or threats of sharing non-consensual intimate images also happens largely within contexts of intimate partner violence.\n\nMental health\nStress, breakups, and job loss can all aggravate pre-existing abusive behavior and coercive control, says Clare McGlynn, Professor of Law at Durham University, in a BBC news report. She attributes the rise in cases of intimate image abuse to \"the increased use of the internet and social media, as well as heightened emotions\" during the lockdown.\n\nThe fight for gender equality and freedom from gender-based violence will continue until all young boys and girls are empowered with the knowledge, skills, and attitudes to be caring and respectful souls. We need to intensify our effort to create safer spaces, whether offline or online, for women. We don\'t only need policies to serve as beacons. We must understand that laws are only as good as the people who implement and abide by them.\n\nThe world is haunted by trauma and fear, thanks to the constant abuse of human rights. Fighting violence against women remains unfinished business. If humanity collaborates and perseveres hard enough, gender and development will not only survive but prosper. Maybe, just Like Celie, we will learn that unbelievable gender-related hardships and struggles come with reconciliation and forgiveness. – Rappler.com Aries N. Oliveros used to teach campus journalism. He loves science and feature writing. He is currently the executive secretary of the executive director of the Philippine Science High School System.\n\nWrite your feature story.','teacher'),
(1314,1314,'WRITE A COLUMN ON THE FOLLOWING DATA\n\nNovember 12, 2024 · DepEd: No plan to move students’ summer break to April-May\n-The Department of Education (DepEd) has no plans to move the school “summer “break back to April and May amid the hot weather.\n\n-“At the moment, there are no plans to revert,” DepEd spokesman Michael Poa said in Viber message to reporters on Wednesday, when asked about the calls to move the school break back to April and May.\n\n-He said school heads have the discretion to suspend in-person classes and use alternative delivery modes or blended learning “if the environment is not conducive to learning.”\n\n-It was Senate basic education committee chairman Sherwin Gatchalian who said it is “time” to bring back the April-May summer break, following reports that students were hospitalized during a surprise fire drill in a school in Laguna.\n\nWrite your column.','teacher'),
(1315,1315,'Instructions: Draw an editorial based on the given editorial below.\n\nGet Ready for Yolanda\nIf typhoon Yolanda/Haiyan stays on track and does not weaken, it will arrive in the Visayas on Friday with winds stronger than those of super typhoon Ruping.\n\nThose of us who lived or worked in Cebu on Nov. 10-14, 1990 remember Ruping’s tracks well. Its center winds, which spun at 145 to 200 kilometers per hour, left more than 200 dead in Central Visayas alone. The typhoon unmoored a foreign vessel, which then hit the only bridge between Cebu and Mactan. Ruping also cut off power and water service to Cebu from one week to a month.\n\nWe remember, too, the resilience shown by the region’s residents, the concerted effort to regain our bearings and fix or replace up to P10.8 billion worth of damaged infrastructure. “The storm scarred Cebu,” a Sun.Star editorial observed then, “but also revealed its strengths.”\n\nYolanda is scheduled to enter the Philippine area of responsibility on Thursday morning, forcing communities in Bohol and Cebu from the disaster recovery stage back to disaster preparedness.\n\nBut unlike the earthquake that struck our communities three weeks ago, we have received and continue to receive warnings about Yolanda, including this: The US Navy’s Joint Typhoon Warning Center expects the typhoon will bring 222-kph winds.\n\nThere will be time later to check on the progress we’ve made or failed to make, in enforcing the National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Plan for 2011-2028.\n\nWe’ll have many opportunities to reexamine and address “the underlying causes” that leave people vulnerable to disaster. And there will be time (and resources, for we are a resilient and resourceful lot) to “build back better.”\n\nFor now, however, let us all focus on preparing ourselves for the typhoon that approaches. Start at home. Prepare a basic family kit.\n\nThe US Federal Emergency Management Administration recommends preparing enough ready-to-eat food for three days; a gallon of clean water per person per day; a first aid kit (and maintenance medications, we may add); a transistor radio and flashlight with spare batteries; a whistle to signal for help, if needed; dust masks or a cotton shirt in case there’s a need to filter the air; and important family documents packed in a waterproof, portable container.\n\n“Hazards become disasters only if vulnerable people and resources are exposed to them,” the plan goes. Yes, we are resilient, Cebu. This time let’s also be prepared.\n\nDraw your editorial cartoon on paper, then describe it here.','teacher'),
(1316,1316,'Instructions: Given the editorial cartoon below. Discuss the different characters and how does it symbolize.\n\nDiscuss the different characters and how does it symbolize.','teacher'),
(1317,1317,'Instructions: With the given editorial cartoon below and the news given. Draw an improvement on the editorial cartoon.\n\nThe News\nManila, Philippines --- The 2013 elections should be the last time Filipinos will use Smartmatic\'s precinct count optical scan (PCOS) machines in an election.\n\nThis was the call of seven party-list congressmen as they revealed that a congressional probe on controversies raised about the credibility of the results of the 2013 midterm elections will be the first order of the day when the 16th Congress commences next month.\n\nThe seven congressmen belonging to the Makabayan Coalition bewailed the failure of the Commission on Elections (Comelec) to deliver on its promise for worry-free and credible elections, saying that its insistence on using the expensive Smartmatic automated election machines was among the principal reasons for this.\n\nLed by Bayan Muna Rep. Neri Javier Colmenares, the incumbent and incoming Makabayan congressmen said a thorough congressional probe is needed to determine the truth about Comelec\'s supposed removal of key safeguards against election cheating and technical problems.\n\nThese include the belated release of the source codes, the massive glitches and transmission failures of PCOS machines, and the reported 60-30-10 vote pattern.\n\nColmenares, vice chairman of the House Committee on Suffrage and Electoral Reform, said the legislative inquiry will also look into the discrepancies found on random manual audits (RMA) and other violations of the automated elections law. For starters, the Makabayan lawmaker wants the PCSO machines junked and not tapped for the presidential elections in 2016.\n\nAside from Colmenares the other members of Makabayan\'s 16th Congress nominees are lawyer Isagani Zarate of Bayan Muna; Fernando Hicap of Anakpawis; lawyer Terry Ridon of Kabataan; and incumbent Reps. Luz Ilagan and Emmi de Jesus of Gabriela, and Antonio Tinio of ACT Teachers Partylist.\n\nAll efforts must be exhausted to find out if the automated election system was used for electoral fraud. What we are sure about is that the faulty PCOS machines must be scrapped and never be again used for the elections, said Colmenares.\n\nHe added, \"Smartmatic and the Commission on Elections should be held accountable for non-compliance to and violations of automated election laws and the unconscionable expenses for a defective and non-transparent foreign-controlled voting technology.\"\n\nThe senior Makabayan solon insisted that the Comelec and Smartmatic have joined forces to deceive the public when they claimed that the PCOS source code has been turned over to the Philippines before the 2010 elections when it was not.\n\nAccording to him, the data was only delivered too late in May 2013 to frustrate efforts to review the said human readable instructions for possible glitches and fraud.\n\n\"The numerous glitches and massive failure of PCOS machines to transmit results are more than enough reasons for the government to scrap the PCOS system and sue Smartmatic. It is believed that aside from 18,400 machines which failed to transmit votes there are thousands more of machines that failed to read, count, and transmit votes but used other machines,\" added Colmenares.\n\nColmenares said that \"instead of credible elections, the Smartmatic automated elections only caused serious doubts in the mind of election watchdogs and the public because of the Comelec\'s denial of the source code review, the absence of key safeguards, and the massive glitches and failures of PCOS machines.\n\nDraw your improved editorial cartoon on paper, then describe it here.','teacher'),
(1318,1318,'Learning Competency with Code: Explain the importance of the principles of radio broadcasting. (SPJ9PTJ-Ia-2)\n\nBackground Information for Learners:\nRadio broadcasting is a hot media. Hot in the sense that it brings news immediately, right as it is happening, also because it is a man or woman talking to other men other women, be they young, adult or old. It is a voice. But one must tread carefully when it comes to fastness. One should never spread neither rumor nor hearsay. History has shown us just how far disrespecting exactness and objectivity can lead.\n\nThe principles of Radio Broadcasting are:\n1. On-air Delivery – the right tone\n2. Respecting Others\n3. Accuracy\n4. Balance\n5. Clarity\n6. No Commenting\n7. Protect Your Sources\n8. Keep in Touch with the Newsroom\n\nColumn A\n1. Be precise, choose every word carefully.\n2. A journalist must safeguard his sources.\n3. We speak more slowly on air than in real life, but we need to describe, to tell stories, to create pictures.\n4. Stick to the facts.\n5. The anchor and the editor need to know how you are doing so as to properly work on the broadcast.\n6. Use simple language so the audience can get the focus.\n7. Everybody is owed privacy and respect.\n8. Try to show all sides of the argument.\nA. On-air Delivery – the right tone\nR. Respecting Others\nD. Accuracy\nS. Balance\nW. Clarity\nH. No Commenting\nL. Protect Your Sources\nP. Keep in Touch with Newsroom\n\nMystery Question:\nWho is the American newspaper publisher, known for introducing sensationalism, who cabled this to Frederic Remington in 1898?\n\nWrite the answer to the mystery question.\n\nHaving your own style and on air presence requires perpetual work.\nALWAYS\nNEVER\n\nThe audience’s job is to give other people a voice, to let them be heard.\nALWAYS\nNEVER\n\nIn radio broadcasting, one must fact-check everything.\nALWAYS\nNEVER\n\nIn dealing with a controversy, a radio broadcaster must provide one side of the arguments.\nALWAYS\nNEVER\n\nIf your audience cannot get what you are saying, all the investigating you have done will have been done in vain.\nALWAYS\nNEVER\n\nCommenting carelessly is judging, expressing an opinion only.\nALWAYS\nNEVER\n\nInforming the general public is looking for the truth, even just the half-truth.\nALWAYS\nNEVER\n\nKeeping in touch with the newsroom is true only in peace time and not in conflict areas.\nALWAYS\nNEVER\n\n1. On-air Delivery – the right tone\n\n2. Respecting Others\n\n3. Accuracy\n\n4. Balance\n\n5. Clarity\n\n6. No Commenting\n\n7. Protect Your Sources\n\n8. Keep in Touch with the Newsroom\n\nReflection: The most important learning I gained …','teacher'),
(1319,1319,'Title: My First Baseball Game\nAuthor: Randy Ryan\nWords: 237\n\nHe sent back three hot dogs. “This is great,” I said to my uncle. “Not so great,” he said. “We just struck out.” While I had been watching the hotdogs, the team had struck out. It went on that way for the rest of the game. We got peanuts. We even got another hot dog. And the Cubs kept losing. We’d all hope every time a batter came to bat.\n\nOne by one they struck out. Still, everyone was staying happy. Even at the end. The score was 7 to 0. The Cubs never got a run. “Tomorrow, they’ll win,” one person said. “Right. This was just a bad day for them,” another said. “The pitcher tomorrow is really good.” When we were on the way home, I asked my uncle, “Why was everyone so sure they will win next time?” “That’s what it means to be a Cubs fan.\n\nWe believe in tomorrow. When they win, it’s the best. Every year, we hope. Every game we hope. That’s loyalty. It’s about supporting your team no matter what. Cubs fans are loyal. And it’s about having fun as a family. Did you have fun?” As our uncle was about to drive home to Indiana, Felicia said, “It was great. I see what you meant. Even when they lose, they are like a family, they stick together. Thanks for taking us. And thanks for being loyal to us.”','teacher'),
(1320,1320,'Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','teacher'),
(1321,1321,'Title: The Bridge to Tomorrow\nWords: 181\n\nWhen fourteen-year-old Marco transferred to his new school, everything seemed unfamiliar. The classrooms were larger, the teachers spoke faster, and the students already had their own groups of friends. During his first week, he preferred sitting quietly at the back of the room, hoping no one would notice him.\n\nOne morning, their English teacher announced a reading activity. Each learner would read a passage aloud before the class. Marco\'s heart beat rapidly. He enjoyed reading silently, but speaking before others made him nervous.\n\nWhen his turn came, he stood slowly and held the reading selection with trembling hands. He took a deep breath and began reading. At first, his voice was soft, but after several sentences he noticed that his classmates were listening attentively. His teacher smiled encouragingly whenever he paused correctly or pronounced a difficult word clearly.\n\nAfter the activity, several classmates congratulated him. One even asked if they could become partners for the next reading assignment. Marco realized that confidence grows through practice. Every page he read became another step across the bridge toward new friendships and greater opportunities.','teacher'),
(1322,1322,'Title: The Voice Within\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 146\n\nThere is a quiet voice inside,\nA faithful friend, a trusted guide.\nIt tells us when to stand up tall,\nTo help someone who starts to fall.\nIt whispers, \"Choose the path that\'s right,\nEven when it\'s out of sight.\"\nIt gives us courage, calm, and grace\nTo meet each challenge that we face.\nSometimes storms may cloud the day,\nAnd doubts may try to block the way.\nYet deep within, that gentle light\nStill shines to guide us through the night.\nA dream begins with one small step,\nWith every promise wisely kept.\nSuccess is built on what we do,\nOn honest hearts that stay sincere and true.\nListen well when choices call.\nYour inner voice can conquer all.\nWith hope and faith beside your way,\nYou\'ll grow much stronger every day.\nBelieve your voice.\nBelieve your dream.\nBecome much more\nThan you may seem.','teacher'),
(1323,1323,'Title: Tomorrow Begins Today\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 103\n\nTomorrow waits beyond the hill,\nPrepared for those with strength and will.\nIt does not ask how rich you are,\nNor where you live, nor near nor far.\nIt simply asks,\n\"Will you prepare?\nWill you keep learning everywhere?\"\nA page today,\nA book tonight,\nCan help your future shine more bright.\nMistakes may come,\nAnd plans may bend,\nYet every challenge has an end.\nEach sunrise brings another chance\nTo learn,\nTo lead,\nTo dream,\nTo dance.\nSo build your future,\nBrick by brick,\nWith patient hands that never quit.\nTomorrow starts\nWith what you do—\nAnd all tomorrow\nStarts with you.','teacher'),
(1324,1324,'Title: Beyond the Mountain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 96\n\nBeyond the mountain stands a dream,\nBrighter than the morning gleam.\nMany travelers lose their way,\nYet hopeful hearts decide to stay.\nThey climb through rain,\nThey climb through heat,\nWith tired hands and aching feet.\nEach rocky trail,\nEach winding road,\nMakes lighter every heavy load.\nThe summit waits for those who try,\nWho never fear to ask, \"Why?\"\nFor every step, though small it seems,\nLeads us closer to our dreams.\nSo if the journey feels too long,\nRemember this encouraging song:\nNo mountain stands forever high—\nThe brave will always reach the sky.','teacher'),
(1325,1325,'Title: Digital Footprints\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nEvery message that we send,\nEvery photo that we lend,\nLeaves a mark upon the way,\nLong beyond the passing day.\nKindness posted,\nTruth expressed,\nMakes the internet its best.\nWords can heal,\nOr words can harm,\nHolding either peace or alarm.\nThink before you click \"Send,\"\nChoose the message you defend.\nBuild a world that\'s safe and bright,\nGuided always by what\'s right.\nThough our screens may glow each night,\nCharacter remains our light.\nLet our footprints clearly show\nRespect wherever we may go.','teacher'),
(1326,1326,'Title: The Library Window\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nBeside the library window wide,\nCountless worlds are found inside.\nHeroes, oceans, stars, and kings,\nLive among the printed things.\nEvery shelf contains a door,\nOpening to something more.\nHistory whispers,\nScience sings,\nAdventure spreads its eager wings.\nA single book can change a mind,\nLeaving fear and doubt behind.\nReaders travel far each day\nThough they never move away.\nTurn each page with eager eyes,\nSeek the truth,\nBe brave,\nBe wise.\nFor every reader soon will find\nA stronger heart,\nA brighter mind.','teacher'),
(1327,1327,'Title: A Teacher\'s Gift\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 97\n\nA teacher plants a tiny seed,\nWith every lesson, thought, and deed.\nNot made of gold or precious stone,\nBut wisdom learners call their own.\nA word of praise, a guiding hand,\nCan help a fearful learner stand.\nA patient smile, a simple cheer,\nCan chase away uncertainty and fear.\nEach question asked, each answer found,\nMakes knowledge grow on fertile ground.\nThough years may pass and seasons shift,\nLearning remains a lasting gift.\nOne day the learner leads the way,\nHelping others every day.\nThe greatest treasure we can give\nIs teaching others how to live.','teacher'),
(1328,1328,'Title: The Silent River\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 95\n\nThe river never boasts aloud,\nNor seeks applause from any crowd.\nYet day by day it shapes the land\nWith quiet strength and patient hand.\nIt bends around the hardest stone,\nStill finding pathways of its own.\nIts waters teach us not to fight,\nBut gently choose the wiser right.\nThough storms may darken overhead,\nThe river steadily moves ahead.\nIt never stops because of fear;\nIts destination still is clear.\nLike rivers flowing to the sea,\nOur dreams become reality\nWhen steady hearts refuse to stray\nAnd choose to grow a little each day.','teacher'),
(1329,1329,'Title: Hope in the Storm\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nDark clouds gather in the sky,\nThunder rumbles rolling by.\nBranches bend beneath the rain,\nYet flowers bloom once more again.\nEvery storm will someday cease,\nGiving way to light and peace.\nCourage grows through every trial,\nOften hidden for a while.\nThough the winds may shake the trees,\nHope remains within the breeze.\nMorning always follows night,\nBringing warmth and golden light.\nSo when life seems hard to bear,\nKeep believing, keep your care.\nEven storms that make us strong\nCannot last forever long.','teacher'),
(1330,1330,'Title: Every Dream Matters\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 84\n\nSome dream high among the stars,\nOthers heal invisible scars.\nSome create amazing art,\nOthers serve with faithful heart.\nEvery dream deserves its place,\nEvery goal its patient race.\nGreat achievements always start\nFrom hope inside a willing heart.\nDreams are built through daily choice,\nThrough discipline and honest voice.\nStep by step the future grows,\nMore than anyone yet knows.\nHold your dream with courage true.\nLet determination carry you.\nFor every dream that lights the night\nCan change tomorrow with its light.','teacher'),
(1331,1331,'Title: We Rise Together\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nOne small hand can plant a tree,\nTwo can build community.\nMany hearts that work as one\nFinish what cannot be done.\nDifferent voices, different minds,\nEach unique in countless kinds.\nYet together we become\nStronger than we are alone.\nHelping others makes us grow.\nSharing all the things we know.\nKindness spreads from friend to friend,\nWith no limit, with no end.\nLet us build a brighter day,\nChoosing hope in every way.\nWhen we stand through every weather,\nWe discover—\nWe rise together.','teacher'),
(1332,1332,'Title: The Last Seat in the Classroom\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 260\n\nThe last seat in Room Seven was always empty until the second week of classes. On Monday morning, a quiet boy named Adrian entered the room carrying an old backpack and a neatly folded notebook. He smiled politely as the teacher introduced him to the class.\n\nSince all the front seats were already occupied, Adrian sat at the back. During lessons, he listened carefully and wrote detailed notes, but he rarely spoke. Some classmates assumed he was shy, while others simply forgot he was there.\n\nOne afternoon, the class was assigned a group science project. Adrian\'s group struggled to organize their ideas. They argued over the experiment and could not agree on who would present.\n\nAfter listening quietly, Adrian finally raised his hand. \"May I suggest something?\" he asked.\n\nHis classmates nodded.\n\nHe explained how they could divide the work fairly. He also showed them a simple experiment he had learned in his previous school. Everyone agreed to follow his plan.\n\nPresentation day arrived. Their experiment worked perfectly, and the class applauded their teamwork. Their teacher praised not only the project but also the way everyone cooperated.\n\nDuring recess, Adrian no longer sat alone. His classmates invited him to eat with them, asking questions about his old school and sharing stories about their own experiences.\n\nAdrian realized that sometimes people are not noticed because they remain silent. Yet kindness, preparation, and willingness to help often speak louder than words. From that day forward, the last seat in the classroom became known as the place where a quiet leader once sat.','teacher'),
(1333,1333,'Title: The New Student\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 227\n\nLena transferred to a new school halfway through the school year. Everything felt unfamiliar—from the classrooms to the faces she passed along the hallways. Although her teachers were welcoming, she found it difficult to begin conversations with her classmates.\n\nDuring lunch breaks, Lena usually sat alone beneath a large acacia tree near the school garden. She spent her time reading novels while other students laughed and played nearby.\n\nOne rainy afternoon, the school announced a clean-up drive after classes. Every section was assigned an area to organize. Lena quietly joined her classmates in clearing fallen leaves and arranging flower pots.\n\nAs they worked together, one of her classmates noticed that Lena knew the names of many different plants. Curious, they asked how she learned so much.\n\n\"My grandmother loves gardening,\" Lena explained. \"She taught me how to care for flowers and vegetables.\"\n\nSoon everyone was asking questions. Lena demonstrated how to prepare seedlings and explained why certain plants needed more sunlight than others.\n\nThe following week, their adviser appointed Lena as one of the leaders of the class gardening project. Her classmates eagerly worked beside her, planting vegetables and flowering shrubs that later brightened the school grounds.\n\nLena realized that friendships often begin with shared work rather than shared words. By simply offering her knowledge and helping others, she found a place where she truly belonged.','teacher'),
(1334,1334,'Title: One Honest Decision\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 238\n\nIt was dismissal time when Joshua noticed a black wallet lying near the school gate. Students hurried past without seeing it. He picked it up and looked around, hoping someone would return to claim it.\n\nInside the wallet were several identification cards, a few receipts, and a considerable amount of money. Joshua immediately realized that someone must be worried about losing it.\n\nHis friends suggested different ideas. One jokingly said, \"Nobody saw you. Just keep it.\"\n\nJoshua smiled but shook his head. \"If this belonged to my father, I would hope someone returned it.\"\n\nInstead of going home immediately, he brought the wallet to the principal\'s office. The school staff checked the identification card and contacted the owner, who happened to be the father of one of the students.\n\nLess than an hour later, the grateful owner arrived. He counted the money and found that nothing was missing.\n\n\"You have saved me from many problems today,\" the man said warmly. \"This money was meant to pay our workers.\"\n\nHe offered Joshua a reward, but the boy politely declined.\n\n\"My parents taught me that honesty is worth more than money,\" Joshua replied.\n\nThe principal later shared Joshua\'s story during the Monday flag ceremony. His classmates applauded as he received a Certificate of Good Citizenship.\n\nJoshua discovered that doing the right thing may not always bring material rewards, but it earns something even greater—the trust and respect of others.','teacher'),
(1335,1335,'Title: The Forgotten Library Card\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 198\n\nEvery Wednesday afternoon, the Grade 8 learners visited the school library. It had become one of Sophia\'s favorite places because it offered more than books. It provided quiet spaces where she could think, study, and imagine.\n\nOne afternoon, she eagerly searched for a science encyclopedia recommended by her teacher. After finding the book, she walked toward the circulation desk.\n\nThen she realized something.\n\nHer library card was missing.\n\nShe searched every pocket of her bag, checked her notebooks, and even looked beneath the tables. It was nowhere to be found.\n\nFeeling disappointed, Sophia informed the librarian, who calmly smiled.\n\n\"Sometimes,\" the librarian said, \"finding something begins by remembering your last careful moment.\"\n\nSophia retraced her steps. She remembered reviewing her assignment under a large narra tree before entering the library.\n\nWhen she returned outside, she found the card exactly where she had placed it between the pages of her notebook.\n\nRelieved, she borrowed the encyclopedia and thanked the librarian.\n\nThat afternoon she learned that careful habits save both time and worry. Since then, she kept all important school cards inside a small transparent organizer attached to her bag.\n\nSmall acts of organization, she realized, often prevent bigger problems.','teacher'),
(1336,1336,'Title: The Science Fair Challenge\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 195\n\nThe annual Science Fair was only three weeks away, and excitement filled the campus. Every Grade 8 section had been challenged to present an invention that solved a community problem.\n\nGroup Three decided to create a simple rainwater collection system that could help households conserve water during the dry season.\n\nAt first, everything seemed easy. However, the prototype leaked during testing.\n\nSome members became discouraged.\n\n\"We should change our project,\" one student suggested.\n\nTheir team leader, Camille, disagreed.\n\n\"Real scientists don\'t stop after one failure,\" she said. \"Every mistake teaches us something.\"\n\nThe group carefully examined the design. They discovered that one connector pipe was slightly loose. After making adjustments and sealing the joints properly, they tested the system again.\n\nThis time, the container successfully collected rainwater without leaking.\n\nOn presentation day, the judges praised not only the invention but also the team\'s explanation of the scientific process behind their improvements.\n\nAlthough they placed second overall, the judges selected their project to be displayed during the municipal science exhibit.\n\nCamille smiled as she looked at her teammates.\n\n\"We didn\'t succeed because everything worked the first time,\" she said.\n\n\"We succeeded because we never stopped improving.\"','teacher'),
(1337,1337,'Title: A Letter to My Future Self\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 194\n\nAs part of their English class activity, each learner was asked to write a letter addressed to themselves ten years in the future.\n\nMost students wrote about the careers they hoped to pursue. Some dreamed of becoming engineers, doctors, teachers, artists, or entrepreneurs.\n\nMiguel stared at his blank paper for several minutes before writing his first sentence.\n\n\"Dear Future Me,\n\nI hope you still remember the promises you made when you were fourteen.\"\n\nHe continued writing about the values he wanted to keep—honesty, kindness, humility, and perseverance.\n\nHe reminded himself that success should never be measured only by money or awards, but also by the number of lives positively influenced.\n\nBefore sealing the letter inside an envelope, he added one final paragraph.\n\n\"If life becomes difficult, remember the dreams that inspired you to begin. Keep learning, keep serving, and never stop believing that every day is another opportunity to become a better person.\"\n\nThe teacher collected all the letters and promised to return them during the learners\' graduation from senior high school.\n\nAs Miguel walked home, he smiled.\n\nPerhaps the most important promise we ever make is the one we make to ourselves.','teacher'),
(1338,1338,'Title: The Volunteer\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 202\n\nA powerful typhoon had passed through the municipality, leaving many families in need of assistance. Several classrooms were damaged, trees blocked the roads, and many homes lost electricity.\n\nThe school organized a volunteer day to help clean the campus and prepare learning spaces for classes to resume.\n\nAmong the volunteers was Aaron, a Grade 7 learner who had never joined a community service activity before.\n\nHe expected the work to be tiring, and it certainly was.\n\nStudents swept classrooms, gathered broken branches, arranged donated books, and repainted faded signboards.\n\nDuring lunch, Aaron noticed an elderly maintenance worker struggling to carry several heavy boxes.\n\nWithout being asked, Aaron immediately helped him.\n\nThe man smiled gratefully.\n\n\"You remind me that kindness is strongest when nobody asks for it,\" he said.\n\nBy late afternoon, the school grounds looked cleaner than they had before the storm.\n\nAlthough Aaron returned home physically exhausted, he felt deeply satisfied.\n\nThat evening, he reflected on the day\'s work.\n\nVolunteering had taught him that serving the community is not measured by the size of one\'s contribution but by the willingness to offer time, effort, and compassion.\n\nSometimes the greatest reward for helping others is discovering that we have grown stronger ourselves.','teacher'),
(1339,1339,'Title: The Lost Wallet\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 246\n\nFriday afternoons were always busy at Manolo Integrated High School. Students hurried toward the school gate while others waited for their parents or rode the last jeepneys home.\n\nAs Claire walked across the covered court, she noticed a brown wallet beneath one of the benches. She picked it up and looked around, but no one seemed to be searching for it.\n\nInside were several identification cards, a driver\'s license, receipts, and enough cash to pay for a week\'s groceries. Claire immediately imagined how worried the owner must be.\n\nHer friend suggested taking the wallet to the nearest store because someone there might recognize its owner. Another classmate thought they should wait outside the gate.\n\nClaire remembered what their Values Education teacher often said: \"When you find something valuable, place it in the hands of someone who is responsible.\"\n\nShe went directly to the Guidance Office. The guidance counselor carefully examined the identification card and discovered that the wallet belonged to the father of a Grade 8 learner.\n\nWithin thirty minutes, the grateful parent arrived.\n\n\"I thought I had lost this forever,\" he said with relief. \"This contains my salary for the week.\"\n\nHe offered Claire a reward, but she politely refused.\n\n\"My reward is knowing everything was returned safely,\" she replied.\n\nDuring the next flag ceremony, the school recognized Claire for her honesty.\n\nHer classmates learned that integrity is demonstrated not only during difficult moments but also through simple decisions that no one may ever see.','teacher'),
(1340,1340,'Title: Race Against Time\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nOnly two days remained before the Regional Robotics Competition, and Team Innovate was still struggling with their robot\'s navigation program.\n\nEvery afternoon after classes, the four members met inside the computer laboratory. While other students were already heading home, they continued testing sensors, correcting computer code, and redesigning small mechanical parts.\n\nSometimes the robot stopped without warning. At other times, it turned in the wrong direction or failed to recognize obstacles.\n\n\"We\'re running out of time,\" Ethan sighed.\n\nTheir adviser smiled calmly.\n\n\"Success rarely belongs to those who never fail,\" she said. \"It belongs to those who learn quickly from every failure.\"\n\nThe group divided the remaining tasks. Ethan corrected the programming, Mia recalibrated the sensors, Joshua improved the wheels, while Hannah documented every modification.\n\nLate Friday evening, they performed one final test.\n\nThe robot moved smoothly through every checkpoint without making a single mistake.\n\nAlthough the competition was challenging, Team Innovate proudly earned second place.\n\nAs they packed their equipment, Ethan realized that winning a medal was rewarding, but learning perseverance, teamwork, and problem-solving was an even greater achievement.','teacher'),
(1341,1341,'Title: The School Garden\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nAt the beginning of the school year, the vacant lot beside the library looked dry and neglected. Weeds covered the ground, and very few students paid attention to it.\n\nTheir Science teacher proposed a challenge.\n\n\"What if we transformed this empty space into a vegetable garden?\"\n\nAt first, many students doubted the idea.\n\nThe soil looked rocky, and the weather had been unusually hot.\n\nNevertheless, the class accepted the challenge.\n\nEvery Wednesday morning, they watered seedlings, removed weeds, added compost, and measured plant growth.\n\nSome vegetables grew quickly, while others struggled.\n\nThe students carefully observed how sunlight, rainfall, and soil nutrients affected each plant.\n\nAfter three months, the once-empty lot had become a colorful garden filled with tomatoes, eggplants, pechay, okra, and herbs.\n\nThe harvested vegetables were donated to the school\'s feeding program.\n\nDuring the recognition ceremony, the principal congratulated the class.\n\n\"You did not simply grow vegetables,\" she said.\n\n\"You cultivated responsibility, patience, and teamwork.\"\n\nThe students proudly realized that meaningful change often begins with small acts repeated consistently.','teacher'),
(1342,1342,'Title: The Community Hero\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nEvery morning before sunrise, Mang Ernesto could already be seen sweeping the streets surrounding the public market.\n\nMany people knew him simply as the town\'s sanitation worker. Few realized that he had been serving the community for more than twenty-five years.\n\nDespite the hot weather and occasional heavy rain, he reported for work every day with a smile.\n\nOne Saturday, Grade 8 learners interviewed him for their Araling Panlipunan project about community helpers.\n\n\"What motivates you to continue this work?\" one student asked.\n\nMang Ernesto smiled before answering.\n\n\"When people wake up and find clean streets, they can begin their day safely and comfortably. Knowing that my work helps others is enough reason to continue.\"\n\nThe students listened attentively.\n\nThey realized that heroism is not always found in newspapers or television.\n\nSometimes heroes quietly perform ordinary tasks with extraordinary dedication.\n\nInspired by the interview, the class organized a monthly clean-up campaign within their barangay.\n\nMang Ernesto happily joined them.\n\nBy working together, the learners discovered that every citizen has the power to improve the community through simple acts of service.','teacher'),
(1343,1343,'Title: Beyond the Screen\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 168\n\nLike many teenagers, Daniel enjoyed spending hours playing online games and watching videos after school.\n\nAt first, he believed there was nothing wrong with it.\n\nHowever, he gradually noticed several changes.\n\nHe stayed up late every night.\n\nHe arrived at school feeling sleepy.\n\nHis assignments became incomplete, and his quiz scores slowly declined.\n\nOne afternoon, his older sister invited him to join a weekend hiking activity organized by their youth group.\n\nDaniel hesitated.\n\nHe almost refused.\n\nStill, he decided to give it a try.\n\nThe hike led them through forest trails, rivers, and scenic viewpoints.\n\nWithout realizing it, Daniel spent an entire day without checking his phone.\n\nInstead, he laughed with new friends, learned about native trees, and appreciated the beauty of nature.\n\nThat evening he reflected on his experience.\n\nTechnology is useful, he realized, but balance is essential.\n\nSince then, Daniel scheduled daily screen time while making room for reading, exercise, family conversations, and outdoor activities.\n\nLife beyond the screen offered experiences no device could fully replace.','teacher'),
(1344,1344,'Title: The Basketball Captain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nThe championship game was only minutes away.\n\nEveryone expected Team Falcons to win because they had remained undefeated throughout the season.\n\nAs captain, Marcus felt tremendous pressure.\n\nWhen the first quarter ended, however, his team trailed by twelve points.\n\nSome players began blaming one another for missed shots and defensive mistakes.\n\nDuring the timeout, Marcus gathered everyone into a circle.\n\n\"We cannot change the first quarter,\" he said calmly.\n\n\"But we can decide how we play the next three.\"\n\nHis teammates nodded.\n\nInstead of focusing on individual scoring, they emphasized teamwork.\n\nThey passed the ball more carefully, communicated during defense, and encouraged one another after every play.\n\nLittle by little, the gap narrowed.\n\nWith only fifteen seconds remaining, the score was tied.\n\nMarcus passed the ball to the newest member of the team, who made the winning basket.\n\nThe crowd erupted with applause.\n\nAfter the game, reporters asked Marcus why he did not take the final shot himself.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"A captain\'s greatest victory is helping the entire team succeed.\"','teacher'),
(1345,1345,'Title: The Secret of the Old Mango Tree\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 269\n\nThe oldest tree inside San Isidro National High School stood quietly behind the library. Its thick trunk and wide branches had provided shade for generations of learners. Teachers often joked that the tree had witnessed more examinations, graduations, and celebrations than anyone else in the school.\n\nOne afternoon, while researching for a local history project, three Grade 8 learners noticed a small metal box partially hidden beneath one of the tree\'s large roots. Curious but careful, they informed their adviser before touching it.\n\nTogether, they carefully removed the box from the soil. Inside they found several yellowed photographs, handwritten letters, and an old class program dated more than forty years earlier.\n\nThe school principal contacted retired teachers and alumni to help identify the people shown in the photographs. During the following week, former students visited the school and shared stories about their experiences as learners many years ago.\n\nThey spoke about studying without computers, participating in school programs under the same mango tree, and helping build classrooms through community volunteer work.\n\nThe learners listened with great interest.\n\nThey realized that although school buildings, technology, and teaching methods continue to change, the values of respect, perseverance, and service remain constant through every generation.\n\nInspired by what they had learned, the Student Government organized a Heritage Corner inside the library. The photographs, letters, and stories were carefully preserved so future learners could appreciate the rich history of their school.\n\nWhenever students gathered beneath the old mango tree, they no longer saw only its strong branches and broad leaves.\n\nThey saw living proof that every generation leaves behind lessons for the next.','teacher'),
(1346,1346,'Title: A New Beginning\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 233\n\nGraduation day had finally arrived.\n\nRows of proud parents filled the covered court while graduates adjusted their togas and smiled nervously at one another.\n\nAmong them sat Nicole, quietly remembering her first day in junior high school.\n\nShe had once been afraid to answer questions in class.\n\nReading aloud made her anxious, and group presentations seemed impossible.\n\nLittle by little, everything changed.\n\nHer teachers patiently guided her.\n\nHer classmates encouraged her to participate.\n\nShe joined reading activities, accepted leadership roles, and volunteered during school programs.\n\nEach experience strengthened her confidence.\n\nWhen Nicole\'s name was called to receive the Leadership Award, the audience applauded warmly.\n\nAs she walked across the stage, she remembered every challenge she had overcome—not because life had become easier, but because she had become stronger.\n\nAfter the ceremony, a Grade 7 learner approached her.\n\n\"Ate Nicole,\" he asked, \"what advice can you give someone who is afraid of making mistakes?\"\n\nNicole smiled.\n\n\"Never let fear stop you from trying,\" she replied. \"Every mistake teaches something valuable. Every challenge prepares you for a greater opportunity.\"\n\nYears later, Nicole hoped to become a teacher. She wanted to help young learners discover what her own teachers had helped her realize:\n\nEducation is not simply about earning high grades.\n\nIt is about becoming a person who can improve the lives of others.\n\nEvery ending, she believed, is simply the beginning of another journey.','teacher'),
(1347,1347,'Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','teacher'),
(1348,1348,'Title: Finding My Voice\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 238\n\nAt the beginning of the school year, Aaron preferred listening instead of speaking. Whenever teachers asked volunteers to read aloud, he lowered his head and hoped someone else would be chosen. He worried that his classmates might laugh if he mispronounced a difficult word.\n\nHis English teacher noticed his hesitation and encouraged him to spend fifteen minutes each day reading aloud at home. At first, Aaron struggled with unfamiliar vocabulary and paused frequently. However, he continued practicing every evening.\n\nAfter several weeks, reading became easier. He learned to recognize punctuation marks, read with better expression, and pronounce longer words correctly. His confidence gradually increased.\n\nOne day, the school announced a storytelling contest. Although Aaron felt nervous, he decided to participate. He practiced faithfully before his parents, younger sister, and even in front of a mirror.\n\nDuring the contest, Aaron spoke clearly and confidently. He remembered to pause at commas, change his tone during conversations, and emphasize important ideas. The audience listened attentively from beginning to end.\n\nAlthough another learner won first place, Aaron received a special award for Most Improved Reader.\n\nWalking back to his seat, he realized that success is not always measured by trophies. Sometimes it is measured by the courage to do something that once seemed impossible.\n\nFrom that day forward, Aaron no longer feared reading aloud. Instead, he looked forward to every opportunity to learn, improve, and inspire others through the power of words.','teacher'),
(1349,1349,'Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','teacher'),
(1350,1350,'Title: The Bridge to Tomorrow\nWords: 181\n\nWhen fourteen-year-old Marco transferred to his new school, everything seemed unfamiliar. The classrooms were larger, the teachers spoke faster, and the students already had their own groups of friends. During his first week, he preferred sitting quietly at the back of the room, hoping no one would notice him.\n\nOne morning, their English teacher announced a reading activity. Each learner would read a passage aloud before the class. Marco\'s heart beat rapidly. He enjoyed reading silently, but speaking before others made him nervous.\n\nWhen his turn came, he stood slowly and held the reading selection with trembling hands. He took a deep breath and began reading. At first, his voice was soft, but after several sentences he noticed that his classmates were listening attentively. His teacher smiled encouragingly whenever he paused correctly or pronounced a difficult word clearly.\n\nAfter the activity, several classmates congratulated him. One even asked if they could become partners for the next reading assignment. Marco realized that confidence grows through practice. Every page he read became another step across the bridge toward new friendships and greater opportunities.','teacher'),
(1351,1351,'Title: The Voice Within\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 146\n\nThere is a quiet voice inside,\nA faithful friend, a trusted guide.\nIt tells us when to stand up tall,\nTo help someone who starts to fall.\nIt whispers, \"Choose the path that\'s right,\nEven when it\'s out of sight.\"\nIt gives us courage, calm, and grace\nTo meet each challenge that we face.\nSometimes storms may cloud the day,\nAnd doubts may try to block the way.\nYet deep within, that gentle light\nStill shines to guide us through the night.\nA dream begins with one small step,\nWith every promise wisely kept.\nSuccess is built on what we do,\nOn honest hearts that stay sincere and true.\nListen well when choices call.\nYour inner voice can conquer all.\nWith hope and faith beside your way,\nYou\'ll grow much stronger every day.\nBelieve your voice.\nBelieve your dream.\nBecome much more\nThan you may seem.','teacher'),
(1352,1352,'Title: Tomorrow Begins Today\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 103\n\nTomorrow waits beyond the hill,\nPrepared for those with strength and will.\nIt does not ask how rich you are,\nNor where you live, nor near nor far.\nIt simply asks,\n\"Will you prepare?\nWill you keep learning everywhere?\"\nA page today,\nA book tonight,\nCan help your future shine more bright.\nMistakes may come,\nAnd plans may bend,\nYet every challenge has an end.\nEach sunrise brings another chance\nTo learn,\nTo lead,\nTo dream,\nTo dance.\nSo build your future,\nBrick by brick,\nWith patient hands that never quit.\nTomorrow starts\nWith what you do—\nAnd all tomorrow\nStarts with you.','teacher'),
(1353,1353,'Title: Beyond the Mountain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 96\n\nBeyond the mountain stands a dream,\nBrighter than the morning gleam.\nMany travelers lose their way,\nYet hopeful hearts decide to stay.\nThey climb through rain,\nThey climb through heat,\nWith tired hands and aching feet.\nEach rocky trail,\nEach winding road,\nMakes lighter every heavy load.\nThe summit waits for those who try,\nWho never fear to ask, \"Why?\"\nFor every step, though small it seems,\nLeads us closer to our dreams.\nSo if the journey feels too long,\nRemember this encouraging song:\nNo mountain stands forever high—\nThe brave will always reach the sky.','teacher'),
(1354,1354,'Title: Digital Footprints\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nEvery message that we send,\nEvery photo that we lend,\nLeaves a mark upon the way,\nLong beyond the passing day.\nKindness posted,\nTruth expressed,\nMakes the internet its best.\nWords can heal,\nOr words can harm,\nHolding either peace or alarm.\nThink before you click \"Send,\"\nChoose the message you defend.\nBuild a world that\'s safe and bright,\nGuided always by what\'s right.\nThough our screens may glow each night,\nCharacter remains our light.\nLet our footprints clearly show\nRespect wherever we may go.','teacher'),
(1355,1355,'Title: The Library Window\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nBeside the library window wide,\nCountless worlds are found inside.\nHeroes, oceans, stars, and kings,\nLive among the printed things.\nEvery shelf contains a door,\nOpening to something more.\nHistory whispers,\nScience sings,\nAdventure spreads its eager wings.\nA single book can change a mind,\nLeaving fear and doubt behind.\nReaders travel far each day\nThough they never move away.\nTurn each page with eager eyes,\nSeek the truth,\nBe brave,\nBe wise.\nFor every reader soon will find\nA stronger heart,\nA brighter mind.','teacher'),
(1356,1356,'Title: A Teacher\'s Gift\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 97\n\nA teacher plants a tiny seed,\nWith every lesson, thought, and deed.\nNot made of gold or precious stone,\nBut wisdom learners call their own.\nA word of praise, a guiding hand,\nCan help a fearful learner stand.\nA patient smile, a simple cheer,\nCan chase away uncertainty and fear.\nEach question asked, each answer found,\nMakes knowledge grow on fertile ground.\nThough years may pass and seasons shift,\nLearning remains a lasting gift.\nOne day the learner leads the way,\nHelping others every day.\nThe greatest treasure we can give\nIs teaching others how to live.','teacher'),
(1357,1357,'Title: The Silent River\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 95\n\nThe river never boasts aloud,\nNor seeks applause from any crowd.\nYet day by day it shapes the land\nWith quiet strength and patient hand.\nIt bends around the hardest stone,\nStill finding pathways of its own.\nIts waters teach us not to fight,\nBut gently choose the wiser right.\nThough storms may darken overhead,\nThe river steadily moves ahead.\nIt never stops because of fear;\nIts destination still is clear.\nLike rivers flowing to the sea,\nOur dreams become reality\nWhen steady hearts refuse to stray\nAnd choose to grow a little each day.','teacher'),
(1358,1358,'Title: Hope in the Storm\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nDark clouds gather in the sky,\nThunder rumbles rolling by.\nBranches bend beneath the rain,\nYet flowers bloom once more again.\nEvery storm will someday cease,\nGiving way to light and peace.\nCourage grows through every trial,\nOften hidden for a while.\nThough the winds may shake the trees,\nHope remains within the breeze.\nMorning always follows night,\nBringing warmth and golden light.\nSo when life seems hard to bear,\nKeep believing, keep your care.\nEven storms that make us strong\nCannot last forever long.','teacher'),
(1359,1359,'Title: Every Dream Matters\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 84\n\nSome dream high among the stars,\nOthers heal invisible scars.\nSome create amazing art,\nOthers serve with faithful heart.\nEvery dream deserves its place,\nEvery goal its patient race.\nGreat achievements always start\nFrom hope inside a willing heart.\nDreams are built through daily choice,\nThrough discipline and honest voice.\nStep by step the future grows,\nMore than anyone yet knows.\nHold your dream with courage true.\nLet determination carry you.\nFor every dream that lights the night\nCan change tomorrow with its light.','teacher'),
(1360,1360,'Title: We Rise Together\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nOne small hand can plant a tree,\nTwo can build community.\nMany hearts that work as one\nFinish what cannot be done.\nDifferent voices, different minds,\nEach unique in countless kinds.\nYet together we become\nStronger than we are alone.\nHelping others makes us grow.\nSharing all the things we know.\nKindness spreads from friend to friend,\nWith no limit, with no end.\nLet us build a brighter day,\nChoosing hope in every way.\nWhen we stand through every weather,\nWe discover—\nWe rise together.','teacher'),
(1361,1361,'Title: The Last Seat in the Classroom\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 260\n\nThe last seat in Room Seven was always empty until the second week of classes. On Monday morning, a quiet boy named Adrian entered the room carrying an old backpack and a neatly folded notebook. He smiled politely as the teacher introduced him to the class.\n\nSince all the front seats were already occupied, Adrian sat at the back. During lessons, he listened carefully and wrote detailed notes, but he rarely spoke. Some classmates assumed he was shy, while others simply forgot he was there.\n\nOne afternoon, the class was assigned a group science project. Adrian\'s group struggled to organize their ideas. They argued over the experiment and could not agree on who would present.\n\nAfter listening quietly, Adrian finally raised his hand. \"May I suggest something?\" he asked.\n\nHis classmates nodded.\n\nHe explained how they could divide the work fairly. He also showed them a simple experiment he had learned in his previous school. Everyone agreed to follow his plan.\n\nPresentation day arrived. Their experiment worked perfectly, and the class applauded their teamwork. Their teacher praised not only the project but also the way everyone cooperated.\n\nDuring recess, Adrian no longer sat alone. His classmates invited him to eat with them, asking questions about his old school and sharing stories about their own experiences.\n\nAdrian realized that sometimes people are not noticed because they remain silent. Yet kindness, preparation, and willingness to help often speak louder than words. From that day forward, the last seat in the classroom became known as the place where a quiet leader once sat.','teacher'),
(1362,1362,'Title: The New Student\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 227\n\nLena transferred to a new school halfway through the school year. Everything felt unfamiliar—from the classrooms to the faces she passed along the hallways. Although her teachers were welcoming, she found it difficult to begin conversations with her classmates.\n\nDuring lunch breaks, Lena usually sat alone beneath a large acacia tree near the school garden. She spent her time reading novels while other students laughed and played nearby.\n\nOne rainy afternoon, the school announced a clean-up drive after classes. Every section was assigned an area to organize. Lena quietly joined her classmates in clearing fallen leaves and arranging flower pots.\n\nAs they worked together, one of her classmates noticed that Lena knew the names of many different plants. Curious, they asked how she learned so much.\n\n\"My grandmother loves gardening,\" Lena explained. \"She taught me how to care for flowers and vegetables.\"\n\nSoon everyone was asking questions. Lena demonstrated how to prepare seedlings and explained why certain plants needed more sunlight than others.\n\nThe following week, their adviser appointed Lena as one of the leaders of the class gardening project. Her classmates eagerly worked beside her, planting vegetables and flowering shrubs that later brightened the school grounds.\n\nLena realized that friendships often begin with shared work rather than shared words. By simply offering her knowledge and helping others, she found a place where she truly belonged.','teacher'),
(1363,1363,'Title: One Honest Decision\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 238\n\nIt was dismissal time when Joshua noticed a black wallet lying near the school gate. Students hurried past without seeing it. He picked it up and looked around, hoping someone would return to claim it.\n\nInside the wallet were several identification cards, a few receipts, and a considerable amount of money. Joshua immediately realized that someone must be worried about losing it.\n\nHis friends suggested different ideas. One jokingly said, \"Nobody saw you. Just keep it.\"\n\nJoshua smiled but shook his head. \"If this belonged to my father, I would hope someone returned it.\"\n\nInstead of going home immediately, he brought the wallet to the principal\'s office. The school staff checked the identification card and contacted the owner, who happened to be the father of one of the students.\n\nLess than an hour later, the grateful owner arrived. He counted the money and found that nothing was missing.\n\n\"You have saved me from many problems today,\" the man said warmly. \"This money was meant to pay our workers.\"\n\nHe offered Joshua a reward, but the boy politely declined.\n\n\"My parents taught me that honesty is worth more than money,\" Joshua replied.\n\nThe principal later shared Joshua\'s story during the Monday flag ceremony. His classmates applauded as he received a Certificate of Good Citizenship.\n\nJoshua discovered that doing the right thing may not always bring material rewards, but it earns something even greater—the trust and respect of others.','teacher'),
(1364,1364,'Title: The Forgotten Library Card\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 198\n\nEvery Wednesday afternoon, the Grade 8 learners visited the school library. It had become one of Sophia\'s favorite places because it offered more than books. It provided quiet spaces where she could think, study, and imagine.\n\nOne afternoon, she eagerly searched for a science encyclopedia recommended by her teacher. After finding the book, she walked toward the circulation desk.\n\nThen she realized something.\n\nHer library card was missing.\n\nShe searched every pocket of her bag, checked her notebooks, and even looked beneath the tables. It was nowhere to be found.\n\nFeeling disappointed, Sophia informed the librarian, who calmly smiled.\n\n\"Sometimes,\" the librarian said, \"finding something begins by remembering your last careful moment.\"\n\nSophia retraced her steps. She remembered reviewing her assignment under a large narra tree before entering the library.\n\nWhen she returned outside, she found the card exactly where she had placed it between the pages of her notebook.\n\nRelieved, she borrowed the encyclopedia and thanked the librarian.\n\nThat afternoon she learned that careful habits save both time and worry. Since then, she kept all important school cards inside a small transparent organizer attached to her bag.\n\nSmall acts of organization, she realized, often prevent bigger problems.','teacher'),
(1365,1365,'Title: The Science Fair Challenge\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 195\n\nThe annual Science Fair was only three weeks away, and excitement filled the campus. Every Grade 8 section had been challenged to present an invention that solved a community problem.\n\nGroup Three decided to create a simple rainwater collection system that could help households conserve water during the dry season.\n\nAt first, everything seemed easy. However, the prototype leaked during testing.\n\nSome members became discouraged.\n\n\"We should change our project,\" one student suggested.\n\nTheir team leader, Camille, disagreed.\n\n\"Real scientists don\'t stop after one failure,\" she said. \"Every mistake teaches us something.\"\n\nThe group carefully examined the design. They discovered that one connector pipe was slightly loose. After making adjustments and sealing the joints properly, they tested the system again.\n\nThis time, the container successfully collected rainwater without leaking.\n\nOn presentation day, the judges praised not only the invention but also the team\'s explanation of the scientific process behind their improvements.\n\nAlthough they placed second overall, the judges selected their project to be displayed during the municipal science exhibit.\n\nCamille smiled as she looked at her teammates.\n\n\"We didn\'t succeed because everything worked the first time,\" she said.\n\n\"We succeeded because we never stopped improving.\"','teacher'),
(1366,1366,'Title: A Letter to My Future Self\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 194\n\nAs part of their English class activity, each learner was asked to write a letter addressed to themselves ten years in the future.\n\nMost students wrote about the careers they hoped to pursue. Some dreamed of becoming engineers, doctors, teachers, artists, or entrepreneurs.\n\nMiguel stared at his blank paper for several minutes before writing his first sentence.\n\n\"Dear Future Me,\n\nI hope you still remember the promises you made when you were fourteen.\"\n\nHe continued writing about the values he wanted to keep—honesty, kindness, humility, and perseverance.\n\nHe reminded himself that success should never be measured only by money or awards, but also by the number of lives positively influenced.\n\nBefore sealing the letter inside an envelope, he added one final paragraph.\n\n\"If life becomes difficult, remember the dreams that inspired you to begin. Keep learning, keep serving, and never stop believing that every day is another opportunity to become a better person.\"\n\nThe teacher collected all the letters and promised to return them during the learners\' graduation from senior high school.\n\nAs Miguel walked home, he smiled.\n\nPerhaps the most important promise we ever make is the one we make to ourselves.','teacher'),
(1367,1367,'Title: The Volunteer\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 202\n\nA powerful typhoon had passed through the municipality, leaving many families in need of assistance. Several classrooms were damaged, trees blocked the roads, and many homes lost electricity.\n\nThe school organized a volunteer day to help clean the campus and prepare learning spaces for classes to resume.\n\nAmong the volunteers was Aaron, a Grade 7 learner who had never joined a community service activity before.\n\nHe expected the work to be tiring, and it certainly was.\n\nStudents swept classrooms, gathered broken branches, arranged donated books, and repainted faded signboards.\n\nDuring lunch, Aaron noticed an elderly maintenance worker struggling to carry several heavy boxes.\n\nWithout being asked, Aaron immediately helped him.\n\nThe man smiled gratefully.\n\n\"You remind me that kindness is strongest when nobody asks for it,\" he said.\n\nBy late afternoon, the school grounds looked cleaner than they had before the storm.\n\nAlthough Aaron returned home physically exhausted, he felt deeply satisfied.\n\nThat evening, he reflected on the day\'s work.\n\nVolunteering had taught him that serving the community is not measured by the size of one\'s contribution but by the willingness to offer time, effort, and compassion.\n\nSometimes the greatest reward for helping others is discovering that we have grown stronger ourselves.','teacher'),
(1368,1368,'Title: The Lost Wallet\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 246\n\nFriday afternoons were always busy at Manolo Integrated High School. Students hurried toward the school gate while others waited for their parents or rode the last jeepneys home.\n\nAs Claire walked across the covered court, she noticed a brown wallet beneath one of the benches. She picked it up and looked around, but no one seemed to be searching for it.\n\nInside were several identification cards, a driver\'s license, receipts, and enough cash to pay for a week\'s groceries. Claire immediately imagined how worried the owner must be.\n\nHer friend suggested taking the wallet to the nearest store because someone there might recognize its owner. Another classmate thought they should wait outside the gate.\n\nClaire remembered what their Values Education teacher often said: \"When you find something valuable, place it in the hands of someone who is responsible.\"\n\nShe went directly to the Guidance Office. The guidance counselor carefully examined the identification card and discovered that the wallet belonged to the father of a Grade 8 learner.\n\nWithin thirty minutes, the grateful parent arrived.\n\n\"I thought I had lost this forever,\" he said with relief. \"This contains my salary for the week.\"\n\nHe offered Claire a reward, but she politely refused.\n\n\"My reward is knowing everything was returned safely,\" she replied.\n\nDuring the next flag ceremony, the school recognized Claire for her honesty.\n\nHer classmates learned that integrity is demonstrated not only during difficult moments but also through simple decisions that no one may ever see.','teacher'),
(1369,1369,'Title: Race Against Time\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nOnly two days remained before the Regional Robotics Competition, and Team Innovate was still struggling with their robot\'s navigation program.\n\nEvery afternoon after classes, the four members met inside the computer laboratory. While other students were already heading home, they continued testing sensors, correcting computer code, and redesigning small mechanical parts.\n\nSometimes the robot stopped without warning. At other times, it turned in the wrong direction or failed to recognize obstacles.\n\n\"We\'re running out of time,\" Ethan sighed.\n\nTheir adviser smiled calmly.\n\n\"Success rarely belongs to those who never fail,\" she said. \"It belongs to those who learn quickly from every failure.\"\n\nThe group divided the remaining tasks. Ethan corrected the programming, Mia recalibrated the sensors, Joshua improved the wheels, while Hannah documented every modification.\n\nLate Friday evening, they performed one final test.\n\nThe robot moved smoothly through every checkpoint without making a single mistake.\n\nAlthough the competition was challenging, Team Innovate proudly earned second place.\n\nAs they packed their equipment, Ethan realized that winning a medal was rewarding, but learning perseverance, teamwork, and problem-solving was an even greater achievement.','teacher'),
(1370,1370,'Title: The School Garden\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nAt the beginning of the school year, the vacant lot beside the library looked dry and neglected. Weeds covered the ground, and very few students paid attention to it.\n\nTheir Science teacher proposed a challenge.\n\n\"What if we transformed this empty space into a vegetable garden?\"\n\nAt first, many students doubted the idea.\n\nThe soil looked rocky, and the weather had been unusually hot.\n\nNevertheless, the class accepted the challenge.\n\nEvery Wednesday morning, they watered seedlings, removed weeds, added compost, and measured plant growth.\n\nSome vegetables grew quickly, while others struggled.\n\nThe students carefully observed how sunlight, rainfall, and soil nutrients affected each plant.\n\nAfter three months, the once-empty lot had become a colorful garden filled with tomatoes, eggplants, pechay, okra, and herbs.\n\nThe harvested vegetables were donated to the school\'s feeding program.\n\nDuring the recognition ceremony, the principal congratulated the class.\n\n\"You did not simply grow vegetables,\" she said.\n\n\"You cultivated responsibility, patience, and teamwork.\"\n\nThe students proudly realized that meaningful change often begins with small acts repeated consistently.','teacher'),
(1371,1371,'Title: The Community Hero\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nEvery morning before sunrise, Mang Ernesto could already be seen sweeping the streets surrounding the public market.\n\nMany people knew him simply as the town\'s sanitation worker. Few realized that he had been serving the community for more than twenty-five years.\n\nDespite the hot weather and occasional heavy rain, he reported for work every day with a smile.\n\nOne Saturday, Grade 8 learners interviewed him for their Araling Panlipunan project about community helpers.\n\n\"What motivates you to continue this work?\" one student asked.\n\nMang Ernesto smiled before answering.\n\n\"When people wake up and find clean streets, they can begin their day safely and comfortably. Knowing that my work helps others is enough reason to continue.\"\n\nThe students listened attentively.\n\nThey realized that heroism is not always found in newspapers or television.\n\nSometimes heroes quietly perform ordinary tasks with extraordinary dedication.\n\nInspired by the interview, the class organized a monthly clean-up campaign within their barangay.\n\nMang Ernesto happily joined them.\n\nBy working together, the learners discovered that every citizen has the power to improve the community through simple acts of service.','teacher'),
(1372,1372,'Title: Beyond the Screen\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 168\n\nLike many teenagers, Daniel enjoyed spending hours playing online games and watching videos after school.\n\nAt first, he believed there was nothing wrong with it.\n\nHowever, he gradually noticed several changes.\n\nHe stayed up late every night.\n\nHe arrived at school feeling sleepy.\n\nHis assignments became incomplete, and his quiz scores slowly declined.\n\nOne afternoon, his older sister invited him to join a weekend hiking activity organized by their youth group.\n\nDaniel hesitated.\n\nHe almost refused.\n\nStill, he decided to give it a try.\n\nThe hike led them through forest trails, rivers, and scenic viewpoints.\n\nWithout realizing it, Daniel spent an entire day without checking his phone.\n\nInstead, he laughed with new friends, learned about native trees, and appreciated the beauty of nature.\n\nThat evening he reflected on his experience.\n\nTechnology is useful, he realized, but balance is essential.\n\nSince then, Daniel scheduled daily screen time while making room for reading, exercise, family conversations, and outdoor activities.\n\nLife beyond the screen offered experiences no device could fully replace.','teacher'),
(1373,1373,'Title: The Basketball Captain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nThe championship game was only minutes away.\n\nEveryone expected Team Falcons to win because they had remained undefeated throughout the season.\n\nAs captain, Marcus felt tremendous pressure.\n\nWhen the first quarter ended, however, his team trailed by twelve points.\n\nSome players began blaming one another for missed shots and defensive mistakes.\n\nDuring the timeout, Marcus gathered everyone into a circle.\n\n\"We cannot change the first quarter,\" he said calmly.\n\n\"But we can decide how we play the next three.\"\n\nHis teammates nodded.\n\nInstead of focusing on individual scoring, they emphasized teamwork.\n\nThey passed the ball more carefully, communicated during defense, and encouraged one another after every play.\n\nLittle by little, the gap narrowed.\n\nWith only fifteen seconds remaining, the score was tied.\n\nMarcus passed the ball to the newest member of the team, who made the winning basket.\n\nThe crowd erupted with applause.\n\nAfter the game, reporters asked Marcus why he did not take the final shot himself.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"A captain\'s greatest victory is helping the entire team succeed.\"','teacher'),
(1374,1374,'Title: The Secret of the Old Mango Tree\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 269\n\nThe oldest tree inside San Isidro National High School stood quietly behind the library. Its thick trunk and wide branches had provided shade for generations of learners. Teachers often joked that the tree had witnessed more examinations, graduations, and celebrations than anyone else in the school.\n\nOne afternoon, while researching for a local history project, three Grade 8 learners noticed a small metal box partially hidden beneath one of the tree\'s large roots. Curious but careful, they informed their adviser before touching it.\n\nTogether, they carefully removed the box from the soil. Inside they found several yellowed photographs, handwritten letters, and an old class program dated more than forty years earlier.\n\nThe school principal contacted retired teachers and alumni to help identify the people shown in the photographs. During the following week, former students visited the school and shared stories about their experiences as learners many years ago.\n\nThey spoke about studying without computers, participating in school programs under the same mango tree, and helping build classrooms through community volunteer work.\n\nThe learners listened with great interest.\n\nThey realized that although school buildings, technology, and teaching methods continue to change, the values of respect, perseverance, and service remain constant through every generation.\n\nInspired by what they had learned, the Student Government organized a Heritage Corner inside the library. The photographs, letters, and stories were carefully preserved so future learners could appreciate the rich history of their school.\n\nWhenever students gathered beneath the old mango tree, they no longer saw only its strong branches and broad leaves.\n\nThey saw living proof that every generation leaves behind lessons for the next.','teacher'),
(1375,1375,'Title: A New Beginning\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 233\n\nGraduation day had finally arrived.\n\nRows of proud parents filled the covered court while graduates adjusted their togas and smiled nervously at one another.\n\nAmong them sat Nicole, quietly remembering her first day in junior high school.\n\nShe had once been afraid to answer questions in class.\n\nReading aloud made her anxious, and group presentations seemed impossible.\n\nLittle by little, everything changed.\n\nHer teachers patiently guided her.\n\nHer classmates encouraged her to participate.\n\nShe joined reading activities, accepted leadership roles, and volunteered during school programs.\n\nEach experience strengthened her confidence.\n\nWhen Nicole\'s name was called to receive the Leadership Award, the audience applauded warmly.\n\nAs she walked across the stage, she remembered every challenge she had overcome—not because life had become easier, but because she had become stronger.\n\nAfter the ceremony, a Grade 7 learner approached her.\n\n\"Ate Nicole,\" he asked, \"what advice can you give someone who is afraid of making mistakes?\"\n\nNicole smiled.\n\n\"Never let fear stop you from trying,\" she replied. \"Every mistake teaches something valuable. Every challenge prepares you for a greater opportunity.\"\n\nYears later, Nicole hoped to become a teacher. She wanted to help young learners discover what her own teachers had helped her realize:\n\nEducation is not simply about earning high grades.\n\nIt is about becoming a person who can improve the lives of others.\n\nEvery ending, she believed, is simply the beginning of another journey.','teacher'),
(1376,1376,'Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','teacher'),
(1377,1377,'Title: Finding My Voice\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 238\n\nAt the beginning of the school year, Aaron preferred listening instead of speaking. Whenever teachers asked volunteers to read aloud, he lowered his head and hoped someone else would be chosen. He worried that his classmates might laugh if he mispronounced a difficult word.\n\nHis English teacher noticed his hesitation and encouraged him to spend fifteen minutes each day reading aloud at home. At first, Aaron struggled with unfamiliar vocabulary and paused frequently. However, he continued practicing every evening.\n\nAfter several weeks, reading became easier. He learned to recognize punctuation marks, read with better expression, and pronounce longer words correctly. His confidence gradually increased.\n\nOne day, the school announced a storytelling contest. Although Aaron felt nervous, he decided to participate. He practiced faithfully before his parents, younger sister, and even in front of a mirror.\n\nDuring the contest, Aaron spoke clearly and confidently. He remembered to pause at commas, change his tone during conversations, and emphasize important ideas. The audience listened attentively from beginning to end.\n\nAlthough another learner won first place, Aaron received a special award for Most Improved Reader.\n\nWalking back to his seat, he realized that success is not always measured by trophies. Sometimes it is measured by the courage to do something that once seemed impossible.\n\nFrom that day forward, Aaron no longer feared reading aloud. Instead, he looked forward to every opportunity to learn, improve, and inspire others through the power of words.','teacher'),
(1378,1378,'Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','teacher'),
(1379,1379,'Title: The Bridge to Tomorrow\nWords: 181\n\nWhen fourteen-year-old Marco transferred to his new school, everything seemed unfamiliar. The classrooms were larger, the teachers spoke faster, and the students already had their own groups of friends. During his first week, he preferred sitting quietly at the back of the room, hoping no one would notice him.\n\nOne morning, their English teacher announced a reading activity. Each learner would read a passage aloud before the class. Marco\'s heart beat rapidly. He enjoyed reading silently, but speaking before others made him nervous.\n\nWhen his turn came, he stood slowly and held the reading selection with trembling hands. He took a deep breath and began reading. At first, his voice was soft, but after several sentences he noticed that his classmates were listening attentively. His teacher smiled encouragingly whenever he paused correctly or pronounced a difficult word clearly.\n\nAfter the activity, several classmates congratulated him. One even asked if they could become partners for the next reading assignment. Marco realized that confidence grows through practice. Every page he read became another step across the bridge toward new friendships and greater opportunities.','teacher'),
(1380,1380,'Title: The Voice Within\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 146\n\nThere is a quiet voice inside,\nA faithful friend, a trusted guide.\nIt tells us when to stand up tall,\nTo help someone who starts to fall.\nIt whispers, \"Choose the path that\'s right,\nEven when it\'s out of sight.\"\nIt gives us courage, calm, and grace\nTo meet each challenge that we face.\nSometimes storms may cloud the day,\nAnd doubts may try to block the way.\nYet deep within, that gentle light\nStill shines to guide us through the night.\nA dream begins with one small step,\nWith every promise wisely kept.\nSuccess is built on what we do,\nOn honest hearts that stay sincere and true.\nListen well when choices call.\nYour inner voice can conquer all.\nWith hope and faith beside your way,\nYou\'ll grow much stronger every day.\nBelieve your voice.\nBelieve your dream.\nBecome much more\nThan you may seem.','teacher'),
(1381,1381,'Title: Tomorrow Begins Today\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 103\n\nTomorrow waits beyond the hill,\nPrepared for those with strength and will.\nIt does not ask how rich you are,\nNor where you live, nor near nor far.\nIt simply asks,\n\"Will you prepare?\nWill you keep learning everywhere?\"\nA page today,\nA book tonight,\nCan help your future shine more bright.\nMistakes may come,\nAnd plans may bend,\nYet every challenge has an end.\nEach sunrise brings another chance\nTo learn,\nTo lead,\nTo dream,\nTo dance.\nSo build your future,\nBrick by brick,\nWith patient hands that never quit.\nTomorrow starts\nWith what you do—\nAnd all tomorrow\nStarts with you.','teacher'),
(1382,1382,'Title: Beyond the Mountain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 96\n\nBeyond the mountain stands a dream,\nBrighter than the morning gleam.\nMany travelers lose their way,\nYet hopeful hearts decide to stay.\nThey climb through rain,\nThey climb through heat,\nWith tired hands and aching feet.\nEach rocky trail,\nEach winding road,\nMakes lighter every heavy load.\nThe summit waits for those who try,\nWho never fear to ask, \"Why?\"\nFor every step, though small it seems,\nLeads us closer to our dreams.\nSo if the journey feels too long,\nRemember this encouraging song:\nNo mountain stands forever high—\nThe brave will always reach the sky.','teacher'),
(1383,1383,'Title: Digital Footprints\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nEvery message that we send,\nEvery photo that we lend,\nLeaves a mark upon the way,\nLong beyond the passing day.\nKindness posted,\nTruth expressed,\nMakes the internet its best.\nWords can heal,\nOr words can harm,\nHolding either peace or alarm.\nThink before you click \"Send,\"\nChoose the message you defend.\nBuild a world that\'s safe and bright,\nGuided always by what\'s right.\nThough our screens may glow each night,\nCharacter remains our light.\nLet our footprints clearly show\nRespect wherever we may go.','teacher'),
(1384,1384,'Title: The Library Window\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nBeside the library window wide,\nCountless worlds are found inside.\nHeroes, oceans, stars, and kings,\nLive among the printed things.\nEvery shelf contains a door,\nOpening to something more.\nHistory whispers,\nScience sings,\nAdventure spreads its eager wings.\nA single book can change a mind,\nLeaving fear and doubt behind.\nReaders travel far each day\nThough they never move away.\nTurn each page with eager eyes,\nSeek the truth,\nBe brave,\nBe wise.\nFor every reader soon will find\nA stronger heart,\nA brighter mind.','teacher'),
(1385,1385,'Title: A Teacher\'s Gift\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 97\n\nA teacher plants a tiny seed,\nWith every lesson, thought, and deed.\nNot made of gold or precious stone,\nBut wisdom learners call their own.\nA word of praise, a guiding hand,\nCan help a fearful learner stand.\nA patient smile, a simple cheer,\nCan chase away uncertainty and fear.\nEach question asked, each answer found,\nMakes knowledge grow on fertile ground.\nThough years may pass and seasons shift,\nLearning remains a lasting gift.\nOne day the learner leads the way,\nHelping others every day.\nThe greatest treasure we can give\nIs teaching others how to live.','teacher'),
(1386,1386,'Title: The Silent River\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 95\n\nThe river never boasts aloud,\nNor seeks applause from any crowd.\nYet day by day it shapes the land\nWith quiet strength and patient hand.\nIt bends around the hardest stone,\nStill finding pathways of its own.\nIts waters teach us not to fight,\nBut gently choose the wiser right.\nThough storms may darken overhead,\nThe river steadily moves ahead.\nIt never stops because of fear;\nIts destination still is clear.\nLike rivers flowing to the sea,\nOur dreams become reality\nWhen steady hearts refuse to stray\nAnd choose to grow a little each day.','teacher'),
(1387,1387,'Title: Hope in the Storm\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nDark clouds gather in the sky,\nThunder rumbles rolling by.\nBranches bend beneath the rain,\nYet flowers bloom once more again.\nEvery storm will someday cease,\nGiving way to light and peace.\nCourage grows through every trial,\nOften hidden for a while.\nThough the winds may shake the trees,\nHope remains within the breeze.\nMorning always follows night,\nBringing warmth and golden light.\nSo when life seems hard to bear,\nKeep believing, keep your care.\nEven storms that make us strong\nCannot last forever long.','teacher'),
(1388,1388,'Title: Every Dream Matters\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 84\n\nSome dream high among the stars,\nOthers heal invisible scars.\nSome create amazing art,\nOthers serve with faithful heart.\nEvery dream deserves its place,\nEvery goal its patient race.\nGreat achievements always start\nFrom hope inside a willing heart.\nDreams are built through daily choice,\nThrough discipline and honest voice.\nStep by step the future grows,\nMore than anyone yet knows.\nHold your dream with courage true.\nLet determination carry you.\nFor every dream that lights the night\nCan change tomorrow with its light.','teacher'),
(1389,1389,'Title: We Rise Together\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nOne small hand can plant a tree,\nTwo can build community.\nMany hearts that work as one\nFinish what cannot be done.\nDifferent voices, different minds,\nEach unique in countless kinds.\nYet together we become\nStronger than we are alone.\nHelping others makes us grow.\nSharing all the things we know.\nKindness spreads from friend to friend,\nWith no limit, with no end.\nLet us build a brighter day,\nChoosing hope in every way.\nWhen we stand through every weather,\nWe discover—\nWe rise together.','teacher'),
(1390,1390,'Title: The Last Seat in the Classroom\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 260\n\nThe last seat in Room Seven was always empty until the second week of classes. On Monday morning, a quiet boy named Adrian entered the room carrying an old backpack and a neatly folded notebook. He smiled politely as the teacher introduced him to the class.\n\nSince all the front seats were already occupied, Adrian sat at the back. During lessons, he listened carefully and wrote detailed notes, but he rarely spoke. Some classmates assumed he was shy, while others simply forgot he was there.\n\nOne afternoon, the class was assigned a group science project. Adrian\'s group struggled to organize their ideas. They argued over the experiment and could not agree on who would present.\n\nAfter listening quietly, Adrian finally raised his hand. \"May I suggest something?\" he asked.\n\nHis classmates nodded.\n\nHe explained how they could divide the work fairly. He also showed them a simple experiment he had learned in his previous school. Everyone agreed to follow his plan.\n\nPresentation day arrived. Their experiment worked perfectly, and the class applauded their teamwork. Their teacher praised not only the project but also the way everyone cooperated.\n\nDuring recess, Adrian no longer sat alone. His classmates invited him to eat with them, asking questions about his old school and sharing stories about their own experiences.\n\nAdrian realized that sometimes people are not noticed because they remain silent. Yet kindness, preparation, and willingness to help often speak louder than words. From that day forward, the last seat in the classroom became known as the place where a quiet leader once sat.','teacher'),
(1391,1391,'Title: The New Student\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 227\n\nLena transferred to a new school halfway through the school year. Everything felt unfamiliar—from the classrooms to the faces she passed along the hallways. Although her teachers were welcoming, she found it difficult to begin conversations with her classmates.\n\nDuring lunch breaks, Lena usually sat alone beneath a large acacia tree near the school garden. She spent her time reading novels while other students laughed and played nearby.\n\nOne rainy afternoon, the school announced a clean-up drive after classes. Every section was assigned an area to organize. Lena quietly joined her classmates in clearing fallen leaves and arranging flower pots.\n\nAs they worked together, one of her classmates noticed that Lena knew the names of many different plants. Curious, they asked how she learned so much.\n\n\"My grandmother loves gardening,\" Lena explained. \"She taught me how to care for flowers and vegetables.\"\n\nSoon everyone was asking questions. Lena demonstrated how to prepare seedlings and explained why certain plants needed more sunlight than others.\n\nThe following week, their adviser appointed Lena as one of the leaders of the class gardening project. Her classmates eagerly worked beside her, planting vegetables and flowering shrubs that later brightened the school grounds.\n\nLena realized that friendships often begin with shared work rather than shared words. By simply offering her knowledge and helping others, she found a place where she truly belonged.','teacher'),
(1392,1392,'Title: One Honest Decision\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 238\n\nIt was dismissal time when Joshua noticed a black wallet lying near the school gate. Students hurried past without seeing it. He picked it up and looked around, hoping someone would return to claim it.\n\nInside the wallet were several identification cards, a few receipts, and a considerable amount of money. Joshua immediately realized that someone must be worried about losing it.\n\nHis friends suggested different ideas. One jokingly said, \"Nobody saw you. Just keep it.\"\n\nJoshua smiled but shook his head. \"If this belonged to my father, I would hope someone returned it.\"\n\nInstead of going home immediately, he brought the wallet to the principal\'s office. The school staff checked the identification card and contacted the owner, who happened to be the father of one of the students.\n\nLess than an hour later, the grateful owner arrived. He counted the money and found that nothing was missing.\n\n\"You have saved me from many problems today,\" the man said warmly. \"This money was meant to pay our workers.\"\n\nHe offered Joshua a reward, but the boy politely declined.\n\n\"My parents taught me that honesty is worth more than money,\" Joshua replied.\n\nThe principal later shared Joshua\'s story during the Monday flag ceremony. His classmates applauded as he received a Certificate of Good Citizenship.\n\nJoshua discovered that doing the right thing may not always bring material rewards, but it earns something even greater—the trust and respect of others.','teacher'),
(1393,1393,'Title: The Forgotten Library Card\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 198\n\nEvery Wednesday afternoon, the Grade 8 learners visited the school library. It had become one of Sophia\'s favorite places because it offered more than books. It provided quiet spaces where she could think, study, and imagine.\n\nOne afternoon, she eagerly searched for a science encyclopedia recommended by her teacher. After finding the book, she walked toward the circulation desk.\n\nThen she realized something.\n\nHer library card was missing.\n\nShe searched every pocket of her bag, checked her notebooks, and even looked beneath the tables. It was nowhere to be found.\n\nFeeling disappointed, Sophia informed the librarian, who calmly smiled.\n\n\"Sometimes,\" the librarian said, \"finding something begins by remembering your last careful moment.\"\n\nSophia retraced her steps. She remembered reviewing her assignment under a large narra tree before entering the library.\n\nWhen she returned outside, she found the card exactly where she had placed it between the pages of her notebook.\n\nRelieved, she borrowed the encyclopedia and thanked the librarian.\n\nThat afternoon she learned that careful habits save both time and worry. Since then, she kept all important school cards inside a small transparent organizer attached to her bag.\n\nSmall acts of organization, she realized, often prevent bigger problems.','teacher'),
(1394,1394,'Title: The Science Fair Challenge\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 195\n\nThe annual Science Fair was only three weeks away, and excitement filled the campus. Every Grade 8 section had been challenged to present an invention that solved a community problem.\n\nGroup Three decided to create a simple rainwater collection system that could help households conserve water during the dry season.\n\nAt first, everything seemed easy. However, the prototype leaked during testing.\n\nSome members became discouraged.\n\n\"We should change our project,\" one student suggested.\n\nTheir team leader, Camille, disagreed.\n\n\"Real scientists don\'t stop after one failure,\" she said. \"Every mistake teaches us something.\"\n\nThe group carefully examined the design. They discovered that one connector pipe was slightly loose. After making adjustments and sealing the joints properly, they tested the system again.\n\nThis time, the container successfully collected rainwater without leaking.\n\nOn presentation day, the judges praised not only the invention but also the team\'s explanation of the scientific process behind their improvements.\n\nAlthough they placed second overall, the judges selected their project to be displayed during the municipal science exhibit.\n\nCamille smiled as she looked at her teammates.\n\n\"We didn\'t succeed because everything worked the first time,\" she said.\n\n\"We succeeded because we never stopped improving.\"','teacher'),
(1395,1395,'Title: A Letter to My Future Self\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 194\n\nAs part of their English class activity, each learner was asked to write a letter addressed to themselves ten years in the future.\n\nMost students wrote about the careers they hoped to pursue. Some dreamed of becoming engineers, doctors, teachers, artists, or entrepreneurs.\n\nMiguel stared at his blank paper for several minutes before writing his first sentence.\n\n\"Dear Future Me,\n\nI hope you still remember the promises you made when you were fourteen.\"\n\nHe continued writing about the values he wanted to keep—honesty, kindness, humility, and perseverance.\n\nHe reminded himself that success should never be measured only by money or awards, but also by the number of lives positively influenced.\n\nBefore sealing the letter inside an envelope, he added one final paragraph.\n\n\"If life becomes difficult, remember the dreams that inspired you to begin. Keep learning, keep serving, and never stop believing that every day is another opportunity to become a better person.\"\n\nThe teacher collected all the letters and promised to return them during the learners\' graduation from senior high school.\n\nAs Miguel walked home, he smiled.\n\nPerhaps the most important promise we ever make is the one we make to ourselves.','teacher'),
(1396,1396,'Title: The Volunteer\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 202\n\nA powerful typhoon had passed through the municipality, leaving many families in need of assistance. Several classrooms were damaged, trees blocked the roads, and many homes lost electricity.\n\nThe school organized a volunteer day to help clean the campus and prepare learning spaces for classes to resume.\n\nAmong the volunteers was Aaron, a Grade 7 learner who had never joined a community service activity before.\n\nHe expected the work to be tiring, and it certainly was.\n\nStudents swept classrooms, gathered broken branches, arranged donated books, and repainted faded signboards.\n\nDuring lunch, Aaron noticed an elderly maintenance worker struggling to carry several heavy boxes.\n\nWithout being asked, Aaron immediately helped him.\n\nThe man smiled gratefully.\n\n\"You remind me that kindness is strongest when nobody asks for it,\" he said.\n\nBy late afternoon, the school grounds looked cleaner than they had before the storm.\n\nAlthough Aaron returned home physically exhausted, he felt deeply satisfied.\n\nThat evening, he reflected on the day\'s work.\n\nVolunteering had taught him that serving the community is not measured by the size of one\'s contribution but by the willingness to offer time, effort, and compassion.\n\nSometimes the greatest reward for helping others is discovering that we have grown stronger ourselves.','teacher'),
(1397,1397,'Title: The Lost Wallet\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 246\n\nFriday afternoons were always busy at Manolo Integrated High School. Students hurried toward the school gate while others waited for their parents or rode the last jeepneys home.\n\nAs Claire walked across the covered court, she noticed a brown wallet beneath one of the benches. She picked it up and looked around, but no one seemed to be searching for it.\n\nInside were several identification cards, a driver\'s license, receipts, and enough cash to pay for a week\'s groceries. Claire immediately imagined how worried the owner must be.\n\nHer friend suggested taking the wallet to the nearest store because someone there might recognize its owner. Another classmate thought they should wait outside the gate.\n\nClaire remembered what their Values Education teacher often said: \"When you find something valuable, place it in the hands of someone who is responsible.\"\n\nShe went directly to the Guidance Office. The guidance counselor carefully examined the identification card and discovered that the wallet belonged to the father of a Grade 8 learner.\n\nWithin thirty minutes, the grateful parent arrived.\n\n\"I thought I had lost this forever,\" he said with relief. \"This contains my salary for the week.\"\n\nHe offered Claire a reward, but she politely refused.\n\n\"My reward is knowing everything was returned safely,\" she replied.\n\nDuring the next flag ceremony, the school recognized Claire for her honesty.\n\nHer classmates learned that integrity is demonstrated not only during difficult moments but also through simple decisions that no one may ever see.','teacher'),
(1398,1398,'Title: Race Against Time\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nOnly two days remained before the Regional Robotics Competition, and Team Innovate was still struggling with their robot\'s navigation program.\n\nEvery afternoon after classes, the four members met inside the computer laboratory. While other students were already heading home, they continued testing sensors, correcting computer code, and redesigning small mechanical parts.\n\nSometimes the robot stopped without warning. At other times, it turned in the wrong direction or failed to recognize obstacles.\n\n\"We\'re running out of time,\" Ethan sighed.\n\nTheir adviser smiled calmly.\n\n\"Success rarely belongs to those who never fail,\" she said. \"It belongs to those who learn quickly from every failure.\"\n\nThe group divided the remaining tasks. Ethan corrected the programming, Mia recalibrated the sensors, Joshua improved the wheels, while Hannah documented every modification.\n\nLate Friday evening, they performed one final test.\n\nThe robot moved smoothly through every checkpoint without making a single mistake.\n\nAlthough the competition was challenging, Team Innovate proudly earned second place.\n\nAs they packed their equipment, Ethan realized that winning a medal was rewarding, but learning perseverance, teamwork, and problem-solving was an even greater achievement.','teacher'),
(1399,1399,'Title: The School Garden\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nAt the beginning of the school year, the vacant lot beside the library looked dry and neglected. Weeds covered the ground, and very few students paid attention to it.\n\nTheir Science teacher proposed a challenge.\n\n\"What if we transformed this empty space into a vegetable garden?\"\n\nAt first, many students doubted the idea.\n\nThe soil looked rocky, and the weather had been unusually hot.\n\nNevertheless, the class accepted the challenge.\n\nEvery Wednesday morning, they watered seedlings, removed weeds, added compost, and measured plant growth.\n\nSome vegetables grew quickly, while others struggled.\n\nThe students carefully observed how sunlight, rainfall, and soil nutrients affected each plant.\n\nAfter three months, the once-empty lot had become a colorful garden filled with tomatoes, eggplants, pechay, okra, and herbs.\n\nThe harvested vegetables were donated to the school\'s feeding program.\n\nDuring the recognition ceremony, the principal congratulated the class.\n\n\"You did not simply grow vegetables,\" she said.\n\n\"You cultivated responsibility, patience, and teamwork.\"\n\nThe students proudly realized that meaningful change often begins with small acts repeated consistently.','teacher'),
(1400,1400,'Title: The Community Hero\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nEvery morning before sunrise, Mang Ernesto could already be seen sweeping the streets surrounding the public market.\n\nMany people knew him simply as the town\'s sanitation worker. Few realized that he had been serving the community for more than twenty-five years.\n\nDespite the hot weather and occasional heavy rain, he reported for work every day with a smile.\n\nOne Saturday, Grade 8 learners interviewed him for their Araling Panlipunan project about community helpers.\n\n\"What motivates you to continue this work?\" one student asked.\n\nMang Ernesto smiled before answering.\n\n\"When people wake up and find clean streets, they can begin their day safely and comfortably. Knowing that my work helps others is enough reason to continue.\"\n\nThe students listened attentively.\n\nThey realized that heroism is not always found in newspapers or television.\n\nSometimes heroes quietly perform ordinary tasks with extraordinary dedication.\n\nInspired by the interview, the class organized a monthly clean-up campaign within their barangay.\n\nMang Ernesto happily joined them.\n\nBy working together, the learners discovered that every citizen has the power to improve the community through simple acts of service.','teacher'),
(1401,1401,'Title: Beyond the Screen\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 168\n\nLike many teenagers, Daniel enjoyed spending hours playing online games and watching videos after school.\n\nAt first, he believed there was nothing wrong with it.\n\nHowever, he gradually noticed several changes.\n\nHe stayed up late every night.\n\nHe arrived at school feeling sleepy.\n\nHis assignments became incomplete, and his quiz scores slowly declined.\n\nOne afternoon, his older sister invited him to join a weekend hiking activity organized by their youth group.\n\nDaniel hesitated.\n\nHe almost refused.\n\nStill, he decided to give it a try.\n\nThe hike led them through forest trails, rivers, and scenic viewpoints.\n\nWithout realizing it, Daniel spent an entire day without checking his phone.\n\nInstead, he laughed with new friends, learned about native trees, and appreciated the beauty of nature.\n\nThat evening he reflected on his experience.\n\nTechnology is useful, he realized, but balance is essential.\n\nSince then, Daniel scheduled daily screen time while making room for reading, exercise, family conversations, and outdoor activities.\n\nLife beyond the screen offered experiences no device could fully replace.','teacher'),
(1402,1402,'Title: The Basketball Captain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nThe championship game was only minutes away.\n\nEveryone expected Team Falcons to win because they had remained undefeated throughout the season.\n\nAs captain, Marcus felt tremendous pressure.\n\nWhen the first quarter ended, however, his team trailed by twelve points.\n\nSome players began blaming one another for missed shots and defensive mistakes.\n\nDuring the timeout, Marcus gathered everyone into a circle.\n\n\"We cannot change the first quarter,\" he said calmly.\n\n\"But we can decide how we play the next three.\"\n\nHis teammates nodded.\n\nInstead of focusing on individual scoring, they emphasized teamwork.\n\nThey passed the ball more carefully, communicated during defense, and encouraged one another after every play.\n\nLittle by little, the gap narrowed.\n\nWith only fifteen seconds remaining, the score was tied.\n\nMarcus passed the ball to the newest member of the team, who made the winning basket.\n\nThe crowd erupted with applause.\n\nAfter the game, reporters asked Marcus why he did not take the final shot himself.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"A captain\'s greatest victory is helping the entire team succeed.\"','teacher'),
(1403,1403,'Title: The Secret of the Old Mango Tree\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 269\n\nThe oldest tree inside San Isidro National High School stood quietly behind the library. Its thick trunk and wide branches had provided shade for generations of learners. Teachers often joked that the tree had witnessed more examinations, graduations, and celebrations than anyone else in the school.\n\nOne afternoon, while researching for a local history project, three Grade 8 learners noticed a small metal box partially hidden beneath one of the tree\'s large roots. Curious but careful, they informed their adviser before touching it.\n\nTogether, they carefully removed the box from the soil. Inside they found several yellowed photographs, handwritten letters, and an old class program dated more than forty years earlier.\n\nThe school principal contacted retired teachers and alumni to help identify the people shown in the photographs. During the following week, former students visited the school and shared stories about their experiences as learners many years ago.\n\nThey spoke about studying without computers, participating in school programs under the same mango tree, and helping build classrooms through community volunteer work.\n\nThe learners listened with great interest.\n\nThey realized that although school buildings, technology, and teaching methods continue to change, the values of respect, perseverance, and service remain constant through every generation.\n\nInspired by what they had learned, the Student Government organized a Heritage Corner inside the library. The photographs, letters, and stories were carefully preserved so future learners could appreciate the rich history of their school.\n\nWhenever students gathered beneath the old mango tree, they no longer saw only its strong branches and broad leaves.\n\nThey saw living proof that every generation leaves behind lessons for the next.','teacher'),
(1404,1404,'Title: A New Beginning\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 233\n\nGraduation day had finally arrived.\n\nRows of proud parents filled the covered court while graduates adjusted their togas and smiled nervously at one another.\n\nAmong them sat Nicole, quietly remembering her first day in junior high school.\n\nShe had once been afraid to answer questions in class.\n\nReading aloud made her anxious, and group presentations seemed impossible.\n\nLittle by little, everything changed.\n\nHer teachers patiently guided her.\n\nHer classmates encouraged her to participate.\n\nShe joined reading activities, accepted leadership roles, and volunteered during school programs.\n\nEach experience strengthened her confidence.\n\nWhen Nicole\'s name was called to receive the Leadership Award, the audience applauded warmly.\n\nAs she walked across the stage, she remembered every challenge she had overcome—not because life had become easier, but because she had become stronger.\n\nAfter the ceremony, a Grade 7 learner approached her.\n\n\"Ate Nicole,\" he asked, \"what advice can you give someone who is afraid of making mistakes?\"\n\nNicole smiled.\n\n\"Never let fear stop you from trying,\" she replied. \"Every mistake teaches something valuable. Every challenge prepares you for a greater opportunity.\"\n\nYears later, Nicole hoped to become a teacher. She wanted to help young learners discover what her own teachers had helped her realize:\n\nEducation is not simply about earning high grades.\n\nIt is about becoming a person who can improve the lives of others.\n\nEvery ending, she believed, is simply the beginning of another journey.','teacher'),
(1405,1405,'Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','teacher'),
(1406,1406,'Title: Beyond the Finish Line\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 230\n\nWhen the Regional Innovation Challenge was announced, four Grade 8 learners decided to develop a portable water filtration device using inexpensive and locally available materials.\n\nThe project demanded weeks of careful planning, repeated testing, and countless revisions. Sometimes the filter failed to produce clean water. On other occasions, the materials proved too expensive for ordinary families.\n\nInstead of giving up, the team carefully recorded every observation. They consulted science teachers, studied reliable references, and listened to suggestions from community members.\n\nTheir persistence eventually paid off.\n\nThe final prototype successfully filtered muddy water into clear, usable water within a short period of time. During the competition, the judges praised not only the invention itself but also the learners\' explanation of the scientific principles behind its design.\n\nThe team received the award for Outstanding Community Innovation.\n\nDuring the closing ceremony, one judge shared a message that remained with the learners long after the competition ended.\n\n\"The finish line is never the end of learning,\" he said. \"It simply marks the beginning of greater responsibilities.\"\n\nAs they returned home, the learners understood the meaning of those words.\n\nTheir greatest achievement was not the trophy they carried.\n\nIt was the knowledge, perseverance, teamwork, and compassion they had developed while working together to solve a real community problem.\n\nTrue education prepares learners not only to succeed personally but also to improve the lives of others.','teacher'),
(1407,1407,'Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','teacher'),
(1408,1408,'Title: The Bridge to Tomorrow\nWords: 181\n\nWhen fourteen-year-old Marco transferred to his new school, everything seemed unfamiliar. The classrooms were larger, the teachers spoke faster, and the students already had their own groups of friends. During his first week, he preferred sitting quietly at the back of the room, hoping no one would notice him.\n\nOne morning, their English teacher announced a reading activity. Each learner would read a passage aloud before the class. Marco\'s heart beat rapidly. He enjoyed reading silently, but speaking before others made him nervous.\n\nWhen his turn came, he stood slowly and held the reading selection with trembling hands. He took a deep breath and began reading. At first, his voice was soft, but after several sentences he noticed that his classmates were listening attentively. His teacher smiled encouragingly whenever he paused correctly or pronounced a difficult word clearly.\n\nAfter the activity, several classmates congratulated him. One even asked if they could become partners for the next reading assignment. Marco realized that confidence grows through practice. Every page he read became another step across the bridge toward new friendships and greater opportunities.','teacher'),
(1409,1409,'Title: The Voice Within\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 146\n\nThere is a quiet voice inside,\nA faithful friend, a trusted guide.\nIt tells us when to stand up tall,\nTo help someone who starts to fall.\nIt whispers, \"Choose the path that\'s right,\nEven when it\'s out of sight.\"\nIt gives us courage, calm, and grace\nTo meet each challenge that we face.\nSometimes storms may cloud the day,\nAnd doubts may try to block the way.\nYet deep within, that gentle light\nStill shines to guide us through the night.\nA dream begins with one small step,\nWith every promise wisely kept.\nSuccess is built on what we do,\nOn honest hearts that stay sincere and true.\nListen well when choices call.\nYour inner voice can conquer all.\nWith hope and faith beside your way,\nYou\'ll grow much stronger every day.\nBelieve your voice.\nBelieve your dream.\nBecome much more\nThan you may seem.','teacher'),
(1410,1410,'Title: Tomorrow Begins Today\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 103\n\nTomorrow waits beyond the hill,\nPrepared for those with strength and will.\nIt does not ask how rich you are,\nNor where you live, nor near nor far.\nIt simply asks,\n\"Will you prepare?\nWill you keep learning everywhere?\"\nA page today,\nA book tonight,\nCan help your future shine more bright.\nMistakes may come,\nAnd plans may bend,\nYet every challenge has an end.\nEach sunrise brings another chance\nTo learn,\nTo lead,\nTo dream,\nTo dance.\nSo build your future,\nBrick by brick,\nWith patient hands that never quit.\nTomorrow starts\nWith what you do—\nAnd all tomorrow\nStarts with you.','teacher'),
(1411,1411,'Title: Beyond the Mountain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 96\n\nBeyond the mountain stands a dream,\nBrighter than the morning gleam.\nMany travelers lose their way,\nYet hopeful hearts decide to stay.\nThey climb through rain,\nThey climb through heat,\nWith tired hands and aching feet.\nEach rocky trail,\nEach winding road,\nMakes lighter every heavy load.\nThe summit waits for those who try,\nWho never fear to ask, \"Why?\"\nFor every step, though small it seems,\nLeads us closer to our dreams.\nSo if the journey feels too long,\nRemember this encouraging song:\nNo mountain stands forever high—\nThe brave will always reach the sky.','teacher'),
(1412,1412,'Title: Digital Footprints\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nEvery message that we send,\nEvery photo that we lend,\nLeaves a mark upon the way,\nLong beyond the passing day.\nKindness posted,\nTruth expressed,\nMakes the internet its best.\nWords can heal,\nOr words can harm,\nHolding either peace or alarm.\nThink before you click \"Send,\"\nChoose the message you defend.\nBuild a world that\'s safe and bright,\nGuided always by what\'s right.\nThough our screens may glow each night,\nCharacter remains our light.\nLet our footprints clearly show\nRespect wherever we may go.','teacher'),
(1413,1413,'Title: The Library Window\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nBeside the library window wide,\nCountless worlds are found inside.\nHeroes, oceans, stars, and kings,\nLive among the printed things.\nEvery shelf contains a door,\nOpening to something more.\nHistory whispers,\nScience sings,\nAdventure spreads its eager wings.\nA single book can change a mind,\nLeaving fear and doubt behind.\nReaders travel far each day\nThough they never move away.\nTurn each page with eager eyes,\nSeek the truth,\nBe brave,\nBe wise.\nFor every reader soon will find\nA stronger heart,\nA brighter mind.','teacher'),
(1414,1414,'Title: A Teacher\'s Gift\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 97\n\nA teacher plants a tiny seed,\nWith every lesson, thought, and deed.\nNot made of gold or precious stone,\nBut wisdom learners call their own.\nA word of praise, a guiding hand,\nCan help a fearful learner stand.\nA patient smile, a simple cheer,\nCan chase away uncertainty and fear.\nEach question asked, each answer found,\nMakes knowledge grow on fertile ground.\nThough years may pass and seasons shift,\nLearning remains a lasting gift.\nOne day the learner leads the way,\nHelping others every day.\nThe greatest treasure we can give\nIs teaching others how to live.','teacher'),
(1415,1415,'Title: The Silent River\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 95\n\nThe river never boasts aloud,\nNor seeks applause from any crowd.\nYet day by day it shapes the land\nWith quiet strength and patient hand.\nIt bends around the hardest stone,\nStill finding pathways of its own.\nIts waters teach us not to fight,\nBut gently choose the wiser right.\nThough storms may darken overhead,\nThe river steadily moves ahead.\nIt never stops because of fear;\nIts destination still is clear.\nLike rivers flowing to the sea,\nOur dreams become reality\nWhen steady hearts refuse to stray\nAnd choose to grow a little each day.','teacher'),
(1416,1416,'Title: Hope in the Storm\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nDark clouds gather in the sky,\nThunder rumbles rolling by.\nBranches bend beneath the rain,\nYet flowers bloom once more again.\nEvery storm will someday cease,\nGiving way to light and peace.\nCourage grows through every trial,\nOften hidden for a while.\nThough the winds may shake the trees,\nHope remains within the breeze.\nMorning always follows night,\nBringing warmth and golden light.\nSo when life seems hard to bear,\nKeep believing, keep your care.\nEven storms that make us strong\nCannot last forever long.','teacher'),
(1417,1417,'Title: Every Dream Matters\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 84\n\nSome dream high among the stars,\nOthers heal invisible scars.\nSome create amazing art,\nOthers serve with faithful heart.\nEvery dream deserves its place,\nEvery goal its patient race.\nGreat achievements always start\nFrom hope inside a willing heart.\nDreams are built through daily choice,\nThrough discipline and honest voice.\nStep by step the future grows,\nMore than anyone yet knows.\nHold your dream with courage true.\nLet determination carry you.\nFor every dream that lights the night\nCan change tomorrow with its light.','teacher'),
(1418,1418,'Title: We Rise Together\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 85\n\nOne small hand can plant a tree,\nTwo can build community.\nMany hearts that work as one\nFinish what cannot be done.\nDifferent voices, different minds,\nEach unique in countless kinds.\nYet together we become\nStronger than we are alone.\nHelping others makes us grow.\nSharing all the things we know.\nKindness spreads from friend to friend,\nWith no limit, with no end.\nLet us build a brighter day,\nChoosing hope in every way.\nWhen we stand through every weather,\nWe discover—\nWe rise together.','teacher'),
(1419,1419,'Title: The Last Seat in the Classroom\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 260\n\nThe last seat in Room Seven was always empty until the second week of classes. On Monday morning, a quiet boy named Adrian entered the room carrying an old backpack and a neatly folded notebook. He smiled politely as the teacher introduced him to the class.\n\nSince all the front seats were already occupied, Adrian sat at the back. During lessons, he listened carefully and wrote detailed notes, but he rarely spoke. Some classmates assumed he was shy, while others simply forgot he was there.\n\nOne afternoon, the class was assigned a group science project. Adrian\'s group struggled to organize their ideas. They argued over the experiment and could not agree on who would present.\n\nAfter listening quietly, Adrian finally raised his hand. \"May I suggest something?\" he asked.\n\nHis classmates nodded.\n\nHe explained how they could divide the work fairly. He also showed them a simple experiment he had learned in his previous school. Everyone agreed to follow his plan.\n\nPresentation day arrived. Their experiment worked perfectly, and the class applauded their teamwork. Their teacher praised not only the project but also the way everyone cooperated.\n\nDuring recess, Adrian no longer sat alone. His classmates invited him to eat with them, asking questions about his old school and sharing stories about their own experiences.\n\nAdrian realized that sometimes people are not noticed because they remain silent. Yet kindness, preparation, and willingness to help often speak louder than words. From that day forward, the last seat in the classroom became known as the place where a quiet leader once sat.','teacher'),
(1420,1420,'Title: The New Student\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 227\n\nLena transferred to a new school halfway through the school year. Everything felt unfamiliar—from the classrooms to the faces she passed along the hallways. Although her teachers were welcoming, she found it difficult to begin conversations with her classmates.\n\nDuring lunch breaks, Lena usually sat alone beneath a large acacia tree near the school garden. She spent her time reading novels while other students laughed and played nearby.\n\nOne rainy afternoon, the school announced a clean-up drive after classes. Every section was assigned an area to organize. Lena quietly joined her classmates in clearing fallen leaves and arranging flower pots.\n\nAs they worked together, one of her classmates noticed that Lena knew the names of many different plants. Curious, they asked how she learned so much.\n\n\"My grandmother loves gardening,\" Lena explained. \"She taught me how to care for flowers and vegetables.\"\n\nSoon everyone was asking questions. Lena demonstrated how to prepare seedlings and explained why certain plants needed more sunlight than others.\n\nThe following week, their adviser appointed Lena as one of the leaders of the class gardening project. Her classmates eagerly worked beside her, planting vegetables and flowering shrubs that later brightened the school grounds.\n\nLena realized that friendships often begin with shared work rather than shared words. By simply offering her knowledge and helping others, she found a place where she truly belonged.','teacher'),
(1421,1421,'Title: One Honest Decision\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 238\n\nIt was dismissal time when Joshua noticed a black wallet lying near the school gate. Students hurried past without seeing it. He picked it up and looked around, hoping someone would return to claim it.\n\nInside the wallet were several identification cards, a few receipts, and a considerable amount of money. Joshua immediately realized that someone must be worried about losing it.\n\nHis friends suggested different ideas. One jokingly said, \"Nobody saw you. Just keep it.\"\n\nJoshua smiled but shook his head. \"If this belonged to my father, I would hope someone returned it.\"\n\nInstead of going home immediately, he brought the wallet to the principal\'s office. The school staff checked the identification card and contacted the owner, who happened to be the father of one of the students.\n\nLess than an hour later, the grateful owner arrived. He counted the money and found that nothing was missing.\n\n\"You have saved me from many problems today,\" the man said warmly. \"This money was meant to pay our workers.\"\n\nHe offered Joshua a reward, but the boy politely declined.\n\n\"My parents taught me that honesty is worth more than money,\" Joshua replied.\n\nThe principal later shared Joshua\'s story during the Monday flag ceremony. His classmates applauded as he received a Certificate of Good Citizenship.\n\nJoshua discovered that doing the right thing may not always bring material rewards, but it earns something even greater—the trust and respect of others.','teacher'),
(1422,1422,'Title: The Forgotten Library Card\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 198\n\nEvery Wednesday afternoon, the Grade 8 learners visited the school library. It had become one of Sophia\'s favorite places because it offered more than books. It provided quiet spaces where she could think, study, and imagine.\n\nOne afternoon, she eagerly searched for a science encyclopedia recommended by her teacher. After finding the book, she walked toward the circulation desk.\n\nThen she realized something.\n\nHer library card was missing.\n\nShe searched every pocket of her bag, checked her notebooks, and even looked beneath the tables. It was nowhere to be found.\n\nFeeling disappointed, Sophia informed the librarian, who calmly smiled.\n\n\"Sometimes,\" the librarian said, \"finding something begins by remembering your last careful moment.\"\n\nSophia retraced her steps. She remembered reviewing her assignment under a large narra tree before entering the library.\n\nWhen she returned outside, she found the card exactly where she had placed it between the pages of her notebook.\n\nRelieved, she borrowed the encyclopedia and thanked the librarian.\n\nThat afternoon she learned that careful habits save both time and worry. Since then, she kept all important school cards inside a small transparent organizer attached to her bag.\n\nSmall acts of organization, she realized, often prevent bigger problems.','teacher'),
(1423,1423,'Title: The Science Fair Challenge\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 195\n\nThe annual Science Fair was only three weeks away, and excitement filled the campus. Every Grade 8 section had been challenged to present an invention that solved a community problem.\n\nGroup Three decided to create a simple rainwater collection system that could help households conserve water during the dry season.\n\nAt first, everything seemed easy. However, the prototype leaked during testing.\n\nSome members became discouraged.\n\n\"We should change our project,\" one student suggested.\n\nTheir team leader, Camille, disagreed.\n\n\"Real scientists don\'t stop after one failure,\" she said. \"Every mistake teaches us something.\"\n\nThe group carefully examined the design. They discovered that one connector pipe was slightly loose. After making adjustments and sealing the joints properly, they tested the system again.\n\nThis time, the container successfully collected rainwater without leaking.\n\nOn presentation day, the judges praised not only the invention but also the team\'s explanation of the scientific process behind their improvements.\n\nAlthough they placed second overall, the judges selected their project to be displayed during the municipal science exhibit.\n\nCamille smiled as she looked at her teammates.\n\n\"We didn\'t succeed because everything worked the first time,\" she said.\n\n\"We succeeded because we never stopped improving.\"','teacher'),
(1424,1424,'Title: A Letter to My Future Self\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 194\n\nAs part of their English class activity, each learner was asked to write a letter addressed to themselves ten years in the future.\n\nMost students wrote about the careers they hoped to pursue. Some dreamed of becoming engineers, doctors, teachers, artists, or entrepreneurs.\n\nMiguel stared at his blank paper for several minutes before writing his first sentence.\n\n\"Dear Future Me,\n\nI hope you still remember the promises you made when you were fourteen.\"\n\nHe continued writing about the values he wanted to keep—honesty, kindness, humility, and perseverance.\n\nHe reminded himself that success should never be measured only by money or awards, but also by the number of lives positively influenced.\n\nBefore sealing the letter inside an envelope, he added one final paragraph.\n\n\"If life becomes difficult, remember the dreams that inspired you to begin. Keep learning, keep serving, and never stop believing that every day is another opportunity to become a better person.\"\n\nThe teacher collected all the letters and promised to return them during the learners\' graduation from senior high school.\n\nAs Miguel walked home, he smiled.\n\nPerhaps the most important promise we ever make is the one we make to ourselves.','teacher'),
(1425,1425,'Title: The Volunteer\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 202\n\nA powerful typhoon had passed through the municipality, leaving many families in need of assistance. Several classrooms were damaged, trees blocked the roads, and many homes lost electricity.\n\nThe school organized a volunteer day to help clean the campus and prepare learning spaces for classes to resume.\n\nAmong the volunteers was Aaron, a Grade 7 learner who had never joined a community service activity before.\n\nHe expected the work to be tiring, and it certainly was.\n\nStudents swept classrooms, gathered broken branches, arranged donated books, and repainted faded signboards.\n\nDuring lunch, Aaron noticed an elderly maintenance worker struggling to carry several heavy boxes.\n\nWithout being asked, Aaron immediately helped him.\n\nThe man smiled gratefully.\n\n\"You remind me that kindness is strongest when nobody asks for it,\" he said.\n\nBy late afternoon, the school grounds looked cleaner than they had before the storm.\n\nAlthough Aaron returned home physically exhausted, he felt deeply satisfied.\n\nThat evening, he reflected on the day\'s work.\n\nVolunteering had taught him that serving the community is not measured by the size of one\'s contribution but by the willingness to offer time, effort, and compassion.\n\nSometimes the greatest reward for helping others is discovering that we have grown stronger ourselves.','teacher'),
(1426,1426,'Title: The Lost Wallet\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 246\n\nFriday afternoons were always busy at Manolo Integrated High School. Students hurried toward the school gate while others waited for their parents or rode the last jeepneys home.\n\nAs Claire walked across the covered court, she noticed a brown wallet beneath one of the benches. She picked it up and looked around, but no one seemed to be searching for it.\n\nInside were several identification cards, a driver\'s license, receipts, and enough cash to pay for a week\'s groceries. Claire immediately imagined how worried the owner must be.\n\nHer friend suggested taking the wallet to the nearest store because someone there might recognize its owner. Another classmate thought they should wait outside the gate.\n\nClaire remembered what their Values Education teacher often said: \"When you find something valuable, place it in the hands of someone who is responsible.\"\n\nShe went directly to the Guidance Office. The guidance counselor carefully examined the identification card and discovered that the wallet belonged to the father of a Grade 8 learner.\n\nWithin thirty minutes, the grateful parent arrived.\n\n\"I thought I had lost this forever,\" he said with relief. \"This contains my salary for the week.\"\n\nHe offered Claire a reward, but she politely refused.\n\n\"My reward is knowing everything was returned safely,\" she replied.\n\nDuring the next flag ceremony, the school recognized Claire for her honesty.\n\nHer classmates learned that integrity is demonstrated not only during difficult moments but also through simple decisions that no one may ever see.','teacher'),
(1427,1427,'Title: Race Against Time\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nOnly two days remained before the Regional Robotics Competition, and Team Innovate was still struggling with their robot\'s navigation program.\n\nEvery afternoon after classes, the four members met inside the computer laboratory. While other students were already heading home, they continued testing sensors, correcting computer code, and redesigning small mechanical parts.\n\nSometimes the robot stopped without warning. At other times, it turned in the wrong direction or failed to recognize obstacles.\n\n\"We\'re running out of time,\" Ethan sighed.\n\nTheir adviser smiled calmly.\n\n\"Success rarely belongs to those who never fail,\" she said. \"It belongs to those who learn quickly from every failure.\"\n\nThe group divided the remaining tasks. Ethan corrected the programming, Mia recalibrated the sensors, Joshua improved the wheels, while Hannah documented every modification.\n\nLate Friday evening, they performed one final test.\n\nThe robot moved smoothly through every checkpoint without making a single mistake.\n\nAlthough the competition was challenging, Team Innovate proudly earned second place.\n\nAs they packed their equipment, Ethan realized that winning a medal was rewarding, but learning perseverance, teamwork, and problem-solving was an even greater achievement.','teacher'),
(1428,1428,'Title: The School Garden\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nAt the beginning of the school year, the vacant lot beside the library looked dry and neglected. Weeds covered the ground, and very few students paid attention to it.\n\nTheir Science teacher proposed a challenge.\n\n\"What if we transformed this empty space into a vegetable garden?\"\n\nAt first, many students doubted the idea.\n\nThe soil looked rocky, and the weather had been unusually hot.\n\nNevertheless, the class accepted the challenge.\n\nEvery Wednesday morning, they watered seedlings, removed weeds, added compost, and measured plant growth.\n\nSome vegetables grew quickly, while others struggled.\n\nThe students carefully observed how sunlight, rainfall, and soil nutrients affected each plant.\n\nAfter three months, the once-empty lot had become a colorful garden filled with tomatoes, eggplants, pechay, okra, and herbs.\n\nThe harvested vegetables were donated to the school\'s feeding program.\n\nDuring the recognition ceremony, the principal congratulated the class.\n\n\"You did not simply grow vegetables,\" she said.\n\n\"You cultivated responsibility, patience, and teamwork.\"\n\nThe students proudly realized that meaningful change often begins with small acts repeated consistently.','teacher'),
(1429,1429,'Title: The Community Hero\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 180\n\nEvery morning before sunrise, Mang Ernesto could already be seen sweeping the streets surrounding the public market.\n\nMany people knew him simply as the town\'s sanitation worker. Few realized that he had been serving the community for more than twenty-five years.\n\nDespite the hot weather and occasional heavy rain, he reported for work every day with a smile.\n\nOne Saturday, Grade 8 learners interviewed him for their Araling Panlipunan project about community helpers.\n\n\"What motivates you to continue this work?\" one student asked.\n\nMang Ernesto smiled before answering.\n\n\"When people wake up and find clean streets, they can begin their day safely and comfortably. Knowing that my work helps others is enough reason to continue.\"\n\nThe students listened attentively.\n\nThey realized that heroism is not always found in newspapers or television.\n\nSometimes heroes quietly perform ordinary tasks with extraordinary dedication.\n\nInspired by the interview, the class organized a monthly clean-up campaign within their barangay.\n\nMang Ernesto happily joined them.\n\nBy working together, the learners discovered that every citizen has the power to improve the community through simple acts of service.','teacher'),
(1430,1430,'Title: Beyond the Screen\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 168\n\nLike many teenagers, Daniel enjoyed spending hours playing online games and watching videos after school.\n\nAt first, he believed there was nothing wrong with it.\n\nHowever, he gradually noticed several changes.\n\nHe stayed up late every night.\n\nHe arrived at school feeling sleepy.\n\nHis assignments became incomplete, and his quiz scores slowly declined.\n\nOne afternoon, his older sister invited him to join a weekend hiking activity organized by their youth group.\n\nDaniel hesitated.\n\nHe almost refused.\n\nStill, he decided to give it a try.\n\nThe hike led them through forest trails, rivers, and scenic viewpoints.\n\nWithout realizing it, Daniel spent an entire day without checking his phone.\n\nInstead, he laughed with new friends, learned about native trees, and appreciated the beauty of nature.\n\nThat evening he reflected on his experience.\n\nTechnology is useful, he realized, but balance is essential.\n\nSince then, Daniel scheduled daily screen time while making room for reading, exercise, family conversations, and outdoor activities.\n\nLife beyond the screen offered experiences no device could fully replace.','teacher'),
(1431,1431,'Title: The Basketball Captain\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 170\n\nThe championship game was only minutes away.\n\nEveryone expected Team Falcons to win because they had remained undefeated throughout the season.\n\nAs captain, Marcus felt tremendous pressure.\n\nWhen the first quarter ended, however, his team trailed by twelve points.\n\nSome players began blaming one another for missed shots and defensive mistakes.\n\nDuring the timeout, Marcus gathered everyone into a circle.\n\n\"We cannot change the first quarter,\" he said calmly.\n\n\"But we can decide how we play the next three.\"\n\nHis teammates nodded.\n\nInstead of focusing on individual scoring, they emphasized teamwork.\n\nThey passed the ball more carefully, communicated during defense, and encouraged one another after every play.\n\nLittle by little, the gap narrowed.\n\nWith only fifteen seconds remaining, the score was tied.\n\nMarcus passed the ball to the newest member of the team, who made the winning basket.\n\nThe crowd erupted with applause.\n\nAfter the game, reporters asked Marcus why he did not take the final shot himself.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"A captain\'s greatest victory is helping the entire team succeed.\"','teacher'),
(1432,1432,'Title: The Secret of the Old Mango Tree\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 269\n\nThe oldest tree inside San Isidro National High School stood quietly behind the library. Its thick trunk and wide branches had provided shade for generations of learners. Teachers often joked that the tree had witnessed more examinations, graduations, and celebrations than anyone else in the school.\n\nOne afternoon, while researching for a local history project, three Grade 8 learners noticed a small metal box partially hidden beneath one of the tree\'s large roots. Curious but careful, they informed their adviser before touching it.\n\nTogether, they carefully removed the box from the soil. Inside they found several yellowed photographs, handwritten letters, and an old class program dated more than forty years earlier.\n\nThe school principal contacted retired teachers and alumni to help identify the people shown in the photographs. During the following week, former students visited the school and shared stories about their experiences as learners many years ago.\n\nThey spoke about studying without computers, participating in school programs under the same mango tree, and helping build classrooms through community volunteer work.\n\nThe learners listened with great interest.\n\nThey realized that although school buildings, technology, and teaching methods continue to change, the values of respect, perseverance, and service remain constant through every generation.\n\nInspired by what they had learned, the Student Government organized a Heritage Corner inside the library. The photographs, letters, and stories were carefully preserved so future learners could appreciate the rich history of their school.\n\nWhenever students gathered beneath the old mango tree, they no longer saw only its strong branches and broad leaves.\n\nThey saw living proof that every generation leaves behind lessons for the next.','teacher'),
(1433,1433,'Title: A New Beginning\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 233\n\nGraduation day had finally arrived.\n\nRows of proud parents filled the covered court while graduates adjusted their togas and smiled nervously at one another.\n\nAmong them sat Nicole, quietly remembering her first day in junior high school.\n\nShe had once been afraid to answer questions in class.\n\nReading aloud made her anxious, and group presentations seemed impossible.\n\nLittle by little, everything changed.\n\nHer teachers patiently guided her.\n\nHer classmates encouraged her to participate.\n\nShe joined reading activities, accepted leadership roles, and volunteered during school programs.\n\nEach experience strengthened her confidence.\n\nWhen Nicole\'s name was called to receive the Leadership Award, the audience applauded warmly.\n\nAs she walked across the stage, she remembered every challenge she had overcome—not because life had become easier, but because she had become stronger.\n\nAfter the ceremony, a Grade 7 learner approached her.\n\n\"Ate Nicole,\" he asked, \"what advice can you give someone who is afraid of making mistakes?\"\n\nNicole smiled.\n\n\"Never let fear stop you from trying,\" she replied. \"Every mistake teaches something valuable. Every challenge prepares you for a greater opportunity.\"\n\nYears later, Nicole hoped to become a teacher. She wanted to help young learners discover what her own teachers had helped her realize:\n\nEducation is not simply about earning high grades.\n\nIt is about becoming a person who can improve the lives of others.\n\nEvery ending, she believed, is simply the beginning of another journey.','teacher'),
(1434,1434,'Your teacher will listen as you read a passage aloud. Read at your own pace, clearly and with expression. If you do not know a word, try your best and keep going. Tap Start reading when you begin and I’m done when you finish.','teacher'),
(1435,1435,'Title: Beyond the Finish Line\nAuthor: BULIG Development Team (Original)\nWords: 230\n\nWhen the Regional Innovation Challenge was announced, four Grade 8 learners decided to develop a portable water filtration device using inexpensive and locally available materials.\n\nThe project demanded weeks of careful planning, repeated testing, and countless revisions. Sometimes the filter failed to produce clean water. On other occasions, the materials proved too expensive for ordinary families.\n\nInstead of giving up, the team carefully recorded every observation. They consulted science teachers, studied reliable references, and listened to suggestions from community members.\n\nTheir persistence eventually paid off.\n\nThe final prototype successfully filtered muddy water into clear, usable water within a short period of time. During the competition, the judges praised not only the invention itself but also the learners\' explanation of the scientific principles behind its design.\n\nThe team received the award for Outstanding Community Innovation.\n\nDuring the closing ceremony, one judge shared a message that remained with the learners long after the competition ended.\n\n\"The finish line is never the end of learning,\" he said. \"It simply marks the beginning of greater responsibilities.\"\n\nAs they returned home, the learners understood the meaning of those words.\n\nTheir greatest achievement was not the trophy they carried.\n\nIt was the knowledge, perseverance, teamwork, and compassion they had developed while working together to solve a real community problem.\n\nTrue education prepares learners not only to succeed personally but also to improve the lives of others.','teacher'),
