(1685,1685,'manila, Philippines — mount bulusan in sorsogon province has contined to show an increase in volcanic earthquakes, state seism ologists said on Tuesday\n\naA total of ninety-one volcano-tectonic earth qakes “associated with rock fracturing” with a depth of 2 to 4 kilometers have been recorded since 1:38 a.m. on monday,\n\nacording the philippine institute of volcanology and Seismology (Phivolcs).\n\nincreased seismic activity and pres surization of the volcano edifice may endicated that hydrothermal processes may be occurring beneath the vol cano and may lead to\n\nsteam-driven eruptions at any of th e summit vents, Phivolcs said in a statement\n\nAlso, the volcano’s southwestern andsouth eastern flanks have been inflated since Feb. 2023 based on Phivolcs’ grounddeformation data from con tinuous GPS and electronic tilt monit oring\n\nOn the hand other, Phivolcs said degassing activity from the summit crater and active vents has been very weak to modirate when the\n\nvolcanosummit has been visibleble\n\nConsidering these fac tors, the statevolcanologist said bulusan volcano remains alert under level 1, meaning it is still at a low level ofunrest.\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ____________________________________________________________\n\nCopyread the news below using appropriate copy reading symbols, write the slug and instructions with two headlines and unit counts using flush left and flush right. This article was taken last 2020 in the Philippine Star.','teacher'),
(1686,1686,'STUDENT INVENTORS\nThree senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\nA. Write Your Headline (6–10 words only):\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________\nB. Write Your Lead (1–2 sentences only):\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________\nC. Why is it important for journalists to write clear and accurate headlines and leads in science and technology news?\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _______________________________________________________\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1687,1687,'I. Background of the Task You are a student journalist assigned to write a technology news article.\nYour task is to develop the context and main body content of the story based on the information below:\nNews Scenario:\nThree senior high school students developed a low-cost mobile app that detects early signs of dengue outbreaks using location-based reports and temperature data.\n\nII. Review: What Is Context in News Writing?\nContext provides background information that helps the reader understand:\n•     Why the news matters\n•     What problem it addresses\n•     The current situation related to the issue\n\nIII. Review: What Is the Main Body of a News Article?\nThe main body expands the story by adding:\n•     Important details\n•     Quotes (real or simulated)\n•     Effects or implications\n•     Supporting data or explanations\n\nIV. Activity Instructions Using the news scenario above, complete the tasks below:\nA. Write the Context Paragraph Explain the background of the dengue issue and why a detection app is important. (3–4 sentences)\n\nYour Answer:\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________\n\nB. Write the Main Body Paragraph Add more details about the app, how it works, who created it, and its possible impact. You may include invented quotes to enrich the story. (4–6 sentences)\nYour Answer:\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ __________________________________________________________________v________ ___________________________________________________________________________\n\nV. Reflection Question How can technology created by students contribute to solving real community problems?\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1688,1688,'I.    Write 3–4 sentences that give additional details about the app, dengue data, student motivations, or expert opinions.\nYour Answer:\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________\n\nII.   Write the Conclusion Paragraph Write 2–3 sentences explaining the significance of the students’ innovation and its potential impact on the community or the future.\nYour Answer:\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________\n\nIII.   Reflection How does supporting information make a news article more reliable and informative?\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1689,1689,'1. Pick someone in our school whose daily work you don’t fully know — it could be a janitor, cafeteria staff, security guard, teacher, or any support person.\n\n2. Write at least 6 good questions to interview them. For example: ●    What does a typical workday look like for you here? ●    What part of your job do most students not see? ●    What is something you are proud to have done at this school? ●    What challenges do you face? ●    How did you come to work here? ●    What advice would you give to students?\n\n3. Arrange and conduct a short interview. Be polite, introduce yourself (“Hi, I’m [Your Name]. I’m writing a feature for class.”), and take notes. If they agree, you can record the conversation.\n\n4. Use a graphic organizer (or bullet-point notes) to plan your feature: ●    Think about how you will hook your reader (start with a quote, story, or interesting fact) ●    List key details you want to include (their routines, quotes, anecdotes) ●    Plan a closing / reflection: why does their story matter?\n\n5. Write a 4-paragraph feature article: ●    Paragraph 1 (Lead): Begin with something engaging — a scene, quote, or anecdote. ●    Paragraphs 2–3 (Body): Describe their daily life, their challenges, and what makes them special, using quotes. ●    Paragraph 4 (Conclusion): Reflect on why their story is important to the school community. Example: Title: A Day in the Life of Mrs. Gomez, Our Cafeteria Manager Mrs. Gomez unlocks the cafeteria door at 6:30 a.m., long before most of the students arrive. She pushes a heavy cart filled with trays, cleaning supplies, and fresh bread. “I always like to start early,” she says, smiling. “I want everything to be ready when the kids come in.” By 7:00, she’s already busy: setting up breakfast stations, warming up the ovens, and arranging milk cartons. She hums softly as she works, greeting the first students with a friendly “Good morning!” She knows many of them by name and asks how their morning is going or what they think of the new menu. During lunch, Mrs. Gomez doesn’t just serve food — she listens. A student once told her they didn’t like any sandwiches, so she helped make a special salad just for them. “If I can make someone happy with food, then I feel like I’ve done something good,” she says. She also keeps an eye on the younger kids, making sure they can reach drinks, and helping them open lids when they need it. As afternoon classes begin, she starts cleaning — stacking trays, washing dishes, and sweeping crumbs off the floor. “I don’t just work here to feed students,” she explains. “I work here because I care.” When the cafeteria is quiet again, she takes a moment to look at a small bulletin board where students write thank-you notes. “These reminders make every early morning worth it,” she says. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________','teacher'),
(1690,1690,'1. Walk around the school or think of places in the school that many people might not notice: a quiet stairwell, a small garden, a forgotten hallway, a storage room, or a maintenance shed.\n\n2. Spend 5–10 minutes observing your chosen place. Write down: ● What you see, hear, smell, and feel. ● Who uses the place and for what? ● Why this place might be “hidden” or overlooked.\n\n3. Interview at least one person who knows the place (teacher, staff, or student): ● Ask about memories connected to the place. ● Ask why they go there and what it means to them. ● Ask how the place has changed over time (if it has).\n\n4. Plan your feature: Use a simple organizer to decide your hook, main observations / quotes, and ending.\n\n5. Write a 3-paragraph article: ● Paragraph 1: Describe the place vividly (sensory details, setting). ● Paragraph 2: Include the interview and what people say about the place. ● Paragraph 3: Explain why this place matters or what it represents. Sample: Title: The Quiet Corner by the Art Room Just outside the art room, hidden behind tall easels and paint-splattered drop cloths, lies a narrow hallway nook. It’s not a place many students pay attention to — but if you pause there, you’ll notice it’s special. The walls are lined with colorful sketches and half-finished canvases. There’s a small wooden stool where a few students leave their headphones while they paint or draw. When I visited, I heard the soft scrape of charcoal against paper and the steady breathing of someone sketching. The air smelled like turpentine and resh clay. I saw a few pencil shavings on the floor, and bits of pastel powder dusted on a nearby shelf. Ms. Rivera, the art teacher, told me that students sometimes come here during lunch just to think or make art quietly. “Some of them feel overwhelmed in class, so this corner gives them space,” she said. She remembered a student who sat here for a whole lunchtime working on a self-portrait. To her, this spot is more than just an empty hallway—it’s a retreat. “It’s where creativity happens without pressure,” she explained. Even though it\'s tucked away, that little corner holds the heart of many students’ artistic moments. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________','teacher'),
(1691,1691,'1. Choose an object in our school (or from home) that has meaning: maybe a trophy, an old piece of equipment, a piece of art, a forgotten musical instrument, or something in the staff room.\n\n2. Research or interview someone about the object: ●   Where did it come from? ●   Who used it? ●   What stories or memories are connected to it? ●   Why is it still kept here?\n\n3. Examine the object closely: note its color, shape, texture, any marks, age-wear, etc.\n\n4. Use a planning sheet or graphic organizer to map out: ● A hook (how you will start your story) ● The history / background ● Personal stories / quotes surrounding the object ● A meaningful conclusion\n\n5. Write a feature-style narrative (3 paragraphs): ●   Paragraph 1: Describe the object in detail so that readers can “see” it. ●   Paragraph 2: Tell the history or the stories linked to it, using interview quotes. ●   Paragraph 3: Reflect on why this object matters — for the school, for people, for memory.\n\n6. Revise and add a title (e.g., “The Trophy No One Talks About”).\n\n7. If possible, take a photo or draw the object and include it with your article. Example: Title: The Old School Bell That Never Rung In the back storage room, tucked away on a dusty wooden shelf, sits a large, round brass bell. It’s dulled from years of neglect, and the once-shiny surface is covered in patches of dark green tarnish. The label reads: “School Bell — Class of 1978.” No longer hung in the hallway or rung during assemblies, it feels like a forgotten piece of school history. Mr. Delacruz, a retired teacher, remembers when the bell used to ring. “Every Monday morning,” he recalled, “a student would pull the rope to call everyone together for the weekly assembly.” He smiled as he told me how students used to joke about ringing the bell just to skip class — but no one ever dared. Over time, the school built a new PA system, and the bell stopped being used. But alumni still ask about it when they come back. Some touch the brass gently, others snap photos. “It’s more than just a bell,” said Mr. Delacruz. “It’s a symbol of our school’s past — of days when things were simpler.” Now, the bell stays silent, but it continues to have meaning. For former and current students, it reminds them of generations who walked these halls, of their shared memories, and of the passage of time. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________________________________________','teacher'),
(1692,1692,'Activity 1. Identify the parts of column writing\n\nHeadline                       Body                     Opinion Conclusion                     Introduction\n\n________1. In conclusion, introducing mindfulness exercises at school is a simple yet effective way to support students’ mental health and learning.\nEven a few minutes each day can have lasting positive effects on their overall well-being.\n\n________2. Mindfulness is the practice of being fully aware of the present moment and noticing thoughts, feelings, and surroundings without judgment. Studies show it can reduce stress, enhance focus, and help students cope with the demands of school life.\n\n_______3. Mindfulness in Schools: Improving Focus and Well-Being.\n\n_______4. Students who practice mindfulness techniques such as deep breathing, meditation, or journaling report lower stress levels and increased concentration. Teachers observe improved behavior, more participation in class, and a calmer classroom atmosphere. Mindfulness also encourages emotional awareness and empathy, which strengthens relationships with peers.\n\n_______5. I believe all students should incorporate mindfulness into their daily routines because it promotes calmness, mental clarity, and better focus. Developing this skill early can improve both emotional well-being and academic performance.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1693,1693,'Arrange the sentences in the proper order and rewrite them clearly.\nI believe students should join recycling programs because it builds responsibility and awareness of environmental issues. Practicing these habits at school encourages care for the planet and community.\nRecycling turns waste into reusable materials, helping reduce pollution and protect the environment. Schools teach students to separate plastics, paper, and metals, giving them skills that go beyond the classroom.\nIn conclusion, recycling in schools is more than sorting waste—it develops responsibility, teamwork, and care for the environment. Students who participate can make a real difference at school and beyond.\nThe Importance of Recycling in Schools Many schools set up recycling bins, hold competitions, and run workshops to promote awareness. Recycling also teaches teamwork and sustainability, showing students that their actions can positively affect their surroundings.\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1694,1694,'_____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________\n\nWrite a short column or article (5–6 sentences) about “Participating in School Clubs”. Then label: ● Headline ● Introduction ● Body ● Conclusion','teacher'),
(1695,1695,'“Citizens Protest Over Corruption in Flood-Control Projects”\n\nIn 2025, many Filipinos are deeply worried about corruption in the government. Reports have emerged that billions of pesos meant for flood- control infrastructure — projects to prevent flooding — may have been misused. Wikipedia+2The Diplomat+2 Some of these projects are allegedly “ghost” projects — meaning the money was spent, but the actual construction was never finished or was done very poorly. Wikipedia Because of this, many people think these mismanaged flood-control systems make flooding worse instead of better. Wikipedia Public dissatisfaction grew, and on September 21, 2025, a huge number of people protested in Luneta Park (Manila) and other cities. This protest is called the “Trillion Peso March”, and it demands transparency, accountability, and changes in how government funds are handled.\nWikipedia+2Wikipedia+2 The protests reflect a growing concern: corruption is now one of the top issues for Filipinos, according to recent surveys.\nBusinessMirror+2Philstar+2 Business groups have also joined the calls for reform, saying that corruption hurts ordinary people and weakens institutions. The Diplomat\n\nDraw a cartoon that captures the mass protest during the “Trillion Peso March.” They should highlight:\n●   People united in protest (students, church groups, citizens)\n●   Signs or placards (e.g., “Transparency,” “Stop Corruption,” “Where’s the Flood Money?”)\n●   A river or flood scene in the background, symbolizing the flood-control projects under fire\n●   A building labeled “Government / DPWH / Congress” to symbolize the authorities being held accountable\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1696,1696,'Imagine what will happen in the future if corruption in flood-control projects is not fixed. In a cartoon:\n●   Show a future city flooded because flood-control systems failed (e.g., a broken dyke, collapsed barriers)\n●   Include citizens wading in water, or houses submerged\n●   Add exaggerated metaphorical elements: e.g., a corrupt official sitting on a pile of money while water rises, or a ghost-like blueprint of flood infrastructure (representing “ghost projects”)\n\nDiscussion Questions:\n●   What scientific / engineering failures might happen if infrastructure is poorly built?\n●   How would people’s lives change?\n●   What message do you want to warn the public about?\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1697,1697,'Design a cartoon that shows a positive, transparent, and effective solution to the flood-control problem. They should use their imagination and STEM thinking:\n●   Maybe a “Transparent Commission” (a group of engineers + citizens) overseeing flood-control contracts\n●   A “Smart Flood Device” that uses sensors and water-level data to allocate funds wisely\n●   A public ledger or “budget book” open in front of people, showing exactly where money goes\n\nReflection Questions:\n\n1. Why is corruption in infrastructure especially dangerous in a country prone to flooding?\n\n2. How can ordinary citizens (students, community members) use science, technology, and advocacy to demand better infrastructure?\n\n3. What role do transparency and accountability play in engineering projects?\n\n4. How would a well-engineered flood control system benefit everyone (not just the rich or powerful)?','teacher'),
(1698,1698,'Write your short radio greeting here:\n\n_____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _______________________\n\nWrite a two or three-sentence greeting that a radio host says at the start of a show. Example: “Good morning! You’re tuned in to The Voice News Radio, bringing you today’s top stories. Stay with us for the latest updates from around the world and your community.”','teacher'),
(1699,1699,'Example:\n“Good day! Today will be sunny with a high temperature of 28 degrees.\nExpect cooler winds this evening. Bring a hat if you’re going outside.”\n\nWrite your weather report here:\n\n_____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _______________________\n\nWrite a short weather update for your city or a made-up place. Include the following information below: ● Greeting ● Today’s weather ● Temperature ● A simple forecast (e.g. “Sunny this afternoon”, “cloudy skies”) ● Advice for listeners (e.g. “Don’t forget your jacket!”)','teacher'),
(1700,1700,'Write your weather report here:\n\n_____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________ __________________________________________________________\n\nThink of any school event and write a brief news script about a specific school event (contests, sports game, club activity, or announcement). Example: “The Grade 8 Science Quiz Bee will be held this Friday at 2 p.m. in the library. All participants should arrive early. Teachers encourage everyone to join and show support.”','teacher'),
(1701,1701,'Write a news article based on the following facts which are given at random\n\n- Ferdinand Paglinawan – Police Officer 2 of the QC Traffic Sector 2. He was the one who identified the fatality.\n- The incident happened in Barangay North Fairview, Quezon City yesterday morning.\n- Michael Franca- He was killed while 14 others were injured. It happened when a speeding bus rammed a Manila Electric Co. post. Franca is 32 years old. He lives on Saint Catherine Street, Barangay Holy Spirit.\n- according to the investigator, the victim was apparently hit by the bus.\nAfterwards he was dragged and pinned between the bus and the wall of the building. The building smashed after the concrete Meralco post split due to impact.\n\n- Joel Bacus – name of the driver. He is 37 years old.\nAccording to Paglinawan, Bacus claimed that the bus lost its brakes and he could no longer control the steering wheel.\n- the bus is on its way to Baclaran from Bulacan when the incident happened. Around 9 a.m.\n\n- the driver will be charged with reckless imprudence resulting in damage to property with multiple physical injuries and homicide.\n\n- thirteen passengers of the bus sustained bruises and abrasions. They were brought to the nearby hospital for treatment.\n\n- Franca was reportedly crossing Don Mariano Marcos Avenue when a Rubino Elena bus struck him. The bus has a plate number PWZ-900.\n\n- The driver sustained wounds in his foot. He was also brought to the hospital even as he was placed under the custody of the Quezon City Traffic Sector 2. ‘the victim’s death was brutal’– said by Paglinawan to The Star in an interview.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1702,1702,'Write a news article based on the following facts given at random.\n\nAlioden Dalaig - Chief of the Commission on Elections (Comelec) legal department. He’s a lawyer. He was found slumped unconscious with a gunshot wound in the chest. He was shot dead in Manila yesterday morning.\n\nJames Jimenez – Comelec spokesman. He confirmed that Dalaig was killed but failed to elaborate on the motive of the attack. Words of Jimenez: “I’m here confrming it was Attorney Dalaig who was killed.\nRight now, members of his family and the Comelec family are waiting for autopsy results.”\n\nFilomena – name of Dalaig’s wife. She claimed that her huisband had been receiving death threats lately. Jimenez further said that they would leave it up to the police to investigate the motive behind Dalaig’s killing.\nWe were very shocked. I could not believe it when people started calling me about it. He is a big loss for the Comelec. The wife failed to elaborate on the threats made to her husband.\n\nSPO4 Alfredo Dela Rosa – He led the investigation. According to him there were no witnesses so far to describe how Dalaig was shot.\nAccording to Dela Rosa Dalaig was carrying P300,000 cash but ruled robbery out as possible motive. Dalaig’s money and personal belongings were intact following the attack.\n\nDalaig was pronounced dead upon arrival at the Ospital ng Maynila, this was according to officials. Dalaig was found unconscious in front of the Hyatt Hotel at the corner of M.H. Del Pilar and Pedro Gil streets in Ermita, Manla.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1703,1703,'Write news articles based on the following facts which are given at random\n\n-Kuen Chua – He is 46 years old. He was rescued by the operatives of the Police Anti-Crime and Emergency Response (PACER) yesterday morning.\n-The rescue took place at 11:30 a.m. This happened after 17 days of captivity.\n-The rescue happened following an encounter with members of the kidnap gang at Belen Street in Barangay Gulod, Novaliches, Quezon City\n-The police rescued unharmed the Filipino-Chinese businessman from his kidnappers.in Quezon City.\n-Immediately following Chua’s rescue, PACER operatives went to various locations to arrest the remaining members of the kidnap-for-ransom gang.\n-Senior Superintendent Leonardo Espina – PACER Chief. According to him a still unidentified member of the kidnap gang was killed while another suspect, identified as Christopher Nanson. He is 35 years old, and was arrested.\n-A certain Adriano Ventulla of Antipolo City – He is the alleged owner of the safehouse. where Chua was kept. He will be invited for questioning.\n-Chua was kidnapped last Sept. 10 by four armed men wearing t-shirts that bore the word “police.” The kidnappers initially demanded P30 million for Chua’s release.\n-Espina described Nanson as a native of Camalig, Albay. He is a resident of Castro street, which is in Sta. Lucia in Novaliches, Quezon City.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1704,1704,'Read the editorial “It’s Time to Modernize Our School Dress Code.” After reading, carefully analyze the writer’s stand, arguments, and overall structure. Then, answer the guide questions provided below in complete sentences. Make sure your answers show your understanding of how editorial writing presents opinions, supports claims with reasons, and encourages readers to take action.\n\nIt’s Time to Modernize Our School Dress Code School dress codes are meant to promote discipline and unity, but our current policy no longer reflects the needs of today’s students. While rules are important, some parts of the dress code have become outdated, uncomfortable, and unfair. It is time for the school to update and modernize the policy to create a more inclusive environment.\nFirst, some of the restrictions no longer match modern clothing trends.\nStudents are often reprimanded for wearing outfits that are common, appropriate, and comfortable for everyday use. This not only embarrasses students but also disrupts their learning. Instead of focusing on small details like sleeve length or hair accessories, the dress code should focus on what truly matters: comfort, safety, and respect.\nSecond, certain rules tend to affect some students more than others.\nFor example, girls are more frequently disciplined for minor dress code issues, which can make them feel judged or singled out. A modernized dress code should be fair and equal, avoiding rules that target specific groups. A clearer, gender-neutral policy would make the school feel more supportive for everyone.\nFinally, updating the dress code can help students feel more confident.\nWhen students are comfortable with what they wear, they are more likely to participate in class and stay focused. A simple change in policy can lead to a more positive school atmosphere.\nUpdating the dress code doesn’t mean removing rules—it means improving them. Our school has the chance to create a policy that respects both tradition and the needs of today’s learners. By revising the dress code, we can promote fairness, comfort, and confidence for all students. It’s time to make the change.\nGuide Questions:\n\n1. What stand or opinion does the writer take about the school dress code, and how is it clearly expressed in the editorial?\n\n2. What reasons does the writer give to support the claim that the dress code needs to be updated? Identify at least two.\n\n3. How does the writer show awareness of different perspectives, and why is acknowledging opposing views important in an editorial?\n\n4. Which part of the editorial serves as the “call to action,” and how does it encourage readers to support change?\n\n5. How does the structure of the editorial (introduction, body, conclusion) help make the argument clear and persuasive?','teacher'),
(1705,1705,'II. Reason 1 + Supporting Evidence What is your first reason? What facts, examples, or explanations support it?\nReason 1: _________________________________________________ Supporting Evidence: ________________________________________\n\nIII. Reason 2 + Supporting Evidence Give another strong reason with support.\nReason 2: _________________________________________________ Supporting Evidence: ________________________________________\n\nIV. Reason 3 + Supporting Evidence Provide a third reason to strengthen your argument.\nReason 3: _________________________________________________ Supporting Evidence: ________________________________________\n\nV. Counterargument + Rebuttal What might the opposing side say? How will you respond or refute it?\nPossible Counterargument: ___________________________________\n\nYour Rebuttal: ______________________________________________\n\nVI. Call to Action / Closing Message What do you want readers to think, feel, or do after reading your editorial?\n\nBottom of Form\n\nUsing the Editorial Outline Template, choose a topic and organize your ideas before writing your editorial. Fill in each section of the outline by stating your thesis, providing three supporting reasons with evidence, identifying a possible counterargument, and writing a strong call to action or closing message. Make sure your answers are complete and clearly express your opinion and supporting points. This outline will serve as the foundation for your full editorial draft. Title of Editorial: ___________________________________________ I. Thesis (Your Stand / Main Opinion) State your clear position on the issue. Example: The school dress code needs to be updated to be fair and modern. My Thesis:','teacher'),
(1706,1706,'1. Write a complete editorial using the following structure: ● Introduction – hook + thesis ● Body Paragraphs – 2–3 major arguments ● Counterargument + rebuttal ● Conclusion – call to action or impactful final thought','teacher'),
(1707,1707,'Write a feature article about an “unsung hero” in sports. This could be:\n●   A lesser-known athlete\n●   A coach\n●   A referee\n●   A teammate who works hard but doesn’t get much attention\n\nInclude:\n●   Hook in the introduction (interesting opening)\n●   Background about the person (who they are, what sport, level)\n●   Their struggles, efforts, or sacrifices\n●   What makes them a “hero” (values, character, actions)\n●   A strong closing that leaves the reader thinking\n\nRequirements:\n●   3–5 paragraphs (250–350 words)\n●   Use at least two quotations (you can invent them)\n●   Use descriptive and factual language, like a magazine or website article\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1708,1708,'Write a match report about an important game (real or imaginary).\nInclude:\n●   Introduction:\no    What sport? o    Which teams played? o    When and where?\n●   Body:\no    Key moments in the game (turning points, best plays) o    At least 3 simple statistics, such as:\n▪   Final score\n▪   Number of goals/points by a key player\n▪   Fouls, errors, or saves\n●   Conclusion:\no    Overall performance of both teams o    Who stood out and why Length: 250–300 words\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1709,1709,'Write a compare-and-contrast composition about:\nAn individual sport (e.g., tennis, swimming) vs a team sport (e.g., basketball, volleyball).\n●   Introduction:\no    Briefly define both sports you chose.\n●   Body:\no    Explain similarities (e.g., both require training, discipline). o    Explain differences (e.g., pressure, communication, responsibility). o    Use words like: similarly, in contrast, however, on the other hand.\n●   Conclusion:\no    State which type you personally prefer and why.\nLength: 2–3 paragraphs, 200–250 words\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1710,1710,'manila, Philippines — Around 10.3 million piople world wide, mostly cildren, contractd measles last year, up by twenty percent compared with 8.6 million cases in 2022, according to newest estimates from the World Health Organization (who) and the United States Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (cdc).\n\nIna dequate immunization coberage was blamed for outbreaks of the highly contagious and deadly disease in different countries, including the Philippines biased on the Nov. 14 issue of “Progress Toward Measles Elimination—Worldwide, 2000-2023,” a joint publication of the WHO an\n\nd CDC.\n\nAn estim ated 83 percent of children worldwide received their first dose of measlesvaccine last year, while only 74 percent received the recommind ded second dose.\nA vaccine coverage of at least 95 percent is needed in each country and\n\ncom munity to prevent out bbreaks and protectpopulations from measle s.\n\nAs a result of global-scale gaps in vaccination coverage, 57 countries, including the philipines, experienced “large or disruptive”\n\nmeasles outbreaks in two thousand twenty-three. This was 60 percent higher than the 36 countries in the previous year.\n\nThe new data also showed an estimmated 107, 500 peopple, mostly children younger than five years old, died due to measles in 2023. Although this was an 8-percent\n\ndecrease from the previous year, WHO and CDC noted “far too many children” were still dyying from the preventable disease.\n\nIn the Philip pines, only sixty nine per cent of the more than 9.6 millionchildren aged 5 and below have received the coplete\n\ntwo doses of the mandatd measles, mumps and rubella vaccine—way below the 95 percent TARGET.\n\nCopyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.','teacher'),
(1711,1711,'Muslim house of representatives leaders are supportive of the Du30 administrations move to allow the burialing of the remain of the late former Pres. Bongbong E. Marcos at the Libingan ng mga Bayani (LNM) in Taguig Province\n\nI fuly suport the President on her decision to bury the remain of former Vice- President Marcos the Libingan ng mga Bayan. He (DUTERTE) is our leader and that’s he’s pre rogative. Let hisstory judge this and finnally pout closure on matter, Sema said loudly?\n\nShe noted that over under faith Islamic, they beleive that a burying place do not determine wheather a diead persons will go to hell or heaven and all are all are account table during judgment t day.”\n\n“It all depends on what actions we have done while we were alive. Are we a baad or good person? That’s the basis on wheather we are going to get the blessings or recieve punishment after death,” Sema xplaine.\n\nDeputy Speaker and Magindanao Rep. Bai SandraSema & Lanao del Norte Rep. Abudullah Dimaporo declared their full support to Dutertes President decision to bury the remains of Marco at the LNM, 9 days be4 the expiry of the 20-day status quo ante order by the Supreme Court (SD) stoopping the government preparations 4 the burial.\n\nDimaporo said the Chief Executive is correct when he set the burial of the later dictator at the LNMB. Senior Supt. Dionardo Carlos, PNP spokesman, said they categorized those who were not killed in police operations as Deaths Under Investigation (DUI).\n\n“As the people’s representative, our Pres. can speak for us, and our incumben President has spoken for us that Marcos beburied atthe Libingan ng Mga Bayani,” she sad.\n\nHe said the ball is now in the hands and feet of the Superme Court and appealled to concern parties to let “the Supereme Court decide on the legalty of the tissue.”\n\nNear 2,500 individuals have sofar ben killed amid the Duterte admin all out war on legal drugs and lawlessness, data from Phillippine Natl Pol lice (PNP) showed.\n\nThe Office of the Solicitor Gen (osg) has submitted to the High Basketball Court documents purportedly showing that Marco was soldier, war hero and a Medal of Val or recipient to affirm it’s planed hero’s burial for the super late dictate or at the LNMB.\n\nCopyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and flush left.','teacher'),
(1712,1712,'senior citicen party list Rep. rodolfo Ordanes proposed on thursday that the philippine health Insurance Corp. (PhilHealth) be placed under the department of finance (DOF) to prepared for the likelihood of the country having an aging population by 2030\n\nHe also suggGested that the social security system (SSS), Home develoment Mutual (Pag-Ibig) Fund and PhilHealth be expanded to include more workers in the informal economy.\n\nRodolfo Ordanes cited a report by the comission on Population and Development, which said that due to slow population growth, the Philippine population could be classified as aging by 2030. This would mean an increase in the number of filipinos 60 years old and above and a\n\ndecrease in those 15 years old and below be expanded to include more workers in the informal economi senior citicen party list Rep. rodolfo Ordanes proposed on thursday that the philippine health Insurance Corp. (PhilHealth) be placed under the department of finance (DOF) to prepared for the likelihood of the country having an aging population by 2030.\n\npreparing well for a larger and growing population of citizens senior involves strengtening our pension and health finance institutions, more employ ment and entrepreneurs and a continually growing economy. All these three factors will secure the future of future seniors he said.\n\nHe explained that strengthe ning pension and helth finance institutions would invove\n\ntransferring PhilHeath under the DOF where\n\n“PhilHealth pro perly belongs together with the sss, (Government Service Insurance System) a\n\nCopyread the news below using appropriate copy reading symbols, write the slug and instructions with two headlines and unit counts using flush left and flush right.','teacher'),
(1713,1713,'Creating catchy headlines and writing engaging leads for science news articles.\n\n✔ Write a catchy headline that summarizes the story in a few words.\n\n✔ Write a lead paragraph (1-2 sentences) that answers the questions:\n\nWhat? Who? Why is it important?\nScience News Story:\nScientists have developed a new solar panel that is twice as efficient as existing models. This breakthrough could greatly reduce the cost of solar energy and help combat climate change by making renewable energy more accessible worldwide.\n\nTask 1: Write a catchy headline for the story.\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ________________________.\nTask 2: Write a lead paragraph for the article.\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________________.\n\n✔ Read the short science news story below.','teacher'),
(1714,1714,'Evaluating science news articles and writing an original article based on scientific studies.\n\n✔ Answer the following questions based on your reading:\nScience News Article A collaborative research team from Japan has developed an innovative lithium-air battery prototype with a 1 watt-hour capacity featuring a 4 cm by 4 cm carbon electrode. This battery runs stably and shows promise for industrial applications in electric vehicles and aircraft. The new design addresses key challenges by boosting power output and extending battery life, which could revolutionize transportation technologies by offering energy densities comparable to gasoline.\n\nTasks:\nPart 1: Comprehension and Analysis\n\n1. What is the main scientific discovery or event covered in the article? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n2. Who conducted the scientific study? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n3. Is the scientific study a primary source or a secondary source? Explain the difference. ___________________________________________________________________\n\n4. Why do you think the journalist chose to write about this topic? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n5. Identify and list at least three key facts presented in the news article. ___________________________________________________________________ Part 2: Writing Practice Write a 300–400-word science news article based on the study and article you read. ___________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _____________________________________________________________________.','teacher'),
(1715,1715,'Composing a clear and logical Sci-Tech paragraph using evidence and scientific principles.\n\nPART I – ADVANCED HEADLINE ANALYSIS\n\n✔ Analyze the sample headlines below. For each item, answer the questions that follow.\nSample Headlines:\nA. “NanoShield Spray Makes Surfaces Virus-Resistant for 24 Hours”\nB. “HoloClass Glasses Bring 3D Lessons to Life in the Classroom”\nC. “BlueCarbon Robots Revive Dying Coral Reefs Faster Than Divers”\n\nQuestions:\n\n1. Which headline uses the strongest scientific concept? Explain why. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________\n\n2. Which headline appeals most to education technology readers? Give two reasons. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________\n\n3. Which headline suggests the greatest environmental impact? Support your answer with specific words found in the headline. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ PART II – LEAD WRITING CHALLENGE ✔ Choose one headline from Part I and write a complete news lead (3–4 sentences) covering the 5W’s and 1H. Write your lead here: ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ PART III – SCIENCE-ACCURACY CHECK ✔ Below are statements from a draft Sci-Tech article. Identify whether each line is ACCURATE or MISLEADING based on basic scientific principles. Then explain why. “The new hydrogen battery produces electricity by burning hydrogen inside the device.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _____________________________________________ “Robotic coral planters can restore damaged reefs because robots can swim faster than fish.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _____________________________________________ “A UV sanitation box kills germs using ultraviolet light that destroys their DNA.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _____________________________________________ PART IV – MINI DATA INTERPRETATION TASK ✔ Scientists tested a new device that detects air pollution. Below are the results: Day    Pollution Level (ppm)   Device Reading Accuracy 1            125                      93% 2            201                      95% 3            98                       92% 4            230                      96% Answer the questions: On which day was air pollution highest? __________________________________ Which day showed the highest accuracy of the device? ___________________________________________________________________ Suppose you were writing a Sci-Tech news article—what is the most important fact from the table that should appear in your lead? Why? ___________________________________________________________________ PART V – ADVANCED WRITING TASK ✔ Based on the data, headlines, and concepts above, write ONE full Sci- Tech paragraph (7–10 sentences) explaining why accurate technology is essential in solving modern scientific problems. Write your paragraph here: ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ __________________________________________________________________________.','teacher'),
(1716,1716,'1. Pick a Social Media Trend or Issue o     Choose a trend, challenge, or issue related to social media that is relevant to teenagers: mental health, “cancel culture,” misinformation, viral challenges, body image, privacy, digital activism, etc.\n\n2. Research o     Find data, news stories, and real-life examples about your topic. o     Interview at least two people: classmates, teachers, or someone in your community who has an opinion or experience with this trend or issue.\n\n3. Plan Your Feature o     Use a graphic organizer to outline: ▪   A hook (a surprising fact, a personal story, or a question) ▪   Background / context ▪   Real-life stories / quotes from your interviews ▪   A reflection or call to action: Why should people care? What can be done?\n\n4. Write Your Feature Article o     Write a 5-paragraph feature: ▪   Lead: Start with an anecdote or fact related to the trend ▪   Body 1: Explain the trend / issue (what it is, how it started) ▪   Body 2: Share stories or impact (quotes from interviews) ▪   Body 3: Discuss positive and negative effects / complexities ▪   Conclusion: Reflect on what the trend means for you and your peers propose a solution, raise a question, or call to action. Example: Title: The Invisible Weight: Social Media and Teen Mental Health Lead (Paragraph 1): Every night around 10 PM, 15-year-old Kayla opens TikTok, swipes through a stream of dance videos, beauty tutorials, and vloggers talking about their “glow-up” journeys. She pauses on a clip where a popular creator talks about how they feel “not good enough” without filters. “Sometimes I feel like that too,” Kayla says quietly. For many teenagers, the glow of their phone screen doesn’t just light up their room — it also lights up insecurities. Body 1 (Explaining the Trend): social media plays a huge role in teenage life today. According to a 2025 Pew Research Center survey, 45% of teens say they spend too much time on social media — a number that’s risen steadily in recent years. While many teens say these platforms help them feel more connected (74%) or creative (63%), others admit there’s a cost: nearly 1 in 5 say social media hurts their mental health. At the same time, a World Health Organization report found that about 11% of adolescents show signs of problematic social media use — struggling to control their usage and feeling negative consequences. Body 2 (Stories & Impact): To understand how this plays out in real life, I spoke with two classmates, Marco and Jess. Marco, 16, says his TikTok notifications are a “double- edged sword.” “When I get a lot of likes I feel proud, but when I don’t, I question why I even posted,” he says. Jess, 14, says Instagram is a place for both connection and competition. “I follow people who seem to have perfect lives — but when I compare, I feel small,” she admits. Their experiences reflect a bigger pattern: that behind every “like” or share is often a struggle with self-worth. Body 3 (Complexities — Positives & Negatives): social media isn’t all bad. Many teens use it as a place for self-expression, community, and even healing. In the same Pew survey, 34% of teens said they sometimes get mental health information from these platforms — and of those, 63% say it’s an important source. But the negatives are real, too. According to the Mayo Clinic, what teens do on social media matters: viewing content about self-harm, unrealistic beauty standards, or risky behavior can increase anxiety and low self-esteem. Experts suggest that moderation may be key — because using social media doesn’t necessarily have to be harmful, but overuse or exposure to certain content can be. Conclusion (Reflection / Call to Action): So what can we, as teens, do about this? First, we can set healthier boundaries — limit screen time, mute stressful accounts, and take “digital detox” breaks. Second, we can support each other: talk openly when we feel the pressure of comparison or when social media messes with our self- esteem. And finally, we can push for a change: schools can host workshops about healthy social media habits, and we can encourage creators to show real life — not just the highlight reel. If we learn to use social media with purpose, maybe we can make it a place that builds us up — instead of weighing us down.','teacher'),
(1717,1717,'1. Pick an Environmental Issue Choose a topic that matters to you and your community. Examples include: o     Plastic pollution o     Air quality / smog o     Water pollution or water scarcity o     Deforestation or loss of green space o     Climate change impacts (heat waves, flooding) o     Waste management / recycling problems\n\n2. Research & Gather Information o     Find reliable sources (news articles, government websites, scientific reports) to learn about your chosen issue. o     Use the “Telling the Story” Climate journalism advice: understand the science, but write in a way that real people can understand. o     Collect data (statistics, facts) that help explain the issue in your area or school.\n\n3. Interview People o     Talk to at least two people who are connected to the issue: classmates, teachers, local environmental group members, school staff, or community leaders. o     Prepare at least 6 questions. Some examples: ▪   What changes have you noticed in our environment / community? ▪   How does this issue affect you (school, home, daily life)? ▪   What do you think causes this problem? ▪   Have you seen any efforts or solutions? ▪   If you could change one thing, what would it be? ▪   What would you like other students to know about this issue?\n\n4. Plan Your Feature Article o Use a graphic organizer to structure your article. o Identify: ▪   Lead / Hook -How will you draw your readers in? (A story, surprising fact, or quote.) ▪   Body - Key points, people, and data. ▪   Closing / Reflection - What should readers take away? What can be done?\n\n5. Write Your Draft Write a 4- or 5-paragraph feature article: ▪   Paragraph 1 (Lead): Open with a scene, personal story, or fact that highlights the environmental issue. ▪   Paragraph 2: Explain what the problem is, with data / background. ▪   Paragraph 3: Include voices from your interviews — real people, quotes, personal impact. ▪   Paragraph 4: Describe current efforts or possible solutions. ▪   Paragraph 5 (Conclusion): Reflect on why this issue matters and suggest a call-to-action or hope for change. Example: Title: Sachets, Streets, and the Sea: How Plastic Pollution Affects Our Community Paragraph 1 (Lead): On a typical morning, 14-year-old Mia walks beside the small creek that winds behind her neighborhood. She used to toss snack wrappers and empty sachet packets on the ground — “they’re small,” she would say, “they’ll just disappear.” But now, she pauses. The creek is no longer clear: she sees bits of colorful plastic bobbing on the surface. “When I was younger, I never thought my trash could end up in the sea,” she admits. Paragraph 2 (Background / Problem): Plastic pollution is a massive problem in the Philippines. According to the Department of Environment and Natural Resources (DENR), about 24% of the country’s daily 61,000 metric tons of solid waste is plastic. Every year, the Philippines generates around 2.7 million metric tons of plastic waste, much of which ends up in landfills, rivers, or the ocean. Single-use plastics, like sachets, are especially problematic — they don’t biodegrade, they break down into microplastics, and they contribute to flooding by clogging drainage systems. Paragraph 3 (Voices / Stories): I spoke with Mr. Navarro, a barangay (village) official, who says, “Plastic waste isn’t just dirty — it’s dangerous.” He explained that clogged waterways during the rainy season make flooding worse, especially when heavy rains come. I also met Marisol; a local youth volunteer who helps organize barangay cleanups. “We collect plastic sachets every weekend,” she told me. “It’s heartbreaking to see how many people don’t bother to put trash in bins.” Paragraph 4 (Solutions / Efforts): There are hopeful signs. In 2025, the government launched the National Plastic Action Partnership (NPAP) to address plastic waste with stronger collaboration among communities, businesses, and government agencies. At the local level, students in Mia’s school started a “Plastic to Purpose” drive: they collect used sachets, sort them, and send them to a recycling center. For the microplastics that don’t get recycled, they’re talking to barangay officials about installing filters in the creek to trap litter before it reaches the sea. Paragraph 5 (Reflection / Call to Action): Mia now thinks twice before throwing anything out. “I realized my small actions matter,” she says. But she also knows that change isn’t just on her shoulders. We — as a school and a community — need to act. We can reduce single-use plastics, support local cleanups, and pressure companies to package products responsibly. If enough of us do something, maybe one day, the creek won’t be plastic-filled, and our streets won’t feed our seas.','teacher'),
(1718,1718,'1. Choose Your Focus Pick a specific aspect of poverty in your community or school. Some ideas: o     Poverty’s effect on students’ learning or attendance o     Families struggling with low income in your neighborhood o     Homelessness or housing instability o     The cycle of intergenerational poverty o     Local organizations or efforts helping people in poverty\n\n2. Research o     Find reliable sources about poverty: local news, NGO websites, government reports, or academic articles. o     Use these to gather data and facts (e.g., poverty rate in your area, causes, impact). For example, poverty is a “powerful risk factor for reading and writing difficulties” in school. o     Read personal stories or essays about poverty to help frame human experiences.\n\n3. Interview People o     Talk to at least two people affected by or knowledgeable about poverty: classmates, teachers, or community members. o     Prepare at least 6 interview questions, such as: ▪   What challenges do people in poverty face here? ▪   How has poverty affected students\' performance or attendance? ▪   What programs or help are available in our community? ▪   What do you think causes poverty to persist? ▪   What changes do you wish to see to help people escape poverty? ▪   How does poverty affect someone’s daily life (food, education, home)?\n\n4. Plan Your Feature o     Use a graphic organizer (or notebook) to organize your ideas: ▪   Lead/Hook: How will you start? (e.g., a personal story, a surprising fact) ▪   Body: Key points data, personal stories, interviews ▪   Reflection / Solutions: What can be done, or what hope do people have ▪   Conclusion: A strong closing thought that ties back to your hook\n\n5. Write Your Draft sWrite a 4- or 5-paragraph feature article: ▪   Lead / Opening – Begin with a story, anecdote, or strong fact about poverty in your area. ▪   Body Paragraph 1 – Explain the issue using your research / data. ▪   Body Paragraph 2 – Include voices from your interviews — what people said, their experiences. ▪   Body Paragraph 3 (if you do 5 paragraphs) – Talk about current efforts, programs, or possible solutions. ▪   Conclusion – Reflect on why the issue matters, what you learned, and a call to action or hopeful message. Example: Title: Invisible Yet Real: The Hidden Struggles of Middle-Income Families Paragraph 1 (Lead): From the outside, the Ramos family’s home looks like any other in North Quezon City — a tidy two-storey house, a small car in the driveway, and children running out to play. But inside, Mama Luz worries every month: “We don’t make much more than what we spent two decades ago,” she confides. Her husband’s salary barely keeps up with rising costs, and for many people, they are “just about getting by.” Paragraph 2 (Background / Issue): While “poverty” often conjures images of extreme hardship, there is a growing number of working poor families — those whose income is above the poverty line but still isn’t enough to cover all basic needs or cushion emergencies. According to the Philippine Statistics Authority, income thresholds to meet basic needs (food and non-food) are rising. For 2023, a five-member family needed about ₱13,873/month. Even though the national poverty rate has dropped to 15.5% in 2023, many families feel the squeeze from inflation, medical costs, and school fees. Paragraph 3 (Voices / Stories): I talked to Luz’s son, Dante, a Grade 9 student: “Sometimes I skip snack money so my little sister can eat.” His older sister, Maribel, is working part- time in a coffee shop. She says, “I pay for my own school supplies, but Mum worries about paying rent if something happens.” Their mother adds that she sometimes hesitates to ask for help — pride and stigma make it hard for her to acknowledge their struggle. Paragraph 4 (Current Efforts / Possible Solutions): Some communities are responding: a local church in their area started a “Community Care Fund” where residents contribute a small amount each month to help others pay for utilities or school fees. There’s also a non-profit that provides financial literacy training so families can budget better and save more. But Luz believes these efforts must be expanded: “More people need to know about help, and it must be easier to apply.” Paragraph 5 (Reflection / Call to Action): The Ramos family’s story reminds us that poverty isn’t always obvious — it can hide in homes that look “fine” on the outside. As classmates and friends, we need to challenge what we think poverty looks like. Schools can offer workshops on financial literacy or partner with local NGOs. We can also support policies that help all working families, not just those at the very bottom. Because when we see more clearly, we can help more meaningfully.','teacher'),
(1719,1719,'Spot the Column Elements\n\nTask: Highlight or list the identified parts.\n\nRead the short sample column below and label the following: ● Lead (introduction) ● Opinion statement ● Supporting arguments ● Conclusion Sample Text: \"Every morning, students face the same struggle: waking up too early. While adults believe starting school at 7:30 AM teaches discipline, the truth is that teens need more sleep. Studies show that lack of sleep affects memory, mood, and grades. If schools truly care about student well-being, they should consider starting classes later. After all, healthy students learn better.\"','teacher'),
(1720,1720,'“Two-Side Cartoon Challenge”\n\nTask Requirements:\n●   Use two symbols\n●   Add at least one exaggeration\n●   Write a 3–4 sentence interpretation\n\nDraw a cartoon with two contrasting sides (Before vs. After / Problem vs. Solution). Examples: ● Before and after social media addiction ● Pollution vs. clean environment ● Responsible vs. irresponsible AI use','teacher'),
(1721,1721,'Look at the picture.\n\n● Keep the issue but change the characters, symbols, or setting. ● Produce a new cartoon with a different angle or message.','teacher'),
(1722,1722,'Caption First!\n\nWrite a strong editorial caption first.\n\nThen create a cartoon that matches the caption.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1723,1723,'Write your greeting here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________\n\nWrite a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements: ● A warm greeting ● Station name ● A short welcome message Example: “Good morning! You’re listening to One Voice News Radio, bringing you the latest news and updates all day! Stay tuned for today’s top stories. We’ve got important reports and interviews lined up just for you!”','teacher'),
(1724,1724,'Write your report here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________\n\nWrite a short weather report about the information provided below. ● Greeting ● Today’s weather ● Temperature ● A simple forecast (e.g. “Sunny this afternoon”, “cloudy skies”) ● Advice for listeners (e.g. “Don’t forget your jacket!”) Example: “Good day, everyone! Today in Bayabason, Maramag, Bukidnon, expect cloudy skies with temperatures around 22 degrees. Don’t forget to bring your umbrellas, be safe!','teacher'),
(1725,1725,'● A short closing line Example:\n“Good afternoon, students! Don’t forget that the Art Club meets tomorrow at 3 p.m. in Room 12. Everyone is welcome to join. This is FIS News Radio reminding you to stay creative!”\n\nWrite your short school announcement script here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________\n\nWrite a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions). Requirements: ● Greeting ● What the announcement is about ● Important details (date, time, place)','teacher'),
(1726,1726,'Write a news article based on the following facts. Write an appropriate headline for your article.\n\nThe Philippines will host the APEC Summit in 2015.\n“We welcome the offer of the People’s Republic of China, the Philippines, and Peru to host APEC in 2014, 2015, and 2016 respectively,” said the 2012 Vladivostok Leaders’ Declaration. “We welcome the invitation from the President of Indonesia to meet again in Bali in 2013,” it added.\nThe 21 APEC members agreed to end by 2015 raising new barriers for the following: investment, trade in goods & services, imposing new export restrictions, implementing the World Trade Organization (WTO) measures with effects to protect members in the face of the global economic downturn.\nFor food security, APEC member economies reaffirmed its commitment to a 10% improvement in supply-chain performance by 2015, in terms of reduction of time, cost, and uncertainty of moving goods and services through the Asia-Pacific region.\nThe representatives of the summit had a sumptuous dinner with Cornish Crab Salad on Lemon Blini, Pressed Duck Terrine with Fruit Chutney, Roulade of Goat Cheese with Caramelized Walnuts, Assortment of Palmiers and Cheese Straws, Scottish Smoked Salmon Rose on Beetroot Blini, Miniature Watercress and Asparagus Tart and Quail Eggs with Celery Salt.\nThe environment was given focus with the leaders expressing concern on trafficking of endangered and protected wildlife species. A commitment was directed at strengthening efforts in combating illegal trade of environmental resources.\nOther commitments for strengthening are as follows: drive against terrorism; improve travel and tourism for job creation and disaster management.\nMembers of the APEC are Australia, Brunei Darussalam, Canada, Chile, China, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Japan, Republic of Korea, Malaysia, Mexico, New Zealand, Papua New Guinea, Peru, Philippines, Russia, Singapore, Taiwan, Thailand, United States of America, and Vietnam.\n\nThe news was formally announced at the end of the 20th Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Economic Leaders’ Meeting, Vladivostok, Russia\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1727,1727,'Write a news article based on the following facts. Write an appropriate headline for your article.\n\nDuterte: Send home 24,000 atuarantined OFWs in 1 week, expedite release of COVID test results By: Krissy Aguilar - Reporter/@KAguilarINQ        INQUIRER.net / 07:19 AM May 25, 2020 MANILA, Philippines — President Rodrigo Duterte has given the Department of Labor and Employment (DOLE), Overseas Workers Welfare Administration (OWWA) and the Department of Health (DOH) a week to send home some 24,000 overseas Filipino workers (OFWs) who are awaiting their coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID- 19) results in various quarantine facilities, Malacañang said Monday.\n“Inutusan po ng ating Presidente and DOLE, OWWA, at ang DOH na meron lamang silang isang linggo para masiguro na lahat po ng 24,000 na OFWs na kapatid nating mga Pilipino na nagaantay ng resulta ay mapauwi sa kani kanilang probinsya,” Presidential spokesperson Harry Roque said on state- run PTV4.\n(The President ordered DOLE, OWWA and DOH to make sure that the 24,000 OFWs awaiting their COVID-19 test result are sent home within week.)\nAccording to Roque, the President said the concerned agencies may utilize all government resources to ferry the OFWs back to their provinces.\nDuterte also said there is a need to boost coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-\n\n19. testing nationwide. “Nagsabi din ang ating Pangulo na kinakailangan palakasin na ang PCR [polymerase chain reaction] testing sa iba’t ibang lugar ng Pilipinas, hindi lamang dito sa Metro Manila nang sa ganon, pupuwede na silang umuwi diretso sa kanilang mga probinsya ang       mga OFWs at doon na sila magpakuha ng PCT COVID-19 test,” he said. (The President also saw the need to beef up PCR testing nationwide and not only in Metro Manila. That way, OFWs can be tested in their home provinces.) A number of repatriated OFWs have complained that they have been confined to quarantine facilities beyond the mandatory 14-day isolation period due to the delay in the release of their COVID-19 test results.','teacher'),
(1728,1728,'Write a news article based on the following facts. Write an appropriate headline for your article.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1729,1729,'Should Schools Adopt a Later Start Time?\n\nMany high school students struggle with fatigue, poor concentration, and declining mental health, largely because school starts too early.\nResearch shows that teenagers have different sleep cycles than adults and naturally stay up later at night. It is time for schools to adopt later start times to improve students’ health, academic performance, and overall well- being.\n\nFirst, early school hours negatively impact students’ physical and mental health. Sleep deprivation can lead to stress, anxiety, poor immunity, and reduced cognitive functioning. By starting school later, students are more likely to get the recommended 8–10 hours of sleep, leading to better mood, energy, and focus in class.\n\nSecond, academic performance improves when students are well- rested. Studies indicate that schools with later start times have higher attendance rates, better test scores, and increased engagement in lessons.\nWhen students are alert, they can absorb and retain information more effectively, resulting in stronger academic outcomes.\n\nSome argue that changing the start time may interfere with after- school activities, sports, and parental schedules. While adjustments are necessary, the benefits of better sleep and mental health far outweigh the logistical challenges. Schools can modify schedules gradually or provide flexible extracurricular timings to accommodate students and families.\n\nIn conclusion, shifting school start times is a simple yet powerful way to support teenagers’ physical health, mental well-being, and academic success. Educators, parents, and policymakers must work together to make this change. It’s time to prioritize students’ needs and give them the rest they deserve.\n\nGuide Questions:\n\n1. What is the writer’s stance on school start times, and how is it clearly expressed in the editorial?\n\n2. Identify at least two reasons the writer gives to support the claim that schools should start later.\n\n3. How does the writer acknowledge opposing views, and why is this important in editorial writing?\n\n4. Which part of the editorial functions as the “call to action,” and how does it persuade the reader to act?\n\n5. How does the structure (introduction, body, counterargument, conclusion) enhance the clarity and persuasiveness of the editorial?\n\n6. What kind of evidence does the writer use to strengthen their argument? (e.g., studies, statistics, logical reasoning)\n\n7. If you were to add one more reason to support the editorial, what would it be, and why?','teacher'),
(1730,1730,'Step 1: Brainstorm Issues List at least 5 problems you notice in your school or community:\n\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n5.\n\nStep 2: Choose One Issue Which issue do you care about the most?\n\nStep 3: Narrow the Issue Answer these prompts to clarify your editorial focus:\n\n    Why is this issue important?\n\n    Who is affected by this issue?\n\n    What change do you want to see happen?\n\nStep 4: Draft Your Working Thesis Write a clear statement expressing your opinion on this issue:\nThesis: __________________________________________________\n\nLook for issues or problems in your community that you feel need attention. Identify one that interests you and gather details or examples to support why it matters. Be ready to share your findings with the class.','teacher'),
(1731,1731,'Worksheet / Template:\n\nStep 1: Editorial Planning (Outline)\n\nTitle: ____________________________\n\nThesis / Stand:\n\nArgument 1 + Evidence:\n\nArgument 2 + Evidence:\n\nCounterargument + Rebuttal:\n\nCall to Action / Closing Message:\n\nStep 2: Draft Your Mini Editorial Write 3 paragraphs based on your outline.\n\nParagraph 1 – Introduction:\n\nParagraph 2 – Body (Arguments + Evidence):\n\nParagraph 3 – Conclusion (Counterargument + Call to Action):\n\nDraft a short editorial sharing your opinion on a topic of your choice. Then revise it to improve clarity, organization, and supporting details.','teacher'),
(1732,1732,'Write a script-style narration for a 3–4 minute segment of a sports documentary about:\n   A famous sports rivalry, or    A memorable championship game, or    The rise of a young athlete    Write as if you are the narrator of a documentary.\n   Include: Narration lines (what the voice-over says) o   Short scene directions in brackets [ ] describing what viewers see    Example:\n   Narrator: For years, the two teams battled for the crown…    [Slow-motion replay of the winning goal.]    Use:\no   Clear, vivid language o   Formal but dramatic tone Length: 300–400 words\n\nSkills: Script format, descriptive and informative writing, organizing events.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1733,1733,'Write an explanatory essay about the science behind one sports-related topic, such as:\n    How muscles work during a sprint     Why proper warm-up prevents injury     How reaction time affects performance in sports     The role of nutrition and hydration in athletes     Introduction:\no     Introduce your chosen topic. o     Explain why it is important in sports.\n   Body:\no     Use clear, factual explanations. o     Include at least one simple diagram idea (you can describe it instead of drawing):\n   Example: “A labeled diagram could show the major leg muscles used in sprinting.” o     Use correct but understandable terms (e.g., stamina, coordination, lactic acid).\n   Conclusion:\no     Summarize the main points. o     End with practical advice for student athletes.\nLength: 450–550 words Skills: Expository writing, using academic tone, explaining concepts clearly.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1734,1734,'Imagine your school wants to promote sports participation. Create a short written campaign made up of:\n   One main caption/post (like for Facebook or Instagram)\n   Two short supporting posts or slogans    A short explanation paragraph of the message you want to send Instructions:\n\n1. Main caption/post (120–180 words) o   Encourage students to join a sport or be more active. o   Mention benefits: physical health, mental health, friendships, discipline, etc. o   Use a friendly, motivational tone.\n\n2. Two supporting posts or slogans (1–3 sentences each) o   Example: “More games, less screen time. Your future self will thank you.”\n\n3. Explanation paragraph (100–150 words) o   Explain who your target audience is (e.g., Grade 7–10 students). o   Explain why you chose your words and style (funny, serious, inspiring). Skills: Audience awareness, persuasive language, concise writing, modern media style.','teacher'),
(1735,1735,'President Ferdinand Marcos Sr. on Sunday condenmed the actions by Chinas air force on\n\nAugust 8 against a Filipino military planes patrolling over Scarborough Fair Shoal.\n\nMarcos called the People’s Liberation Army Air Force’s actions “unjustified, illegal and\n\nreckless as the Philippine Force Air (PAF) aircraft was conducting a routine marittime security operator in Phillippine airspace.\n\n“We have hardly started to calm the waters, and it is already worrying that there could be instability in our airspace” Palace’s statement ready.\n\n“The Philippines will always remain com mitted to proper diplomacy and peaceful means of resolving disputes. However, we strongly urge China to demonstrate that it is fully capable of responsible action, both in the seas and in the skies,” it adds.\n\nOn August 8, multirole fighter jets of the Chinese Air Force executed dangeruos maneuvers and dropping flares near the PAF patrol plane while it was flying in the airspace over\n\nScarborough Shoal.\n\nMulti-role fighter jets is designed to performance a variety of combat and operational roles. This aerial vessels are usually equip with missiles and guns.\n\nThis is the first and last reported incident of China’s harassment in Philippine airspace.\n\nChina’s coast safeguard and maritime militias often escalate tensions in parts of the South Philippine Sea, which has on several occasions, left Philippine Coast Guard personnel injured.\n\nThe August 8 incident was condemned by the Arm Forces of the Philippines (afp) on Saturday.\n\nThe incident posed a threat to the Philippine Air Force aircraft and its crew, interfered with\n\nlawful flight operations in airspace within Philippine sovereignty and jurisdiction, and\n\ncontravened international law and regulations governing the safety of aviation, the AFP’s statement read.\n\nBeijing assert nearly the entire South China Sea, dismissing competing claims from several\n\nSoutheast Asian nations, including the Philippines, as well as a\n\n2016 international ruling that discredits it’s claims as having no legal foundation.\n\nScarbrough Shoal, a triangular chain of reefs and rocks, is 240 kilometres (150 miles) west of\n\nthe Philipppines\' main island of luzon and nearly 900 kilometres from the nearest major league Chinese landmass of Hainan.\n\nCopyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using barline and droplines.','teacher'),
(1736,1736,'MANIKLA, Philipine- the family jose Jaclyn express belief Yesterday that there were not\n\nplay foul on the dead the awardwinning actorm the philipine national police (pnp) said.\n\nAndis jose daughter in jose with the late actress mark gil, con\n\nfirmed that he pased away dues to atack heart.\n\nIt is with sadness ghreat that she announce the un timely pasing my nanay mary jane guck popularly known Jacklyn jose in the ages of sixty in the morning at march 2 dues myro\n\ncardial infarction jose said a pres conference.\nJosw were found death at his house on Quezon city in Sunday.\n\nThe family are asking for private. They beeive that there are no play foul in his dead. We are\n\nsympatizing with his family friends pnp spokes person col. Jean Fajardo tell repoorters\n\nThe daeth jose were confirmed her agency talent ppl entertainment. The p\n\nrogram will prioritize the city residents, he clarified.\n\nJose earn recognition diferent award giving bodies. He win the beat actor award at the\n\ncannies film festival in 2016 for his rule on brillante mendozas ma Rosa.\nThe senates exctended it’s deepest …. Sincesrest condolence to the family jose.\n\nAt the proppertime, we will passed a resolution honnoring his contrivutoins in the industry\n\nentertain ment senate president juan Miguel zubiri said.\n\nCopyread the article below using appropriate copy reading symbols. Write the slug, printers’ direction with two headline using crossline and flush left. The news was written by Mark Ernest Villeza in Philippine Star on March 5, 2024.','teacher'),
(1737,1737,'Manila- The Philippine embasy on wash ington d.c. in Monday disputes the reccent remarks\n\nthe Chinese embassy manila aganst ambassador jose manuel romualdez. In a stataement , the Philippine embasy said Romualdez are not a mouth piece for “ another countries nor is she sprading “erroneuos china treat”and “Sinophobia remark”.\n\n“Her statements regarding the devellopments on the south sea china has ben consestent with\n\nthe position expresed the philippine governments highest oficials particularly regardless the treat pose by un lawful agresive and provocation actions the Chinese caost guard and Chinese maritime militin again vesels Philippine and personel and filipino\n\nfisherman the read state ment.\n\nThis actions it added includes the used of laserss water canons and others dangeruos manevers biolation in maritime international reggulations. the China embasy have urged\n\nRomulo to “stopped spraeding eroneous informatoin” . it also acused her stiring up trouble at the south china sea.\n\nChina have always ben committed to property handlings the south china sea disputes relevant parties thrugh dailogue and consoltation, whiles firmlly safe guarding it’s teritorial\n\nsouveregnty and martime rights & interests, the chinese embasy sad in Sun.\nDespite the 2016\n\npermanent court arbitrations decidion invalidtingChina’s 9-dash linemap cl\n\naim nearly the entires south chian sea Chinese asets maritime comtinue asset aggresion on bajo de Masinloc andthe Sprately island Group.\n\nThe leaders of the three nations are expected to call for peace and stability following recent incidents in the West Philippine Sea.\n\n“Conflating Philipine eforts defend it’s teritorial and Mariitime entitlements with ser\n\nVing the interets another cuntry negate the philippines’ Legitimmate rihgts and interests of protect and depend our nation’s national souvereignty sovereign rightd and jurisdiction the\n\nwest philppine sea and the Philippine govern ment will not stood forit, the Philipine embasy said.\n\nThe Philippine constantly maintain its respects and support\n\nfor and comitment to a International rules-based order, particularly the united nationas\n\nconvention on the law of the Sea (UNIclos), and the 2016 arbitral award , an\n\nd will contineu work clossely withlike- minded parners in these regard, it aded.\n\nCopyread the news below using appropriate copy reading symbols, write the slug and instructions with two headlines and unit counts using flush left and flush right. The news was written by Ma. Teresa Montemayor in Philippine Star.','teacher'),
(1738,1738,'Task 2: Write a lead paragraph for the article here.\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________.\n\n Read the short news story below.  Write a catchy headline summarizing the main point in a few words.  Write a lead paragraph (1-2 sentences) answering: What happened? Why is it important?  Environmental News Story: A recent study found that urban trees significantly improve air quality by reducing harmful pollutants like nitrogen dioxide and particulate matter. The research highlights the importance of green spaces in cities for public health and climate resilience. Tasks: Task 1: Write a catchy headline for the story here. ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________.','teacher'),
(1739,1739,'1. What is the medical breakthrough or discovery? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n2. What disease or condition does it target? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n3. How does the therapy work in simple terms? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n4. Why is this breakthrough important? What impact could it have? ___________________________________________________________________\n\n5. Identify any challenges or limitations mentioned. ___________________________________________________________________ Task 2: Write a short science news article (250-350 words) based on the information above. ___________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________.','teacher'),
(1740,1740,'Composing an opinion paragraph using evidence and reasoning.\n\nAnswer the following:\n\n1. Which headline shows the greatest technological advancement? Explain. __________________________________________________________________\n\n2. Which headline suggests a discovery about conditions beyond Earth? Why? __________________________________________________________________\n\n3. Which headline has the most impact for the Philippines? Support your answer. __________________________________________________________________ PART II – ADVANCED LEAD WRITING (5W1H) Choose ONE headline from Part I.  Write a 4–5 sentence lead paragraph. Write your lead here: ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _____________________________. PART III – SCIENCE ACCURACY CHECK  Identify whether the statement is SCIENTIFICALLY ACCURATE or MISLEADING. Provide a short explanation. “Astronauts float in the International Space Station because there is no gravity in space.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _________________________________________________ “Ion propulsion pushes spacecraft forward by shooting out charged particles at high speed.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _________________________________________________ “The dark side of the Moon is always dark because sunlight never reaches it.” Accurate / Misleading: __________ Explanation: _________________________________________________ PART IV – SPACE DATA INTERPRETATION NASA scientists tested three propulsion systems for a future Mars mission. Here are the results: Propulsion Type   Speed (km/s)        Fuel Efficiency (%)Travel Time to Mars (month) Chemical Rocket         5.4                 30%               7.0 Ion Thruster                   12.1               78%               4.2 Nuclear Engine          18.7                65%               2.9 Answer the following:\n\n1. Which propulsion type is the fastest? ________________________________________________________________\n\n2. Which propulsion type uses fuel most efficiently? _________________________________________________________________\n\n3. Which propulsion system would be most newsworthy for a Sci-Tech article? Explain why. __________________________________________________________________\n\n3. If you were writing the lead paragraph, what is the single most important fact to include from the table? Why? PART V – OPINION COLUMN  Write a one-paragraph Sci-Tech opinion column (8–10 sentences) answering: “Should countries invest more in space exploration or focus on Earth-based problems? Support your argument using scientific facts and real or realistic space technologies.” Write your paragraph here: ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _____________________________.','teacher'),
(1741,1741,'Human Interest Story “A Day in the Life”\nStep-by-Step Instructions:\n\n1. Choose a subject: Select a person in your school or community whose daily life or experiences are interesting. Examples: a teacher, a school staff member, a local artist, or a peer with a unique hobby.\n\n2. Plan your approach: Think of 5–7 questions to ask during an interview, focusing on their daily routine, challenges, achievements, and personal interests.\n\n3. Observe: Spend time observing your subject in their environment. Take detailed notes about their actions, expressions, and interactions.\n\n4. Conduct an interview: Use your prepared questions. Record or write down their responses verbatim to include direct quotes in your article.\n\n5. Write your feature article (400–500 words): o    Lead: Begin with an engaging anecdote or scene from their day. o    Body: Share their story, including quotes, descriptive details, and personal insights. o    Conclusion: Reflect on what makes this person’s life inspiring, interesting, or unique. EXAMPLE: Mr. Daniel, the school janitor Lead: Every morning at 5:30 a.m., while most of the town is still asleep, Mr. Daniel is already sweeping the empty corridors of Riverside High School. The faint smell of disinfectant fills the air as he hums a soft tune, preparing the school for another day of learning. Body: Mr. Daniel has worked at Riverside High for over 15 years. “I enjoy seeing the students smile when the school is clean,” he says with a warm grin. His day begins with sweeping and mopping floors, followed by restocking classrooms and checking that all facilities are in order. Despite his busy schedule, he always finds time to greet students by name. “It’s the small things that make my day worthwhile,” he explains. Outside work, Mr. Daniel volunteers at the local community center, helping children with homework and organizing weekend sports activities. Conclusion: Mr. Daniel’s dedication reminds everyone that every role in the school community matters. Through his hard work and kindness, he quietly makes Riverside High a brighter place for students and staff alike.','teacher'),
(1742,1742,'1. Select a trend: Identify a current trend among teenagers (e.g., social media challenge, fashion style, hobby, or app).\n\n2. Research: Use the internet, social media, and news articles to gather information about the trend. Note its origin, popularity, and impact.\n\n3. Interview peers: Ask classmates or friends why they follow this trend and what they like about it. Record at least 2–3 quotes.\n\n4. Plan your article: Create an outline: o    Introduction: Hook the reader with a fun fact, question, or statistic about the trend. o    Body: Explain what the trend is, why it is popular, and who participates. Include quotes and interesting details. o    Impact: Discuss how it affects teens or society (positively or negatively). o    Conclusion: Summarize the trend’s importance or speculate about its future.\n\n5. Write your feature article (400–500 words): Use clear, engaging language and maintain a friendly, lively tone.\n\n6. Edit and polish: Check for grammar, smooth flow, and correct facts. Make sure quotes and data are accurate. EXAMPLE: DIY Resin Art Lead: Over the past year, a colorful wave of creativity has taken over social media: DIY resin art. From glittery keychains to miniature landscapes, teenagers are turning their free time into artistic masterpieces. Body: Resin art involves mixing resin with pigments, glitter, and molds to create small decorative items. Teens like 16-year-old Maya explain why they love it: “It’s relaxing, and I love seeing the final product shine.” The trend has gained attention on Instagram and TikTok, with thousands of posts under #ResinArt. Schools and online communities now host workshops where beginners can learn to craft their own resin pieces. Experts note that resin art encourages creativity and patience, giving teenagers a productive way to spend their time. Impact: While resin art is mostly positive, some teens spend large amounts on supplies, which can be costly. However, many view it as a hobby that boosts confidence and skills. Conclusion: From glitter-filled coasters to custom jewelry, DIY resin art shows how a simple craft can inspire a generation of young creators. As more teens experiment with colors and shapes, the trend is likely to grow even further.','teacher'),
(1743,1743,'Place Feature – “Hidden Gems in Our Community”\nStep-by-Step Instructions:\n\n1. Choose a location: Find a lesser-known or interesting place in your community or school (park, café, library corner, historical site).\n\n2. Visit and observe: Spend 15–30 minutes noting details about the surroundings, atmosphere, people, sounds, smells, and activities.\n\n3. Talk to people: If possible, ask visitors or staff about their experiences at the place. Record at least 1–2 quotes.\n\n4. Plan your article: Organize your observations: o    Lead: Open with a vivid description or scene-setting that immediately immerses the reader. o    Body: Describe the place in detail, include quotes, history (if relevant), and why it is unique. o    Conclusion: Suggest why readers should visit or appreciate this hidden gem.\n\n5. Write your feature article (400–500 words): Use descriptive language, imagery, and personification. Make the reader feel as if they are visiting the place themselves.\n\n6. Revise and proofread: Ensure your article flow well, has correct grammar, and creates a; strong impression of the location. Example: Maple Grove Park Lead: Tucked behind the main road in downtown Oakville, Maple Grove Park is a serene escape from the city’s hustle. The soft rustling of leaves and the scent of blooming flowers greet visitors as they step onto winding cobblestone paths. Body: The Park is dotted with wooden benches, perfect for reading or quiet reflection. Children play near the small fountain, while joggers circle the walking trails. “I come here every morning; it’s peaceful and refreshing,” says local resident Mr. Cruz. Historically, Maple Grove Park was established in 1920 and still retains its century-old oak trees. Birdsong fills the air, and butterflies often flutter past the flowerbeds, adding to the park’s charm. Conclusion: Whether you’re seeking a quiet spot to read, a place to exercise, or a peaceful escape from the busy city, Maple Grove Park is a hidden gem worth exploring. It proves that sometimes, the most beautiful places are the ones we least expect.','teacher'),
(1744,1744,'Identify persuasive techniques used in a professional-style column.\n\n1. Identify 5 persuasive techniques.\n\n2. Explain how each technique strengthens the argument.','teacher'),
(1745,1745,'“Twist the Satire”\n\nTask:\n     Change the symbols, characters, or exaggerations      Keep the issue but show a different or deeper angle\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1746,1746,'Select a relevant social or political issue and create a high-satire cartoon showing:\n   Irony or sarcasm    Symbolic metaphors    Exaggeration    Social commentary Possible Themes:\n   Fake news    Political responsibility    Environmental destruction    Education inequality    Data privacy    Consumerism\n\n✔ Producing layered, analytical editorial cartoons\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1747,1747,'Draw a cartoon following the instructions below:\n“Silent but Deadly” – Zero Text Cartoon No words allowed:\n   No caption    No labels    No speech bubbles\n\nOnly symbols, pose, and imagery may be used to express the message.\n\nWrite your answer.','teacher'),
(1748,1748,'Write your greeting here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________\n\nWrite a 10-15 second greeting that a radio host might say at the start of a show. Write your greeting in the space provided below. Requirements:  A warm greeting  Station name  A short welcome message Example: “Good morning! You’re listening to One Voice News Radio, bringing you the latest news and updates all day! Stay tuned for today’s top stories. We’ve got important reports and interviews lined up just for you!”','teacher'),
(1749,1749,'Write your report here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________\n\nWrite a short weather report about the information provided below.  Greeting  Today’s weather  Temperature  A simple forecast (e.g. “Sunny this afternoon”, “cloudy skies”)  Advice for listeners (e.g. “Don’t forget your jacket!”) Example: “Good day, everyone! Today in Bayabason, Maramag, Bukidnon, expect cloudy skies with temperatures around 22 degrees. Don’t forget to bring your umbrellas, be safe!','teacher'),
(1750,1750,'Write your short school announcement script here:\n\n___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________________________ _________________________\n\nWrite a short school announcement about an upcoming event (field trip, contest, club meeting), or a reminder (uniform, deadlines, project submissions). Requirements:  Greeting  What the announcement is about  Important details (date, time, place)  A short closing line Example: “Good afternoon, students! Don’t forget that the Art Club meets tomorrow at 3 p.m. in Room 12. Everyone is welcome to join. This is FIS News Radio reminding you to stay creative!”','teacher');
