/image-298.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [73.19999694824219, 430.2099914550781, 205.0699920654297, 554.8699951171875], \"xref\": 298, \"width\": 359, \"height\": 359}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-299.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [216.85000610351562, 430.2099914550781, 348.7200012207031, 554.8699951171875], \"xref\": 299, \"width\": 359, \"height\": 359}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-300.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [359.7099914550781, 430.2099914550781, 491.5799865722656, 554.8699951171875], \"xref\": 300, \"width\": 359, \"height\": 359}]'),
(34,'                                                           27\n\n\nTongue Twister Badges\n\n\n\n\n\nTongue Twister Star','assets/module/page-034.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [4.949900150299072, 775.8502807617188, 588.3499145507812, 832.7952880859375], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-303.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [148.64999389648438, 438.8000183105469, 295.5, 585.6500244140625], \"xref\": 303, \"width\": 445, \"height\": 445}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-304.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [328.79998779296875, 439.4399719238281, 475.5999755859375, 586.2899780273438], \"xref\": 304, \"width\": 445, \"height\": 445}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-305.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [152.5, 601.7000122070312, 296.0, 745.2000122070312], \"xref\": 305, \"width\": 445, \"height\": 445}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-306.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [332.29998779296875, 601.31201171875, 475.79998779296875, 744.81201171875], \"xref\": 306, \"width\": 445, \"height\": 445}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-307.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [317.0, 254.30999755859375, 465.0, 390.30999755859375], \"xref\": 307, \"width\": 421, \"height\": 373}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-309.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [149.0, 243.30999755859375, 287.3500061035156, 378.30999755859375], \"xref\": 309, \"width\": 398, \"height\": 401}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-311.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [142.0, 95.30999755859375, 288.0, 239.30999755859375], \"xref\": 311, \"width\": 416, \"height\": 408}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-312.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [312.0, 98.30999755859375, 452.6499938964844, 237.30999755859375], \"xref\": 312, \"width\": 394, \"height\": 376}]'),
(35,'                                                             28\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material\n\nLearning Competency: Talk about oneself and one’s family\n\nInstruction: Tell me something about yourself.\n\nRubrics\n\n       1              2              3              4\n Pupil mentioned   Pupil mentioned   Pupil mentioned   Pupil mentioned\n  only his name     2 – 3 details     4 details about   5 or more details\n                   about himself         himself        about himself\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material\n\nLearning Competency: Follow one to two steps directions\n\nInstructions: 1. Go to the door and close it.\n\n              2. Run back to your seat.\n\nRubrics\n\n       1              2              3              4\n  Pupil attempted    Pupil executed     Pupil executed    Pupil executed\n  to do what has    wrongly what      incompletely     accurately what\n    been said.       has been said.     what has been     has been said.\n                                              said\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material\n\nInstructions: You ask your younger sister to keep her toys away. What\n\nwill you say? Give her at least two instructions.\n\nRubrics\n\n         1               2             3             4\n       Pupil give          Pupil gave 1     Pupil gave 2      Pupil gave\n  incomplete/inaccurate      accurate       accurate and    more than 2\n         direction.             direction.        complete       accurate and\n                                                  directions.       complete\n                                                                     directions.','assets/module/page-035.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8505249023438, 587.14990234375, 832.7985229492188], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(36,'                                                            29\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material\n\nLearning Competency: Use describing words for people, objects, things\nand places (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.)\n\nInstructions: Show a red ball.\n\nTeacher says: Tell me something about the object I am holding.\n\nRubrics\n\n       1              2              3              4\n  Pupil says “ball”     Pupil says “a       Pupil says “A      Pupil says “It is\n                              ball”             red ball”        a red ball.” (or\n                                                                                 tell more\n                                                    something about\n                                                                                                       it.)\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material\n\nLearning Competency: Talk about topics of interest\n\nInstruction: Tell me what you think about this picture.\n\n\n\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material\n\nLearning Competency: Asking Questions\n\nInstructions: Things you should know:\n\n    •  Number of Players: 2-10\n\n    •  Setup: Choose 1 player to be “it,” and have them think of a secret\n      person, place or thing.\n\n    •  Object of the game: Other players attempt to guess the secret person,\n      place, or thing, but they only get 20 questions to do so.','assets/module/page-036.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8505249023438, 587.14990234375, 832.7985229492188], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-323.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [154.6999969482422, 382.6300048828125, 291.41998291015625, 474.6300048828125], \"xref\": 323, \"width\": 321, \"height\": 208}]'),
(37,'                                                            30\n\n\n\"This game works best with a small to medium-sized group of people, so that\neveryone gets a chance to ask a question. Choose a random player to be “it.”\nThis could be the youngest player in the group, the player who’s had the most\nrecent birthday, or something silly, like who can eat a piece of pizza the\nfastest.\n\nQuestion 1\n\n\n\n\n\nThink of a person, place, or thing for other players to guess if you’re “it. ”Think\nof someone or something that you know enough about to answer some basic\nquestions about, and keep  it a secret! If you choose a person, they can be\nliving, deceased, or even fictional. Make sure you choose a person, place, or\nthing that most people in your group know about.\n\n    •  For example, your item could be “Manny Pacquiao,” since he is famous\n     enough that most people will be able to make guesses about him. You\n      could also choose something like your town, Philippines, or even clouds\n      or the sun.\n\n    •  Avoid items like “my mom” or “my dog” unless you are with your siblings\n      or best friends, since the players might not know enough about them\n      to guess.\n\n\n\n\n\nQuestion 2\n\n\n\n\nAsk yes-or-no questions if you\'re not \"it. \"If you’re not “it,” your goal is to figure\nout what the “it” person is thinking of by asking questions to narrow down\nthe possibilities. Try using a fairly general opening question that can be\nanswered with “yes” or “no” to narrow down your options.','assets/module/page-037.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8505249023438, 587.14990234375, 832.7985229492188], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-327.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [175.5, 136.10000610351562, 310.3399963378906, 258.20001220703125], \"xref\": 327, \"width\": 412, \"height\": 373}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-328.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [175.0, 477.3999938964844, 338.6499938964844, 600.1099853515625], \"xref\": 328, \"width\": 500, \"height\": 375}]'),
(38,'                                                            31\n\n\nFor example:\n    •   “Is it alive?”\n    •  “Is it an object?”\n    •   “Is it fictional?”\n    •  “Is it bigger than a toaster?\nQuestion 3\n\n\n\n\n\nTake turns asking more specific yes-or-no questions to narrow it down. You\ncan ask questions in any order that you’d like, but make sure each player\ngets to ask at least 1 question.  If a player asks a question that can’t be\nanswered with “yes” or “no,” ask them to rephrase it so that it can be, since\nthe person who’s “it” can only say “yes” or “no.”\n\n    •  For example, a player couldn’t ask, “How old are they?” or “What do\n      they look like?” They could ask, “Are they older than 50?” or, “Do they\n     have blonde hair?”\n\nThink about the questions that have already been asked before you ask new\nquestions. For example, you could go from \"Is it a person?\" to \"Is it a man?\"\nto \"Are they alive?\" to \"Do they live in the Philippines?\"\n\nPlay until you reach 10 questions or someone guesses the right answer.\nBecome the next “it” person if you guess the correct answer. The person who\nguesses the right answer is next to be “it,” and they think up their own secret\nperson, place, or thing. If no one guessed the person, place, or thing at all,\nwhoever wants to go next can have a turn. Keep the game going until everyone\nhas had a chance to be “it.”\n\n    •   If someone guesses correctly but they’ve already been “it,” let someone\n      else have a turn instead. Giving everyone a turn makes the game more\n      inclusive and lets everybody have some fun!','assets/module/page-038.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8505249023438, 587.14990234375, 832.7985229492188], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-331.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [154.60000610351562, 155.38998413085938, 344.08001708984375, 297.489990234375], \"xref\": 331, \"width\": 579, \"height\": 434}]'),
(39,'                                                             32\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material DESCRIBE and DRAW\n\nInstructions: 1. Show a picture. The picture might be a page from a\nstorybook that the children are familiar with, or a picture like one of these.\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Ask children what they see. “What do you see in the pictures?” or “What do\nyou notice?” Learners’ answers may vary. Accept their responses. We want to\nget children to begin to describe what they see in their own words.\n\n    •   “I see a house.”\n    •   “I see a door.”\n    •   “I see a girl flying.”\n    •   “I see a weird tree.”\n    •   “I see squares.”\n\n3. Take the children\'s lead and respond to their ideas by asking follow-up\nquestions.\n\n    •  \"Where do you see the squares? Where do you see the tree?\"\n    •  \"Can you tell me more about the         ?\"\n    •  \"What do you notice about what’s in the back corner?\"\n    •  \"How do you know that this is a triangle? Are those both triangles?\"\n\n4. Ask students to draw what they see.\n\n    •  Keep the picture available; this is not a memory task.\n    •  This is not a tracing task either. We are asking children to draw what\n     they see. (Expect a wide variety!)\n    •  Children often begin by drawing portions of the picture (just the tree,\n       just the oval for the body of the tree, etc.)','assets/module/page-039.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8505249023438, 587.14990234375, 832.7985229492188], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-334.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [72.8499984741211, 135.25003051757812, 507.8900146484375, 266.1500244140625], \"xref\": 334, \"width\": 1000, \"height\": 301}]'),
(40,'                                                            33\n\n\n5. Move around and check in with them as they are drawing. You can ask\nquestions based on what they drew to get them to add detail to their drawing.\nIt  is important to remember  that the children should not think  that the\ndrawing has to look exactly like the picture. “You draw what YOU see.” (You\nmight want to remind children that what they see might be different from\nwhat someone else sees.)\n\n6. As children finish, ask them to tell you about what they drew. Older\nchildren could describe their own drawing to a partner, and vice versa. After\ndiscussion, children should go back and add detail to their own drawing.\n\n7. Consider sharing a couple of examples of children\'s drawings and ask the\nchild to describe to the class what they drew. Let other children add what they\nsee. Use this as an opportunity to highlight one idea you want to engage the\ngroup in (perhaps noticing a particular shape, or using specific language such\nas above or below, or describing where an object is in relation to other\nobjects).','assets/module/page-040.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8497314453125, 585.89990234375, 832.7977294921875], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-337.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [90.75, 319.2799987792969, 242.83999633789062, 475.7799987792969], \"xref\": 337, \"width\": 431, \"height\": 470}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-338.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [276.70001220703125, 319.2799987792969, 435.1000061035156, 475.7799987792969], \"xref\": 338, \"width\": 200, \"height\": 168}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-339.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [100.0, 510.79998779296875, 464.0799865722656, 770.8499755859375], \"xref\": 339, \"width\": 599, \"height\": 428}]'),
(41,'34','assets/module/page-041.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8497314453125, 585.89990234375, 832.7977294921875], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-342.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [20.100000381469727, 82.60000610351562, 293.77001953125, 275.75], \"xref\": 342, \"width\": 248, \"height\": 175}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-343.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [316.8500061035156, 80.5, 562.5999755859375, 273.6199951171875], \"xref\": 343, \"width\": 228, \"height\": 188}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-344.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [131.5500030517578, 525.1199951171875, 467.72003173828125, 731.1699829101562], \"xref\": 344, \"width\": 800, \"height\": 408}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-345.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [157.8000030517578, 296.8200378417969, 431.489990234375, 525.9200439453125], \"xref\": 345, \"width\": 207, \"height\": 173}]'),
(42,'                                                            35\n\n\n\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material TONGUE TWISTER\n\n1. Sheena leads, Sheila needs.\n\n2. How can a clam cram in a clean cream can?\n\n3. Top chopstick shops stock top chopsticks.\n\n4. Selfish shellfish.\n\n5. I saw a kitten eating chicken in the kitchen.\n\n6. Near an ear, a nearer ear, a nearly eerie ear.\n\n7. Roberta ran rings around the Roman ruins.\n\n8. He threw three free throws.\n\n9. A happy hippo hopped and hiccupped.\n\n10. Toy boat. Toy boat. Toy boat.\n\n11. Smelly shoes and socks shock sisters.\n\n12. Four fine fresh fish for you.\n\n13. Red lorry, yellow lorry (x3)\n\n14. Freshly-fried flying fish. (x3)','assets/module/page-042.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8499755859375, 586.659912109375, 832.75], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-348.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [114.25, 74.5, 500.7200012207031, 306.0], \"xref\": 348, \"width\": 736, \"height\": 441}]'),
(43,'                                                             36\n\nAdditional Intervention Material STORY CHAIN\n\nLearning Competency: Retelling a short story.\n\nInstructions: A Story Chain is a simple method of passing a story around the\nclass, giving each student plenty of practice in storytelling.\n\nPrepare a Story\n\nChoose short stories that can be told in 1-3 minutes. You may consider these\nsample stories suggested.\n\nRunning the Activity\n\nLet the pupils come together in pairs. Each pair is given a short picture story.\nGive them time to work through it by making their own story about the\npictures. At the teachers signal, students A will tell the story to student B.\nBreak up the pair and each child gets a new partner. They take turns in\nretelling the story they heard from their first partners to the new partner.\n\nYou can do this several times. At the end you get individuals from the class\nto recite the last story they heard and then compare it with the original, see if\nit has improved in the storytelling, see what facts have changed completely\nand maybe see if the original storyteller can actually recognize their story!\n\n\nSample stories:\n\nStory 1 Mia and the Shiny Stone\n\nI have a cat named Mia. It is an orange cat. While playing in the garden, Mia\nfound a shiny blue stone. She found it under the red rose buds. The stone\nwas so smooth and bright that Mia held it gently in her paws. Suddenly, the\nstone made a tiny ding sound! Mia’s ears perked up.\n\nStory 2 Pip the Rabbit and the Wobbly Carrot\n\nMy father bought me a rabbit. I named him Pip. Pip, is a white rabbit. It dug\nup a purple carrot. It felt soft and wobbly in his teeth. Pip was about to take\na bite but the carrot jiggled on its own!\n\nStory 3 Lila the Bird’s Lost Song\n\nMy sister Jessa has a blue bird that forgot how to sing. It did not cheep. No\ntrills, no sound, just silence. The bird found a tiny paper note under a tree\nand pecked it. The note made a sweet tinkle sound.','assets/module/page-043.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [4.949900150299072, 778.30029296875, 588.3499145507812, 835.2453002929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(44,'                                                             37\n\n\nStory 4 \"The Shell at the Beach\"\n\n\"Yesterday, I walked along the sandy beach and found a small shell. It glowed\npurple and green in the sun! I held it to my ear and listened to the sound of\nthe sea. I wanted to keep it forever.\"\n\n Story 5 \"The Bell on the Playground\"\n\n\"At recess today, I found a tiny brass bell under the slide. When I shook it, it\nsounded like soft music! I showed it to my friend Leo. Then we took turns\nringing it. It was the prettiest sound I’d ever heard.\"\nStory 6 \"The Feather in the Garden\"\n\n\"One morning, I was watering flowers in the garden. A feather floated down.\nIt was soft and white, and it fluttered in the wind before landing in my hand.\nI think it came from the big bird in the tree above our house.\"\n\nUnknown word: fluttered (moved lightly up and down)\n\nStory 7 \"The Toy in My Closet\"\n\n\"I was cleaning my closet and found an old toy car I’d forgotten. When I\npushed it, the wheels whirred fast across the floor! It was red and shiny, even\nafter all these years. I couldn’t wait to play with it again.\"\nUnknown word: whirred (made a soft, buzzing sound)\n\nStory 8 \"The Stone in the Forest\"\n\n\"On our walk in the forest, I spotted a smooth stone by the path. It was\npolished until it looked like glass. It was like the color of the sky. I put it in\nmy pocket to show my mom. My Mom loves pretty rocks.\"\n\nUnknown word: polished (made smooth and shiny)\n\nStory 9 \"The Butterfly on the Flower\"\n\n\"I sat on the porch and watched a butterfly land on a pink rose. Its wings were\norange and black, and it darted from flower to flower. I held my breath so I\nwouldn’t scare it away. It stayed for five whole minutes!\"\n\nUnknown word: darted (moved quickly and suddenly)\n\nStory 10 \"The Note in the Library\"\n\n\"I was looking for a book in the library when I found a note under a shelf. It\nwas scrawled in colorful crayon and said, ‘Meet me at the park!’ I wondered\nwho wrote it—maybe my friend Lila?\"\n\nUnknown word: scrawled (written quickly and messily)','assets/module/page-044.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [4.949900150299072, 778.30029296875, 588.3499145507812, 835.2453002929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(45,'                                                             38\n\n\nStory 11 \"The Leaf on the Bench\"\n\n\"I sat on the park bench and picked up a leaf from the ground.  It was\ncrimson—a bright, deep red—like a little fire! I pressed it between two pages\nof my notebook to keep it safe. It’s my favorite leaf ever.\"\n\nUnknown word: crimson (a dark red color)\n\nStory 12 \"The Cookie Cutter in the Kitchen\"\n\n\"I was helping mom bake cookies when I found a tiny cookie cutter in the\ndrawer. It had an intricate shape—like a little star with lots of points! We used\nit to make star cookies, and they tasted so good.\"\n\nUnknown word: intricate (having many small, detailed parts)\n\nStory 13 \"The Button at the Bus Stop\"\n\n\"While waiting for the bus, I found a button on the ground. It was silver and\nit glinted in the sunlight. It is like a tiny moon! I showed it to the bus driver.\nThe driver helped me put it in the ‘lost and found’ box.\"\nUnknown word: glinted (shone with a quick, bright flash)\n\n\nAdditional Intervention Material PICTURE TALK\n\nPicture  talk  activities  are  engaging  ways  to  enhance  children\'s\ncommunication   skills,  imagination,  and  comprehension  through\ndiscussions bas ed on images.\n\nWhat is a Picture Talk Activity?\n\nA picture talk activity involves using images to spark discussions among\nchildren. This method encourages kids to observe, analyze, and express their\nthoughts about  the  pictures,  fostering  their communication and  literacy\nskills.  It can be adapted to various topics, making  it a versatile tool for\nlearning.\n\nSteps to Create Picture Talk Activities\n\n   1. Choose a Topic: Select a theme that interests the children, such as\n      nature, family, or sports. This helps to engage them more effectively.\n\n   2. Select a Picture: Find a picture that aligns with the chosen topic. It\n      should be  visually  stimulating and  rich  in  details  to  encourage\n      discussion.\n\n   3. Project the Image: Display the picture on a screen or print it out for\n      the children to see. This allows everyone to focus on the same visual\n      stimulus.','assets/module/page-045.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [4.949900150299072, 778.30029296875, 588.3499145507812, 835.2453002929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(46,'TEACHER’S\n  GUIDE','assets/module/page-046.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(47,'                                                              39\n\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 1: ORAL LANGUAGE – TALKING ABOUT ONESELF AND\n ONE’S FAMILY\n\n   I. OBJECTIVES\n\n At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to:\n\n         1. Share at least 3 key details about oneself (name, age, favorite\n\n            activity) using clear voice and proper pronunciation;\n\n         2. Talk about 2-3 family members and one thing they like to do\n\n            together;\n\n         3. Maintain eye contact with their audience when speaking.\n\n   II. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n       Topic:  Sharing  Information  About  Oneself  and  One’s  Family\n\n       References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula\n\n       and early childhood language development resources\n\n       Materials: Visual aid (chart) with sentence starters:\n     \"My name is…\", \"I am     years old…\",\n      \"My family has…\", \"We like to…\"\n        Picture cards (e.g., of a child, parents, siblings, toys, activities like playing, eating)\n       \"About Me\" worksheet (simple, with spaces for drawing and writing key details)\n       Evaluation checklist\n  III. PROCEDURE\n  1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES\n\n      Warm-Up\n\n            Lead a \"Hello Circle\" – students stand in a circle, take turns\n\n       saying \"Hello, my name is [name]\" while waving to the class.\n\n       Encourage them to speak loudly enough for everyone to hear.','assets/module/page-047.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(48,'                                                            40\n\n     Motivation\n\n         Show a colorful picture of a happy family doing an activity (e.g.,\n\n      picnicking). Ask: \"Who do you see in this picture?\" and \"What do you\n\n       like to do with your own family?\" Let 2-3 students share a quick\n\n      thought to get them excited to talk more.\n\n\n\n\n\n2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES\n\n           Introduce Key Details: Display the visual aid with sentence\n\n       starters. Explain that when we talk about ourselves and our family, we\n\n     can use these sentences to make our ideas clear.\n\n           Modeling: Share your own example in simple terms: \"Hi, my name\n\n        is [Teacher’s Name]. I am [age]. My favorite activity is reading. My family\n\n      has [2 people] – my mom and dad. We like to cook together on\n\n     weekends!\" Use picture cards to point to each detail as you speak.\n\n            Practice Sentence Starters: Have the class repeat the sentence\n\n       starters together 2-3 times to build confidence.\n\n3. GUIDED PRACTICE\n\n           Pair-and-Share Activity: Divide students into pairs. Give each\n\n      pair a set of picture cards to use as clues.\n\n             1. Student A uses  the  sentence  starters  to  share  about\n\n              themselves and their family.','assets/module/page-048.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-361.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [151.5, 195.5, 420.25, 366.8800048828125], \"xref\": 361, \"width\": 586, \"height\": 586}]'),
(49,'                                                             41\n\n\n\n             2. Student B listens, then shares their own information.\n\n             3. Circulate around  pairs  to  support: remind them  to speak\n\n              clearly, make eye contact, and use the picture cards if they get\n\n             stuck.\n\n            After 8 minutes, ask 2-3 volunteer pairs to share one thing they\n\n            learned about each other with the class.\n\nIV. EVALUATION\n\nUse an informal checklist to assess each student during pair-and-share:\n\n\n 1              2              3              4\n Pupil  mentioned Pupil mentioned  Pupil mentioned  Pupil mentioned\n  only his name   2  –  3   details  4  details  about  5 or more details\n                   about himself      himself           about himself\n\nV. AGREEMENT\n\n\nFor homework, students will:\n\n     Talk to one family member about their favorite activity.\n\n     Draw a picture of themselves and that family member doing that\n\nactivity.\n\n    Be ready to share what they learned with the class in our next lesson.','assets/module/page-049.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(50,'                                                           42\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 2: ORAL LANGUAGE – FOLLOWING 1-TO-2-STEP ORAL\nDIRECTIONS\n\nI. OBJECTIVES\nAt the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to:\n\n       1. Listen carefully to follow 1-step and 2-step oral directions accurately;\n\n       2. Repeat the directions in their own words before completing them;\n\n       3. Perform the actions in the correct order.\n\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n      Topic: Following 1-to-2-Step Oral Directions\n\n      References: Adapted from elementary  oral language  curricula and\n\n     motor skill development resources\n\n      Materials: Visual aid (chart) with action words and pictures:\n      go, close, run, pick up, sit, give Simple props\n      (classroom items): door, chair, book, pencil, toy\n      \"Mission Card\" set (with pre-written 1-2 step directions) Informal\n      evaluation checklist\nIII. PROCEDURE\n1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES\n\n     Warm-Up (5 minutes):\n\n               Lead the class in an \"Action Song\" (e.g., \"Head, Shoulders,\n\n           Knees, and Toes\") – have them follow along with your oral cues to\n\n             practice listening and moving.\n\n     Motivation (5 minutes):\n\n                 Say: \"Today, you’re all secret agents on a special mission!\n\n         To complete the mission, you need to listen very carefully to my\n\n             directions. If you follow them right, you’ll get a ‘Secret Agent\n\n          Badge’ at the end!\" Show a fun, hand-drawn badge to build\n\n            excitement.','assets/module/page-050.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-366.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [216.0, 611.760009765625, 408.8900146484375, 712.010009765625], \"xref\": 366, \"width\": 424, \"height\": 220}]'),
(51,'                                                              43\n\n\n     2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES\n\n                Introduce 1-Step Directions: Display the visual aid with action\n\n          words. Give a simple 1-step direction (e.g., \"Go to the chair\") and do it\n\n           yourself to model. Ask 3-4 students to try one at a time, reminding them\n\n           to listen first.\n\n                Introduce 2-Step Directions: Explain that 2-step directions have\n\n         two actions to do in order. Model with: \"Go to the door, then close it.\"\n\n           Point to the visual cards for \"go\" and \"close\" as you speak. Have\n\n           students repeat the direction back to you before trying it.\n\n                 Practice Repeating Directions: Teach them the phrase, \"Let me\n\n          say  that  back:  [repeat  direction]\"  to make  sure  they  understand.\n\n           Practice with 2-3 more 2-step directions (e.g., \"Pick up the book, then\n\n          give it to me\").\n     3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION (5 minutes)\n Ask: \"When I said ‘Go to the door and close it,’ what was the first thing  you did?\n    The second thing?\" (Let students share to confirm order.)\n Follow up: \"Why is it important to listen to all the direction before you start?\" (e.g.,\n\n        \"So, you don’t forget the second step,\" \"So, you do it right\")\n\nRemind them: \"Repeating the direction back helps you make sure you heard it\n\n                                  correctly!\"ss\n\n\n     4. GUIDED PRACTICE (10 minutes)\n\n                 Secret Agent Mission Trail: Set up 3 simple stations around the\n\n          classroom. Give each small group (4-5 students) a \"Mission Card\" with\n\n          a 1 or 2-step direction for each station.\n\n                 Station 1: \"Go to the window and tap it twice.\"\n\n                 Station 2: \"Pick up one pencil and put it on the table.\"\n\n                 Station 3: \"Run to the whiteboard, then sit down in your chair.\"\n\n             Each student takes a turn leading their group through a station:\n\n          they read the mission card aloud, their group repeats  it back, then\n\n         everyone does the action together. Circulate to support and remind\n\n          students      to     follow     order    and     speak      clearly','assets/module/page-051.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(52,'                                                            44\n\n\nIV. EVALUATION (5 minutes)\n\n     Give 1-2 steps directions orally. Rate the learner’s performance based\non the rubrics.\n\nRubrics\n\n 1              2              3              4\n Pupil attempted  Pupil  executed  Pupil   executed   Pupil executed\n to do what has  wrongly   what  incompletely       accurately what\n been said.        has been said.    what has been     has been said.\n                                       said\n\n\n\nV. AGREEMENT\n     For homework, students will:\n\n     Be a \"family helper\" by following one 2-step direction from a parent or\n\n      guardian (e.g., \"Set one plate on the table and put a fork next to it\").\n\n     Ask their family member to give them a \"thumbs up\" if they did it right.\n\n      Share what direction they followed with the class in our next lesson.\n\n\n LESSON PLAN 3: ORAL LANGUAGE – GIVING AND FOLLOWING 1-TO-2-\n         STEP ORAL DIRECTIONS IN DAILY SITUATIONS\n\nI. OBJECTIVE\n          At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to:\n\n       1. Give clear 1-2 step oral directions using polite language (e.g., \"can\n\n         you,\" \"please\");\n\n       2. Follow 1-2 step directions given by a peer;\n\n       3. Identify 2 key elements of effective directions (clarity, order) and\n\n       polite communication.\n\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n     Topic: Giving and Following 1-2-Step Directions (e.g., Asking a Sibling\n\n      to Tidy Up Toys)\n\n      References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula\n\n     and social skills development resources','assets/module/page-052.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(53,'                                                             45\n\n\n      Materials:\n\n            Visual aid (chart) with:\n\n             Polite phrase starters: \"Can you…?\", \"Please…\", \"Thank you!\"\n\n           Action words with pictures: keep away, put in, pick up, close\n\n         Toy props (dolls, cars, blocks, a toy box)\n\n           Scenario cards (e.g., \"Your younger sister left toys on the floor\",\n\n          \"Your brother left books on the couch\")\n\n           Informal evaluation checklist\nIII. PROCEDURE\n\n1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES\n\n     Warm-Up (5 minutes):\n\n          Lead a \"Follow My Lead\" game: Give 1-2 step directions (e.g., \"Pick\n\n     up one block and put  it on your desk\") and have the class do them\n\n       together. This review following directions before moving to giving them.\n\n      Motivation (5 minutes):\n\n         Show a photo of a messy playroom with toys scattered. Ask: \"Have\n\n     you ever seen a room like this? If your younger sister made this mess,\n\n     what would you say to ask her to clean it up?\" Let 2-3 students share\n\n      quick ideas, then say: \"Today, we’ll learn to ask nicely with clear steps\n\n      so she knows exactly what to do!\"\n\n\n\n\n\n2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES (15 minutes)\n\n            Introduce Polite Directions: Display the visual aid with polite\n\n      phrases. Explain that when asking someone to do something, we use\n\n      kind words like \"can you\" and \"please\" to be respectful.','assets/module/page-053.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-102.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [185.9499969482422, 501.8500061035156, 441.6499938964844, 671.5], \"xref\": 102, \"width\": 609, \"height\": 404}]'),
(54,'                                                              46\n\n\n\n          Model Giving 2-Step Directions: Use the toy props to act out the\n       scenario: \"Let’s pretend this doll is my younger sister, and these\n\n     toys are on the floor. I’ll say: “‘Can you please keep your toys\n\n    away and put them in the toy box?’\" Point to the action pictures\n\n      (\"keep away,\" \"put in\") as you speak to show order.\n\n            Practice Making Directions: Have the class repeat the model\n\n      direction together. Then, guide them to make their own 2-step direction\n\n      for the same scenario (e.g., \"Can you pick up your cars and put them\n\n     on the shelf?\").\n\n\n\n\n\n3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION\n\n           Ask: \"What polite words did I use when asking my sister?\" (Let\n\n      students point to the visual aid.)\n\n           Follow up: \"What was the first step I asked her to do? The second\n\n      step? Why is order important?\" (e.g., \"So, she doesn’t get confused,\"\n\n      \"So, she does everything needed\").\n\n           Ask: \"How do you think your sister would feel if you asked nicely\n\n       vs. yelling?\" (Emphasize that polite language helps others want to help.)\n\n4. GUIDED PRACTICE\n\n           Role-Play Activity: Divide students into pairs. Give each pair a\n\n      scenario card and toy props.\n\n             1. One student plays the \"older sibling\" and gives a clear 2-step\n\n      direction using polite language.\n\n             2. The other student plays the \"younger sibling,\"  listens, and\n\n      follows the directions.\n\n             3. Pairs switch roles after 5 minutes.','assets/module/page-054.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-375.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [221.60000610351562, 240.66000366210938, 373.6500244140625, 367.05999755859375], \"xref\": 375, \"width\": 465, \"height\": 387}]'),
(55,'                                                             47\n\n\n      Circulate around pairs to support: Remind them to use polite phrases,\n\n     keep directions clear, and follow steps in order. Example scenario\n\n     prompt for pairs: \"Your younger sister left her dolls on the bed. What 2\n\n      things will you ask her to do?\"\n\n\nIV. EVALUATION\n     Use an informal checklist to assess each student during the role-play:\n\n\n                Uses polite       Gives clear        Listens\n                 language (can     2-step directions   carefully and\n      Criteria     you, please)       in the correct      follows peer’s\n              when giving      order              directions\n                   directions                           accurately\n Excellent\n Good\n Satisfactory\n\nNote: Assess by observing each pair once. Give positive feedback like, \"Great\njob asking nicely!\" or \"Perfect order of steps!\"\n\nV. AGREEMENT\n     For homework, students will:\n\n          Find a time to give one 2-step direction to a family member\n\n       (sibling, parent, or grandparent) using polite language (e.g., \"Can you\n\n      please pass the spoon and put it on my plate?\").\n\n         Ask the family member if they understood the directions.\n\n      Share what direction they gave and how it went with the class in our\n\n      next lesson.','assets/module/page-055.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(56,'                                                            48\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 4: ORAL LANGUAGE – USING DESCRIBING WORDS FOR\nPEOPLE, OBJECTS, THINGS, AND PLACES\n\nI. OBJECTIVE\n     At the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to:\n\n       1. Use describing words to talk about people, objects, things, and\n\n          places. (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.)\n\n       2. Match describing words to the attributes of what they are talking\n\n         about.\n\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n     Topic: Recognizing and Using Describing Words (Attributes: Color,\n\n            Shape, Size, Height, Weight, Length, Distance)\n\n      References: Adapted from elementary oral language curricula\n\n               and vocabulary development resources\n\n      Materials:\n\n            Visual aid (chart) titled \"My Describing Word Toolkit\" – with words\n\n          and pictures: red, round, big, tall, heavy, long, far, cozy\n\n           Props with different attributes: colored blocks, toys (ball, box, car,\n\n              doll), books (thick, thin), plants  (tall, short), photos of places\n\n           (sunny park, quiet room)\n\n            \"Describe It!\" cards (with pictures of people, objects, or places)\n\n            Informal evaluation checklist\nIII. PROCEDURE\n\n1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES\n     Warm-Up (5 minutes):\n\n               Lead a \"Word Clue\" game: Say, \"I’m thinking of something\n\n             in this room that’s red. Can you guess what it is?\" Have students\n\n              call out guesses, then reveal the object. Repeat with 3-4 more\n\n            clues (e.g., \"It’s round,\" \"It’s big\").','assets/module/page-056.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(57,'                                                            49\n\n     Motivation (5 minutes):\n             Show a plain drawing of a cat and a colorful, detailed\n\n           drawing of the same cat. Ask: \"Which drawing tells you more\n\n           about  the  cat? Why?\"  Explain:  \"Today,  we’ll  learn  about\n\n            ‘describing words’ – these are like magic clues that help us tell\n\n            others exactly what something looks like, feels like, or where it\n\n               is!\"\n\n\n\n\n\n2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES\n\n            Introduce Describing Words: Display  the \"Describing Word\n\n      Toolkit\" chart. Point to each word and its picture, and say: \"These words\n\n      describe attributes – what something is like. ‘Red’ is a color, ‘round’ is\n\n      a shape, ‘big’ is a size, and ‘far’ is a distance.\" Have students repeat\n\n     each word aloud.\n\n      Describing words toolkit (see intervention materials)\n\n      1. Colors\n\n           Red, blue, green, yellow, orange, purple, pink, black, white,\n\n     brown\n\n      2. Sizes\n\n            Big, small, tall, short, long, tiny, huge\n\n      3. Shapes\n\n          Round, square, triangle, circle, flat','assets/module/page-057.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-384.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [154.9499969482422, 213.3699951171875, 316.5, 363.1499938964844], \"xref\": 384, \"width\": 490, \"height\": 454}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-219.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [325.04998779296875, 213.17001342773438, 472.54998779296875, 360.6700134277344], \"xref\": 219, \"width\": 447, \"height\": 447}]'),
(58,'                                                             50\n\n\n      4. How things feel\n\n             Soft, hard, smooth, rough, sticky, fuzzy, warm, cold\n\n      5. How things look\n\n             Bright, dark, shiny, dull, clean, dirty, wet, dry\n\n      6. Feelings (for people/animals)\n\n          Happy, sad, angry, scared, excited, sleepy, friendly\n\n      7. Numbers  (to describe how many)\n\n          One, two, three, many, few, some\n\n\n         Model Using Describing Words: Pick up a red, round ball and\n\n      say: \"This is a red, round ball. It’s also small enough to hold in my\n\n      hand.\" Then point to a tall plant: \"This is a tall, green plant that’s near\n\n      the window.\" Model for a person too: \"My friend Lila has curly, brown\n\n      hair and she is happy today.\"\n\n            Practice Naming Attributes: Hold up different props (e.g., thick\n\n     book, heavy toy car) and ask: \"What describing words can we use for\n\n      this?\" Have students share ideas and match them to the chart.\n\n3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION\n\n           Ask: \"Why do we use describing words when we talk?\" (Let\n\n      students share: \"So others can picture what we’re talking about,\" \"To\n\n     be clear,\" \"To make stories more fun\").\n\n           Follow up: \"Can we use more than one describing word for\n\n     something? Let’s try with the box –  is  it square, blue, or both?\"\n\n     (Emphasize that we can combine words to give more details).\n\n           Ask: \"What describing words can we use for places, like a park?\"\n\n       (e.g., \"sunny,\" \"big,\" \"busy\").','assets/module/page-058.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(59,'                                                           51\n\n    4. GUIDED PRACTICE\n\n\n           \"Describe It! Share It!\" Activity: Divide students into pairs. Give\n\n     each pair a \"Describe It!\" card with a picture (e.g., a cozy bedroom, a\n\n      small yellow bird, a tall boy).\n\n             1. One student looks at the picture and thinks of 2-3 describing\n\n     words for it.\n\n             2. They say the describing words aloud (e.g., \"It’s cozy, quiet, and\n\n      has a big bed\") and their partner guesses what’s in the picture.\n\n             3. Pairs switch roles after 5 minutes.\n\n            Circulate to support: Remind students to use words from the\n\n      toolkit and match them to the picture’s attributes.\n\n\nIV. EVALUATION\n\n          Use an informal checklist to assess each student during the pair\n\n       activity:\n\n                   Recognizes   Uses describing      Matches\n                and names at words to talk about   describing\n          Criteria    least 4        people/objects/places words to the\n                    describing                           correct\n                  words                               attributes\n       Excellent\n\n      Good\n\n       Satisfactory\n\n\nNote: Assess by listening to each pair’s descriptions. Give positive feedback\n\nlike, \"Great use of ‘cozy’ for the bedroom!\" or \"Perfect – ‘tall’ matches the boy!\"\n\nV. AGREEMENT\n\nFor homework, students will:\n        1. Pick one thing at home (a toy, a pet, a room) and think of 3 describing\n\n     words for it.\n\n        2. Tell a family member about it using those words (e.g., \"My dog is\n\n     brown, fluffy, and small\").\n\nBe ready to share their description with the class in our next lesson.','assets/module/page-059.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(60,'                                                            52\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 5: ORAL LANGUAGE – TALKING ABOUT TOPICS OF\n              INTEREST THROUGH PICTURE DISCUSSION\n\n\nI. OBJECTIVE\n\nAt the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to:\n\n      1. Share their thoughts, feelings, and observations about a picture on\n\n         a topic of interest;\n\n      2. Use simple sentence starters to organize their ideas clearly;\n\n      3. Listen  respectfully  to  peers’  thoughts and  ask one  follow-up\n\n         question.\n\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n      Topic: Talking About Topics of Interest (e.g., Toys, Pets, Parks, Festivals)\n\n       \"Tell Me What You Think About This Picture\"\n\n      References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula\n\n     and expressive language development resources\n\n      Materials:\n\n      Visual aid  (chart) with sentence  starters:  \"I see…\",  \"I think…\",  \"I\n\n      like…\", \"It makes me feel…\"\n\n      Colorful pictures of kid-friendly topics (e.g., a dog playing in a park, a\n\n      birthday party with toys, a colorful festival, a cozy pet corner)\n\n      \"Picture Card Packs\" (one pack per small group, with 3-4 pictures on\n\n       different interests) informal evaluation checklist.\n\n1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES\n\n     Warm-Up\n\n          Lead a \"Quick Share\" circle: Ask students to say one sentence\n\n      about something they love (e.g., \"I love my cat\" or \"I love playing with\n\n      blocks\"). Encourage them to speak clearly and look at the class.\n\n      valuation checklist','assets/module/page-060.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(61,'                                                             53\n\n\n     Motivation\n\n          Hold up a bright picture of a dog playing in a sunny park. Ask:\n\n    \"Who likes dogs? What do you see in this picture? What do you think\n\n      the dog is feeling?\" Let 2-3 students call out quick ideas, then say:\n\n     \"Today, we’ll learn to share our thoughts about pictures we like – and\n\n      hear what our friends think too!\"\n\n2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES\n\n            Introduce  Picture  Discussion:  Display  the  visual  aid  with\n\n      sentence  starters. Explain: \"When we  talk about a  picture,  these\n\n      sentences help us share what’s in our minds clearly. ‘I see’ tells what’s\n\n      in the picture, ‘I think’ tells our ideas, and ‘I like’ tells what we enjoy\n\n      about it.\"\n\n          Model Sharing Thoughts: Focus on the dog park picture and\n\n      share your own ideas: \"I see a brown dog running with a red ball. I think\n\n      the dog is happy because it’s playing in the sun. I like this picture\n\n      because it makes me want to go to the park too!\" Point to each sentence\n\n       starter on the chart as you speak.\n\n            Practice Sentence Starters: Have the class repeat the sentence\n\n       starters together. Then, guide them to share one thought about the dog\n\n      picture using one of the starters (e.g., \"I see green grass\" or \"It makes\n\n    me feel happy\").\n3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION\n\n          Ask: \"What did I do to help you understand my thoughts about\n\n      the picture?\" (Let students point to the sentence starters.)\n\n           Follow up: \"Why is it fun to talk about pictures we like?\" (e.g.,\n\n     \"Because we can share what we love,\" \"We can learn what our friends\n\n       like too\").\n\n          Remind them: \"When others are talking, we listen carefully –\n\n       that’s how we show we care about their ideas. We can even ask a small\n\n      question, like ‘Why do you like that?’ to learn more!\"','assets/module/page-061.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(62,'                                                               54\n\n\n    4. GUIDED PRACTICE\n\n                \"Picture Talk\" Groups: Divide students into small groups (4-5\n\n          students each) and give each group a \"Picture Card Pack\" with topics\n\n           they’ll enjoy.\n\n                 1. One student picks a picture from the pack and shares their\n\n         thoughts using at least 2 sentence starters.\n\n                 2. The other students  listen, then one peer asks a follow-up\n\n          question (e.g., \"What do you think the cat in the picture is eating?\").\n\n                 3. Everyone in the group takes a turn picking a picture and\n\n          sharing.\n\n               Circulate around groups to support: Remind them to use the\n\n         sentence starters, speak clearly, and listen respectfully.\n\n\n     III. EVALUATION\n\n   Use an informal checklist to assess each student during group discussion:\n                  Shares              Uses at least 2  Listens\n                   thoughts/observations sentence       respectfully to\n         Criteria    about the picture      starters to     peers and asks\n                                        organize ideas a follow-up\n                                                       question\n     Excellent\n\n    Good\n\n     Satisfactory\n\nNote: Assess by observing each student’s participation. Give positive feedback   like,\n\n  \"Great use of ‘I think’!\" or \"Nice question – that helped us learn more!\"\n\n\n    IV. AGREEMENT\n\n    For homework, students will:\n\n          1. - Find a picture at home (in a book, on a calendar, or on a device\n\n         with family help) of something they love.\n\n          2. - Talk to a family member about  it using the sentence starters: \"I\n\n         see…\", \"I think…\", \"I like…\".\n\n   Be ready to share their picture and thoughts with the class in our next lesson.','assets/module/page-062.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(63,'                                                            55\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 6: ORAL LANGUAGE – DESCRIBE AND DRAW\n\nI. OBJECTIVE\n\nAt the end of the 45-minute lesson, the students will be able to:\n\n      1. Use specific describing words (color, shape, size, position) to clearly\n\n         describe a picture;\n\n      2. Listen carefully to a peer’s description and draw what they hear;\n\n      3. Identify 2-3 key details that make a description easy to understand.\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n      Topic: \"Describe and Draw\" – Using Oral Language to Share Visual\n\n              Details\n\n      References: Adapted from elementary oral communication curricula\n\n     and expressive/receptive language development resources\n\n      Materials:\n\n     Teacher’s model picture (colorful, with clear details: e.g., a red\n\n      bird on a green tree branch next to a yellow flower)\n\n      Set of \"secret pictures\" (one per pair, different from each other, with\n\n      simple, distinct elements)\n\n      Paper and crayons for each student\n      Visual aid (chart) with key describing words: color words, round, square,\n\n       big, small, on top, next to, under\n\n      Informal evaluation checklist\n\nIII. PROCEDURE\n\n1. PREPARATORY ACTIVITIES\n     Warm-Up\n          Lead  a \"Quick Draw\" game:  Say, \"I’m going  to  describe\n\n     something—draw what you hear!\" Describe a simple object (e.g., \"a\n\n      small, blue square\") and have students draw  it. Show their drawings\n\n     and talk about how clear words helped them.\n     Motivation\n\n          Hold up your model picture (but don’t show it to the class yet).\n\n      Say: \"I have a secret picture! If I describe it well enough, you’ll be able\n\n      to draw  it without even seeing  it. Want to try? Today, we’ll play a\n\n      ‘Describe and Draw’ game where we’re like ‘picture messengers’—using\n\n     words to send images to our friends!\"','assets/module/page-063.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(64,'                                                            56\n\n\n2. DEVELOPMENTAL ACTIVITIES\n            Introduce Key  Description  Details:  Display  the  visual  aid.\n\n      Explain: \"When we describe a picture, we need to share details like what\n\n      color things are, what shape they are, how big they are, and where they\n\n       are. These details help the person drawing know exactly what to put on\n\n       their paper.\"\n\n           Teacher Modeling: Show the model picture to the class for 10\n\n      seconds, then hide it. Describe it slowly and clearly: \"First, there’s a big,\n\n      green tree branch in the middle. On top of the branch, there’s a small,\n\n      red bird with a yellow beak. Next to the bird, there’s a bright yellow\n\n      flower with five petals. The sky behind is light blue.\" As you describe,\n\n      point to the words on the visual aid. Have students draw along as you\n\n      speak.\n\n          Check and Discuss: Show  the model  picture again and  let\n\n      students compare their drawings. Ask: \"What details helped you draw\n\n         it right?\" (e.g., \"‘on top’ told me where the bird was,\" \"‘red’ told me its\n\n       color\").\n3. ANALYSIS / DISCUSSION\n\n           Ask: \"What would happen if I forgot to say ‘small’ about the bird?\n\n     Or ‘next to the bird’ about the flower?\" (Let students share: \"I might\n\n     draw a big bird,\" \"I might put the flower somewhere else\").\n\n           Follow up: \"Why is it important to describe things in order (e.g.,\n\n        ‘first the branch, then the bird’)?\" (e.g., \"So the drawer knows what to\n\n     draw first,\" \"It’s easier to follow\").\n\n          Remind students: \"As a describer, your job is to be clear. As a\n\n      drawer, your job is to listen closely—no peeking at the picture!\"\n\n4. GUIDED PRACTICE\n\n             Pair \"Describe and Draw\" Activity: Divide students into pairs.\n\n     Give one student in each pair a \"secret picture\" (they must not show it','assets/module/page-064.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(65,'                                                            57\n\n      to their partner). Assign roles: Describer (has the picture) and Drawer\n\n       (listens and draws).\n\n             1. The Describer looks at their picture and describes it using key\n\n       details and the visual aid words.\n\n             2. The Drawer listens carefully and draws what they hear—they\n\n     can ask clarifying questions (e.g., \"Is the cat big or small?\").\n\n             3. After 5 minutes, have pairs switch roles with a new secret\n\n      picture.\n\n            Circulate around pairs to support: Remind Describers  to use\n\n      specific details and Drawers to listen actively.\nIV. EVALUATION\n\nUse an informal checklist to assess each student in both roles\n\n                Uses specific     Captures key     Listens\n      Criteria     describing words details from the   respectfully and\n                    (color, shape,     description       asks clarifying\n                   position)        As Drawer        questions (when\n               As Describer                       drawing)\n Excellent\n\n Good\n\n Satisfactory\n\n\nNote: Assess by observing pairs and looking at their drawings. Give positive\n\nfeedback like, \"Great job saying ‘under the tree’—that helped your partner\n\ndraw it right!\"\n\nV. AGREEMENT\n\n For homework, students will:\n\n   1. Pick a favorite toy at home (e.g., a doll, car, teddy bear).\n   2. Describe the toy in detail to a family member, who will draw it based on\n\n       their description.\n\n   3. Bring both the toy and the family’s drawing to share with the class in\n\n     our next lesson.','assets/module/page-065.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(66,'                                                            58\n\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 7: ORAL LANGUAGE USING PICTURE STORY CHAIN\n  STRATEGY\n\nI. OBJECTIVES\n\nBy the end of the lesson, students will be able to:\n\n      1. Identify key elements (characters, setting, action) in a sequence of\n\n          pictures.\n      2. Contribute at least one sentence to a group \"picture story chain.\"\n\n      3. Speak clearly and listen attentively when participating in the group\n\n           activity.\n\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n     Topic: Oral Language Development through Picture Story Chain\n\n      Strategy: Picture Story Chain (collaborative storytelling using a\n\n     sequence of images)\n\n      Materials:\n\n            5-6 colorful, simple picture cards (no text) showing a logical\n\n           sequence (e.g., a cat waking up → going outside → finding a ball\n\n     → playing with it → going home → sleeping again)\n\n           Whiteboard   or   chart   paper\n\n          Marker\n\n           Sticky notes for student contributions\n\n              Skills: Speaking, listening, sequencing, critical thinking\n\n           Values Integration: Cooperation, respect for others’ ideas\nIII. LEARNING PROCEDURE\n\n      A. MOTIVATION\n\n           \"Guess the Next Picture\" Game: Show the first 2 picture cards\n\n          one by one.\n\n           Ask: \"What do you see in this picture? What do you think will\n\n          happen next?\"\n\n           Let 2-3 students share their guesses to activate prior knowledge\n\n          and spark curiosity.','assets/module/page-066.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(67,'                                                       59\n\nB. LESSON PROPER\n\n1. INTRODUCTION TO PICTURE STORY CHAIN\n\n  Show all 4-5 picture cards in order on the board.\n\n   Explain: “Today, we’ll make a story together using these pictures!\n\n   This is called a \'Picture Story Chain.\' Each of you will add one\n\n   sentence to the story, just like linking chains together.\" Point to\n\n   each picture and ask students to name what they see (e.g., \"This is\n\n   a cat. This is a garden. This is a ball.\") to build vocabulary.\n\n2. MODELING THE ACTIVITY\n\n       Start the story with the first picture: \"Once upon a time, there\n\nwas a little cat named Mimi. She woke up early in her cozy bed.\"\n\n    Move to the second picture and add: \"Mimi jumped out of bed\n\nand walked outside to the garden.\n\n     Write these sentences on the chart paper to show how the story\n\nbuilds.\n\n     Emphasize: \"Each sentence should connect to the picture and\n\nthe one before it!\"','assets/module/page-067.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [6.199900150299072, 775.8504028320312, 587.14990234375, 832.7984008789062], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-405.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [48.099998474121094, 482.9499816894531, 160.9499969482422, 595.7999877929688], \"xref\": 405, \"width\": 350, \"height\": 350}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-406.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [164.60000610351562, 483.5000305175781, 279.75, 598.6500244140625], \"xref\": 406, \"width\": 349, \"height\": 349}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-407.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [283.45001220703125, 481.6399841308594, 398.6000061035156, 596.7899780273438], \"xref\": 407, \"width\": 349, \"height\": 349}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-408.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [402.3500061035156, 480.9900207519531, 517.5, 596.1400146484375], \"xref\": 408, \"width\": 349, \"height\": 349}]'),
(68,'                                                       60\n\n\n\n3. GROUP PICTURE STORY CHAIN\n\nDivide the class into 5 small groups (4-5 students each).\n\nGroup 1\n\n\n\n\n\nGroup 2','assets/module/page-068.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [20.649999618530273, 772.1499633789062, 579.6600341796875, 829.0999755859375], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-411.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [376.95001220703125, 153.3400115966797, 513.3499755859375, 292.83001708984375], \"xref\": 411, \"width\": 417, \"height\": 405}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-412.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [244.0500030517578, 149.86001586914062, 382.79998779296875, 296.27001953125], \"xref\": 412, \"width\": 424, \"height\": 425}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-413.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [110.5, 152.81997680664062, 249.85000610351562, 297.16998291015625], \"xref\": 413, \"width\": 425, \"height\": 419}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-414.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [243.60000610351562, 604.7470092773438, 390.25, 750.9769897460938], \"xref\": 414, \"width\": 448, \"height\": 447}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-415.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [97.05000305175781, 457.5199890136719, 536.5, 603.7999877929688], \"xref\": 415, \"width\": 1342, \"height\": 447}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-417.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [181.64999389648438, 297.19000244140625, 312.0, 433.6400146484375], \"xref\": 417, \"width\": 342, \"height\": 351}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-418.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [316.45001220703125, 297.19000244140625, 443.70001220703125, 433.6300048828125], \"xref\": 418, \"width\": 337, \"height\": 350}]'),
(69,'                                                       61\n\n\n\n\n\nGroup 3\n\n\n\n\n\nGroup 4','assets/module/page-069.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-421.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [101.75, 134.5499725341797, 221.35000610351562, 254.0999755859375], \"xref\": 421, \"width\": 365, \"height\": 365}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-422.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [358.3500061035156, 135.72999572753906, 475.6000061035156, 252.92999267578125], \"xref\": 422, \"width\": 358, \"height\": 358}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-423.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [229.5500030517578, 133.95001220703125, 348.6000061035156, 252.95001220703125], \"xref\": 423, \"width\": 364, \"height\": 364}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-424.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [81.6500015258789, 463.25, 191.20001220703125, 572.7999877929688], \"xref\": 424, \"width\": 332, \"height\": 332}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-425.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [197.60000610351562, 462.14996337890625, 308.79998779296875, 573.3499755859375], \"xref\": 425, \"width\": 337, \"height\": 337}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-426.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [316.54998779296875, 463.25, 428.3499755859375, 573.4500122070312], \"xref\": 426, \"width\": 339, \"height\": 334}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-427.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [435.29998779296875, 462.94000244140625, 536.3999633789062, 572.489990234375], \"xref\": 427, \"width\": 307, \"height\": 332}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-428.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [162.89999389648438, 270.6000061035156, 287.1499938964844, 394.8500061035156], \"xref\": 428, \"width\": 380, \"height\": 380}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-429.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [291.25, 270.5999755859375, 413.7099914550781, 393.04998779296875], \"xref\": 429, \"width\": 374, \"height\": 374}]'),
(70,'                                                         62\n\nGroup 5\n\n\n\n\n\n   Give each group a set of the picture cards arranged in order.\n\n    Instructions for groups:\n\n     • The first student looks at the first picture and says one sentence.\n     • The next student looks at the second picture and says a sentence\n\n    that connects to the first.\n\n     •  Continue until all pictures are used and the story has an ending.\n\n     • One student from each group will share the complete story with the\n\n    class.\n\n  Roam around groups to support:\n\n   Encourage shy students to participate (e.g., \"Can you say, \'Mimi found\n\n   a red ball\'?\")\n\n   Help students connect their sentences (e.g., \"Great! After Mimi plays\n\n   with the ball, what happens next?\")\n\n   Write key sentences on sticky notes for each group to display.\n\n\n    C. GENERALIZATION\n\n   Gather the class and have both groups share their stories.\n\n   Ask: \"How did working together help us make the story?\" (Expected\n\n   answer: \"We shared ideas, listened to each other, and made a long\n\n    story together.\")\n\n   Conclude: \"Pictures can help us tell\n\n    stories, and when we work in a chain, we can make amazing stories\n\n   with our friends!\"','assets/module/page-070.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-432.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [90.5, 96.79997253417969, 186.3000030517578, 192.5999755859375], \"xref\": 432, \"width\": 293, \"height\": 293}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-433.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [189.1999969482422, 94.9499740600586, 287.3280029296875, 192.5999755859375], \"xref\": 433, \"width\": 300, \"height\": 298}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-434.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [291.3500061035156, 93.19999694824219, 387.1500244140625, 189.0], \"xref\": 434, \"width\": 293, \"height\": 293}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-435.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [388.8999938964844, 91.99500274658203, 482.3999938964844, 190.19000244140625], \"xref\": 435, \"width\": 286, \"height\": 300}]'),
(71,'                                                           63\n\nIV. EVALUATION\n\n      Formative Assessment: Observe students during the group activity\n\nand check if they:\n\n      Contribute at least one sentence.\n\n     Speak clearly.\n\n      Listen to others.\n\n     Use a simple checklist to record participation and understanding.\n\nV. ASSIGNMENT\n\nAt home, ask students to find 3 simple pictures (from a book or magazine)\n\nand make a short story with their family using the picture story chain\n\nstrategy. They will share their story with the class in the next lesson.\n\n\nVI. REFLECTION\n\n(For teacher’s use only: Note which students struggled with\n\nspeaking/listening, which pictures were most effective, and adjustments for\n\nfuture lessons.)\n\n\nPicture Card Suggestion (Description for Visuals):\n\n1. A small cat lying in a bed with eyes closed.\n\n2. The cat standing by an open door, looking outside.\n\n3. The cat in a garden with green grass and flowers.\n\n4. The cat playing with a red ball.\n\n5. The cat walking back towards the house with the ball.\n\n6. The cat back in bed, sleeping with the ball beside it.','assets/module/page-071.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(72,'                                                         64\n\n  LESSON PLAN 8: ORAL LANGUAGE USING PICTURE TALK ACTIVITIES\n\n\nI. OBJECTIVES\n\nBy the end of the lesson, students will be able to:\n\n1. Describe visible details in a picture using simple words and phrases.\n\n2. Ask and answer basic questions about the picture (who, what, where,\n\nwhen, why).\n\n3. Share a short personal connection to the picture in 1-2 clear sentences.\n\n\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n      Topic: Oral Language Development through Picture Talk\n\n       Activities: \"See & Say,\" \"Picture Question Circle,\" \"My Connection\n\n      Share\"\n\n      Materials:\n          1 large, colorful, detailed picture (e.g., a group of children\n\n            playing at a park with slides, swings, trees, a dog, and ice\n\n          cream vendor)\n\n           Smaller copies of the same picture for each group (optional)\n\n           Whiteboard or chart paper Marker\n\n           \"Question Cards\" (with who/what/where/when/why written on\n\n           them)\n\n              Skills: Speaking, listening, observing, questioning\n\n           Values Integration: Sharing, active listening, appreciating\n\n             others’ experiences\n\nIII.  . LEARNING PROCEDURE\n\n      A. MOTIVATION\n\n           \"Mystery Picture Peek:\" Hold up the large picture with most of it\n\n      covered, showing only a small part (e.g., the dog’s tail).\n\n           Ask: \"What do you think this is? What do you think is in the\n\n       rest of the picture?\"\n\n           Let 3-4 students guess, then reveal the full picture with a big\n\n      \"ta-da!\" to build excitement.','assets/module/page-072.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(73,'                                                        65\n\n\n\nB. LESSON PROPER\n1. ACTIVITY 1: SEE & SAY\n\nDisplay the large picture prominently.\n\nExplain: \"Let’s play \'See & Say\'! Look closely at the picture, and when\n\nyou see something, stand up and say what it is. For example: \'I see a\n\nslide!\'\"\n\nLet students take turns sharing what they notice. As they speak, write\n\nkey words on the board (e.g., children, park, slide, dog, ice cream).\n\nEncourage more details: \"Great! What color is the slide?\" or \"How\n\nmany children do you see?\"\n\nCheck-in: Point to words on the board and ask students to point to\n\nthe corresponding item in the picture.\n\n2. ACTIVITY 2: PICTURE QUESTION CIRCLE\n\nHave students sit in a large circle.\n\nGive out the 5 \"Question Cards\" to 5 different students.\n\nInstructions:\n\n1. The student with the \"who\" card asks: \"Who is in the picture?\"\n\n  Let 2-3 students answer.\n\n2. Pass the card to the next person, then the \"what\" card holder\n\n   asks: \"What are the children doing?\"\n\n3. Continue with \"where\" (\"Where are they?\"), \"when\" (\"When do you\n\n   think this is happening?\"), and \"why\" (\"Why do you think they’re\n\n   happy?\").\n\nModel how to ask questions clearly and how to listen before\n\nanswering. For shy students, offer sentence starters: \"I think they’re\n\nat...\" or \"Maybe it’s because...\"\n\n\n3. ACTIVITY 3: \"MY CONNECTION SHARE\"\n\nExplain: \"Now, think about a time you did something like what’s in\n\nthe picture. Can you share 1 or 2 sentences about it?\"','assets/module/page-073.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(74,'                                                           66\n\n     Model with your own connection: \"I remember going to the park with\n\n    my friend. We played on the swings and ate ice cream!\"\n\n     Let students share in pairs first (to build confidence), then invite 4-5\n\n      pairs to share their connections with the class. Praise all\n\n      contributions: \"That’s a wonderful story! I’m glad you had fun at the\n\n     park too.\"\nC. GENERALIZATION\n\n      Gather students back together and ask: \"What did we learn today\n\n      about pictures?\" (Expected answers: \"Pictures have many things to\n\n      see,\" \"We can ask questions about them,\" \"We can tell stories about\n\n      our own lives from them.\")\n\n     Conclude: \"Pictures are like secret doors to talking and sharing\n\n       stories. You can use any picture to practice speaking!\"\n\nIV. EVALUATION\n     Formative Assessment: Ask each student to say one thing they\n\n     saw in the picture or one connection they made. Use a simple\n\n     smiley face checklist to record if they spoke clearly and\n\n      participated\nV. ASSIGNMENT\n\n     At home, find a picture in a book or on a calendar. Tell your family 3\n\n      things you see in it and one time you did something similar. Be ready\n\n      to share with the class tomorrow!\n\nVI. REFLECTION\n\n(For teacher’s use only: Note which students struggled with\n\nquestioning/sharing connections, which parts of the picture sparked the\n\nmost discussion, and adjustments for future lessons.)\n\nPicture Description (for the main visual):\n\nA bright, sunny park with green grass and tall trees. Three children (2 girls,\n\n1 boy) are playing – one on the slide, one on the swings, and one petting a\n\nbrown dog. An ice cream vendor with a colorful cart stands nearby, handing\n\nan ice cream cone to a girl. There are blue skies with white clouds, and a\n\nbench where a woman is sitting and reading a book.','assets/module/page-074.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(75,'                                                            67\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 9: RECITE, CLAP & SMILE – “Little Voices Shining\n\nThrough Poems\"\n\n\nI. OBJECTIVES\n\nBy the end of the lesson, students will be able to:\n\n      1. Recite a short, familiar poem or nursery rhyme with a clear, loud\n\n            voice.\n\n      2. Match their tone (happy, silly, soft) to the poem’s feeling.\n\n      3. Use simple gestures or actions to go with key words in the poem.\n\n      4. Listen respectfully to peers’ recitations and give a positive \"thumbs\n\n         up.\"\n\nII. MATERIALS\n\n      Picture Poem Pack: Short, colorful poems/nursery rhymes with big\n\n     images (e.g., \"Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star,\" \"Baa, Baa, Black Sheep,\"\n\n      \"Five Little Ducks,\" \"The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\").\n\n     Audio clips of fun, upbeat recitations (with sound effects, e.g., a\n\n     \"quack\" for ducks, \"twinkle\" chimes).\n\n      Visual \"Smile & Shine Recitation”\n\n      Checklist\" (with emojis: 🗣️ = clear voice, 😊 = happy tone, ✋ =\n\n       gestures). Simple props (stuffed animals, toy spiders, paper stars, tiny\n\n       hats) for performances.\n\n    A \"Shining Star\" sticker for each student.\n\nIII. PROCEDURE\n      A. Warm-Up: \"Rhyme & Clap Dance\"\n        Gather students in a circle.\n\n       The teacher says a rhyming word pair (e.g., \"cat – hat\") and\n\n         claps twice. Students repeat the words and clap back.\n\n       Add simple dances: \"dog – frog\" → jump like a frog; \"bird – word\" →\n\n          flap wings.\n\n        Goal: Get them moving, practicing rhymes, and using their voices\n\n          confidently.','assets/module/page-075.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(76,'                                                     68\n\n\nB. Motivation\n                  The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\n\n                        by Anonymous\n\n             Itsy bitsy spider\n\n          climbed up the waterspout.\n\n         down came the rain\n\n          and washed the spider out;\n\n           out came the sun\n\n          and dried up all the rain;\n\n          and the itsy-bitsy spider\n\n          climbed up the spout again.\n\n\n   Hold up a picture poem book, \"The Itsy-Bitsy Spider\" and point\n\n   to the images.\n\n  Ask: \"Who knows this poem? What does the spider do?\"\n\n  Play a 1-minute audio clip of the poem with a silly, wiggly voice and\n\n   \"spider crawl\" sound effects.\n\nC. LESSON PROPER\n\n   \"How did the voice sound? Was it happy? Silly?\"\n\nExplain: \"Today, we’ll pick a poem, practice saying it with our best\n\nvoice, and add dances – just like the audio!\"\n\nMain Activity: \"My Turn to Recite!\" (15 minutes)\n\nBreak into 3 simple, hands-on steps:\n\n      Step 1: Pick & Explore (5 minutes)\n\nLay out the Picture Poem Pack on tables. Students choose a poem\n\nthey know or like (based on the pictures).\n\nThe teacher helps each student point to the words as they say the\n\npoem aloud once (to practice reading along with their voice).\n\nExample: A student picks \"Five Little Ducks\" and points to each duck\n\nas they count down.\n\n      Step 2: Practice with a Buddy & Props (7 minutes)\n\nPair students up (buddy up a confident reciter with a quieter one).','assets/module/page-076.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(77,'                                                            69\n\n\n     Give each pair a prop (e.g., stuffed duck for \"Five Little Ducks,\" toy\n\n      spider for \"Itsy Bitsy\").\n\n     Buddies take turns reciting:\n\n     Use the Smile & Shine Checklist to remind each other: 🗣️ \"Speak\n\n      loud!\" 😊 \"Smile!\" ✋ \"Add a dance!\"\n\n     Example: For \"Twinkle, Twinkle,\" students hold up paper stars and\n\n       twirl them in the air when they say \"twinkle.\"\n\n      Step 3: Polish with the Teacher (3 minutes)\n\n     The teacher circulates to listen to 1–2 lines from each student, giving\n\n      quick feedback: \"Great job wiggling like a spider!\" or \"Try speaking a\n\n        little louder so I can hear you better!\"\n\n      Closure: \"Little Voices Showcase\" (5 minutes)\n\n      Invite 4–5 volunteers to recite their poem for the class (or have all\n\n      pairs share in small \"mini-circles\" if time is tight).\n\n      After each recitation, the class gives a positive response: \"Thumbs up\n\n      for [student’s name]!\" or a quick clap.\n\n     Ask 1–2 students: \"What was fun about reciting your poem?\" (e.g.,\n\n     \"Jumping like a duck!\")\n\n     End with a class cheer: \"Recite, clap, smile – our little voices shine!\"\n\n     Give every student a \"Shining Star\" sticker for participating.\nIV. EVALUATION (Formative)\n\n     Use the visual Smile & Shine Checklist to observe:\n\n          🗣️ Clear, audible voice\n\n    😊 Tone matches the poem’s feeling (happy, silly, etc.)\n\n    ✋ Simple gestures/actions to support key words\n\n          Observe participation in the warm-up, buddy practice, and\n\n           showcase.\n\n          Check that students listen respectfully to peers.','assets/module/page-077.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(78,'                                                            70\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 10: TALK, PLAY & SHARE – LITTLE VOICES\nCONNECTING\n\nCommunicative Language Teaching (CLT) Strategy\n\n\nI. OBJECTIVES\n\nBy the end of the lesson, students will be able to:\n\n       1. Greet peers and ask simple questions (e.g., \"What’s your name?\"\n\n         \"What do you have?\").\n\n        2. Share personal information about a favorite toy (e.g., \"This is my\n\n          teddy. He’s soft.\").\n\n      4. Collaborate with a friend to create a short, simple \"toy story\" using\n\n        spoken words.\n\n      5. Listen respectfully to peers and respond with a simple comment\n\n            (e.g., \"Cool!\" \"I like it too!\").\n\nII. MATERIAL\n\n      Stuffed toys or toy pictures (one per student; or let students bring\n\n       their own favorite toy).\n\n      \"Talking Flashcards\" with simple questions/responses (e.g., 🗣️\n\n      \"What’s this?\" → 🗣️ \"It’s a...\").\n\n    A \"Chat Box\" with small items (toy car, doll, ball) for picking prompts.\n\n      Visual \"Talk & Connect Checklist\" (emojis: 👋 = greet, ❓ = ask, 💬 =\n\n       share, 👂 = listen).\n\n      \"Friendship Sticker\" for each student.\nIII. PROCEDURE\n\n    1. Preparatory Activities\n\n   A. Warm-Up/Motivation: \"Hello, Friend! Circle\" (5 minutes)\n\n      Gather students in a circle.\n\n     The teacher models: \"Hi! My name is [Teacher’s name]. How are you,\n\n      [Student’s name]?\"','assets/module/page-078.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(79,'                                                          71\n\n\n   Students take turns greeting the friend next to them, using the same\n\n    pattern. Add a fun twist: they can wave, high-five, or do a silly dance\n\n   while greeting.\n\n   Goal: Practice real, face-to-face interaction and basic greetings.\n 2. Developmental Activities\n\n   Introduction: \"Our Toys, Our Stories\" (5 minutes)\n\n   Hold up a stuffed toy and say: \"This is my bunny. Her name is Floppy.\n\n   She likes to jump!\"\n\n   Ask: \"Who has a toy at home? What’s your toy like?\" Let 2–3 students\n\n   share quickly.\n3. Analysis/Discussion\n\n   Explain: \"Today, we’ll talk to our friends about our toys—ask them\n\n   questions, share stories, and play together with words!\"\n\n  Show the Talk & Connect Checklist and point to each emoji: \"These\n\n   are our super skills for talking today!\"\n4. Guided Practice\n\n   Main Activity: \"Chat & Play Stations\"\n\n   Divide students into 3 small groups. Rotate stations every 5 minutes\n\n   (keep it fast-paced for little attention spans!).\n\n    Station 1: \"Toy Show & Tell Chat\"\n\n   Task: Students take turns showing their toy (or picture) and sharing\n\n   1–2 things about it (e.g., \"This is my car. It goes ‘vroom!’\").\n\n         Peer Interaction: After each share, friends ask a simple question\n\n   (using flashcards as a guide): \"What color is it?\" or \"Do you like it?\"\n\n  Why it’s CLT: Real sharing and question-asking about personal\n\n    things.\n\n   Station 2: \"Find a Toy Match\"\n\n        Task: Students walk around the station with their toy/picture.\n\n   They ask each other: \"Do you have a [car/doll/teddy]?\"\n\n   Goal: Find a friend with the same type of toy. Once they match, they\n\n   high-five and say: \"We both have a [toy]!\"\n\n  Why it’s CLT: Real-world \"searching and matching\" conversation.','assets/module/page-079.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(80,'                                                            72\n\n      Station 3: \"Make a Toy Story Together\"\n\n     Task: Pairs pick a toy from the Chat Box. They create a 2-sentence\n\n      story together (e.g., \"This is a ball. The ball bounces to the dog!\").\n\n           Bonus: Add simple actions (bounce like a ball, bark like a dog)\n\n      while saying the story.\n\n    Why it’s CLT: Collaborative communication to create something\n\n      meaningful.\n            Closure: \"Share Our Toy Stories\" (5 minutes)\n            Invite 3–4 pairs to share their short toy story with the class.\n\n      After each story, the class responds with a positive comment: \"That’s\n\n      funny!\" or \"I love your ball! “Ask 1–2 students: \"What was fun about\n\n      talking to your friend today?\" (e.g., \"I found a friend with a teddy\n\n       too!\").\n\n     End with a class cheer: \"Talk, play, share—we’re great at caring and\n\n      sharing!\"\n\n     Give every student a \"Friendship Sticker\" for using their talking skills.\nIV. EVALUATION\n\n     Use the visual Talk & Connect Checklist to observe:\n\n            Greets peers appropriately.\n\n          Asks simple questions.\n\n           Shares ideas clearly (even if words are simple).\n\n            Listens when others talk.\n\n     Note how students interact—do they take turns? Do they respond to\n\n       their friends’ words?\n\n    No formal testing—focus on participation and effort to communicate.','assets/module/page-080.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(81,'                                                            73\n\n\n\n    LESSON PLAN 11: ORAL LANGUAGE – WORD FRIENDS (WORD\n                        ASSOCIATION)\n\nI. OBJECTIVE\n\n     At the end of the lesson, the pupils are expected to:\n\n             1. Think of words that go together (e.g., “cat” → “milk,” “paws,”\n\n              “meow”).\n\n             2. Find 3 ways words connect: same meaning, opposites, or\n\n               things that go together.\n\n             3. Share their word friends and explain why they connect.\n\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n      Topic: Word Friends (How Words Connect)\n\n      Materials:\n\n          Big pictures (cat, cake, park, sun).\n\n         Toy props (toy cat, ball, small umbrella).\n\n           Smiley face stickers.\n\n           Simple chart with examples:\n\n         Same meaning: “happy” → “glad.”\n\n            Opposites: “big” → “small.”\n\n           Things that go together: “bread” → “butter.”\n III. PROCEDURE\n\n      1. Warm-Up: “Word Catch!” (5 minutes)\n\n         Game: Toss a soft ball.\n\n           Teacher says a word (e.g., “red”).\n\n           Student catches the ball and says a word friend (e.g., “apple”).\n\n            Class claps if it makes sense!\n      2. Motivation: “Guess My Word Friend!” (5 minutes)\n\n          Hold up a toy cat.\n\n           Ask: “What words do you think of?\n\n         Meow? Milk? Paws?”\n\n            Say: “Today, we’ll play with word friends—words that love to go\n\n             together!”','assets/module/page-081.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(82,'                                                            74\n\n\n      3. Activity 1: “Word Friends on the Board” (10 minutes)\n\n           Step 1: Stick a picture of a cat on the board.\n\n           Ask: “What words go with cat?” Students shout out (e.g., “milk,”\n\n              “tail”). Add their words with sticky notes.\n\n           Step 2: Show how words connect:\n\n                 “Milk” → goes with cat.\n                  “Small” → opposite of big.\n\n               “Happy” → same as glad.\n      4. Activity 2: “Word Chain Game” (10 minutes)\n\n          Groups of 4: Sit in circles.\n\n           Teacher gives a starting word (e.g., “sun”).\n\n          Each student adds a word friend (e.g., “sun” → “hot” → “ice\n\n              cream” → “sprinkles”).\n\n     Use props (e.g., toy umbrella for “rain”) to help them think!\n      5. Wrap-Up: “Why Word Friends?” (5 minutes)\n\n           Ask:\n\n         “Why is ‘cat’ friends with ‘milk’?” (They go together!)\n\n        “How do word friends help us talk?” (They make stories fun!)\n\n            Sticker Time: Give everyone a smiley sticker for participating!\n\nIV. EVALUATION\n\n      Observe:\n\n          Did the student share a word friend?\n\n          Did they try to explain the connection (even with gestures)?\n\n           Symbols:\n\n      🌟 = Shared 3+ words and explained.\n\n     👍 = Shared 1-2 words.\n\n      😊 = Tried their best!\n\nV. HOMEWORK\n\n      1. Draw & Tell: Draw a cat or sun and tell your family 2-word friends.\n\n      2. Bring a Prop: Bring a small toy or picture of their favorite word\n\n         friend for show-and-tell next class.','assets/module/page-082.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(83,'                                                            75\n\n\n    TEACHER TIPS\n\n     Use actions (e.g., meowing for “cat”) to help explain connections.\n     Keep it silly! If a student says “cat → spaceship,” ask, “Why?” and\n\n     laugh together.\n\n      Sing a goodbye song: “Bye-bye, word friends, see you soon!”\n\n\n\n\nLESSON PLAN 12: ORAL LANGUAGE – FUN WITH TONGUE TWISTERS\n\n\nI. OBJECTIVE\n\n     At the end of the lesson, the pupils are expected to:\n\n             1. Say simple tongue twisters clearly, focusing on sounds like\n\n               /p/, /b/, and /s/.\n\n             2. Practice saying tongue twisters slowly, then faster.\nII. SUBJECT MATTER\n\n     Topic: Tongue Twisters for Clear Speaking\n\n      References: Grade 1 Phonics Activities\n\n      Materials:\n\n          Big posters with 3 tongue twisters (with pictures):\n\n             1. “Peter Piper picked pickled peppers.” (Picture: Peter and\n\n                  peppers)\n\n             2. “Betty Botter bought butter.” (Picture: Betty and butter)\n\n             3. “Six snails slide slowly.” (Picture: snails sliding)\n\n         Sound cards with pictures (e.g., popcorn for /p/, ball for /b/,\n\n           snake for /s/).\n\n          “Tongue Twister Stars/Badge” stickers.\n\n         Thumbs-up chart for group activities.','assets/module/page-083.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(84,'                                                           76\n\n\nIII. PROCEDURE\n      1. Preparatory Activities\n\n            A. Warm-Up (5 minutes):\n                  Play “Copy the Sound!”:\n\n                Teacher says: “/p/ like popping popcorn!” (Students\n\n                mimic popping and say /p/).\n\n               Repeat with /b/ (bounce like a ball) and /s/ (slither like a\n\n                  snake).\n\n            B. Motivation (3 minutes):\n\n                 Say: “Let’s play with silly sentences that twist our\n\n                  tongues! Who can say ‘Peter Piper’ fast without mixing up\n\n                words?”\n\n      2. Developmental Activities\n\n            Introduce Tongue Twisters:\n\n         Show the poster and read “Peter Piper” slowly. Clap for each\n\n            word.\n\n           Point to the /p/ sound card every time you say “p.”\n\n          Say it faster like a robot! 🤖\n\n          Group Practice:\n\n           Repeat with “Betty Botter” (/b/ sound) and “Six snails” (/s/\n\n               sound).\n\n           Students take turns saying one line in pairs.\n      3. Analysis/Discussion\n\n           Ask:\n\n         “Why is ‘Peter Piper’ tricky to say?” (They’ll say: “Too many /p/\n\n              sounds!”).\n\n        “How do tongue twisters help us?” (Answer: “They make our\n\n           mouths strong for clear talking!”).\n      4. Guided Practice\n\n          Tongue Twister Game:\n\n            In small groups, students pick a tongue twister.','assets/module/page-084.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(85,'                                                            77\n\n\n\n          Say it slowly, then fast. Friends give a thumbs up if they hear\n\n            the sounds clearly!\n\n         Add fun: Hop or clap while saying it!\n\n\nIV. EVALUATION\n\n          Use a simple checklist:\n\n\n     Give “Tongue Twister Star” stickers to everyone who tries!\n\n\n Criteria    🌟 (Great!)   👍 (Good!)       😊(Keep Trying!)\n\n Says sounds\n\n clearly\n\n Tries slow and\n\n fast\n\n Participates with\n\n a smile\n\n\nV. AGREEMENT\n\n           For homework:\n\n      1. Practice one tongue twister with your family.\n\n      2. Draw a picture of your favorite tongue twister (e.g., Peter Piper or\n\n           snails).\n\n\nNOTE FOR TEACHER:\n\n     Keep it playful! Praise effort over perfection.\n\n     Use lots of movements (clapping, hopping) to make sounds\n\n     memorable','assets/module/page-085.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [15.25, 771.8499755859375, 574.260009765625, 828.7999877929688], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(86,'','assets/module/page-086.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}, {\"path\": \"assets/module/image-473.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [66.94999694824219, 316.45001220703125, 511.5999755859375, 477.69000244140625], \"xref\": 473, \"width\": 1379, \"height\": 500}]'),
(87,'                                                               78\n\n\n  LESSON 1: TALKING ABOUT ONE’S SELF AND ONE’S FAMILY\n\n  POST-TEST (5 items)\n\n   Assesses ability to share organized, detailed information.\n\n   1. “Introduce yourself: tell me your name, age, and one thing that makes\n\n  you happy.”\n\n   2. “Describe your family—how many members are there, and what does your\n\n   family like to do together?”\n\n   3. “Tell me about a time you had fun with your family.”\n\n   4. “What is something special about one of your family members?”\n\n   5. “Share one thing you love about your home.”\n\n                                                           Needs\n                  Excellent (4pts)                     Satisfactory\n Criteria                      Good (3pts)                 Improvement\n                                                        (2pts)\n                                                                            (1pt)\n\n\n\n                 Covers self (name,   Covers self     Covers self +   Only shares\n\n Completeness    age, likes)                       1 family        1-2 small                              +2 family\n                 +3+family                                detail            facts about\n                                  members/det\n                 members/details                                           self\n                                                ails\n\n\n\n Clarity         Speech is clear,     Mostly clear   Somewhat     Hard to\n\n                   loud, and easy to   with minor     unclear or      understand;\n\n                understand        pauses          quiet         mumbles\n\n\n                 Shares info in a     Mostly logical  Somewhat    No clear order\n\n                   logical order        order           disorganized\nOrganization\n                       (self-first, then\n\n                    family)','assets/module/page-087.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(88,'                                                            79\n\nLESSON 2: FOLLOW ONE-TO-TWO-STEP PHYSICAL DIRECTIONS\n\n(e.g., “Go to the door, close it, and run back here”)\n\nPOST-TEST (5 items)\n\nAssesses ability to follow clear 2-step directions accurately.\n\n1. “Close the door gently, and walk back to your seat.” (2-step)\n\n2. “Open your book to page 5, and put it on your lap.” (2-step)\n\n3. “Jump twice, and sit down quietly.” (2-step)\n\n4. “Draw a circle on the board and return to your seat.” (2-step)\n\n5. “Give one crayon to your seatmate and keep the other.” (2-step)\n\n\n                                                   Needs\n                   Excellent                  Satisfactory\n    Criteria               Good (3pts)              Improvement\n                    (4pts)                       (2pts)\n                                                                  (1pt)\n\n                 Performs all   Performs    Performs 1-2  Performs few\n    Correctness\n                    steps exactly   all steps      steps          or no steps\n    of Steps\n                   as           minor        correctly       correctly\n\n                    instructed     error\n\n                 Follows the   Follows    Mixes up    No sense of\n\n    Order of      exact order   order with   most steps     order\n\n    Steps         given       1 small\n\n                             mix-up\n\n                Completes   Completes   Takes time    Hesitates\n\n                   steps         steps at      to            heavily or\n    Timeliness\n                  quickly and   steady        start/finish    refuses to try\n\n                    confidently    pace','assets/module/page-088.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(89,'                                                            80\n\n\nLESSON 3: GIVE & FOLLOW ONE-TO-TWO-STEP VERBAL DIRECTIONS\n\nPOST-TEST (5 items)\n\nAssesses ability to give clear 2-step verbal instructions.\n\n“Give at least two instructions”\n\n\n1. “Ask your younger sister to keep her toys away—say two instructions in\n\none sentence.” (e.g., “Please pick up your toys and put them in the box.”)\n\n2. “Tell your friend how to get a book from the shelf and give it to you—use\n\ntwo instructions.”\n\n3. “What would you say to ask someone to set the table: one instruction for\n\nthe plate, one for the fork?”\n\n4. “Ask your brother to turn off the TV and come to dinner—say it clearly\n\nwith two steps.”\n\n5. “Tell me two instructions to ask a classmate to clean up their desk.”\n\n\n                                                  Needs\n               Excellent                      Satisfactory\n Criteria               Good (3pts)                Improvement\n                (4pts)                          (2pts)\n                                                                 (1pt)\n\n              Gives 2+     Gives 2         Gives a     Gives 0-1\n\n Number of    clear,          instructions (1   clear        vague/unclear\n\n Instructions   distinct        slightly          instruction   instruction\n\n                instructions   unclear)\n\n             Language is  Mostly      A bit too     Confusing, too\n\n               simple,       simple/friendly  complex or   formal, or\n\n Clarity &      friendly,      with minor       stern         rude\n\n Tone        and easy for   formality\n\n              a child to\n\n             understand\n\n                Instructions  Mostly relevant  Slightly off-   Unrelated to\n\n                directly       to the task        topic         the task\n\n Relevance     relate to\n\n              “keeping\n\n               toys away”','assets/module/page-089.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(90,'                                                             81\n\n\n LESSON 4: RECOGNIZE DESCRIBING WORDS\n\n (color, shape, size, height, weight, length, distance, etc.)\n\n POST-TEST (5 items)\n\n Assesses ability to use specific describing words.\n 1. “Describe this teddy bear—tell me its color, size, and how it feels.”\n\n 2. “Look at this square book—tell me two more words to describe it (e.g.,\n\n heavy, thick).”\n\n 3. “Describe this small, blue car—use three describing words.”\n\n 4. “Look at this long measuring tape—what word tells us how far it\n\n stretches?”\n\n 5. “Describe your classroom—use two words for its size and one for its\n\n color.”\n\n\n\n                                                    Needs\n                        Good (3pts)    Satisfactory\nCriteria       Excellent                             Improveme\n                                                 (2pts)                 (4pts)\n                                                          nt\n\n                                                                   (1pt)\n               All words are   Most words   Some words  No relevant\n\n               exactly          are relevant     are relevant   describing\nRelevance\n               describing the                             words\n\n               item/place/per\n             son\n              Uses 4+        Uses    2-3   Uses 1 type   Repeats the\n\n                 different         different        of descriptor  same word or\n\nVariety        types of         types                        uses none\n\n                 descriptors\n\n                   (e.g. color +\n\n               shape\n\n                 +size)\n               Descriptors are  Mostly correct  Partially      Mostly\n\n           100% correct   with 1 minor    correct         incorrect\n\nAccuracy                     mistake','assets/module/page-090.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(91,'                                                            82\n\n\nLESSON 5: TALK ABOUT TOPICS OF INTEREST\n\n (e.g., “Tell me what you think about this picture”)\n\nPOST-TEST (5 items)\n\nAssesses ability to share detailed, organized thoughts.\n1. “I’m showing you a picture of a birthday party—tell me what you think\n\nabout it.”\n\n2. “Tell me about your favorite game—how to play it and why you like it.”\n\n3. “Look at this picture of a rainbow—what do you feel when you see a\n\nrainbow? Why?”\n\n4. “What is your favorite subject in school? Explain what you like to do\n\nduring your class.”\n\n5. “Look at this picture of a forest—what would you explore there and why?”\n\n\n                                                      Needs\n\n   Criteria       Excellent    Good (3pts)   Satisfactory   Improvement\n                      (4pts)                         (2pts)             (1pt)\n\n               Talks only\n\n               about         Mostly     Somewhat off-   Unrelated to\n\nRelevance       details/ideas    relevant to   topic at times    the picture\n\n             from the       the picture\n\n                 picture\n\n               Shares 3+\n\n                ideas/opinion   Shares 2    Shares 1       Only says 1-2\n\n                 s (e.g., “I like   ideas/opinio simple         words (e.g.,\n\nDepth of        the cat         ns           idea/observati    “It’s nice”)\n\nthought        because it’s              on\n\n                      fluffy”)\n\n              Speech is      Mostly     Somewhat\n\n                  clear, with      clear with    unclear or     Hard to\n\nClarity        good eye      minor        quiet           understand\n\n                contact        pauses','assets/module/page-091.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(92,'                                                               83\n\n\n  LESSON 6: DESCRIBE AND DRAW\n\n   (Show children a picture; they will describe it so someone else can draw it)\n\n   POST-TEST (5 items)\n\n   Assesses ability to describe with details so listener can draw accurately.\n\n    1. “I’m showing you a picture of a cat sitting on a chair—describe it: color of\n\n    the cat, shape of the chair, where the cat is sitting.”\n\n    2. “Describe this picture of a tree with apples—tell me the tree’s color, how\n\n   many apples, and if the apples are big or small.”\n\n    3. “I’m showing you a picture of a boy playing with a ball—describe the boy’s\n\n    clothes, the ball’s color, and what he’s doing.”\n\n    4. “Describe this picture of a book on a table—tell me the book’s color, the\n\n    table’s shape, and where the book is on the table.”\n\n    5. “I’m showing you a picture of a rainbow over a lake—describe the colors of\n\n    the rainbow, the lake’s color, and where the rainbow is.”\n                                                           Needs\n\n    Criteria     Excellent (4pts)   Good (3pts)     Satisfactory  Improvement\n\n                                                         (2pts)             (1pt)\n\n               Describes 5+\n\n              key elements     Describes 3-4    Describes 1-2   Describes no\n\n                 (people, objects,  key elements    key elements   key elements\n\n Detail          colors\n\n                Description is                                    Impossible to\n\n               so clear,         Mostly clear     Somewhat      understand\n\n             someone could   enough to        confusing       what’s being\n\n Clarity       draw it           follow                            described\n\n                 easily\n\n               Describes in a\n\n                 logical order     Mostly logical   Somewhat     No clear order\n\n                    (e.g., left to       order            disorganized\n\nOrganization    right, top to\n\n               bottom)','assets/module/page-092.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(93,'                                                          84\n\nLESSON 7: USING STORY CHAIN\n\n(Each student has their own story; pair up to tell it in first person, then\n\nswitch pairs to retell the partner’s story)\n\nPOST-TEST (5 items)\n\nAssesses ability to tell own story clearly and retell a partner’s story\n\nAccurately.\n\n(Setup: Give student a short made-up story to practice first, then pair with a\n\nclassmate)\n\n1. “Tell your partner your story in first person—speak clearly so they can\n\nhear all the details.”\n\n2. “Now, retell your partner’s story to me—include at least two key details\n\n(e.g., who was in it, what happened).”\n\n3. “Tell your story again—make sure to start with ‘I’ and keep it in order.”\n\n4. “Retell your partner’s story—use ‘he/she/they’ when talking about the\n\npeople in it.”\n\n5. “Tell me one key detail from your partner’s story that you liked.”\n\nLESSON 8: USING PICTURE TALK\n\nPost-Test\n 1. Tell a short story (2-3 sentences) about the picture.\n\n2. What might happen after the scene in the picture?\n\n3. How would you feel if you were in this picture?\n\nLESSON 9: USING POEMS\n\nPost-Test\n\n 1. Recite the entire poem with actions.\n\n2. What is the poem mostly about? (Example: \"It’s about rain.\")\n\n3. Draw a picture of your favorite part of the poem.\n\nLESSON 10: USING TALK, PLAY AND SHARE\n\nPost-Test\n\n1. Create a group story: Each student adds one sentence.\n\n(Example: \"Once, a dog met a cat...\")\n\n2. Act out a problem-solving scenario (e.g., sharing crayons).\n\n3. Ask a friend: \"What did you enjoy doing today?\"','assets/module/page-093.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(94,'                                                            85\n\n\n LESSON 11: USING TONGUE TWISTERS\n\n POST-TEST (5 items)\n\n Assesses ability to say tongue twisters clearly and with better pace.\n\n 1. “Say ‘Red lorry, yellow lorry’ two times in a row—speak clearly, not too\n\n fast.”\n\n 2. “Say ‘She sells seashells by the seashore’ once—make sure I can hear\n\n every word.”\n\n 3. “Say ‘Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers’ slowly, then a little\n\n faster (still clear!).”\n\n 4. “Say ‘Six sick sheep shepherds’ sheep’ two times—don’t mix up\n\n the words.”\n\n 5. “Say ‘How much wood would a woodchuck chuck?’ once—speak with a\n\n steady pace.”\n\n\n                                                        Needs\n\n   Criteria      Excellent     Good (3pts)    Satisfactory  Improvement\n\n                    (4pts)                            (2pts)             (1pt)\n\n              Pronounces              Some       Many\n\n                   all words     Most correct    mispronunciat  mispronunciation\n\nPronunciation  correctly      pronunciation   ions but still    s; hard to follow\n\n              (even at                         clear\n\n               speed)\n\n              Says the      Says it\n\n              tongue        smoothly      Says it with    Stumbles\n\n                 twister        once, with 1     2- 3 pauses    heavily or can’t\n\nFluency       smoothly 2+   pause                              finish\n\n               times\n\n               Tries           Tries willingly,\n\nEffort &        enthusiastic   mostly          Tries but is    Reluctant to try\n\nConfidence      ally, speaks   loud            quiet/hesitant  or gives up\n\n                loudly','assets/module/page-094.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]'),
(95,'                                                          86\n\n\n\nLESSON 12: USING WORD ASOCIATION\n\nPOST-TEST (5 items)\n\nAssesses ability to think of multiple related words and explain connections\n\n1. “I say ‘dog’—tell me two words related to ‘dog’ and explain why (e.g., ‘bone’\n\nbecause dogs like bones).”\n\n2. “I say ‘school’—tell me three words that go with ‘school’.”\n\n3. “I say ‘rain’—tell me two words related to ‘rain’ and say what they have to\n\ndo with rain.”\n\n4. “I say ‘toy’—tell me three words that make you think of ‘toy’.”\n\n5. “I say ‘food’—tell me two words related to ‘food’ and explain why.”','assets/module/page-095.jpg','[{\"path\": \"assets/module/image-12.jpeg\", \"bbox\": [27.100000381469727, 768.5499877929688, 586.1099853515625, 825.5], \"xref\": 12, \"width\": 2079, \"height\": 158}]');
